</table>', NULL, '[{"text":"(A) - (I), (B) - (II), (C) - (III), (D) - (IV)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A) - (II), (B) - (I), (C) - (III), (D) - (IV)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A) - (I), (B) - (II), (C) - (IV), (D) - (III)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A) - (III), (B) - (IV), (C) - (I), (D) - (II)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '(A) Kolbe''s Reaction -> Salicylic acid (II); (B) Reimer-Tiemann Reaction -> Salicylaldehyde (I); (C) Williamson synthesis -> Ethyl methyl ether (III); (D) Cumene Process -> Phenol (IV). Mapping: (A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV).', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.050Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (264, 5, NULL, NULL, 'Which structure of proteins gives rise to fibrous and globular proteins?', NULL, '[{"text":"Primary Structure","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Secondary Structure","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Tertiary Structure","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Quaternary Structure","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '• Tertiary structure refers to the overall three-dimensional folding of a single polypeptide chain. This unique 3D shape, determined by various interactions (hydrogen bonding, disulfide bridges, hydrophobic interactions, ionic bonds), is what dictates whether a protein takes on afibrous (elongated, insoluble) orglobular (compact, soluble) form.

• Primary structure is just the amino acid sequence.

• Secondary structure refers to local folding patterns like alpha-helices and beta-sheets.

- Quaternary structure involves the arrangement of multiple polypeptide subunits, but the fundamental fibrous/globular nature is established at the tertiary level for each subunit.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.050Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (265, 5, NULL, NULL, 'The function of the enzymes in various chemical reactions:', NULL, '[{"text":"Increase activation energy","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Biochemical enhancers","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Decreases the rate of reaction","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Act as biological catalysts","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Enzymes are biological catalysts. Their primary function is to speed up (increase the rate of) biochemical reactions by lowering the activation energy required for the reaction to proceed.

They do not increase activation energy (Option 1 is incorrect).

They do not decrease the rate of reaction (Option 3 is incorrect).', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.051Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (266, 5, NULL, NULL, 'The units of rate constant of four reactions are given below. Arrange them in the increasing order of re

$$ (A)\mathbf{s}^{-1} $$

$$ (B)\mathrm{m o l}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1} $$

$$ (C)mol^{1/2}L^{-1/2}s^{-1} $$

$$ (D)L^{1/2}mol^{-1/2}s^{-1} $$', NULL, '[{"text":"(A), (B), (C), (D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(D),(A),(C),(B)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B),(A),(D),(C)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B),(C),(A),(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'The general unit for a rate constant $ k $ for an $ n^{\text{th}} $ order reaction is:

Units of $ k = (\text{mol L}^{-1})^{1-n} \text{s}^{-1} $ or more commonly $ \text{L}^{n-1} \text{mol}^{1-n} \text{s}^{-1} $

Let''s find the order (n) for each unit:

$$ \left(\mathbf{A}\right)\mathrm{s}^{-1} $$

Compare to $ (\text{mol L}^{-1})^{1-n}\text{s}^{-1} $. For units of $ \text{mol L}^{-1} $ to disappear, 1 - n = 0.

$$ 1-n=0\Rightarrow n=1 $$

Order = 1 (First Order)

$$ (\mathbf{B})\mathrm{m o l}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1} $$

Compare to $ (\mathrm{mol~L}^{-1})^{1-n}\mathrm{s}^{-1} $. For $ mol~L^{-1} $ to have a power of 1, 1-n=1.

$$ 1-n=1\Rightarrow n=0 $$

$$ \mathrm{O r d e r}=0\ (\mathrm{Z e r o O r d e r}) $$

$$ \mathrm{(C)}\mathrm{m o l}^{1/2}\mathrm{L}^{-1/2}\mathrm{s}^{-1} $$

This is equivalent to $ (\text{mol L}^{-1})^{1/2} \text{s}^{-1} $.

$$ 1-n=\frac{1}{2}\Rightarrow n=1-\frac{1}{2}=\frac{1}{2} $$

$$ Order=\frac{1}{2}\ (Half~Order) $$

$$ \mathrm{L^{1/2}m o l^{-1/2}s^{-1}} $$

This is equivalent to $ (\text{mol L}^{-1})^{-1/2}\text{s}^{-1} $.

$$ 1-n=-\frac{1}{2}\Rightarrow n=1+\frac{1}{2}=\frac{3}{2} $$

Order = $ \frac{3}{2} $ (One and a half Order)

Increasing Order of Reaction:

0<0.5<1<1.5. So, (B)<(C)<(A)<(D)', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.051Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (267, 5, NULL, NULL, 'Ethanolic acid can be converted into ethanol using reagent', NULL, '[{"text":"Sn + HCl","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Na and C₂H₅OH","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"LiAlH₄ in H₂O","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"H₂ and Pt","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.051Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'none', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (268, 5, NULL, NULL, 'Calculate the molality of 25 g of ethanoic acid (CH₃COOH) in 250 g benzene', NULL, '[{"text":"16.64 mol kg $ ^{-1} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"1.664 mol kg $ ^{-1} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"166.4 mol kg $ ^{-1} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"6.64 mol kg $ ^{-1} $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Formula for Molality (m): $ m = \frac{\text{moles of solute}}{\text{mass of solvent (in kg)}} $

Calculate Moles of Solute (Ethanole Acid, $ CH_{3}COOH $):

Molar mass of $ CH_{3}COOH $:

C = 12.01 g/mol,

H = 1.008 g/mol,

$$ O=16.00g/mol $$

Molar mass = 2(12.01) + 4(1.008) + 2(16.00) = 24.02 + 4.032 + 32.00 = 60.052 g/mol

$$ \mathrm{M o l e s}=\frac{\mathrm{m a s s}}{\mathrm{m o l a r m a s s}} $$

$$ \mathrm{Moles~of~CH_{3}COOH}=\frac{25\mathrm{g}}{60.052\mathrm{g/mol}}\approx0.4163\mathrm{mol} $$

Convert Mass of Solvent to kg:

$$ Mass of benzene=250g=0.250kg $$

$$ Calculate~Molality:\quad m=\frac{0.4163mol}{0.250kg}=1.6652mol kg^{-1} $$', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.052Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (269, 5, NULL, NULL, '_ is the rate of a reaction at a particular moment of time.', NULL, '[{"text":"Order of reaction","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Average rate","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Instantaneous rate","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Rate constant","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '• Instantaneous rate is defined as the rate of a chemical reaction at a specific moment in time. It''s determined by finding the slope of the tangent to the concentration-time curve at that particular instant.

• Average rate is the rate over a measurable period of time.

• Order of reaction describes how the reaction rate depends on reactant concentrations.

• Rate constant is a proportionality constant in the rate law.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.052Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (270, 5, NULL, NULL, 'The order of increasing boiling point for the following compounds is

(A) CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃

(B) CH₃CH₂CH₂ CH₂OH

(C) $ C_{2}H_{5}OC_{2}H_{5} $

(D) CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO', NULL, '[{"text":"(A), (B), (C), (D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A), (C), (B), (D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A), (C), (D), (B)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C), (B), (D), (A)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Boiling point increases with stronger intermolecular forces (IMFs).

Boiling point increases with stronger intermolecular forces (IMFs).

(A) $ CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}CH_{3} $ (Alkane): Only weak London Dispersion Forces (LDFs). (Lowest BP)

(C) $ C_{2}H_{5}OC_{2}H_{5} $ (Ether): LDFs and moderate dipole-dipole forces. No H-bonding.

(D) $ CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}CHO $ (Aldehyde): LDFs and stronger dipole-dipole forces (due to polar C=O). No H-bonding.

(B) $ CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}CH_{2}OH $ (Alcohol): Strongest forces due to hydrogen bonding, plus dipole-dipole and LDFs. (Highest BP)

Order: Alkane < Ether < Aldehyde < Alcohol', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.052Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (271, 5, NULL, NULL, 'Write the IUPAC name for the coordination compound:

[Cr(NH3)3(H2O)3]Cl3', NULL, '[{"text":"triamminetriaquachromium (III) chloride","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"triaquatriamminechromium (III) chloride","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"tris(amminetriaqua)chromium (III) chloride","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"triammine-tris(aqua)chromium (III) chloride","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '1. Ligands: "ammine" (NH $ _{3} $) and "aqua" (H $ _{2} $O). Both are neutral.

2. Count & Alphabetize: 3 ammine, 3 aqua. "ammine" comes before "aqua". ⇒triammine, triqua.

3. Metal: Chromium (Cr).

4. Oxidation State: NH $ _{3} $(0) + H $ _{2} $O (0) + Cr (x) = +3 (due to 3Cl $ ^{-} $ counterions) $ \Rightarrow $ x = +3 (Chromium(III)).

5. Counter-ion: Chloride.

Final Name: triammine-triaqua-chromium(III) chloride.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.053Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (272, 5, NULL, NULL, '50 g of ethylene glycol $ (\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{2}) $ is mixed with 900 g of water. The freezing point of the solution will be', NULL, '[{"text":"271.49 K","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"270.15 K","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"269.52 K","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"273.15 K","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '1. Molar Mass of Ethylene Glycol $ (\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{2}) $: 62.07 g/mol

2. Moles of Solute: 50 g/62.07 g/mol ≈ 0.8055 mol

3. Molality (m): 0.8055 mol/0.900 kg ≈ 0.895 mol/kg

4. Freezing Point Depression $ \left(\Delta T_{f}\right) $:

$$ \Delta T_{f}=i\times K_{f}\times m $$

$$ i=1\mathrm{(n o n-e l e c t r o l y t e)} $$

$$ \Delta T_{f}=1\times1.86\mathrm{~K~k g/m o l}\times0.895\mathrm{~m o l/k g}\approx1.665\mathrm{~K~} $$

5. Freezing Point of Solution $ (T_{f}) $

$$ T_{f}^{0}(\mathrm{w a t e r})=273.15\mathrm{K} $$

$$ T_{f}=T_{f}^{0}-\Delta T_{f}=273.15\mathrm{~K}-1.665\mathrm{~K}=271.485\mathrm{~K} $$

This value is approximately 271.49 K.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.053Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (273, 5, NULL, NULL, 'The major product of the following reaction is', NULL, '[{"text":"","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Anisole undergoes Friedel-Crafts reaction, i.e., the alkyl and acyl groups are introduced at ortho and para positions by reaction with alkyl halide and acyl halide in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride (a Lewis acid) as catalyst.', NULL, NULL, 2025, '/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2025-05-23-shift-2/q40-figure-1.jpg', 'Figure for question 40', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.053Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2025-05-23-shift-2/q40-figure-1.jpg","alt":"Figure for question 40","kind":"figure"},{"url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2025-05-23-shift-2/q40-options.jpg","alt":"Printed answer choices for question 40","kind":"options"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (274, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the relational algebra operation denoted by:

Course X Student:

where ''Course'' and ''Student'' are two relations and X is an operation.', NULL, '[{"text":"Union","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Set Difference","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Cartesian Product","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Intersection","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '. Cartesian Product

- The Cartesian Product combines every tuple of the first relation with every tuple of the second relation.

• Example:

Course = {C1, C2}, Student = {S1, S2, S3}

• Course $ \times $ Student = $ \{(C1,S1),(C1,S2),(C1,S3),(C2,S1),(C2,S2),(C2,S3)\} $

Other operations like Union, Intersection, or Set Difference do not combine all tuples in this way.

Tip: Whenever you see $ \times $ in relational algebra, think "every combination" $ \rightarrow $ Cartesian Product.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.055Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
