Option 1 is a MAC address, option 2 has 256 (invalid), option 4 has -1 (invalid), so only option 3 is correct.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.058Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (285, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Arrange the following python code segments in order with respect to exception handling.

(A) except ZeroDivisionError:

print("Zero denominator not allowed")

(B) finally:

print("Over and Out")

(C) try:

n=50

d=int(input("enter denominator"))

d/n=q

print("division performed")

(D) else:

print("Result="，q)', NULL, '[{"text":"(C),(A),(B),(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C),(A),(D),(B)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B),(A),(D),(C)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C),(B),(D),(A)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '. (C), (A), (D), (B)
Explanation: In Python:

• try: block comes first to execute code that may raise an exception.

except: block handles specific exceptions if they occur.

- else: block executes if no exception occurs.

- finally: block executes always, whether an exception occurred or not.

So the sequence is: $ \text{try} \rightarrow \text{except} \rightarrow \text{else} \rightarrow \text{finally} $.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.058Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (286, 7, NULL, NULL, '_ is the process of transforming data or an object in memory (RAM) to a stream of bytes called byte streams.', NULL, '[{"text":"read()","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"write()","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Pickling","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"De-serialization","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '. Pickling.

- Pickling is the process of converting a Python object in memory into a byte stream so it can be saved to a file or sent over a network.

- De-serialization (or unpickling) is the reverse process-converting byte streams back into Python objects.

- read() and write() are file operations, not object-to-byte conversion processes.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.058Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (287, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the correct code to read data from the file notes.dat in a binary file:', NULL, '[{"text":"import pickle f1=open(\"notes.dat\",\"r\") data=pickle.load(f1) print(data) f1.close()","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"import pickle f1=open(\"notes.dat\",\"rb\") data=f1.load() print(data) f1.closen","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"import pickle f1=open(\"notes.dat\",\"rb\") data=pickle.load(f1) print(data) f1.close()","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"import pickle f1=open(\"notes.dat\", \"rb\") data=f1.read() print(data) f1.close()","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '.

- To read data from a binary file using pickle, the file must be opened in binary read mode ("rb").

- The pickle.load() function is used to load the object from the file.

- Other options are incorrect because:

• Option 1: Opens in text mode "r", not binary.

- Option 2: Uses f1.load() instead of pickle.load(f1) and has a typo (f1.closen).

- Option 4: Uses f1.read(), which reads raw bytes but doesn''t deSERIALIZE the object.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.059Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (288, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the correct python statement to open a text file "data.txt" in both read and write mode.', NULL, '[{"text":"file.open(\"data.txt\")","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"file.open(\"data.txt\",\"r+\")","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"file.open(\"data.txt\".rw\")","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"file.open(\"data.txt\",\"rw+\")","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '. file.open("data.txt","r+").

• "r+" mode opens a file for both reading and writing.

• "rw" and "rw+" are invalid modes in Python.

• file.open("data.txt") opens the file in read-only mode by default.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.059Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (289, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the type of expression where operators are placed before the corresponding operands:', NULL, '[{"text":"Polish expression","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Infix expression","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Postfix expression","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Reverse polish expression","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '. Polish expression.

- In a Polish expression (prefix notation), the operator comes before its operands.

○ Example: +53 means 5 + 3.

- Infix expression: Operator is between operands $(5 + 3)$.

• Postfix expression: Operator is after operands (5 +).

- Reverse Polish expression is another name for postfix notation, not prefix.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.060Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (290, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Evaluate the postfix expression:

2457*5/+', NULL, '[{"text":"29","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"30","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"31","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"0","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '.31

1. Postfix evaluation: Operands are pushed to a stack; operators act on the most recent operands.

Expression: 24 5 7 * 5 / +

Step 1: Push 24 → Stack: [24]

• Step 2: Push 5 → Stack: [24, 5]

Step 3: Push 7 $ \rightarrow $ Stack: [24, 5, 7]

• Step 4: * → Pop 7 and 5, multiply: $ 5 * 7 = 35 \rightarrow $ Push 35 → Stack: [24, 35]

Step 5: Push 5 $ \rightarrow $ Stack: [24, 35, 5]

Step 6: / $ \rightarrow $ Pop 5 and 35, divide: 35 / 5 = 7 $ \rightarrow $ Push 7 $ \rightarrow $ Stack: [24, 7]

• Step 7: + → Pop 7 and 24, add: 24 + 7 = 31 → Push 31 → Stack: [31]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.060Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (291, 7, NULL, NULL, 'STACK follows _ principle, where insertion and deletion is from end/ends only.', NULL, '[{"text":"LIFO, two","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"FIFO, two","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"HFO, top","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"LIFO, one","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '. LIFO, one.

• A STACK follows the LIFO (Last In, First Out) principle.

- Insertion (push) and deletion (pop) happen from one end only, called the top of the stack.

- FIFO (First In, First Out) applies to queues, not stacks.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.061Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (292, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Given a scenario:

Suppose there is a web-server hosting a website to declare results. This server can handle a maximum of 100 concurrent requests to view results, So, as to serve thousands of user requests. a _ would be the most appropriate data structure to use.', NULL, '[{"text":"Stack","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Queue","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"List","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Dictionary","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '. Queue.

- A queue follows the FIFO (First In, First Out) principle, which is ideal for handling requests in the order they arrive.

In the given scenario, users'' requests to view results should be served in the order they arrive, especially when the server has a limit on concurrent requests.

- Stack (LIFO) would serve the most recent request first, which is not fair.

- List and Dictionary don''t inherently manage order or concurrency like a queue does.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.062Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (293, 7, NULL, NULL, 'To perform enqueue and dequeue efficiently on a queue, which of the following operations are required?

A) isEmpty

B) peek

C) isfull

D) update', NULL, '[{"text":"(A), (B) and (D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A), (B) and (C) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B) (C) and (D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A), (C) and (D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '. (A), (B) and (C) only.

- isEmpty (A): Checks if the queue is empty before performing dequeue to avoid underflow.

- isFull (C): Checks if the queue is full before performing enqueue to avoid overflow.

- peek (B): Allows viewing the front element without removing it, which is often needed in efficient queue operations.

• update (D): Not a standard operation for basic queue functionality.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.063Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (294, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Distance table:

Identify the correct place where we have to use repeaters.', NULL, '[{"text":"Between A to B","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Between A to C","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Between A to D","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Between A to E","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '. Between A to D.

- In networking, repeaters are used when the signal distance exceeds the maximum allowable range for proper transmission.

• From the distance table:

○ A to B = 20 m (within limit)

○ A to C = 50 m (within limit)

o A to D = 110 m (exceeds typical Ethernet limit of 100 m) → repeater needed

o A to E = 0 m (no distance, no repeater needed)

So, a repeater is required between A and D to ensure signal integrity.', NULL, NULL, 2025, '/local-papers/assets/computer-science-2025/q22-figure-1.jpg', 'Figure for question 22', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.064Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/computer-science-2025/q22-figure-1.jpg","alt":"Figure for question 22","kind":"figure"},{"url":"/local-papers/assets/computer-science-2025/q22-table-2.jpg","alt":"Table for question 22","kind":"table"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (295, 7, NULL, NULL, 'In MAC address, Organisational Unique Identifier (OUI) consist of _.', NULL, '[{"text":"32 bits","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"48 bits","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"24 bits","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"64 bits","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, ') 24 bits

A MAC (Media Access Control) address is a 48-bit unique identifier for a network device. It is divided into two parts: the OUI and the device-specific ID. The OUI identifies the manufacturer of the device and occupies the first 24 bits (or first 3 bytes) of the MAC address. The remaining 24 bits are used by the manufacturer to assign unique IDs to each device.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.065Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (296, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Given a list numList of n elements and key value K, arrange the following steps for finding the position of the key K in the numList using binary search algorithm i.e. BinarySearch(numList, key)

(A) Calculate mid = (first + last) / 2

(B) SET first = 0, last = n-1
(C) PRINT "Search unsuccessful"
IF numList[mid] = key DRIIT "Flempt found at position" mid+1
STOP
ELSE
IF numList[mid] > key, THEN last = mid-1
EL SE first = mid + 1', NULL, '[{"text":"(A),(B),(D),(C)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(D),(B),(A),(C)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B),(A),(D),(C)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(D),(A),(B),(C)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, ')(B),(A),(D),(C)

In binary search, you first initialize the pointers with first = 0 and last = n-1 (B). Then, calculate the middle index mid = (first + last) // 2 (A). Compare numList[mid] with the key: if equal, print the position; if numList[mid] > key, update last = mid-1; otherwise, update first = mid+1 (D). If the key is not found after the search, print "Search unsuccessful" (C).', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.065Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (297, 7, NULL, NULL, 'In binary search after every pass of the algorithm, the search area:', NULL, '[{"text":"gets doubled","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"gets reduced by half","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"remains same","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"gets reduced by one third","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, ') gets reduced by half

Binary search works by repeatedly dividing the search area in half. You compare the key with the middle element: if the key is smaller, you discard the right half; if larger, you discard the left half. This reduces the search area by half each time, making the search very efficient.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.066Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (298, 7, NULL, NULL, 'For binary search, the list is in ascending order and the key is present in the list. If the middle element is less than the key, it means:', NULL, '[{"text":"The key is in the first half.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The key is in the second half.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The key is not in the list.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The key is the middle element.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, ') The key is in the second half

In an ascending sorted list, all elements after the middle element are greater than the middle element. If the middle element is less than the key, the key cannot be in the first half (which contains smaller elements). Therefore, the key must be in the second half of the list.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.066Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (299, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Arrange the following in order related to bubble sort for a list of elements:

(A)

-94212630

(D)

-94122630', NULL, '[{"text":"(A), (B), (D), (C)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A),(C),(B),(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B),(A),(D),(C)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C),(B),(D),(A)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '. (A), (C), (D), (B)

Bubble sort works by repeatedly swapping adjacent elements if they are in the wrong order.

• After each pass, the largest element "bubbles up" to its correct position.

- Applying this to the given list gives the sequence:

1. (A) - during first pass (partial swaps start)

2. (C) - after first pass

3. (D) - after second pass

4. (B) - final sorted list', NULL, NULL, 2025, '/local-papers/assets/computer-science-2025/q27-table-1.jpg', 'Table for question 27', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.066Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/computer-science-2025/q27-table-1.jpg","alt":"Table for question 27","kind":"table"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (300, 7, NULL, NULL, 'The amount of time an algorithm takes to process a given data can be called its:', NULL, '[{"text":"Process time","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Time period","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Time complexity","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Time bound","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '. Time complexity

- Time Complexity is a theoretical measure that tells us how much time an algorithm takes to run depending on the size of the input data.

- It helps us compare different algorithms without actually running them - by analyzing how their runtime increases as input grows.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.067Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'banner', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (301, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Given data:

Weight of 20 student in kgs=[35, 35, 40, 40, 40, 50, 50, 50, 50, 50, 60, 65, 65, 70, 70, 72, 75, 75, 78, 78]

Find the mode.', NULL, '[{"text":"50","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"55","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"57.4","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"57","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '.50

The mode is the value that occurs most frequently in a given dataset.

The highest frequency = 5, which corresponds to 50 kg.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.067Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (302, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Match List-I with List-II', NULL, '[{"text":"\\left(\\mathrm{A}\\right)-\\left(1\\right),\\left(\\mathrm{B}\\right)-\\left(\\mathrm{II}\\right),\\left(\\mathrm{C}\\right)-\\left(\\mathrm{III}\\right),\\left(\\mathrm{D}\\right)-\\left(\\mathrm{IV}\\right)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A) - (III), (B) - (1), (C) - (II), (D) - (IV)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A) - (1), (B) - (II), (C) - (IV), (D) - (I1I)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A) - (III), (B) - (IV), (C) - (1), (D) - (11)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '. (A) $ \rightarrow $ (III), (B) $ \rightarrow $ (I), (C) $ \rightarrow $ (II), (D) $ \rightarrow $ (IV)', NULL, NULL, 2025, '/local-papers/assets/computer-science-2025/q32-table-1.jpg', 'Table for question 32', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.067Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/computer-science-2025/q32-table-1.jpg","alt":"Table for question 32","kind":"table"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (303, 7, NULL, NULL, 'In SQL table, the set of values which a column can take in each row is called _.', NULL, '[{"text":"Tuple","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Attribute","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Domain","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Relation","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '. Domain

In SQL, each column (attribute) in a table can take values from a specific set of permissible values, known as its domain.

For example, if a column "Age" can only have integer values between 0 and 120, then that range defines the domain of the column.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.068Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (304, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Which of the following is synonym for Meta - data?', NULL, '[{"text":"Data Dictionary","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Database Instance","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Database Schema","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Data Constraint","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '. Data Dictionary

Meta-data means "data about data" - it describes the structure, organization, and properties of data stored in a database.

A Data Dictionary stores this kind of information - such as table names, column names, data types, constraints, etc. Hence, it acts as a catalog of metadata.

Answer: 4. Scalar

Single row functions operate on one row at a time and return one result per row.

These are also called scalar functions, because they return a single (scalar) value for each row of input.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.068Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (305, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Ms Ritika wants to delete the table ''sports'' permanently. Help her in selecting the correct SQL command from the following.', NULL, '[{"text":"DELETE FROM SPORTS;","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"DROP SPORTS;","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"DROP TABLE SPORTS;","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"DELETE* FROM SPORTS;","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '. DROP TABLE SPORTS;

In SQL:

- The DELETE command removes rows (data) from a table but keeps the table structure.

- The DROP TABLE command removes the entire table permanently, including its structure and data.

Hence, to delete the table ''sports'' permanently, the correct command is: DROP TABLE SPORTS;', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.068Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (306, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Which of the following are text functions?

(A) MIDO

(B) INSTRO

(C) SUBSTRO

(D) LENGTH)', NULL, '[{"text":"(A), (B) and (D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A), (B) and (C) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A),(B),(C)and(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B), (C) and (D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '. (A), (B), (C) and (D)

In SQL, the following are text (string) functions:

• MIDO → Extracts a substring from text (sometimes written as SUBSTRING() or MID().

• INSTR0 → Returns the position of a substring within a string.

SUBSTR() → Extracts a part of a string (similar to MID).

• LENGTH() → Returns the number of characters in a string.

So, all four - MID, INSTR, SUBSTR, LENGTH - are text functions.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.069Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (307, 7, NULL, NULL, '_ is a language used to design web pages.', NULL, '[{"text":"Web browser","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"HTTP","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"HTML","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"WWW","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '. HTML

HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard language used to create and design web pages.

It defines the structure of a webpage using elements like headings, paragraphs, links, images, and tables.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.069Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (308, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Match List-I with List-II', NULL, '[{"text":"(A) - (I), (B) - (II), (C) - (III), (D) - (IV)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(I),(B)-(I),(C)-(IV),(D)-(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A) - (I), (B) - (II), (C) - (IV), (D) - (III)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A) - (II), (B) - (IV), (C) - (I), (D) - (III)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '. (A) $ \rightarrow $ (II), (B) $ \rightarrow $ (I), (C) $ \rightarrow $ (IV), (D) $ \rightarrow $ (III)', NULL, NULL, 2025, '/local-papers/assets/computer-science-2025/q40-table-1.jpg', 'Table for question 40', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.069Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/computer-science-2025/q40-table-1.jpg","alt":"Table for question 40","kind":"table"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (309, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Bandwidth of a channel is:', NULL, '[{"text":"The range of frequencies available for transmission of data through that channel.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The path of message travels between source and destination.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The set of rules on the Internet.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"I he data or inormation that heeds to be exchanged.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '. The range of frequencies available for transmission of data through that channel.

Bandwidth refers to the range of frequencies that a communication channel can use for data transmission.

A higher bandwidth means more data can be transmitted per second - just like a wider road allows more vehicles to pass.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.069Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (310, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Data Transfer Rate 1 Gbps is equal to:

(A) 1024 Mbps

(B) 1024 Kbps

(C) $ 2^{30} $ bps

(D) 22 bps', NULL, '[{"text":"(A) and (D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A) and (C) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B) and (D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B) and (C) only","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '. (A) and (C) only (because 1 Gbps = 1024 Mbps).1 Gbps stands for

1 Gbps (Gigabit per second) means 1 billion bits per second.

In networking terms:

1 Gbps=1024 Mbps1 \text{Gbps}=1024 \text{Mbps}1 Gbps=1024 Mbps

Other options like Kbps, bps (230 bps or 22 bps) are much smaller and

incorrect.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.070Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (311, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the wired transmission media for the following:

"They are less expensive and commonly used in telephone lines and LANs. These cables are of two types: Unshielded and shielded."', NULL, '[{"text":"Optical Fibre","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Coaxial Cable","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"TWisted pair cabie","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Microwaves","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '. Twisted Pair Cable

The description - "less expensive, commonly used in telephone lines and LANs, available in two types: Unshielded and Shielded"- clearly refers to Twisted Pair Cable.

• Unshielded Twisted Pair (UTP): Used in telephone lines and LAN connections.

• Shielded Twisted Pair (STP): Used where extra protection from interference is needed.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.070Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (312, 7, NULL, NULL, 'The term Cookie is defined as:', NULL, '[{"text":"A computer cookie is a small file or data packet which is stored by a website on the server''s computer.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A cookie is edited only by the website that created it, the client''s computer acts as a host to store the cookie.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Cookies are used by the user to store data on the computer.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A cookie is a security system to protect your data.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '. A cookie is edited only by the website that created it, the client''s computer acts as a host to store the cookie.

A cookie is a small file or data packet that a website stores on the user''s (client''s) computer, not on the server.

It helps websites remember user information such as login details, preferences, or items in a shopping cart.

Only the website that created the cookie can read or modify it.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.070Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (313, 7, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the concept behind the below-given scenario:

If an attacker limits or stops an authorized user to access a service, device or any such resource by overloading that resource with illegitimate requests.', NULL, '[{"text":"Snooping","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Eavesdropping","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Deniar or service","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Plagiarism","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '. Denial of Service

The scenario describes a situation where an attacker overloads a server or network with fake or illegitimate requests, making it unavailable to legitimate users.

This is known as a Denial of Service (DoS) attack.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.071Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (314, 7, NULL, NULL, 'The HTTPS based websites require:', NULL, '[{"text":"Search Engine Optimization","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Digital authenticity","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"WWW Certificate","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"SSL Digital Certificate","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '. SSL Digital Certificate

Websites that use HTTPS (HyperText Transfer Protocol Secure) ensure secure data transfer between the browser and the server.

To establish this secure connection, they require an SSL (Secure Sockets Layer) Digital Certificate, which encrypts the data and verifies the website''s authenticity.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.071Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (315, 7, NULL, NULL, 'State the output of the following query.
SELECT ENGTH(MID(''INFORMATICS PRACTICES'',5,-5));', NULL, '[{"text":"NO OUTPUT","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"5","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"0","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"ERROR","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '. ERROR

Let''s analyze the query:

SELECT LENGTH(MID(''INFORMATICS PRACTICES'',5,-5));

- The function MID(''INFORMATICS PRACTICES'', 5, -5) tries to extract a substring starting from position 5 with a negative length (-5).

- In SQL, the MID() (or SUBSTRING()) function does not accept negative length values.

- Using a negative number for length produces an invalid argument error.

Hence, the query will not execute successfully.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.071Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
