SELECT ROUND(9873.567,-2);', NULL, '[{"text":"9900","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"9873","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"9800","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"9873.5","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '.9900

The SQL function ROUND(number, n) rounds a number to n digits.

- If n is positive, it rounds to that many decimal places.

- If n is negative, it rounds to the nearest 10, 100, 1000, etc.

Here:

Here:
ROUND(9873.567, -2)

- The -2 means round to the nearest hundred.

• 9873.567 → the hundreds digit is 8 and the tens digit is 7 ( $ \geq $ 5), so it rounds up to 9900.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.072Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
