is equal to', NULL, '[{"text":"$$ \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{3} $$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$$ \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3} $$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '$$ Evaluation~of~\int_{0}^{1}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{1+x}-\sqrt{x}} $$
1. Rationalize the Denominator

Multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, $ \sqrt{1 + x} + \sqrt{x} $:

$$ \begin{aligned}\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+x}-\sqrt{x}}&=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+x}-\sqrt{x}}\cdot\frac{\sqrt{1+x}+\sqrt{x}}{\sqrt{1+x}+\sqrt{x}}\\&=\frac{\sqrt{1+x}+\sqrt{x}}{(\sqrt{1+x})^{2}-(\sqrt{x})^{2}}\\&=\frac{\sqrt{1+x}+\sqrt{x}}{(1+x)-x}\\&=\frac{\sqrt{1+x}+\sqrt{x}}{1}\end{aligned} $$

2. Evaluate the Integral

The integral becomes:

$$ \begin{aligned}I&=\int_{0}^{1}\Big((1+x)^{1/2}+x^{1/2}\Big)dx\\&\quad Integrate term by term:\\I&=\left[\frac{(1+x)^{3/2}}{3/2}+\frac{x^{3/2}}{3/2}\right]_{0}^{1}\\&=\frac{2}{3}\Big[(1+x)^{3/2}+x^{3/2}\Big]_{0}^{1}\\&\quad\text{Apply the limits:}\end{aligned} $$

$$ \begin{align*}I=\frac{2}{3}\Big[\Big((1+1)^{3/2}+1^{3/2}\Big)-\Big((1+0)^{3/2}+0^{3/2}\Big)\Big]\\=\frac{2}{3}\Big[\Big(2^{3/2}+1\Big)-(1+0)\Big]\\=\frac{2}{3}\Big[2\sqrt{2}\Big]\\I=\frac{4\sqrt{2}}{3}\end{align*} $$', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.416Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (854, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The area (in sq. units) of a triangle formed by vertices O, A, and B where $ \overrightarrow{OA} = \hat{i} + 2\hat{j} + 3\hat{k} $ and $ \overrightarrow{OB} = $', NULL, '[{"text":"$$ 3\\sqrt{5} $$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$$ 4\\sqrt{5} $$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"6\\sqrt{5}","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2\\sqrt{5}","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'The area of a triangle formed by vectors $ \overrightarrow{a} $ and $ \overrightarrow{b} $ originating from the same vertex is given by:

$$ Area=\frac{1}{2}|\vec{a}\times\vec{b}| $$

Given $ \vec{a} = \vec{O}A = \hat{i} + 2\hat{j} + 3\hat{k} $ and $ \vec{b} = \vec{O}B = -3\hat{i} - 2\hat{j} + \hat{k} $.

1. Calculate the Cross Product $ \overrightarrow{OA} \times \overrightarrow{OB} $

$$ \vec{O}\vec{A}\times\vec{O}\vec{B}=\left[\begin{matrix}{\hat{i}}&{\hat{j}}&{\hat{k}}\\ {1}&{2}&{3}\\ {-3}&{-2}&{1}\\ \end{matrix}\right] $$

$$ \begin{aligned}=\hat{i}((2)(1)-(3)(-2))-\hat{j}((1)(1)-(3)(-3))+\hat{k}((1)(-2)-(2)(-3))\\=\hat{i}(2+6)-\hat{j}(1+9)+\hat{k}(-2+6)\\=8\hat{i}-10\hat{j}+4\hat{k}\end{aligned} $$

2. Calculate the Magnitude $ \left|\overrightarrow{OA} \times \overrightarrow{OB}\right| $

$$ \begin{aligned}\left|\overrightarrow{OA}\times\overrightarrow{OB}\right|&=\sqrt{8^{2}+(-10)^{2}+4^{2}}\\&=\sqrt{64+100+16}\\&=\sqrt{180}\end{aligned} $$

Simplify the square root: $ \sqrt{180} = \sqrt{36 \times 5} = 6\sqrt{5} $.

3. Calculate the Area

$$ \mathrm{Area}=\frac{1}{2}|\overrightarrow{OA}\times\overrightarrow{OB}|=\frac{1}{2}(6\sqrt{5})=\mathbf{3}\sqrt{5}\mathrm{~sq.~units} $$

[The correct option is 1.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.417Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (855, 3, NULL, NULL, '$$ \int_{-1}^{1}\frac{x^{3}+|x|+1}{x^{2}+2|x|+1}dx is equal to $$

$$ (1)\frac{1}{2}\log_{e}2-1 $$

(2) $ 2\log_{e}2 $

(3) $ 2 \log_{e} 2 - 2 $', NULL, '[{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"3","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"4","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '$$ Evaluation of\int_{-1}^{1}\frac{x^{3}+|x|+1}{x^{2}+2|x|+1}dx $$

The integral is over a symmetric interval $ [-1,1] $. We split the integrand $ f(x) $ into odd and even functions.

$$ f(x)=\frac{x^{3}+|x|+1}{x^{2}+2|x|+1}=\frac{x^{3}}{x^{2}+2|x|+1}+\frac{|x|+1}{x^{2}+2|x|+1} $$

$$ \operatorname{Let}f_{1}(x)=\frac{x^{3}}{x^{2}+2|x|+1}\text{and}f_{2}(x)=\frac{|x|+1}{x^{2}+2|x|+1}. $$

$$ f_{1}(-x)=\frac{(-x)^{3}}{(-x)^{2}+2|-x|+1}=\frac{-x^{3}}{x^{2}+2|x|+1}=-f_{1}(x) $$

Since $ f_{1}(x) $ is an odd function, its integral over a symmetric interval is zero:

$$ \int_{-1}^{1}f_{1}(x)dx=\int_{-1}^{1}\frac{x^{3}}{x^{2}+2|x|+1}dx=0 $$

$$ f_{2}(-x)=\frac{\left|-x\right|+1}{(-x)^{2}+2|-x|+1}=\frac{|x|+1}{x^{2}+2|x|+1}=f_{2}(x) $$

Since $ f_{2}(x) $ is an even function, the integral simplifies:

$$ \int_{-1}^{1}f_{2}(x)dx=2\int_{0}^{1}f_{2}(x)dx $$

$$ For x\in[0,1],|x|=x. $$

$$ f_{2}(x)=\frac{x+1}{x^{2}+2x+1}=\frac{x+1}{(x+1)^{2}}=\frac{1}{x+1} $$

$$ \begin{array}{c}The~total~integral~I~is~0+2\int_{0}^{1}\frac{1}{x+1}dx:\\I=2\int_{0}^{1}\frac{1}{x+1}dx\\=2\left[\log_{e}\left|x+1\right|\right]_{0}^{1}\\=2\left(\log_{e}(1+1)-\log_{e}(0+1)\right)\\=2\left(\log_{e}2-\log_{e}1\right)\\=2\left(\log_{e}2-0\right)\\=\mathbf{2}\log_{e}\mathbf{2}\end{array} $$

[The correct option is 2.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.417Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (856, 3, NULL, NULL, '$$ \mathrm{If}\;x=a(\cos t+\log\tan\frac{t}{2}),\;y=a\sin t,\;\mathrm{then}\;\mathrm{value}\;\mathrm{of}\;\frac{dy}{dx}\;\mathrm{at}\;t=\pi/4\mathrm{is} $$

(2) 1

(3)-1

(4)2', NULL, '[{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"3","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"4","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Derivative of Parametric Equations at $ t = \frac{\pi}{4} $

\[\begin{array}{c} \displaystyle\text{Given the parametric equations:}\\ \quad x=a\left(\cos t+\log\tan\frac{t}{2}\right),\quad y=a\sin t\\ \displaystyle\quad

Using the double angle identity $ \sin t = 2\sin(t/2)\cos(t/2) $:

$$ \frac{dx}{dt}=a\left(-\sin t+\frac{1}{\sin t}\right) $$

Combine the terms:

$$ \begin{array}{l}\frac{dx}{dt}=a\left(\frac{1-\sin^{2}t}{\sin t}\right)\\=a\left(\frac{\cos^{2}t}{\sin t}\right)\end{array} $$

$$ Calculate\frac{dy}{dx} $$

$$ \begin{aligned}\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt}=\frac{a\cos t}{a\left(\frac{\cos^{2}t}{\sin t}\right)}=\frac{\cos t\sin t}{\cos^{2}t}=\frac{\sin t}{\cos t}=\tan t\\ \text{EVALUATE at}t=\frac{\pi}{4}\\ \left.\frac{dy}{dx}\right|_{t=\pi/4}=\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)=\mathbf{1}\end{aligned} $$

[The correct option is 2.]

The area (in sq. units) of the region bounded by the curve $y=\cos x$ between $x=-\frac{\pi}{2}$, $x=\frac{\pi}{2}$ and the x-axis is (1)0

(2)3

(3)2

(4)1

Options:

A. 1

B. 2

C. 3

D. 4

Answer: C
Solution:
Area Bounded by $ y = \cos x $

The area $A$ is bounded by the curve $y=\cos x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-\frac{\pi}{2}$ to $x=\frac{\pi}{2}$.

Set up the Integral

The curve $ y = \cos x $ is non-negative (above the x-axis) in the interval $ \left[-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}\right] $.

The area is given by the definite integral:

$$ A=\int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\cos x dx $$

Use Symmetry

Since $ f(x) = \cos x $ is an even function $ (\cos(-x) = \cos x) $, we can simplify the integral:

$$A = 2 \int_{0}^{\pi/2} \cos x \, dx$$

Evaluate the Integral

$$A = 2 [\sin x]_{0}^{\pi/2}$$

$$= 2 \left( \sin \left( \frac{\pi}{2} \right) - \sin(0) \right)$$

$$= 2(1 - 0)$$

$$= 2 \, \text{sq.} \, \text{units}$$

The correct option is 3.', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.418Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (857, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If the direction ratios of two lines are $a, b, c$ and $(b-c), (c-a), (a-b)$ respectively, then the angle between', NULL, '[{"text":"$$ \\frac{\\pi}{4} $$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$$ \\frac{\\pi}{3} $$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"\\frac{\\pi}{2}","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"\\frac{2\\pi}{3}","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Angle Between Lines from Direction Ratios

Let the direction ratios of the two lines be $ L_{1} $ and $ L_{2} $.

$ L_{1} $ direction ratios: $ a_{1}=a $, $ b_{1}=b $, $ c_{1}=c $.

$ L_{2} $ direction ratios: $ a_{2}=b-c $, $ b_{2}=c-a $, $ c_{2}=a-b $.

The angle $ \theta $ between the lines is given by:

$$ \cos\theta=\frac{a_{1}a_{2}+b_{1}b_{2}+c_{1}c_{2}}{\sqrt{a_{1}^{2}+b_{1}^{2}+c_{1}^{2}}\sqrt{a_{2}^{2}+b_{2}^{2}+c_{2}^{2}}} $$

If $ a_{1}a_{2} + b_{1}b_{2} + c_{1}c_{2} = 0 $, the lines are perpendicular, and $ \theta = \pi/2 $.

$$ \begin{aligned}&Calculate~the~Numerator\\&N=a_{1}a_{2}+b_{1}b_{2}+c_{1}c_{2}\\&=a(b-c)+b(c-a)+c(a-b)\\&=ab-ac+bc-ba+ca-cb\\&=(ab-ba)+(-ac+ca)+(bc-cb)\\&\quad=0+0+0\\&\quad-\mathbf{0}\end{aligned} $$

Since $ a_{1}a_{2} + b_{1}b_{2} + c_{1}c_{2} = 0 $, the angle between the lines is $ \theta = \arccos(0) $.

$$ \theta=\frac{\pi}{2} $$

[The correct option is 3.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.419Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (858, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If $y=\sin^{-1}x$, then $(1-x^{2})\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}$ is equal to', NULL, '[{"text":"$$ -x\\frac{dy}{dx} $$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$$ x\\frac{dy}{dx} $$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{y}{x}\\frac{dy}{dx} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$$ \\frac{-y}{x}\\frac{dy}{dx} $$","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '1. First Derivative $ \frac{dy}{dx} $

$$ \frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^{2}}} $$

Square both sides:

$$ \left(\frac{dy}{dx}\right)^{2}=\frac{1}{1-x^{2}} $$

Rearrange to clear the fraction:

$$ (1-x^{2})\left(\frac{dy}{dx}\right)^{2}=1\quad(*) $$

2. Second Derivative $ \frac{d^2y}{dx^2} $

Differentiate equation (*) with respect to x using the product rule and chain rule:

$$ \frac{d}{dx}\left[(1-x^{2})\left(\frac{dy}{dx}\right)^{2}\right]=\frac{d}{dx}(1) $$

$$ \left[\frac{d}{d x}(1-x^{2})\right]\left(\frac{d y}{d x}\right)^{2}+(1-x^{2})\left[\frac{d}{d x}\left(\frac{d y}{d x}\right)^{2}\right]=0 $$

$$ (-2x)\biggl(\frac{dy}{dx}\biggr)^{2}+(1-x^{2})\biggl(2\frac{dy}{dx}\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}\biggr)=0 $$

3. Simplify and Isolate

Divide the entire equation by $ 2\frac{dy}{dx} $ (since $ \frac{dy}{dx} \neq 0 $):

$$ -x\left(\frac{dy}{dx}\right)+(1-x^{2})\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}=0 $$

Rearrange to find the required expression:

$$ (1-x^{2})\frac{d^{2}y}{d x^{2}}=\mathbf{x}\frac{\mathbf{d}\mathbf{y}}{\mathbf{d}\mathbf{x}} $$

[The correct option is 2.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.419Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (859, 3, NULL, NULL, '(A) $ P(S|B)=1 $

(B) $ P(A \cap B) = P(A) + P(B) + P(A \cup B) $

(C) $ P(\overline{A}|B) = 1 - P(A|B) $

(D) $ P(A|B) = \frac{P(A \cap B)}{P(B)} $, $ P(B) \neq 0 $', NULL, '[{"text":"(A) and (B) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A), (C) and (D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B) and (C) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C) and (D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Analysis of Probability Statements

$$ (A)\;P(S|B)=1 $$

This is TRUE. Since $B$ is always a subset of the sample space $S$ ($B \subseteq S$), the event $S$ is guaranteed to occur when $B$ occurs.

$$ P(S|B)=\frac{P(S\cap B)}{P(B)}=\frac{P(B)}{P(B)}=1 $$

$$ (B)\;P(A\cap B)=P(A)+P(B)+P(A\cup B) $$

This is FALSE. The correct formula is $ P(A \cap B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \cup B) $.

$$ (C)\;P(\bar{A}|B)=1-P(A|B) $$

This is TRUE. This is the complementary property for conditional probability.

$$ P(\bar{A}|B)=\frac{P(\bar{A}\cap B)}{P(B)}=\frac{P(B)-P(A\cap B)}{P(B)}=1-\frac{P(A\cap B)}{P(B)}=1-P(A|B) $$

$$ \left(\mathrm{D}\right)P(A|B)=\frac{P(A\cap B)}{P(B)},\quad P(B)\neq0 $$

This is TRUE. This is the definition of conditional probability.

Hence, the correct statements are (A), (C), and (D).

[The correct option is 2.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.420Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (860, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The foot of the perpendicular drawn from the point (1,6,3) to the line $ \frac{x}{1}=\frac{y-1}{2}=\frac{z-2}{3} $ is:', NULL, '[{"text":"$ (-1,-1,1) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ (1,2,3) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ (1,3,5) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ (1,-3,5) $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Foot of the Perpendicular

The given line $L$ is $\frac{x}{1} = \frac{y-1}{2} = \frac{z-2}{3}$.

Let the general point on the line be M and set the ratio equal to $ \lambda $:

$$ \frac{x}{1}=\frac{y-1}{2}=\frac{z-2}{3}=\lambda $$

The coordinates of M are: $ M = (\lambda, 2\lambda + 1, 3\lambda + 2) $

The given point is $ P = (1, 6, 3) $. M is the foot of the perpendicular from P.

Direction Ratios:

The direction ratios of the line L (denominator values) are 1, 2, 3.

The direction ratios of the segment PM are:

$$ PM=(\lambda-1),(2\lambda+1-6),(3\lambda+2-3) $$

$$ PM=(\lambda-1),(2\lambda-5),(3\lambda-1) $$

Condition for Perpendicularity:

Since PM is perpendicular to L, the dot product of their direction ratios must be zero:

$$ 1(\lambda-1)+2(2\lambda-5)+3(3\lambda-1)=0 $$

$$ \lambda-1+4\lambda-10+9\lambda-3=0 $$

$$ 14\lambda-14=0\quad\Rightarrow\quad\lambda=1 $$

Coordinates of M :

$$ x=1,\quad y=2(1)+1=3,\quad z=3(1)+2=5 $$

Hence, the foot of the perpendicular is $ M = (1, 3, 5) $.

[The correct option is 3.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.420Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (861, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If the function f(x)=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}\frac{\sin3x}{x},&\text{if}x\neq0\\ \frac{3k}{2},&\text{if}x=0\end{array}\right. is continuous at x=0, then the value of k is:', NULL, '[{"text":"$ \\frac{2}{3} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"4","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"9","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Continuity of $ f(x) $ at x = 0

For a function $ f(x) $ to be continuous at x = 0, the following condition must hold:

$$ \lim_{x\to0}f(x)=f(0) $$

1. Determine $ f(0) $

From the definition of the function: $ f(0) = \frac{3k}{2} $

2. Calculate $ \lim_{x \to 0} f(x) $

$$ For x\neq0,f(x)=\frac{\sin3x}{x}. $$

We use the standard limit $ \lim_{\theta\to0}\frac{\sin\theta}{\theta}=1 $:

$$ \begin{aligned}&\lim_{x\to0}f(x)=\lim_{x\to0}\frac{\sin3x}{x}\\ &=\lim_{x\to0}\left(\frac{\sin3x}{3x}\cdot3\right)\\ &=1\cdot3=3\\ \end{aligned} $$

3. Equate the Limit and Function Value

For continuity, the limit must equal $ f(0) $:

$$

\lim_{x \to 0} f(x) = f(0)

$$

$$

3 = \frac{3k}{2}

$$

$$

3k = 6 \Rightarrow k = 2

$$

[The correct option is 3.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.421Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (862, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The value of $ \int_{\frac{-\pi}{2}}^{\pi/2}(x^{5}+x^{3}\cos x)dx $ $ \sin x $.', NULL, '[{"text":"0","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ -1 $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\pi $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'The integral is over a symmetric interval $ [-\pi/2,\pi/2] $. We evaluate the integral by analyzing the parity of the integrand $ f(x)=x^{5}+x^{3}\cos x $.

1. Check the Parity of $ f(x) $

A function $f(x)$ is odd if $f(-x) = -f(x)$.

$$ f(-x)=(-x)^{5}+(-x)^{3}\cos(-x) $$

Since $ (-x)^{n} = -x^{n} $ for odd n, and $ \cos(-x) = \cos x $:

$$ f(-x)=-x^{5}+(-x^{3})\cos x $$

$$ f(-x)=-(x^{5}+x^{3}\cos x)=-f(x) $$

$ e \, f(-x) = -f(x) $, the integrand $ f(x) $ is an $ \text{textit}\{\text{odd function}\} $.

2. Apply the Property of Odd Functions

For any odd function $ f(x) $, its definite integral over a symmetric interval $ [-a, a] $ is zero:

$$ \int_{-a}^{a}f(x)dx=0 $$

Here, $ a = \pi/2 $. Therefore:

$$ \int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}(x^{5}+x^{3}\cos x)\,dx=\mathbf{0} $$

[The correct option is 1.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.421Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (863, 3, NULL, NULL, 'A function $f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ defined by $f(x) = \frac{x}{x^2 + 1}$

is (where $ \mathbb{R} $ is the set of real numbers):', NULL, '[{"text":"one-one but not onto","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"onto but not one-one","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"neither one-one nor onto","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"both one-one and onto","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Function Classification: $ f(x)=\frac{x}{x^{2}+1} $

The function is $ f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} $ defined by $ f(x) = \frac{x}{x^2 + 1} $.

1. Check for One-One (Injective)

A function is one-one if $ f(x_1) = f(x_2) $ implies $ x_1 = x_2 $.

Counterexample for One-One:

$$ f(2)=\frac{2}{2^{2}+1}=\frac{2}{5} $$

$$ f\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)=\frac{\frac{1}{2}}{\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{2}+1}=\frac{\frac{1}{2}}{\frac{1}{4}+1}=\frac{\frac{1}{2}}{\frac{5}{4}}=\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{4}{5}=\frac{2}{5} $$

Since $ f(2) = f\left(\frac{1}{2}\right) = \frac{2}{5} $ but $ 2 \neq \frac{1}{2} $, the function is $ \{\text{not one-one}\} $.
2. Check for Onto (Surjective)

A function is onto if the range equals the codomain $ \mathbb{R} $.

Set $y = f(x)$ and solve for $x$:

$$ y=\frac{x}{x^{2}+1} $$

$$ y(x^{2}+1)=x\implies y x^{2}-x+y=0 $$

For $x$ to be real (domain $\mathbb{R}$), the discriminant $D$ must satisfy $D \geq 0$.

$$ D=\left(-1\right)^{2}-4(y)(y)=1-4y^{2} $$

$$ 1-4y^{2}\geq0\implies4y^{2}\leq1\implies y^{2}\leq\frac{1}{4} $$

$$ |y|\leq\frac{1}{2}\implies-\frac{1}{2}\leq y\leq\frac{1}{2} $$

$$ Thus,the range of f(x)is\left[-\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2}\right] $$

Since the range $ \left[-\frac{1}{2}, \frac{1}{2}\right] $ is not equal to $ \mathbb{R} $, the function is $ \backslash \text{textit}\{\text{not onto}\} $.

3. Conclusion

The function is \textit{neither one-one nor onto}.

[The correct option is 3.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.421Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (864, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The matrix $ A = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 5 \\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\ 5 & 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix} $ is a:

(A) Diagonal matrix

(B) Scalar matrix

(C) Square matrix

D) Symmetric matrix', NULL, '[{"text":"(A), (B) and (C) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B) and (C) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C) and (D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A) and (D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '$$ \boldsymbol{A}=\begin{pmatrix}{{{0}}}&{{{0}}}&{{{5}}} \\{{{0}}}&{{{5}}}&{{{0}}} \\{{{5}}}&{{{0}}}&{{{0}}}\end{pmatrix} $$

(A) Diagonal matrix: This is FALSE. A diagonal matrix requires all non-diagonal elements to be zero. Here, $ a_{13} = 5 $ and $ a_{31} = 5 $ are non-zero.

(B) Scalar matrix: This is FALSE. A scalar matrix is a diagonal matrix with equal diagonal elements. A is not diagonal and its diagonal elements (0, 5, 0) a

(C) Square matrix: This is TRUE. A has order $ 3 \times 3 $.

(D) Symmetric matrix: This is TRUE. A matrix A is symmetric if $ A^{T} = A $.

$$ \boldsymbol{A}^{T}=\begin{pmatrix}{{{0}}}&{{{0}}}&{{{5}}} \\{{{0}}}&{{{5}}}&{{{0}}} \\{{{5}}}&{{{0}}}&{{{0}}}\end{pmatrix}=\boldsymbol{A} $$

Since $ A = A^{T} $, the matrix is symmetric.

The only correct statements are (C) and (D).

[The correct option is 3.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.422Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (865, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If A is an invertible matrix of order 3, then

Match List-I with List-II', NULL, '[{"text":"\\ (\\mathrm{A})-(\\mathrm{II}),(\\mathrm{B})-(\\mathrm{IV}),(\\mathrm{C})-(\\mathrm{I}),(\\mathrm{D})-(\\mathrm{III})","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"\\ (\\mathbf{A})-(\\mathbf{I}),(\\mathbf{B})-(\\mathbf{II}),(\\mathbf{C})-(\\mathbf{III}),(\\mathbf{D})-(\\mathbf{IV})","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"\\;(\\mathbf{A})-(\\mathbf{I I I}),\\;(\\mathbf{B})-(\\mathbf{I V}),\\;(\\mathbf{C})-(\\mathbf{I}),\\;(\\mathbf{D})-(\\mathbf{I I})","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"\\ (\\mathrm{A})-(\\mathrm{IV}),(\\mathrm{B})-(\\mathrm{III}),(\\mathrm{C})-(\\mathrm{II}),(\\mathrm{D})-(\\mathrm{I})","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'We use the following general properties for an $ n \times n $ matrix A:

$$ \left|adj A\right|=\left|A\right|^{n-1} $$

$$ \begin{aligned}|A(\mathrm{adj}A)|&=|A||\mathrm{adj}A|=|A|\cdot|A|^{n-1}=|A|^{n}\\&\quad|kA|=k^{n}|A|\end{aligned} $$

$$ \left|\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}\right|=\frac{1}{\left|\boldsymbol{A}\right|} $$

$$ \mathrm{For}\;n=3: $$

(A) |adjA|: Substitute n = 3 into the first property:

$$ \left|adj A\right|=\left|A\right|^{3-1}=\left|A\right|^{2} $$

$$ (A)\mathrm{m a t c h e s}\left(\Pi\right)|A|^{2} $$

(B) $ |A(\text{adj}A)| $: Substitute n = 3 into the second property:

$$ \left|A(\mathrm{adj}A)\right|=\left|A\right|^{3} $$

$$ (B)\mathrm{m a t c h e s}(IV)\left|A\right|^{3} $$

(C) $ |2A| $: Substitute n = 3 and k = 2 into the third property:

$$ |2\boldsymbol{A}|=2^{3}|\boldsymbol{A}|=8|\boldsymbol{A}| $$

$$ (C)\operatorname{m a t c h e s}\operatorname{(I)}8|A| $$

(D) $ |A^{-1}| $: Apply the fourth property:

$$ \left|\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}\right|=\frac{1}{\left|\boldsymbol{A}\right|} $$

$$ (D)\mathrm{m a t c h e s}\left(\mathrm{I I I}\right)\frac{1}{\left|A\right|} $$

Conclusion: The correct match is (A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III).

[The correct option is 1.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, '/local-papers/assets/mathematics-2025-06-02-shift-1/q42-table-1.jpg', 'Table for question 42', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.422Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/mathematics-2025-06-02-shift-1/q42-table-1.jpg","alt":"Table for question 42","kind":"table"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (866, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Consider a closed cylinder of radius r with a fixed surface area.

The volume of the cylinder is maximum when its height is:

$$ \begin{aligned}&1.3r\\ &2.\frac{1}{3}r\\ &3.\frac{r}{2}\\ &4.2r\\ \end{aligned} $$', NULL, '[{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"3","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"4","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '$$ S=2\pi r^{2}+2\pi r h\quad\implies\quad h=\frac{S}{2\pi r}-r $$

Substitute h into the volume formula $ V = \pi r^{2}h $:

$$ V=\pi r^{2}\left(\frac{S}{2\pi r}-r\right)=\frac{Sr}{2}-\pi r^{3} $$

For maximum volume, set $ \frac{dV}{dr}=0 $:

$$ \frac{dV}{dr}=\frac{S}{2}-3\pi r^{2}=0 $$

$$ \Longrightarrow S=6\pi r^{2} $$

Substitute this expression for $S$ back into the surface area equation $S = 2\pi r^{2} + 2\pi rh$:

$$ 6\pi r^{2}=2\pi r^{2}+2\pi r h $$

$$ 4\pi r^{2}=2\pi r h $$

Divide by $ 2\pi r $ to solve for h:

$$ h=\frac{4\pi r^{2}}{2\pi r}=\mathbf{2r} $$

[The correct option is 4.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.423Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (867, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The function $ f(x) = 2\log_{e}(x - 2) - x^{2} + 4x + 1 $, $ (x > 2) $ is increasing on the interval:', NULL, '[{"text":"(1,2)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(2,6)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(2,3)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(3,\\infty)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Interval where $ f(x) $ is Increasing

Given $ f(x) = 2 \log_e(x - 2) - x^2 + 4x + 1 $, with domain $ x > 2 $.

The function is increasing where the first derivative $ f''(x) > 0 $.

1. Calculate the first derivative $ f''(x) $

$$ f^{\prime}(x)=\frac{d}{dx}\big(2\log_{e}(x-2)-x^{2}+4x+1\big) $$

$$ f^{\prime}(x)=2\cdot\frac{1}{x-2}-2x+4 $$

Factor the polynomial terms:

$$ f^{\prime}(x)=\frac{2}{x-2}-2(x-2) $$

2. Set $ f''(x) > 0 $ for Increasing Interval

$$ \frac{2}{x-2}-2(x-2)>0 $$

Since x > 2, x - 2 is positive. Multiply by $ (x - 2) $:

$$ 2-2(x-2)^{2}>0 $$

$$ {\mathrm{D i v i d e~b y~2}}: $$

$$ 1-(x-2)^{2}>0 $$

Rearrange and solve:

$$ (x-2)^{2} 2:

$$ Increasing~Interval=(1,3)\cap(2,\infty)=(\mathbf{2},\mathbf{3}) $$

[The correct option is 3.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.423Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (868, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The value of $ \lambda $ so that the lines $ \frac{1-x}{3}=\frac{7y-14}{2\lambda}=\frac{z-3}{2} $ and $ \frac{7-7x}{3\lambda}=\frac{y-5}{1}=\frac{6-z}{5} $ are at right angle, is:

1. $ \frac{70}{11} $', NULL, '[{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"3","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"4","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Perpendicular Lines: Value of $ \lambda $

Two lines are perpendicular if the dot product of their direction ratios $ \vec{b_1} \cdot \vec{b_2} = 0 $.

1. Extract Direction Ratios

The lines must be in standard form $ \frac{x-x_{0}}{a}=\frac{y-y_{0}}{b}=\frac{z-z_{0}}{c} $, where a,b,c are the direction ratios.

Line 1

$$ L_{1}:\quad\frac{1-x}{3}=\frac{x-1}{-3}\Longrightarrow a_{1}=-3 $$

$$ \frac{7y-14}{2\lambda}=\frac{7(y-2)}{2\lambda}=\frac{y-2}{2\lambda/7}\Longrightarrow b_{1}=\frac{2\lambda}{7} $$

$$ \frac{z-3}{2}\Longrightarrow c_{1}=2 $$

$$ \vec{b_{1}}=\left(-3,\frac{2\lambda}{7},2\right) $$

Line 2

$$ L_{2}:\quad\frac{7-7x}{3\lambda}=\frac{7(1-x)}{3\lambda}=\frac{7(x-1)}{-3\lambda}=\frac{x-1}{-3\lambda/7}\Longrightarrow a_{2}=-\frac{3\lambda}{7} $$

$$ \frac{y-5}{1}\Longrightarrow b_{2}=1 $$

$$ \frac{6-z}{5}=\frac{z-6}{-5}\Longrightarrow c_{2}=-5 $$

$$ \vec{b_{2}}=\left(-\frac{3\lambda}{7},1,-5\right) $$

2. Apply Perpendicularity Condition $ a_{1}a_{2} + b_{1}b_{2} + c_{1}c_{2} = 0 $

$$ (-3)\left(-\frac{3\lambda}{7}\right)+\left(\frac{2\lambda}{7}\right)(1)+(2)(-5)=0 $$

$$ \frac{9\lambda}{7}+\frac{2\lambda}{7}-10=0 $$

$$ \frac{11\lambda}{7}=10 $$

$$ \lambda=\frac{70}{11} $$

[The correct option is 1.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.423Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (869, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The solution of the differential equation $ \frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{x + y}{x - y} $ is', NULL, '[{"text":"$ y = \\frac{x}{2} \\log_{e}(x^{2} + y^{2}) + C $: C is an arbitrary constant","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ y = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} + C $: C is an arbitrary constant","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$y = \\frac{1}{2} \\log_{e}(x^{2} + y^{2}) + C$ : $C$ is an arbitrary constant","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\tan^{-1}\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{e}(x^{2}+y^{2})+C:C $ is an arbitrary constant","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Solution of $ \frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{x+y}{x-y} $

Given the homogeneous equation:

$$ \frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{1+y/x}{1-y/x} $$

Substitute $y = vx \text{ so } \frac{dy}{dx} = v + x \frac{dv}{dx}$:

$$ v+x\frac{dv}{dx}=\frac{1+v}{1-v} $$

Separate variables:

$$ x\frac{dv}{dx}=\frac{1+v}{1-v}-v=\frac{1+v^{2}}{1-v}\implies\int\frac{1-v}{1+v^{2}}dv=\int\frac{dx}{x} $$

Integrate:

$$ \int\frac{1}{1+v^{2}}dv-\frac{1}{2}\int\frac{2v}{1+v^{2}}dv=\int\frac{1}{x}dx $$

$$ \tan^{-1}v-\frac{1}{2}\log_{e}(1+v^{2})=\log_{e}|x|+C^{\prime} $$

$$ Substitute\;v=\frac{y}{x}: $$

$$ \tan^{-1}\frac{y}{x}=\log_{e}|x|+\frac{1}{2}\log_{e}\left(1+\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}}\right)+C^{\prime} $$

$$ \tan^{-1}\frac{y}{x}=\log_{e}|x|+\frac{1}{2}\log_{e}\left(\frac{x^{2}+y^{2}}{x^{2}}\right)+C^{\prime} $$

Simplify using logarithm properties $ \log_{e}(A/B) = \log_{e} A - \log_{e} B $:

$$ \tan^{-1}\frac{y}{x}=\log_{e}|x|+\frac{1}{2}\log_{e}(x^{2}+y^{2})-\frac{1}{2}\log_{e}(x^{2})+C^{\prime} $$

Use $ \log_e(x^2) = 2\log_e|x| $ and simplify constants into $ C $:

$$ \tan^{-1}\frac{y}{x}=\log_{e}|x|+\frac{1}{2}\log_{e}(x^{2}+y^{2})-\log_{e}|x|+C $$

Final solution:

$$ \tan^{-1}\frac{y}{x}=\frac{1}{2}\log_{e}(x^{2}+y^{2})+C $$

[The correct option is 4.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.424Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (870, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The corner points of the bounded feasible region for an LPP are: $ (5,5) $, $ (15,15) $, $ (0,20) $ and $ (0,10) $.

Let $ z = 3x + 9y $ be the objective function.

Then the value of maximum(z) - minimum(z) is:', NULL, '[{"text":"60","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"90","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"120","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"180","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.424Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'none', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (871, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The feasible region corresponding to the linear constraints of a Linear Programming Problem (LPP) is represented by the shaded region in the given figure. Which of the following is not a constraint to the given LPP?', NULL, '[{"text":"$ x + 2y \\leq 10 $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ x + y \\geq 2 $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ x - y \\geq 1 $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ x - y \\leq 1 $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'LPP: Identifying the Non-Constraint

The feasible region is the shaded area bounded by four lines and restricted to $ x \geq 0 $, $ y \geq 0 $ (first quadrant).

Lines forming the boundary are $ x+2y=10 $, $ x+y=2 $, and x-y=1.

Line 1: $ x + 2y = 10 $

The shaded region is below this line.

Test origin $ (0,0)\colon0+2(0)=0\leq10 $.

Constraint: $ x + 2y \leq 10 $ ( $ \text{Option} \, 1 \text{ is a constraint} $)

$$ Line2:x+y=2 $$

The shaded region is above this line.

$$ Test~origin\ (0,0)\colon0+0=0\not\preceq2. $$

Constraint: $ x + y \geq 2 $ ( $ \text{Option} \, 2 \text{ is a constraint} $)

$$ Line3\colon x-y=1 $$

The shaded region is above this line.

$$ Test~origin\ (0,0)\colon0-0=0\not\preceq1. $$

$$ Constraint:x-y\geq1 $$

The three main constraints derived are $ x + 2y \leq 10 $, $ x + y \geq 2 $, and $ x - y \geq 1 $ (plus $ x \geq 0 $, $ y \geq 0 $).

Comparing this with the provided options:

Option 1: $ x + 2y \leq 10 $ (A constraint)

Option 2: $ x + y \geq 2 $ (A constraint)

Option 3: $ x + 2y \geq 10 $ (NOT a constraint)

The boundary is $ x + 2y = 10 $, and the feasible region satisfies $ x + 2y \leq 10 $. Therefore, $ x + 2y \geq 10 $ is NOT a constraint defining the shaded region.

[The correct option is 3.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, '/local-papers/assets/mathematics-2025-06-02-shift-1/q48-figure-1.jpg', 'Figure for question 48', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.424Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/mathematics-2025-06-02-shift-1/q48-figure-1.jpg","alt":"Figure for question 48","kind":"figure"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (872, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If $x = -1$ and $x = -2$ are the extreme points of $f(x) = \alpha \log |x| + \beta x^{2} + x$ then', NULL, '[{"text":"\\alpha=\\frac{-2}{3},\\beta=\\frac{1}{6}","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"\\alpha=\\frac{-2}{3},\\beta=-\\frac{1}{6}","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"\\alpha=\\frac{2}{3},\\beta=\\frac{1}{6}","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"\\alpha=\\frac{2}{3},\\beta=-\\frac{1}{6}","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Extreme Points: Find $ \alpha $ and $ \beta $

Given $ f(x) = \alpha \log |x| + \beta x^2 + x $. Extreme points $ x = -1, x = -2 $ satisfy $ f''(x) = 0 $.

For $ x \neq 0 $, the derivative is:

$$ f^{\prime}(x)=\frac{\alpha}{x}+2\beta x+1 $$

$$ Set~f^{\prime}(x)=0\text{and multiply by}x: $$

$$ \frac{\alpha}{x}+2\beta x+1=0\quad\implies\quad\alpha+2\beta x^{2}+x=0 $$

Substitute the extreme points x = -1 and x = -2:

$$ x=-1:\quad\alpha+2\beta(-1)^{2}+(-1)=0\quad\implies\quad\alpha+2\beta=1 \tag*{(1)}$$

$$ x=-2:\quad\alpha+2\beta(-2)^{2}+(-2)=0\quad\implies\quad\alpha+8\beta=2 \tag*{(2)}$$

Subtract (1) from (2):

$$ \left(\alpha+8\beta\right)-\left(\alpha+2\beta\right)=2-1\implies6\beta=1\implies\beta=\frac{1}{6} $$

$$ Substitute\ \beta=\frac{1}{6}into\ (1): $$

$$ \alpha+2\left(\frac{1}{6}\right)=1\implies\alpha+\frac{1}{3}=1\implies\alpha=1-\frac{1}{3}=\frac{2}{3} $$

$$ Final~values:\alpha=\frac{2}{3},\beta=\frac{1}{6}. $$

[The correct option is 3.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.425Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (873, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The value of $ \lambda $ for which the two vectors $ (2\hat{i} - \hat{j} + 2\hat{k}) $ and $ (3\hat{i} + \lambda\hat{j} + \hat{k}) $ are perpendicular, is:', NULL, '[{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"3","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"4","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Perpendicular Vectors: Find $ \lambda $

Two vectors $ \vec{a} $ and $ \vec{b} $ are perpendicular if their dot product is zero, i.e. $ \vec{a} \cdot \vec{b} = 0 $.

Let $ \vec{a} = 2\mathbf{i} - \mathbf{j} + 2\mathbf{k} $ and $ b = 3\mathbf{i} + \lambda\mathbf{j} + \mathbf{k} $.

Applying the dot product condition:

$ \vec{a} \cdot \vec{b} = (2)(3) + (-1)(\lambda) + (2)(1) = 0 $

$ 6 - \lambda + 2 = 0 $

$ 8 - \lambda = 0 $

Hence, $ \lambda = 8 $

[The correct option is 2.]', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:38.425Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (874, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If $ z = 3z + 4y $ be the objective function of a linear programming problem (LPP) and (3,1), (2,4), (0,4), (5,0) be corner points of the bounded feasible region. Then the maximum value of objective function is', NULL, '[{"text":"13","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"10","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"22","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"14","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Solution:

The maximum value of the objective function is the maximum value it obtains at the corner points.

The maximum value of the objective function is the maximum value it obtained at point $ x = 3x + 4y $
At $ (x,y)=(3,1):z=3\times3+4\times1=9+4=13 $
At $ (x,y)=(2,4):z=3\times2+4\times4=6+16=22 $
At $ (x,y)=(0,4):z=3\times0+4\times4=0+16=16 $
At $ (x,y)=(5,0):z=3\times5+4\times0=15+0=15 $
Therefore the maximum value of the objective function is 22
Correct Option: (3)', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.873Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (875, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If $A$ be a square matrix of order 3 such that $|A|=2$, then $|adj(2A)|$ is equal to', NULL, '[{"text":"16","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"32","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"64","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"256","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'We know that if the order of a square matrix A is n, then

we know that if the order of a square matrix A is n, then

$ |adj(A)| = |A|^{n-1} $

and

$ |cA| = c^n|A| $

Here, the order of matrix A is 3.

$ \therefore |adj(2A)| = |2A|^{3-1} = |2A|^2 = (2^3|A|)^2 = (8|A|)^2 = 64|A|^2 $

If $ |A| = 2 $, then

$ |adj(2A)| = 64 \times 2^2 = 64 \times 4 = 256 $

Correct Option: (4)', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.873Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (876, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Match List-I with List-II', NULL, '[{"text":"\\ (A)-\\ (II),\\ (B)-\\ (IV),\\ (C)-\\ (III),\\ (D)-\\ (I)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"\\ (\\mathrm{A})-(\\mathrm{I I I}),\\ (\\mathrm{B})-(\\mathrm{I V}),\\ (\\mathrm{C})-(\\mathrm{I}),\\ (\\mathrm{D})-(\\mathrm{I I})","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"\\ (\\mathrm{A})-(\\mathrm{I I I}),\\ (\\mathrm{B})-(\\mathrm{I V}),\\ (\\mathrm{C})-(\\mathrm{I I}),\\ (\\mathrm{D})-(\\mathrm{I})","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"\\ (\\mathrm{A})-(\\mathrm{II}),(\\mathrm{B})-(\\mathrm{III}),(\\mathrm{C})-(\\mathrm{IV}),(\\mathrm{D})-(\\mathrm{I}) A.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'We know that the order of a differential equation is the order of the highest derivative present.

and the degree of a differential equation (if defined) is the power of the highest order derivative after the equation is free from radicals and fractions.

$$ \frac{d^{4}y}{dx^{4}}+2\log_{e}\left(\frac{d^{3}y}{dx^{3}}\right)=0 $$

Since $ \log_{e}\left(\frac{d^{3}y}{dx^{3}}\right) $ involves a logarithm of a derivative,

the equation is not polynomial in its derivatives. Hence, the degree is not defined.

$$ \Rightarrow(A)\rightarrow(III) $$

$$ e^{(\frac{d y}{d x})^{3}}+3y(\frac{d^{2}y}{d x^{2}})^{3}=0 $$

The highest derivative is $ \frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} $.

$$ \Rightarrow(B)\rightarrow(IV) $$

$$ \frac{d^{4}y}{dx^{4}}+\left(\frac{dy}{dx}\right)^{2}=0 $$

The equation is polynomial and the highest order derivative is $ \frac{d^4y}{dx^4} $.

Degree is 1.

$ \Rightarrow (C) \rightarrow (I) $

$$ 2\frac{d^{4}y}{d x^{4}}+\left(\frac{d^{2}y}{d x^{2}}\right)^{5}=0 $$

The highest order derivative is $ \frac{d^{4}y}{dx^{4}} $.

Order is 4.

$ \Rightarrow (D) \rightarrow (II) $

Correct Option: (2)', NULL, NULL, 2025, '/local-papers/assets/mathematics-2025-06-03-shift-2/q03-table-1.jpg', 'Table for question 3', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.874Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/mathematics-2025-06-03-shift-2/q03-table-1.jpg","alt":"Table for question 3","kind":"table"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (877, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If A and B are symmetric matrices, then AB - BA is', NULL, '[{"text":"Singular matrix","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Zero matrix","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Symmetric matrix","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Skew symmetric matrix","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Consider the matrix AB - BA.

$$ \left(\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{B}-\boldsymbol{B}\boldsymbol{A}\right)^{T}=\left(\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{B}\right)^{T}-\left(\boldsymbol{B}\boldsymbol{A}\right)^{T}=\boldsymbol{B}^{T}\boldsymbol{A}^{T}-\boldsymbol{A}^{T}\boldsymbol{B}^{T}=\boldsymbol{B}\boldsymbol{A}-\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{B}=-\left(\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{B}-\boldsymbol{B}\boldsymbol{A}\right) $$

Since the transpose of $ (AB - BA) $ is the negative of itself, it is a skew-symmetric matrix.

Correct Option: (4)', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.874Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (878, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If A is an invertible matrix, then which of the following statement(s) is/are TRUE?

(A) $ |A^{-1}| = |A| $

(B) $ (A^{-1})^{-1} = A $

(C) $ A^{-1} = \frac{adj A}{|A|} $

(D) $ (A^{T})^{-1} = (A^{-1})^{T} $', NULL, '[{"text":"(A), (B) and (C) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B) and (C) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B), (C) and (D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'We know that if $A$ is invertible, then $A^{-1} = \frac{\mathrm{adj}A}{|A|}$ and $|A^{-1}| = \frac{1}{|A|}$.

(A) $ |A^{-1}| = |A| $ is false since $ |A^{-1}| = \frac{1}{|A|} $.

(B) $ (A^{-1})^{-1} = A $ is true.

(C) $ A^{-1} = \frac{adj A}{|A|} $ is true.

(D) $ (A^{T})^{-1} = (A^{-1})^{T} $ is also true.

Correct Option: (4)', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.874Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (879, 3, NULL, NULL, '$ \int_{0}^{a}\frac{\sqrt{x}}{\sqrt{x}+\sqrt{a-x}}dx $ is equal to', NULL, '[{"text":"a","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{a}{2} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{a}{3} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2a","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Let I = \int_{0}^{a} \frac{\sqrt{x}}{\sqrt{x} + \sqrt{a - x}} \, dx

Using \int_{0}^{a} f(x) \, dx = \int_{0}^{a} f(a - x) \, dx,

I = \int_{0}^{a} \frac{\sqrt{a - x}}{\sqrt{a - x} + \sqrt{x}} \, dx

2I = \int_{0}^{a} \left[ \frac{\sqrt{x}}{\sqrt{x} + \sqrt{a - x}} + \frac{\sqrt{a - x}}{\sqrt{a - x} + \sqrt{x}} \right] \, dx

2I = \int_{0}^{a} 1 \, dx = a \Rightarrow I = \frac{a}{2}

$ \boxed{\text{Correct Option: (2)}} $', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.875Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (880, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Let X denotes the number of heads in a simultaneous toss of three coins, then $ P(0 < X < 3) $ is', NULL, '[{"text":"$ \\frac{1}{2} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{3}{4} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{7}{8} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Possible outcomes: HHH, HHT, HTH, HTT, THH, THT, TTH, TTT.

$ P(X=0)=\frac{1}{8} $, $ P(X=1)=\frac{3}{8} $, $ P(X=2)=\frac{3}{8} $, $ P(X=3)=\frac{1}{8} $

$ P(0 < X < 3) = P(X = 1) + P(X = 2) = \frac{3}{8} + \frac{3}{8} = \frac{6}{8} = \frac{3}{4} $

Correct Option: (2)', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.875Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (881, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The area (in sq. units) of the region bounded by y = -1, y = 2, $ x = y^{3} $ and x = 0 is equal to 1.4

2.8

3. $ \frac{17}{4} $

4. $ \frac{15}{4} $', NULL, '[{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"3","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"4","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Bounded by y = -1, y = 2, $ x = y^{3} $, x = 0.

$$

\begin{aligned}

A &= \int_0^2 (y^3 - 0) \, dy + \int_{-1}^0 (0 - y^3) \, dy \\

\int y^3 \, dy &= \frac{y^4}{4} \\

\int_0^2 y^3 \, dy &= \frac{16}{4} - 0 = 4 \\

\int_{-1}^0 (-y^3) \, dy &= -\left(0 - \frac{1}{4}\right) = \frac{1}{4} \\

A &= 4 + \frac{1}{4} = \frac{17}{4} \\

\end{aligned}

$$

Correct Option: (3)

The general solution of the differential equation $ \frac{dy}{dx} = -4xy^{2} $ is given by

1. $ 2x^{2}-y=C $ : C is an arbitrary constant

2. $ 2x^{2}-\frac{1}{y}=C $ : C is an arbitrary constant

3. $ 2x^{2}+\frac{1}{y^{2}}=C $ : C is an arbitrary constant

4. $ 2x^{2}+\frac{1}{y}=C $ : C is an arbitrary constant

Options:

A. 1

B. 2

C. 3

D. 4

Answer: B

Solution:

$$\frac{dy}{dx} = -4xy^2$$

$$\frac{dy}{y^2} = -4x \, dx$$

$$\int \frac{dy}{y^2} = \int -4x \, dx$$

$$-\frac{1}{y} = -2x^2 + C$$

$$\frac{1}{y} = 2x^2 - C$$

$$2x^2 - \frac{1}{y} = C$$

$$\left| \text{Correct Option: (2)} \right|$$', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.875Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (882, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Assume $A, B$ and $C$ are matrices of order $m \times n$, $n \times 3$ and $3 \times q$ respectively. The restrictions on $m, n$ and $q$ so that $AB + BC$ is defined are', NULL, '[{"text":"$n$ is arbitrary, $q = 2$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$m = n, q = 3$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$q = 3, m = 3$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$q$ is arbitrary, $n = 2$","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Given $ A:m \times n $, $ B:n \times 3 $, $ C:3 \times q $.

AB has order $ m \times 3 $, BC has order $ n \times q $.

AB + BC defined only if m = n and q = 3.

Correct Option: (2)', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.876Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (883, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Which of the following terms are associated with a linear programming problem?

(A) Constraints

(B) Independent events

(C) Feasible region

(D) Objective function', NULL, '[{"text":"(A) and (C) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A), (C) and (D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B), (C) and (D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A), (B) and (D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'The terms associated with linear programming problems are: constraints, feasible region, objective function. The term "independent events" is associated with probability.

Correct Option: (2)', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.876Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (884, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Which of the following functions has a local minima at $x = 0?$

(A) $f(x) = x^{3}$

(B) $f(x) = |x|$

(C) $f(x) = x^{2}$

(D) $f(x) = x^{-2}$', NULL, '[{"text":"(A), (B) and (C) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B), (C) and (D) only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B) and (C) only","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '(A) $ f(x) = x^3 : f''(0) = 0, f''''(0) = 0 $ inflection point.

(B) $ f(x) = |x| $ minimum at x = 0.

(C) $ f(x) = x^2 : f''(0) = 0, f''''(0) = 2 > 0 $ local minimum.

(D) $ f(x) = x^{-2} $ not defined at x = 0.

Hence only (B) and (C).

Correct Option: (4)', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.877Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (885, 3, NULL, NULL, '\int_{0}^{8}\left(x^{\frac{2}{3}}+1\right)dx is equal to', NULL, '[{"text":"$ \\frac{28}{3} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{25}{3} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{184}{3} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{136}{5} $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, NULL, NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.880Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'none', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (886, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Function $f(x)=x^{x}, x>0$ decreases on the interval', NULL, '[{"text":"$(0,\\frac{1}{e})$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$(0,e)$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$(\\frac{1}{e},\\infty)$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$(e,\\infty)$","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '$$

\begin{aligned}

f(x) &= x^x = e^{x \ln x}, \, f''(x) = x^x (\ln x + 1) \\

f''(x) &= 0 \Rightarrow \ln x = -1 \Rightarrow x = \frac{1}{e} \\

f''(x) & < 0 \Rightarrow \ln x < -1 \Rightarrow x < \frac{1}{e} \\

\end{aligned}

$$

Decreasing on $(0, \frac{1}{e})$

Correct Option: (1)', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.880Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (887, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If x = at^{2}, y = 2at; then \frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} is equal to', NULL, '[{"text":"0","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{1}{t} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ -\\frac{1}{t^{2}} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ -\\frac{1}{2at^{3}} $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '$$ x=at^{2},y=2at $$

$$ \frac{dx}{dt}=2at,\ \frac{dy}{dt}=2a $$

$$ \frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt}=\frac{1}{t} $$

$$ \frac{d(dy/dx)}{dt}=-\frac{1}{t^{2}} $$

$$ \frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}=\frac{-1/t^{2}}{dx/dt}=\frac{-1/t^{2}}{2at}=-\frac{1}{2at^{3}} $$

Correct Option: (4)

Core Mathematics', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.881Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (888, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The number of arbitrary constants in the particular solution of a differential equation of order 4 and degree 3 is', NULL, '[{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"3","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"4","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'The general solution of a differential equation of order n contains n arbitrary constants.

For a differential equation of order 4, the general solution contains 4 arbitrary constants.

The particular solution has no arbitrary constants.

Correct Option: (1)', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.882Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (889, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If $A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 5 \\ 7 & 12 \end{bmatrix}$, $B = \begin{bmatrix} 9 & 1 \\ 7 & 8 \end{bmatrix}$ and C are three matrices such that $3A + 5B + 2C = 0$, then the matrix C is equal to', NULL, '[{"text":"$ \\begin{bmatrix}-48&-20\\\\-56&-76\\end{bmatrix} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\begin{bmatrix}-24&-10\\\\-28&-38\\end{bmatrix} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\left[\\begin{array}{cc}-48&20\\\\56&-76\\end{array}\\right] $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\begin{bmatrix}24&10\\\\28&38\\end{bmatrix} $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '$$ A=\begin{bmatrix}1&5\\ 7&12\end{bmatrix},\quad B=\begin{bmatrix}9&1\\ 7&8\end{bmatrix},\quad3A+5B+2C=0 $$

$$ 2C=-3A-5B $$

$$ -3A=\begin{bmatrix}-3&-15\\ -21&-36\end{bmatrix},\quad-5B=\begin{bmatrix}-45&-5\\ -35&-40\end{bmatrix} $$

$$ -3A-5B=\begin{bmatrix}-48&-20\\ -56&-76\end{bmatrix} $$

$$ C={\frac{1}{2}}\left[\begin{matrix}{-48}&{-20}\\ {-56}&{-76}\\ \end{matrix}\right]=\left[\begin{matrix}{-24}&{-10}\\ {-28}&{-38}\\ \end{matrix}\right] $$

$$ \boxed{\mathrm{C o r r e c t~O p t i o n:}\;(2)} $$', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.883Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (890, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If $ \vec{a} $ is any vector, then $ |\vec{a} \times \hat{i}|^2 + |\vec{a} \times \hat{j}|^2 + |\vec{a} \times \hat{k}|^2 $ is equal to', NULL, '[{"text":"$ |\\vec{a}|^{2} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ 2|\\vec{a}|^{2} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ 3|\\vec{a}|^{2} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ 4|\\vec{a}|^{2} $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '$$ \vec{a}=a_{1}\hat{i}+a_{2}\hat{j}+a_{3}\hat{k} $$

$$ \vec{a}\times\hat{i}=\det\begin{bmatrix}\hat{i}&\hat{j}&\hat{k}\\ a_{1}&a_{2}&a_{3}\\ 1&0&0\end{bmatrix}=-a_{3}\hat{j}+a_{2}\hat{k} $$

$$ |\vec{a}\times\hat{i}|^{2}=a_{3}^{2}+a_{2}^{2} $$

$$ \vec{a}\times\hat{\boldsymbol{j}}=\det\begin{bmatrix}\hat{\boldsymbol{i}}&\hat{\boldsymbol{j}}&\hat{\boldsymbol{k}}\\ \boldsymbol{a}_{1}&\boldsymbol{a}_{2}&\boldsymbol{a}_{3}\\ 0&1&0\end{bmatrix}=-\boldsymbol{a}_{3}\hat{\boldsymbol{i}}+\boldsymbol{a}_{1}\hat{\boldsymbol{k}} $$

$$ |\vec{a}\times\hat{\boldsymbol{j}}|^{2}=a_{3}^{2}+a_{1}^{2} $$

$$ \vec{a}\times\hat{k}=\det\begin{bmatrix}\hat{i}&\hat{j}&\hat{k}\\ a_{1}&a_{2}&a_{3}\\ 0&0&1\end{bmatrix}=a_{2}\hat{i}-a_{1}\hat{j} $$

$$ |\vec{a}\times\hat{k}|^{2}=a_{2}^{2}+a_{1}^{2} $$

$$ |\vec{a}\times\hat{i}|^{2}+|\vec{a}\times\hat{j}|^{2}+|\vec{a}\times\hat{k}|^{2}=2(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+a_{3}^{2})=2|\vec{a}|^{2} $$

Correct option: (2)', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.883Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (891, 3, NULL, NULL, 'A bag contains 4 red and 6 black balls. Two balls are drawn in succession without replacement. The probability that the first is red and the second is black is $ 1 \cdot \frac{2}{5} $.

$ 2 \cdot \frac{2}{3} $

$ 3 \cdot \frac{4}{15} $

$ 4 \cdot \frac{6}{25} $', NULL, '[{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"3","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"4","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Total balls = 4 + 6 = 10

$ P(\text{first red}) = \frac{4}{10} $

$ P(\text{second black}|\text{first red}) = \frac{6}{9} $

$ P(\text{first red and second black}) = \frac{4}{10} \times \frac{6}{9} = \frac{24}{90} = \frac{4}{15} $

Correct option: (3)', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.883Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (892, 3, NULL, NULL, '$$\int e^{2x}\left(\sin x+\frac{1}{2}\cos x\right)dx\text{ is equal to}$$

1. $-\frac{1}{2}e^{2x}\cos x+C$, $C\text{ is an arbitrary constant}$$

2. $ \frac{1}{2}e^{2x}\sin x+C $, C is an arbitrary constant

3. $ e^{2x} \sin x + C $, C is an arbitrary constant', NULL, '[{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"3","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"4 4. $ -e^{2x}\\cos x+C $, C is an arbitrary constant","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '$$ I=\int e^{2x}\left(\sin x+\frac{1}{2}\cos x\right)dx $$

$$ \int e^{ax}\sin bx\,dx=\frac{e^{ax}}{a^{2}+b^{2}}(a\sin bx-b\cos bx) $$

$$ \int e^{ax}\cos bx dx=\frac{e^{ax}}{a^{2}+b^{2}}(a\cos bx+b\sin bx) $$

$$ a=2,b=1 $$

$$ I=e^{2x}\left[\frac{1}{5}(2\sin x-\cos x)+\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{5}(2\cos x+\sin x)\right]+C $$

$$ I=\frac{e^{2x}}{5}\Biggl[(2\sin x-\cos x)+\frac{1}{2}(2\cos x+\sin x)\Biggr]+C $$

$$ I=\frac{e^{2x}}{5}\cdot\frac{5}{2}\sin x+C=\frac{1}{2}e^{2x}\sin x+C $$

Correct option: (2)', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.884Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (893, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Let $f(x)=\begin{cases}|x|+3&if x\leq-3\\-2x&if-3<x<3\\6x+2&if x\geq3\end{cases}$, then which of the following is true?', NULL, '[{"text":"$f(x)$ is discontinuous at both $x=3$ and $x=-3$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$f(x)$ is continuous at both $x=3$ and $x=-3$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=-3$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=3$","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '$$f(x)=\begin{cases}|x|+3,&x\leq-3\\-2x,&-3<x<3\\6x+2,&x\geq3\end{cases}$$
$$f(-3)=6,\quad\lim_{x\to-3}f(x)=6,\quad\lim_{x\to-3^{+}}f(x)=6$$
$$f(3)=20,\quad\lim_{x\to3}f(x)=-6,\quad\lim_{x\to3^{+}}f(x)=20$$
$$f(x)\text{continuous at }x=-3,\text{but discontinuous at }x=3.$$
$$\boxed{\text{Correct option:}(4)}$$', NULL, NULL, 2025, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:45.885Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (894, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The system of equations

$ x + y + z = 4 $

$ x + 2y + 3z = 12 $

$ x + 3y + \lambda z = \mu $

has a unique solution if', NULL, '[{"text":"$ \\lambda = 5 $, $ \\mu $ can be any real number","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\lambda = 5 $, $ \\mu \\neq 20 $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\lambda \\neq 5 $, $ \\mu $ can be any real number","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\lambda = 5 $, $ \\mu = 20 $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Possible outcomes: HHH, HHT, HTH, HTT, THH, THT, TTH, TTT.

$ P(X=0)=\frac{1}{8} $, $ P(X=1)=\frac{3}{8} $, $ P(X=2)=\frac{3}{8} $, $ P(X=3)=\frac{1}{8} $

