○ (D) - (I) Organisms can be divided into discrete categories.

Hence, the correct answer is option 4.', NULL, NULL, 2024, '/local-papers/assets/english-2024-05-29-shift-1/q33-table-1.jpg', 'Table for question 33', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:46.250Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/english-2024-05-29-shift-1/q33-table-1.jpg","alt":"Table for question 33","kind":"table"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (1412, 1, NULL, NULL, 'The synonym of ''fastidious'' is:', NULL, '[{"text":"strong","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"finicky","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"brave","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"weak","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'The correct answer is: Option 2: finicky.
Key Points

- The word "fastidious" means very attentive to and concerned about accuracy and detail; very concerned about matters of cleanliness. (अत्यधिक सूक्ष्म ध्यान रखने वाला)

Example: She was fastidious about keeping her house clean and tidy.

• "Finicky" refers to someone who is excessively particular, difficult to please, and fussy about details. (सूक्ष्मता से ध्यान देने वाला)

Example: He is very finicky about his food, always checking the ingredients before eating.

• Hence, we can infer that the synonym of ''fastidious'' is ''finicky''.

Therefore, the correct answer is: Option 2.
Additional Information

Here are the other options explained along with their Hindi meanings and example sentences:

• Strong (मजबूत): Having the power to move heavy weights or perform other physically demanding tasks.

Example: He is very strong and can lift heavy furniture easily.

• Brave (साहसी): Ready to face and endure danger or pain; showing courage.

Example: She was very brave to speak out against the injustice she witnessed.

• Weak (कमजोर): Lacking physical strength and energy.

Example: After recovering from the illness, he felt very weak and needed help to walk.', NULL, NULL, 2024, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:46.250Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (1413, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Rearrange the phrases to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) BMI model is an obsolete method for calculating

(B) suggesting that the athlete get a fat to muscle mass index scan done

(C) the fitness level of an individual

(D) the dietician said that the', NULL, '[{"text":"(A), (C), (B), (D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(D), (A), (C), (B)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B), (D), (A), (C)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C), (D), (B), (A)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'The correct answer is ''2''
Key Points

• The arrangement of the phrases needs to form a coherent sentence that conveys clear meaning.

• Starting with (D) places the subject of the sentence at the beginning.

• Following with (A) and (C) provides the necessary details about the BMI model.

• Ending with (B) offers the additional advice related to the initial statement.

Correct sentence: the dietician said that the BMI model is an obsolete method for calculating the fitness level of an individual, suggesting that the athlete gets a fat to muscle mass index scan done.
Additional Information

• Option 1: Arranging the phrases in this order does not make a coherent or grammatically correct sentence.

• Option 3: Starting with (B) places the advice out of context before introducing the statement.

• Option 4: This order also fails to create a grammatically correct and meaningful sentence.', NULL, NULL, 2024, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:46.251Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (1414, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Fill in the blank with the most appropriate word:

The British Raj regularly _ vernacular newspapers that printed articles advocating for Indian home rule.', NULL, '[{"text":"prescribed","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"proscribed","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"probated","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"popularised","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'The correct answer is ''proscribed''.
Key Points

• The word "proscribed" means to forbid, especially by law. (प्रतिबंधित करना)

- The British Raj would often forbid or ban vernacular newspapers that promoted ideas of Indian home rule.

• The other options: "prescribed," "probated," and "popularised" do not fit the context as appropriately.

Therefore, the correct answer is ''Option 2''.

Complete Sentence: The British Raj regularly $ \underline{\text{proscribed}} $ vernacular newspapers that printed articles advocating for Indian home rule.
Additional Information

• Prescribed (निधीरित करना): means to recommend or authorize use of something.

• Probated (वसीयत का प्रमाणिकरण करना): means to establish the validity of a will.

• Popularised (लोकोंप्रिय बनाना): means to make something widely known or used.', NULL, NULL, 2024, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:46.252Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (1415, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the most appropriate word to fill in the blank in the following sentence:

The alibi offered by the suspect in the chain-snatching incident did not appear to be _.', NULL, '[{"text":"credible","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"creditable","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"credulous","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"considerable","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'The correct answer is ''credible''.
Key Points

• The word "credible" means able to be believed; convincing. (विश्वसनीय)

• An alibi is a claim or piece of evidence that one was elsewhere when an act, typically a criminal one, is alleged to have taken place.

- The other options: "creditable," "credulous," and "considerable" do not fit the context as appropriately.

Therefore, the correct answer is ''Option 1''.

Complete Sentence: The alibi offered by the suspect in the chain-snatching incident did not appear to be $ \underline{\text{credible.}} $
Additional Information

• Creditable (श्रेयरकर): means deserving public acknowledgment and praise but not necessarily outstanding or successful.

• Credulous (आतिसरली): means having or showing too great a readiness to believe things.

• Considerable (คาบ): means notably large in size, amount, or extent.', NULL, NULL, 2024, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:46.252Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (1416, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct synonym for the given word: Pique', NULL, '[{"text":"Resentment","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Ravage","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Calm","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Docile","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'The correct answer is: Option 1 i.e. Resentment.
Key Points

• The word "pique" means a feeling of irritation or resentment resulting from a slight, especially to one''s pride. (नारायण)

Example: She left the meeting in a fit of pique after her ideas were dismissed.

• "Resentment" refers to a feeling of anger or displeasure about someone or something unfair. (नारजगी)

Example: He felt deep resentment towards his boss for not recognizing his hard work.

• Hence, we can infer that the synonym of ''pique'' is ''resentment''.

Therefore, the correct answer is: Option 1 i.e. Resentment.
Additional Information

Here are the other options explained along with their Hindi meanings and example sentences:

• Ravage (तबाही): Cause severe and extensive damage to.

• Example: The hurricane ravaged the coastal towns.

• Calm (शांत): Not showing or feeling nervousness, anger, or other emotions.

Example: She remained calm throughout the crisis.

• Docile (विन्फ्): Ready to accept control or instruction; submissive.

Example: The docile dog followed its owner without hesitation.', NULL, NULL, 2024, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:46.253Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (1417, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct antonym for ''taciturn'' from the options given below:', NULL, '[{"text":"reserved","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"vehement","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"garrulous","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"ungraceful","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'The correct answer is: Option 3 i.e. ''garrulous''.
Key Points

• The word "taciturn" means someone who is reserved or uncommunicative in speech; saying little. (अलपभाषी)

Example: Despite being in a group of friends, he remained taciturn and did not participate much in the conversation.

• "Garrulous" refers to someone who is excessively talkative, especially on trivial matters. (वाचल)

• Example: The garrulous woman at the party wouldn''t stop talking about her pet cat.

• Hence, we can infer that the opposite of ''taciturn'' is ''garrulous''.

Therefore, the correct answer is: Option 3 i.e. ''garrulous''.
Additional Information

Here are the other options explained along with their Hindi meanings and example sentences:

• Reserved (संकोची): Slow to reveal emotion or opinions.

Example: He was reserved about his personal life and rarely shared details with others.

• Vehement (๓๓): Showing strong feeling; forceful, passionate, or intense.

○ Example: She was vehement in her opposition to the new policy.

• Ungracious (आश्रम): Lacking social graces; rude.

Example: His ungracious behavior at the dinner party was noticed by everyone.

Question: Choose the correct antonym for ''taciturn'' from the options given below:

The correct answer is: Option 3 i.e. ''garrulous''.', NULL, NULL, 2024, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:46.254Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (1418, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Match the blanks in List-I with the verb forms in List-II :', NULL, '[{"text":"(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(III),(B)-(I),(C)-(IV),(D)-(II)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A) - $ IV $, (B) - $ III $, (C) - $ II $, (D) - $ I $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'The correct answer is option 2.
Key Points

• From the given options, only option 2 creates correct sentences.

Let''s look at the sentences:

• "(A) - (III)'' means "She plays with her dog every day." This is correct as "plays" is the simple present tense, which is used for habitual actions.

○ "(B) - (I)'' means "She played the violin at the concert beautifully." This is correct as "played" is the simple past tense, which is used for actions completed in the past.

° "(C) - (IV)'' means "She has been playing badminton her whole life." This is correct as "has been playing" is the present perfect continuous tense, which is used for actions that started in the past and are still continuing.

• "(D) - (II)'' means "She had been playing video games the whole of yesterday." This is correct as "had been playing" is the past perfect continuous tense, which is used for actions that were ongoing in the past until another action occurred.

Hence, the correct answer is option 2.', NULL, NULL, 2024, '/local-papers/assets/english-2024-05-29-shift-1/q40-table-1.jpg', 'Table for question 40', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:46.255Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/english-2024-05-29-shift-1/q40-table-1.jpg","alt":"Table for question 40","kind":"table"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (1419, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Match the words in List-I with the meanings in List-II :', NULL, '[{"text":"(A)-(II),(B)-(III),(C)-(I),(D)-(IV)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(III),(B)-(IV),(C)-(II),(D)-(I)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(IV),(B)-(I),(C)-(III),(D)-(II)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'The correct answer is $ (A) $ - $ (II) $, $ (B) $ - $ (III) $, $ (C) $ - $ (I) $, $ (D) $ - $ (IV) $.
Key Points

• From the given options, only the combination of phrases $ (A) - (II) $, $ (B) - (III) $, $ (C) - (I) $, $ (D) - (IV) $''creates correct sentences.

Let''s look at the meanings:

○ "(A) - (II)" Numismatist: One who collects coins.

○ "(B) - (III)" Philatelist: One who collects stamps.

○ "(C) - (I)"Itinerant: One who journeys from place to place.

○ "(D) - (IV)" Cacographist: One who is bad at spellings.

Hence, the correct answer is option 1.', NULL, NULL, 2024, '/local-papers/assets/english-2024-05-29-shift-1/q41-table-1.jpg', 'Table for question 41', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:46.255Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/english-2024-05-29-shift-1/q41-table-1.jpg","alt":"Table for question 41","kind":"table"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (1420, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Complete the sentences given in List-I with the appropriate prepositions given in List-II :', NULL, '[{"text":"(A)-(IV),(B)-(III),(C)-(II),(D)-(I)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(III),(B)-(IV),(C)-(I),(D)-(II)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(I),(B)-(II),(C)-(IV),(D)-(III)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A) - $ (\\mathrm{III}) $, (B) - $ (\\mathrm{I}) $, (C) - $ (\\mathrm{IV}) $, (D) - $ (\\mathrm{II}) $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'The correct answer is ''2''.
Key Points

• (A) I was delighted with the present you gave me. (III - with)

(B) The letter I wrote was full of mistakes. (IV - of)

(C) A strange thing happened to me a few days ago. (I - to)

• (D) I hope you succeed in getting the job you want. (II - in)

Correct matching: (A) - (III), (B) - (IV), (C) - (I), (D) - (II).
Additional Information

• With: Used when pleased and grateful for something.

• Of: Used to indicate what something is filled or loaded with.

• To: Used when indicating direction or addressing someone affected by an action.

• In: Used when referring to being successful or achieving something.', NULL, NULL, 2024, '/local-papers/assets/english-2024-05-29-shift-1/q42-table-1.jpg', 'Table for question 42', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:46.256Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/english-2024-05-29-shift-1/q42-table-1.jpg","alt":"Table for question 42","kind":"table"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (1421, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the word opposite in meaning to the underlined word : Allegations about the industrialist''s involvement in financial irregularities were $ \underline{\text{spurious}} $.', NULL, '[{"text":"false","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"unethical","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"genuine","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"impulsive","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'The correct answer is: genuine.
Key Points

• The word "spurious" means something that is false or not what it appears to be; counterfeit or fake. (नकली)

Example: The claim that the painting was an original by Picasso turned out to be spurious.

• "Genuine" refers to something that is authentic, real, and not fake or counterfeit. (أسليل)

Example: She has a genuine interest in helping others and often volunteers at the local shelter.

• Hence, we can infer that the opposite of ''spurious'' is ''genuine''.

Therefore, the correct answer is: genuine.
Additional Information

Here are the other options explained along with their Hindi meanings and example sentences:

• False (☐): Not true; incorrect.

Example: The report was based on false information and needed to be corrected.

• Unethical (अनैतिक): Not morally correct.

Example: His unethical behavior led to his dismissal from the company.

• Impulsive (आवेगो): Acting or done without forethought.

Example: She made an impulsive decision to quit her job and travel the world.', NULL, NULL, 2024, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:46.256Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (1422, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Match the idioms in List-I with the meanings in List-II :', NULL, '[{"text":"(A)-(I),(B)-(II),(C)-(III),(D)-(IV)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(I),(B)-(III),(C)-(II),(D)-(IV)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(I),(B)-(II),(C)-(IV),(D)-(III)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A) - $ (\\mathrm{III}) $, (B) - $ (\\mathrm{IV}) $, (C) - $ (\\mathrm{I}) $, (D) - $ (\\mathrm{II}) $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Key Points

• From the given options, only the combination of idioms and meanings in ''option 4'' is correct.

Let''s look at the correct matches:

(A) to sit on the fence - (III) delay in making a decision

○ (B) a sitting duck - (IV) an easy target for attack or criticism

(C) sit pretty - (I) to be in a good position

(D) sit tight - (II) to not take any action/remain in a place

Hence, the correct answer is option 4.', NULL, NULL, 2024, '/local-papers/assets/english-2024-05-29-shift-1/q44-table-1.jpg', 'Table for question 44', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:46.257Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/english-2024-05-29-shift-1/q44-table-1.jpg","alt":"Table for question 44","kind":"table"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (1423, 1, NULL, NULL, 'The Green Blazer stood out prominently under the bright sun and blue sky. In all that jostling crowd one could not help noticing it. Villagers in shirts and turbans, townsmen in coats and caps, beggars bare-bodied and women in multi-coloured saris were thronging the narrow passage between the stalls and moving in great confused masses, but still the Green Blazer could not be missed. The jabber and babble of the marketplace was there, as people harangued, disputed prices, haggled or greeted each other; over it all boomed the voice of a Bible-preacher, and when he paused for breath, from another corner the loudspeaker of a health van amplified on malaria and tuberculosis. Over and above it all the Green Blazer seemed to cry out an invitation. Raju could not ignore it. It was not in his nature to ignore such a persistent invitation. He kept himself half-aloof from the crowd; he could not afford to remain completely aloof nor keep himself in it too conspicuously. Wherever he might be, he was harrowed by the fear of being spotted by a policeman; today he wore a loin-cloth and was bare-bodied, and had wound an enormous turban over his head, which over-shadowed his face completely and he hoped that he would be taken for a peasant from a village. He sat on a stack of cast-off banana stalks beside a shop awning and watched the crowd. When he watched a crowd, he did it with concentration. It was his professional occupation. Constitutionally he was an idler and had just the amount of energy to watch in a crowd and put his hand into another person''s pocket. It was a gamble, of course. Sometimes he got nothing out of a venture, counting himself lucky if he came out with his fingers intact.
Raju is afraid of being spotted by the policemen because :', NULL, '[{"text":"he didn''t want to be out at the beginning of the game.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"he was not fond of policemen.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"he wanted to win this game.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"he didn''t want to be caught before the kill. Answer: D","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'The correct answer is ''he didn''t want to be caught before the kill''.
Key Points

- The passage describes Raju as someone who engages in pickpocketing, which he considers a gamble.

- He is afraid of being spotted by the policeman because if he is identified, he might be caught before he can complete his attempt to pickpocket.

- This is evident from his efforts to disguise himself by wearing a loin-cloth, being bare-bodied, and wearing an enormous turban to look like a peasant.

- Raju''s fear suggests that he aims to avoid detection until he has successfully completed his theft.

Therefore, the correct answer is ''Option 4''.
Additional Information

• Option 1: There is no mention of a "game" in a literal sense.

• Option 2: While Raju may not be fond of policemen, his primary concern is not getting caught.

• Option 3: The passage does not describe Raju wanting to win a game; it focuses on his criminal activity.', NULL, NULL, 2024, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:46.257Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (1424, 18, NULL, 116, 'Which of the following means ''A is the son-in-law of G''?', NULL, '[{"text":"$ A \\times U\\% S \\times G $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ A + S\\% U \\times G $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ A - S + U \\times G $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ A \\times U\\% S + G $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Decoding symbols:

So, checking each option one by one:

Option 1) A × U % S × G → A is the husband of U who is the sister of S who is the husband of G.

Here, A is G''s husband''s brother-in-law.

Option 2) A + S % U × G → A is the son of S who is the sister of U who is the husband of G.

Here, A is G''s niece.

Option 3) A−S + U × G → A is the brother of S who is the son of U who is the husband of G.

Here, A is G''s son.

Option 4) $ A \times U \% S + G \rightarrow A $ is the husband of U who is the sister of S who is the son of G.

Here, A is G''s son-in-law.

Hence, the correct answer is"Option 4".', NULL, NULL, 2024, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T20:59:46.259Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (1425, 18, NULL, 117, 'II. All computers can never be keyboards.', NULL, '[{"text":"Only conclusion I follows","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Only conclusion II follows","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Neither conclusion I nor II follows","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Both conclusions I and II follow","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'The least possible Venn diagram for the given statements is as shown below:
Conclusions:

I. All mouses are laptops→Follow(Asall mouses are computers and all computers are laptops. As whole mouses comes in computers which wholecomesin laptops, therefore it is true)

II. All computers can never be keyboards→Does not follow (Asno keyboard is a mouseandall mouses are computers.As no direct relation is given between computers and keyboards, therefore it is false.)

