$ =\frac{\text{magnetic dipole moment}}{\text{volume}} $

and its unit is also A/m.', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.955Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4929, 4, NULL, NULL, 'A current of 5 A is flowing at 220 V in the primary coil of a transformer. If the voltage produced in the secondary coil is 2200 V and 50% of power is lost, then the current in the secondary coil will be', NULL, '[{"text":"0.25 A","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"0.5 A","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2.5 A","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"5 A","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'We have, efficiency = $ \frac{1}{2} $

$ \Rightarrow \frac{P_0}{P_i} = \frac{1}{2} \Rightarrow \frac{V_0 I_0}{V_i I_i} = \frac{1}{2} \Rightarrow \frac{2200}{220} \times \frac{I_0}{5} = \frac{1}{2} $

$ \Rightarrow I_0 = \frac{1}{4} A = 0.25 A $', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.956Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4930, 4, NULL, NULL, 'Electric field in plane electromagnetic wave is given by:

$ Ey = 5 \times 10^{-7} \sin (0.25 \times 10^{3}x + 2.5 \times 10^{11}t) $

The magnetic field associated with it is', NULL, '[{"text":"$ B_{x} = 0.5 \\times 10^{-7} \\sin (0.25 \\times 10^{3}x + 2.5 \\times 10^{11}t) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ B_{x} = 1.66 \\times 10^{-15} \\sin (0.25 \\times 10^{3}x + 2.5 \\times 10^{11}t) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ B_{x} = 9 \\times 10^{-7} \\sin (0.25 \\times 10^{3}x + 2.5 \\times 10^{11}t) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ B_{x} = 3.6 \\times 10^{-15} \\cos (0.25 \\times 10^{3}x + 2.5 \\times 10^{11}t) $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'We have,

$ B_{0} = \frac{E_{0}}{C} = \frac{5 \times 10^{-7}}{3 \times 10^{8}} = 1.67 \times 10^{-15} \, T $

E and B should be perpendicular to each other and also perpendicular to direction of propagation.

i.e., $ \hat{C} \times \hat{E} = \hat{B} $

$ \Rightarrow \quad \hat{B} = -\hat{i} \times \hat{j} \Rightarrow \hat{B} = -\hat{k} $

So, $ \vec{B} = 1.67 \times 10^{-15} \sin (0.25 \times 10^3 x + 2.5 \times 10^{11} t) (-\hat{k}) $', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.956Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4931, 4, NULL, NULL, 'Nowadays optical fibers are extensively used for transmitting audio and video signals through long distances. The optical fibers work on', NULL, '[{"text":"Double refraction","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Refraction","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Total internal reflection","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Reflection","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Optical fibers use the principle of total internal reflection for transmitting audio and video signals through long distances.', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.956Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4932, 4, NULL, NULL, 'Match List-I and List-II', NULL, '[{"text":"A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'The correct matching will be A-(II), B-(IV), C-(I), D-(III)

So, correct option is (d).', NULL, NULL, 2022, '/local-papers/assets/physics-2022-08-06/q20-table-1.jpg', 'Table for question 20', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.957Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/physics-2022-08-06/q20-table-1.jpg","alt":"Table for question 20","kind":"table"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4933, 4, NULL, NULL, 'A convex mirror of focal length F_{1} is placed at a distance d from a convex lens of focal length F_{2}. A beam of light rays coming from infinity falling on convex lens-convex mirror combination and returns black to infinity. Distance d is', NULL, '[{"text":"$ (F_{1}+F_{2}) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ (F_{1}-F_{2}) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ (F_{2}-2F_{1}) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ (F_{2}+2F_{1}) $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Case I:

Case II: Here, we will have $ d = F_{2} - F_{1} $. But as there is no such option like this, we will see another case.

Here, $d = F_{2} - 2F_{1}$ for ray to return back to infinity.', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.957Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4934, 4, NULL, NULL, 'The graph shows the relationship between object distance (u) and image distance (v) for an equiconvex lens. The focal length of the lens is', NULL, '[{"text":"0.5 cm","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"0.05 cm","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"5.00 cm","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"5.5 cm","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'By lens formula,

$$ \frac{1}{v}-\frac{1}{u}=\frac{1}{f} $$

From graph, when $u = -10$ cm, $v = +10$ cm

So, $ \frac{1}{10}-\frac{1}{-10}=\frac{1}{f} $

$ \Rightarrow $ $ \frac{2}{10}=\frac{1}{f} $ $ \Rightarrow f=5 $ cm', NULL, NULL, 2022, '/local-papers/assets/physics-2022-08-06/q22-figure-1.jpg', 'Figure for question 22', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.957Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/physics-2022-08-06/q22-figure-1.jpg","alt":"Figure for question 22","kind":"figure"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4935, 4, NULL, NULL, 'A short pulse of white light is incident from air to a glass slab at normal incidence, after travelling through the slab, the first colour to emerge is

(a) Blue (b) Green (c) Violet (d) Red

There are four light rays incident on a right angled prism. The refractive index of prism material for the rays are 1.43, 1.39, 1.38, 1.21 respectively. The ray that suffers total internal reflection is', NULL, '[{"text":"I","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"II","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"III","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"IV","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'We know that,

$$ V=f\quad\lambda\Longrightarrow V\propto\lambda\quad[\because f=\mathrm{c o n s t.}] $$

Thus, the first colour to emerge will be red, as largest wavelength will have fastest speed.', NULL, NULL, 2022, '/local-papers/assets/physics-2022-08-06/q23-figure-1.jpg', 'Figure for question 23', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.958Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/physics-2022-08-06/q23-figure-1.jpg","alt":"Figure for question 23","kind":"figure"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4936, 4, NULL, NULL, 'A hollow double concave lens is made of very thin transparent material. It can be filled with air or either of the two liquids $L_{1}$ or $L_{2}$ having refractive indices $n_{1}$ and $n_{2}$ respectively ($n_{2} > n_{1} > 1$). The lens will diverge a parallel beam of light if it is filled with', NULL, '[{"text":"Air inside and placed in air","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Air inside and immersed in $L_{1}$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$L_{1}$ inside and immersed in $L_{2}$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$L_{2}$ inside and immersed in $L_{1}$","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'By lensmaker formula,

$$ \frac{1}{f}=(^{g}\mu_{m}-1)\left(\frac{2}{R}\right)=\left(\frac{\mu_{g}}{\mu_{m}}-1\right)\left(\frac{2}{R}\right) $$

Clearly, if $ \mu_{m} \mu_{L_{1}}$) to diverge the parallel ray.', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.958Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4937, 4, NULL, NULL, 'A star is seen using a telescope whose objective lens has a diameter of 250 cm. The wavelength of light coming from the star is 500 nm. The limit of resolution of telescope is', NULL, '[{"text":"$ 1.2 \\times 10^{-7} $ radians","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ 2.4 \\times 10^{-7} $ radians","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ 1.5 \\times 10^{-7} $ radians","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ 3.9 \\times 10^{-7} $ radians Passage:(Q.No.27-31) Read the information given below to answer In Young''s double slit experiment as shown in figure, interference of light waves were observed on the screen. Thomas Young made two pinholes $ S_{1} $ and $ S_{2} $ (very close to each other) on an opaque screen in front of parent source S $ S_{1} $ and $ S_{2} $ behaved as coherent sources, and produced interference patten on the screen which has alternate bright fringe Parent source and dark fringes. This experiment proved Huygen''s wave theory of Light.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'For telescope, limit of resolution is given by

$ d\theta=\frac{1.22\lambda}{D} $ $ d\theta=\frac{1.22\times500\times10^{-9}}{2.5}=2.4\times10^{-7} $ radian', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.961Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4938, 4, NULL, NULL, 'In YDSE experiment position of bright fringes and da fringes is given by', NULL, '[{"text":"$ x_n = n \\frac{\\lambda D}{d} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ x_n = n \\frac{\\lambda d}{D} $ n = 0, ±1, ±2 _ $ x_n = \\left(n + \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\frac{\\lambda D}{d} $ n = 0, ±1, ±2 _","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ x_n = n \\lambda $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ x_n = n \\frac{\\lambda}{d} $ n = 0, ±1, ±2 _ $ x_n = \\left(n + \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\lambda $ n = 0, ±1, ±2 _ $$ \\mathbf{n}=\\pm1,\\pm2\\underline{\\quad\\quad} $$","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Linear position for $ n^{\text{th}} $ bright fringe is given

$ x_{n} = \frac{n \lambda D}{d}, n = 0, \pm 1, \pm 2, \ldots $

Linear position for $ n^{\text{th}} $ dark fringe is given by

$ x_{n} = \frac{\binom{n + 1}{2} \lambda D}{d}, n = 0, \pm 1, \pm 2, \ldots $', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.962Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4939, 4, NULL, NULL, 'Path difference between the waves meeting at point P is given by', NULL, '[{"text":"$ \\frac{xD}{d} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{xd}{D} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{2xD}{d} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{1xd}{2D} $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'We know that

$$ x=\frac{\Delta x D}{d}\Rightarrow\Delta x=\frac{x d}{D} $$', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.963Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4940, 4, NULL, NULL, 'In YDSE the distance between two consecutive bright fringes is given by 3 mm. What would be the fringe width if YDSE is performed in a medium of refractive index 1.2?', NULL, '[{"text":"3 mm","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"1.5 mm","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2.5 mm","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"0.5 mm","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'We have, fringe width ( $ \beta $) = $ \frac{\lambda D}{\mu d} $

In air, $ \beta_{air} = \frac{\lambda D}{d} $ In medium, $ \beta_{med} = \frac{\lambda D}{1.2d} $

So, $ \frac{\beta_{med}}{\beta_{air}} = \frac{1}{1.2} $

$ \Rightarrow \beta_{med} = \frac{\beta_{air}}{1.2} = \frac{3\ mm}{1.2} = 2.5\ mm $', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.963Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4941, 4, NULL, NULL, 'In YDSE as shown, the path difference $ SS_1 - SS_2 = \frac{\lambda}{3} $.

This causes the shift in position of central bright point. The new position of central bright point is', NULL, '[{"text":"$ -\\frac{\\lambda D}{d} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ -2 \\frac{\\lambda D}{d} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ -3 \\frac{\\lambda D}{d} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ -\\frac{1}{3} \\frac{\\lambda D}{d} $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Here, path difference = $ \Delta x + \frac{\lambda}{3} $, where $ \Delta x = \frac{xd}{D} $

For central maxima, path difference = 0

$$0 = \Delta x + \frac{\lambda}{3} \Rightarrow \Delta x = -\frac{\lambda}{3} \frac{xd}{D} = -\frac{\lambda}{3} \Rightarrow x = \frac{-\lambda D}{3d}$$

So, central maxima will shift by $ \left(\frac{\lambda D}{3d}\right) $ distance downward.', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.964Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4942, 4, NULL, NULL, 'If $\lambda_{e},\lambda_{p}$ and $\lambda_{\alpha}$, be the de-Broglie wavelength of electron, proton and A-particle, respectively, then', NULL, '[{"text":"$\\lambda_{p} < \\lambda_{\\alpha} < \\lambda_{e}$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$\\lambda_{e} < \\lambda_{\\alpha} < \\lambda_{p}$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$\\lambda_{e} < \\lambda_{p} < \\lambda_{\\alpha}$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$\\lambda_{\\alpha} < \\lambda_{p} < \\lambda_{e}$","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'We have

$$ \lambda=\frac{h}{\sqrt{2mq\Delta V}}\text{or},\lambda\propto\frac{1}{\sqrt{mq}} $$

$$ \left(\sqrt{mq}\right)_{e}=\sqrt{m_{e}e},\quad\left(\sqrt{mq}\right)_{p}=\sqrt{1836m_{e}e} $$

$$ (\sqrt{mq})_{\alpha}=\sqrt{4m_{p}\times2e} $$

$$ =\sqrt{4\times1836m_{e}\times2e} $$

$$ \sqrt{(mq)_{e}} \lambda_{p}>\lambda_{\alpha} $$', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.965Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4943, 4, NULL, NULL, 'An electron is accelerated to potential V. If mass of electron is m = 9.1 \times 10^{-31} kg its charge is e = 1.6 \times 10^{-19} C, then debroglie wavelength of electron is', NULL, '[{"text":"$ \\lambda = \\frac{125}{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{V}}}\\mathrm{nm} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\lambda = \\frac{1.227}{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{V}}}\\mathrm{nm} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\lambda=\\frac{\\text{eh}}{\\sqrt{2mV}}\\text{nm} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\lambda=\\frac{3.6}{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{V}}}\\text{nm} $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'We have,

$$ \begin{aligned}&\lambda=\frac{h}{\sqrt{2mqV}}=\frac{6.63\times10^{-34}}{\sqrt{2\times9.1\times10^{-31}\times1.6\times10^{-19}\times V}}\\ &=\frac{1.227}{\sqrt{V}}\times10^{-9}m=\frac{1.227}{\sqrt{V}}nm\\ \end{aligned} $$', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.965Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4944, 4, NULL, NULL, 'Keeping the frequency of the incident radiation and the accelerating potential fixed, the intensity of light is varied and the resulting photoelectric current is measured each time by the graph', NULL, '[{"text":"","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Photocurrent increases linearly with increase in intensity because

$$ I\uparrow\uparrow\Rightarrow\frac{\mathrm{no.~of~photons}}{\mathrm{time}}\uparrow\uparrow\Rightarrow\frac{\mathrm{no.~of~photoelectrons}}{\mathrm{time}}\uparrow\uparrow $$

Photocurrent $ \uparrow\uparrow $', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.966Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/physics-2022-08-06/q34-options.jpg","alt":"Printed answer choices for question 34","kind":"options"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4945, 4, NULL, NULL, 'The study of hydrogen atom spectrum has mainly five series. Choose the correct sequence of these series in increasing order of their shortest wavelength.

A. Lyman Series

B. Pfund Series

C. Paschen Series

D. Bracket Series

E. Balmer Series', NULL, '[{"text":"B, A, D, C, E","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"B, C, D, E, A","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A, E, C, D, B","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A, C, E, B, D","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'In hydrogen atoms, spectral series corresponds to

(a) Lyman Series _ Ultraviolet

(b) Balmer Series _ Visible

(c) Paschen Series _ Infra-red

(d) Brackett Series _ Infra-red

(e) Pfund Series _ For Infra-red', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.967Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4946, 4, NULL, NULL, 'The Bohr model for the spectra of a H-atom

A. Will not be applicable: to hydrogen in the molecular form

B. Will not be applicable as it is for a He-atom

C. Is valid only at room temperature

D. Predicts continuous as well as discrete spectral lines', NULL, '[{"text":"A, B only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A, B, C only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A, B, D only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"B, C, D only","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'The Bohr model of atom is applicable to $H^{n}$ or $H^{n}$ like atoms. So, (a) and (b) are correct.

As it is valid at nearly all temperature.

So, (c) is incorrect.

It cannot predict continuous spectral line.

So, (d) is incorrect.', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.967Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4947, 4, NULL, NULL, 'Two nuclei have mass number in the ratio 1:27, then the ratio of their radii and densities should be respectively', NULL, '[{"text":"1:1 and 1:3","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"1:9 and 1:1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"1:3 and 1:1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"1:1 and 1:9 Passage:(Q.No.38-41) Read the information given below to answer In $\\alpha$-decay, the mass number of daughter nucleus is four less than that of decaying nucleus (parent nucleus). While atomic number decreases by two. In general, $\\alpha$-decay of parent nucleus $+2^{He}$ $ _{Z}^{A}X $ results in daughter nucleus $ _{Z-2}^{A-4}Y $ $$ \\mathrm{^{A}_{Z}X}\\xrightarrow{\\quad}\\mathrm{^{A-4}_{Z-2}Y}+2^{\\mathrm{He}^{4}} $$ From Einstein''s mass energy equivalence relation and energy conservation, it is clear that this spontaneous decay is possible only when mass of decay products is less than the mass of the products. By referring the table of nuclear masses, one can check that the total mass of daughter nucleus and $ \\alpha $-particle is indeed less than that of parent nucleus. The disintegration energy or the Q value can be calculated by formula $ Q=\\Delta m.c^{2} $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Radius of nuclei is given as

$$ R=R_{0}A^{1/3} $$

$$ \frac{R_{1}}{R_{2}}=\left(\frac{A_{1}}{A_{2}}\right)^{1/3}\Rightarrow\frac{R_{1}}{R_{2}}=\left(\frac{1}{27}\right)^{1/3}=\frac{1}{3} $$

and, nuclear density is independent of mass number.

So, $ \frac{\rho_{1}}{\rho_{2}}=\frac{1}{1} $', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.968Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4948, 4, NULL, NULL, 'In $\alpha$-decay, the mass number of the daughter nucleus is', NULL, '[{"text":"One more than that of parent nucleus","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"One less than that of parent nucleus","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Two more than that of parent nucleus","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Four less than that of parent nucleus","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'We have,

$$ \mathrm{^{A}_{Z}X}\xrightarrow{\quad}\mathrm{^{A-4}_{Z-2}Y}+\mathrm{_{2}He^{4}} $$

So, mass of daughter nucleus is less than parent nucleus.', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.968Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4949, 4, NULL, NULL, '$ A \xrightarrow{\alpha} B \xrightarrow{\beta} C \xrightarrow{\beta} D $

In the process $ A \rightarrow B $, $ \alpha $-particle is emitted by nucleus and in process $ B \rightarrow C $ and $ C \rightarrow D $ each, one- $ \beta $-particle is emitted.', NULL, '[{"text":"A, B, C are Isobars","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"B, C, D are Isobars","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A, B, C are Isobars","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A, B, C, D all are Isotopes","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'For β-decay only atomic number changes whereas mass number remains same. So, B, C, D are isobars.', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.968Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4950, 4, NULL, NULL, 'α-particle is:', NULL, '[{"text":"Nucleus of Hydrogen atom","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Hydrogen atom","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Nucleus of Helium atom","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Helium atom","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '''α'' particle is basically doubly positive charge helium ion. So, it is simply a helium nuclei.', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.969Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4951, 4, NULL, NULL, 'During the $\alpha$-decay process, the mass of all decay product is', NULL, '[{"text":"Equal to the mass of initial nucleus","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Less than the mass of initial nucleus","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Equal to the mass of $\\alpha$-particle","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Greater than the mass of initial nucleus","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'This decay is spontaneous and spontaneous decay is possible only when mass of decay product is less than the mass of the products.', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.969Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4952, 4, NULL, NULL, 'An α-decay process is given by

$ \ce{^A_{Z}X ->^{A-4}_{Z-2} Y + ^4_{Z-2}He} $

If mass of nuclei X, Y and He are $ m_x $, $ m_y $ and $ m_{He} $ respectively, then Q-value is given by', NULL, '[{"text":"$ (m_x - m_y - m_{He})c^2 $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ (m_y + m_{He} - m_x)c^2 $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ (m_x + m_y - m_{He})c^2 $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ (m_x - m_y + m_{He})c^2 $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '$ Q_{\text{Value}} = \Delta m c^2 $

$ \Rightarrow Q = (m_X - m_Y - m_{\mathrm{He}})c^- $

For $ ^{A}_{Z}X \longrightarrow ^{A-4}_{Z-2}Y + ^{4}_{2}He $', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.970Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4953, 4, NULL, NULL, 'In the circuit diagram $D_{1}, D_{2}, D_{3}$ and $D_{4}$ are diodes. Choose the correct answer from the options given below', NULL, '[{"text":"$ D_{1} $ and $ D_{2} $ are forward biased","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ D_{1} $ and $ D_{3} $ are forward biased","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ D_{2} $ and $ D_{4} $ are forward biased","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"All diodes are forward biased","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'For forward biasing, +ve terminal of battery is connected to p-side and -ve terminals of battery is connected to n-side.

So, $D_{1}$ and $D_{3}$ must be forward biased.

Hence, wave form of output Y is', NULL, NULL, 2022, '/local-papers/assets/physics-2022-08-06/q43-figure-1.jpg', 'Figure for question 43', NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.970Z', NULL, NULL, '[{"url":"/local-papers/assets/physics-2022-08-06/q43-figure-1.jpg","alt":"Figure for question 43","kind":"figure"}]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4954, 4, NULL, NULL, 'Three amplifiers are connected in series, (cascaded). The first amplifier has voltage gain of 5, second has voltage gain of 10 and $ 3^{rd} $ has voltage gain of 20. If the Input signal is 0.1 V, then the final output of AC signal will be', NULL, '[{"text":"5 V","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"25 V","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"100 V","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"50 V","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'If three amplifier are cascaded in series. Then,

$$ \mathrm{(V o l t a g e g a i n)_{n e t}}=(A_{V})_{n e t}=A_{v_{1}}\cdot A_{v_{2}}\cdot A_{v_{3}} $$

$$ =5\times10\times20=1000 $$

$$ =(A_{V})_{\mathrm{net}}\times\mathrm{Input} $$

$$ =1000\times0.1=100V $$', NULL, NULL, 2022, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:28.970Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (4955, 4, NULL, NULL, 'Ratio of free electron density to hole density of a p-type semiconductor is', NULL, '[{"text":"Equal to one","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Less than one","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Greater than one","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Can be less than or greater than one depending upon semiconductor material.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'In p-type semiconductor,

