##### It struck me', NULL, '[{"text":"To hit as a kick","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"To attack suddenly","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"To come to mind suddenly","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"To take down or dismantle","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.431Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'none', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5468, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the meaning of the phrase ‘kith and kin’', NULL, '[{"text":"Friends","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Relatives","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Parents","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Students","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.432Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'none', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5469, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Use the correct form of the word "RESOLVE" to fill in the blank: Did you make any New Year''s ___ this year?', NULL, '[{"text":"resolve","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"resolving","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"resolvation","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"resolution","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests the ability to use the correct "part of speech." While "resolve" is a verb, the sentence requires a noun to follow the adjective "New Year''s."

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Resolve (Verb): To settle or find a solution.

• Resolution (Noun): A firm decision to do or not to do something.

• Resolvation: This is not a standard English word.

In the context of New Year''s goals, the standard term is "New Year''s resolution."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct form is "resolution."

Quick Tip: To distinguish between the verb and noun: "I resolve (verb) to exercise more." "My resolution (noun) is to exercise more."', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.434Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5470, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the appropriate word for the meaning of the underlined word: Ravi came up on the stage and started speaking impromptu.', NULL, '[{"text":"Quickly","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Unrehearsed","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Incoherently","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Impressively","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The question asks for the definition of the word "impromptu" within the context of public speaking.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The word "impromptu" describes an act (like a speech or musical performance) done without being planned, organized, or practiced beforehand.

• Unrehearsed: This is a perfect synonym.

- Incoherently: This means confused or unclear; while an impromptu speech can be incoherent, the word itself doesn''t mean that.

• Impressively: This refers to the quality of the result, not the lack of preparation.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The meaning of impromptu is "unrehearsed."

Quick Tip: "Impromptu" comes from the Latin phrase in promptu, meaning "in readiness." It implies that the speaker was ready to speak even without a script!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.434Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5471, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Rearrange the following phrases into a meaningful sentence: One common mistake that ___.

(A) thinking that it

(B) would be a waste of time

(C) to work together

(D) many people have made is', NULL, '[{"text":"(D),(A),(C),(B)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B),(C),(A),(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C)，(A)，(B)，(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C)，(B)，(D)，(A)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '##### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Sentence rearrangement requires identifying the subject, the verb, and the logical flow of information.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. The introductory phrase is "One common mistake that..." 2. This needs to be followed by a phrase describing who made the mistake: (D) "many people have made is" 3. Now we need to define the mistake: (A) "thinking that it" 4. This leads to the action within the thought: (C) "to work together" 5. Finally, the conclusion of the thought: (B) "would be a waste of time."

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The complete sentence is: "One common mistake that many people have made is thinking that

it to work together would be a waste of time." (Note: Logically, the correct sequence for a standard sentence would be D-A-C-B).

Step 4: Final Answer:

The correct order is (D), (A), (C), (B).

Quick Tip: When rearranging, look for "hinge" words. "Is" (in D) usually precedes "thinking" (in A), and "it" (in A) needs to refer to something, like the act of "working together" (in C).', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.435Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5472, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Fill in the blank with the correct word from the options given below to make a meaningful sentence:- Ten tourists were ___ injured in the accident and a few of them succumbed to their injuries on the way to the hospital.', NULL, '[{"text":"spurious","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"gravely","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"fatefully","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"vitally","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question requires choosing an adverb that correctly describes the severity of physical injuries in a tragic context.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Gravely: This means "in a serious or solemn manner" or "to a degree that causes great alarm." In medical or accident contexts, "gravely injured" is the standard colocation for life-threatening harm.

• Spuriously: Means falsely or fake (e.g., a spurious claim).

• Fatefully: Suggests something having far-reaching and typically disastrous consequences.

but it describes the event rather than the physical state of the person.

• Vitally: Means essentially or energetically; it doesn''t fit the context of an injury.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct word is "gravely."

Quick Tip: The clue is "succumbed to their injuries." This tells you the injuries were extremely serious. "Gravely" and "Seriously" are the most common words used in news reports for such situations.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.436Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5473, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Rearrange the following phrases into a meaningful sentence: The need for a scientific understanding ___.

(A) the Rio Earth Summit, the first of its kind

(B) involving world leaders on environment and development

(C) of natural ecosystems including forests was the main objective of

(D) of phenomena like climate change, biodiversity, and conservation', NULL, '[{"text":"(C)，(D)，(A)，(B)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B)，(C)，(D)，(A)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(D)，(C)，(A)，(B)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(D)，(B)，(A)，(C)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '##### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To solve this, we must link the preposition "of" following "understanding" to its objects, and then find the verb that completes the main clause.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. The subject starts with: "The need for a scientific understanding..." 2. Understanding of what? (D) "...of phenomena like climate change, biodiversity, and conservation" 3. Continuing the object list: (C) "...of natural ecosystems including forests was the main objective of" 4. Objective of what event? (A) "...the Rio Earth Summit, the first of its kind" 5. Describing the

summit: (B) "...involving world leaders on environment and development."

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The full sentence is: "The need for a scientific understanding of phenomena like climate change, biodiversity, and conservation of natural ecosystems including forests was the main objective of the Rio Earth Summit, the first of its kind involving world leaders on environment and development." The correct sequence is (D), (C), (A), (B).

Quick Tip: Always look for prepositions. "Understanding" is almost always followed by "of." Since both (D) and (C) start with "of," check which one flows better from the word "understanding."', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.436Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5474, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Fill in the blank with the correct word from the options given below to make a meaningful sentence:- In my haste to stop the boiling milk from overflowing, I accidentally ___ my fingers.', NULL, '[{"text":"sizzled","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"scorched","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"scalded","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"sunburnt","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

English has specific words for burns caused by different sources (dry heat vs. wet heat vs. radiation).

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Scalded: This is the specific term for a burn caused by hot liquids (like milk, water) or steam.

- Scorched: Usually refers to burning the surface of something (like cloth or skin) with dry heat or a flame, often resulting in discoloration.

• Sizzled: This is the sound of something frying; it is not typically used as a verb for a person getting injured.

• Sunburnt: Specifically refers to a burn from UV radiation (the sun).

Step 3: Final Answer:

Since the injury was caused by boiling milk, the correct word is "scaledd."

Quick Tip: Remember: Wet = Scald. Dry = Scorch or Burn. If it involves tea, soup, or steam, you''ve been scalded!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.437Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5475, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Fill in the blank with the correct option: - The two friends appear to have fallen _____ over a minor issue.', NULL, '[{"text":"down","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"out","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"into","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"by","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests your knowledge of phrasal verbs. A phrasal verb is a verb combined with a preposition or adverb that creates a meaning different from the original verb.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The verb "fall" changes meaning significantly based on the preposition used:

• Fall out: To have an argument or a disagreement that leads to a break in a friendship.

• Fall down: To drop to the ground.

Fall into: To become involved in something accidentally or to start doing something without intending to.

Fall by: Usually used in the phrase "fall by the wayside," meaning to fail or be forgotten.

Since the context involves friends and a "minor issue," "fall out" is the only logical choice.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct phrasal verb is "fallen out."

Quick Tip: To remember "fall out," imagine two friends standing inside a circle of friendship. When they argue, one "falls out" of the circle!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.437Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5476, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Fill in the blank with the correct word from the options given below to make a meaningful sentence: - His salary is in ___ with the amount of work he does.', NULL, '[{"text":"accord","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"coordination","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"balance","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"keeping","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question focuses on "collocations"—words that are naturally used together in English to sound correct to a native speaker.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The phrase "in keeping with" is a standard English idiom meaning "in agreement with" or "consistent with."

• In keeping with: Used to say that something is appropriate for a specific situation.

• Accord: Usually used as "in accord with," but "in keeping with" is the much more common phrase when discussing salaries and work expectations.

• Coordination/Balance: While these words deal with equality, they do not fit the idiomatic structure of the sentence.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct phrase is "in keeping with."

Quick Tip: If you are struggling between "accord" and "keeping," look at the context. We use "keeping" when something matches a standard or a level (like a salary matching effort).', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.437Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5477, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Fill in the blank with the correct word from the options given below to make a meaningful sentence:- Sara was so ___ that she did not take much time to make up her mind.', NULL, '[{"text":"impervious","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"impetuous","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"inarticulate","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"inerrant","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests vocabulary related to personality traits, specifically those involving decision-making.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The sentence mentions that Sara "did not take much time to make up her mind," which suggests a quick, perhaps impulsive, nature.

- Impetuous: Acting or done quickly and without thought or care. This perfectly matches the context.

• Impervious: Unable to be affected by something (e.g., impervious to rain).

• Inarticulate: Unable to express oneself clearly.

• Inerrant: Incapable of being wrong or making mistakes.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct word is "impetuous."

Quick Tip: Think of "Impetuous" as "Impulsive." Both start with "Imp-" and describe someone who jumps into action without thinking it through!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.438Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5478, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct meaning of the word “Taciturn”.', NULL, '[{"text":"Very talkative","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Reserved or silent","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Extremely angry","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Careless in behaviour","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This is a vocabulary question focused on character traits. Understanding root words can often help identify the meaning of such terms.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The word "Taciturn" comes from the Latin word tacitus, meaning "silent."

• A taciturn person is someone who says very little and seems unsociable or reserved.

• It is the opposite of "Loquacious" or "Talkative" (Option A).

• It does not necessarily imply anger or carelessness; it simply refers to a lack of speech.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct meaning is "Reserved or silent."

Quick Tip: Associate Taciturn with the word Tacit (implied without being spoken). Both relate to a lack of overt verbal communication!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.439Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5479, 1, NULL, NULL, 'The word "Sporadic" means: ___.', NULL, '[{"text":"Continuous","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Regular","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Occurring at irregular intervals","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Permanent ___","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This word is used to describe the frequency or pattern of an occurrence.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

"Sporadic" refers to things that happen only occasionally or in a scattered, random manner.

• If rain is sporadic, it stops and starts without a predictable pattern.

• It is the antonym of words like "Continuous" (Option A) and "Regular" (Option B).

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct meaning is "Occurring at irregular intervals."

Quick Tip: Think of the word "Spore." Just as spores are scattered randomly by the wind, sporadic events are scattered randomly in time.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.440Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5480, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Rearrange the following sentences to form a meaningful paragraph:

A. Finally, the results were announced.

B. The students entered the examination hall.

C. They started writing their answers carefully.

D. The invigilator distributed the question papers.', NULL, '[{"text":"BADC","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"BDCA","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"BDAC","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"BACD","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Rearranging sentences (Parajumbles) requires finding a logical timeline or a cause-and-effect relationship between the statements.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. The process begins with the students arriving: (B) "The students entered the examination hall." 2. Once they are inside, the next logical step is getting the material: (D) "The invigilator distributed the question papers." 3. After receiving the papers, the students perform the task: (C) "They started writing their answers carefully." 4. The word "Finally" indicates the conclusion of the entire process: (A) "Finally, the results were announced."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The logical sequence is BDCA.

Quick Tip: Keywords like "Finally," "In conclusion," or "Lastly" are strong indicators of the final sentence in a sequence.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.440Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5481, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Rearrange the following words to make a meaningful sentence:

A. always

B. honesty

C. the best policy

D. is', NULL, '[{"text":"BADC","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"BDAC","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"BACD","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"BCDA","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests the ability to reconstruct a well-known English proverb by identifying the Subject, Verb, and Complement.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. The Subject of the sentence is (B) "Honesty." 2. This is followed by the Verb: (D) "is." 3. To emphasize the frequency or nature, the adverb is placed next: (A) "always." 4. The sentence concludes with the Complement: (C) "the best policy."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The sentence is "Honesty is always the best policy," which corresponds to BDAC.

Quick Tip: In simple English sentences, the structure is usually S-V-O (Subject + Verb + Object/Complement). Identify the "actor" (Honesty) and the "action" (is) first!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.440Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5482, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Rearrange the following sentences correctly:

A. The rain stopped after an hour.

B. Dark clouds gathered in the sky.

C. Children started playing outside again.

D. Soon, it began to rain heavily.', NULL, '[{"text":"BADC","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"BDAC","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"BACD","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"DCBA","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

A meaningful paragraph usually follows a chronological order of events—in this case, the cycle of a rainstorm.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. First, the weather changes: (B) "Dark clouds gathered in the sky." 2. The gathering clouds lead to the action: (D) "Soon, it began to rain heavily." 3. Next, the event concludes: (A) "The rain stopped after an hour." 4. Finally, the aftermath: (C) "Children started playing outside again." (The word "again" implies they had stopped during the rain).

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct chronological order is BDAC.

Quick Tip: Look for transition words. "Soon" (in D) usually follows an initial observation (the clouds in B), and "again" (in C) implies a return to a previous state after an interruption (the rain).', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.441Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5483, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the poetic device used in the line: “The wind whispered through the trees.”', NULL, '[{"text":"Simile","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Metaphor","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Personification","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Hyperbole","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Poetic devices (or figures of speech) are tools used by writers to create rhythm, enhance meaning, or help the reader visualize a scene.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Personification is a figure of speech in which human qualities are given to animals, objects, or ideas.

• In the line "The wind whispered," the act of "whispering" is a human action.

By attributing this human behavior to the wind (a non-human element), the author uses personification to make the description more vivid and evocative.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The poetic device used is Personification.

Quick Tip: To spot personification, ask yourself: "Is an object doing something only a human can do?" If the answer is yes (like a dancing flower or a weeping willow), it''s personification!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.441Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5484, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Which poetic device is used in the phrase: “Time is a thief.”', NULL, '[{"text":"Alliteration","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Metaphor","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Oxymoron","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Irony","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Comparison devices like similes and metaphors are used to link two unrelated things to highlight a shared characteristic.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

A Metaphor is a direct comparison between two unlike things without using the words "like" or "as."

• In "Time is a thief," Time is being directly equated to a thief to suggest that it "steals" moments of our lives away.

• If the line were "Time is like a thief," it would be a simile.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The phrase uses a Metaphor.

Quick Tip: Metaphor = A is B (Direct).

Simile = A is like B (Indirect).

Think of a metaphor as a "stronger" comparison because it says one thing is the other.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.442Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5485, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the poetic device in the line: “She sells seashells on the seashore.”', NULL, '[{"text":"Assonance","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Simile","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Alliteration","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Repetition","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'The correct option is (C).', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.443Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5486, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Match the following phobias correctly:

A. Xenophobia — P. Fear of strangers/foreigners

B. Gerascophobia — Q. Fear of growing old

C. Necrophobia — R. Fear of dead bodies/death', NULL, '[{"text":"A-P, B-Q, C-R","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A-Q, B-P, C-R","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A-R, B-Q, C-P","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A-P, B-R, C-Q","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Phobias are intense, irrational fears of specific objects or situations. Many phobia names are derived from Greek or Latin roots that describe the source of the fear.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

By breaking down the root words, we can identify the meanings:

• Xenophobia: Derived from xenos (stranger/guest). It refers to the fear or hatred of that which is perceived to be foreign or strange. → Matches P.

• Gerascophobia: Derived from geras (old age). It is a clinical phobia involving an abnormal or persistent fear of growing old. → Matches Q.

• Necrophobia: Derived from nekros (dead body). It is the fear of death or dead things.

→ Matches R.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct matching sequence is A-P, B-Q, and C-R.

Quick Tip: To remember Necro, think of "Necropolis" (city of the dead). For Xeno, think of "Xenon" (a gas named for being ''strange''). For Geras, think of "Geriatrics" (the branch of medicine dealing with the elderly).', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.444Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5487, 1, NULL, NULL, 'He is very fond  $ \underline{of} $ music. The underlined word is:', NULL, '[{"text":"Conjunction","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Preposition","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Adverb","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Interjection","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

A "Preposition" is a word used to link nouns, pronouns, or phrases to other words within a sentence. They typically show relationships of time, place, or direction.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The word "of" is used here to connect the adjective "fond" with its object "music."

Prepositions act as connectors that show the relationship between the subject and the object.

• "Fond" is a specific adjective that is almost always followed by the fixed preposition "of."

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The underlined word is a Preposition.

Quick Tip: Common prepositions include: in, on, at, by, with, and of. They are usually short words that "position" the relationship between different parts of the sentence.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.446Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5488, 1, NULL, NULL, '$ \underline{\text{Wow}} $! That was an amazing performance. The underlined word is:', NULL, '[{"text":"Interjection","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Adjective","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Adverb","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Conjunction","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

An "Interjection" is a word or phrase used to express strong feeling or sudden emotion. They are grammatically independent from the rest of the sentence.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The word "Wow" is used to express excitement or surprise.

• It is often followed by an exclamation mark (!).

• It does not modify a noun (like an adjective) or a verb (like an adverb); it stands alone as an emotional outburst.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The underlined word is an Interjection.

Quick Tip: Interjections are the "emotional labels" of a sentence. Examples include: Ouch!, Oh!, Alas!, and Hurray!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.447Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5489, 1, NULL, NULL, 'She is  $ \underline{\text{smarter}} $ than her brother. The underlined word is:', NULL, '[{"text":"Adverb","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Noun","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Adjective","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Verb","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

An "Adjective" is a word that describes or modifies a noun or pronoun. Adjectives can have different degrees: positive, comparative, and superlative.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

In this sentence, "smarter" is used to describe the pronoun "She."

• Specifically, it is the comparative degree of the adjective "smart."

• It is used here to compare two people (the sister and the brother).

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The underlined word is an Adjective.

Quick Tip: If a word ends in "-er" and is used with "than" to compare two things, it is almost always a comparative adjective.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.447Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5490, 1, NULL, NULL, 'By the time we reached the station, the train ___.', NULL, '[{"text":"left","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"has left","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"had left","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"leaves","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests the use of the Past Perfect Tense. We use this tense to show that one action in the past happened before another action in the past.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

There are two past actions here: 1. We reached the station. 2. The train left. The phrase "By the time" indicates that the train''s departure happened first. To describe the earlier of two past actions, we use "had + past participle" (had left).

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct form is "had left."

Quick Tip: Think of the Past Perfect as the "Past of the Past." If you are already talking about the past (reached), and something happened even earlier, use "had."', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.447Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5491, 1, NULL, NULL, 'She ___ in Delhi since 2015.', NULL, '[{"text":"lives","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"lived","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"is living","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"has been living","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question focuses on the Present Perfect Continuous Tense. This tense is used to describe an action that started in the past and is still continuing in the present.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The keyword in this sentence is "since 2015."

• Whenever we see "since" or "for" followed by a time duration, it usually indicates that the action has been ongoing.

• The structure for this tense is: Subject + has/have + been + Verb(ing).

• "Is living" (Option C) describes what is happening right now but doesn''t account for the long duration starting in 2015.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct phrase is "has been living."

Quick Tip: "Since" refers to a specific point in time (since 2015), while "for" refers to a duration of time (for 11 years). Both usually trigger the Present Perfect Continuous tense!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.448Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5492, 1, NULL, NULL, 'If I ___ you, I would apologize.', NULL, '[{"text":"am","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"was","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"were","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"had been","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'This is an example of a Second Conditional sentence, which deals with hypothetical or imaginary situations that are contrary to current facts.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

In formal English, when using the "if" clause to express a hypothetical situation (especially with the verb "to be"), we use "were" regardless of the subject (I, he, she, it).

This is known as the Subjunctive Mood.

Even though we usually say "I was," in a hypothetical "If" statement, "If I were" is the grammatically correct choice.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct word is "were."

Quick Tip: Look for the word "would" in the second part of the sentence. The combination of "If... were" and "would" is the classic structure for imaginary advice!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.449Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5493, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Look! The children _____ in the rain.', NULL, '[{"text":"play","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"played","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"are playing","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"have played","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests the Present Continuous Tense, used for actions happening exactly at the time of speaking.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The word "Look!" is a command that draws attention to an action happening right now.

• For ongoing actions in the present, we use the is/am/are + Verb(ing) structure.

• Since "children" is a plural noun, we use "are playing."

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct tense is "are playing."

Quick Tip: Exclamations like "Look!", "Listen!", or "Be quiet!" are almost always clues that the next verb should be in the Present Continuous tense.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.449Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5494, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Rearrange the following parts of a sentence to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) can be seen as an extension of citizenship, starting with

(B) the Factory Acts of the 1840s which protected

(C) the growing importance of social rights for children

(D) children from unrestricted exploitation at work', NULL, '[{"text":"(A),(B),(C),(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A), (C), (B), (D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B), (A), (D), (C)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C),(A),(B),(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Sentence rearrangement requires identifying the subject (who/what the sentence is about) and then following the logical flow of the argument.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. We need a subject to start. Part (C) "the growing importance of social rights for children" introduces the main topic. 2. Next, we need a verb or an explanation of what that importance does. Part (A) "can be seen as an extension of citizenship, starting with" follows (C) perfectly. 3. Part (A) ends with "starting with," so we need a specific example. Part (B) "the Factory Acts of the 1840s which protected" provides that example. 4. Part (B) ends with "protected," which requires an object. Part (D) "children from unrestricted exploitation at work" completes the thought.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct sequence is (C)  $ \rightarrow $ (A)  $ \rightarrow $ (B)  $ \rightarrow $ (D).

Quick Tip: Check the links between parts: "starting with" (in A) naturally leads to a specific historical event (the Factory Acts in B). Identifying these "hooks" makes rearrangement much faster!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.450Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5495, 1, NULL, NULL, 'A person who eats too much is a/an ___.', NULL, '[{"text":"glutton","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"epicure","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"gourmand","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"pagan","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This is a "One Word Substitution" question, which tests your vocabulary regarding specific human behaviors and personality traits.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Glutton: A person who eats or drinks excessively (often implying greed).

• Epicure: A person who takes particular pleasure in fine food and drink (a connoisseur).

• Gourmand: Someone who enjoys eating and eats large amounts, but usually with a

more positive connotation of appreciation than a glutton.

Pagan: A person holding religious beliefs other than those of the main world religions.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct term for eating too much is a glutton.

Quick Tip: To remember the difference: An **Epicure** cares about *quality*, while a **Glutton** cares only about *quantity*.

(A) catapulted', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.452Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5496, 1, NULL, NULL, 'The unprecedented display of remarkable innovation and creativity ___ him into an enviable position.', NULL, '[{"text":"catapulted","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"capsized","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"condemned","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"commemorated","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This "Fill in the Blank" question tests your ability to choose a verb that logically fits the context of "sudden success" or "rapid movement" to a higher status.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Catapulted: To hurl or launch something (metaphorically, to move someone suddenly into a new situation or status).

• Capsized: To overturn in the water (used for boats).

• Condemned: To express complete disapproval of; typically in a legal or moral sense.

• Commemorated: To recall and show respect for (someone or something) in a ceremony.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

"Catapulted" is the only word that fits the positive, high-energy movement described in the sentence.

Quick Tip: Think of a physical **catapult**—it launches things forward with great force. In business or career contexts, "catapulted" is a common way to describe a "big break" or rapid promotion.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.452Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5497, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the word closest in meaning to the underlined word: What are you trying to  $ \underline{\text{insinuate}} $ through this message?', NULL, '[{"text":"Express","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Affirm","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Allude","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Propagate","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This is a synonym-based question. "Insinuate" is a verb often used to describe indirect communication.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Insinuate: To suggest or hint at something bad or unpleasant in an indirect and unpleasant way.

• Express: To state something directly and clearly.

• Affirm: To state as a fact; assert strongly and publicly.

• Allude: To suggest or call attention to something indirectly; to hint at.

• Propagate: To spread and promote (an idea, theory, etc.) widely.

Step 3: Final Answer:

"Allude" is the closest synonym as both words involve indirect suggestion rather than direct statement.

Quick Tip: An "insinuation" is usually a "sneaky" hint. If someone says "I''m not saying you''re late, but the meeting started an hour ago," they are **insinuating** that you are late.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.453Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5498, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Rearrange the given parts to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) while their white coats provide camouflage from

(B) predators and help them silently approach prey

(C) stay warm with thick fur, insulated feet, and fluffy tails.

(D) arctic foxes, adapted to the harsh Arctic winter,', NULL, '[{"text":"(A),(D),(C),(B)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(D),(A),(C),(B)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(D), (C), (A), (B)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C)，(B)，(D)，(A)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To rearrange a sentence, identify the subject first, followed by the actions (verbs) and the results or supporting details.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. **Part (D)*** is the best starting point as it introduces the subject: "arctic foxes, adapted to the harsh Arctic winter," 2. **Part (C)*** follows logically by describing how they adapt: "stay warm with thick fur, insulated feet, and fluffy tails," 3. **Part (A)*** introduces an additional feature using a conjunction: "while their white coats provide camouflage from", 4. **Part (B)*** completes the thought by explaining what they are camouflaged from and why: "predators and help them silently approach prey".

Step 3: Final Answer:

The logical sequence is (D)  $ \rightarrow $ (C)  $ \rightarrow $ (A)  $ \rightarrow $ (B).

Quick Tip: Look for commas as clues. The comma at the end of (D) and (C) suggests they are part of a list or introductory clause that must lead into the next thought.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.454Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5499, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Select the word closest in meaning to the word: Petulant', NULL, '[{"text":"Placid","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Patient","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Irritable","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Insignificant","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This is a vocabulary question focused on synonyms. Understanding the "tone" of a word (positive, negative, or neutral) can help eliminate wrong choices.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Petulant: Definition: (of a person or their manner) childishly sulky or bad-tempered.

• Placid: Calm and peaceful (Antonym).

• Patient: Able to accept delays or trouble without getting angry (Antonym).

• Irritable: Having or showing a tendency to be easily annoyed or made angry (Synonym).

• Insignificant: Too small or unimportant to be worth consideration (Unrelated).

Step 3: Final Answer:

The closest meaning to "Petulant" is "Irritable."

Quick Tip: "Petulant" is often used to describe someone acting like a spoiled child. Think of a "pet" that is "annoyed"—it helps link the sound to the meaning of being irritable.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.454Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5500, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Once you ___ into the routine, you will find the work is quite easy.', NULL, '[{"text":"are settled","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"had settled","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"settled","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"will settle","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question deals with conditional time clauses. In English, we do not use the future tense ("will") in a clause starting with "once," "when," or "if" when the main clause already uses the future tense.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The sentence structure is: Once + [Present Tense], [Future Tense].

• The main clause is "you will find..." (Future).

• The "once" clause needs a present form. "Are settled" acts as a present state.

• "Will settle" (Option D) is incorrect because we avoid double "will" in these structures.

- "Settled" (Option C) and "had settled" (Option B) are past tenses and do not fit a future prediction.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct choice is "are settled."

Quick Tip: Remember the "No Future in Time Clauses" rule. Even if you are talking about the future, words like *if, when, before, after, as soon as,* and *once* are followed by the **Present Tense**', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.455Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5501, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Rearrange the following parts to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) surviving written literature from ancient Mesopotamia because of

(B) through the ages, including the Iliad and the Odyssey

(C) the Epic of Gilgamesh is often cited as the earliest

(D) its significantly enduring impact on ancient literary works', NULL, '[{"text":"(C),(A),(D),(B)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B)，(D)，(A)，(C)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C)，(A)，(B)，(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B)，(C)，(A)，(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Sentence rearrangement requires linking modifiers to the nouns they describe and identifying the logical cause-and-effect or chronological flow.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. **Part (C)** acts as the subject and main claim: "the Epic of Gilgamesh is often cited as the earliest". 2. **Part (A)** completes the noun phrase started in (C): "surviving written literature from ancient Mesopotamia because of". 3. **Part (D)** explains the reason behind the fame/citation: "its significantly enduring impact on ancient literary works". 4. **Part (B)** provides specific examples of those "literary works": through the ages, including the Iliad and the Odyssey".

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct sequence is (C)  $ \rightarrow $ (A)  $ \rightarrow $ (D)  $ \rightarrow $ (B).

Quick Tip: Look for the link between (D) and (B). Part (D) ends with "literary works," and Part (B) starts with examples of famous literary works (Iliad and Odyssey). This pair (D-B) must stay together.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.456Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5502, 1, NULL, NULL, 'From the given options, choose the word that best expresses the meaning of the given word: Grisly', NULL, '[{"text":"gloomy","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"greasy","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"disgusting","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"boorish","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This is a vocabulary question testing your knowledge of synonyms. "Grisly" is a descriptive adjective often used in dark or morbid contexts.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Grisly: Causing horror or disgust; typically involving details of death or injury.

• Gloomy: Dark or poorly lit; feeling distressed or pessimistic.

• Greasy: Covered with or resembling an oily substance.

• Disgusting: Arousing revulsion or strong indignation (The closest match for horror/revulsion).

• Boorish: Rough and bad-mannered; coarse.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The best synonym for "Grisly" from the given options is "disgusting."

Quick Tip: Don''t confuse "Grisly" with "Grizzly" (the bear) or "Grisly" with "Gristly" (tough meat). "Grisly" usually describes a crime scene or a horror movie plot!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.456Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5503, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct word from the options given below to substitute the highlighted words: A careful ''preservation and protection'' of the flora and fauna is the need of the hour.', NULL, '[{"text":"Conservation","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Promotion","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Enhancement","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Management","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This is a "One Word Substitution" task where you replace a phrase with a more precise technical or formal term.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Conservation: The specific term for the planned management of natural resources to prevent exploitation, destruction, or neglect (Matches "preservation and protection").

• Promotion: The act of encouraging the progress or existence of something.

• Enhancement: The process of improving the quality, value, or extent of something.

• Management: The process of dealing with or controlling things or people.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The term that specifically means preservation and protection of nature is "Conservation."

Quick Tip: While "Preservation" means keeping something exactly as it is, "Conservation" often implies the wise and sustainable use of resources so they last for the future.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.457Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5504, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Match the phrasal verbs given in List-I with their correct meanings in List-II:




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-I (Phrasal Verbs)</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II (Meanings)</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) Nod off</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(i) Fall asleep</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) Jack up</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(ii) Raise prices</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C) Look up to</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(iii) Finish something</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) Wrap up</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(iv) To respect</td></tr></table>', NULL, '[{"text":"(A)-(i), (B)-(ii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(iii)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(iii), (B)-(i), (C)-(iv), (D)-(ii)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(ii), (B)-(i), (C)-(iv), (D)-(iii)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(iii), (B)-(iv), (C)-(ii), (D)-(i)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Phrasal verbs consist of a verb and a particle (preposition or adverb) that together create a meaning different from the original verb.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s match each phrasal verb with its definition:

(A) Nod off: This means to begin sleeping, especially while sitting up or in a situation where you shouldn''t (e.g., in a meeting). Matches with (i).

(B) Jack up: This is an informal way to say someone has suddenly increased something, typically prices or interest rates. Matches with (ii).

• (C) Look up to: This means to admire or respect someone greatly. Matches with (iv).

• (D) Wrap up: This means to complete or finish a task, meeting, or project. Matches with (iii).

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct matching sequence is (A)-(i), (B)-(ii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(iii).

Quick Tip: To remember "Jack up," think of a car **jack**—it raises the car higher. Similarly, "jacking up" prices raises them higher!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.457Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5505, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Select the word closest in meaning to the word: Postulate', NULL, '[{"text":"Highlight","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Disprove","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Refute","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Hypothesize","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To "postulate" is to suggest or assume the existence, fact, or truth of something as a basis for reasoning, discussion, or belief. It is common in scientific and mathematical contexts.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Hypothesize: To put forward an idea or theory as a starting point for further investigation.

This is the closest synonym.

• Highlight: To draw special attention to something.

- Disprove / Refute: These are antonyms; they mean to prove that a statement or theory is wrong or false.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The word closest in meaning to "Postulate" is "Hypothesize."

Quick Tip: In geometry, a postulate is an axiom—something we accept as true without proof so that we can build other theorems on top of it!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.463Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5506, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Match the words given in List-I with the appropriate meanings given in List-II:




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-I (Words)</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II (Meanings)</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) Centrifugal</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(i) Anything tending to move away from the centre</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) Effeminate</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(ii) Words inscribed on the tomb</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C) Ambidextrous</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(iii) Womanish in habits</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) Epitaph</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(iv) One who can use either hand without any problem</td></tr></table>', NULL, '[{"text":"(A)-(i), (B)-(iv), (C)-(iii), (D)-(ii)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(iv), (B)-(ii), (C)-(iii), (D)-(i)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(i), (B)-(iii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(ii)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(iii), (B)-(ii), (C)-(i), (D)-(iv)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This matching exercise tests "One Word Substitution," where complex descriptions are represented by a single specialized term.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

(A) Centrifugal: Derived from Latin centrum (center) and fugere (to flee). It describes moving away from the center. Matches (i).

• (B) Effeminate: Used to describe a man who has characteristics or habits traditionally associated with women. Matches (iii).

• (C) Ambidextrous: Derived from ambi (both) and dexter (right/skillful). It means being equally skillful with both hands. Matches (iv).

• (D) Epitaph: A short text honoring a deceased person, strictly those inscribed on a tombstone. Matches (ii).

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct match is (A)-(i), (B)-(iii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(ii).

Quick Tip: To remember "Centrifugal," think of a "Centrifuge" machine that spins things so fast they fly toward the edges (away from the center)!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.463Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5507, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Select the word or phrase that best completes the following sentence: Desert flowers can be invisible for years ___ appear suddenly after heavy rain.', NULL, '[{"text":"alternatively or","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"consequently and","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"nevertheless, so","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"actually, but","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests the use of conjunctions and adverbs to show contrast between two states: being "invisible" and "appearing suddenly."

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The sentence describes a surprising contrast.

• "Actually" emphasizes the reality of their invisibility.

"But" is the coordinating conjunction used to introduce a statement that contrasts with what has already been mentioned.

• Options (A), (B), and (C) do not provide the logical "contrast" required to link "invisible"

with "appear."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The sentence should read: "...can be invisible for years, actually, but appear suddenly..."

Quick Tip: When you see a sentence where the second half is the "opposite" of the first half (Invisible vs. Appear), always look for contrast markers like but, however, yet, or nevertheless.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.464Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5508, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the word that is closest in meaning to the word: Relegate', NULL, '[{"text":"Elevate","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Demote","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Acknowledge","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Celebrate","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To "relegate" someone or something means to assign them to a lower or less important position, rank, or condition.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Demote: To move someone to a lower rank or less senior position. This is the closest synonym.

• Elevate: To raise to a higher position (Antonym).

• Acknowledge: To accept or admit the existence or truth of something.

• Celebrate: To acknowledge a significant happy day or event with a social gathering or enjoyable activity.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The word closest in meaning to "Relegate" is "Demote."

Quick Tip: In sports, especially European football, if a team performs poorly, they are relegated to a lower league. If they do well, they are promoted.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.465Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5509, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Rearrange the following parts to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) into simpler molecules that can be absorbed into the bloodstream

(B) digestion is an intricate process that begins in the mouth

(C) where food is broken down mechanically and chemically

(D) and continues in the stomach and intestines', NULL, '[{"text":"(B),(C),(A),(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B)，(D)，(C)，(A)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B),(A),(C),(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C)，(B)，(A)，(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

When rearranging a descriptive sentence, follow the logical or chronological sequence of the process being described.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Part (B) introduces the subject and the starting point: "digestion is an intricate process that begins in the mouth". 2. Part (D) follows the timeline of the process: "and continues in the stomach and intestines". 3. Part (C) explains what happens during those stages: "where food is broken down mechanically and chemically". 4. Part (A) provides the final result/purpose of that breakdown: "into simpler molecules that can be absorbed into the bloodstream".

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct order is (B)  $ \rightarrow $ (D)  $ \rightarrow $ (C)  $ \rightarrow $ (A).

Quick Tip: Always look for "and" (Part D) to see what it connects. Here, it connects "begins in the mouth" with "continues in the stomach."', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.465Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5510, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Select the word opposite in meaning to the word: Stoic', NULL, '[{"text":"Serene","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Complaining","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Resigned","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"stolid","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

A "Stoic" person can endure pain or hardship without showing their feelings or complaining. To find the opposite, we look for someone who expresses their distress or dissatisfaction.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Serene: Calm, peaceful, and untroubled (Similar in vibe to stoic).

• Complaining: Expressing dissatisfaction or annoyance about something (Direct antonym).

• Resigned: Accepting that something undesirable cannot be avoided (Similar to stoic).

• Stolid: Calm, dependable, and showing little emotion or animation (Synonym).

Step 3: Final Answer:

The opposite of "Stoic" is "Complaining."

Quick Tip: A Stoic suffers in silence. A Complainer makes sure everyone knows they are suffering!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.465Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5511, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Select the word opposite in meaning to the word: Presumptuous', NULL, '[{"text":"Pretentious","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Brash","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Bountiful","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Unassuming","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

A "presumptuous" person fails to observe the limits of what is permitted or appropriate. They are often overconfident or arrogant in their behavior.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Unassuming: Not pretentious or arrogant; modest. This is the direct opposite of being presumptuous.

• Pretentious / Brash: These are synonyms or near-synonyms for presumptuous, involving arrogance or overconfidence.

• Bountiful: Large in quantity; abundant.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The opposite of "Presumptuous" is "Unassuming."

Quick Tip: To remember "Presumptuous," think of someone who presumes they have more authority or friendship than they actually do. They overstep!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.466Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5512, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Fill in the blank with the most appropriate option in the following sentence: Though Sharon complained and cried bitterly, Mom did not ___ on her.', NULL, '[{"text":"let in","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"let off","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"let out","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"let up","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests phrasal verbs related to intensity and pressure.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Let up: To become less intense or to stop being harsh. In this context, it means Mom did not stop being strict or demanding despite the crying.

• Let in: To allow someone to enter.

• Let off: To allow someone to go without punishment (e.g., "let off with a warning"). While similar, "let up on someone" specifically refers to the persistence of pressure or discipline.

• Let out: To release or utter a sound.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct phrase is "let up."

Quick Tip: We often use "let up" for weather: "The rain didn''t let up all day." It means the intensity didn''t decrease.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.466Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5513, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Select the word opposite in meaning to the word: Resonant', NULL, '[{"text":"Resounding","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Cacophonous","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Smothered","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Pulsating","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

"Resonant" describes a sound that is deep, clear, and continuing to sound or echo. An opposite

would be a sound that is blocked, muffled, or prevented from echoing.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Smothered: A sound that is muffled, suppressed, or stifled. This is the best antonym.

• Resounding: Deep and loud; very similar to resonant (Synonym).

• Cacophonous: Involving a harsh, discordant mixture of sounds (Refers to quality of sound, not the ability to echo).

Pulsating: Expanding and contracting with regular movements.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The opposite of "Resonant" is "Smothered."

Quick Tip: A "Resonant" voice fills the whole room. A "Smothered" voice sounds like it''s coming from under a pillow!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.467Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5514, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Re-arrange the following phrases to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) a great buzzard was

(B) and scorching sun.

(C) blob against the blinding

(D) circling high overhead a', NULL, '[{"text":"(A),(D),(C),(B)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A), (C), (B), (D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B), (A), (D), (C)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C)，(B)，(D)，(A)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Identify the subject and its action, then connect the modifiers that describe the setting.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Part (A) starts with the subject: "a great buzzard was". 2. Part (D) provides the verb/action: "circling high overhead a". 3. Part (C) provides the visual description of the bird: "blob against the blinding". 4. Part (B) completes the description of the sky: "and scorching sun."

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The sentence is: "A great buzzard was circling high overhead a blob against the blinding and scorching sun." (The sequence is A-D-C-B).

Quick Tip: "Blinding" and "Scorching" are both adjectives describing the sun, so (C) and (B) must follow each other to complete that phrase.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.468Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5515, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Rearrange the following parts to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) into simpler molecules that can be absorbed into the bloodstream

(B) digestion is an intricate process that begins in the mouth

(C) where food is broken down mechanically and chemically

(D) and continues in the stomach and intestines', NULL, '[{"text":"(B),(C),(A),(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B)，(D)，(C)，(A)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B),(A),(C),(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C)，(B)，(A)，(D)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To form a logical sentence, we must follow the sequence of the biological process (digestion) and ensure grammatical connectivity between the phrases.

Step 2: Identifying the Sequence:

- Part (B) is the independent clause and the logical start: "digestion is an intricate process that begins in the mouth".

• Part (D) continues the timeline of the process: "and continues in the stomach and intestines".

• Part (C) describes what happens in those locations: "where food is broken down mechanically and chemically".

- Part (A) concludes with the end goal of the process: "into simpler molecules that can be absorbed into the bloodstream".

### Step 3: Final Answer:

The full sentence is: "Digestion is an intricate process that begins in the mouth and continues in the stomach and intestines where food is broken down mechanically and chemically into simpler molecules that can be absorbed into the bloodstream."

Quick Tip: Look for transition markers. "Begins" in part (B) naturally pairs with "continues" in part (D), helping you establish the first half of the sequence immediately.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.471Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5516, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Select the word opposite in meaning to the word: Presumptuous', NULL, '[{"text":"Pretentious","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Brash","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Bountiful","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Unassuming","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

An antonym is a word that means the opposite of another word. "Presumptuous" refers to someone who oversteps their bounds or is overly bold/arrogant.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Presumptuous: Overconfident, arrogant, or failing to observe the limits of what is appropriate.

• Unassuming: Modest, humble, and not bold or arrogant. This is the direct opposite.

• Pretentious/Brash: These are synonyms or related in meaning to being bold or showy.

• Bountiful: Means large in quantity or generous.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The opposite of "Presumptuous" is "Unassuming."

Quick Tip: The root "presume" means to take for granted. A presumptuous person "presumes" they are welcome or important, whereas an unassuming person stays humble.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.471Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5517, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Select the word opposite in meaning to the word: Resonant', NULL, '[{"text":"Resounding","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Cacophonous","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Smothered","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Pulsating","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

"Resonant" describes a sound that is deep, clear, and echoes. To find the opposite, we look for a word that describes a sound that is muffled or prevented from echoing.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Resonant: Producing a deep, clear, reverberating sound.

- Smothered: A sound that is stifled, muffled, or suppressed. This acts as the best antonym in terms of sound quality.

• Resounding: Similar to resonant; loud and echoing.

• Cacophonous: Describes a harsh, unpleasant mixture of sounds.

• Pulsating: Rhythmic throbbing or vibrating.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The opposite of "Resonant" is "Smothered."

Quick Tip: Think of a bell. A "resonant" bell ring continues to vibrate in the air. If you put a cloth over the bell, the sound becomes "smothered."', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.472Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5518, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Select the most appropriate expression to fill in the blank in the following sentence: Given her fearful and timid nature in unfamiliar situations, we can say that she is ___.', NULL, '[{"text":"audacious","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"timorous","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"reckless","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"intrepid","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question requires identifying a word that matches the descriptors "fearful" and "timid" provided in the sentence context.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Timorous: Showing or suffering from nervousness or a lack of confidence. It is a direct synonym for timid and fearful.

• Audacious / Intrepid: These are antonyms meaning bold, brave, or fearless.

• Reckless: Heedless of danger or the consequences of one''s actions.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The most appropriate word is "timorous."

Quick Tip: To remember "timorous," notice that it shares the same root as "timid." Both words describe a lack of courage or confidence.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.473Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5519, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Select the word closest in meaning to the word: Postulate', NULL, '[{"text":"Highlight","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Disprove","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Refute","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Hypothesize","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To "postulate" is to suggest or assume the existence, fact, or truth of something as a basis for reasoning, discussion, or belief.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Hypothesize: To provide a tentative explanation or an assumption that can be tested. This is the closest in meaning.

• Highlight: To draw attention to something.

• Disprove/Refute: These mean to prove that something is false.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The word closest in meaning to "Postulate" is "Hypothesize."

Quick Tip: In scientific methods, you first postulate an idea, then you test it. It''s the "starting point" of an argument or theory.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.474Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5520, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Match the words given in List-I with the appropriate meanings given in List-II




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-I (Words)</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II (Meanings)</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) Centrifugal</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(i) Anything tending to move away from the centre</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) Effeminate</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(ii) Words inscribed on the tomb</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C) Ambidextrous</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(iii) Womanish in habits</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) Epitaph</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(iv) One who can use either hand without any problem</td></tr></table>', NULL, '[{"text":"(A)-(i), (B)-(iv), (C)-(iii), (D)-(ii)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(iv), (B)-(ii), (C)-(iii), (D)-(i)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(i), (B)-(iii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(ii)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A)-(iii), (B)-(ii), (C)-(i), (D)-(iv)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This is a "One Word Substitution" exercise where specific technical or descriptive terms are matched to their definitions.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

(A) Centrifugal: A force or tendency that moves objects away from the axis of rotation. Matches (i).

• (B) Effeminate: Having characteristics regarded as typical of a woman; unmanly. Matches (iii).

• (C) Ambidextrous: Able to use the right and left hands equally well. Matches (iv).

• (D) Epitaph: A phrase or form of words written in memory of a person who has died, especially as an inscription on a tombstone. Matches (ii).

Step 4: Final Answer:

The correct matching sequence is (A)-(i), (B)-(iii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(ii).

Quick Tip: Break down "Ambidextrous": Ambi means "both" and dexter means "skillful." Literally, it means being "skillful with both."', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.475Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5521, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Select the word or phrase that best completes the following sentence:— Desert flowers can be invisible for years ___ appear suddenly after heavy rain.', NULL, '[{"text":"alternatively or","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"consequently and","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"nevertheless, so","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"actually, but","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The sentence presents a contrast between flowers being "invisible" and then "appearing suddenly." To connect these two contrasting ideas, we need a contrastive conjunction.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Actually: Used to emphasize the surprising truth of their long invisibility.

• But: A coordinating conjunction used to introduce a statement that contrasts with the previous one.

• Options (A), (B), and (C) do not provide the correct logical transition to show how a long period of absence leads to a sudden appearance.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The completed sentence is: "Desert flowers can be invisible for years actually, but appear suddenly after heavy rain."

Quick Tip: When a sentence describes a shift from one state (invisible) to its opposite (appear), always look for "but," "yet," or "however" in the options!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.476Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5522, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the word that is closest in meaning to the word: Relegate', NULL, '[{"text":"Elevate","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Demote","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Acknowledge","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Celebrate","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

"Relegate" means to assign an inferior rank or position to someone or something. It often implies a reduction in importance or status.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Demote: To move someone to a lower rank or less senior position. This is a direct synonym.

• Elevate: To raise to a more important or higher level (Antonym).

• Acknowledge: To accept or admit the existence or truth of.

• Celebrate: To honor or praise publicly.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The word closest in meaning to "Relegate" is "Demote."

Quick Tip: In sports, a team is "relegated" when they are moved down to a lower division because of poor performance.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.477Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5523, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Re-arrange the following phrases to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) a great buzzard was

(B) and scorching sun.

(C) blob against the blinding

(D) circling high overhead a', NULL, '[{"text":"(A),(D),(C),(B)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A), (C), (B), (D)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B),(A),(D),(C)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C)，(B)，(D)，(A)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

A meaningful sentence usually follows the Subject + Verb + Object/Description pattern.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Part (A) introduces the subject: "A great buzzard was". 2. Part (D) adds the action: "circling high overhead a". 3. Part (C) describes how it looked: "blob against the blinding". 4. Part (B) completes the environmental description: "and scorching sun."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct order is (A), (D), (C), (B).

Quick Tip: "Blinding" and "scorching" are coordinate adjectives that both describe the "sun." This tells you that (C) and (B) must stay together in that order.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.480Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5524, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Rearrange the given parts to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) and strengthens economic self-reliance

(B) manufacturing, creates jobs, encourages

(C) make in India promotes local

(D) innovation, attracts global investments', NULL, '[{"text":"(D), (C), (B), (A)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(A),(D),(C),(B)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(B), (A), (D), (C)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"(C), (B), (D), (A)","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Identify the main subject/campaign (Make in India) and list the verbs/actions it performs in a logical, comma-separated sequence ending with "and."

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Part (C) is the opening: "Make in India promotes local". 2. Part (B) follows with the objects of promotion: "manufacturing, creates jobs, encourages". 3. Part (D) continues the list: "innovation, attracts global investments". 4. Part (A) uses "and" to conclude the list of benefits: "and strengthens economic self-reliance".

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct order is (C), (B), (D), (A).

Quick Tip: In a long list of actions, the part starting with "and" (Part A) almost always goes at the very end of the sentence!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.481Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5525, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the SYNONYM of AMELIORATE:', NULL, '[{"text":"Worsen","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Diminish","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Maintain","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Improve","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

A synonym is a word that has the same or nearly the same meaning as another word.

"Ameliorate" is a formal verb used to describe the act of making something better.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Ameliorate: To make (something bad or unsatisfactory) better.

• Improve: To make or become better. This is the direct synonym.

• Worsen: To make something worse (Antonym).

• Diminish: To make or become less.

• Maintain: To keep something at the same level or state.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The synonym of "Ameliorate" is "Improve."

Quick Tip: Think of "Ameliorate" as a fancy way to say "fix" or "upgrade" a situation. If conditions in a city are bad, the government tries to ameliorate them.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.483Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5526, 1, NULL, NULL, '''The team ___ playing exceptionally well this season.''', NULL, '[{"text":"are","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"were","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"is","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"have been","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests Subject-Verb Agreement with collective nouns. A collective noun (like team, family, or jury) takes a singular verb when the group is acting as a single unit.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

In this sentence, "The team" is viewed as a single collective entity performing the action of playing.

• is: Singular present tense, appropriate for a single unit.

- are: Plural present tense, used if the members were acting individually (e.g., "The team are putting on their uniforms").

• were / have been: These do not fit the singular focus of the current season''s performance as well as "is" in this specific context.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct sentence is: "The team is playing exceptionally well this season."

Quick Tip: In American English, collective nouns almost always take a singular verb (is). In British English, they can take plural verbs (are) if the focus is on the individuals.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.484Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5527, 1, NULL, NULL, '''To burn the midnight oil'' means:', NULL, '[{"text":"To waste resources","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"To be very angry","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"To set fire to something","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"To work very late into the night","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

An idiom is a group of words established by usage as having a meaning not deductible from those of the individual words.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The phrase originates from the time before electricity, when people had to use oil lamps to see. If you were "burning the midnight oil," it meant you were staying up past midnight to study or work by the light of a lamp.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The idiom "To burn the midnight oil" means to work or study very late into the night.

Quick Tip: Use this idiom when talking about exam preparation: "I had to burn the midnight oil to finish my project before the deadline."', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.484Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5528, 1, NULL, NULL, 'The politician''s speech was so ___ that even his opponents were moved.', NULL, '[{"text":"banal","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"monotonous","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"garbled","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"eloquent","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The sentence implies that the speech had a powerful, positive effect because it "moved" (emotionally touched) even those who disagreed with the politician. We need a word that describes high-quality, persuasive speaking.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Eloquent: Fluent or persuasive in speaking or writing. This perfectly fits the context of moving an audience.

• Banal: So lacking in originality as to be obvious and boring.

- Monotonous: Dull, tedious, and lacking in variation in tone or pitch.

• Garbled: Reproduced in a confused and distorted way; unclear.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct word is "eloquent."

Quick Tip: To "move" an audience usually requires Pathos (emotional appeal). An eloquent speaker knows exactly how to use words to create that emotional connection.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.485Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5529, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the error: ''He is knowing the answer but refused to tell us.''', NULL, '[{"text":"is knowing","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"answer","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"refused","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"tell","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question deals with Stative Verbs. These are verbs that describe a state, condition, or

mental process rather than a physical action.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Verbs like know, believe, understand, and love are generally not used in the continuous (-ing) form. We do not "be knowing" something; we either "know" it or we don''t.

• Incorrect: "He is knowing the answer."

• Correct: "He knows the answer" (Present) or "He knew the answer" (Past).

Since the second half of the sentence is in the past tense ("refused"), the first part should logically be "He knew."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The error is in the phrase "is knowing."

Quick Tip: Stative verbs are like "filters" of the mind or heart. Because they aren''t physical actions you can see someone doing, we rarely use them with -ing.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.486Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5530, 1, NULL, NULL, 'One who can speak two languages fluently is called a:', NULL, '[{"text":"Multilingual","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Monolingual","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Polyglot","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Bilingual","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This is a one-word substitution question based on numerical prefixes in linguistics. The prefix "bi-" refers to the number two.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Bilingual: Capable of speaking two languages fluently.

- Monolingual: Speaking only one language.

• Multilingual / Polyglot: Someone who can speak or use several (usually more than three) languages.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The term for someone speaking two languages fluently is "Bilingual."

Quick Tip: To remember prefixes: Mono = 1, Bi = 2, Tri = 3, Poly/Multi = Many.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.486Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5531, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Her ___ attitude towards criticism helped her grow professionally.', NULL, '[{"text":"hostile","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"defensive","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"receptive","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"dismissive","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The sentence states that the attitude "helped her grow." Therefore, we need a positive word that describes someone who is open to feedback and learning.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Receptive: Willing to consider or accept new suggestions and ideas. This leads to growth.

• Hostile: Showing or feeling opposition or dislike; unfriendly.

• Defensive: Very anxious to challenge or avoid criticism.

• Dismissive: Feeling or showing that something is unworthy of consideration.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct word is "receptive."

Quick Tip: Look for the "result" in the sentence. If the result is "growth," the input attitude must be open and positive.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.487Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5532, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the SYNONYM of EPHEMERAL:', NULL, '[{"text":"Permanent","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Eternal","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Transient","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Lasting","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

"Ephemeral" describes something that lasts for a very short time. We need a word that captures this sense of briefness.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Ephemeral: Lasting for a very short time.

• Transient: Lasting only for a short time; impermanent. This is the synonym.

• Permanent / Eternal / Lasting: These are all antonyms, describing things that endure for a long time.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The synonym of "Ephemeral" is "Transient."

Quick Tip: "Ephemeral" is often used in nature, like "ephemeral flowers" that bloom and die in a single day.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.487Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5533, 1, NULL, NULL, '''Neither the manager nor the employees _____ informed about the decision.''', NULL, '[{"text":"was","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"is","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"were","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"has been","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests the "Rule of Proximity" in subject-verb agreement. When subjects are joined by "neither/nor" or "either/or," the verb must agree with the subject closest to it.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The two subjects are "the manager" (singular) and "the employees" (plural).

• The subject closest to the blank is "the employees."

• Since "employees" is plural, the verb must also be plural.

• "were" is the plural form suitable for the past passive context.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct sentence is: "Neither the manager nor the employees were informed about the decision."

Quick Tip: Always look at the noun right before the verb in "neither...nor" sentences. If that noun is plural, the verb is plural!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.488Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5534, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the word closest in meaning to "innocuous".', NULL, '[{"text":"Harmful","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Harmless","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Aggressive","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Suspicious","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The word "innocuous" is an adjective used to describe something that does not cause any physical or emotional injury. It is derived from the Latin root innocuus, where "in-" means "not" and "nocere" means "to harm."

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Innocuous: Not harmful or offensive.

• Harmless: This is the direct synonym, meaning unlikely to cause injury.

• Harmful: The direct antonym (opposite).

• Aggressive: Ready or likely to attack or confront.

• Suspicious: Having or showing a cautious distrust of someone or something.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The word closest in meaning to "innocuous" is "Harmless."

Quick Tip: Think of "innocuous" as a "no-harm" word. It is often used to describe a comment that wasn''t meant to be mean, like "an innocuous remark."', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.490Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5535, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the word opposite in meaning to “vindicative”.', NULL, '[{"text":"Revengeful","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Forgiving","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Cruel","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Arrogant","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The word "vindictive" (often spelled "vindicative" in older or specific legal contexts, though "vindictive" is the standard modern usage for the personality trait) refers to a strong desire for revenge.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Vindictive: Having or showing a strong or unreasoning desire for revenge.

- Forgiving: Ready and willing to stop feeling angry or resentful toward someone for an offense. This is the direct opposite.

• Revengeful: A direct synonym for vindictive.

- Cruel / Arrogant: These are negative traits but do not specifically address the "revenge vs. mercy" aspect.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The opposite of "vindicative" is "Forgiving."

Quick Tip: To remember "vindictive," think of the word Vendetta (a blood feud). A vindictive person is someone who wants to start or continue a vendetta.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.490Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5536, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the grammatically correct sentence.', NULL, '[{"text":"Neither of the boys were ready for the competition.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Neither of the boys was ready for the competition.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Neither of the boys are ready for the competition.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Neither of the boys have been ready for the competition.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests the rule of distributive pronouns. Words like "Each," "Either," and "Neither" are distributive pronouns and are always considered singular in formal grammar.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

In the phrase "Neither of the boys," the word "Neither" is the actual subject of the sentence, not "boys."

Because the subject is singular, the verb must also be singular.

• "Neither" means "not one and not the other" (referring to them individually).

• "was" is singular, while "were," "are," and "have been" are plural.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct sentence is: "Neither of the boys was ready for the competition."

Quick Tip: Whenever you see "of the [plural noun]" after "Each/Either/Neither," ignore the plural noun. Focus only on the first word to decide the verb!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.490Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5537, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct option to complete the sentence.

By the time we reached the station, the train ___.', NULL, '[{"text":"left","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"has left","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"had left","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"leaves","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This sentence involves two actions that happened in the past at different times. In English grammar, when two past actions are related, we use the Past Perfect tense for the action that happened first and the Simple Past for the action that happened second.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Action 1 (First): The train departed.

• Action 2 (Second): We reached the station.

Since the train leaving happened before reaching the station, we must use the Past Perfect (had + past participle).

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct completion is "had left."

Quick Tip: Think of the Past Perfect as the "Past of the Past." If you are already talking about the past ("reached"), anything that happened before that needs "had."', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.491Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5538, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct passive form of the sentence: “They are organizing a cultural fest.”', NULL, '[{"text":"A cultural fest is organized by them.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A cultural fest was being organized by them.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A cultural fest is being organized by them.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A cultural fest has been organized by them.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The original sentence is in the Present Continuous tense (Subject + is/am/are + v-ing + Object). To convert this to passive voice, we use the formula: Object + is/am/are + being + V3 (past participle) + by + Subject.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Subject: They → becomes "them" (object).

• Object: A cultural fest  $ \rightarrow $ becomes the new subject.

- Verb Change: "are organizing" (continuous) must include "being" to maintain the continuous aspect in passive form.

Applying the formula: "A cultural fest" (singular) + "is" + "being" + "organized" + "by them."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct passive form is: "A cultural fest is being organized by them."

Quick Tip: If the active sentence has an "-ing" verb, the passive sentence MUST have the word "being." This is an easy way to eliminate wrong options!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.492Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5539, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct indirect narration.

### Riya said, “I will complete the project tomorrow.”', NULL, '[{"text":"Riya said that she will complete the project tomorrow.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Riya said that she would complete the project the next day.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Riya said that she completed the project tomorrow.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Riya said she would have completed the project the next day.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

When converting from Direct to Indirect Speech, we must apply three main changes: Tense change, Pronoun change, and Time/Place word changes.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Pronoun Change: "I" (referring to Riya) changes to "she."

• Tense Change: "will" changes to its past form, "would."

• Time Word Change: "tomorrow" changes to "the next day" or "the following day."

• Connector: Use "that" after the reporting verb "said."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct indirect narration is: "Riya said that she would complete the project the next day."

Quick Tip: Always remember the "Backshift." If the reporting verb is in the past ("said"), the verbs inside the quotes must move one step back into the past (will  $ \rightarrow $ would, can  $ \rightarrow $ could).', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.494Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5540, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Arrange the following parts in the correct order.

P. the scientist presented

Q. at the international conference

R. his groundbreaking research

S. confidently', NULL, '[{"text":"PQRS","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"PRSQ","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"PSRQ","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"QPRS","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Sentence rearrangement requires identifying the Subject, Verb, and Object (SVO), followed by adverbs of manner and prepositional phrases for location or time.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Subject + Verb (P): "The scientist presented"

Adverb of Manner (S): "confidently" (This describes how he presented).

• Object (R): "his groundbreaking research" (This describes what he presented).

• Location (Q): "at the international conference" (This describes where).

While (B) "P R S Q" is also common in casual English, (C) "P S R Q" provides a more formal and fluid flow by placing the adverb closer to the verb. In many standard examinations, P-S-R-Q is the preferred structural order.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct order is P S R Q.

Quick Tip: A good rule of thumb is: Who (P) + How (S) + What (R) + Where (Q).', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.495Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5541, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Read the passage carefully:

Many students believe that success depends entirely on intelligence. However, researchers have found that consistency and disciplined effort often play a greater role in long-term achievement than natural talent alone.

What is the main idea of the passage?', NULL, '[{"text":"Intelligence is unnecessary for success.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Talent guarantees achievement.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Consistent effort is important for success.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Researchers dislike talented students.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Finding the "main idea" involves identifying the author''s primary argument. Pay close attention to transition words like "However," as they often shift the focus from a common belief to the author''s actual point.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• The passage acknowledges a common belief (intelligence = success).

• It uses "However" to introduce a different perspective.

- It highlights "consistency and disciplined effort" as key factors that often outweigh "natural talent."

• Option (C) directly reflects this emphasis on effort.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The main idea is that consistent effort is important for success.

Quick Tip: The word "However" is a giant signpost. The most important information in a short passage usually comes immediately after it.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.496Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5542, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the best replacement for the underlined part:

Each of the players  $ \underline{\text{have performed}} $ exceptionally well.', NULL, '[{"text":"has performed","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"were performing","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"perform","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"have been performing","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Similar to the "Neither of the boys" rule, the word "Each" is a distributive pronoun that always takes a singular verb, even when followed by a plural prepositional phrase (of the players).

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Subject: "Each" (Singular).

• Verb needed: Singular form.

• "Have" is plural; "has" is singular.

Therefore, "have performed" must be replaced with "has performed" to agree with "Each."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct replacement is "has performed."

#### Quick Tip: Think of "Each" as "Every single one." Since "one" is singular, the verb must be singular too!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.498Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5543, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the word that is closest in meaning to “obfuscate”.', NULL, '[{"text":"Clarify","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Confuse","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Simplify","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Reveal","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '## Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Vocabulary questions require analyzing the root meaning, context, and usage of a word to determine its nearest synonym among the given choices.

## Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Identify the definition of the target word: "Obfuscate" means to render obscure, unclear, or unintelligible.

Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Obfuscate means to deliberately make something difficult to understand or perceive (e.g.,

"The writer used complex jargon to obfuscate his true meaning"). Therefore, Confuse is the closest synonym. Clarify, Simplify, and Reveal are all antonyms of obfuscate, as they deal with making things clearer or easier to see.

##### Step 4: Final Answer:

The word closest in meaning to “obfuscate” is Confuse.

Quick Tip: Think of the "ob-" prefix in obfuscate as related to words like obscure or obstruct—all of which involve blocking or clouding view or understanding.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.501Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5544, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the correctly spelt word.', NULL, '[{"text":"Acquaintence","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Acquaintance","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Acquaintance","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Acquaintance","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Spelling tests assess knowledge of phonetic rules, silent letters, and conventional letter groupings in English vocabulary.

Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Break down the word into its syllables or root construction: ac + quaint + ance.

Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

The root component is "quaint" (familiar or unusual), preceded by the prefix combination "ac-". The suffix used here is -ance (denoting a state or condition), not "-ence". Combining these elements systematically yields Acquaintance (a person one knows slightly, but who is not a close friend).

##### Step 4: Final Answer:

The correctly spelt word is Acquaintance.

Quick Tip: Remember that an acquaintance is someone you are bound to check ain''t a stranger. Focus on the word "quaint" hidden inside it!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.502Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5545, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct idiom to complete the sentence:

"When the manager resigned suddenly, the company employees were left ___."', NULL, '[{"text":"in hot water","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"under the weather","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"high and dry","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"at sixes and sevens","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Idioms are expressions whose figurative meanings cannot be understood directly from the literal definitions of individual words. The correct option must logically match the contextual narrative of the sentence.

#### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Analyze the meaning of each idiom in relation to a sudden abandonment or crisis:

• In hot water: In trouble or facing punishment.

• Under the weather: Feeling sick or unwell.

• High and dry: Left stranded, helpless, or abandoned without resources.

• At sixes and sevens: In a state of total confusion or disarray.

##### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

The sentence states that a manager resigned suddenly, leaving the employees without leadership or support to handle the ongoing operations. While "at sixes and sevens" (confused)

is a close option, the phrase high and dry specifically emphasizes being abandoned in a difficult, vulnerable, and helpless situation due to someone else''s sudden actions.

##### Step 4: Final Answer:

The most appropriate idiom to complete the sentence is high and dry.

Quick Tip: The phrase high and dry originally described a ship stuck out of water on land, unable to move or save itself—perfect for describing a team stranded without a boss!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.502Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5546, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Select the sentence with the correct grammatical usage.', NULL, '[{"text":"Neither of the boys were ready for the exam.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Neither of the boys was ready for the exam.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Neither of the boys are ready for the exam.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Neither of the boys have been ready for the exam.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question deals with subject-verb agreement rules, specifically when using distributive pronouns like "neither", "either", or "each".

#### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

The pronoun "neither" means "not one nor the other of two." It is inherently singular in formal grammar and must be paired with a singular verb.

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

In the sentence structure "Neither of the [plural noun]" , the actual subject is Neither, not the plural noun that follows the preposition "of" (boys).

• Because "Neither" is singular, it requires the singular past-tense verb was.

• Options (a), (c), and (d) use plural verbs ("were", "are", "have been"), creating an error in subject-verb agreement.

##### Step 4: Final Answer:

The grammatically correct sentence is: "Neither of the boys was ready for the exam."

Quick Tip: To avoid confusion, mentally cross out the phrase starting with "of". Read it as: "Neither [was] ready for the exam." It makes the correct singular verb choice obvious!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.503Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5547, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the option that best paraphrases the sentence:

#### “Technological advancement has significantly altered human interaction.”', NULL, '[{"text":"Technology has reduced the need for communication entirely.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Human interaction is unaffected by modern inventions.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Advances in technology have greatly changed the way people interact.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Technology only affects professional communication.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '##### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Paraphrasing means rewriting a statement using different words while keeping the exact same meaning, tone, and scope as the original message.

#### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Match key synonyms and retain core logical relations:

• "Technological advancement"  $ \rightarrow $ "Advances in technology"

• "Significantly altered"  $ \rightarrow $ "Greatly changed"

• "Human interaction"  $ \rightarrow $ "The way people interact"

##### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Option (a) changes the scope by making an extreme claim ("reduced the need... entirely"). Option (b) states the exact opposite of the original sentence. Option (c) perfectly retains the meaning by substituting words with accurate synonyms without adding or subtracting constraints. Option (d) introduces a limitation ("only affects professional") that was not present in the broad original text.

##### Step 4: Final Answer:

The option that best paraphrases the sentence is (c).

Quick Tip: A good paraphrase never introduces extreme absolute words like entirely, only, always, or never unless they were already present in the original sentence.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.504Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5548, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Fill in the blank with the correct option:

"If she _____ harder, she would have succeeded."', NULL, '[{"text":"studies","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"studied","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"had studied","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"has studied","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '## Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This sentence belongs to the Third Conditional category. Third conditional sentences are used to express hypothetical situations in the past—events that did not happen, along with their imaginary results.

## Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

The standard structural formula for a Third Conditional sentence is:

If + Past Perfect Clause (had +  $ V_{3} $),… would have + Past Participle ( $ V_{3} $)

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

The result clause is explicitly given as "...she would have succeeded" (would have +  $ V_{3} $). Following the conditional rule, the conditional "if" clause must take a verb phrase in the past perfect tense. Among the given choices, only had studied satisfies the past perfect requirement (had +  $ V_{3} $). Choosing "studies" (present), "studied" (simple past), or "has studied" (present perfect) breaks the structural logic of this conditional structure.

#### Step 4: Final Answer:

The complete grammatically correct sentence is: “If she had studied harder, she would have succeeded.”

Quick Tip: Think of it as a past balancing scale: If you spot "would have" on one side of the sentence, you almost always need "had" on the other side!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.505Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5549, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Read the following passage and answer the question:

Climate change is one of the most pressing challenges of the modern era. Scientists warn that rising temperatures may lead to severe environmental consequences, including floods, droughts, and biodiversity loss. Governments across the world are attempting to develop sustainable solutions, but implementation remains uneven.

What is the central idea of the passage?', NULL, '[{"text":"Governments have solved climate change completely.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Climate change poses serious global challenges requiring action.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Biodiversity loss is unrelated to climate change.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Scientists disagree about global warming.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Finding the central idea requires capturing the broad theme that unifies every sentence in the passage rather than focusing on a single detail or an over-generalized claim.

Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Analyze the logical development of the short text:

• Sentence 1 establishes the main topic: "pressing challenges."

• Sentence 2 provides details on risks: "severe environmental consequences."

• Sentence 3 outlines the ongoing response: "governments attempting solutions."

Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Option (a) is completely false because the text notes that implementation "remains uneven."

Option (b) accurately sums up the primary points: it points out the problems (challenges) and acknowledges the ongoing effort to fix them (action). Option (c) is explicitly contradicted by the text, which lists biodiversity loss as a consequence of climate change. Option (d) is incorrect because the passage says "scientists warn" collectively, showing agreement rather than conflict.

##### Step 4: Final Answer:

The central idea of the passage is that climate change poses serious global challenges requiring action.

Quick Tip: The central idea is usually stated clearly right in the very first sentence of short informational passages. Use it as your anchor!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.506Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5550, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the figure of speech used in the line:

“Life is a journey filled with unexpected turns.”', NULL, '[{"text":"Simile","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Oxymoron","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Metaphor","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Alliteration","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Figures of speech identify comparisons made between two completely different concepts to highlight a shared characteristic.

### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Look closely at how the comparison is set up:

• If it says A is like or as B, it is a Simile.

• If it says A is B directly, it is a Metaphor.

Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

The statement directly asserts that "Life is a journey." It does not use comparative words like "like" or "as" to soften the imagery. By equating life directly to a journey with twists and turns, it functions as a Metaphor, carrying the qualities of travel and exploration over to human existence.

##### Step 4: Final Answer:

The figure of speech used is a Metaphor.

Quick Tip: “Life is like a box of chocolates” is a simile. “Life is a highway” is a metaphor. It all comes down to dropping the word "like"!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.506Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5551, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the word opposite in meaning to “ephemeral.”', NULL, '[{"text":"Temporary","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Fleeting","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Permanent","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Fragile","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Antonym questions require identifying the word that carries the inverse or opposite logical meaning of the given vocabulary term.

#### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Define the core term: "Ephemeral" is derived from Greek roots meaning "lasting only a day." It describes something short-lived or transient.

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Temporary and Fleeting mean lasting a very short time, making them synonyms of ephemeral. Fragile means easily broken, which is a different concept entirely. Permanent means lasting or intended to last indefinitely without change, which is the exact polar opposite of a short-lived,

transient state.

##### Step 4: Final Answer:

The word opposite in meaning to “ephemeral” is Permanent.

Quick Tip: Think of morning dew or a shooting star—they are classic examples of ephemeral things. Their opposite is something built to last forever, like a mountain range (permanent).', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.507Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5552, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct passive or active form of the given sentence. “They have to paint the ceiling before they can install the new light fixtures.”', NULL, '[{"text":"The ceiling has to be painted before the new light fixtures can be installed.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The ceiling have to be painted before installing the new light fixtures.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Before the new light fixtures can install, the ceiling has to be painted.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The ceiling must be painted by them prior to installing the light fixtures.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Voice conversion involves shifting the focus of a sentence from the performer of the action (active voice) to the recipient of the action (passive voice). When a sentence contains multiple clauses connected by a conjunction, both clauses must be converted into their corresponding passive structures while maintaining subject-verb agreement and the original tense.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s break down the given active sentence into its two component clauses: 1. Clause 1 (Active):

"They have to paint the ceiling..." The active structure uses "have to + verb (base form)". Passive

structure rule: "Object + has/have to + be + past participle (V3)". Since "The ceiling" is singular, it takes "has to be": "The ceiling has to be painted..." 2. Clause 2 (Active): "...before they can install the new light fixtures." The active structure uses the modal verb "can + verb (base form)". Passive structure rule: "Modal + be + past participle (V3)". The object is "the new light fixtures" (plural): "...before the new light fixtures can be installed."

Combining both converted clauses gives: "The ceiling has to be painted before the new light fixtures can be installed." This aligns with Option (a). Option (b) fails due to incorrect subject-verb agreement ("ceiling have to"), and Option (c) leaves the second clause in an ungrammatical active state ("fixtures can install").

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct passive form is option (a).

Quick Tip: Always check subject-verb agreement after shifting an object to the subject position! A singular passive subject like "the ceiling" must be paired with "has to be," never "have to be."', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.512Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5553, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct passive or active form of the given sentence. “We expect that the committee will approve the project by tomorrow.”', NULL, '[{"text":"It is expected that the project will be approved by the committee by tomorrow.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The committee is expected to have approved the project by tomorrow.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Approval of the project by the committee is expected tomorrow.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"That the project will be approved by the committee by tomorrow is expecting.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

When an active sentence contains a reporting verb (like expect, believe, say, think) followed by a complete "that-clause" object, it can be converted into the passive voice using an impersonal passive construction. This structure uses the introductory dummy pronoun "It" paired with the passive form of the reporting verb, followed by the passive conversion of the subordinate clause.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the sentence structure: 1. Main Clause (Active): "We expect [that...]" This is in the Simple Present tense. Impersonal passive rule: "It + is/are + V3 + that...". This yields: "It is expected that..." 2. Subordinate Clause (Active): "...the committee will approve the project by tomorrow." This is in the Simple Future tense ("will + verb"). Passive structure rule: "Object + will + be + V3 + by + Subject". This transforms to: "...the project will be approved by the committee by tomorrow."

Putting the full structure together results in Option (a): "It is expected that the project will be approved by the committee by tomorrow." Option (b) changes the aspect unnecessarily, while Option (d) incorrectly retains an active continuous participle ("is expecting").

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct passive form is option (a).

Quick Tip: When an active sentence starts with an introductory phrase like "We expect that..." or "People say that...", look for options starting with "It is expected that..." or "It is said that..." to find the correct passive fit quickly!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.513Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5554, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct passive or active form of the given sentence. "He likes people flattering him."', NULL, '[{"text":"He likes to be flattered by people.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"He likes being flattered.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Flattering him is liked by people.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"He is liked when people flatter him.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This sentence features a gerund phrase ("flattering him") acting as the direct object of the principal verb "likes". When converting sentences containing a gerund phrase into the passive voice, the main verb ("likes") remains unchanged because the subject ("He") still performs

the emotional preference. The passive transformation occurs completely within the gerund element itself.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s look at the object verbal component: Active Gerund structure: "flattering him" (verb-ing + object pronoun). Passive Gerund conversion rule: "being + past participle (V3)". The passive equivalent of "flattering him" becomes "being flattered".

Now attach this passive gerund back to the unchanged initial subject and verb phrase:

"He likes" + "being flattered"  $ \Longrightarrow $ "He likes being flattered."

The phrase "by people" is omitted because it represents an indefinite, obvious agent that adds no new meaning. While Option (b) attempts a passive infinitive ("to be flattered"), it alters the original active gerund structure ("flattering"). Options (c) and (d) shift the sentence focus entirely away from his personal preference. Thus, Option (a) is the best choice.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct passive form is option (b).

Quick Tip: When turning an active gerund (verb-ing) into a passive phrase, look for options containing "being + V3". This helps you quickly spot the correct structure without changing the meaning of the main verb!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.514Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5555, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct passive or active form of the given sentence. "They let the prisoner leave early."', NULL, '[{"text":"The prisoner was let leave early.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The prisoner was let to leave early.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The prisoner was allowed leave early.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Leaving early was what the prisoner was let do.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

In English grammar, the causative verb "let" is followed by an object and a bare infinitive (a verb without "to") in the active voice. When converting sentences with "let" into the passive voice, the verb "let" retains its bare infinitive complement. It does not take a full infinitive ("to leave"), unlike typical verbs converted into passive structures.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the syntax of the active sentence: Active structure: Subject ("They") + verb ("let") + object ("the prisoner") + bare infinitive ("leave") + extension ("early"). Passive rule for ''let'' (retaining ''let''): New Subject ("The prisoner") + auxiliary verb ("was", since the sentence is simple past) + past participle ("let") + bare infinitive ("leave") + extension ("early").

Applying this rule gives us Option (a): "The prisoner was let leave early."

Note on standard grammatical variations: Because "was let leave" sounds awkward to many native speakers, standard English frequently swaps "let" out for its synonym "allow" when making a passive shift. However, "allow" requires a full infinitive with "to" ("The prisoner was allowed to leave early"). Since Option (c) leaves out the word "to" ("was allowed to leave"), it is ungrammatical. Therefore, Option (a) is the only structurally sound choice.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct passive form is option (a).

Quick Tip: Remember that the three principal forms of the verb "let" are identical: let (V1) → let (V2) → let (V3). When converting to passive, keep the secondary action verb bare: "was let leave", not "was let to leave".', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.515Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5556, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Transform the following sentences into Indirect or Direct speech. "Had I known the truth, I would not have spoken so harshly," said Evelyn.', NULL, '[{"text":"Evelyn said that if she had known the truth, she would not have spoken so harshly.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Evelyn regretted having spoken harshly without knowing the truth.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Evelyn said that had she known the truth she hadn''t spoken so harshly.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Evelyn told that she wouldn''t speak harshly if she knew the truth.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '##### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

When reporting conditional sentences in indirect speech, the tense adjustments depend on the type of conditional statement. The given sentence is a Type 3 (impossible/past) conditional, which uses an inverted past perfect structure ("Had I known..." is equivalent to "If I had known..."). In indirect speech, Type 3 conditional sentences do not undergo a tense shift because the past perfect and perfect conditional forms are already at their furthest past limit. Pronouns must be adjusted to match the speaker''s perspective.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the required structural changes step by step: 1. Reporting Verb: "said Evelyn" transforms to "Evelyn said that..." 2. First-Person Pronoun Shifts: The pronoun "I" refers directly to Evelyn, so it changes to the third-person feminine singular pronoun "she". 3. Conditional Clause Conversion: The inverted phrase "Had I known the truth" can be rewritten with its explicit conjunction as "if she had known the truth". 4. Main Clause Tense Retention: The main consequence clause "I would not have spoken so harshly" retains its original perfect conditional tense, changing only the pronoun: "she would not have spoken so harshly".

Combining these elements yields Option (a): "Evelyn said that if she had known the truth, she would not have spoken so harshly." Option (b) paraphrases the sentiment rather than directly transforming the speech structure. Options (c) and (d) introduce incorrect tense modifications to the clauses.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct indirect speech form is option (a).

Quick Tip: An inverted conditional phrase like "Had I known" is just a formal way of saying "If I had known". When converting past perfect or third conditional sentences to indirect speech, leave the tenses exactly as they are!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.516Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5557, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the word most nearly opposite in meaning to the capitalized word. “BILIOUS”', NULL, '[{"text":"Amiable","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Peevish","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Valiant","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Sallow","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Antonym identification requires analyzing the contextual, descriptive, and emotional weight of a given vocabulary word. "BILIOUS" historically relates to gastric distress or an excess of bile, but in modern vocabulary usage, it describes someone who is remarkably bad-tempered, irritable, or constantly cross. Therefore, we must look for an antonym that describes a pleasant, warm, and good-natured temperament.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the definitions of all the options provided: BILIOUS: Irritable, bad-tempered, or extremely cranky. Amiable (Antonym): Displaying a friendly, pleasant, and highly agreeable manner. This is the exact opposite of being irritable and bad-tempered. Peevish (Synonym): Easily irritated, especially by unimportant things. Valiant: Showing courage or determination; brave. Sallow: (Of a person''s face or complexion) of an unhealthy yellow or pale brown color, which is a physical symptom often associated with being bilious.

Because "Amiable" represents a pleasant and friendly disposition, it stands as the correct opposite to the bitter and irritable nature of "Bilious".

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The word most nearly opposite in meaning to BILIOUS is Amiable.

Quick Tip: To remember "bilious," think of "belly ache." Someone with a belly ache is usually cranky, bad-tempered, and irritable! The perfect cure for a cranky person is someone who is friendly and "amiable."', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.516Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5558, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the word most nearly opposite in meaning to the capitalized word. “INSOUCIANT”', NULL, '[{"text":"Flamboyant","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Nonchalant","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Languid","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Anxious","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To find the correct antonym, we first define the core disposition of the prompt word. "INSOUCIANT" describes a state of being completely carefree, unconcerned, or nonchalant. The direct polar opposite of a carefree state of mind is one filled with worry, apprehension, and mental stress.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s break down the definitions of each option: INSOUCIANT: Showing a casual lack of concern; entirely carefree and indifferent. Anxious (Antonym): Experiencing worry, unease, or nervousness, typically about an imminent event or something with an uncertain outcome. This is the precise psychological opposite of being carefree. Nonchalant (Synonym): Feeling or appearing casually calm and relaxed; unconcerned. Languid: Displaying or having a disinclination for physical exertion or effort; slow and relaxed. Flamboyant: Tending to attract attention because of their exuberance, confidence, and stylishness.

Since "Insouciant" implies an absence of anxiety or concern, "Anxious" is its perfect contextual opposite.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The word most nearly opposite in meaning to INSOUCIANT is Anxious.

Quick Tip: The word "insouciant" comes from a French root meaning "not caring." If you are insouciant, you are entirely stress-free. The opposite of a stress-free person is an anxious one!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.517Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5559, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the word most nearly opposite in meaning to the capitalized word. “TANTAMOUNT”', NULL, '[{"text":"Incommensurate","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Commensurate","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Redundant","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Obsolete","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '##### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The word "TANTAMOUNT" is an adjective used to state that two things are virtually equivalent or equal in value, significance, or effect (e.g., "a statement tantamount to an admission of guilt"). To find its opposite, we must identify a word that describes two things that are unequal, disproportionate, or completely out of sync with each other.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the vocabulary choices: TANTAMOUNT: Equivalent in value, significance, or effect; equal to. Incommensurate (Antonym): Out of proportion; unequal or inadequate in size, degree, or value compared to something else. This perfectly opposes the concept of equivalence. Commensurate (Synonym): Corresponding in size, amount, or degree; proportionate or equal. Redundant: Not or no longer needed or useful; superfluous. Obsolete: No longer produced or used; out of date.

Because "Tantamount" indicates that two things equal each other in impact, "Incommensurate" is the correct antonym because it points out a clear disproportion or inequality.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The word most nearly opposite in meaning to TANTAMOUNT is Incommensurate.

Quick Tip: Think of the "Equal" sign (=) for Tantamount and Commensurate. To find the antonym, look for the negative prefix "In-" in Incommensurate, which acts like a "Not Equal" sign (≠)!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.517Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5560, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the word most nearly opposite in meaning to the capitalized word. “PERNICIOUS”', NULL, '[{"text":"Salubrious","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Nefarious","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Innocuous","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Devastating","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '##### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

"PERNICIOUS" is a powerful descriptive adjective that indicates something is highly destructive, deeply harmful, or fatal in a subtle, gradual way (such as a pernicious rumor or a pernicious disease). When seeking its opposite, we are looking for a word that denotes something highly health-giving, wholesome, or beneficial.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s look at the precise definitions of all four options: PERNICIOUS: Having a harmful, destructive, or deadly effect, especially in a gradual or subtle manner. Salubrious (Antonym): Health-giving, beneficial, wholesome, or highly favorable to sanitary well-being. This is the most complete and direct opposite to the life-destroying nature of pernicious. Nefarious (Synonym-adjacent): Wicked, villainous, or criminal in nature. Innocuous: Not harmful or offensive; harmless. While "innocuous" means neutral/harmless, "salubrious" goes a step further by being actively beneficial and health-promoting, making it the strongest and most complete vocabulary antonym for a destructive force. Devastating (Synonym): Highly destructive, damaging, or causing severe shock.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, "Salubrious" stands out as the most precise opposite choice.

The word most nearly opposite in meaning to PERNICIOUS is Salubrious.

Quick Tip: Associate "Pernicious" with "Poisonous" (both start with P and mean harmful). Associate "Salubrious" with "Salute to health" (both start with Sal and mean healthy). This simple letter pairing keeps them clear in your mind!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.518Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5561, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Despite losing his job, he managed to _____ by doing small freelance projects.', NULL, '[{"text":"white elephant","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"eke out a living","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"at sixes and sevens","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Fabian policy","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This sentence requires filling in the blank with an appropriate English idiom that aligns with the context of financial survival. The subject has lost his primary job but is performing small, temporary tasks to sustain himself. The chosen phrase must mean making just enough money to survive or get by with difficulty.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the meanings of all the idiomatic choices: White elephant: A possession that is useless or troublesome, especially one that is expensive to maintain. This does not fit the context of earning money. Eke out a living (Correct): To earn just enough money to survive

or buy life''s necessities, usually with great effort or by supplementing income through small tasks. This perfectly completes the sentence structure logically and grammatically. At sixes and sevens: A state of total confusion or disarray. It does not fit the blank as a verb phrase. Fabian policy: A policy of cautious delay or avoiding direct confrontation. This is entirely irrelevant to financial survival.

Therefore, "eke out a living" is the only option that logically explains how he survived financially after losing his job.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct idiomatic phrase to fill the blank is option (b).

Quick Tip: To "eke out" something means to add to it or stretch it out carefully. Think of stretching your last bit of savings or making small earnings last as long as possible!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.521Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5562, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Hobson''s choice means:', NULL, '[{"text":"A difficult puzzle","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"No real choice at all","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A secret agreement","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Extreme happiness","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Idioms often have fascinating historical origins that explain their current figurative definitions. The phrase "Hobson''s choice" describes a specific situation where an individual is offered a choice that seems optional on the surface, but in reality, there is only one true possibility available: take exactly what is offered or leave it.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The phrase comes from Thomas Hobson (1544–1631), a stable owner in Cambridge, England. Hobson kept a large collection of excellent horses for students and travelers to rent. However,

to ensure his best horses weren''t overworked, he gave his customers a strict rule: they could either take the horse in the stall nearest the stable door or take no horse at all. Therefore, even though customers thought they had a selection, they actually had no real choice at all. This perfectly matches Option (b).

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The meaning of Hobson''s choice is option (b).

Quick Tip: Remember the simple rule of Hobson''s stable: "Take this one option, or take nothing at all!" It''s an illusion of choice.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.521Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5563, 1, NULL, NULL, 'The abandoned airport project became a _____ for the government.', NULL, '[{"text":"white elephant","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"cortege","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"discourse","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"brevity","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '##### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This sentence context describes a major public infrastructure venture ("airport project") that has been abandoned. For a government, such an idle, expensive asset represents a massive drain on financial resources without providing any practical public utility. The missing vocabulary item must be an idiom that characterizes a burdensome, useless, and costly possession.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the definition and suitability of each option: White elephant (Correct): An idiom used to describe a possession that is completely useless, unwanted, or unprofitable, especially one that is extremely expensive to maintain or build. An abandoned infrastructure project fits this description perfectly. Cortege: A solemn procession, especially for a funeral. This is

completely irrelevant to an engineering or financial project. Discourse: Written or spoken communication or debate. This does not fit the financial context. Brevity: Concise and exact use of words in writing or speech; shortness of time. This does not make grammatical or contextual sense here.

Thus, "white elephant" is the only option that logically describes the government''s financial burden.

### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct idiomatic phrase to fill the blank is option (a).

Quick Tip: The term "white elephant" comes from an old custom where the King of Siam would give a rare white elephant to courtiers he disliked. The cost of feeding and housing the sacred beast was so high that it would financially ruin the recipient!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.522Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5564, 1, NULL, NULL, 'A long procession, especially for a funeral, is called a:', NULL, '[{"text":"philistine","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"cortege","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"itinerary","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"discourse","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests single-word substitutions for descriptive phrases. We need to identify a specific collective noun or term that directly defines a formal, organized group of people or vehicles moving along together during a funeral service.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s examine the dictionary definitions of all four vocabulary options: Philistine: A person who is hostile or indifferent to culture, the arts, or refined intellect. Cortege (Correct): A formal, slow-moving procession, specifically one accompanying a funeral, consisting of mourners,

vehicles, or a casket. This perfectly matches the prompt''s definition. Itinerary: A detailed plan or route proposed for a journey or trip. Discourse: A formal, structured conversation, speech, or piece of writing on a specific subject.

Therefore, "cortege" is the precise lexical term for a funeral procession.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct one-word substitution is option (b).

Quick Tip: Think of a "cortege" as a formal, slow-moving column of vehicles or people accompanying a funeral carriage. It shares historical roots with the word "court," implying a structured, formal escort.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.522Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5565, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the pair of antonyms.', NULL, '[{"text":"adroit – skillful","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"verbose - lengthy","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"corpulent – emaciated","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"philately – stamp collection","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This problem requires evaluating the semantic relationship between pairs of words. While three of the options present pairs of synonyms or direct definitions, one pair consists of words with completely opposite meanings (antonyms). We must verify each relationship to isolate the antonym pair.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the relationships in each pair: Option (a): Adroit means clever or skillful in using the hands or mind. Therefore, adroit and skillful are synonyms. Option (b): Verbose means using or expressed in more words than are needed, which is equivalent to being lengthy. Therefore, these are synonyms. Option (c): Corpulent means excessively fat, large, or bulky. On the other hand, emaciated means abnormally thin or weak, especially because of illness or

a lack of food. Because one means excessively fat and the other means abnormally thin, they are antonyms. This satisfies the question requirement. Option (d): Philately is the study or collection of postage stamps. This is a word paired with its direct definition.

Thus, option (c) is the only pair that represents opposites.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The pair of antonyms is option (c).

Quick Tip: To remember "corpulent," think of "corps/corpus," which means body—someone who has a lot of "body" is fat. To remember "emaciated," focus on the "mac" root, which sounds like "meager" or lacking mass!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.523Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5566, 1, NULL, NULL, 'An _____ speaker can explain difficult ideas very clearly and skillfully.', NULL, '[{"text":"itinerant","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"adroit","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"emaciated","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"cantankerous","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This context requires selecting a descriptive adjective that characterizes a person''s speaking ability, specifically their skillfulness and clarity when handling complex topics. The missing vocabulary word must denote high competence, cleverness, or dexterity in performance.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s evaluate the definitions of all four options to find the correct match: Itinerant: Traveling from place to place. This describes a physical movement pattern, not a communication skill. Adroit (Correct): Clever, adept, or highly skillful in using the hands or mind. When used to describe a speaker, it means they are exceptionally skilled at structuring arguments and explaining complex points smoothly. Emaciated: Abnormally thin or weak, especially because

of illness or a lack of food. This is a physical condition and is completely irrelevant here. Cantankerous: Bad-tempered, argumentative, and uncooperative. A cantankerous speaker would be hostile rather than clear and skillful.

Therefore, "adroit" is the only adjective that logically and grammatically fits the blank to complete the description.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct word to fill the blank is option (b).

Quick Tip: To remember "adroit," think of the word "add right." An adroit person adds the right skills to any task to get it done cleverly and smoothly!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.523Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5567, 1, NULL, NULL, '“Brevity” means:', NULL, '[{"text":"Loudness","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Intelligence","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Shortness in speech or writing","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Confusion","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests direct lexical definitions. The abstract noun "Brevity" deals with properties of language, communication, or time duration. We must identify its core meaning from the multiple-choice selection.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The noun "Brevity" comes from the Latin root brevis, which means short (the same root that gives us words like brief and abbreviate).

In English, it is used to define: 1. Concise and exact use of words in writing or speech. 2. The quality of lasting for a very short duration of time.

Looking at our choices, Option (c) ("Shortness in speech or writing") matches this definition.

perfectly. It is famously referenced in William Shakespeare''s play Hamlet through the quote, "Brevity is the soul of wit," meaning a good point should be made using as few words as possible.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct definition of Brevity is option (c).

Quick Tip: Link "Brevity" directly to the word "Brief." Both share the exact same root and denote keeping things short and directly to the point!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.524Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5568, 1, NULL, NULL, 'The professor''s explanation was so _____ that students lost interest quickly.', NULL, '[{"text":"verbose","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"adroit","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"taciturn","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"concise","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The clue in this sentence is the cause-and-effect relationship: "students lost interest quickly." This indicates that the professor''s lecture style had a negative quality—likely being too long, tedious, or repetitive. The missing word must describe a style of speaking or writing that uses far more words than necessary.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the vocabulary options: Verbose (Correct): Using or expressed in more words than are needed; wordy and repetitive. A verbose explanation easily becomes boring, causing students to lose interest, which fits the sentence perfectly. Adroit: Skillful or clever. A skillful explanation would keep students engaged rather than bored. Taciturn: Reserved or uncommunicative in speech; saying very little. A professor cannot give a long explanation if they are taciturn. Concise: Giving a lot of information clearly and in a few words. This is a positive quality that prevents boredom.

Thus, "verbose" is the only word that logically explains why the students became bored and disconnected.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct word to fill the blank is option (a).

Quick Tip: Think of "Verbose" as being packed with "Verbs" and words! It describes someone who talks endlessly in circles instead of getting straight to the point.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.525Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5569, 1, NULL, NULL, 'A person who travels from place to place is known as:', NULL, '[{"text":"itinerant","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"emaciated","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"sagacious","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"corpulent","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This problem requires identifying a single-word substitution for a specific behavioral lifestyle: traveling continuously from location to location, often for work or trade (such as itinerant preachers or itinerant laborers).

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s review the definitions of each option to find the correct fit: Itinerant (Correct): Traveling from place to place; wandering or unfixed. It comes from the Latin word iter, meaning a journey (the same root behind itinerary). This matches the definition exactly. Emaciated: Abnormally thin or weak due to illness or lack of food. Sagacious: Having or showing keen mental discernment and good judgment; wise. Corpulent: Excessively fat or bulky in body composition.

Because options (b), (c), and (d) describe physical or intellectual traits rather than movement, "itinerant" is the correct choice.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct one-word substitution is option (a).

Quick Tip: Connect the word "Itinerant" to your travel "Itinerary." Since an itinerary is a travel plan, an itinerant person is someone whose lifestyle is constantly defined by an itinerary!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.525Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5570, 1, NULL, NULL, 'RECONDITE', NULL, '[{"text":"Manifest","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Arcane","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Pedantic","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Cryptic","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Vocabulary tests require analyzing the precise semantic definitions of words, often focusing on advanced literary, academic, or GRE-level terms. The word "recondite" is an adjective used to describe knowledge or subjects that are deep, hidden from view, and difficult for an ordinary person to comprehend.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the precise meanings of the given options: RECONDITE: (Of a subject or knowledge) little known; abstruse, obscure, or hidden from mental view. Manifest (Antonym): Clear, obvious, or easily perceived by the eye or mind. Arcane (Synonym): Understood by few;

mysterious, secret, or obscure. This perfectly matches the core meaning of "recondite" as both refer to specialized, hidden knowledge. Pedantic: Excessively concerned with minor details or rules; displaying academic learning in an annoying way. Cryptic: Having a meaning that is mysterious, obscure, or written in a hidden code (usually applied to messages, expressions, or puzzles rather than fields of knowledge).

Thus, "Arcane" is the most accurate synonym for "Recondite".

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The closest synonym to RECONDITE is Arcane (Option b).

Quick Tip: To remember both words, link them together in a phrase describing specialized academic fields: "Recondite philosophies and arcane rituals." Both words share the prefix-vibe of being hidden away in a secret archive!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.530Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5571, 1, NULL, NULL, 'PULVERULENT', NULL, '[{"text":"Dusty or crumbling","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Extremely powerful","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Richly decorated","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Wet and marshy","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Using etymological roots (the origin of words) can help decode rare vocabulary words. The adjective "pulverulent" originates from the Latin root word pulvis or pulver-, which directly translates to "dust". Understanding this root connection makes it easy to isolate the correct definition.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s evaluate the definitions of the options provided: PULVERULENT: Consisting of or covered with fine powder or dust; crumbling easily into fine particles. Extremely powerful: Described by words like potent, omnipotent, or mighty. Richly decorated: Described by words like ornate,

baroque, or flamboyant. Wet and marshy: Described by words like miry, swampy, or boggy. Since the word is directly linked to dust and powder formations, option (a) is the accurate semantic definition.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

PULVERULENT means dusty or crumbling (Option a).

Quick Tip: Connect this word to a common English verb you already know: Pulverize (which means to crush something into tiny, fine particles or dust). A substance that has been pulverized becomes pulverulent!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.531Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5572, 1, NULL, NULL, 'TURPID', NULL, '[{"text":"Foul or depraved","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Swollen and distended","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Muddy and cloudy","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Lethargic","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Advanced English vocabulary tests often include words that look and sound nearly identical but have completely distinct definitions (homophones or near-homophones). The word "turpid" must be carefully distinguished from its close linguistic relatives: turgid (swollen) and turbid (muddy).

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s look at the definitions of the word and clear up any confusion with similar terms: TURPID: Viscous, foul, base, or morally depraved. It is directly related to the noun "turpitude" (as in moral turpitude, which refers to wicked or shameful behavior). Therefore, "foul or depraved" is the correct definition. Swollen and distended: This is the definition for Turgid (or tumid). Muddy and cloudy: This is the definition for Turbid (used to describe thick, cloudy liquids or

muddy water containing stirred-up sediment). Lethargic: This is closely associated with Torpid (meaning mentally or physically inactive; sluggish).

By clarifying these subtle differences, we can confidently choose option (a).

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

TURPID means foul or depraved (Option a).

Quick Tip: Keep these four similar-looking words organized in your mind with a quick memory chart:

• Turpid → Depraved (Think: Moral turpitude)

• Turbid  $ \rightarrow $ Muddy (Think: Turbulence in water)

• Turgid → Swollen (Think: Turgor pressure in plant cells)

• Torpid → Sluggish (Think: Inactive/dormant like a torpedo at rest)', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.532Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5573, 1, NULL, NULL, 'AVUNCULAR', NULL, '[{"text":"Kind and friendly like an uncle","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Greedy and predatory","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Pertaining to birds","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Ancestral or ancient","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Advanced English vocabulary contains specialized adjectives derived from Latin roots that describe behaviors characteristic of specific family relationships. The word "avuncular" comes directly from the Latin noun avunculus, which translates to "maternal uncle".

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the semantic definitions of all the options: AVUNCULAR: Kind, warm, patient, and friendly, specifically reminiscent of the supportive, non-disciplinary relationship an uncle typically shares with his nieces or nephews. Greedy and predatory: Described by adjectives

like rapacious, voracious, or predatory. Pertaining to birds: Described by the adjective Avian. (A common trap due to the similar starting letters ''av-''). Ancestral or ancient: Described by adjectives like atavistic, hereditary, or ancient.

Therefore, option (a) perfectly captures the exact dictionary definition of the word.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

AVUNCULAR means kind and friendly like an uncle (Option a).

Quick Tip: To build a stronger relational vocabulary, group "avuncular" with these other family-derived adjectives:

• Avuncular → Like an uncle

• Patrilineal / Paternal → Like a father

• Matrilineal / Maternal → Like a mother

• Fraternal → Like a brother

• Sororal → Like a sister', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.532Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5574, 1, NULL, NULL, 'The unexpected loss of funding meant they had to ___ their expansion plans for the upcoming quarter.', NULL, '[{"text":"scale back","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"scale up","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"phase out","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"cast aside","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Sentence completion questions rely on recognizing contextual clues to determine the logical tone of a sentence. In this sentence, the key constraint is a negative event: an "unexpected loss of funding". This structural constraint dictates that the missing phrasal verb must describe a reduction, compromise, or retraction of growth plans.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s evaluate how each phrasal verb fits the financial context of the sentence: Scale back (Correct): To reduce the size, scope, or extent of an operation or plan. If an organization suddenly loses money, the most logical immediate step is to downsize or reduce their growth targets for that quarter. Scale up (Antonym): To increase the size or scope of something. It is illogical to increase expansion when you lack funds. Phase out: To gradually bring something to an end or discontinue it in systematic stages. This is typically used for products or long-standing policies, not immediate quarterly growth schedules. Cast aside: To discard or reject something completely because it is no longer wanted. While it sounds plausible, it implies total emotional or physical abandonment, whereas "scale back" is the standard business terminology for managing budget deficits.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct phrasal verb to fill the blank is "scale back" (Option a).

Quick Tip: Think of the word "scale" as a mechanical volume knob or a slider on a map. When you lose resources, you must slide the knob downward, which means you scale back. When you gain resources, you slide it upward, which means you scale up!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.533Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5575, 1, NULL, NULL, 'PERNICIOUS', NULL, '[{"text":"Salubrious","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Nefarious","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Innocuous","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Devastating","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Analyzing structural descriptions in texts involves understanding words that convey harm or damage. The word "pernicious" is an impactful adjective used to describe things that cause serious harm, injury, or ruin, often in a quiet, subtle, or hidden manner over a long period.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s look at the definitions of all the choices to find the closest semantic match: PERNICIOUS: Having a harmful effect, especially in a gradual or subtle way; highly destructive, fatal, or ruinous. Salubrious (Antonym): Health-giving; healthy or pleasant. Nefarious: Wicked, villainous, or criminal (typically used to describe actions, people, or plots rather than general damaging effects). Innocuous (Antonym): Not harmful or offensive; safe. Devastating (Synonym): Highly destructive, damaging, or overwhelming. Out of all the choices, "devastating" is the closest operational match to describe the severe, ruinous impact implied by "pernicious".

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The closest synonym to PERNICIOUS is Devastating (Option d).

Quick Tip: A classic medical and literary phrase to keep memorized is "pernicious anemia" or "pernicious rumors". These are threats that sneak up quietly but have a devastating or highly destructive outcome if left unchecked!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.533Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5576, 1, NULL, NULL, 'During the trial, the defense attorney used expert testimonies to ___ the claims made by the prosecution''s star witness.', NULL, '[{"text":"bear out","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"bear down","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"cave in","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"hold up","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Sentence completion questions involving phrasal verbs require analyzing the context and relationship between the subjects. Here, a defense attorney is using "expert testimonies" in relation to "claims". In a legal setting, evidence or testimony is introduced either to support/confirm a claim or to refute it. We must look at the definitions of the phrasal verbs to

see which one logically fits this context.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the meaning of each phrasal verb: Bear out (Correct): To support, confirm, substantiate, or corroborate a statement or claim. If the expert testimonies matched or supported the defense''s perspective of the witness''s claims (or supported the defense''s own claims about them), "bear out" fits perfectly as a term meaning to substantiate. Bear down: To press down heavily, exert maximum effort, or move threateningly toward someone. This does not fit the context of managing arguments or testimonies. Cave in: To collapse inward, or to yield/submit under pressure. This is an action a person takes when giving up, not a function of a testimony. Hold up: While "hold up" can mean to remain valid or stand up to scrutiny (e.g., "The claims didn''t hold up"), it is an intransitive verb phrase in that context. You cannot actively "hold up the claims" to mean confirm them in standard legal vocabulary testing the way you can "bear them out".

Therefore, "bear out" is the most appropriate phrasal verb.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct phrasal verb to fill the blank is "bear out".

Quick Tip: Think of "bear out" as meaning "to carry out the truth" of something. Whenever you see a sentence where evidence, facts, data, or testimonies confirm a theory or statement, "bear out" is the standard phrase to use!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.534Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5577, 1, NULL, NULL, 'She was so exhausted by the grueling schedule that she felt ready to ___ under the sheer pressure.', NULL, '[{"text":"cave in","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"buckle up","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"give out","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"tire out","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The context of this sentence highlights a person experiencing extreme exhaustion due to a "grueling schedule" and facing "sheer pressure". The preposition "under" immediately following the blank is a critical structural clue. We need a phrasal verb that naturally pairs with "under" to mean collapsing, surrendering, or breaking down due to mental or physical stress.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s look at how the options interact with the word "under": Cave in (Correct): To collapse inward physically, or figuratively to yield, surrender, or break down under pressure. The phrase "cave in under pressure" is a idiomatic expression in English. Buckle up: To fasten one''s seatbelt in a vehicle. (If the option were "buckle under", it could mean to yield, but "buckle up under" is incorrect). Give out: To break down, stop functioning, or be exhausted (e.g., "Her legs gave out"). While it matches the exhaustion theme, it does not grammatically take the preposition "under" in this manner (you don''t say "give out under the pressure" to mean surrender, you just say "give out"). Tire out: To make someone completely exhausted. This is a transitive verb phrase usually requiring an object (e.g., "The schedule tired her out"), and it is redundant when "exhausted" is already used in the sentence.

Thus, "cave in" is the grammatically and contextually perfect selection.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct phrasal verb to fill the blank is "cave in".

Quick Tip: Visualize a structural cave-off. When a tunnel or ceiling has too much weight or pressure placed on top of it, it collapses inward—it caves in. People do the same thing mentally when they are overwhelmed by stress!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.534Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5578, 1, NULL, NULL, 'He promised to ___ the discrepancies in the ledger before the auditors arrived.', NULL, '[{"text":"iron out","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"clear off","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"gloss over","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"wipe out","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The context involves a "ledger" containing "discrepancies" (errors, inconsistencies, or mismatches) that need to be dealt with before an official review by "auditors". To satisfy auditors, accounting errors must be actively resolved, corrected, or smoothed out so that everything balances correctly.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the figurative meanings of the choices: Iron out (Correct): To resolve, smooth over, eliminate, or find a solution for minor problems, errors, or discrepancies. Just as an iron removes wrinkles from fabric to make it smooth, "ironing out discrepancies" means fixing accounting errors so the ledger is smooth and accurate. Clear off: To remove items from a surface (e.g., "clear off the table") or to pay off a debt. It does not mean solving specific line-item discrepancies. Gloss over: To deliberately ignore, conceal, or treat an error lightly to avoid trouble. While a dishonest accountant might try to do this before auditors arrive, "promising" to do so usually implies a constructive action to rectify errors rather than an act of active concealment, making (a) the standard professional expression tested here. Wipe out: To completely destroy or erase something. You cannot simply erase portions of an official ledger without creating more auditing issues.

Thus, "iron out" is the most precise and idiomatic choice.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct phrasal verb to fill the blank is "iron out".

Quick Tip: Think of any problem, misunderstanding, or mathematical discrepancy as an ugly wrinkle on a clean shirt. To make the situation perfect and professional, you need to use your tools to iron out those wrinkles!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.535Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5579, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the word closest in meaning to “ebullient”.', NULL, '[{"text":"Gloomy","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Cheerful and enthusiastic","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Silent","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Careless","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Vocabulary questions checking for the "closest in meaning" require identifying a direct synonym. The word "ebullient" is an adjective derived from the Latin root ebullire, which literally translates to "boiling over" or "bubbling up". In contemporary English usage, it figuratively describes a person''s behavior, mood, or energy levels.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the precise definitions of all the options provided: EBULLIENT: Overflowing with fervor, enthusiasm, energy, or high spirits. It behaves like a liquid that is joyfully bubbling over. Gloomy (Antonym): Dark, dim, depressed, or deeply sad. Cheerful and enthusiastic

(Synonym): Full of visible happiness, high energy, and eagerness. This perfectly aligns with the definition of ebullient. Silent: Refusing to speak or making no audible sound. Careless: Not giving sufficient attention or thought to avoiding harm or errors. Therefore, option (b) is the exact synonym choice.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The word closest in meaning to ebullient is Cheerful and enthusiastic (Option b).

Quick Tip: To remember this word, think of it as sharing a root concept with a boiling pot of water (e-bull-ient). Just like a boiling liquid has high energy and bubbles up, an ebullient individual is bubbling over with cheerful and enthusiastic energy!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.537Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5580, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the word opposite in meaning to “disdain”.', NULL, '[{"text":"Respect","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Anger","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Pride","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Doubt","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Antonym questions require tracking down a word that carries the inverse semantic value of the prompt. The word "disdain" operates as both a noun and a verb, representing a total lack of admiration, value, or esteem toward an object, person, or concept deemed inferior.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s look at the definitions to contrast the target word with the multiple choice options:

DISDAIN: The feeling that someone or something is unworthy of one''s consideration, respect, or honor; contempt, scorn, or looking down on someone.

Respect (Antonym): A feeling of deep admiration for someone or something elicited by their abilities, qualities, or achievements. Because this is the direct opposite of viewing someone with worthless contempt, it is the perfect

antonym. Anger: A strong feeling of annoyance, displeasure, or hostility. Pride: A feeling of deep pleasure or satisfaction derived from one''s own achievements or possessions. Doubt: A feeling of uncertainty or lack of conviction.

Thus, "Respect" is the correct opposite.

### Step 3: Final Answer:

The word opposite in meaning to disdain is Respect (Option a).

Quick Tip: Think of the prefix Dis- as indicating a negative direction or reversal. When you display disdain toward someone, you strip away all credit and treat them with zero worth. The ultimate positive inversion of treating someone with value and high regard is showing them respect!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.538Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5581, 1, NULL, NULL, 'A person who collects stamps is called a:', NULL, '[{"text":"Numismatist","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Archaeologist","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Philatelist","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Linguist","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This problem belongs to the "One Word Substitution" category, which evaluates knowledge of specialized terminology used to categorize professions, hobbies, and academic fields of expertise.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s define each professional term to trace their respective disciplines: Numismatist: A person who collects, studies, or examines coins, tokens, paper currency, and medals. Archaeologist: A scientist who studies human history and prehistory through the excavation of historical sites and the analysis of physical artifacts. Philatelist (Correct): A person who collects, studies, or values postage stamps, postmarks, and related postal history materials. The term comes from

the Greek roots philos (loving) and ateleia (exemption from tax/prepaid post). Linguist: A person who specializes in the scientific study of human language and its structural or historical developments.

This confirms that choice (c) is the correct term.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

A person who collects stamps is called a Philatelist (Option c).

Quick Tip: Keep this essential pair locked down together in your vocabulary review logs to score easy marks on competitive exams:

• Philatelist = Collects and studies postage stamps.

• Numismatist = Collects and studies currency and coins.

These two hobby terms are among the most frequently tested items in competitive exams!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.538Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5582, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correctly spelt word.', NULL, '[{"text":"Deliterious","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Deleterious","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Deletearious","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Delaterious","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Spelling tests assess knowledge of standard English prefixes and suffix roots. The target word is an advanced formal adjective meaning causing harm or damage. It originates from the classical Greek word délêtios (meaning noxious or destructive).

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s break down the precise syllabic construction of the correct term: The word is assembled in four distinct vocal beats: De - le - te - ri - ous. Notice that the first three syllables are consistently

anchored by the vowel character ''e'': De- (1st), -le- (2nd), and -te- (3rd). It concludes with the standard structural descriptive adjective suffix "-ous". Combining these phonetic blocks gives the layout: D-E-L-E-T-E-R-I-O-U-S.

Let''s review the breakdown errors in the other choices: Option (a) incorrectly swaps the second ''e'' for an ''i'' (Deliterious). Option (c) introduces an incorrect letter ''a'' before the suffix (Deletearious). Option (d) incorrectly substitutes an ''a'' in the second syllable (Delaterious). Hence, option (b) is the only authentic spelling.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correctly spelt word is Deleterious (Option b).

Quick Tip: To remember this word effortlessly, link it directly to the key function "Delete"! If something is deleterious, it is harmful enough that you would want to delete it entirely from your surroundings. Both share the exact same starting block: D-E-L-E-T-E!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.538Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5583, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Fill in the blank with the correct option:

The church bells began to ___ loudly at noon.', NULL, '[{"text":"peel","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"peal","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"pale","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"poll","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This problem evaluates your command over homophones—words that sound identical when spoken aloud but possess completely distinct meanings, origins, and spelling structures. Resolving the blank requires analyzing the context clue of the sentence ("church bells") to locate the correct verb association.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s look at the operational definitions of all four homophones to find the proper fit: peel: To strip or remove the outer skin, rind, or covering layer from something (e.g., peeling an orange or a potato). This is completely unrelated to sound. peal (Correct): To ring or sound out loudly and continuously. As a noun or verb, it specifically refers to a loud, ringing chime of bells or a sudden echoing crash of thunder (e.g., a peal of thunder, bells pealing). This fits the context perfectly. pale: Lacking intensity of color, headings, or light-colored; or a boundary stake. poll: A record of voting, an election survey, or to cut off the horns/top of something. Therefore, "peal" is the uniquely accurate option.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The complete sentence is: "The church bells began to peal loudly at noon." (Option b)

Quick Tip: To separate these two easily confused words, anchor them to unique visual rhymes:

• You p-e-e-l a banana to e-a-t it.

• A bell will p-e-a-l out an a-u-d-i-o ring.

Connecting the ''a'' in peal to the ''a'' in audio ensures you pick the correct acoustic term every single time!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.539Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5584, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct meaning of “petulance”.', NULL, '[{"text":"Deep wisdom","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Childish irritability","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Great courage","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Honest behaviour","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Vocabulary questions checking definitions test your knowledge of formal nouns that describe emotional states and behavioral attitudes. The noun "petulance" tracks a specific negative

temperament, often linked to an impatient or moody reaction when an individual fails to get their own way.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the precise meaning of the word alongside the given options: PETULANCE: The quality of being childishly sulky, bad-tempered, bad-mannered, or easily annoyed over trivial matters. It mimics the display of an impatient, angry tantrum. Deep wisdom: An invalid definition; wisdom represents mature discernment and deep understanding, which is the complete opposite of petulance. Childish irritability (Correct): This matches the definition exactly. A petulant person shows sudden, unwarranted annoyance over minor inconveniences, much like a stubborn child throwing a tantrum. Great courage: An invalid choice; courage is bravery in the face of fear or pain. Honest behaviour: An invalid choice; honesty deals with integrity, truthfulness, and transparency.

This confirms that choice (b) is the unique definition matching the term.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct meaning of petulance is Childish irritability (Option b).

Quick Tip: To keep this definition locked in your memory, think of a spoiled, demanding pet throwing a noisy tantrum when it doesn''t get its treats on time! Connecting Pet-ulance to a sulky, annoyed pet will instantly remind you of childish irritability!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.539Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5585, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Select the sentence with the correct usage of “furtherance”.', NULL, '[{"text":"He spoke in furtherance of the new policy.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"She furtherance the work yesterday.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The furtherance was quickly running.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"They are furtherance to school.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To identify the correct usage of a word, we must understand its definition and its grammatical category (part of speech). The word "furtherance" is an abstract noun that means the advancement, promotion, or fostering of a scheme, plan, interest, or policy. It is standardly used within the idiomatic prepositional phrase "in furtherance of [something]".

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the syntax and grammatical function of the word across the given choices:

Option (a) [Correct]: "He spoke in furtherance of the new policy." Here, "furtherance" functions accurately as a noun inside the prepositional block "in furtherance of," meaning he spoke to help advance or promote the new policy. This is completely correct. Option (b) [Incorrect]: "She furtherance the work yesterday." Here, the word is incorrectly forced into acting as a past-tense verb. The correct verb would be furthered ("She furthered the work..."). Option (c) [Incorrect]: "The furtherance was quickly running." This sentence treats "furtherance" as a concrete noun capable of physical motion (running), which makes no semantic sense. Option (d) [Incorrect]: "They are furtherance to school." This sentence completely violates basic grammar by substituting a noun where a continuous action verb like "going or walking is required.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct sentence is: "He spoke in furtherance of the new policy." (Option a)

Quick Tip: To easily master this word, remember that furtherance is a formal noun that almost always appears in the exact structural format: "in furtherance of + [a goal, cause, or plan]!" Spotting this standard phrase framework will instantly lead you to the right choice on any vocabulary exam!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.540Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5586, 1, NULL, NULL, 'What is a hamlet?', NULL, '[{"text":"A large kingdom","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A type of poem","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A small village","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A musical instrument","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This vocabulary problem assesses knowledge of nouns that specify geographical human settlements based on structural scale, size, and population density.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s define the terms to see how they match up: A large kingdom: An extensive realm or territory ruled entirely by a monarch (king or queen). A type of poem: Irrelevant; while Hamlet is the title of a legendary tragedy written by William Shakespeare, the common noun "Hamlet" is not a poetic form. A small village (Correct): In human geography, a hamlet is a very small human settlement that is structurally smaller than a standard village. Historically, a hamlet is distinguished by lacking its own dedicated parish church or marketplace, often consisting of just a tiny cluster of houses. A musical instrument: An incorrect option with no connection to the word.

Therefore, choice (c) is the accurate definition.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

A hamlet is a small village (Option c).

Quick Tip: Think of the descending hierarchy of human settlements to easily keep these size terms organized in your mind:

 $$ \mathrm{C i t y}\to\mathrm{T o w n}\to\mathrm{V i l l a g e}\to\mathrm{H a m l e t} $$ 

A hamlet sits at the very bottom of the scale as a cozy, tiny cluster of houses out in the countryside!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.540Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5587, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the figure of speech in the expression: “Deafening silence”', NULL, '[{"text":"Hyperbole","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Metaphor","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Oxymoron","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Personification","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Figures of speech are literary devices that use language in non-literal ways to create emphasis, imagery, or dramatic effect. When two entirely contradictory or opposite terms are deliberately placed side-by-side to highlight a striking paradox, the literary device is called an oxymoron.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the mechanics of the target phrase alongside the definitions of the literary figures: Hyperbole: An extreme exaggeration used to add emphasis or humor, not a contradiction (e.g., "I am so hungry I could eat a horse"). Metaphor: A direct comparison between two unrelated things without using comparison words like "like" or "as" (e.g., "Time is a thief"). Oxymoron (Correct): A literary device that couples two completely opposing or conflicting ideas next to each other. In the phrase "Deafening silence", the adjective deafening implies an intensely loud noise, while the noun silence means a total absence of sound. Placing them together emphasizes an intense, heavy, or uncomfortable quietness. Personification: Attributing human characteristics, emotions, or behaviors to non-human objects or abstract ideas (e.g., "The wind whispered through the trees").

This confirms that the phrase is an oxymoron.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The figure of speech used in the expression is an Oxymoron (Option c).

Quick Tip: Whenever you spot a two-word description where the two words completely fight against one another''s literal definitions, you are looking at an Oxymoron! Keep these famous examples in mind for easy recognition:

• Deafening silence

• Open secret

Seriously funny

• Bittersweet memories', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.541Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5588, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Read the passage and answer the question.

Riya loved reading adventure stories. Every weekend, she visited the library and borrowed two new books. Her friends often asked her for recommendations because she always selected exciting novels.

Why did Riya''s friends ask her for book recommendations?', NULL, '[{"text":"She wrote books herself","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"She worked in a library","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"She usually chose interesting books","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"She read books in a library","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Reading comprehension questions evaluate your ability to identify explicit facts and causal relationships directly stated within a given text. To find the correct answer, look for explicit textual clues like "because" or "since" that link an action to its root cause.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s locate the exact sentence in the passage that explains the behavior of Riya''s friends: The passage states: "Her friends often asked her for recommendations because she always selected exciting novels." The word "because" marks the direct cause behind their requests. The text explicitly tells us that her friends valued her taste because she always picked out "exciting novels". Let''s check how the options match this fact: Option (a) claims she wrote books, which is never mentioned in the passage. Option (b) claims she worked in a library, but the text only says she visited the library as a regular reader. Option (c) states that she usually chose interesting books. The word "interesting" is a direct, accurate synonym for the word "exciting" used in the text.

Therefore, option (c) is the only factually correct choice supported by the text.

### Step 3: Final Answer:

Riya''s friends asked her for book recommendations because she usually chose interesting books (Option c).

Quick Tip: In reading comprehension, remember this golden rule: Stick strictly to the provided text! Avoid making outside guesses or assumptions. Even if an option sounds plausible (like working in a library), if it isn''t explicitly written in the passage, it is incorrect. Always trace the "because" keyword to find your answer!', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.542Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5589, 1, NULL, NULL, '"Beat a dead horse" means:', NULL, '[{"text":"Continue useless effort","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Repeat an already settled matter","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Encourage someone","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Waste time on something hopeless","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question requires us to determine the correct idiomatic meaning of the common phrase "beat a dead horse".

We must select the option that best captures the figurative essence of the expression.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Literal Origin: The phrase "beat a dead horse" (or "flog a dead horse") originates from the literal, futile act of beating a horse that is already dead to try to make it pull a load. Because a deceased animal cannot move, any effort spent beating it is entirely useless and a waste of physical energy.

• Figurative Meaning: In modern English usage, this idiom is used metaphorically to describe a situation where someone continues to expend time, energy, or resources on an issue that has already been resolved, cannot be changed, or is completely hopeless.

• Evaluating Option (A): "Continue useless effort" is a general description of the idiom but lacks the specific context of dealing with a completed or hopeless matter.

• Evaluating Option (B): "Repeat an already settled matter" represents one common scenario of this idiom, particularly when someone brings up an argument that has already been decided.

• Evaluating Option (D): "Waste time on something hopeless" is the most comprehensive definition of the idiom, as it captures both the futility and the complete lack of hope associated with the action.

• Evaluating Option (C): "Encourage someone" is entirely unrelated to the concept of uselessness or waste of effort and is therefore incorrect.

Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the most accurate overall meaning is (D) Waste time on something hopeless.

Quick Tip: Visualize the literal scenario of the idiom to deduce its meaning.

Since beating a horse that is already dead can never produce any helpful result, the idiom naturally represents doing something completely hopeless and wasteful.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.547Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5590, 1, NULL, NULL, '"Stand out" means:', NULL, '[{"text":"Be noticeable","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Be hidden","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Be prominent","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Be ordinary","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This question tests the understanding of the phrasal verb "stand out".

We need to find the option that correctly explains what it means when an object or person stands out.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Literal and Figurative Definition: The phrasal verb "stand out" means to project outward or to be extremely easy to see or notice because of being different, better, or more distinctive than other surrounding elements.

• Analyzing Option (A) ''Be noticeable'': This is the primary and most common meaning. When someone or something stands out, they draw attention and are easily noticed by observers.

• Analyzing Option (B) ''Be hidden'': This is the direct antonym of standing out, as hiding involves blending in or escaping notice.

• Analyzing Option (C) ''Be prominent'': "Be prominent" is also a strong synonym that is correct in many contexts. However, "be noticeable" is the core, everyday meaning of the phrase that is widely accepted in basic definitions.

• Analyzing Option (D) ''Be ordinary'': Being ordinary is the opposite of standing out, as standing out implies having an extraordinary, unique, or distinct quality that differentiates oneself from the crowd.

Step 3: Final Answer:

Thus, the phrasal verb "stand out" is correctly defined as (A) Be noticeable.

Quick Tip: To verify the meaning of a phrasal verb, substitute the option directly into a sentence. For example: "Her bright yellow jacket made her stand out in the crowd" is equivalent to "Her bright yellow jacket made her noticeable in the crowd."', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.548Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5591, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correctly spelled words.', NULL, '[{"text":"Mischievous","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Mischievous","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Entrepreneur","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Enterpreneur","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The task is to identify the correctly spelled words from the given list.

This question tests spelling knowledge of words that are frequently misspelled in written

English due to pronunciation errors or foreign origins.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Analyzing Option (A) and (B): The word meaning playfully troublesome or harmful is spelled "mischievous" (m-i-s-c-h-i-e-v-o-u-s). Many people mistakenly pronounce this word as having four syllables ("mis-chee-vee-us"), which leads to the common incorrect spelling "mischievious" with an extra "i". Thus, Option (A) is correct and (B) is incorrect.

• Analyzing Option (C) and (D): The word for an individual who starts a business, taking on financial risks, is spelled "entrepreneur" (e-n-t-r-e-p-r-e-n-e-u-r). Because it is a French loanword, it is highly prone to misspelling. Option (D) "Enterpreneur" contains spelling errors at the beginning ("enter" instead of "entre") and end ("nuer" instead of "neur"). Thus, Option (C) is correct.

- Conclusion: Both Option (A) and Option (C) represent correctly spelled words.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correctly spelled words are (A) Mischievous and (C) Entrepreneur.

Quick Tip: For "mischievous", remember it has only three syllables: mis-chie-vous. Keeping this pronunciation in mind will prevent you from writing the extra "i" in "mischievous".

For French loanwords like "entrepreneur", break them down into syllables: en-tre-pre-neur.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.549Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5592, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct pair.', NULL, '[{"text":"Pale – light in color","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Pail-bucket","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Peel-remove skin","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Pale – fruit skin","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This question evaluates homophones, spelling, and vocabulary definitions.

We must examine each word and its corresponding definition to identify the correct pairing.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Homophones and Vocabulary: Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and definitions. Here, we are looking at "pale", "pail", and "peel".

• Analyzing Option (A): "Pale" is an adjective that means light in color, lacking intensity, or whitish. Therefore, "Pale - light in color" is a correct pair.

• Analyzing Option (B): "Pail" is a noun referring to a cylindrical container with a handle, commonly used to carry liquids; it is indeed a bucket. This is also a correct pair.

• Analyzing Option (C): "Peel" is a verb that means to strip or remove the outer skin of a fruit or vegetable. This is also a correct pair.

• Analyzing Option (D): "Pale" paired with "fruit skin" is incorrect because the outer skin of a fruit is called a "peel", not "pale".

• Question Nuance: Because options (A), (B), and (C) are all technically correct pairs, this question might have been designed with a typo, or meant to ask "Choose the incorrect pair", which would make (D) the answer. However, interpreting it as written, we highlight (A) as a primary correct pair.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct pair is (A) Pale – light in color (with B and C also being valid definitions).

Quick Tip: Always pay close attention to homophones in writing section exams.

To remember: "Pail" contains "ai" like "rain", which goes inside a bucket. "Pale" refers to a color shade (lacking color).', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.549Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5593, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct active/passive voice conversions.', NULL, '[{"text":"The letter was written by him.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"She sings a song.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A song is sung by her.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The work completed yesterday.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This question requires us to identify active and passive voice sentences that form a correct grammatical conversion pair.

We will analyze the structure of each sentence to find the active-passive relationship.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Active and Passive Voice Rules: In an active sentence, the subject performs the action on the object. In a passive sentence, the object becomes the subject, the verb is modified with a form of "be" and a past participle, and the original subject is added as an agent after "by".

• Analyzing Option (B) ''She sings a song'': This is a grammatically correct active voice sentence in the Simple Present tense.

- Subject: "She"

- Verb: "sings" (Simple Present)

- Object: "a song"

• Analyzing Option (C) ''A song is sung by her'': This is the correct passive conversion of Option (B).

- The object "a song" becomes the passive subject.

- The singular present tense auxiliary "is" matches the simple present verb "sings".

- The past participle of "sing" is "sung".

- The pronoun "she" becomes the object pronoun "her" following "by".

- Thus, (B) and (C) represent a complete, correct conversion pair.

• Analyzing Option (A): "The letter was written by him" is a valid passive sentence, but its active counterpart ("He wrote the letter") is not listed here to form a pair.

• Analyzing Option (D): "The work completed yesterday" is grammatically incomplete in the passive voice; it should be "The work was completed yesterday".

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct active/passive voice conversion pair is (B) She sings a song. and (C) A song is sung by her.

Quick Tip: To convert an active sentence to passive, always match the tense of the auxiliary verb to the tense of the main verb in the active sentence.

Simple Present (sings) must always use present auxiliaries (is/am/are) + past participle (sung).', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.550Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5594, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Fill in the blanks with suitable phrasal verbs.

A. Please _____ the meeting till tomorrow.

B. Good friends always _____ each other.

C. Her talent helped her _____ in the competition.

D. We should never _____ others.', NULL, '[{"text":"A - put off, B - stand by, C - stand out, D - look down on","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A - stand by, B - put off, C - look down on, D - stand out","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A - stand out, B - look down on, C - put off, D - stand by","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A - look down on, B - stand out, C - stand by, D - put off","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This question requires us to match the correct phrasal verbs to the blank spaces in the sentences to make them grammatically correct and contextually meaningful.

We will define each phrasal verb and match them systematically.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Analyzing Sentence A: "Please _____ the meeting till tomorrow."

- The sentence refers to shifting a meeting to another day.

- The phrasal verb "put off" means to postpone or delay an event.

- Therefore, Blank A matches with "put off".

• Analyzing Sentence B: "Good friends always _____ each other."

- This sentence describes a characteristic of loyal friends.

- The phrasal verb "stand by" means to support someone or remain loyal to them, especially during difficult times.

- Therefore, Blank B matches with "stand by".

• Analyzing Sentence C: "Her talent helped her ___ in the competition."

- The sentence describes how a competitor''s talent helped her perform exceptionally well.

- The phrasal verb "stand out" means to be prominent, superior, or easily noticed compared to others.

- Therefore, Blank C matches with "stand out".

• Analyzing Sentence D: "We should never _____ others."

- This sentence states a moral rule about how to treat people.

- The phrasal verb "look down on" means to think of or treat someone as inferior.

- Therefore, Blank D matches with "look down on".

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct matching sequence is A - put off, B - stand by, C - stand out, and D - look down on, which corresponds to Option (A).

Quick Tip: When dealing with multiple-choice matching questions, solve the easiest phrasal verb first. Postponing a meeting is a very common context for "put off", which lets you quickly eliminate options that do not start with "put off".', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.551Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5595, 1, NULL, NULL, '"Recalcitrant" means:', NULL, '[{"text":"Obedient","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Stubborn","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Resistant","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Defiant","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks us to identify the correct meaning of the vocabulary word "recalcitrant" from the given multiple-choice options.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Definition of Recalcitrant: The word "recalcitrant" is an adjective that describes a

person who has an obstinately uncooperative attitude toward authority or discipline.

• Etymology: It originates from the Latin word "recalcitrare", which literally translates to "to kick back" (from "re-" meaning back and "calcitrare" meaning to kick, from "calx" meaning heel).

• Analyzing Option (A) - Obedient: This is the exact antonym of recalcitrant, as an obedient person complies with rules or authority.

• Analyzing Option (B) - Stubborn: While "stubborn" is a close synonym representing determination not to change one''s mind, it is more general and does not necessarily imply active resistance to authority.

• Analyzing Option (C) - Resistant: This refers to offering resistance to something, but it is less descriptive of an active, rebellious behavioral attitude compared to other options.

• Analyzing Option (D) - Defiant: This term refers to showing open resistance and bold disobedience to authority, which captures the rebellious, active "kicking back" connotation of recalcitrant most accurately.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, while "stubborn" and "resistant" are near-synonyms, "defiant" is the most precise and contextually appropriate option that matches the core definition of "recalcitrant".

Quick Tip: When faced with multiple closely related synonyms in vocabulary questions, look closely at the etymological root of the word.

"Recalcitrant" contains the root related to kicking back against authority, which aligns perfectly with being "defiant".

Learning roots, prefixes, and suffixes can help you distinguish between subtle differences in synonyms.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.553Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5596, 1, NULL, NULL, '"Make up" means:', NULL, '[{"text":"Invent a story","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Reconcile","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Apply cosmetics","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Break friendship","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This question asks for the definition of the highly versatile phrasal verb "make up".

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Nature of Phrasal Verbs: Phrasal verbs in English often carry multiple idiomatic meanings depending on the context in which they are used.

• Analyzing Option (A) - Invent a story: "Make up" commonly means to fabricate or invent a story, excuse, or explanation. For instance, "He had to make up an excuse for being late to class." This is a highly frequent usage tested in competitive exams.

• Analyzing Option (B) - Reconcile: "Make up" also means to restore friendly relations after a quarrel or disagreement. For example, "After their argument, they decided to make up and move forward."

• Analyzing Option (C) - Apply cosmetics: This is another literal/common meaning, referring to applying make-up or cosmetics to the face. For example, "The actors need to make up before going on stage."

• Analyzing Option (D) - Break friendship: This is incorrect, as "break up" or "fall out" would represent ending a relationship or friendship.

- Note on Exam Context: Because options (A), (B), and (C) are all grammatically and semantically valid definitions of "make up", the question represents a polysemous word scenario. In standard answer keys where a single option is circled, (A) is often designated as the primary figurative translation, but a comprehensive understanding recognizes the validity of (A), (B), and (C).

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the primary idiomatic meaning highlighted in standard assessments is option (A), though options (B) and (C) are also linguistically correct.

Quick Tip: Phrasal verbs are highly contextual and often have multiple disparate meanings.

Create a mind map for common phrasal verbs like "make up", "bring up", "take off", and "go over" to memorize their various uses.

Always eliminate the option that is definitively anonymous or unrelated, such as "break friendship" in this case.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.553Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'banner', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5597, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the nouns.', NULL, '[{"text":"Progeny","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Entrepreneur","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Mischievous","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Furtherance","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '##### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question requires us to analyze a list of words and identify which of them function as nouns in standard English grammar.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Option (A) - Progeny: This is a noun referring to the offspring or descendants of a person, animal, or plant. For example, "They were proud of their progeny." Since it represents a category of living beings, it is classified as a noun.

• Option (B) - Entrepreneur: This is a noun representing a person who sets up a business or businesses, taking on financial risks in the hope of profit. The suffix "-eur" typically denotes an agent or person performing an action, confirming its status as a noun.

• Option (C) - Mischievous: This is an adjective, not a noun. It describes a person, animal, or behavior that is playfully malicious or causes minor trouble. The suffix "-ous" is a classic adjective-forming suffix. For example, "The mischievous puppy chewed the shoe."

• Option (D) - Furtherance: This is an abstract noun meaning the advancement or promotion of a scheme, plan, or interest. The suffix "-ance" is a standard suffix used to convert verbs (to further) into abstract nouns (furtherance).

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the nouns in the list are options (A) Progeny, (B) Entrepreneur, and (D) Furtherance, whereas option (C) is an adjective.

Quick Tip: Identifying parts of speech is simplified by analyzing word suffixes.

Suffixes like "-ance", "-ence", "-er", "-or", "-eur", and "-ity" almost always indicate nouns.

Suffixes like "-ous", "-ful", "-less", and "-ive" generally indicate adjectives.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.554Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5598, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Fill in the blanks with suitable words:

A. The teacher asked the students to _____ attention in class.

B. She was _____ with joy after hearing the result.

C. His remarks were completely _____ and offensive.

D. The scientist made a remarkable _____ in medicine.', NULL, '[{"text":"A - pay, B - elated, C - inappropriate, D - breakthrough","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A - elated, B - pay, C - breakthrough, D - inappropriate","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A - inappropriate, B - breakthrough, C - pay, D - elated","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A - breakthrough, B - inappropriate, C - elated, D - pay","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks us to fill in the blanks of four independent sentences using the most appropriate vocabulary words from the options provided.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Analyzing Sentence A: "The teacher asked the students to _____ attention in class."

In English, "pay attention" is a fixed collocation meaning to listen, watch, or focus on something carefully. Thus, "pay" is the correct verb here.

• Analyzing Sentence B: "She was ___ with joy after hearing the result." The prepositional phrase "with joy" requires an adjective that describes an intense state of

happiness. "Elated", which means extremely happy or proud, fits perfectly.

• Analyzing Sentence C: "His remarks were completely ___ and offensive." The coordinating conjunction "and" connects words of similar grammatical function and tone. "Offensive" is a negative adjective, so we need another negative adjective. "Inappropriate" perfectly complements "offensive".

• Analyzing Sentence D: "The scientist made a remarkable ___ in medicine." The verb phrase "made a remarkable..." requires a noun that denotes a major discovery or progress. "Breakthrough" means an important discovery or event that helps to solve a situation or provide progress, making it the ideal fit.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, matching each sentence with its correct vocabulary term yields the sequence: A - pay, B - elated, C - inappropriate, and D - breakthrough, corresponding to option (A).

Quick Tip: In fill-in-the-blank questions involving multiple sentences, start with the absolute certainties. Common collocations like "pay attention" or "make a breakthrough" can be solved instantly, allowing you to use the process of elimination for trickier sentences.

Check that the selected words match the grammatical parts of speech required by each blank.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.554Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5599, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Match the figures of speech:

Column A Column B

A. Simile 1. Repetition of consonant sounds

B. Metaphor 2. Direct comparison

C. Alliteration 3. Human qualities to objects

D. Personification 4. Implied comparison', NULL, '[{"text":"A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question requires us to match classical figures of speech in Column A with their correct definitions listed in Column B.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Analyzing Simile (A): A simile is a figure of speech that directly compares two different things using comparative words such as "like" or "as". Therefore, it matches description 2, "Direct comparison". For example, "as brave as a lion."

• Analyzing Metaphor (B): A metaphor is a figure of speech that makes an implicit, implied, or hidden comparison between two things that are unrelated but share some common characteristics. Unlike similes, it does not use "like" or "as". Thus, it matches description 4, "Implied comparison". For example, "time is a thief."

• Analyzing Alliteration (C): Alliteration is the literary device where the same consonant sound is repeated at the beginning of adjacent or closely connected words in a sentence. This matches description 1, "Repetition of consonant sounds". For example, "Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers."

• Analyzing Personification (D): Personification is a figurative device in which non-human things, objects, or abstract ideas are given human traits, emotions, or intentions. This matches description 3, "Human qualities to objects". For example, "the wind whispered through the trees."

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the correct matching is A matches with 2, B with 4, C with 1, and D with 3, which corresponds to option (A).

Quick Tip: Remember that a simile always makes an explicit direct comparison using "like" or "as", whereas a metaphor makes an indirect or implied comparison without these words.

Alliteration concerns the repetition of consonant sounds specifically at the start of words, which is easy to spot visually.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.555Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5600, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the correctly spelt word from the options given below:', NULL, '[{"text":"Innocuos","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Innoccuous","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Innocuous","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Innocous","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This spelling-based question asks us to identify the standard, correct spelling of a specific vocabulary word meaning harmless or safe.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Definition and Use: The word "innocuous" is an adjective used to describe something that is not harmful, offensive, or injurious. For example, "The medicine was completely innocuous, having no side effects."

• Etymological Roots: It comes from the classical Latin "innocuus", formed by combining the prefix "in-" (meaning "not") and the root adjective "nocuus" (meaning "harmful", derived from the verb "nocere", which means "to hurt").

• Analyzing Option (A) - Innocuos: This spelling incorrectly omits the letter ''u'' before the ending letter ''o'', which disrupts the correct standard pronunciation and orthography.

• Analyzing Option (B) - Innoccuous: This spelling introduces an incorrect double ''c''. While the word has a double ''n'' near the beginning, it only contains a single ''c'' before the suffix.

• Analyzing Option (C) - Innocuous: This is the perfectly correct orthographic form, maintaining the double ''n'', single ''c'', and the correct "-uous" suffix.

• Analyzing Option (D) - Innocous: This spelling is severely misspelled, missing both the double ''n'' structure and the correct vowel configuration in the suffix.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the only correctly spelled option representing the standard English dictionary form of the word is option (C).

Quick Tip: To master spelling words like "innocuous", break them down by their Latin roots: "in-" (not) + "nocere" (harm) + adjective suffix "-ous".

Be mindful of words that feature double consonants; "innocuous" has a double ''n'' but a single ''c''.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.556Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5601, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Fill in the blanks with suitable vocabulary words:

A. The _____ child refused to obey anyone.

B. The king sought wise _____ from his ministers.

C. The explorer made a great _____ in science.

D. Her speech created a strong _____ on the audience.', NULL, '[{"text":"A - recalcitrant, B - counsel, C - breakthrough, D - impression","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A - counsel, B - recalcitrant, C - impression, D - breakthrough","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A - breakthrough, B - impression, C - recalcitrant, D - counsel","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A - impression, B - breakthrough, C - counsel, D - recalcitrant","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question tests contextual vocabulary usage by asking us to fill in the blanks of four sentences with the most suitable words from the list.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Analyzing Sentence A: "The ___ child refused to obey anyone." The blank requires an adjective describing a child who is stubbornly disobedient. "Recalcitrant" means having an obstinately uncooperative attitude toward authority, which fits this context perfectly.

• Analyzing Sentence B: "The king sought wise _____ from his ministers." In political and historical contexts, rulers seek advice or guidance. "Counsel" functions as a noun meaning advisory advice, making it the correct term.

• Analyzing Sentence C: "The explorer made a great _____ in science." In academic and scientific fields, a major discovery or advancement is referred to as a "breakthrough". Thus, "breakthrough" is the correct noun for this blank.

• Analyzing Sentence D: "Her speech created a strong ___ on the audience." The idiom "to create an impression on someone" means to leave a lasting impact or positive

effect. This makes "impression" the correct choice here.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the correct mapping of words to their respective blanks is A - recalcitrant, B - counsel, C - breakthrough, and D - impression, which matches option (A).

Quick Tip: Differentiate between "counsel" (advice/to advise) and "council" (a group of people gathered for consultation).

In fill-in-the-blank vocabulary matches, cross-reference words with the syntactic structure of the sentences (e.g., checking if a noun, adjective, or verb is required).', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.556Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5602, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Fill in the blanks with correct prepositions:

A. She is fond _____ music.

B. He apologized _____ his mistake.

C. The train arrived _____ time.

D. They were divided _____ two groups.', NULL, '[{"text":"A - of, B - for, C - on, D - into","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A - for, B - of, C - into, D - on","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A - on, B - into, C - of, D - for","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A - into, B - on, C - for, D - of","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This question tests our command of English prepositions by requiring us to complete four sentences using correct fixed prepositions and common prepositional phrases.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Analyzing Sentence A: "She is fond _____ music." The adjective "fond" is always paired with the fixed preposition "of" when indicating a liking or affection for something. Thus, "of" is the correct choice.

• Analyzing Sentence B: "He apologized ___ his mistake." The verb "apologize" takes different prepositions depending on the object. You apologize "to" a person, but you apologize "for" an action or mistake. Therefore, "for" is correct here.

• Analyzing Sentence C: "The train arrived ___ time." The phrase "on time" is an idiomatic prepositional phrase meaning punctual or according to the schedule, which is standard in English usage.

• Analyzing Sentence D: "They were divided ___ two groups." When splitting a whole entity into separate component parts or groups, the verb "divide" is followed by the preposition "into".

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Combining these matches results in the sequence A - of, B - for, C - on, and D - into, which perfectly aligns with option (A).

Quick Tip: Prepositions are often "fixed" in English, meaning they must follow specific verbs, nouns, or adjectives regardless of literal meaning.

Memorize preposition pairs like "fond of", "apologize for", and "depend on" to solve grammar questions quickly.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.557Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'banner', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5603, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Match the grammar terms:

Column A Column B

A. Noun 1. Describes noun

B. Verb 2. Action word

C. Adjective 3. Naming word

D. Adverb 4. Describes verb', NULL, '[{"text":"A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A-1, B-4, C-2, D-3","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A-4, B-1, C-3, D-2","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This matching question tests the fundamental definitions of the primary parts of speech in English grammar.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Analyzing Noun (A): A noun is a word that functions as the name of some specific thing or set of things, such as living creatures, objects, places, actions, qualities, states of existence, or ideas. Therefore, it is commonly defined as a "Naming word", matching with 3.

• Analyzing Verb (B): A verb is a word used to describe an action, state, or occurrence. It forms the main grammatical engine of a sentence. It is characterized as an "Action word", matching with 2.

• Analyzing Adjective (C): An adjective is a word that modifies, qualifies, or describes a noun or a pronoun by providing more details about its size, color, quantity, or qualities.

Thus, it "Describes noun", matching with 1.

• Analyzing Adverb (D): An adverb is a word that modifies or describes a verb, an adjective, or another adverb, often indicating how, when, where, or to what extent an action is performed. Therefore, it "Describes verb", matching with 4.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct matches are A-3, B-2, C-1, and D-4, which corresponds to option (A).

Quick Tip: Understanding the basic definitions of parts of speech is essential for sentence structure analysis and syntax error detection.

Always remember that adjectives describe nouns, while adverbs describe verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.557Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5604, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the option that contains the correctly spelt word:', NULL, '[{"text":"Accomodation","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Accommodation","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Accommodation","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Accommodation","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question presents four spelling variations of the same word and asks us to identify the standard, correct spelling.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Word Definition: "Accommodation" is a noun referring to a room, group of rooms, or building in which someone may live or stay. It can also mean a convenient arrangement, agreement, or compromise.

• Analyzing the Correct Spelling: The standard dictionary spelling of the word is "Accommodation". It contains a double ''c'' and a double ''m''.

• Analyzing Common Pitfalls: This word is highly notorious in English spelling tests because people frequently fail to include both sets of double letters.

• Analyzing Option (A) - Accomodation: This option is incorrect as it contains a double ''c'' but misses the double ''m'' (it has only a single ''m'').

• Analyzing Option (B) - Accommodation: This option is incorrect because it contains a double ''m'' but misses the double ''c'' (having only a single ''c'').

• Analyzing Option (D) - Accommodation: This option is incorrect because it changes the ''o'' to a ''u'', which is phonetically and orthographically wrong.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the only option with the correct double ''c'' and double ''m'' spelling is option (C).

Quick Tip: To easily memorize the spelling of "accommodation", use this simple mnemonic memory aid:

"Accommodation is big enough to accommodate two cats (cc) and two mice (mm)." This will help you remember to include both double letters every time.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.558Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5605, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the option that best defines the vocabulary word: Meticulous', NULL, '[{"text":"Highly critical of others and easily dissatisfied.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Operating at a very fast and efficient pace.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Showing great attention to detail; very careful and precise.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Hesitant to make decisions due to fear of failure.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Concept: Vocabulary acquisition requires understanding subtle contextual nuances. The word meticulous originates from the Latin word for fearful, but over time its meaning shifted to describe someone who is overly careful, scrupulous, and exceptionally precise about small details.

### Step 1: Analyzing the options based on standard definitions.

While a meticulous person might notice minor flaws, option (A) incorrectly emphasizes a critical attitude toward others. Option (B) contradicts the typical nature of meticulousness, which often requires taking one''s time rather than rushing. Option (D) confuses precision with indecisiveness. Therefore, option (C) is the most accurate definition.

Quick Tip: To remember meticulous, connect it with the word metric. A meticulous person measures everything down to the exact metric millimeter.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.564Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5606, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Complete the sentence with the correct homonym pair: The project manager visited the construction ___ to check if the new structure was within the line of ___.', NULL, '[{"text":"sight, site","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"site, sight","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"cite, sight","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"site, cite","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Concept: Homonyms and confusable words sound similar but have distinct spellings and meanings:

- Site: A physical location, place, or ground area used for a specific purpose.

• Sight: The faculty or act of seeing; a view within a field of vision.

Cite: To quote, mention, or refer to a source as an authority.

Step 1: Evaluating the context of each blank.

The first blank requires a physical place where construction happens, which corresponds to site. The second blank refers to the field of vision or view, which corresponds to sight. This makes site, sight the only correct sequence.

Quick Tip: Remember that S-i-t-e is linked to Situation/Space (location), while S-i-g-h-t contains g-h just like the word Li-g-h-t (which is required to see).

___', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.565Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5607, 1, NULL, NULL, 'What is the meaning of the idiom: "Spill the beans"?', NULL, '[{"text":"To make a chaotic mess or cause an accident.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"To reveal secret information unintentionally or indiscreetly.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"To waste valuable resources on a useless project.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"To speak aggregate lies to protect someone.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Concept: Idioms are phrases where the figurative meaning cannot be understood literally from the individual words. "Spill the beans" historically relates to ancient voting systems where colored beans were dropped into jars to cast secret ballots; prematurely dropping or spilling them exposed the hidden results.

Step 1: Differentiating figurative meaning from literal expressions.

Options (A) and (C) mistake the physical action of spilling for a literal or financial mess. Option (D) suggests lying, which is the opposite of exposing a hidden truth. Thus, option (B) is the accurate idiomatic meaning.

Quick Tip: When a secret slips out unexpectedly, imagine a container breaking open—the secret information "spills out" into the open for everyone to see.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.565Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5608, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the option that is an antonym for the word: Loquacious', NULL, '[{"text":"Garrulous","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Verbose","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Articulate","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Taciturn","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Concept: Antonym questions require identifying words with directly opposite semantic meanings. Loquacious means extremely talkative or wordy.

Step 1: Analyzing the definitions of the options.

Garrulous and Verbose are synonyms for loquacious, as they describe being overly talkative or using too many words.

• Articulate means fluent and clear in speech, which describes quality rather than quantity.

- Taciturn refers to a person who is naturally reserved, quiet, and says very little.

Because taciturn is the direct opposite of talkative, it is the correct antonym.

Quick Tip: Associate Loqua- with Eloquence and speech (talking a lot), and associate Taciturn with being Silent or tacit.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.566Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5609, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Complete the sentence using the correct phrasal verb of ''Put'': We had to ___ our travel plans because of the sudden heavy rainfall.', NULL, '[{"text":"put up with","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"put off","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"put out","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"put across","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Concept: Phrasal verbs combine a base verb with a preposition or particle to form a completely new idiomatic meaning.

• Put off: To postpone or delay an event to a future time.

• Put up with: To tolerate or endure something unpleasant.

• Put out: To extinguish a fire, or to cause someone inconvenience.

• Put across: To communicate or express an idea clearly.

Step 1: Matching the context to the correct particle.

The context indicates that travel plans had to be delayed due to bad weather. Therefore, the phrasal verb meaning "to postpone" (put off) is required.

Quick Tip: Think of "off" as pushing something away into the future distance—hence, put off means to postpone.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.566Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5610, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Which of the following sentences uses the vocabulary word Abstruse correctly?', NULL, '[{"text":"She was so abstruse in her spending that she ran out of savings within a month.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The instructions were clear and abstruse, allowing us to build the desk in ten minutes.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The professor’s lecture on quantum physics was so abstruse that only a few advanced students could follow it.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"He has an abstruse personality and makes friends everywhere he goes.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Concept: The adjective abstruse is used to describe abstract concepts, theories, texts, or subjects that are profound, complex, obscure, and incredibly difficult for an average mind to grasp or comprehend.

Step 1: Checking contextual alignment for each option.

In option (A), reckless spending should be described as extravagant. In option (B), "clear and abstruse" is a contradiction because clear instructions cannot be obscure. In option (D), an open, friendly personality should be called gregarious or affable. Option (C) uses the word perfectly to describe a dense, complex scientific lecture.

Quick Tip: Think of Abs-truse as something that feels Abs-tract and hard to understand.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.567Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'banner', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5611, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the correct preposition to complete the sentence: He has been working on this data analysis project ___ last Monday.', NULL, '[{"text":"for","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"from","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"since","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"by","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Concept: In the Present Perfect Continuous tense structure (has/have been + verb-ing), specific prepositions connect time markers:

• Since: Used to specify a precise, definite starting point in time.

• For: Used to indicate a duration or overall length of time.

Step 1: Determining if the time marker is a point or a duration.

"Last Monday" is a specific day and a definite point on the calendar where the action commenced.

rather than a total count of days or hours. Therefore, since is the grammatically correct preposition.

Quick Tip: Use Since for specific calendar dates, days, years, or times (e.g., since 2021, since 9 AM). Use For when you can count the units of time (e.g., for 5 days, for 3 hours).', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.567Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5612, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Find the correctly spelled word from the options below:', NULL, '[{"text":"Extravegant","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Extravagent","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Exstravagant","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Extravagant","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, 'Concept: Spelling accuracy requires identifying core root letters and silent phonetic transitions. The term stems from the Latin extravagari, combining extra (outside) and vagari (to wander).

Step 1: Breaking down common orthographic errors.

Option (A) mistakenly uses an ''e'' after the ''v''. Option (B) replaces the regular noun/adjective suffix ending -ant with -ent. Option (C) adds an unnecessary phonetic ''s'' following the ''E''. The authentic, standard spelling is Extravagant.

Quick Tip: Break the word down phonetically into three simple chunks that all contain the vowel ''a'': Extra + vag + ant.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.568Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5613, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the correct tense structure to fill the blank: By the time the teacher entered the classroom, the students _____ writing their notes.', NULL, '[{"text":"have finished","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"will finish","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"had finished","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"finish","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Concept: When two separate actions occurred sequentially in the past, the past perfect tense (had + past participle) must be used to express the action that completed first, while the simple past tense handles the action that occurred later.

Step 1: Establishing the sequence of events in the past.

The sentence establishes two actions: the teacher entering the room (entered - Simple Past) and the students completing their writing. Because the students completed their work before the teacher entered, the earlier action must use the past perfect form: had finished.

Quick Tip: Whenever a sentence contains "By the time" followed by a past tense verb, look for the had + V3 structure in the corresponding clause.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.568Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5614, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the word closest in meaning to "offspring."', NULL, '[{"text":"Ancestors","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Progeny","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Heritage","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Relatives","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks to find the synonym of the word “offspring” among the given options.

Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Define each word to determine which one is closest in meaning to the target word.

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- “Offspring” refers to a person’s child or children, or the young of an animal.

- Option (A) “Ancestors” refers to people from whom one is descended, which is the opposite of offspring.

- Option (B) “Progeny” refers to a descendant or descendants of a person, animal, or plant, making it a direct synonym for offspring.

- Option (C) “Heritage” refers to valued objects and qualities passed down through generations, such as historic buildings or cultural traditions.

- Option (D) “Relatives” refers to people connected by blood or marriage, which is a much broader term.

Step 4: Final Answer:

Therefore, the word closest in meaning to “offspring” is “Progeny”.

Quick Tip: In vocabulary questions, look at the prefix and suffix of the words. For example, “pro-” in progeny often relates to moving forward or producing, which aligns with the concept of future generations.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.571Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5615, 1, NULL, NULL, 'What does “recalcitrant” mean?', NULL, '[{"text":"Helpful","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Obedient","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Stubborn","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Generous","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks for the definition or meaning of the word “recalcitrant”.

### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Analyze the contextual usage and synonyms of the word “recalcitrant”.

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- “Recalcitrant” describes someone who has an obstinately uncooperative attitude toward authority or discipline.

- Common synonyms for this word include stubborn, defiant, rebellious, or uncooperative.

- Options (A) “Helpful”, (B) “Obedient”, and (D) “Generous” are positive traits and generally contrast with or are unrelated to the meaning of recalcitrant.

#### Step 4: Final Answer:

Thus, the correct meaning of “recalcitrant” is “Stubborn”.

Quick Tip: The word recalcitrant comes from the Latin root “recalcitrare”, which literally means “to kick back”. This can help you associate the word with stubborn or resistant behavior.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.571Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5616, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct meaning of “furtherance.”', NULL, '[{"text":"Delay","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Advancement","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Opposition","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Refusal","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks to identify the correct definition of the noun “furtherance”.

### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Relate the root verb “further” to the noun form to deduce its meaning.

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- The verb “further” means to help forward or promote.

- Adding the suffix “-ance” creates a noun that describes the state or action of promoting or helping progress.

- Hence, “furtherance” means the advancement or scheme of promoting a plan, interest, or policy.

- Options (A) “Delay”, (C) “Opposition”, and (D) “Refusal” act as antonyms or contrasting terms.

Step 4: Final Answer:

Therefore, the correct meaning of “furtherance” is “Advancement”.

Quick Tip: Identifying the root word “further” can easily guide you to “advancement” because both imply moving forward or progressing.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.571Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5617, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Identify the grammatically correct sentence.', NULL, '[{"text":"He do not play cricket.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"They has finished the task.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"She enjoys reading novels.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"I were waiting outside.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

We need to check each given sentence for grammatical correctness, specifically looking at subject-verb agreement and auxiliary verb usage.

##### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Analyze the subject and the verb of each sentence:

- Singular subjects require singular verbs.

- Plural subjects require plural verbs.

##### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- Option (A) "He do not play cricket." is incorrect. "He" is a singular subject, so it must take the singular auxiliary verb "does". The correct sentence is "He does not play cricket."

- Option (B) "They has finished the task." is incorrect. "They" is a plural subject and must take the plural auxiliary verb "have". The correct sentence is "They have finished the task."

- Option (C) "She enjoys reading novels." is correct. The singular subject "She" agrees with the singular verb "enjoys".

- Option (D) "I were waiting outside." is incorrect. The first-person singular pronoun "I" takes the singular past tense verb "was". The correct sentence is "I was waiting outside."

Step 4: Final Answer:

The grammatically correct sentence is “She enjoys reading novels.”

Quick Tip: Always double-check subject-verb agreement first when asked to find grammatically correct sentences, as this is one of the most common testing points.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.572Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5618, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct passive voice of: “She completed the project.”', NULL, '[{"text":"The project completed by her.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The project was completed by her.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The project has completed her.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The project is completed by she.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

We need to convert the given active voice sentence into passive voice.

### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Identify the components of the active sentence:

- Subject: “She”

- Verb: “completed” (Simple Past)

- Object: “the project”

The structure of a passive sentence in Simple Past is:

Object + was/were + past participle of verb + by + Subject (objective pronoun)

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- Apply the passive conversion rule:

1. Bring the object to the front: “The project”

2. Choose the correct auxiliary verb based on tense and number: “was” (since the project is singular and the active verb is in the past tense).

3. Write the past participle of the main verb: “completed”

4. Add the preposition “by” followed by the object pronoun of the original subject (“she” becomes “her”): “by her”

- This gives the sentence: “The project was completed by her.”

- Option (A) lacks the auxiliary verb “was”.

- Option (C) uses present perfect tense unnecessarily.

- Option (D) incorrectly uses present tense “is” and the subject pronoun “she” instead of the

object pronoun “her”.

#### Step 4: Final Answer:

The correct passive voice sentence is “The project was completed by her.”

Quick Tip: Always preserve the tense of the active voice sentence. Since the original sentence is in the simple past tense, the passive voice must contain “was” or “were”.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.572Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5619, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the sentence with the correct use of “suite.”', NULL, '[{"text":"He wore a black suite to the party.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"We booked a luxury suite in the hotel.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"The lawyer filed a suite in court.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"This shirt suite you well.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question tests the correct usage of the word “suite” as distinguished from “suit”.

### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Determine the definition of each term to see which sentence uses “suite” correctly:

- “Suite” refers to a set of connected rooms, especially in a hotel.

- “Suit” refers to a set of outer clothes, a legal action in court, or a verb meaning to be appropriate or look good on someone.

Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- Option (A) uses “suite” incorrectly; it should be “suit” because it refers to clothing.

- Option (B) uses “suite” correctly to refer to a set of hotel rooms.

- Option (C) uses “suite” incorrectly; it should be “suit” because it refers to a lawsuit.

- Option (D) uses “suite” incorrectly; it should be “suits” as a verb meaning to look good on someone.

### Step 4: Final Answer:

The correct sentence is “We booked a luxury suite in the hotel.”

Quick Tip: Remember that “suite” is pronounced like “sweet” and is associated with hotel rooms, while “suit” is pronounced like “soot” and refers to clothing or law.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.572Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5620, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Which sentence is the most logical beginning of a paragraph about digital education?', NULL, '[{"text":"Therefore, students performed better in exams.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"As a result, schools introduced smart classrooms.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Technology has changed modern education significantly.","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Consequently, online learning became common.","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

We need to identify the most suitable topic or introductory sentence to start a paragraph about digital education.

#### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

The opening sentence of a paragraph should introduce the core theme independently. It should not contain transitional words or phrases that refer to prior arguments or events.

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- Option (A) starts with “Therefore”, which is used to introduce a conclusion. This implies preceding context.

- Option (B) starts with “As a result”, indicating a consequence of an event mentioned earlier.

- Option (D) starts with “Consequently”, which also shows a cause-and-effect relationship from prior context.

- Option (C) introduces a broad, independent statement about how technology affects education. This is a classic topic sentence and provides a logical start.

### Step 4: Final Answer:

The most logical beginning sentence is “Technology has changed modern education significantly.”

Quick Tip: Avoid choosing sentences that begin with transitional adverbs like “therefore”, “consequently”, “however”, or “as a result” for introductory positions, as they require prior sentences to make sense.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.573Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5621, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Which pair is an example of homophones?', NULL, '[{"text":"Accept – Refuse","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Pale - Pail","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Fast - Slow","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Council – Meeting","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

We need to identify which word pair consists of homophones.

### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Homophones are words that share the exact same pronunciation but differ in meaning, spelling, or both.

### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- Option (A) “Accept” and “Refuse” are antonyms with different pronunciations and spellings.

- Option (B) “Pale” (light-colored or white) and “Pail” (a bucket) are pronounced exactly the same but spelled differently and have different meanings. This matches the definition of homophones.

- Option (C) “Fast” and “Slow” are antonyms with different pronunciations.

- Option (D) "Council" and "Meeting" are related concepts but do not share pronunciation or spelling.

#### Step 4: Final Answer:

The correct pair of homophones is “Pale – Pail”.

Quick Tip: Say the words out loud. If they sound completely identical but mean different things and are spelled differently, they are homophones.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.573Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5622, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the word which is closest in meaning to salubrious.', NULL, '[{"text":"Harmful","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Healthy","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Expensive","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Bitter","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The objective of this question is to identify the synonym or the word that is closest in meaning to the given vocabulary word, "salubrious".

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Meaning of Salubrious: The word "salubrious" is an adjective used to describe

something that is favorable to or promoting health or well-being.

• Etymology: It originates from the Latin word "salubris", which comes from "salus", meaning health, safety, or welfare.

• Analysis of Option (A) - Harmful: This word refers to something causing or likely to cause damage, injury, or harm. This is the opposite of the target word.

• Analysis of Option (B) - Healthy: This term means beneficial to physical health or promoting physical well-being. This corresponds directly to the definition of salubrious, making it the correct option.

• Analysis of Option (C) - Expensive: This term refers to something costing a high price or a large sum of money. It has no relevance to health or well-being.

• Analysis of Option (D) - Bitter: This term describes a sharp, pungent, or unpleasant taste, or a feeling of deep anger and resentment. It is semantically distant from salubrious.

- Usage in Context: One might refer to a "salubrious climate" to describe a pleasant, health-giving environment that helps a patient recover from an illness.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the word closest in meaning to "salubrious" is "Healthy", which corresponds to option (B).

Quick Tip: Familiarizing yourself with Latin and Greek roots can boost your performance in vocabulary sections.

The root "sal-" or "salu-" consistently relates to health, safety, or wellness, as seen in words like salutary, salute, and salvage.

If you spot this root in an unfamiliar word, look for options that focus on health or positive well-being.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.575Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5623, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Select the antonym of the word expedite.', NULL, '[{"text":"Delay","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Complete","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Improve","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Accelerate","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question requires us to find the antonym, which is the word opposite in meaning, to the vocabulary word "expedite".

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Meaning of Expedite: To "expedite" is a verb that means to make an action or process happen sooner or be accomplished more quickly.

• Etymology: It is derived from the Latin word "expeditus", which is the past participle of "expedire", meaning to extricate, set free, or literally "to free the feet" from shackles.

• Analysis of Option (A) - Delay: To "delay" means to make someone or something late, slow, or to postpone an action. This is the direct opposite of speeding up a process,

making it the correct antonym.

• Analysis of Option (B) - Complete: To "complete" means to finish making or doing something. While expediting can help complete a task faster, completion itself is not the antonym of expedition.

• Analysis of Option (C) - Improve: To "improve" means to make or become better in quality or status. It refers to quality enhancement rather than speed.

• Analysis of Option (D) - Accelerate: To "accelerate" means to undergo a rapid increase in speed or to hasten a process. This is a synonym of expedite, not an antonym.

• Contextual Usage: A company might pay extra money to "expedite" shipping so that a package arrives in one day instead of one week.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

Consequently, the word opposite in meaning to "expedite" is "Delay", which corresponds to option (A).

Quick Tip: When looking for antonyms, be careful not to accidentally select a synonym, as synonyms are often included as distractors.

In this question, "accelerate" is a strong synonym designed to test your focus.

Always read all four choices carefully before making your final selection.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.575Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5624, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correctly spelt word.', NULL, '[{"text":"Orninthology","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Orinthology","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Ornithology","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Ornethology","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The objective of this question is to identify the single correctly spelled word from the four given spelling variations.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Meaning of Ornithology: "Ornithology" is a noun that refers to the scientific branch of zoology that focuses on the study of birds.

• Etymological Roots: The word comes from the Greek "ornithos", which means bird, and the suffix "-logia", which means the study of a subject. Combining these yields "ornith-o-logy".

• Analysis of Option (A) - Orninthology: This spelling is incorrect because it introduces an extra, silent letter "n" in the second syllable, creating "orninth-", which is not a valid root.

• Analysis of Option (B) - Orinthology: This spelling is incorrect because it omits the first "n" entirely, leading to "orinth-", which fails to match the proper pronunciation and spelling.

• Analysis of Option (C) - Ornithology: This is the exact correct spelling of the word, adhering perfectly to its classical Greek origins of "ornis" or "ornitho-".

• Analysis of Option (D) - Ornethology: This spelling is incorrect because it replaces the vowel "i" in the third syllable with an "e", creating "-neth-", which is grammatically

wrong.

• Significance of Spelling: Spelling errors in academic words often occur around transition vowels or repeated consonants, so understanding the base root helps eliminate errors.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correctly spelled word is "Ornithology", which corresponds to option (C).

Quick Tip: For scientific studies and academic fields, breaking the word into its prefix, root, and suffix is highly effective.

The suffix "-ology" is standard for fields of study, so your main focus should be on the correct form of the root.

Knowing that "ornith-" is the standard scientific prefix for birds immediately leads to the correct spelling.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.578Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5625, 1, NULL, NULL, 'The idiom axe to grind means:', NULL, '[{"text":"To sharpen a weapon","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"To have a selfish motive","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"To work very hard","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"To end a friendship","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question requires us to determine the idiomatic meaning of the common English phrase "an axe to grind".

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Idiomatic Definition: The phrase "to have an axe to grind" means to have a strong personal opinion or a selfish, hidden motive for doing something.

- Origin of the Idiom: The idiom is historical, commonly associated with an essay written by Charles Miner in 1810 or sometimes attributed to Benjamin Franklin.

- The Origin Story: In the story, a man flatters a young boy into turning a heavy grindstone so that the man can sharpen his axe. Once the axe is sharp, the man walks away without thanking the boy. This represents using others for one''s own private motives.

• Analysis of Option (A) - To sharpen a weapon: This is the literal meaning of grinding an axe, which is incorrect because idioms carry a figurative meaning rather than a literal one.

• Analysis of Option (B) - To have a selfish motive: This matches the figurative meaning of having a private interest or hidden agenda to serve. Therefore, it is correct.

• Analysis of Option (C) - To work very hard: While turning a grindstone is hard work, this is not the figurative meaning of the idiom.

• Analysis of Option (D) - To end a friendship: This is unrelated to the concept of private motives or axes, making it incorrect.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The idiomatic meaning of "axe to grind" is "To have a selfish motive", corresponding to option (B).

Quick Tip: Idioms in English examinations almost never mean what they literally say.

If you see an option that describes the physical, literal action of the words in the idiom, such as "sharpening a weapon", you can safely eliminate it.

Look for figurative meanings that involve human behavior, motives, or relationships.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.579Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5626, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the meaning of the idiom run down.', NULL, '[{"text":"To criticize someone","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"To move upward","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"To decorate something","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"To discover a secret","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The task is to identify the correct idiomatic meaning of the phrasal verb or idiom "run down" from the provided options.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Idiomatic Definition: The phrasal verb "run down" has multiple standard meanings. One of its main figurative meanings is to criticize, disparage, or speak poorly of someone in an unfair or persistent manner.

- Other Definitions: It can also mean to lose power or stop working (like a battery), to be in poor physical condition (exhausted), or to hit someone with a vehicle.

• Analysis of Option (A) - To criticize someone: This corresponds to the common idiomatic usage where one "runs someone down" to lower their reputation. It is the

correct option here.

• Analysis of Option (B) - To move upward: This is the literal opposite of the word "down" and is not an idiomatic meaning of "run down".

• Analysis of Option (C) - To decorate something: This is completely unrelated to any known definition of "run down".

• Analysis of Option (D) - To discover a secret: This might be confused with other phrasal verbs like "run to ground" or "track down", but it does not represent "run down".

Example Sentence: "It is not professional to constantly run down your former employer during job interviews."

### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct meaning of the idiom "run down" is "To criticize someone", which is represented by option (A).

Quick Tip: Phrasal verbs can have multiple meanings depending on context.

When preparing for competitive exams, study the multiple definitions of common phrasal verbs containing verbs like run, put, look, and take.

In this question, looking for the most common figurative meaning helps identify "to criticize someone" as the correct choice.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.580Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'banner', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5627, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the word opposite in meaning to impede.', NULL, '[{"text":"Obstruct","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Delay","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Assist","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Prevent","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '##### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks us to identify the word that is opposite in meaning (antonym) to the word "impede".

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Meaning of Impede: To "impede" means to delay, hinder, or obstruct someone or something by placing obstacles in their path.

• Etymology: It comes from the Latin verb "impedire", which literally means to "shackle the feet", derived from "in-" (into) and "pes" (foot).

• Analysis of Option (A) - Obstruct: To "obstruct" means to block or get in the way of progress. This is a synonym of impede, not an antonym.

• Analysis of Option (B) - Delay: To "delay" means to make something slow or late. This is a common consequence of impeding, making it a closely related synonym.

• Analysis of Option (C) - Assist: To "assist" means to help, facilitate, or support someone or something to make progress easier. This is the direct opposite of impeding, making it the correct antonym.

• Analysis of Option (D) - Prevent: To "prevent" means to stop something from happening or to hinder completely. This is a synonym of impede.

Example Sentence: "The heavy snow storm did not impede the rescue efforts, as local volunteers arrived to assist the emergency crews."

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Thus, the word opposite in meaning to "impede" is "Assist", which matches option (C).

Quick Tip: Understanding prefixes can help you quickly identify the orientation of a word.

The prefix "im-" or "in-" often carries a negative or restrictive meaning, while the root "ped-" refers to feet.

Conversely, words that mean to help often have positive connotations.

This contrast makes it easier to spot "assist" as the correct opposing word.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.581Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5628, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct meaning of the word philately.', NULL, '[{"text":"Study of coins","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Collection of stamps","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Study of birds","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Collection of paintings","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The goal of this question is to identify the precise definition of the noun "philately" from the given list of options.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Definition of Philately: "Philately" is defined as the collection, study, and appreciation of postage stamps and related postal history materials.

• Etymology: The term was coined in 1864 by the French stamp collector Georges Herpin. It is derived from the Greek words "philos" (meaning loving or fond of) and "ateleia" (meaning exemption from tax or duty, which refers to the postage stamp indicating prepaying of fees).

• Analysis of Option (A) - Study of coins: The scientific study or collection of coins, tokens, and paper money is called "numismatics", making this option incorrect.

• Analysis of Option (B) - Collection of stamps: This is the exact definition of philately, making it the correct option.

• Analysis of Option (C) - Study of birds: The scientific study of birds is called "ornithology", so this option is incorrect.

• Analysis of Option (D) - Collection of paintings: There is no single classical term like philately for this, but it is typically referred to as art collecting or curation.

• Contextual Relevance: Philatelists are people who collect stamps, often valuing them for historical, geographical, and artistic significance.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct meaning of "philately" is "Collection of stamps", which is option (B).

Quick Tip: In competitive English exams, questions on "one-word substitutions" or "specialized terms" are very common.

Make a list of terms ending in "-ology" (the study of) and "-y" or "-ics" relating to hobbies or collections. Memorizing these terms systematically can help you secure easy marks.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.581Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5629, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correct meaning of the word numismatics.', NULL, '[{"text":"Study of birds","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Collection of stamps","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Study or collection of coins","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Study of plants","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The objective of this question is to find the correct meaning of the term "numismatics" from the given choices.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Definition of Numismatics: "Numismatics" refers to the scientific study or collection of currency, which includes coins, paper money, tokens, medals, and related objects.

- Etymology: It is derived from the Latin word "numismatis", which comes from the Greek "nomisma", meaning coin, custom, or usage.

• Analysis of Option (A) - Study of birds: This is known as "ornithology", so it is incorrect.

• Analysis of Option (B) - Collection of stamps: This is known as "philately", which was the focus of the previous question, making it incorrect.

• Analysis of Option (C) - Study or collection of coins: This perfectly matches the definition of numismatics, making it the correct choice.

• Analysis of Option (D) - Study of plants: The scientific study of plants is "botany", which is incorrect here.

Practical Application: Numismatists play an important role in historical research, as studying old coins helps determine the economic state, trade routes, and metallurgy of ancient civilizations.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct meaning of "numismatics" is "Study or collection of coins", corresponding to option (C).

Quick Tip: Notice how questions 8 and 9 are closely related.

Often, options in one question can provide hints or answers for other questions in the same paper.

Keeping track of terms like philately and numismatics helps build a strong vocabulary for competitive examinations.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.582Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5630, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the word closest in meaning to judicial.', NULL, '[{"text":"Legal","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Musical","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Medical","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Historical","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The goal is to find the word closest in meaning (synonym) to "judicial" from the given options.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Meaning of Judicial: "Judicial" is an adjective that relates to a court of law, the administration of justice, or the office of a judge.

• Etymology: It originates from the Latin "judicialis", which comes from "judicium" (judgment) and "judex" (judge).

• Analysis of Option (A) - Legal: "Legal" relates to the law, its administration, or the court system. Because the judicial branch administers the law, "judicial" is highly related to "legal". Among the options, it is the closest synonym.

• Analysis of Option (B) - Musical: This relates to music, performance, or sound, which is entirely unrelated to the court system.

• Analysis of Option (C) - Medical: This relates to the science or practice of medicine and healing, which has no connection to the judiciary.

• Analysis of Option (D) - Historical: This relates to history or past events, which is not synonymous with judicial matters.

• Contextual Usage: A "judicial review" is a legal process in which a court reviews the lawfulness of a decision or action made by a public body.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The word closest in meaning to "judicial" is "Legal", corresponding to option (A).

Quick Tip: Always look for the most logical connection when choosing synonyms.

The root "jud-" always refers to law, judgment, or justice, as seen in words like judge, judgment, and judicious.

Connecting the root "jud-" to "law" leads you directly to the correct option "Legal".', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.582Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5631, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Match the word "ventriloquism" with its correct meaning.', NULL, '[{"text":"Speaking in a way that makes the voice seem to come from somewhere else","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Using too many words to express an idea","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Fear of public speaking","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Speaking informally","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Concept: Ventriloquism is the art of speaking in such a way that the sound appears to come from another source, usually a puppet or dummy.

Step 1: Understand the word root.

The word originates from:

Latin: “venter” = belly, “loqui” = to speak

Historically, it meant:

"speaking from the stomach"

Step 2: Identify the correct meaning.

A ventriloquist creates the illusion that:

• The voice comes from somewhere else

- Another object or puppet is speaking

Thus:

Option (A)

Quick Tip: Remember:

Ventriloquism  $ \rightarrow $ Throwing the voice

Usually associated with puppets and stage performances.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.585Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5632, 1, NULL, NULL, 'What does “circumlocution” mean?', NULL, '[{"text":"Silent behaviour","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Speaking indirectly using many words","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Humorous imitation","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Deep hatred for society","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Concept: Circumlocution refers to using unnecessary or indirect words instead of speaking clearly and briefly.

Step 1: Understand the word origin.

The word comes from:

“circum” = around

and

“locution” = speech

Thus, it literally means:

"speaking around the point"

Step 2: Identify the correct meaning.

Circumlocution involves:

• Using too many words

• Speaking indirectly

- Avoiding direct expression

Thus:

Option (B)

Quick Tip: Remember:

Circumlocution  $ \rightarrow $ Indirect lengthy speech

Example: Instead of saying “He died,” saying:

“He passed away from this mortal world.”', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.586Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5633, 1, NULL, NULL, 'A “colloquialism” is:', NULL, '[{"text":"A scientific theory","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A formal legal phrase","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"An informal everyday expression","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"A poetic device","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Concept: A colloquialism is a word or phrase commonly used in informal conversation.

Step 1: Understand colloquial language.

Colloquial language:

• Is informal

• Is used in daily speech

• May vary regionally

Step 2: Identify the correct meaning.

Examples of colloquial expressions:

• “What''s up?”

• “Gonna”

• “Buddy”

These are informal everyday expressions.

Thus:

Option (C)

Quick Tip: Remember:

Used commonly in conversation rather than formal writing.

Colloquialism  $ \rightarrow $ Informal speech', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.586Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5634, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Choose the correctly spelt word.', NULL, '[{"text":"Cristallize","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Crystallise","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Crystallize","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Crystalize","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Concept: Correct spelling is important in English vocabulary and grammar.

Step 1: Examine each option carefully.

• Cristallize  $ \rightarrow $ Incorrect spelling

• Crystallise → British variant exists, but standard accepted form here is “Crystallize”

• Crystallize → Correct spelling

• Crystalize → Missing one “l”

Step 2: Identify the standard correct spelling.

The correctly spelt word is:

Crystallize

Quick Tip: Remember:

Crystal + lize  $ \rightarrow $ Crystallize

Watch for double letters in spellings.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:37.587Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
