## 2. Who among the following gave the slogan “Do or Die” during the Indian National Movement?

(A) Subhas Chandra Bose

(B) Jawaharlal Nehru

(C) Mahatma Gandhi

(D) Bal Gangadhar Tilak

Correct Answer: (C) Mahatma Gandhi

Solution:

Concept:

Important slogans, speeches and political statements are highly important for CUET History examinations because many direct factual questions are asked from them.

The slogan “Do or Die” is associated with one of the most important phases of the Indian freedom struggle.

Step 1: Understanding the historical background.

By 1942, Indian leaders had become frustrated with British policies during World War II. The British government refused to grant immediate independence to India.

As a result, the Indian National Congress launched the Quit India Movement.

Step 2: Identifying the leader associated with the slogan.

On 8 August 1942, Mahatma Gandhi addressed the nation at the Bombay session of the All India Congress Committee.

During this speech, Gandhi gave the powerful slogan:

#### “Do or Die”

This slogan inspired millions of Indians to participate in the freedom struggle.

Step 3: Checking the remaining options.

• Subhas Chandra Bose gave the slogan:

“Give me blood and I will give you freedom.”

• Bal Gangadhar Tilak gave the slogan:

“Swaraj is my birthright and I shall have it.”

• Jawaharlal Nehru is not associated with the slogan “Do or Die.”

Thus, only Mahatma Gandhi is correctly matched.

Therefore, the correct answer is:

(C) Mahatma Gandhi



<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Quick Tip: Important Nationalist Slogans:</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>“Do or Die”  $ \rightarrow $ Mahatma Gandhi</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>“Jai Hind”  $ \rightarrow $ Subhas Chandra Bose</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>“Swaraj is my birthright”  $ \rightarrow $ Bal Gangadhar Tilak</td></tr></table>

## 3. The Cabinet Mission came to India in:

(A) 1940

(B) 1942

(C) 1945

(D) 1946

Correct Answer: (D) 1946

Solution:

Concept:

Questions based on years and constitutional developments are frequently asked in CUET examinations.

The Cabinet Mission was an important turning point in the process of transfer of power from British rule to Indian leadership.

Step 1: Understanding the purpose of the Cabinet Mission.

After World War II, the British government realized that it could no longer continue colonial rule in India.

To discuss the transfer of power and constitutional arrangements, the British government sent a high-level delegation known as the Cabinet Mission.

Step 2: Identifying the year of arrival.

The Cabinet Mission arrived in India in:

The Mission consisted of:

• Lord Pethick-Lawrence

• Sir Stafford Cripps

• A.V. Alexander

### Step 3: Understanding its significance.

The Cabinet Mission proposed:

• Formation of a Constituent Assembly

• Federal structure for India

• Grouping of provinces

Although its plan ultimately failed completely, it played an important role in the final phase of Indian independence.

Therefore, the correct answer is:

(D) 1946

### Quick Tip: Important Timeline:

1942 → Quit India Movement

1946 → Cabinet Mission

1947 → Indian Independence

## 4. Which among the following Acts introduced the system of Dyarchy in the provinces?

(A) Government of India Act, 1909

(B) Government of India Act, 1919

(C) Government of India Act, 1935

(D) Indian Councils Act, 1892

Correct Answer: (B) Government of India Act, 1919

Solution:

### Concept:

Constitutional reforms introduced by the British government are highly important for CUET History examinations.

Questions are frequently asked regarding:

• Important Acts

• Features of Acts

• Constitutional reforms

• Timeline-based developments

The concept of Dyarchy was introduced as part of British administrative reforms in India.

Step 1: Understanding the meaning of Dyarchy.

The term “Dyarchy” means:

##### Dual Government

Under this system, provincial subjects were divided into two categories:

• Reserved Subjects

• Transferred Subjects

Reserved subjects remained under the control of the Governor and executive council, while transferred subjects were handled by Indian ministers.

Step 2: Identifying the Act associated with Dyarchy.

The system of Dyarchy was introduced by the:

### Government of India Act, 1919

This Act is also known as:

### Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms

The reforms aimed to provide limited self-government to Indians.

Step 3: Checking the remaining options carefully.

• Government of India Act, 1909 introduced separate electorates but not Dyarchy.

Government of India Act, 1935 abolished Dyarchy in provinces and introduced provincial autonomy.

• Indian Councils Act, 1892 expanded legislative councils but did not introduce Dvarchy.

Hence, only the Government of India Act, 1919 introduced Dyarchy. Therefore, the correct answer is:

(B) Government of India Act, 1919

Quick Tip: Remember important Acts:

1909 → Separate Electorates

1919 → Dyarchy

1935  $ \rightarrow $ Provincial Autonomy', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.916Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5939, 11, NULL, NULL, 'Who founded the Forward Bloc in 1939?', NULL, '[{"text":"Jawaharlal Nehru","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Sardar Patel","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Subhas Chandra Bose","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Rajendra Prasad","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '### Concept:

Political organizations and revolutionary groups formed during the Indian National Movement are important areas for CUET examinations.

The Forward Bloc was formed during a period of ideological conflict within the Indian National Congress.

Step 1: Understanding the political background.

Subhas Chandra Bose was one of the most dynamic nationalist leaders of modern India. He believed in adopting more aggressive methods to achieve independence from British rule. However, differences developed between Bose and the conservative leadership within the Congress.

Step 2: Identifying the founder of the Forward Bloc.

In 1939, after resigning from the presidency of the Indian National Congress, Subhas Chandra Bose founded:

### Forward Bloc

The main objective of this organization was:

• Uniting left-wing elements within the Congress

• Strengthening anti-colonial struggle

- Achieving complete independence

Step 3: Checking the remaining options.

• Jawaharlal Nehru did not establish the Forward Bloc.

• Sardar Patel was associated with organizational leadership in Congress.

• Rajendra Prasad later became the first President of independent India.

Thus, the correct answer is:

(C) Subhas Chandra Bose

## Quick Tip: Important Organizations:

Forward Bloc → Subhas Chandra Bose

