Servants of India Society  $ \rightarrow $ Gopal Krishna Gokhale', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.916Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5940, 11, NULL, NULL, 'The Jallianwala Bagh massacre took place on:', NULL, '[{"text":"13 April 1919","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"15 August 1919","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"26 January 1920","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"9 August 1942","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Concept:

Direct questions based on exact dates are extremely important in CUET History examinations. The Jallianwala Bagh massacre is one of the most significant and tragic events of the Indian freedom struggle.

Step 1: Understanding the historical context.

In 1919, the British government passed the:

### Rowlatt Act

This Act allowed detention without trial and severely restricted civil liberties.

People across India protested against this repressive legislation.

Step 2: Identifying the date of the massacre.

On:

## 13 April 1919

thousands of people gathered at Jallianwala Bagh in Amritsar.

General Dyer ordered troops to fire upon the unarmed crowd without warning.

Hundreds of innocent people were killed.

Step 3: Understanding the significance of the event.

The massacre shocked the entire nation and destroyed Indian faith in British justice.

Rabindranath Tagore renounced his knighthood in protest against this brutal incident.

The event also intensified the nationalist movement in India.

Therefore, the correct answer is:

(A) 13 April 1919

### Quick Tip: Important Dates:

1919 → Jallianwala Bagh Massacre

1930 → Dandi March

1942 → Quit India Movement', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.916Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5941, 11, NULL, NULL, 'Which among the following movements was launched by Mahatma Gandhi in response to the Rowlatt Act?', NULL, '[{"text":"Civil Disobedience Movement","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Quit India Movement","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Khilafat Movement","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Rowlatt Satyagraha","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '### Concept:

The Rowlatt Act of 1919 was one of the most repressive laws passed by the British government in India.

Questions related to Gandhian movements and British policies are extremely important for CUET History examinations.

Step 1: Understanding the Rowlatt Act.

After World War I, the British government wanted to suppress revolutionary activities in India. Therefore, the Imperial Legislative Council passed the Rowlatt Act in:

1919

The Act allowed the government to:

• Arrest people without warrant

- Imprison individuals without trial

• Restrict freedom of expression

Because of these harsh provisions, Indians strongly opposed the Act.

Step 2: Identifying Gandhi''s response.

Mahatma Gandhi considered the Rowlatt Act unjust and oppressive.

To protest against it, he launched:

#### Rowlatt Satyagraha

This movement emphasized:

• Peaceful protest

• Hartals

- Non-violent resistance

Civil opposition to unjust laws

Thus, the movement launched specifically against the Rowlatt Act was:

Rowlatt Satyagraha

Step 3: Analyzing the remaining options.

Civil Disobedience Movement was launched in 1930.

• Quit India Movement was launched in 1942.

• Khilafat Movement was related to the Ottoman Caliphate issue.

• Rowlatt Satyagraha directly opposed the Rowlatt Act.

Therefore, the correct answer is:

(D) Rowlatt Satyagraha

Quick Tip: Important Timeline:
1919 → Rowlatt Act
1919 → Rowlatt Satyagraha
1919 → Jallianwala Bagh Massacre
These events are highly important for chronology questions in CUET.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.917Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5942, 11, NULL, NULL, 'The Cabinet Mission came to India in the year:', NULL, '[{"text":"1940","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"1942","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"1946","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"1947","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Concept:

The Cabinet Mission was one of the most important constitutional developments before Indian independence.

Questions related to constitutional reforms, acts, and missions are highly important for CUET examinations.

Step 1: Understanding why the Cabinet Mission was sent.

After World War II:

• British rule in India weakened significantly.

• Demands for independence intensified.

• Communal tensions between Congress and Muslim League increased.

To solve the constitutional deadlock, the British government sent the Cabinet Mission to India.

### Step 2: Identifying the members of the Cabinet Mission.

The Cabinet Mission consisted of:

• Lord Pethick-Lawrence

• Stafford Cripps

• A.V. Alexander

The mission arrived in India in:

1946

#### Step 3: Importance of the Cabinet Mission Plan.

The mission proposed:

• Formation of Constituent Assembly

• Federal structure for India

• Grouping of provinces

Although the plan ultimately failed, it played an important role in the transfer of power. Therefore, the correct answer is:

(C) 1946



<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Quick Tip: Important Timeline:</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>1942 → Quit India Movement</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>1946 → Cabinet Mission</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>1947 → Independence and Partition</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Chronology questions are extremely common in CUET History.</td></tr></table>', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.917Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5943, 11, NULL, NULL, 'Which among the following leaders founded the Forward Bloc in 1939?', NULL, '[{"text":"Jawaharlal Nehru","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Bhagat Singh","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Subhas Chandra Bose","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Vallabhbhai Patel","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '### Concept:

CUET History frequently includes questions related to organizations, political parties, and revolutionary groups formed during the freedom struggle.

The Forward Bloc was an important nationalist organization.

Step 1: Understanding the background of Forward Bloc.

Subhas Chandra Bose became President of the Indian National Congress in:

1938

and again in:

However, ideological conflicts developed between Bose and the Congress leadership. As a result:

• Bose resigned from the Congress presidency.

He formed a new political group called the Forward Bloc.

Step 2: Identifying the founder.

The Forward Bloc was founded in:

1939

by:

Subhas Chandra Bose

Step 3: Analyzing the remaining options.

• Jawaharlal Nehru did not found the Forward Bloc.

Bhagat Singh was associated with revolutionary organizations like HSRA.

• Vallabhbhai Patel was a Congress leader but not founder of Forward Bloc.

Therefore, the correct answer is:

(C) Subhas Chandra Bose

Quick Tip: Remember:

1939 → Forward Bloc → Subhas Bose

Organization-founder matching questions are very common in CUET.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.917Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5944, 11, NULL, NULL, 'The Rowlatt Act was passed in the year:', NULL, '[{"text":"1905","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"1919","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"1922","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"1935","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Concept:

The Rowlatt Act was one of the most controversial laws introduced by the British government in India.

Questions based on acts, committees, and British policies are repeatedly asked in CUET History.

Step 1: Understanding the provisions of the Rowlatt Act.

The British government passed the Rowlatt Act to suppress revolutionary activities.

The Act allowed the government to:

• Arrest people without warrant

• Detain individuals without trial

• Restrict civil liberties

Because of these harsh provisions, Indians strongly opposed the Act.

Step 2: Identifying the year of passage.

The Rowlatt Act was passed in:

1919

This led to:

• Nationwide protests

• Hartals

• Political unrest

• Jalliianwala Bagh tragedy in the same year

## Step 3: Historical significance.

The opposition to the Rowlatt Act marked an important turning point in India''s freedom struggle.

It strengthened mass nationalist movements under Mahatma Gandhi.

Therefore, the correct answer is:

<div style="text-align: center;"><img src="/local-papers/assets/history-2026-05-29-shift-2/q10-exp-1.jpg" alt="Figure 1 for question 10" /></div>


### Quick Tip: Important Timeline:

1919 → Rowlatt Act

1919 → Jallianwala Bagh Massacre

1920  $ \rightarrow $ Non-Cooperation Movement

These years are highly important for chronology-based questions.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.918Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5945, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If $f(x)=x^{5}-5x^{3}+5x^{2}-1$, then the number of critical points of $f(x)$ is:', NULL, '[{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"3","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"4","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks for the number of critical points of the given function $f(x)$. Critical points are points where the derivative of the function is either zero or undefined. For polynomial functions, the derivative is always defined, so we look for points where $f''(x) = 0$.

Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

1. Find the first derivative of the function,  $ f''(x) $.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.919Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5946, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The value of the integral  $ I = \int_{-3}^{3}(x^{3}-x)dx $ is', NULL, '[{"text":"0","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{3}{2} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{1}{2} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"x","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks to evaluate an indefinite integral of a rational function. The degree of the numerator is higher than the degree of the denominator, so algebraic manipulation or polynomial long division is required before integration.

Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

1. Perform algebraic manipulation to simplify the integrand:

 $$ \frac{x^{4}+2}{x^{2}+1}=\frac{x^{4}-1+3}{x^{2}+1} $$ 

2. Use the algebraic identity  $ a^{2}-b^{2}=(a-b)(a+b) $ for  $ x^{4}-1=(x^{2}-1)(x^{2}+1) $.

3. Apply standard integral formulas:

 $$ \int x^{n}dx=\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}+C\quad(for n\neq-1) $$ 

 $$ \int\frac{1}{1+x^{2}}dx=\tan^{-1}x+C $$ 

##### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Given integral:  $ I = \int \frac{2 + x^{4}}{1 + x^{2}} dx $

First, manipulate the numerator to simplify the fraction:

 $$ I=\int\frac{x^{4}-1+3}{x^{2}+1}dx $$ 

Split the fraction into two terms:

 $$ I=\int\left(\frac{x^{4}-1}{x^{2}+1}+\frac{3}{x^{2}+1}\right)dx $$ 

Factor the numerator of the first term using the difference of squares:  $ x^{4}-1=(x^{2}-1)(x^{2}+1) $.

 $$ I=\int\left(\frac{(x^{2}-1)(x^{2}+1)}{x^{2}+1}+\frac{3}{x^{2}+1}\right)dx $$ 

Simplify the first term:

 $$ I=\int\left(\left(x^{2}-1\right)+\frac{3}{x^{2}+1}\right)dx $$ 

Now, integrate each term separately:

 $$ I=\int x^{2}dx-\int1dx+3\int\frac{1}{x^{2}+1}dx $$ 

Apply the standard integral formulas:

 $$ I=\frac{x^{2+1}}{2+1}-x+3\tan^{-1}x+C $$ 

 $$ I=\frac{x^{3}}{3}-x+3\tan^{-1}x+C $$ 

This result matches option (a).

Step 4: Final Answer:

The integral is equal to  $ \frac{1}{3}x^3 + 3\tan^{-1}x - x + C $.

Quick Tip: When integrating rational functions where the numerator''s degree is greater than or equal to the denominator''s degree, always perform algebraic division (or manipulation) first. This breaks the complex fraction into simpler terms (a polynomial and a proper rational fraction) that are easier to integrate.

3. The value of the integral  $ I = \int_{-3}^{3}(x^{3}-x)dx $ is

(a) 0

(b)  $ \frac{3}{2} $

(c)  $ \frac{1}{2} $

(d) x

Correct Answer: (a) 0

Solution:

Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks to evaluate a definite integral over a symmetric interval (from -3 to 3).

Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

For a definite integral over a symmetric interval [-a, a]:

1. If $f(x)$ is an $\mathbf{odd}$ function ($f(-x) = -f(x)$), then $\int_{-a}^{a} f(x) dx = 0$.

2. If $f(x)$ is an $\mathbf{even}$ function ($f(-x) = f(x)$), then $\int_{-a}^{a} f(x) dx = 2 \int_{0}^{a} f(x) dx$.

##### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Given integral:  $ I = \int_{-3}^{3}(x^{3}-x)dx $

Let  $ f(x)=x^{3}-x $.

First, check if  $ f(x) $ is an odd or even function:

Substitute -x for x:

 $$ f(-x)=(-x)^{3}-(-x) $$ 

 $$ f(-x)=-x^{3}+x $$ 

Factor out -1:

 $$ f(-x)=-(x^{3}-x) $$ 

Since  $ f(-x) = -f(x) $, the function  $ f(x) = x^{3} - x $ is an odd function.

Because the integral is over a symmetric interval [-3,3] and the integrand is an odd function, the value of the integral is 0.

Alternatively, directly integrate:

 $$ \int(x^{3}-x)dx=\frac{x^{4}}{4}-\frac{x^{2}}{2}+C $$ 

Evaluate the definite integral:

 $$ \left[\frac{x^{4}}{4}-\frac{x^{2}}{2}\right]_{-3}^{3}=\left(\frac{3^{4}}{4}-\frac{3^{2}}{2}\right)-\left(\frac{(-3)^{4}}{4}-\frac{(-3)^{2}}{2}\right) $$ 

 $$ =\left(\frac{81}{4}-\frac{9}{2}\right)-\left(\frac{81}{4}-\frac{9}{2}\right) $$ 

Step 4: Final Answer:

The value of the integral is 0.

Quick Tip: Always check for odd/even symmetry when integrating over symmetric intervals  $ [-a, a] $. This often simplifies the problem significantly. The integral of an odd function over a symmetric interval is always zero.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.920Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5947, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The integral  $ \int e^x (\tan^{-1} x + \frac{1}{1+x^2}) dx $ is equal to', NULL, '[{"text":"$$ e^{x}(\\tan^{-1}x+2)+C $$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$$ \\tan^{-1}x+C $$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$$ e^{x}\\tan^{-1}x+C $$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$$ e^{x}\\cot^{-1}x+C $$","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks to evaluate an indefinite integral that has the form  $ \int e^{x}(f(x) + f''(x))dx $.

Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

The standard integral formula for this form is:

 $$ \int e^{x}(f(x)+f^{\prime}(x))dx=e^{x}f(x)+C $$ 

##### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Given integral:  $ I = \int e^x (\tan^{-1} x + \frac{1}{1+x^2}) \, dx $

Identify  $ f(x) $ and  $ f''(x) $:

Let  $ f(x)=\tan^{-1}x $.

Then, the derivative of $f(x)$ is $f''(x) = \frac{d}{dx}(\tan^{-1} x) = \frac{1}{1+x^2}$.

The integral is exactly in the form  $ \int e^{x}(f(x) + f''(x))dx $.

Applying the formula:

 $$ I=e^{x}\tan^{-1}x+C $$ 

Step 4: Final Answer:

The integral is equal to  $ e^x \tan^{-1} x + C $.

Quick Tip: Always look for the pattern  $ \int e^x(f(x) + f''(x))dx $ when you see an  $ e^x $ multiplied by a sum of functions. Identifying  $ f(x) $ and its derivative  $ f''(x) $ simplifies the integral significantly.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.920Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5948, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The value of  $ \int_{0}^{2}|x-2|dx= $', NULL, '[{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"3","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"3/2","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks to evaluate a definite integral involving an absolute value function.

#### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

1. Define the absolute value function:  $ |A| = A $ if  $ A \geq 0 $, and  $ |A| = -A $ if  $ A < 0 $.

2. Split the integral into intervals where the expression inside the absolute value changes sign.

3. Evaluate the integral over each interval.

Alternatively, interpret the integral geometrically as the area under the curve.

##### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Given integral:  $ I = \int_{0}^{2}|x - 2|dx $

Analyze the expression inside the absolute value, x - 2:

- For  $ x \leq 2 $,  $ x - 2 \leq 0 $. So,  $ |x - 2| = -(x - 2) = 2 - x $.

The interval of integration is from 0 to 2. In this entire interval,  $ x \leq 2 $.

Therefore, for  $ x \in [0,2] $,  $ |x-2| $ can be replaced by 2-x.

 $$ I=\int_{0}^{2}(2-x)dx $$ 

Now, integrate term by term:

 $$ I=\left[2x-\frac{x^{2}}{2}\right]_{0}^{2} $$ 

Evaluate at the limits:

 $$ I=\left(2(2)-\frac{2^{2}}{2}\right)-\left(2(0)-\frac{0^{2}}{2}\right) $$ 

 $$ I=\left(4-\frac{4}{2}\right)-\left(0-0\right) $$ 

 $$ I=\left(4-2\right)-0 $$ 

##### Geometric Interpretation:

The function  $ y = |x - 2| $ is a V-shaped graph with its vertex at x = 2.

For the interval [0,2], the function is y = 2 - x.

This is a straight line.

 $$ -At\;x=0,y=2. $$ 

 $$ -At\;x=2,y=0. $$ 

The integral represents the area of a right-angled triangle with base 2 (from x = 0 to x = 2) and height 2 (at x = 0).

 $$  Area=\frac{1}{2}\times base\times height=\frac{1}{2}\times2\times2=2. $$ 

Step 4: Final Answer:

The value of the integral is 2.

Quick Tip: For definite integrals involving absolute value functions over simple intervals, drawing a quick sketch of the graph and calculating the area geometrically (e.g., as triangles or rectangles) can be much faster and less error-prone than algebraic integration.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.920Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5949, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If  $ X + Y = \begin{pmatrix} 5 & 3 \\ 0 & 7 \end{pmatrix} $ and  $ X - Y = \begin{pmatrix} 7 & 1 \\ 2 & 3 \end{pmatrix} $. Then X and Y are', NULL, '[{"text":"$ X = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\end{pmatrix} $ and $ Y = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\end{pmatrix} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ X = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} $ and $ Y = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\end{pmatrix} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$$ X=\\begin{pmatrix}6&2\\\\ 1&5\\end{pmatrix}and Y=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&-1\\\\ -1&2\\end{pmatrix} $$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$X = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $Y = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question provides two matrix equations involving two unknown matrices X and Y. We need to solve for these matrices.

#### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

This is analogous to solving a system of linear equations for scalar variables. We can use addition and subtraction of matrix equations.

Given:

 $$ 1.X+Y=A=\begin{pmatrix}5&3\\ 0&7\end{pmatrix} $$ 

 $$ \boldsymbol{X}-\boldsymbol{Y}=\boldsymbol{B}=\begin{pmatrix}7&1\\ &2&3\end{pmatrix} $$ 

##### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Solve for X:

Add Equation 1 and Equation 2:

 $$ \left(X+Y\right)+\left(X-Y\right)=A+B $$ 

 $$ 2X=\begin{pmatrix}5&3\\ 0&7\end{pmatrix}+\begin{pmatrix}7&1\\ 2&3\end{pmatrix} $$ 

Perform matrix addition (add corresponding elements):

 $$ 2X=\begin{pmatrix}{{{5+7}}}&{{{3+1}}} \\{{{0+2}}}&{{{7+3}}}\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}{{{12}}}&{{{4}}} \\{{{2}}}&{{{10}}}\end{pmatrix} $$ 

Now, divide by 2 (multiply by  $ 1/2 $):

 $$ \boldsymbol{X}=\frac{1}{2}\begin{pmatrix}12&4\\ 2&10\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}6&2\\ 1&5\end{pmatrix} $$ 

##### Solve for Y:

Substitute the value of X back into Equation 1:

 $$ \boldsymbol{X}+\boldsymbol{Y}=\boldsymbol{A} $$ 

 $$ \begin{pmatrix}{{{6}}}&{{{2}}} \\{{{1}}}&{{{5}}}\end{pmatrix}+\boldsymbol{Y}=\begin{pmatrix}{{{5}}}&{{{3}}} \\{{{0}}}&{{{7}}}\end{pmatrix} $$ 

Subtract X from both sides:

 $$ \boldsymbol{Y}=\begin{pmatrix}5&3\\ 0&7\end{pmatrix}-\begin{pmatrix}6&2\\ 1&5\end{pmatrix} $$ 

Perform matrix subtraction:

 $$ \boldsymbol{Y}=\begin{pmatrix}5-6&3-2\\ 0-1&7-5\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}-1&1\\ -1&2\end{pmatrix} $$ 

Step 4: Final Answer:

 $ X = \begin{pmatrix} 6 & 2 \\ 1 & 5 \end{pmatrix} $ and  $ Y = \begin{pmatrix} -1 & 1 \\ -1 & 2 \end{pmatrix} $. This corresponds to option (a).

Quick Tip: Treat matrix equations like scalar equations for addition/subtraction. Add or subtract equations to isolate one variable, then back-substitute. Always perform element-wise addition/subtraction carefully. Double-check your result against options to catch any simple arithmetic errors.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.921Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5950, 3, NULL, NULL, 'A certain type of fish has a 4/5 probability of surviving in a pond, while another fish has a 2/5 probability of survival. What is the probability that exactly one of them survives?', NULL, '[{"text":"2/5","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"9/16","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"14/25","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"16/25","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The problem asks for the probability that exactly one of two independent events (fish survival) occurs.

Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Let $P(A)$ be the probability that the first fish survives and $P(B)$ be the probability that the

second fish survives.

The probability that exactly one of them survives is:

 $ P(\text{exactly one survives}) = P(A \text{ survives and } B \text{ dies}) + P(A \text{ dies and } B \text{ survives}). $

Since the events are independent:

 $ P(\text{exactly one survives}) = P(A) \times P(B'') + P(A'') \times P(B) $.

Where $P(A'')$ is the probability that A dies, and $P(B'')$ is the probability that B dies. $P(X'') = 1 - P(X)$.

##### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Given:

- Probability of first fish surviving,  $ P(A) = 4/5 $.

- Probability of second fish surviving,  $ P(B) = 2/5 $.

First, calculate the probabilities of them dying:

- Probability of first fish dying,  $ P(A'') = 1 - P(A) = 1 - \frac{4}{5} = \frac{1}{5} $.

- Probability of second fish dying,  $ P(B'') = 1 - P(B) = 1 - 2/5 = 3/5 $.

Now, calculate the probability that exactly one of them survives:

 $$ P(\mathrm{e x a c t l y~o n e~s u r v i v e s})=P(A)P(B^{\prime})+P(A^{\prime})P(B) $$ 

 $$ =\left(\frac{4}{5}\right)\times\left(\frac{3}{5}\right)+\left(\frac{1}{5}\right)\times\left(\frac{2}{5}\right) $$ 

 $$ =\frac{12}{25}+\frac{2}{25} $$ 

 $$ =\frac{12+2}{25}=\frac{14}{25} $$ 

Step 4: Final Answer:

The probability that exactly one of them survives is 14/25.

Quick Tip: For "exactly one of two independent events" problems, break it down into two mutually exclusive scenarios: (Event A happens AND Event B does NOT happen) OR (Event A does NOT happen AND Event B happens). Then sum the probabilities of these two scenarios.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.921Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5951, 3, NULL, NULL, 'A card is picked at random from a pack of 52 playing cards. Given that the picked card is a

king, the probability of this card to be a card of spade is:', NULL, '[{"text":"$ \\frac{1}{3} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{4}{13} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{1}{4} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{1}{2} $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The problem asks for a conditional probability: the probability that a card is a spade, given that it is a king.

Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Use the formula for conditional probability:

 $$ P(A|B)=\frac{P(A\cap B)}{P(B)} $$ 

Where:

- A is the event that the card is a spade.

- B is the event that the card is a king.

Alternatively, a more intuitive approach for conditional probability when the sample space is reduced:

 $$ P(A|B)=\frac{Number~of~outcomes~in~(A~and~B)}{Number~of~outcomes~in~B} $$ 

##### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

A standard deck of 52 playing cards consists of 4 suits (Spades, Hearts, Diamonds, Clubs), and each suit has 13 cards (A, 2, ..., 10, J, Q, K).

1. Identify the reduced sample space (Event B):

The condition is "the picked card is a king".

There are 4 kings in a deck (King of Spades, King of Hearts, King of Diamonds, King of Clubs). So, the total number of kings = 4.

2. Identify the favorable outcome within the reduced sample space (Event A and B):

The event A is "the card is a spade".

Among the 4 kings, there is exactly one King of Spades.

So, the number of kings that are also spades = 1.

3. Calculate the conditional probability:

 $$ P(Spade\mid King)=\frac{Number~of~(Spade~and~King)}{Number~of~Kings}=\frac{1}{4} $$ 

Step 4: Final Answer:

The probability is 1/4.

Quick Tip: For conditional probability problems, always clearly define and reduce your sample space first based on the "given" condition. Then, count the favorable outcomes within this reduced sample space. This simplifies complex problems.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.921Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5952, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If  $ \vec{a} $ and  $ \vec{b} $ are unit vectors, then the angle between  $ \vec{a} $ and  $ \vec{b} $ for  $ \sqrt{3}\vec{a}-\vec{b} $ to be a unit vector is given by :', NULL, '[{"text":"$ \\pi/6 $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\pi/4 $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\pi/3 $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ 2\\pi/3 $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The problem involves two unit vectors  $ \vec{a} $ and  $ \vec{b} $. We are given that a linear combination of these vectors,  $ \sqrt{3}\vec{a}-\vec{b} $, is also a unit vector. We need to find the angle between  $ \vec{a} $ and  $ \vec{b} $.

Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

1. If  $ \vec{v} $ is a unit vector, then  $ |\vec{v}|^2 = 1 $.

2. For any vector  $ \vec{v} $,  $ |\vec{v}|^2 = \vec{v} \cdot \vec{v} $.

3. The dot product of two vectors  $ \vec{a} \cdot \vec{b} = |\vec{a}||\vec{b}|\cos\theta $.

4. Since  $ \vec{a} $ and  $ \vec{b} $ are unit vectors,  $ |\vec{a}| = 1 $ and  $ |\vec{b}| = 1 $.

Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Let  $ \vec{v} = \sqrt{3}\vec{a} - \vec{b} $.

Since  $ \vec{v} $ is a unit vector,  $ |\vec{v}|^2 = 1 $.

 $$ |\sqrt{3}\vec{a}-\vec{b}|^{2}=1 $$ 

Expand the square of the magnitude using the dot product property:

 $$ (\sqrt{3}\vec{a}-\vec{b})\cdot(\sqrt{3}\vec{a}-\vec{b})=1 $$ 

 $$ (\sqrt{3}\vec{a})\cdot(\sqrt{3}\vec{a})-2(\sqrt{3}\vec{a}\cdot\vec{b})+\vec{b}\cdot\vec{b}=1 $$ 

 $$ (\sqrt{3})^{2}|\vec{a}|^{2}-2\sqrt{3}(\vec{a}\cdot\vec{b})+|\vec{b}|^{2}=1 $$ 

Since  $ \vec{a} $ and  $ \vec{b} $ are unit vectors,  $ |\vec{a}|^2 = 1 $ and  $ |\vec{b}|^2 = 1 $.

 $$ 3(1)-2\sqrt{3}(\overrightarrow{a}\cdot\overrightarrow{b})+1=1 $$ 

 $$ 4-2\sqrt{3}(\overrightarrow{a}\cdot\overrightarrow{b})=1 $$ 

 $$ 3=2\sqrt{3}(\vec{a}\cdot\vec{b}) $$ 

Now, substitute  $ \vec{a} \cdot \vec{b} = |\vec{a}||\vec{b}|\cos\theta = (1)(1)\cos\theta = \cos\theta $:

 $$ 3=2\sqrt{3}\cos\theta $$ 

Solve for  $ \cos\theta $:

 $$ \cos\theta=\frac{3}{2\sqrt{3}}=\frac{3\sqrt{3}}{2\times3}=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} $$ 

The angle  $ \theta $ whose cosine is  $ \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} $ is  $ 30^\circ $ or  $ \pi/6 $ radians.

Step 4: Final Answer:

The angle between  $ \vec{a} $ and  $ \vec{b} $ is  $ \pi/6 $. This corresponds to option (a).

Quick Tip: When dealing with unit vectors and magnitudes, always square the vector equation to convert magnitudes into dot products. This leverages the properties  $ |\vec{v}|^2 = \vec{v} \cdot \vec{v} $ and  $ \vec{a} \cdot \vec{b} = |\vec{a}||\vec{b}|\cos\theta $ for unit vectors.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.922Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5953, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Consider the function  $ f(x) = -2x^3 - 9x^2 - 12x + 5 $. Then, which of the following is/are correct?', NULL, '[{"text":"$ f(x) $ decreasing in $ (0, \\infty) $ and increasing in $ (-\\infty, 0) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ f(x) $ increasing in $ (-\\infty,1) \\cup (2, \\infty) $ and decreasing in $ (1,2) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ f(x) $ increasing in $ (0, \\infty) $ and decreasing in $ (-\\infty, 0) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ f(x) $ is increasing in $ (-2,-1) $ and decreasing in $ (-\\infty,-2)\\cup(-1,\\infty) $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 3, '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks to determine the intervals where the given function $f(x)$ is increasing or decreasing. This involves analyzing the sign of the first derivative of the function.

#### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

1. Find the first derivative of the function,  $ f''(x) $.

2. Find the critical points by setting  $ f''(x) = 0 $.

3. Test the sign of  $ f''(x) $ in intervals determined by the critical points.

- If  $ f''(x) > 0 $, the function is increasing.

- If  $ f''(x) < 0 $, the function is decreasing.

##### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Given function:  $ f(x) = -2x^3 - 9x^2 - 12x + 5 $.

Differentiate $f(x)$ with respect to $x$:

 $$ f^{\prime}(x)=\frac{d}{dx}(-2x^{3}-9x^{2}-12x+5) $$ 

 $$ f^{\prime}(x)=-6x^{2}-18x-12 $$ 

To find critical points, set  $ f''(x) = 0 $:

 $$ -6x^{2}-18x-12=0 $$ 

Divide by -6:

 $$ x^{2}+3x+2=0 $$ 

Factor the quadratic equation:

(x + 1)(x + 2) = 0

The critical points are x = -1 and x = -2.

These critical points divide the number line into three intervals: (-∞, -2), (-2, -1), and (-1, ∞).

Now, test the sign of  $ f''(x) = -6(x + 1)(x + 2) $ in each interval:

1. Interval (-∞, -2): Choose a test value, e.g., x = -3.
 $ f''(-3) = -6(-3 + 1)(-3 + 2) = -6(-2)(-1) = -12 $.

Since  $ f''(x) < 0 $,  $ f(x) $ is decreasing in (-∞, -2).

2. Interval (-2, -1): Choose a test value, e.g., x = -1.5.
 $ f''(-1.5) = -6(-1.5 + 1)(-1.5 + 2) = -6(-0.5)(0.5) = -6(-0.25) = 1.5 $.

Since  $ f''(x) > 0 $,  $ f(x) $ is increasing in (-2, -1).

3. Interval (-1, ∞): Choose a test value, e.g., x = 0.
 $ f''(0) = -6(0 + 1)(0 + 2) = -6(1)(2) = -12 $.

Since  $ f''(x) < 0 $,  $ f(x) $ is decreasing in (-1, ∞).

Combining the results:

 $ f(x) $ is increasing in (-2, -1).
 $ f(x) $ is decreasing in (-∞, -2) ∪ (-1, ∞).

This matches option (d).

Step 4: Final Answer:

The function  $ f(x) $ is increasing in (-2, -1) and decreasing in (-∞, -2) ∪ (-1, ∞).

Quick Tip: To determine intervals of increasing/decreasing, the sign of the first derivative  $ f''(x) $ is crucial. Always factor  $ f''(x) $ to easily analyze its sign across intervals defined by its roots. Use test points within each interval to determine the sign.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.922Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5954, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Let  $ A = \{1, 2, 3\} $ and  $ B = \{2, 4, 6\} $. A function  $ f: A \to B $ is defined by  $ f(x) = 2x $. The function is:', NULL, '[{"text":"One-one only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Onto only","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Both one-one and onto","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Neither one-one nor onto","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question defines a set A (domain), a set B (codomain), and a function $f(x)=2x$ mapping from A to B. We need to determine if the function is one-one, onto, both, or neither.

### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

1. One-one (Injective): A function $f: A \to B$ is one-one if every distinct element in A maps to a distinct element in B. That is, if $f(x_1) = f(x_2) \Rightarrow x_1 = x_2$.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.923Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5955, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If $A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{pmatrix}$, then $A^{-1}$ is:', NULL, '[{"text":"$\\begin{pmatrix} -2 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\end{pmatrix}$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\end{pmatrix}$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\end{pmatrix}$","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$\\begin{pmatrix} -2 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\end{pmatrix}$","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks to find the inverse of a given 2x2 matrix A.

Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

For a 2x2 matrix  $ A = \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix} $, its inverse  $ A^{-1} $ is given by the formula:

 $$ \boldsymbol{A}^{-1}=\frac{1}{\det(\boldsymbol{A})}\begin{pmatrix}d&-b\\ &\\ -c&a\end{pmatrix} $$ 

Where  $ \det(A) $ is the determinant of A, calculated as  $ ad - bc $.

Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Given matrix: $A=\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\3&4\end{pmatrix}$

Here, a = 1, b = 2, c = 3, d = 4.

1. Calculate the determinant of  $  \mathbf{A}  $ ( $  \det(\mathbf{A})  $):

 $$ \det(A)=ad-bc=(1)(4)-(2)(3)=4-6=-2 $$ 

2. Find the adjoint of A (adj(A)):

Swap the diagonal elements (a and d) and change the signs of the off-diagonal elements (b and c).

 $$ \mathrm{adj}(A)=\begin{pmatrix}4&-2\\ -3&1\end{pmatrix} $$', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.924Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5956, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Evaluate:  $ \begin{vmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\ 5 & 6 & 0 \end{vmatrix} $', NULL, '[{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"-1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"5","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"10","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks to evaluate the determinant of a given 3x3 matrix.

Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Step 2. Key Formula of Approach.

For a 3x3 matrix $M=\begin{pmatrix}a&b&c\\d&e&f\\g&h&i\end{pmatrix}$, its determinant is calculated as:

$\det(M)=a(ei-fh)-b(di-fg)+c(dh-eg)$

Alternatively, expand along any row or column. It''s often easiest to expand along a row or column that contains zeros.

Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Given matrix:  $ M = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\ 5 & 6 & 0 \end{pmatrix} $

Let''s expand the determinant along the first row:

 $$ \det(M)=1\times\begin{vmatrix}1&4\\ 6&0\end{vmatrix}-2\times\begin{vmatrix}0&4\\ 5&0\end{vmatrix}+3\times\begin{vmatrix}0&1\\ 5&6\end{vmatrix} $$ 

Calculate each 2x2 determinant:

 $$ \begin{aligned}-\left|\begin{matrix}{{{1}}}&{{{4}}} \\{{{6}}}&{{{0}}}\end{matrix}\right|&=(1)(0)-(4)(6)=0-24=-24\end{aligned} $$ 

 $$ \begin{aligned}-\left|\begin{matrix}{{{0}}}&{{{4}}} \\{{{5}}}&{{{0}}}\end{matrix}\right|&=(0)(0)-(4)(5)=0-20=-20\end{aligned} $$ 

 $$ \begin{aligned}-\left|\begin{matrix}0&1\\ &5&6\end{matrix}\right|&=(0)(6)-(1)(5)=0-5=-5\end{aligned} $$ 

Substitute these values back:

 $$ \det(M)=1\times(-24)-2\times(-20)+3\times(-5) $$ 

 $$ \det(M)=-24+40-15 $$ 

 $$ \det(M)=16-15=1 $$ 

### Step 4: Final Answer:

The value of the determinant is 1.

Quick Tip: When calculating determinants, choose to expand along the row or column that has the most zeros. This minimizes the number of  $ 2 \times 2 $ determinants you need to calculate, reducing potential errors. Always remember the alternating signs for the cofactors  $ (+ - +) $.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.924Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5957, 3, NULL, NULL, 'A bag contains 5 red balls and 3 blue balls. Two balls are drawn at random without replacement. The probability that both are red is:', NULL, '[{"text":"$ \\left(\\frac{5}{14}\\right) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\left(\\frac{8}{56}\\right) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\left(\\frac{5}{28}\\right) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\left(\\frac{15}{56}\\right) $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

Step 1: Understana $ _{n} $

The problem asks for the probability  $ \sigma $.

consecutively from a bag without replacement.

Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

The probability of multiple events occurring in sequence without replacement is calculated by multiplying the probabilities of each individual event, where the sample space decreases after each draw.

Alternatively, using combinations: The probability of selecting a specific combination of items is  $ \frac{\text{Number of ways to choose desired items}}{\text{Total number of ways to choose items}} $.

### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Given:

- Number of red balls = 5.

- Number of blue balls = 3.

- Total number of balls = 5 + 3 = 8.

### Method 1: Sequential Probability

1. Probability of drawing the first red ball:

There are 5 red balls out of 8 total.

 $$ P(1\mathrm{s t}\mathrm{r e d})=\tfrac{5}{8}. $$ 

2. Probability of drawing the second red ball (without replacement):

After drawing one red ball, there are now 4 red balls left and a total of 7 balls.

 $ P(2\mathrm{nd}\ \mathrm{red}\ |\ 1\mathrm{st}\ \mathrm{red})=\frac{4}{7}. $

3. Probability that both are red:

 $ P(\text{both red}) = P(1\text{st red}) \times P(2\text{nd red} \mid 1\text{st red}) $

 $$ P(\mathrm{both~red})=\tfrac{5}{8}\times\tfrac{4}{7}=\tfrac{20}{56}. $$ 

Simplify the fraction:  $ \frac{20}{56} = \frac{5 \times 4}{14 \times 4} = \frac{5}{14} $.

Method 2: Using Combinations

1. Total number of ways to draw 2 balls from 8:

 $$ \binom{8}{2}=\frac{8\times7}{2\times1}=28 $$ 

2. Number of ways to draw 2 red balls from 5 red balls:

 $$ \binom{5}{2}=\frac{5\times4}{2\times1}=10 $$ 

3. Probability that both are red:

 $$ P(\mathrm{b o t h~r e d})=\frac{\mathrm{N u m b e r~o f~w a y s~t o~d r a w~2~r e d~b a l l s}}{\mathrm{T o t a l~n u m b e r~o f~w a y s~t o~d r a w~2~b a l l s}}=\frac{10}{28}. $$ 

Simplify the fraction:  $ \frac{10}{28} = \frac{5 \times 2}{14 \times 2} = \frac{5}{14} $.

Step 4: Final Answer:

The probability that both balls drawn are red is  $ \frac{5}{14} $. This corresponds to option (A).

Quick Tip: For probability problems involving "without replacement," remember that the denominator (total items) and sometimes the numerator (desired items) decrease after each selection. Combinations provide a reliable way to solve such problems. Simplify fractions to their lowest terms for comparison with options.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.925Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5958, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If  $ f(x)=\begin{cases}x^{2}+k, & x\leq1 \\ 2x+1, & x>1\end{cases} $ is continuous at x=1, then the value of k is:', NULL, '[{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"3","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"4","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.925Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'none', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5959, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Solve:  $ \frac{dy}{dx} = 3x^2 $', NULL, '[{"text":"$ y = x^3 + C $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ y = 3x + C $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ y = x^2 + C $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ y = 9x + C $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks to solve a simple differential equation, which means finding the function $y$ whose derivative with respect to $x$ is $3x^{2}$.

### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

To find y from  $ \frac{dy}{dx} $, we need to perform indefinite integration.

The formula for integrating power functions is:

 $$ \int x^{n}dx=\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}+C\quad(for n\neq-1) $$ 

Where C is the constant of integration.

Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Given differential equation:

 $$ \frac{dy}{dx}=3x^{2} $$ 

To find y, integrate both sides with respect to x:

 $$ \int d y=\int3x^{2}d x $$ 

 $$ y=3\int x^{2}dx $$ 

Apply the power rule for integration (n = 2):

 $$ y=3\left(\frac{x^{2+1}}{2+1}\right)+C $$ 

 $$ y=3\left(\frac{x^{3}}{3}\right)+C $$ 

 $$ y=x^{3}+C $$ 

This matches option (A).

Step 4: Final Answer:

The solution to the differential equation is  $ y = x^{3} + C $.

Quick Tip: Remember that differentiation and integration are inverse operations. If you''re unsure of your integration, you can always differentiate the proposed solution to see if it matches the original derivative.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.926Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5960, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The feasible region of a linear programming problem is always:', NULL, '[{"text":"Circular","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Open","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Convex","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"Irregular","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks about a fundamental property of the feasible region in a linear programming problem (LPP).

### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Feasible Region: In a linear programming problem, the feasible region is the set of all points that satisfy all the given constraints (linear inequalities). These constraints typically define a polygon or a polyhedral set in 2D or 3D space, respectively.

Convexity: A region is said to be convex if, for any two points within the region, the entire line segment connecting these two points also lies entirely within the region.

The intersection of a set of half-planes (which is what linear inequalities define) always results in a convex set. Therefore, the feasible region of a linear programming problem is always convex. This property is crucial because it ensures that if an optimal solution exists, it will occur at one of the vertices (corner points) of the feasible region.

#### Other options:

- (A) Circular: The feasible region is generally a polygon, not necessarily circular.

- (B) Open: It can be bounded (closed) or unbounded (open in one direction), but "open" itself is not a defining characteristic.

- (D) Irregular: While it can have many sides, it''s always a well-defined convex polygon/polyhedron, not simply "irregular."

Therefore, the feasible region of an LPP is always convex.

### Step 4: Final Answer:

The feasible region of a linear programming problem is always Convex.

Quick Tip: Remember the fundamental property of feasible regions in LPPs: they are always convex polygons (or polyhedra). This convexity is what allows the "corner point method" for finding optimal solutions.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.926Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5961, 3, NULL, NULL, 'The principal value of  $ \tan^{-1}(1) $ is:', NULL, '[{"text":"(0)","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:
The question asks for the principal value of the inverse tangent function for the input 1. The principal value refers to the unique value within the restricted range of the inverse trigonometric function.
Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:
The principal value branch for  $ \tan^{-1}(x) $ is  $ \left(-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}\right) $ (exclusive of endpoints).
We need to find the angle  $ \theta $ such that  $ \tan(\theta) = 1 $ and  $ \theta $ lies within this range.
Step 3: Detailed Explanation:
We are looking for an angle  $ \theta $ such that  $ \tan(\theta) = 1 $.
We know that  $ \tan(45^{\circ}) = 1 $.
Converting  $ 45^{\circ} $ to radians:

 $ 45^{\circ}=45\times\frac{\pi}{180} $ radians =  $ \frac{\pi}{4} $ radians.

The value  $ \frac{\pi}{4} $ lies within the principal value branch of  $ \tan^{-1}(x) $, which is  $ (-\frac{\pi}{2},\frac{\pi}{2}) $.

Therefore, the principal value of  $ \tan^{-1}(1) $ is  $ \frac{\pi}{4} $.

Step 4: Final Answer:

The principal value is  $ \frac{\pi}{4} $.

Quick Tip: Memorize the principal value ranges for inverse trigonometric functions:
 $ -\sin^{-1}(x):\left[-\frac{\pi}{2},\frac{\pi}{2}\right] $
 $ -\cos^{-1}(x):[0,\pi] $
 $ -\tan^{-1}(x):(-\frac{\pi}{2},\frac{\pi}{2}) $
These ranges ensure a unique output for each input.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.927Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5962, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Differentiate:  $ y = x^{2} \sin x $', NULL, '[{"text":"$ (2x \\sin x) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ (x^{2} \\cos x) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ (2x \\sin x + x^{2} \\cos x) $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ (2x \\cos x) $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 2, 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:
The question asks to differentiate the given function  $ y = x^{2}\sin x $ with respect to x.
Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:
The function is a product of two functions of x:  $ u(x) = x^{2} $ and  $ \nu(x) = \sin x $. Therefore, we need to use the product rule of differentiation.
Product Rule: If  $ y = u(x)\nu(x) $, then  $ \frac{dy}{dx} = u''(x)\nu(x) + u(x)\nu''(x) $.
Also, remember basic differentiation rules:
 $ -\frac{d}{dx}(x^{n})=nx^{n-1} $

 $$ -\frac{d}{dx}(\sin x)=\cos x $$ 

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Given function:  $ y = x^2 \sin x $

Let  $ u(x) = x^2 $ and  $ v(x) = \sin x $.

Calculate the derivatives of $u(x)$ and $v(x)$:

 $$ -u^{\prime}(x)=\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2})=2x $$ 

 $$ -\nu^{\prime}(x)=\tfrac{d}{dx}(\sin x)=\cos x $$ 

Now, apply the product rule:

 $$ \frac{dy}{dx}=u^{\prime}(x)v(x)+u(x)v^{\prime}(x) $$ 

 $$ \frac{dy}{dx}=(2x)(\sin x)+(x^{2})(\cos x) $$ 

 $$ \frac{dy}{dx}=2x\sin x+x^{2}\cos x $$ 

This matches option (C).

Step 4: Final Answer:

The derivative is  $ 2x \sin x + x^{2} \cos x $.

Quick Tip: Always identify the structure of the function (e.g., product, quotient, chain rule) before differentiating. The product rule is crucial for functions formed by multiplying two variable expressions.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.927Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5963, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Evaluate:  $ \int_{0}^{1}(2x+1)dx $', NULL, '[{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"3","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"4","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.927Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'none', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5964, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Solve:

 $$ \frac{dy}{dx}+y=e^{x},\qquad y(0)=2 $$', NULL, '[{"text":"$ y = e^{x} + e^{-x} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ y = \\frac{1}{2} e^{x} + \\frac{3}{2} e^{-x} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ y = e^{x} + 1 $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"y = 2e^{x}","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Concept:

The given equation is a first-order linear differential equation of the form:

 $$ \frac{dy}{dx}+Py=Q $$ 

where:

 $$ P=1,\qquad Q=e^{x} $$ 

Such equations are solved using the integrating factor (I.F.) method.

The integrating factor is:

 $$ I.F.=e^{\int P dx} $$ 

Step 1: Finding the integrating factor.

Given equation:

 $$ \frac{dy}{dx}+y=e^{x} $$ 

Comparing with:

 $$ \frac{dy}{dx}+Py=Q $$ 

we get:

 $$ p=1 $$ 

Therefore integrating factor:

 $$ I.F.=e^{\int1dx}=e^{x} $$ 

Step 2: Multiplying entire equation by integrating factor.

Multiply both sides by  $ e^{x} $:

 $$ e^{x}\frac{dy}{dx}+ye^{x}=e^{2x} $$ 

Observe that:

 $$ e^{x}\frac{dy}{dx}+ye^{x}=\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x}) $$ 

Thus equation becomes:

 $$ \frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x})=e^{2x} $$ 

Step 3: Integrating both sides.

Integrating:

 $$ \int\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x})dx=\int e^{2x}dx $$ 

we get:

 $$ ye^{x}=\frac{1}{2}e^{2x}+C $$ 

Divide throughout by  $ e^{x} $:

 $$ y=\frac{1}{2}e^{x}+Ce^{-x} $$ 

Step 4: Applying initial condition.

Given:

 $$ y(0)=2 $$ 

Substitute x = 0:

 $$ 2=\frac{1}{2}+C $$ 

Therefore:

 $$ C=\frac{3}{2} $$ 

Substitute back:

 $$ y=\frac{1}{2}e^{x}+\frac{3}{2}e^{-x} $$ 

Step 5: Matching with options.

The obtained solution matches option (B).

Final Answer:

 $$ (B)y=\frac{1}{2}e^{x}+\frac{3}{2}e^{-x} $$ 

Quick Tip: For linear differential equations:

 $$ \frac{dy}{dx}+Py=Q $$ 

use:

 $$ I.F.=e^{\int Pdx} $$ 

Then:

 $$ y(I.F.)=\int Q(I.F.)dx+C $$ 

Always apply the initial condition at the end to determine the constant.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.929Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5965, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Evaluate:

 $$ \int_{0}^{1}\frac{1}{1+x^{2}}dx $$', NULL, '[{"text":"$ \\frac{\\pi}{2} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{\\pi}{4} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"1","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\ln2 $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 1, 'Concept:

Recall the standard integral:

 $$ \int\frac{1}{1+x^{2}}dx=\tan^{-1}x+C $$ 

For definite integrals:

 $$ \int_{a}^{b}f(x)dx=F(b)-F(a) $$ 

where $F(x)$ is antiderivative of $f(x)$.

Step 1: Integrating the function.

Given:

 $$ I=\int_{0}^{1}\frac{1}{1+x^{2}}dx $$ 

Using standard result:

 $$ \int\frac{1}{1+x^{2}}dx=\tan^{-1}x $$ 

Thus:

 $$ I=\left[\tan^{-1}x\right]_{0}^{1} $$ 

Step 2: Applying limits.

Substitute upper and lower limits:

 $$ I=\tan^{-1}(1)-\tan^{-1}(0) $$ 

We know:

 $$ \tan^{-1}(1)=\frac{\pi}{4} $$ 

and

 $$ \tan^{-1}(0)=0 $$ 

Hence:

 $$ I=\frac{\pi}{4} $$ 

Final Answer:

 $$ (B)\frac{\pi}{4} $$ 

Quick Tip: Remember:

 $$ \int\frac{1}{1+x^{2}}dx=\tan^{-1}x+C $$ 

and:

 $$ \tan^{-1}(1)=\frac{\pi}{4} $$', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.929Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5966, 3, NULL, NULL, 'For  $ y = x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 2 $, slope at x = 2:', NULL, '[{"text":"0","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"2","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"4","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"6","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Concept:

The slope of a curve at a point is given by:

 $$ \frac{dy}{dx} $$ 

Thus we differentiate the function and substitute the given value of x.

Step 1: Differentiating the function.

Given:

 $$ y=x^{3}-3x^{2}+2 $$ 

Differentiate term-by-term:

 $$ \frac{dy}{dx}=3x^{2}-6x $$ 

Step 2: Substituting x = 2.

 $$ \frac{dy}{dx}\bigg|_{x=2}=3(2)^{2}-6(2) $$ 

 $$ =12-12 $$ 

 $$ =0 $$ 

Final Answer:

 $$ \mathrm{(A)}0 $$ 

Quick Tip: Slope of curve:

 $$ y=f(x) $$ 

at any point is:

 $$ \frac{dy}{dx} $$ 

Differentiate first, then substitute the given value of x.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.930Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5967, 3, NULL, NULL, 'If

 $$ \begin{aligned}&\boldsymbol{A}=\begin{bmatrix} \\{{{2}}}&{{{3}}} \\{{{1}}}&{{{4}}} \\\end{bmatrix}\\ \end{aligned} $$ 

then  $ A^{-1} = $', NULL, '[{"text":"$ \\frac{1}{5}\\left[\\begin{array}{cc}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{1}{5}\\left[\\begin{array}{cc}4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\frac{1}{3}\\left[\\begin{array}{cc}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} $","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Concept:

For matrix:

 $$ \boldsymbol{A}=\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\ c&d\end{bmatrix} $$ 

inverse is:

 $$ \boldsymbol{A}^{-1}=\frac{1}{ad-bc}\begin{bmatrix}d&-b\\ -c&a\end{bmatrix} $$ 

provided determinant is non-zero.

Step 1: Finding determinant.

 $$ \left|A\right|=(2)(4)-(3)(1) $$ 

 $$ =8-3 $$ 

 $$ =5 $$ 

Step 2: Applying inverse formula.

 $$ \boldsymbol{A}^{-1}=\frac{1}{5}\begin{bmatrix}4&-3\\ -1&2\end{bmatrix} $$ 

Final Answer:

 $$ \begin{aligned}&(A)\frac{1}{5}\begin{bmatrix} \\{{{4}}}&{{{-3}}} \\{{{-1}}}&{{{2}}} \\\end{bmatrix}\\ \end{aligned} $$ 

Quick Tip: For:

 $$ \begin{bmatrix}{{{a}}}&{{{b}}} \\{{{c}}}&{{{d}}}\end{bmatrix} $$ 

inverse is:

 $$ \frac{1}{ad-bc}\left[\begin{aligned}&d&-b\\ &-c&a\end{aligned}\right] $$ 

Interchange diagonal entries and change signs of off-diagonal entries.', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.930Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions ("id", "subject_id", "topic_id", "passage_id", "text", "text_hi", "options", "options_hi", "correct_option", "explanation", "explanation_hi", "difficulty", "year", "image_url", "image_alt", "explanation_image_url", "explanation_image_alt", "status", "supersedes_id", "created_by", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "assets_json", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5968, 3, NULL, NULL, 'Solve:

 $$ \begin{aligned}\left|\begin{matrix}x&1\\ 2&x\end{matrix}\right|&=0\end{aligned} $$', NULL, '[{"text":"$ x = \\pm \\sqrt{2} $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ x = \\pm 1 $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"$ x = \\pm 2 $","image_url":null,"alt":null},{"text":"x = 0","image_url":null,"alt":null}]', NULL, 0, 'Concept:

For a  $ 2 \times 2 $ determinant:

 $$ \begin{vmatrix}{{{a}}}&{{{b}}} \\{{{c}}}&{{{d}}}\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc $$ 

Step 1: Expanding determinant.

 $$ \begin{aligned}\left|\begin{matrix}x&1\\ 2&x\end{matrix}\right|&=x(x)-(1)(2)\\&=x^{2}-2\end{aligned} $$ 

Given determinant equals zero:

 $$ x^{2}-2=0 $$ 

 $$ x^{2}=2 $$ 

Step 2: Solving equation.

 $$ x=\pm\sqrt{2} $$ 

Final Answer:

 $$ (A)\;x=\pm\sqrt{2} $$ 

Quick Tip: For determinant:

 $$ \begin{array}{c|c}{a}&{b}\\ {c}&{d}\end{array} $$ 

always use:

 $$ ad-bc $$', NULL, NULL, 2026, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'published', NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T21:00:46.930Z', NULL, NULL, '[]', 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
