Ph', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ CH_{3}-C-CH-CH_{3} $ Ph OH"},{"id":"B","text":"$ CH_{3}-C-CH-CH_{3} $ OH Ph OH"},{"id":"C","text":"$ \\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\overset{\\mathrm{C}}{\\mathrm{C}}-\\mathrm{CH}-\\mathrm{CH}_{3} $ $ \\mathrm{Ph}\\quad\\mathrm{CH}_{3} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ \\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\overset{\\mathrm{Cl}}{\\mathrm{C}}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{3} $ $ \\mathrm{Ph} $"}]', 0, 'A', NULL, NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[2]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:58:51.645Z', NULL, NULL, 'none', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (2569, 'chemistry-2023-06-13-shift-3', 'q16', 12, 12, 'Vapour pressure of an aqueous solution of non-volatile solute which boils at 101.56°C at 25°C will be (V.P. of pure water at 25°C is 200 mmHg and K_b for water is 0.52 kg mol⁻¹)', '[{"id":"A","text":"198.98 mmHg"},{"id":"B","text":"189.8 mmHg"},{"id":"C","text":"179.6 mmHg"},{"id":"D","text":"184.7 mmHg"}]', 0, 'B', NULL, NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[2]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:58:51.645Z', NULL, NULL, 'none', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (2570, 'chemistry-2023-06-13-shift-3', 'q17', 13, 13, 'In a reaction A and B reacts to form product. The initial rate of reaction (r₀) was determined using different initial concentration of A and B.

What is the initial rate of reaction (r₀) when critical concentration of A and B is 0.50 mol/L and 0.50 mol/L respectively.', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ 56.75 \\times 10^{-4} $ mol $ L^{-1}s^{-1} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ 5.675 \\times 10^{-4} $ mol $ L^{-1}s^{-1} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ 21.67 \\times 10^{-4} $ mol $ L^{-1}s^{-1} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ 2.167 \\times 10^{-4} $ mol $ L^{-1}s^{-1} $"}]', 0, 'A', NULL, NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[2]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:58:51.645Z', NULL, NULL, 'none', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (2571, 'chemistry-2023-06-13-shift-3', 'q18', 14, 14, 'The electronic conductance depends on.

(A) The nature and structure of the metal

(B) Composition of metallic conductor

(C) The number of valence electrons per atom

(D) Temperature

(E) Number of ions', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A), (B) and (C) only"},{"id":"B","text":"(A), (C) and (D) only"},{"id":"C","text":"(C), (D) and (E) only"},{"id":"D","text":"(A), (B) and (E) only"}]', 0, 'B', 'Electrical conductance through movement of electrons is called electronic conductance. Electronic conductance depends upon:

(i) The nature and structure of the metal.

(ii) Number of valence electrons per atom.

(iii) Temperature.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[2]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:58:51.645Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (2572, 'chemistry-2023-06-13-shift-3', 'q19', 15, 15, 'In which of the following actinoid elements 6d subshell is vacant?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Pa, Protactinium"},{"id":"B","text":"Np, Neptunium"},{"id":"C","text":"Lr, Lawrencium"},{"id":"D","text":"Pu, Plutonium"}]', 0, 'D', 'Pu(Z - 94) = [Rn]5f^{6}7s^{2}', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[2]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:58:51.645Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (2573, 'chemistry-2023-06-13-shift-3', 'q20', 16, 16, 'Identify the correct relation between the molar mass of solute and Ebullioscopic constant.', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ M_{2} = \\frac{1000 \\times w_{2} \\times w_{1}}{\\Delta T_{b} \\times K_{b}} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ \\Delta T_{b} = \\frac{1000 \\times w_{2} \\times K_{b} \\times M_{2}}{w_{1}} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ M_{2} = \\frac{1000 \\times w_{2} \\times K_{b}}{\\Delta T_{b} \\times w_{1}} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ M_{2} = \\frac{1000 \\times w_{1} \\times K_{b}}{\\Delta T_{b} \\times w_{2}} $"}]', 0, 'C', 'The ebullioscopic constant is also called the molal elevation constant. It is defined as the elevation in boiling point when one mole of non-volatile solute is added to one kilogram of solvent. It is denoted by Kb.

$ \Delta T_{b} = K_{b} \times \frac{w_{2} \times 1000}{w_{1} \times M_{2}} $', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[2]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:58:51.647Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (2574, 'chemistry-2023-06-13-shift-3', 'q22', 17, 17, 'Match List-I with List-II:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)"}]', 0, 'D', '[A] $ CH_{3}COCH_{3} $ will have alpha-hydrogen and give iodoform test.

[B] The above given reaction,

$ \ce{CH3COOH (aq) + NaHCO3 (s) -> CH3COONa (aq)} $ + $ \ce{H2O (l) + CO2 (g) ->} $

[C] Fehling solution, Schiff''s reagent, tollen''s reagent test is given by aldehydes only.

[D] Compounds with a phenol group will form a blue, violet, purple, green, or red-brown color upon addition of aqueous ferric chloride', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q22-table-1","kind":"table","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2023-06-13-shift-3/q22-table-1.jpg","page":2,"alt":"Table for question 22"}]', '[2]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:58:51.647Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (2575, 'chemistry-2023-06-13-shift-3', 'q23', 18, 18, 'Which of the following is not the characteristics of enzymes?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Non-specific"},{"id":"B","text":"Active at optimum pH"},{"id":"C","text":"Affected by inhibitors"},{"id":"D","text":"Highly efficient"}]', 0, 'A', '1. Speed up chemical reactions.

2. They are required in minute amounts.

3. They are highly specific in their action.

4. They are affected by temperature.

5. They are affected by pH.

6. Some catalyze reversible reactions.

7. Some require coenzymes.

8. They are inhibited by inhibitors.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[3]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:58:51.647Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (2576, 'chemistry-2023-06-13-shift-3', 'q24', 19, 19, 'Match List-I with List-II:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)"}]', 0, 'C', '$ \text{CH}_3\text{CH}_2\text{Br} + \text{KOH}_{(aq)} \rightarrow \text{CH}_3\text{CH}_2\text{OH} + \text{KBr} $

Bromoethane Ethanol

Sol.

$$ \begin{aligned}&\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Br}+\mathrm{KOH}_{(\mathrm{alc})}\rightarrow\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\mathrm{CH}_{2}+\mathrm{KBr}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\\ &\text{Bromoethane}&\text{Ethene}\\ &\mathrm{R}\stackrel{\mathrm{IX}+\mathrm{K}+\mathrm{O}}{\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{N}}=\mathrm{O}\xrightarrow[\text{Potassium nitrite}]{\mathrm{Heat}}\mathrm{R}-\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{N}=\mathrm{O}+\mathrm{KX}\\ &\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Br}+\mathrm{KNO}_{2}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{Heat}}\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{N}=\mathrm{O}+\mathrm{KBr}\\ &\end{aligned} $$

$$ \mathrm{CH_{3}CH_{2}Br}+\mathrm{AgNO_{2}}\rightarrow\mathrm{CH_{3}CH_{2}NO_{2}} $$', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q24-table-1","kind":"table","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2023-06-13-shift-3/q24-table-1.jpg","page":3,"alt":"Table for question 24"}]', '[3]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:58:51.647Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (2577, 'chemistry-2023-06-13-shift-3', 'q25', 20, 20, 'Correct order of intermolecular forces of attractions in the following solids is:

(A) NaCl

(B) I₂

(C) Ice

(D) Iron

(E) Rubber', '[{"id":"A","text":"(B), (D), (C), (E), (A)"},{"id":"B","text":"(D), (B), (C), (E), (A)"},{"id":"C","text":"(E), (B), (C), (D), (A)"},{"id":"D","text":"(E), (C), (B), (A), (A)"}]', 0, 'C', 'For sodium chloride, the Na $ ^{+} $ ion becomes surrounded by the negative side of multiple water molecules and the Cl $ ^{-} $ ions are surrounded by the positive side of multiple water molecules. This interaction is called an ion-dipole intermolecular force, which is considered one of the strongest

[B] Molecular iodine is completely non-polar. As such, the only intermolecular force between molecules of iodine is the London dispersion force.

[C] On freezing of water molecule, it converted to ice that maximizes the number of hydrogen bonds. This indicates the presence of hydrogen bonding in ice that is responsible for its solid structure. Hence, the hydrogen bonding intermolecular forces hold the ice together. Hydrogen bonds are stronger than London dispersion.

[D] Iron has strongest intermolecular forces of attraction so it is hardest element.

[E] Rubber is a natural polymer and possesses elastic properties. It is also termed as an elastomer. Elastomers have the weakest intermolecular forces', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[3]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:58:51.647Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (2578, 'chemistry-2023-06-13-shift-3', 'q26', 21, 21, 'Which of the following statement is/ are correct for complex [NiCl_{4}]^{2-}

(A) Ni has oxidation state + 2

(B) Cl is a weak field ligand

(C) Compound is paramagnetic

(D) $ dsp^{2} $ hybridisation

(E) Low spin complex', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A), (B) and (C) only"},{"id":"B","text":"(D), and (E) only"},{"id":"C","text":"(A), (B), (C) and (D) only"},{"id":"D","text":"(A) only"}]', 0, 'A', 'Cl⁻ is a weak field ligand (i.e., it induces high spin complexes). Therefore, electrons fill all orbitals before being paired

Nickel (Ni) in the complex [NiCl₄]²⁻ is in a +2 oxidation state.

In [NiCl₄]²⁻, Ni is in +2 oxidation state with the configuration 3d⁸ 4s⁰. Cl⁻ ion being weak ligand it cannot pair up the electrons in 3dorbitals. Hence it is paramagnetic

[NiCl₄]²⁻ is a tetrahedral complex with sp3 hybridization

As there are unpaired electrons in the d-orbitals, NiCl₄²⁻ is paramagnetic and is referred to as a high spin outer orbital complex', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[3]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:58:51.648Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
