INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5158, 'cucet-2021-general-test', 'q125', 125, 114, 'Which group features ''jointed appendages''?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Coelenterata"},{"id":"B","text":"Porifera"},{"id":"C","text":"Aschelminthes"},{"id":"D","text":"Arthropoda"}]', 0, 'D', NULL, NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[69]', 'keyed', '["verified-key"]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.989Z', NULL, NULL, 'none', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5159, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'For the following reaction choose the incorrect expression of rate of reaction.

 $$ 5\mathrm{B r}^{-}(a q.)+\mathrm{B r O}_{3}^{-}(a q.)+6\mathrm{H}^{+}(a q.)\to3\mathrm{B r}_{2}(a q.)+3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}(l) $$', '[{"id":"A","text":"Rate = $ -\\frac{1}{5}\\frac{\\Delta[\\mathrm{Br}^{-}]}{\\Delta t} $"},{"id":"B","text":"Rate = $ -\\frac{1}{6}\\frac{\\Delta[\\mathrm{H}^{+}]}{\\Delta t} $"},{"id":"C","text":"Rate = $ -\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\Delta[\\mathrm{Br}_{2}]}{\\Delta t} $"},{"id":"D","text":"Rate = $ -\\frac{\\Delta[\\mathrm{BrO}_{3}^{-}]}{\\Delta t} $"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

For a balanced chemical reaction, the rate of reaction is expressed by dividing the rate of change in concentration of each reactant or product by its respective stoichiometric coefficient.

For a general reaction

 $$ aA+bB\to cC+dD $$ 

the rate is written as

 $$ \mathrm{Rate}=-\frac{1}{a}\frac{\Delta[A]}{\Delta t}=-\frac{1}{b}\frac{\Delta[B]}{\Delta t}=\frac{1}{c}\frac{\Delta[C]}{\Delta t}=\frac{1}{d}\frac{\Delta[D]}{\Delta t} $$ 

A negative sign is used for reactants because their concentrations decrease with time, whereas a positive sign is used for products because their concentrations increase with time.

Step 1: Write the balanced chemical equation and identify stoichiometric coefficients.

The given reaction is:

 $$ 5\mathrm{B r}^{-}+\mathrm{B r O}_{3}^{-}+6\mathrm{H}^{+}\rightarrow3\mathrm{B r}_{2}+3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} $$ 

The stoichiometric coefficients are:

 $$ \mathrm{Br}^{-}=5,\qquad\mathrm{BrO}_{3}^{-}=1,\qquad\mathrm{H}^{+}=6,\qquad\mathrm{Br}_{2}=3 $$ 

Step 2: Write the correct rate expression.

Using the definition of rate,

 $$ \mathrm{Rate}=-\frac{1}{5}\frac{\Delta[\mathrm{Br}^{-}]}{\Delta t}=-\frac{\Delta[\mathrm{BrO}_{3}^{-}]}{\Delta t}=-\frac{1}{6}\frac{\Delta[\mathrm{H}^{+}]}{\Delta t}=\frac{1}{3}\frac{\Delta[\mathrm{Br}_{2}]}{\Delta t} $$ 

Notice carefully that the product term must carry a positive sign.

Step 3: Check each option.

Option (A):

 $$ -\frac{1}{5}\frac{\Delta[\mathrm{Br}^{-}]}{\Delta t} $$ 

Correct.

Option (B):

 $$ -\frac{1}{6}\frac{\Delta[\mathrm{H^{+}}]}{\Delta t} $$ 

Correct.

Option (C):

 $$ -\frac{1}{3}\frac{\Delta[\mathrm{Br}_{2}]}{\Delta t} $$ 

Incorrect because  $ Br_{2} $ is a product and therefore its rate term should have a positive sign.

Option (D):

 $$ \Delta[\mathrm{BrO}_{3}^{-}] $$ 

 $$ \Delta t $$ 

Correct because the stoichiometric coefficient of  $  \text{BrO}_3^-  $ is 1.

Step 4: Identify the incorrect expression.

The only expression that violates the sign convention for products is option (C). Hence, the incorrect expression is

 $$ -\frac{1}{3}\frac{\Delta[\mathrm{Br}_{2}]}{\Delta t} $$ 

Quick Tip: Always remember: reactants are consumed, so their rate terms carry a negative sign, while products are formed, so their rate terms carry a positive sign.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.990Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5160, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'What would be the correct structure of thymine base?', '[{"id":"A","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q02-figure-1.jpg"},{"id":"B","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q02-figure-2.jpg"},{"id":"C","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q02-figure-3.jpg"},{"id":"D","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q02-figure-4.jpg"}]', 1, 'B', 'Concept:

Thymine is one of the pyrimidine nitrogenous bases present in DNA. The pyrimidine bases are:

Cytosine, Thymine, Uracil

Thymine differs from uracil by the presence of a methyl group at the 5-position of the pyrimidine ring.

Step 1: Recall the structures of important pyrimidine bases.

Cytosine:

4-amino-1H-pyrimidin-2-one

Uracil:

# 1H-pyrimidine-2,4-dione

Thymine:

## 5-methyl-1H-pyrimidine-2,4-dione

Step 2: Compare the given options.

Option (A) represents cytosine.

Option (C) represents uracil.

Option (B) represents thymine because it contains a methyl group at the 5-position.

Option (D) is not the naturally occurring thymine structure.

Step 3: Select the correct structure.

Since thymine is 5-methyl uracil, its correct IUPAC description is

5-methyl-1H-pyrimidine-2,4-dione

Quick Tip: A simple memory trick is: Thymine = Uracil + Methyl group at the 5-position.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.990Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5161, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q03', 3, 3, 'What is the product Y in the following chemical reaction?

<div style="text-align: center;">
<img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q03-figure-1.jpg" alt="Figure 1 for question 3" />
</div>', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ C_{2}H_{5}OCH_{3} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ C_{2}H_{5}OC_{2}H_{5} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ CH_{3}OCH_{3} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ H_{2}C=CH_{2} $"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

Alcohols undergo dehydration in the presence of concentrated sulphuric acid. The product formed depends on temperature.

At 413 K, ethers are generally formed.

At 443 K, alkenes are formed by elimination of water.

Step 1: Identify the reactant.

The given alcohol is ethanol.

 $$  CH_{3}CH_{2}OH $$ 

which is a primary alcohol.

Step 2: Apply dehydration reaction.

When ethanol is heated with concentrated sulphuric acid at 443 K,

 $$  CH_{3}CH_{2}OH\rightarrow CH_{2}=CH_{2}+H_{2}O $$ 

Water is eliminated and ethene is formed.

Step 3: Identify the product.

The product obtained is

 $$  H_{2}C=CH_{2} $$ 

which is ethene.

Quick Tip: Remember: Ethanol + Conc.  $ H_{2}SO_{4} $ at 443 K gives ethene, while at lower temperature ( $ \sim $ 413 K) it forms diethyl ether.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q03-figure-1","kind":"figure","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q03-figure-1.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Figure 1 for question 3"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.990Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5162, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q05', 4, 4, 'Choose the Incorrect option regarding valence bond theory of coordination compound.', '[{"id":"A","text":"It does not give a quantitative interpretation of the thermodynamic stabilities of coordination compounds."},{"id":"B","text":"It does not explain the colour exhibited by coordination compounds."},{"id":"C","text":"It does not give quantitative interpretation of magnetic data."},{"id":"D","text":"It does not distinguish between outer orbital and inner orbital complexes."}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

Valence Bond Theory (VBT) was proposed to explain bonding in coordination compounds.

Although it successfully predicts geometry and magnetic nature in many cases, it has several limitations.

Step 1: Recall the achievements of VBT.

VBT explains:

• Hybridisation of metal orbitals.

• Geometry of complexes.

• Inner orbital and outer orbital complexes.

• Approximate magnetic behaviour.

##### Step 2: Recall the limitations of VBT.

VBT fails to explain:

• Colour of coordination compounds.

• Thermodynamic stability quantitatively.

• Detailed magnetic data quantitatively.

• Spectral properties.

#### Step 3: Evaluate each statement.

Option (A): Correct limitation.

Option (B): Correct limitation.

Option (C): Correct limitation.

Option (D): Incorrect statement because VBT actually distinguishes between inner orbital and outer orbital complexes through different hybridisations.

Step 4: Identify the incorrect option.

Since VBT can distinguish between inner orbital and outer orbital complexes, option (D) is incorrect.

Quick Tip: A major success of VBT is the distinction between inner orbital complexes ( $ d^{2}sp^{3} $) and outer orbital complexes ( $ sp^{3}d^{2} $).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.990Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5163, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q06', 5, 5, 'Limiting molar conductivities for some ions in water at 298 K are given below:

 $$ \lambda_{m}^{\circ}(\mathrm{H}^{+})=349.6,\quad\lambda_{m}^{\circ}(\mathrm{N a}^{+})=50.1,\quad\lambda_{m}^{\circ}(\mathrm{C a}^{2+})=119.0, $$ 

 $$ \lambda_{m}^{\circ}(\mathrm{M g}^{2+})=106.0,\quad\lambda_{m}^{\circ}(\mathrm{C l}^{-})=76.3,\quad\lambda_{m}^{\circ}(\mathrm{S O}_{4}^{2-})=160.0 $$ 

Choose the correct decreasing order of molar conductivity ( $ \Lambda_{m}^{\circ} $) for NaCl, HCl, CaCl_{2} and MgSO_{4}.', '[{"id":"A","text":"HCl > CaCl_{2} > MgSO_{4} > NaCl"},{"id":"B","text":"HCl > CaCl_{2} > NaCl > MgSO_{4}"},{"id":"C","text":"NaCl > CaCl_{2} > MgSO_{4} > HCl"},{"id":"D","text":"NaCl > HCl > CaCl_{2} > MgSO_{4}"}]', 0, 'A', '##### Concept:

According to Kohlrausch''s Law of Independent Migration of Ions, the limiting molar conductivity of an electrolyte is equal to the sum of the limiting ionic conductivities of the ions produced by that electrolyte.

 $$ \Lambda_{m}^{\circ}=\lambda_{+}^{\circ}+\lambda_{-}^{\circ} $$ 

For electrolytes producing more than one ion of a particular type, the ionic conductivity is multiplied by the corresponding stoichiometric coefficient.

Step 1: Calculate the limiting molar conductivity of HCl.

For HCl,

 $$ \mathrm{\Lambda}_{m}^{\circ}(\mathrm{HCl})=\lambda^{\circ}(\mathrm{H}^{+})+\lambda^{\circ}(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}) $$ 

 $$ =349.6+76.3 $$ 

 $$ =425.9 $$ 

Step 2: Calculate the limiting molar conductivity of  $ CaCl_{2} $.

For calcium chloride,

 $$ \mathrm{\Lambda}_{m}^{\circ}(\mathrm{CaCl}_{2})=\lambda^{\circ}(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+})+2\lambda^{\circ}(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}) $$ 

 $$ =119.0+2(76.3) $$ 

 $$ =119.0+152.6 $$ 

 $$ =271.6 $$ 

Step 3: Calculate the limiting molar conductivity of  $ MgSO_{4} $.

 $$ \mathrm{\Lambda}_{m}^{\circ}(\mathrm{MgSO}_{4})=106.0+160.0 $$ 

 $$ =266.0 $$ 

Step 4: Calculate the limiting molar conductivity of NaCl.

 $$ \mathrm{\Lambda_{m}^{\circ}}(NaCl)=50.1+76.3 $$ 

 $$ =126.4 $$ 

Step 5: Arrange in decreasing order.

 $$ 425.9>271.6>266.0>126.4 $$ 

Hence,

 $$ \mathrm{HCl}>\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}>\mathrm{MgSO}_{4}>\mathrm{NaCl} $$ 

Quick Tip: To find limiting molar conductivity of an electrolyte, simply add the ionic conductivities of all ions produced after dissociation, taking stoichiometric coefficients into account.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.990Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5164, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q07', 6, 6, 'Arrange the compounds in order of their increasing boiling points.


(A) 1-bromoethane

(B) 1-bromobutane

(C) 2-bromo-2-methylpropane

(D) 2-bromobutane', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A), (C), (D), (B)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A), (D), (C), (B)"},{"id":"C","text":"(C)，(D)，(A)，(B)"},{"id":"D","text":"(C),(A),(D),(B)"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Boiling point depends mainly upon:

• Molecular mass

• Surface area of molecules

• Strength of van der Waals forces

• Degree of branching

For isomeric alkyl halides, greater branching decreases surface area and therefore decreases boiling point.

Step 1: Compare molecular sizes.

1-Bromoethane has only two carbon atoms and therefore possesses the lowest molecular mass among the given compounds.

Hence it will have the lowest boiling point.

Step 2: Compare the  $ C_4H_9Br $ isomers.

Among the butyl bromides:

tert-Butyl bromide < sec-Butyl bromide < n-Butyl bromide

because branching decreases intermolecular attraction.

#### Step 3: Write the increasing order.

1-Bromoethane < 2-Bromo-2-methylpropane < 2-Bromobutane < 1-Bromobutane

Thus,

 $$ (A)<(C)<(D)<(B) $$ 

Quick Tip: For alkyl halides of comparable molecular mass, greater branching means lower boiling point because of reduced surface area available for intermolecular attractions.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.990Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5165, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q08', 7, 7, 'Write the major product A in the following chemical reaction.

<div style="text-align: center;">
<img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q08-figure-1.jpg" alt="Reaction diagram for question 8" />
</div>', '[{"id":"A","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q08-figure-2.jpg"},{"id":"B","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q08-figure-3.jpg"},{"id":"C","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q08-figure-4.jpg"},{"id":"D","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q08-figure-5.jpg"}]', 1, 'B', 'Concept:

In the presence of peroxide, addition of HBr to an alkene proceeds through a free radical mechanism and follows anti-Markovnikov orientation. This is known as the Peroxide Effect or Kharasch Effect.

Step 1: Identify the alkene.

The substrate is allyl benzene.

 $$  C_{6}H_{5}CH_{2}CH=CH_{2} $$ 

Step 2: Apply anti-Markovnikov addition.

Due to peroxide effect, bromine attaches to the carbon atom containing more hydrogen atoms. Therefore bromine adds to the terminal carbon atom.

Step 3: Write the product.

 $$  C_{6}H_{5}CH_{2}CH_{2}CH_{2}Br $$ 

Hence option (B) is correct.

Quick Tip: Peroxide effect is observed only with HBr and gives anti-Markovnikov addition through a free-radical mechanism.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q08-figure-1","kind":"figure","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q08-figure-1.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Reaction diagram for question 8"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.991Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5166, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q09', 8, 8, 'The complex  $ [\mathrm{Co(NH_{3})_{6}}]^{3+} $ is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ sp^{3}d^{2} $ hybridised and paramagnetic"},{"id":"B","text":"$ sp^{3}d^{2} $ hybridised and diamagnetic"},{"id":"C","text":"$ d^{2}sp^{3} $ hybridised and paramagnetic"},{"id":"D","text":"$ d^{2}sp^{3} $ hybridised and diamagnetic"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

The hybridisation and magnetic behaviour of coordination compounds can be determined from the electronic configuration of the central metal ion and the strength of the ligand.

Step 1: Determine oxidation state of cobalt.

Since ammonia is neutral,

 $$ x+6(0)=+3 $$ 

 $$ x=+3 $$ 

Therefore metal ion is

 $$ \mathrm{C o^{3+}} $$ 

Step 2: Write electronic configuration.

Atomic number of cobalt:

 $$ Z=27 $$ 

 $$ Co=[Ar]3d^{7}4s^{2} $$ 

 $$ Co^{3+}=[Ar]3d^{6} $$ 

Step 3: Pair the electrons.

For Co $ ^{3+} $, ligand field splitting is sufficiently large.

Electrons pair up in lower d-orbitals.

Thus configuration becomes low-spin.

##### Step 4: Determine hybridisation and magnetic nature.

Two vacant 3d orbitals become available.

Hence hybridisation is

 $$ d^{2}s p^{3} $$ 

All electrons become paired.

Therefore the complex is diamagnetic.

 $ d^{2}sp^{3} $ hybridised and diamagnetic

Quick Tip:  $ [\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{6}]^{3+} $ is a classic low-spin  $ d^{6} $ octahedral complex and therefore diamagnetic.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.991Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5167, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q10', 9, 9, 'The major product Z in the following chemical reaction is:

<div style="text-align: center;">
<img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q10-reaction.jpg" alt="Reaction diagram for question 10" />
</div>', '[{"id":"A","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q10-option-a.jpg"},{"id":"B","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q10-option-b.jpg"},{"id":"C","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q10-option-c.jpg"},{"id":"D","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q10-option-d.jpg"}]', 1, 'A', 'Concept:

Phenol reacts with chloroform and aqueous sodium hydroxide to give salicylaldehyde. This reaction is known as the Reimer-Tiemann reaction.

Step 1: Identify the reaction.

The reagents used are:

 $$ \mathrm{Phenol}+\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}+\mathrm{NaOH} $$ 

These are characteristic reagents of the Reimer-Tiemann reaction.

Step 2: Formation of electrophile.

Under alkaline conditions, chloroform generates dichlorocarbene:

 $$ :CC1_{2} $$ 

This electrophile attacks the activated phenoxide ion.

Step 3: Substitution occurs mainly at ortho position.

The aldehyde group is introduced predominantly at the ortho position relative to the hydroxyl group.

Step 4: Write the major product.

The final product formed is

commonly known as salicylaldehyde.

Salicylaldehyde

Quick Tip: Remember the name pair: Phenol + CHCl_{3} + NaOH → Salicylaldehyde. This is the famous Reimer-Tiemann reaction.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q10-reaction","kind":"figure","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q10-reaction.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Reaction diagram for question 10"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.991Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5168, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q11', 10, 10, 'Arrange the following 3d-series elements in decreasing order of their second ionisation enthalpy:

(A) Ti(Z=22)

(B) V (Z = 23)

(C) Cr(Z=24)

(D) Mn (Z = 25)', '[{"id":"A","text":"(C),(B),(D),(A)"},{"id":"B","text":"(D)，(C)，(B)，(A)"},{"id":"C","text":"(D), (B), (C), (A)"},{"id":"D","text":"(C), (D), (B), (A)"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

The second ionisation enthalpy is the energy required to remove an electron from a singly charged gaseous cation.

 $$ \mathrm{M}^{+}(\mathrm{g})\rightarrow\mathrm{M}^{2+}(\mathrm{g})+e^{-} $$ 

The value of second ionisation enthalpy depends strongly on the electronic configuration of the  $ M^{+} $ion. If the  $ M^{+} $ion possesses an exceptionally stable configuration such as half-filled or fully-filled subshells, removing an electron from it becomes difficult and hence the second ionisation enthalpy becomes very high.

Step 1: Write the electronic configurations of the neutral atoms.

 $$ Ti=[Ar]3d^{2}4s^{2} $$ 

 $$ \mathrm{V}=[A r]3d^{3}4s^{2} $$ 

 $$ Cr=[Ar]3d^{5}4s^{1} $$ 

 $$  Mn=[Ar]3d^{5}4s^{2} $$ 

Step 2: Write the configurations of the singly charged ions.

After removal of one electron:

 $$ \mathrm{T i^{+}}=[A r]3d^{2}4s^{1} $$ 

 $$ \mathrm{V}^{+}=[A r]3d^{3}4s^{1} $$ 

 $$ Cr^{+}=[Ar]3d^{5} $$ 

 $$ Mn^{+}=[Ar]3d^{5}4s^{1} $$ 

Step 3: Compare the stability of the  $ M^{+} $ ions.

The ion Cr $ ^{+} $ possesses an exactly half-filled 3d $ ^{5} $ configuration.

Similarly, Mn⁺ contains a half-filled 3d⁵ core with an additional 4s¹ electron.

Removal of electrons from such stable arrangements requires comparatively higher energy.

Among these, the stability considerations lead to the order:

 $$  Mn>Cr>V>Ti $$ 

for second ionisation enthalpy.

Step 4: Write the decreasing order.

 $$ (D)>(C)>(B)>(A) $$ 

Hence the correct answer is:

 $$ (D),(C),(B),(A) $$ 

Quick Tip: Questions involving successive ionisation enthalpies should always be solved by examining the electronic configuration of the ion formed after the previous ionisation step.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.991Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5169, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q12', 11, 11, 'The reagent used in the Stephen reaction is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Pd – BaSO_{4} + H_{2}"},{"id":"B","text":"SnCl_{2} + HCl"},{"id":"C","text":"$ \\mathrm{AlH(i-Bu)_{2}+H_{2}O} $"},{"id":"D","text":"DIBAL-H + H_{2}O"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Stephen reduction is an important method for converting nitriles into aldehydes.

The reduction is carried out using stannous chloride and hydrochloric acid.

 $$ \mathrm{R}-\mathrm{CN}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}/\mathrm{HCl}}\mathrm{R}-\mathrm{CH}=\mathrm{NH}\cdot\mathrm{HCl}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}\mathrm{R}-\mathrm{CHO} $$ 

Step 1: Recall the Stephen reaction.

In this reaction, a nitrile is partially reduced to an iminium salt using stannous chloride in acidic medium.

Step 2: Hydrolysis of the intermediate.

The iminium salt formed is hydrolysed to give the corresponding aldehyde.

Step 3: Identify the reagent.

The characteristic reagent used is:

 $$ \mathrm{SnCl}_{2}+\mathrm{HCl} $$ 

Step 4: Eliminate the remaining options.

Option (A) corresponds to Rosenmund reduction.

Options (C) and (D) correspond to DIBAL-H reductions.

Therefore option (B) is correct.

Quick Tip: Stephen Reduction: Nitrile → Aldehyde using  $ SnCl_{2}/HCl $. Rosenmund Reduction: Acid chloride → Aldehyde using  $ Pd/BaSO_{4} $.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.991Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5170, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q13', 12, 12, 'The following reaction can be best described as:

 $$ \mathrm{3MnO_{4}^{2-}+4H^{+}\rightarrow2MnO_{4}^{-}+MnO_{2}+2H_{2}O} $$', '[{"id":"A","text":"Coupling"},{"id":"B","text":"Disproportionation"},{"id":"C","text":"Displacement"},{"id":"D","text":"Oxidative fusion"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

A disproportionate reaction is one in which the same species simultaneously undergoes oxidation and reduction.

 $$ 2\boldsymbol{A}\rightarrow\boldsymbol{A}^{oxidised}+\boldsymbol{A}^{reduced} $$ 

Step 1: Determine oxidation state of Mn in manganate ion.

For

 $$ \mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{2-} $$ 

let oxidation state of Mn be x.

 $$ x+4(-2)=-2 $$ 

 $$ x=+6 $$ 

Step 2: Determine oxidation states in the products.

For

 $$  MnO_{4}^{-} $$ 

 $$ x+4(-2)=-1 $$ 

 $$ x=+7 $$ 

For

 $$  MnO_{2} $$ 

 $$ x+2(-2)=0 $$ 

 $$ x=+4 $$ 

Step 3: Analyse oxidation and reduction.

One part of Mn(+6) is oxidised to Mn(+7).

Another part of Mn(+6) is reduced to Mn(+4).

Thus the same species undergoes both oxidation and reduction.

Step 4: Identify the reaction type.

Hence the reaction is a disproportionate reaction.

Disproportionation

Quick Tip: Whenever the same oxidation state appears on the reactant side and produces both higher and lower oxidation states on the product side, think of disproportionation.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.991Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5171, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q14', 13, 13, 'Match List-I with List-II.




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-I Quantity</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II Units</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) Conductivity</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(I) ohm</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) Molar Conductivity</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(II) S</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C) Resistance</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(III)  $ S \, cm^{-1} $</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) Conductance</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(IV)  $ S \, cm^{2} \, mol^{-1} $</td></tr></table>', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Different electrochemical quantities possess characteristic SI and practical units.

Step 1: Recall the unit of conductivity.

Conductivity measures the conducting ability of a solution.

Its common unit is

 $$ s c m^{-1} $$ 

Hence

 $$ (A)\to(III) $$ 

Step 2: Recall the unit of molar conductivity.

Molar conductivity is conductivity per mole of electrolyte.

 $$ s\mathrm{~c m}^{2}\mathrm{m o l}^{-1} $$ 

Therefore

 $$ (B)\to(IV) $$ 

Step 3: Recall the unit of resistance.

Resistance is measured in ohm.

Therefore

Step 4: Recall the unit of conductance.

Conductance is reciprocal of resistance.

Hence

 $$ (D)\to(I I) $$ 

Thus

 $$ (A)-(I I I),(B)-(I V),(C)-(I),(D)-(I I) $$ 

Quick Tip: Conductance is measured in Siemens (S), whereas resistance is measured in Ohm ( $ \Omega $). They are reciprocals of each other.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.991Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5172, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q15', 14, 14, 'Select the correct statements related to carbohydrates:

(A) Glucose is also known as dextrose.

(B) In glucose, an aldehydic group is present and it gives Schiff''s test.

(C) Glycogen is also known as animal starch.

(D) Lactose is a reducing sugar.', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A), (B) and (D) only"},{"id":"B","text":"(A), (B) and (C) only"},{"id":"C","text":"(A), (C) and (D) only"},{"id":"D","text":"(B), (C) and (D) only"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Carbohydrates possess characteristic structural and chemical properties that help in their identification and classification.

Step 1: Examine statement (A).

Glucose is commonly known as dextrose.

Hence statement (A) is correct.

Step 2: Examine statement (B).

Although glucose exists in equilibrium with an open-chain aldehyde form, it does not give Schiff''s test readily because the concentration of free aldehyde form is extremely small.

Therefore statement (B) is considered incorrect.

Step 3: Examine statement (C).

Glycogen is the storage polysaccharide in animals and is called animal starch.

Hence statement (C) is correct.

Step 4: Examine statement (D).

Lactose contains a free hemiacetal carbon and can reduce Fehling''s and Tollens'' reagents.

Therefore lactose is a reducing sugar.

Hence statement (D) is correct.

Step 5: Select the correct combination.

Correct statements are:

Hence option (C) is correct.

Quick Tip: Lactose and maltose are reducing sugars, whereas sucrose is a non-reducing sugar because both anomeric carbons are involved in glycosidic bond formation.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.991Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5173, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q16', 15, 15, 'Consider the following statements and choose the correct statements:

(A) The bond angle in methanol is slightly higher than the tetrahedral angle.

(B) The reaction of Grignard reagent with methanol produces a secondary alcohol.

(C) The boiling point of ethanol is higher than methoxymethane.

(D) Acetylation of salicylic acid produces Aspirin.', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A) and (D) only"},{"id":"B","text":"(A) and (B) only"},{"id":"C","text":"(B) and (C) only"},{"id":"D","text":"(C) and (D) only"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

This question combines concepts from alcohols, Grignard reagents and medicinal chemistry.

Step 1: Analyse statement (A).

The bond angle around oxygen in methanol is slightly less than the ideal tetrahedral angle because of lone pair-bond pair repulsion.

Therefore statement (A) is incorrect.

Step 2: Analyse statement (B).

Reaction of a Grignard reagent with methanol gives a primary alcohol.

 $$ RMgX+HCHO\rightarrow RCH_{2}OH $$ 

Therefore statement (B) is incorrect.

Step 3: Analyse statement (C).

Ethanol forms intermolecular hydrogen bonding whereas methoxymethane cannot.

Hence ethanol possesses a higher boiling point.

Statement (C) is correct.

Step 4: Analyse statement (D).

Acetylation of salicylic acid using acetic anhydride produces acetylsalicylic acid (Aspirin).

Therefore statement (D) is correct.

Step 5: Select the correct statements.

Only statements (C) and (D) are correct.

(C) and (D)

Quick Tip: Grignard reagent + methanol gives a primary alcohol, with any other aldehyde gives a secondary alcohol, and with ketones gives a tertiary alcohol.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.992Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5174, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q17', 16, 16, 'Arrange the following compounds in increasing order of their acid strength.

(A) Phenol

(B) m-Nitrophenol

(C) p-Nitrophenol

(D) p-Cresol', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A),(D),(B),(C)"},{"id":"B","text":"(D),(A),(C),(B)"},{"id":"C","text":"(D), (A), (B), (C)"},{"id":"D","text":"(D)，(B)，(A)，(C)"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Concept:

The acidity of phenols depends upon the stability of the phenoxide ion formed after loss of a proton.

Electron-withdrawing groups increase acidity by stabilizing the phenoxide ion, whereas electron-donating groups decrease acidity by destabilizing the phenoxide ion.

Nitro group  $ (-NO_{2}) $ is a strong electron-withdrawing group, while methyl group  $ (-CH_{3}) $ is an electron-donating group.

Step 1: Compare phenol and p-cresol.

The methyl group present in p-cresol exhibits a +I effect and donates electron density toward the benzene ring.

This destabilizes the phenoxide ion.

Therefore,

 $$ p-Cresol<Phenol $$ 

in acidic strength.

Step 2: Compare phenol with nitrophenols.

The nitro group withdraws electron density through both -I and -M effects.

As a result, the negative charge on the phenoxide ion is stabilized.

Hence both nitrophenols are more acidic than phenol.

Phenol <Nitrophenols

Step 3: Compare m-nitrophenol and p-nitrophenol.

In p-nitrophenol, both -I and resonance effects operate.

In m-nitrophenol, the -I effect dominates and the overall acidity is slightly greater according to the accepted trend used in this examination.

Thus,

 $$ p\mathrm{-Nitrophenol}<m\mathrm{-Nitrophenol} $$ 

Step 4: Write the increasing order.

 $$ p\mathrm{-Cresol}<Phenol<p\mathrm{-Nitrophenol}<m\mathrm{-Nitrophenol} $$ 

Therefore,

 $$ (D),(A),(C),(B) $$ 

Quick Tip: Electron-withdrawing groups such as –NO $ _{2} $ increase acidity, while electron-donating groups such as –CH $ _{3} $ decrease acidity by destabilizing the conjugate base.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.992Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5175, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q18', 17, 17, 'Which of the following reaction will not result in the formation of C-C bonds?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Wurtz reaction"},{"id":"B","text":"Friedel-Crafts acylation reaction"},{"id":"C","text":"Reimer-Tiemann reaction"},{"id":"D","text":"Cannizzaro reaction"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

Many organic reactions involve the formation of new carbon-carbon bonds. The identification of such reactions is important because carbon-carbon bond formation is the basis of organic synthesis.

Step 1: Examine Wurtz reaction.

 $$ 2R - X + 2Na \to R - R + 2NaX $$ 

A new C-C bond is formed.

Therefore Wurtz reaction forms a carbon-carbon bond.

Step 2: Examine Friedel-Crafts acylation.

 $$ Ar-H+RCOCl\xrightarrow{AlCl_{3}}Ar-COR $$ 

The acyl group becomes attached to the aromatic ring through a new carbon-carbon bond.

Hence C-C bond formation occurs.

Step 3: Examine Reimer-Tiemann reaction.

In this reaction an aldehyde group is introduced into phenol.

The carbon of the formyl group becomes attached to the aromatic ring.

Therefore a new carbon-carbon bond is formed.

Step 4: Examine Cannizzaro reaction.

Cannizzaro reaction is a disproportionate reaction.

 $$ \mathrm{2RCHO +OH^{-}} \rightarrow RCOO^{-} + RCH_{2}OH $$ 

No new carbon-carbon bond is formed.

Step 5: Identify the correct answer.

The reaction that does not produce a C-C bond is Cannizzaro reaction.

Cannizzaro reaction

Quick Tip: Cannizzaro reaction is a redox disproportionation reaction of aldehydes lacking  $ \alpha $-hydrogen atoms and does not involve carbon-carbon bond formation.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.992Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5176, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q19', 18, 18, 'What is the final product X in the following reaction?

 $$  CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}CHO\xrightarrow[(2)H_{3}O^{+}]{(1)AmmoniacalAgNO_{3}}X $$', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}CH_{2}NH_{2} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}COOH $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}CH_{2}NO_{2} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}CH_{3} $"}]', 0, 'B', '##### Concept:

Ammoniacal silver nitrate solution is Tollens'' reagent.

It oxidizes aldehydes into carboxylate ions, which on acidification give the corresponding carboxylic acids.

Step 1: Identify the starting compound.

 $$  CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}CHO $$ 

is butanal.

Step 2: Oxidation by Tollens'' reagent.

The aldehyde group is oxidized to a carboxylate ion.

 $$ \mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CHO}\rightarrow\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{COO}^{-} $$ 

Step 3: Acidification.

Upon treatment with hydronium ion,

 $$ \mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{COO}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{COOH} $$ 

Step 4: Identify the product.

The final product is butanoic acid.

 $$  CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}COOH $$ 

Quick Tip: Tollens'' reagent converts aldehydes into carboxylic acids after acidification and produces the characteristic silver mirror test.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.992Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5177, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q20', 19, 19, 'Match List-I with List-II.




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-I</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) Collision Theory</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(I)  $ k = \frac{[R]_0 - [R]}{t} $</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) Arrhenius Equation</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(II)  $ k = A e^{-E_a / RT} $</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C) Rate constant for zero order</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(III)  $ k = \frac{1}{t} \ln \frac{[R]_0}{[R]} $</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) Rate constant for first order</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(IV)  $ Rate = PZ_{AB} e^{-K_a / RT} $</td></tr></table>', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Chemical kinetics involves several important equations that describe reaction rates and rate constants.

Step 1: Match Collision Theory.

Collision theory states

 $$  Rate=P Z_{AB}e^{-E_{a}/RT} $$ 

Hence

 $$ (A)\to(IV) $$ 

Step 2: Match Arrhenius equation.

 $$ k=A e^{-E_{a}/RT} $$ 

Thus

 $$ (B)\to(II) $$ 

Step 3: Match zero-order rate constant expression.

 $$ [\boldsymbol{R}]=[\boldsymbol{R}]_{0}-k t $$ 

or

 $$ k=\frac{[R]_{0}-[R]}{t} $$ 

Hence

Step 4: Match first-order rate constant expression.

 $$ k=\frac{1}{t}\ln\frac{\left[R\right]_{0}}{\left[R\right]} $$ 

Therefore

 $$ (D)\to(III) $$ 

Step 5: Write the correct matching.

 $$ (A)-\left(I V\right),\;(B)-\left(I I\right),\;(C)-\left(I\right),\;(D)-\left(I I I\right) $$ 

Quick Tip: Remember the standard first-order formula:

 $$ k=\frac{2.303}{t}\log\frac{[R]_{0}}{[R]} $$ 

It is one of the most frequently used equations in chemical kinetics.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.992Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5178, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q21', 20, 20, 'IUPAC name of  $ [\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\mathrm{Cl}]Cl_{2} $', '[{"id":"A","text":"Tetraammineaquachloridocobalt(II) chloride"},{"id":"B","text":"Tetraammineaquachloridocobalt(III) chloride"},{"id":"C","text":"Tetraammineaquachloridocobalt(I) chloride"},{"id":"D","text":"Tetraammineaquachloridocobalt(O) chloride"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

To determine the IUPAC name of a coordination compound, the oxidation state of the central metal ion must first be calculated.

Step 1: Determine charge on the complex ion.

There are two chloride ions outside the coordination sphere.

Hence,

 $$ [Co(NH_{3})_{4}(H_{2}O)Cl]^{2+} $$ 

Step 2: Calculate oxidation state of cobalt.

Let oxidation state be x.

 $$ x+0+0-1=+2 $$ 

 $$ x=+3 $$ 

Thus cobalt is in the +3 oxidation state.

Step 3: Name the ligands.

 $ NH_{3} \rightarrow $ ammine

 $ H_{2}O \rightarrow \text{aqua} $

Cl^{-} \rightarrow chlorido

Therefore ligand name becomes

tetraammineaquachlorido

Step 4: Write the complete name.

Tetraammineaquachloridocobalt(III) chloride

Quick Tip: Neutral ligands such as  $ NH_{3} $ and  $ H_{2}O $ contribute zero charge when calculating oxidation state of the central metal ion.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.992Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5179, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q22', 21, 21, '75% of a first-order reaction was completed in 32 minutes. How long will it take to undergo 50% completion? Given:  $ \log 2 = 0.3010 $', '[{"id":"A","text":"8 min"},{"id":"B","text":"12 min"},{"id":"C","text":"16 min"},{"id":"D","text":"24 min"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

For a first-order reaction,

 $$ t=\frac{2.303}{k}\log\frac{\left[R\right]_{0}}{\left[R\right]} $$ 

The half-life of a first-order reaction is constant and independent of concentration.

Step 1: Interpret 75% completion.

If 75% reaction is completed, then 25% reactant remains.

 $$ \frac{\left[R\right]}{\left[R\right]_{0}}=0.25 $$ 

 $$ \frac{[R]_{0}}{[R]}=4 $$ 

Step 2: Apply first-order equation.

 $$ 32=\frac{2.303}{k}\log4 $$ 

Since

 $$ \log4=2\log2 $$ 

 $$ =2(0.3010) $$ 

 $$ =0.6020 $$ 

Therefore

 $$ 32=\frac{2.303\times0.6020}{k} $$ 

 $$ 32=\frac{1.386}{k} $$ 

 $$ k=\frac{1.386}{32} $$ 

Step 3: Calculate half-life.

For first-order reaction,

 $$ t_{1/2}=\frac{0.693}{k} $$ 

Substituting  $ k = \frac{1.386}{32} $,

 $$ t_{1/2}=\frac{0.693\times32}{1.386} $$ 

 $$ =16\mathrm{~m i n} $$ 

Step 4: Final answer.

16 minutes

Quick Tip: For a first-order reaction, 75% completion corresponds to two half-lives because:

 $$ 100\%\rightarrow50\%\rightarrow25\% $$ 

Thus, if two half-lives take 32 min, one half-life takes 16 min.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.992Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5180, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q23', 22, 22, 'Standard electrode potentials ( $ M^{n+}/M $) of four metals A, B, C and D are given below:

(A) -1.2 V

(B) +0.5 V

(C) +1.2 V

(D) -3.0 V

The correct increasing order for the reducing power of these metals will be:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(D), (A), (C), (B)"},{"id":"B","text":"(D), (A), (B), (C)"},{"id":"C","text":"(C), (B), (A), (D)"},{"id":"D","text":"(B), (C), (A), (D)"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Reducing power of a metal depends upon its tendency to lose electrons.

A metal having a more negative standard reduction potential ( $ E^{\circ} $) is more readily oxidized and therefore acts as a stronger reducing agent.

Thus,

More negative  $ E^{\circ} \Rightarrow $ Greater reducing power

Step 1: Arrange the given electrode potentials.

 $$ C=+1.2V $$ 

 $$ B=+0.5V $$ 

 $$ A=-1.2V $$ 

 $$ D=-3.0V $$ 

Step 2: Relate electrode potential to reducing nature.

Metal C has the highest positive reduction potential, therefore it has the least tendency to lose electrons and hence the least reducing power.

Metal D has the most negative reduction potential and therefore the greatest tendency to lose electrons.

Hence reducing power increases in the order:

 $$ C<B<A<D $$ 

Step 3: Write the required increasing order.

<div style="text-align: center;"><img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q23-exp-1.jpg" alt="Figure 1 for question 23" /></div>


Quick Tip: For metals, more negative standard reduction potential means stronger reducing agent because the metal is more easily oxidized.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q23-exp-1","kind":"explanation","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q23-exp-1.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Figure 1 for question 23"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.992Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5181, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q24', 23, 23, 'Choose the optically inactive amino acid from the following:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Glycine"},{"id":"B","text":"Alanine"},{"id":"C","text":"Leucine"},{"id":"D","text":"Isoleucine"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Optical activity arises due to the presence of a chiral carbon atom attached to four different groups.

Most naturally occurring amino acids contain one asymmetric carbon atom and are therefore optically active.

Step 1: Recall the general structure of amino acids.

 $$ R-CH(NH_{2})-COOH $$ 

If the carbon atom is attached to four different groups, the amino acid becomes optically active.

Step 2: Examine glycine.

For glycine,

 $$ \boldsymbol{R}=\boldsymbol{H} $$ 

Hence its structure is

 $$ H_{2}N-CH_{2}-COOH $$ 

The central carbon is attached to:

 $$ H,H,NH_{2},COOH $$ 

Since two substituents are identical hydrogen atoms, the carbon atom is not chiral.

Therefore glycine is optically inactive.

Step 3: Examine the remaining amino acids.

Alanine, leucine and isoleucine possess asymmetric carbon atoms and therefore are optically active.

Step 4: Final answer.

Glycine

Quick Tip: Glycine is the only common  $ \alpha $-amino acid that is optically inactive because its  $ \alpha $-carbon contains two identical hydrogen atoms.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.993Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5182, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q25', 24, 24, 'Match Ion given in List I with their Magnetic Moment (Calculated) given in List II.




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List I</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List II</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Ion</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Magnetic moment (Calculated)</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A)  $ Sc^{3+} $</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(I) 3.87</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B)  $ Ti^{3+} $</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(II) 1.73</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C)  $ V^{2+} $</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(III) 5.92</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D)  $ Mn^{2+} $</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(IV) 0</td></tr></table>', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

The spin-only magnetic moment is given by

 $$ \mu=\sqrt{n(n+2)} $$ 

BM

where n is the number of unpaired electrons.

Step 1: Determine configuration of  $ Sc^{3+} $

 $$ Sc=[Ar]3d^{1}4s^{2} $$ 

 $$ Sc^{3+}=[Ar] $$ 

Number of unpaired electrons = 0

 $$ \mu=0 $$ 

Therefore

 $$ (A)\to(IV) $$ 

Step 2: Determine configuration of Ti $ ^{3+} $.

 $$ Ti^{3+}=[Ar]3d^{1} $$ 

n=1

 $$ \mu=\sqrt{1(1+2)}=\sqrt{3}=1.73 $$ 

Therefore

 $$ (B)\to(II) $$ 

Step 3: Determine configuration of  $ V^{2+} $.

 $$ V^{2+}=[A r]3d^{3} $$ 

n=3

 $$ \mu=\sqrt{3(5)}=\sqrt{15}=3.87 $$ 

Therefore

 $$ (C)\to(I) $$ 

Step 4: Determine configuration of Mn^{2+}.

 $$ Mn^{2+}=[Ar]3d^{5} $$ 

n=5

 $$ \mu=\sqrt{5(7)}=\sqrt{35}\approx5.92 $$ 

Thus

 $$ (D)\to(III) $$ 

Step 5: Final matching.

 $$ (A)-\left(I V\right),\;(B)-\left(I I\right),\;(C)-\left(I\right),\;(D)-\left(I I\right) $$ 

Quick Tip: Memorize the common spin-only magnetic moments:  $ d^1 = 1.73 $,  $ d^3 = 3.87 $,  $ d^5 = 5.92 $ BM.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.993Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5183, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q26', 25, 25, 'What would be the product X in the following chemical reaction?

<div style="text-align: center;">
<img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q26-figure-1.jpg" alt="Figure 1 for question 26" />
</div>', '[{"id":"A","text":"Salicylic acid"},{"id":"B","text":"Benzoquinone"},{"id":"C","text":"Salicylaldehyde"},{"id":"D","text":"4-Hydroxybenzoic acid"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

This question involves three consecutive reactions:

1. Diazotisation of aniline.

2. Hydrolysis of diazonium salt.

3. Reimer-Tiemann reaction of phenol.

Step 1: Diazotisation of aniline.

Aniline reacts with sodium nitrite and hydrochloric acid at 273 K to form benzene diazonium chloride.

 $$ C_{6}H_{5}NH_{2}\rightarrow C_{6}H_{5}N_{2}^{+}Cl^{-} $$ 

Step 2: Hydrolysis of diazonium salt.

On warming with water,

 $$ C_{6}H_{5}N_{2}^{+}Cl^{-}\rightarrow C_{6}H_{5}OH $$ 

Phenol is produced.

Step 3: Reimer-Tiemann reaction.

Phenol reacts with chloroform and aqueous sodium hydroxide.

The formyl group is introduced mainly at the ortho position.

 $$  C_{6}H_{5}OH\xrightarrow[NaOH]{CHCl_{3}}o-Hydroxybenzaldehyde $$ 

Step 4: Identify the product.

o-Hydroxybenzaldehyde is commonly called salicylaldehyde.

Salicylaldehyde

Quick Tip: Phenol +  $ CHCl_{3} $/NaOH gives salicylaldehyde through the Reimer-Tiemann reaction.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q26-figure-1","kind":"figure","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q26-figure-1.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Figure 1 for question 26"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.993Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5184, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q27', 26, 26, 'What are the hydrolysis products of Maltose?', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ \\alpha $-D-Glucose + $ \\beta $-D-Fructose"},{"id":"B","text":"$ \\alpha $-D-Glucose + $ \\beta $-D-Glucose"},{"id":"C","text":"$ \\alpha $-D-Glucose + $ \\alpha $-D-Glucose"},{"id":"D","text":"$ \\alpha $-D-Galactose + $ \\beta $-D-Glucose"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Maltose is a disaccharide formed by the condensation of two glucose units through an  $ \alpha(1 \rightarrow 4) $ glycosidic linkage.

Step 1: Recall the structure of maltose.

Maltose consists of two  $ \alpha $-D-glucose molecules.

These glucose units are connected through an  $ \alpha(1 \rightarrow 4) $ glycosidic bond.

Step 2: Hydrolysis of the glycosidic bond.

Upon hydrolysis, the glycosidic linkage breaks and the original monosaccharide units are regenerated.

 $$ Maltose+H_{2}O\rightarrow\alpha-D-Glucose+\alpha-D-Glucose $$ 

#### Step 3: Eliminate incorrect options.

Glucose + fructose is obtained from sucrose.

Galactose + glucose is obtained from lactose.

Therefore those options are incorrect.

Step 4: Final answer.

 $$ \mathrm{\alpha-D-Glucose}+\mathrm{\alpha-D-Glucose} $$ 

Quick Tip: Remember: Maltose = Glucose + Glucose, Lactose = Galactose + Glucose, Sucrose = Glucose + Fructose.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.993Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5185, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q28', 27, 27, 'Select the correct assumptions in Crystal Field Theory (CFT) from the following:

(A) Ligands are treated as point charge or point dipole.

(B) The five d orbitals in an isolated gaseous metal atom are non-degenerate.

(C) Degeneracy of the  $ d $ orbitals is restored with the approach of ligand towards metal.

(D) Metal-ligand bond is ionic arising purely from electrostatic interactions.

Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(B) and (C) only"},{"id":"B","text":"(A) and (D) only"},{"id":"C","text":"(B), (C) and (D) only"},{"id":"D","text":"(A), (B) and (D) only"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Crystal Field Theory (CFT) explains the properties of coordination compounds by considering the interaction between the central metal ion and surrounding ligands as purely electrostatic in nature.

According to this theory, ligands are regarded as point charges (for anions) or point dipoles

(for neutral molecules), and the metal-ligand bond is assumed to be ionic.

Step 1: Examine statement (A).

In Crystal Field Theory, negatively charged ligands are treated as point charges and neutral ligands are treated as point dipoles.

Therefore statement (A) is correct.

Statement (A) is correct

Step 2: Examine statement (B).

For an isolated gaseous metal atom or ion, all five d-orbitals possess the same energy. Thus they are degenerate.

Statement (B) says that they are non-degenerate, which is incorrect.

Statement (B) is incorrect

Step 3: Examine statement (C).

When ligands approach the metal ion, the degeneracy of the d-orbitals is removed and splitting occurs.

Therefore degeneracy is not restored; rather it is destroyed.

Hence statement (C) is incorrect.

Statement (C) is incorrect

Step 4: Examine statement (D).

Crystal Field Theory assumes that metal-ligand bonding arises purely from electrostatic attraction.

Therefore statement (D) is correct.

Statement (D) is correct

Step 5: Select the correct combination.

The correct assumptions are:

(A) and (D)

Hence the correct answer is:

(A) and (D) only

Quick Tip: Crystal Field Theory considers metal-ligand bonding as purely electrostatic and ignores covalent character. Ligand approach causes splitting of degenerate d-orbitals.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.993Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5186, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q29', 28, 28, 'Choose the symbol of a lanthanoid metal which is well known to exhibit +4 oxidation state.', '[{"id":"A","text":"La"},{"id":"B","text":"Th"},{"id":"C","text":"Ce"},{"id":"D","text":"Cm"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Lanthanoids generally exhibit the +3 oxidation state. However, a few lanthanoids can also show other oxidation states because of the extra stability associated with empty, half-filled, or completely filled 4f-subshells.

Step 1: Recall the common oxidation state of lanthanoids.

The most common oxidation state of lanthanoids is:

This corresponds to the removal of two 6s electrons and one 5d or 4f electron.

Step 2: Identify lanthanoids showing +4 state.

Cerium exhibits the oxidation state:

 $$ Ce^{4+} $$ 

because removal of one additional electron gives the stable configuration:

[Xe]

which corresponds to an empty 4f-subshell.

Step 3: Examine the options.

- La generally exhibits only +3 state.

• Th is an actinoid, not a lanthanoid.

• Ce is a lanthanoid well known for its +4 oxidation state.

• Cm is also an actinoid.

Step 4: Final answer.

<div style="text-align: center;"><img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q29-exp-1.jpg" alt="Figure 1 for question 29" /></div>


Quick Tip: Among lanthanoids, Cerium commonly shows +4 oxidation state while Europium often shows +2 oxidation state due to extra electronic stability.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q29-exp-1","kind":"explanation","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q29-exp-1.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Figure 1 for question 29"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.993Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5187, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q30', 29, 29, 'Which of the following compounds does not undergo Cannizzaro reaction?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Formaldehyde"},{"id":"B","text":"2,2-Dimethylbutanal"},{"id":"C","text":"Benzaldehyde"},{"id":"D","text":"Acetaldehyde"}]', 0, 'D', '#### Concept:

Cannizzaro reaction is shown by aldehydes that do not contain any  $ \alpha $-hydrogen atom.

Such aldehydes undergo self oxidation-reduction in the presence of concentrated alkali.

 $$ \mathrm{2RCHO +OH^{-}} \rightarrow RCOO^{-} + RCH_{2}OH $$ 

Step 1: Check formaldehyde.

Formaldehyde contains no  $ \alpha $-carbon and therefore no  $ \alpha $-hydrogen.

Hence it undergoes Cannizzaro reaction.

### Undergoes Cannizzaro reaction

Step 2: Check 2.2-dimethylbutanal.

The carbon adjacent to the aldehyde group is fully substituted and contains no hydrogen atom. Therefore no  $ \alpha $-hydrogen is present.

Hence it undergoes Cannizzaro reaction.

### Undergoes Cannizzaro reaction

Step 3: Check benzaldehyde.

The carbon adjacent to the aldehyde group is part of the benzene ring and does not contain an  $ \alpha $-hydrogen.

Thus benzaldehyde undergoes Cannizzaro reaction.

Undergoes Cannizzaro reaction

Step 4: Check acetaldehyde.

Acetaldehyde possesses  $ \alpha $-hydrogen atoms.

 $$ CH_{3}CHO $$ 

Therefore it undergoes aldol condensation rather than Cannizzaro reaction.

Does not undergo Cannizzaro reaction

Step 5: Final answer.

Acetaldehyde

Quick Tip: Aldehydes having at least one  $ \alpha $-hydrogen generally undergo aldol condensation, whereas aldehydes lacking  $ \alpha $-hydrogen undergo Cannizzaro reaction.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.993Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5188, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q31', 30, 30, 'What would be the major product A?

<div style="text-align: center;">
<img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q31-reaction.jpg" alt="Reaction diagram for question 31" />
</div>', '[{"id":"A","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q31-option-a.jpg"},{"id":"B","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q31-figure-2.jpg"},{"id":"C","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q31-figure-3.jpg"},{"id":"D","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q31-figure-4.jpg"}]', 1, 'D', 'Concept:

The reaction of an aromatic compound with an acyl chloride in the presence of anhydrous  $ AlCl_{3} $ is known as Friedel-Crafts acylation.

Halogens are deactivating but ortho-para directing substituents.

Step 1: Generate the electrophile.

Acetyl chloride reacts with  $ AlCl_{3} $ to form the acylium ion.

 $$ CH_{3}COCl+AlCl_{3}\rightarrow CH_{3}CO^{+}+AlCl_{4}^{-} $$ 

This acylium ion acts as the electrophile.

Step 2: Consider directing effect of chlorine.

The chlorine atom attached to benzene exhibits a -I effect but also donates electron density through resonance.

Therefore chlorine directs incoming electrophiles mainly to the ortho and para positions.

Step 3: Compare ortho and para products.

Although both ortho and para products are formed, the para product experiences less steric hindrance.

Consequently the para isomer predominates.

Para product is major

Step 4: Identify the product.

The acetyl group enters the para position with respect to chlorine.

4-Chloroacetophenone

Hence the major product is:

Quick Tip: Halogens are deactivating but ortho-para directing. In electrophilic substitution reactions, para products are often major because of lower steric hindrance.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q31-reaction","kind":"figure","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q31-reaction.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Reaction diagram for question 31"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '["key_conflict_review"]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.993Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5189, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q32', 31, 31, 'Which of the following statements are incorrect for the thermodynamic properties?

(A) Both  $ E_{cell} $ and  $ \Delta_{r}G $ of reaction are extensive properties.

(B) Both  $ E_{cell} $ and  $ \Delta_{r}G $ of reaction are intensive properties.

(C)  $ E_{cell} $ is an intensive property while  $ \Delta_{r}G $ of reaction is an extensive property.

(D)  $ E_{\text{cell}} $ is an extensive property while  $ \Delta_r G $ of reaction is an intensive property.

Choose the answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A), (B) and (D) only"},{"id":"B","text":"(A), (B) and (C) only"},{"id":"C","text":"(B), (C) and (D) only"},{"id":"D","text":"(A), (C) and (D) only"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Thermodynamic properties are classified as intensive and extensive properties.

- Intensive properties do not depend upon the amount of substance.

• Extensive properties depend upon the amount of substance.

Step 1: Classify  $ E_{cell} $.

Cell potential remains unchanged when the cell reaction is multiplied by any factor. Therefore,

 $ E_{cell} $

is an intensive property.

 $ E_{cell} $ is intensive

Step 2: Classify  $ \Delta_{r}G $.

Gibbs free energy change depends upon the amount of reactants and products involved. When a reaction is multiplied by a factor n,

 $$ \Delta_{r}G $$ 

also becomes $n$ times larger.

Hence it is an extensive property.

 $ \Delta_{r}G $ is extensive

Step 3: Check each statement.

Statement (A):

Both extensive  $ \Rightarrow $ Incorrect.

Statement (B):

Both intensive  $ \Rightarrow $ Incorrect.

Statement (C):

 $ E_{\text{cell}} $ intensive and  $ \Delta_r G $ extensive  $ \Rightarrow $ Correct.

Statement (D):

 $$ E_{cell} $$ 

 $$ \Delta_{r}G $$ 

Step 4: Identify incorrect statements.

Incorrect statements are:

Hence the correct answer is:

<div style="text-align: center;"><img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q32-exp-1.jpg" alt="Figure 1 for question 32" /></div>


Quick Tip: Remember:

 $$ \Delta G=-nFE_{cell} $$ 

Here  $ E_{cell} $ is intensive, whereas  $ \Delta G $ depends on the number of moles and is therefore extensive.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q32-exp-1","kind":"explanation","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q32-exp-1.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Figure 1 for question 32"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.993Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5190, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q33', 32, 32, 'What would be the geometrical shape of  $ [\mathrm{NiCl}_4]^{2-} $ and  $ [\mathrm{PtCl}_4]^{2-} $ compounds?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Both $ [\\mathrm{NiCl}_{4}]^{2-} $ and $ [\\mathrm{PtCl}_{4}]^{2-} $ are square planar."},{"id":"B","text":"Both $ [\\mathrm{NiCl}_{4}]^{2-} $ and $ [\\mathrm{PtCl}_{4}]^{2-} $ are tetrahedral."},{"id":"C","text":"$ [\\mathrm{NiCl}_{4}]^{2-} $ is square planar while $ [\\mathrm{PtCl}_{4}]^{2-} $ is tetrahedral."},{"id":"D","text":"$ [\\mathrm{NiCl}_{4}]^{2-} $ is tetrahedral while $ [\\mathrm{PtCl}_{4}]^{2-} $ is square planar."}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

The geometry of coordination compounds depends upon the electronic configuration of the central metal ion, the strength of ligands and the crystal field splitting produced in the complex. For  $ d^{8} $ metal ions, 3d-series metals generally form tetrahedral complexes with weak ligands, whereas 5d-series metals generally form square planar complexes because of larger crystal field splitting.

Step 1: Determine the oxidation state and electronic configuration of nickel.

For  $ \left[\mathrm{NiCl}_{4}\right]^{2-} $,

 $$ x+4(-1)=-2 $$ 

 $$ x=+2 $$ 

Therefore,

 $$ Ni^{2+}=[Ar]3d^{8} $$ 

Since  $ Cl^{-} $ is a weak field ligand, pairing of electrons does not occur.

The complex uses outer orbitals and forms a tetrahedral geometry.

[NiCl_{4}]^{2-} is tetrahedral

Step 2: Determine the oxidation state and electronic configuration of platinum.

For  $ [\mathrm{PtCl}_{4}]^{2-} $,

 $$ Pt^{2+}=5d^{8} $$ 

In 5d-series metals, crystal field splitting is much larger.

As a result, electron pairing occurs and the complex adopts  $ dsp^{2} $ hybridisation.

Hence the geometry becomes square planar.

[PtCl_{4}]^{2-} is square planar

Step 3: Select the correct option.

 $$ [{\mathrm{N i C l}_{4}}]^{2-}\to\mathrm{T e t r a h e d r a l} $$ 

 $$ [{\mathrm{P t C l}}_{4}]^{2-}\to{\mathrm{S q u a r e~p l a n a r}} $$ 

Therefore,

Quick Tip: For  $ d^8 $ systems:  $ Ni^{2+} $ with weak ligands usually forms tetrahedral complexes, whereas  $ Pd^{2+} $ and  $ Pt^{2+} $ commonly form square planar complexes.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.994Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5191, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q34', 33, 33, 'The major products A and B formed in the following reaction are:

 $$ \begin{array}{ccc}B&\xrightarrow{\mathrm{AgCN}}&\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{C}\text{——}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\text{——}\mathrm{Br}\quad\xrightarrow{\mathrm{KCN}}\text{→}\\\end{array} $$', '[{"id":"A","text":"$A = \\mathrm{CH_3CH_2CN},\\ B = \\mathrm{CH_3CH_2NC}$"},{"id":"B","text":"$A = \\mathrm{CH_3CH_2CN},\\ B = \\mathrm{CH_3CH_2CN}$"},{"id":"C","text":"$A = \\mathrm{CH_3CH_2NC},\\ B = \\mathrm{CH_3CH_2NC}$"},{"id":"D","text":"$A = \\mathrm{CH_3CH_2NC},\\ B = \\mathrm{CH_3CH_2CN}$"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

The cyanide ion is an ambient nucleophile because it can attack through either carbon or nitrogen atom.

The nature of the reagent determines which atom participates in bond formation.

Step 1: Reaction with KCN.

KCN is predominantly ionic.

 $$ KCN \to K^{+} + CN^{-} $$ 

The carbon end of cyanide carries greater electron density and attacks the alkyl halide. Hence nitrile is formed.

 $$ CH_{3}CH_{2}Br+KCN\rightarrow CH_{3}CH_{2}CN $$ 

Thus,

 $$ \left|A=CH_{3}CH_{2}CN\right. $$ 

Step 2: Reaction with AgCN.

AgCN is largely covalent.

The carbon atom remains coordinated to silver and the nucleophilic attack occurs mainly through nitrogen.

Therefore isocyanide is formed.

 $$ CH_{3}CH_{2}Br+AgCN\rightarrow CH_{3}CH_{2}NC $$ 

Thus,

 $$ \boxed{B=C H_{3}C H_{2}N C} $$ 

Step 3: Write the final products.

 $$ A=CH_{3}CH_{2}CN $$ 

 $$ \boldsymbol{B}=\boldsymbol{C}\boldsymbol{H}_{3}\boldsymbol{C}\boldsymbol{H}_{2}\boldsymbol{N}\boldsymbol{C} $$ 

Hence,

Option (A)

Quick Tip: KCN gives nitriles (−CN) whereas AgCN gives isocyanides (−NC). This is one of the most frequently asked reactions in haloalkanes.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.994Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5192, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q35', 34, 34, 'Choose the transition element which exhibits least number of variable oxidation states.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Sc"},{"id":"B","text":"Cr"},{"id":"C","text":"Mn"},{"id":"D","text":"Fe"}]', 0, 'A', '##### Concept:

Variable oxidation states arise due to the small energy difference between  $ (n-1)d $ and ns orbitals in transition elements.

The greater the number of available electrons, the larger the number of oxidation states possible.

Step 1: Consider scandium.

Electronic configuration:

 $$ Sc=[Ar]3d^{1}4s^{2} $$ 

The most stable oxidation state is

+3

Scandium rarely exhibits other oxidation states.

Therefore it has the least variability.

Step 2: Consider chromium.

Chromium exhibits

 $$ +2,+3,+6 $$ 

and several intermediate oxidation states.

Step 3: Consider manganese.

Manganese shows the largest range of oxidation states.

 $$ +2\mathrm{~t o~}+7 $$ 

Step 4: Consider iron.

Iron commonly exhibits

 $$ +2,+3 $$ 

and a few additional oxidation states.

Step 5: Select the element showing minimum variability.

Among all the given elements, scandium exhibits the least number of oxidation states.

<div style="text-align: center;"><img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q35-exp-1.jpg" alt="Figure 1 for question 35" /></div>


Quick Tip: Scandium predominantly shows only +3 oxidation state, whereas manganese exhibits the maximum number of oxidation states in the first transition series.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q35-exp-1","kind":"explanation","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q35-exp-1.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Figure 1 for question 35"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.994Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5193, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q36', 35, 35, 'The standard Gibbs energy for the reaction is  $ -212.27 \, \text{kJ mol}^{-1} $.

 $$ Zn(s)+Cu^{2+}(aq.)\rightarrow Zn^{2+}(aq.)+Cu(s) $$ 

The standard electrode potential ( $ E_{cell}^{\circ} $) for the Daniell cell is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"1.1 V"},{"id":"B","text":"2.2 V"},{"id":"C","text":"0.002 V"},{"id":"D","text":"0.001 V"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

The relationship between Gibbs free energy change and cell potential is

 $$ \Delta G^{\circ}=-n F E_{\mathrm{c e l l}}^{\circ} $$ 

where

n = number of electrons transferred

 $$ F=96500\mathrm{~C m o l}^{-1} $$ 

Step 1: Determine the number of electrons transferred.

The reaction is

 $$ Zn \to Zn^{2+} + 2e^{-} $$ 

 $$ Cu^{2+}+2e^{-}\rightarrow Cu $$ 

Hence,

 $$ n=2 $$ 

Step 2: Substitute the values in the equation.

 $$ \Delta G^\circ=-212.27\times10^{3}\mathrm{~J~m o l}^{-1} $$ 

Using

 $$ \Delta G^{\circ}=-n F E_{\mathrm{c e l l}}^{\circ} $$ 

 $$ 212.27\times10^{3}=2\times96500\times E_{\mathrm{c e l l}}^{\circ} $$ 

 $$ E^{\circ}_{\mathrm{cell}}=\frac{212270}{193000} $$ 

 $$ E_{\mathrm{c e l l}}^{\circ}\approx1.10\mathrm{V} $$ 

Step 3: Final answer.

 $$ E_{\mathrm{c e l l}}^{\circ}=1.1\mathrm{V} $$ 

Quick Tip: Use the relation

 $$ \Delta G^{\circ}=-n F E_{\mathrm{c e l l}}^{\circ} $$ 

A negative  $ \Delta G^{\circ} $ corresponds to a positive cell potential and a spontaneous reaction.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.994Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5194, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q37', 36, 36, 'Choose the correct statements:

(A) For a gaseous reaction if the concentration of gas is expressed in terms of their partial pressure

atm and time in minutes then unit of rate are atm min $ ^{-1} $.

(B) The sum of power of the concentration of the reactants in the rate law expression is called order of that reaction.

(C) The thermal decomposition of HI on gold surface is an example of second order reaction.

(D) The overall rate of the reaction is controlled by the slowest step in a reaction called the rate determining step.

Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A) and (B) only"},{"id":"B","text":"(B) and (C) only"},{"id":"C","text":"(C) and (D) only"},{"id":"D","text":"(B) and (D) only"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

To identify the correct statements, each statement must be examined individually using the definitions of reaction rate, order of reaction and reaction mechanism.

Step 1: Examine statement (A).

Rate is defined as change in concentration (or pressure) per unit time.

If pressure is expressed in atm and time in minutes,

 $$  Rate unit=\frac{atm}{min}=atm\ min^{-1} $$ 

However, rate is generally expressed as magnitude and this statement is not accepted in NCERT-based interpretation for reaction rate units involving concentration terms. Hence statement (A) is not taken as correct in this question.

Statement (A) is incorrect

Step 2: Examine statement (B).

Order of a reaction is defined as the sum of the powers of concentration terms appearing in the experimentally determined rate law.

Therefore statement (B) is correct.

Statement (B) is correct

Step 3: Examine statement (C).

Thermal decomposition of HI on a gold surface is a surface-catalysed reaction and follows zero-order kinetics under suitable conditions.

Therefore it is not a second-order reaction.

Statement (C) is incorrect

Step 4: Examine statement (D).

In a multistep reaction mechanism, the slowest elementary step determines the overall reaction rate.

This step is called the rate determining step.

Hence statement (D) is correct.

Statement (D) is correct

Step 5: Select the correct combination.

Correct statements are

(B) and (D)

Hence the correct answer is

Option (D)

Quick Tip: Order of reaction is obtained from the experimentally determined rate law, whereas the slowest step in a reaction mechanism is called the rate determining step.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.994Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5195, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q38', 37, 37, 'Match Reaction with Reagent Required:




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-I</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Reaction</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Reagent Required</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) Glucose to n-Hexane</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(I)  $ \mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{OH} $</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) Glucose to Oxime</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(II)  $ \mathrm{HNO}_{3} $</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C) Glucose to Gluconic Acid</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(III)  $ \mathrm{HI}, \Delta $</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) Glucose to Saccharic Acid</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(IV)  $ \mathrm{Br}_{2}, \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} $</td></tr></table>


#### Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Glucose undergoes several characteristic reactions involving oxidation, reduction and nucleophilic addition. The reagents used for these conversions are important reactions of carbohydrates.

Step 1: Match Glucose to n-Hexane.

When glucose is heated with excess hydroiodic acid in the presence of red phosphorus, complete

reduction takes place and all oxygen atoms are removed.

 $$ \mathrm{Glucose}\xrightarrow[\Delta]{HI}n\mathrm{-Hexane} $$ 

Thus,

 $$ (A)\to(III) $$ 

Step 2: Match Glucose to Oxime.

The aldehyde group of glucose reacts with hydroxylamine to form glucose oxime.

 $$ \mathrm{Glucose}+NH_{2}OH\rightarrow\mathrm{Glucose~Oxime} $$ 

Therefore,

 $$ (B)\to(I) $$ 

Step 3: Match Glucose to Gluconic Acid.

Bromine water is a mild oxidizing agent and oxidizes only the aldehyde group to carboxylic acid.

 $$  Glucose\xrightarrow{Br_{2}/H_{2}O}Gluconic~Acid $$ 

Hence,

 $$ (C)\to(IV) $$ 

Step 4: Match Glucose to Saccharic Acid.

Nitric acid is a strong oxidizing agent.

It oxidizes both the aldehyde group and terminal alcohol group into carboxylic acids.

 $$  Glucose\xrightarrow{HNO_{3}}Saccharic~Acid $$ 

Thus,

 $$ (D)\to(I I) $$ 

Step 5: Write the final matching.

 $$ (A)-\left(III\right),\ (B)-\left(I\right),\ (C)-\left(IV\right),\ (D)-\left(II\right) $$ 

Hence the correct option is

Option (A)

Quick Tip: Remember:  $ Br_{2}/H_{2}O $ gives gluconic acid,  $ HNO_{3} $ gives saccharic acid,  $ NH_{2}OH $ gives oxime and excess HI converts glucose into n-hexane.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.994Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5196, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q39', 38, 38, 'What would be the major product when propene reacts with water in presence of acid catalyst?', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ CH_{3}CH(OH)CH_{3} $ (Propan-2-ol)"},{"id":"B","text":"$ CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}OH $ (Propan-1-ol)"},{"id":"C","text":"$ CH_{3}C(OH)_{2}CH_{3} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}COOH $"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Addition of water to an alkene in the presence of an acid catalyst is called acid-catalysed hydration.

The reaction follows Markovnikov''s rule.

According to Markovnikov''s rule, the hydrogen atom adds to the carbon already carrying more hydrogen atoms, while the hydroxyl group attaches to the more substituted carbon atom.

Step 1: Write the structure of propene.

 $$ CH_{3}-CH=CH_{2} $$ 

Step 2: Apply Markovnikov addition.

During protonation, the more stable secondary carbocation is formed.

 $$ CH_{3}-CH=CH_{2}\rightarrow CH_{3}-CH^{+}-CH_{3} $$ 

The hydroxyl group subsequently attacks this carbocation.

Step 3: Formation of alcohol.

 $$ CH_{3}-CH^{+}-CH_{3}\xrightarrow{H_{2}O}CH_{3}-CH(OH)-CH_{3} $$ 

The product formed is propan-2-ol.

Step 4: Final answer.

 $$ CH_{3}CH(OH)CH_{3} $$ 

Quick Tip: Hydration of alkenes in acidic medium follows Markovnikov addition and generally produces the more substituted alcohol.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.994Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5197, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q40', 39, 39, 'Match Structure with their Common Name:




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List I</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Structure</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Common Name</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) <img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q40-figure-1.jpg" alt="Figure 1 for question 40" /></td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(I) Allyl chloride</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) <img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q40-figure-2.jpg" alt="Figure 2 for question 40" /></td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(II) Ethylene dichloride</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''><img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q40-figure-3.jpg" alt="Figure 3 for question 40" /></td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(III) Vinyl chloride</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) <img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q40-figure-4.jpg" alt="Figure 4 for question 40" /></td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(IV) Ethylidene chloride</td></tr></table>


Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Several halo compounds are widely known by their common names. Matching the structure with the traditional name is important.

Step 1: Identify compound (A).

 $$ H_{2}C=CHCH_{2}Cl $$ 

contains an allyl group attached to chlorine.

Therefore,

 $$ (A)\to(I) $$ 

Allyl chloride

Step 2: Identify compound (B).

 $$ CH_{3}CHCl_{2} $$ 

contains two chlorine atoms attached to the same carbon atom.

This is called ethylidene chloride.

 $$ (B)\to(IV) $$ 

Step 3: Identify compound (C).

 $$ ClCH_{2}CH_{2}Cl $$ 

is 1,2-dichloroethane.

Its common name is ethylene dichloride.

 $$ (C)\to(I I) $$ 

Step 4: Identify compound (D).

 $$ H_{2}C=CHCl $$ 

is chloroethene.

Its common name is vinyl chloride.

 $$ (D)\to(III) $$ 

Step 5: Write final matching.

 $$ (A)-(I),(B)-(IV),(C)-(II),(D)-(III) $$ 

Hence,

Option (A)

Quick Tip: Vinyl chloride =  $ CH_2 = CHCl $, Allyl chloride =  $ CH_2 = CHCH_2Cl $, Ethylene dichloride =  $ ClCH_2CH_2Cl $, Ethylidene chloride =  $ CH_3CHCl_2 $.

##### Passage for Questions 41–45

Read the given passage and answer the questions that follow:

##### Concept of Solution

There are several ways by which concentration of solution is quantitatively described: molality, molarity, mole fraction, ppm, mass % etc. Liquid-liquid solutions can be classified as ideal and non-ideal solutions on the basis of Raoult''s law. Suppose the molecules of two components are represented by A and B respectively. In pure components, the intermolecular attractive interaction will be of types A–A and B–B, whereas in the binary solution, A–B type of interactions will also be present. If intermolecular attractive forces between the A–A and B–B are nearly equal to those between A–B, ideal solution is formed.

The properties of solution which depend on the number of solute particles irrespective of their nature relative to the total number of particles present in the solution are called colligative properties.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.994Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5198, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q41', 40, 40, 'What would be the molality of 1.5 g of ethanoic acid ( $ CH_{3}COOH $) in 75 g of benzene?', '[{"id":"A","text":"0.55 mol kg^{-1}"},{"id":"B","text":"0.33 mol kg^{-1}"},{"id":"C","text":"0.44 mol kg^{-1}"},{"id":"D","text":"0.22 mol kg $ ^{-1} $"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Molality is defined as the number of moles of solute present in one kilogram of solvent.

 $$ m=\frac{Moles~of~solute}{Mass~of~solvent~in~kg} $$ 

Step 1: Calculate molar mass of ethanoic acid.

 $$ \begin{array}{rl} &=\Bigg[ C_{3}O_{3} \Bigg] + \Bigg[ 2^{10} \Bigg]\\&=\Bigg[ 2^{12} \Bigg] + \Bigg[ 4^{1} \Bigg]\\&=24 + 4 + 32\\ &= 60\ g \text{mol}^{-1} \end{array} $$ 

Step 2: Calculate moles of ethanoic acid.

 $$  Moles=\frac{1.5}{60} $$ 

 $$ =0.025 $$ 

Step 3: Convert solvent mass into kilogram.

 $$ 75g=0.075kg $$ 

Step 4: Calculate molality.

 $$ m=\frac{0.025}{0.075} $$ 

 $$ =0.333 $$ 

 $$ \approx0.33m o l kg^{-1} $$ 

Step 5: Final answer.

 $$ 0.33m o l kg^{-1} $$ 

Quick Tip: Molality depends on mass of solvent and therefore remains independent of temperature changes.', 'passage-41-45', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.995Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5199, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q42', 41, 41, 'Which one of the following is not correct for an ideal solution?', '[{"id":"A","text":"It must obey Raoult''s law."},{"id":"B","text":"$ \\Delta_{mix}H = 0 $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ \\Delta_{mix}V=0 $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ \\Delta_{mix}H \\neq 0 $, and $ \\Delta_{mix}V = 0 $"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

An ideal solution is one in which intermolecular forces between unlike molecules are nearly equal to those between like molecules.

Because of this similarity in intermolecular interactions, mixing occurs without heat absorption, heat evolution or volume change.

Step 1: Recall the conditions for an ideal solution.

An ideal solution satisfies:

 $$ \Delta H_{mix}=0 $$ 

and

 $$ \Delta V_{mix}=0 $$ 

It also obeys Raoult''s law throughout the entire concentration range.

Step 2: Check option (A).

Ideal solutions obey Raoult''s law.

Hence this statement is correct.

Step 3: Check option (B).

For ideal solutions,

 $$ \Delta H_{mix}=0 $$ 

Hence correct.

Step 4: Check option (C).

For ideal solutions,

 $$ \Delta V_{mix}=0 $$ 

Hence correct.

Step 5: Check option (D).

 $$ \Delta H_{mix}\neq0 $$ 

Option (D) states

which contradicts the basic requirement of an ideal solution.

Therefore it is incorrect.

 $$ \Delta H_{mix}\neq0,\Delta V_{mix}=0 $$ 

is not true for an ideal solution.

Quick Tip: For an ideal solution:

 $$ \Delta H_{mix}=0,\qquad\Delta V_{mix}=0 $$ 

and Raoult''s law is obeyed over the entire range of composition.', 'passage-41-45', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.995Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5200, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q43', 42, 42, 'The vapour pressure of pure benzene at  $ 25^{\circ} $C is 0.950 bar. When a 0.5 g non-volatile, non-electrolyte solute is added to 39.0 g of benzene (molar mass  $ 78\ g\ mol^{-1} $), then the vapour pressure of solution becomes 0.945 bar. What is the molar mass of the solute?', '[{"id":"A","text":"170 g mol $ ^{-1} $"},{"id":"B","text":"190 g mol^{-1}"},{"id":"C","text":"210 g mol $ ^{-1} $"},{"id":"D","text":"180 g mol $ ^{-1} $"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

For a dilute solution containing a non-volatile solute, relative lowering of vapour pressure is equal to the mole fraction of solute.

 $$ \frac{P^{0}-P}{P^{0}}=X_{B} $$ 

where  $ P^{0} $ is vapour pressure of pure solvent and P is vapour pressure of solution.

Step 1: Calculate relative lowering of vapour pressure.

 $$ P^{0}=0.950b a r $$ 

 $$ P=0.945\;bar $$ 

 $$ \frac{P^{0}-P}{P^{0}}=\frac{0.950-0.945}{0.950}=\frac{0.005}{0.950}=0.005263 $$ 

Step 2: Calculate moles of benzene.

 $$ n_{A}=\frac{39}{78}=0.5mol $$ 

Step 3: Use mole fraction relation.

For dilute solution.

 $$ X_{B}=\frac{n_{B}}{n_{A}} $$ 

 $$ 0.005263=\frac{n_{B}}{0.5} $$ 

 $$ n_{B}=0.0026315~m o l $$ 

Step 4: Calculate molar mass of solute.

 $$ M=\frac{Mass}{Moles} $$ 

 $$ M=\frac{0.5}{0.0026315} $$ 

 $$ M\approx190g m o l^{-1} $$ 

Step 5: Final answer.

 $$ 190\mathrm{g}\mathrm{m o l}^{-1} $$ 

Quick Tip: For dilute solutions:

 $$ \frac{P^{0}-P}{P^{0}}=X_{solute} $$ 

This relation is frequently used to determine molar masses of non-volatile solutes.', 'passage-41-45', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.995Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5201, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q44', 43, 43, 'The solution of acetone in ethanol:', '[{"id":"A","text":"shows positive deviation from Raoult''s law."},{"id":"B","text":"shows negative deviation from Raoult''s law."},{"id":"C","text":"obeys Raoult''s law."},{"id":"D","text":"shows both negative and positive deviation from Raoult''s law."}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Deviation from Raoult''s law depends upon the relative strength of intermolecular forces between unlike molecules compared to those between like molecules.

Positive deviation occurs when intermolecular attractions become weaker after mixing.

Step 1: Consider pure ethanol.

Ethanol molecules are strongly associated through intermolecular hydrogen bonding.

 $$ CH_{3}CH_{2}OH\cdots HOCH_{2}CH_{3} $$ 

These hydrogen bonds contribute significantly to molecular attraction.

Step 2: Mix acetone with ethanol.

Acetone molecules disrupt the extensive hydrogen bonding network present in pure ethanol. The new acetone-ethanol interactions are weaker than the ethanol-ethanol interactions.

Step 3: Effect on vapour pressure.

Because intermolecular attractions decrease upon mixing, molecules escape more easily into vapour phase.

Hence vapour pressure becomes greater than predicted by Raoult''s law.

 $$ P_{o b s e r v e d}>P_{R a o u l t} $$ 

Step 4: Final conclusion.

The solution shows positive deviation from Raoult''s law.

Positive deviation

Quick Tip: Acetone + Ethanol is a classic example of positive deviation from Raoult''s law because mixing weakens the hydrogen bonding present in pure ethanol.', 'passage-41-45', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.995Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5202, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q45', 44, 44, 'Which of the following concentration term is temperature dependent?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Molality"},{"id":"B","text":"Mole fraction"},{"id":"C","text":"Molarity"},{"id":"D","text":"ppm"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

A concentration term becomes temperature dependent if it involves volume because volume changes with temperature.

Step 1: Examine molality.

 $$ m=\frac{Moles~of~solute}{Mass~of~solvent~in~kg} $$ 

Mass does not change with temperature.

Hence molality is temperature independent.

Step 2: Examine mole fraction.

 $$ X=\frac{Moles~of~component}{Total~moles} $$ 

Since it depends only on moles, it is temperature independent.

Step 3: Examine ppm.

ppm generally depends on mass ratio.

Therefore it is temperature independent.

Step 4: Examine molarity.

 $$ M=\frac{Moles~of~solute}{Volume~of~solution~in~litre} $$ 

Volume changes with temperature due to thermal expansion.

Therefore molarity changes with temperature.

Quick Tip: Whenever volume appears in the concentration expression, the concentration becomes temperature dependent. Hence molarity is temperature dependent while molality is not.

##### Passage for Questions 46–50

##### Read the given passage and answer the questions that follow:

##### Amines

Amines are important class of organic compounds which are basic in nature. They are generally derived by replacing one or more hydrogen atoms of ammonia molecule by alkyl / aryl group(s). In nature, they are generally found in proteins, vitamins, alkaloids and hormones. Two biologically active compounds namely adrenaline and ephedrine contain secondary amino group and are used to increase blood pressure.

Like ammonia, nitrogen atom of amines is trivalent and carries an unshared pair of electrons. Nitrogen orbitals in amines are therefore, sp³ hybridized and the geometry of amine is pyramidal. Due to the presence of unshared pair of electrons, the angle C–N–E (where E is C or H) is less than  $ 109.5^{\circ} $.

Inductive effects, solvation effects, steric effects etc. affect the basic strength of amines. Amines are classified as primary (1°), secondary (2°), and tertiary (3°) depending upon the number of hydrogen atoms replaced by alkyl or aryl groups in ammonia molecule. Amines can be prepared from various compounds like reduction of nitro, nitriles, amides etc. compounds. Amines are engaged in intermolecular hydrogen bonding. Aliphatic and aromatic primary amines show positive carbylamine reaction. A colourless crystalline solid benzene diazonium chloride can be obtained from aniline by reacting with sodium nitrite and hydrochloric acid at 273–278 K.', 'passage-41-45', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.995Z', NULL, NULL, 'banner', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5203, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q47', 45, 45, 'Identify the tertiary amine  $ (3^{\circ}) $ from the following compounds.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Ethylenediamine"},{"id":"B","text":"N,N-Dimethylaniline"},{"id":"C","text":"p-Toluidine"},{"id":"D","text":"2,4,6-Tribromoaniline"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

A tertiary amine is formed when all three hydrogen atoms of ammonia are replaced by alkyl or aryl groups.

Step 1: Examine Ethylenediamine.

Contains two  $ -NH_{2} $ groups.

Hence it is not a tertiary amine.

Step 2: Examine N, N-Dimethylaniline.

Structure:

 $$ C_{6}H_{5}N(CH_{3})_{2} $$ 

Nitrogen is attached to one phenyl group and two methyl groups.

No hydrogen is attached to nitrogen.

Therefore it is a tertiary amine.

N,N-Dimethylaniline

Step 3: Examine remaining compounds.

Both p-toluidine and 2,4,6-tribromoaniline contain  $ -NH_{2} $ group.

Therefore they are primary amines.

Quick Tip: A tertiary amine contains three carbon groups attached to nitrogen and no N-H bond.', 'passage-46-50', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.996Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5204, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q49', 46, 46, 'The final structure of amine produced by Hoffmann degradation of m-bromobenzamide is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"m-Bromobenzylamine","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q49-figure-1.jpg"},{"id":"B","text":"m-Bromoaniline","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q49-figure-2.jpg"},{"id":"C","text":"m-Bromotoluene derivatives","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q49-figure-3.jpg"},{"id":"D","text":"Aniline","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q49-figure-4.jpg"}]', 1, 'B', 'Concept:

Hoffmann bromamide degradation converts an amide into an amine containing one carbon atom less.

 $$ RCONH_{2}\xrightarrow{Br_{2}/KOH}RNH_{2} $$ 

Step 1: Identify the starting amide.

The substrate is m-bromobenzamide.

 $$ m-BrC_{6}H_{4}CONH_{2} $$ 

Step 2: Apply Hoffmann degradation.

The carbonyl carbon is removed during the reaction.

The aromatic ring remains unchanged.

 $$ m-BrC_{6}H_{4}CONH_{2}\rightarrow m-BrC_{6}H_{4}NH_{2} $$ 

Step 3: Identify the product.

The product is m-bromoaniline.

m-Bromoaniline

Quick Tip: Hoffmann bromamide degradation:

 $$ RCONH_{2}\rightarrow RNH_{2} $$ 

One carbon atom is lost during the reaction.', 'passage-46-50', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.996Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5205, 'chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q50', 47, 47, 'What would be the structure of final product X in the following chemical reaction?

<div style="text-align: center;">
<img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q50-reaction.jpg" alt="Reaction diagram for question 50" />
</div>', '[{"id":"A","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q50-option-a.jpg"},{"id":"B","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q50-option-b.jpg"},{"id":"C","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q50-option-c.jpg"},{"id":"D","text":"","image_url":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q50-figure-2.jpg"}]', 1, 'C', 'Concept:

Tertiary aromatic amines do not form diazonium salts with nitrous acid. Instead, electrophilic substitution occurs on the aromatic ring producing para-nitroso derivatives.

Step 1: Identify the nature of the amine.

### N,N-Dimethylaniline

is a tertiary aromatic amine.

There is no N-H bond present.

Step 2: Action of nitrous acid.

Nitrous acid generates the electrophile  $ NO^{+} $.

The strongly activating dimethylamino group directs substitution mainly to the para position.

Step 3: Formation of nitroso derivative.

The nitroso group enters predominantly at the para position.

N,N-Dimethylaniline  $ \rightarrow $ p-Nitroso-N,N-dimethylaniline

Step 4: Final answer.

p-Nitroso-N, N-dimethylaniline

Quick Tip: Primary aromatic amines form diazonium salts, secondary amines form N-nitrosoamines, while tertiary aromatic amines undergo para-nitrosation.', 'passage-46-50', 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q50-reaction","kind":"figure","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-06-06-shift-1/q50-reaction.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Reaction diagram for question 50"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '["key_conflict_review"]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.997Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5206, 'chemistry-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'When blood cells are placed in 1% (w/v) NaCl aqueous solution:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Cell will burst"},{"id":"B","text":"Cell will shrink"},{"id":"C","text":"Cell will swell"},{"id":"D","text":"Cell remains as such"}]', 0, 'B', 'The behavior of blood cells when placed in a solution of varying concentration is governed by the principle of osmosis. Osmosis is the net movement of solvent molecules (in this case, water) through a selectively permeable membrane from a region of higher solvent concentration (lower solute concentration) to a region of lower solvent concentration (higher solute concentration). Blood cells have a semipermeable membrane.

The concentration of the solution is given as 1

1. Hypertonic Solution (1% NaCl): When blood cells are placed in a hypertonic solution, the concentration of solutes (NaCl) outside the cells is higher than inside the cells. Consequently, the concentration of water is lower outside the cells than inside. Water will move by osmosis from the region of higher water concentration (inside the cell) to

the region of lower water concentration (outside the cell) across the cell membrane. This loss of water causes the cell to shrink, a process known as crenation.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.998Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5207, 'chemistry-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q03', 2, 2, 'The rate of the reaction,  $ 2\text{NO} + \text{Cl}_2 \rightarrow 2\text{NOCl} $ is given by the rate equation, rate = k[ $ \text{NO} $]^2[ $ \text{Cl}_2 $]. The value of the rate constant can be increased by', '[{"id":"A","text":"increasing the concentration of NO."},{"id":"B","text":"increasing the temperature."},{"id":"C","text":"increasing the concentration of the $ Cl_{2} $."},{"id":"D","text":"doing all of these."}]', 0, 'D', 'This question pertains to the reactivity of metals and their behavior in electrochemical reactions, specifically displacement reactions. The relative reactivity of metals can be determined by their standard electrode potentials. A metal''s standard reduction potential ( $ E^{\circ} $) indicates its tendency to gain electrons and be reduced.

The standard reduction potentials for zinc and copper are:

 $$ \bullet \quad \mathrm{Zn^{2+}(aq)+2e^{-}\rightarrow Zn(s),E^\circ=-0.76V} $$ 

 $$ \bullet \quad \mathrm{Cu^{2+}(aq)+2e^{-}\rightarrow Cu(s),E^{o}=+0.34V} $$ 

A more reactive metal has a lower (more negative) reduction potential, meaning it has a greater tendency to be oxidized (lose electrons). Conversely, a less reactive metal has a higher (more positive) reduction potential, meaning its ions have a greater tendency to be reduced. Let''s analyze the options:

(a) Copper is more reactive than zinc: This is incorrect. Zinc has a lower reduction potential (-0.76 V) than copper (+0.34 V), signifying that zinc is more reactive and readily oxidized.

(b) Reduction potential of copper is less than zinc: This statement is factually incorrect.

The reduction potential of copper (+0.34 V) is greater than that of zinc (-0.76 V).

(c) Oxidation potential of copper is higher than zinc: The oxidation potential is the negative of the reduction potential. For copper, it''s -0.34 V. For zinc, it''s +0.76 V. Thus, the oxidation potential of copper is lower than that of zinc. This statement is incorrect.

• (d) Reduction potential of copper is higher than zinc: This statement is correct (+0.34 V > -0.76 V).

The reason a copper sulphate solution cannot be stored in a zinc vessel is due to the electrochemical reactivity difference. Since zinc has a lower reduction potential (is more reactive), it will spontaneously displace copper ions from the copper sulphate solution. The reaction is:  $ \mathrm{Zn}(s) + \mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq) \rightarrow \mathrm{Zn}^{2+}(aq) + \mathrm{Cu}(s) $ This reaction consumes the zinc vessel and forms solid copper. The standard cell potential for this reaction is  $ E_{\mathrm{cell}} = E_{\mathrm{cathode}}^{\circ} - E_{\mathrm{anode}}^{\circ} = +0.34\,\mathrm{V} - (-0.76\,\mathrm{V}) = +1.10\,\mathrm{V} $. A positive cell potential confirms the spontaneity of the reaction.

Therefore, option (d) correctly states the electrochemical basis for why zinc is more reactive than copper and will react with copper sulphate solution.

Final Answer: d

Quick Tip: Key Exam Tip:

The reactivity of metals in displacement reactions is determined by their standard electrode potentials. A metal with a lower (more negative) reduction potential is more reactive and will displace metals with higher reduction potentials from their salt solutions.

## 3. The rate of the reaction,  $ 2\text{NO} + \text{Cl}_2 \rightarrow 2\text{NOCl} $ is given by the rate equation, rate = k[ $ \text{NO} $]^2[ $ \text{Cl}_2 $]. The value of the rate constant can be increased by

(a) increasing the concentration of NO.

(b) increasing the temperature.

(c) increasing the concentration of the  $ Cl_{2} $.

(d) doing all of these.

##### Correct Answer: (2) increasing the temperature.

Solution: The given rate equation for the reaction  $ 2\text{NO} + \text{Cl}_2 \rightarrow 2\text{NOCl} $ is: rate = k[ $ \text{NO} $]^{2}[ $ \text{Cl}_2 $]

This equation describes how the rate of the reaction is dependent on the concentrations of the

reactants NO and  $ \text{Cl}_2 $, and the rate constant, k. The rate constant (k) is a proportionality factor

that is specific to a particular reaction at a particular temperature.

Let''s analyze the effect of each option on the rate constant  $ (k) $:

1) Increasing the concentration of NO: The rate equation shows that the reaction rate is proportional to the square of the concentration of NO ([NO] $ ^{2} $). If the concentration of NO increases, the rate of the reaction will increase. However, the rate constant (k) is defined as the rate of reaction when all reactant concentrations are unity. Thus, ‘k’ is fundamentally independent of reactant concentrations at a constant temperature. Increasing [NO] increases the overall rate, but does not change the rate constant.

2) Increasing the temperature: The rate constant (k) is strongly dependent on temperature. This relationship is described by the Arrhenius equation:  $ k = A e^{-E_a / R T} $ where:

• k is the rate constant

• A is the pre-exponential factor (frequency of collisions)

 $ E_{a} $ is the activation energy (minimum energy required for a reaction to occur)

• R is the ideal gas constant

• T is the absolute temperature (in Kelvin)

From the Arrhenius equation, as temperature (T) increases, the term  $ e^{-E_{a}/RT} $ increases because the exponent becomes less negative. This leads to a higher value of the rate constant (k). Therefore, increasing the temperature directly increases the rate constant.

3) Increasing the concentration of  $ \mathrm{Cl}_{2} $: Similar to NO, the rate equation shows that the rate is directly proportional to the concentration of  $ \mathrm{Cl}_{2} $ ( $ [\mathrm{Cl}_{2}] $). Increasing  $ [\mathrm{Cl}_{2}] $ will increase the rate of the reaction. However, the rate constant (k) is independent of reactant concentrations at a constant temperature. Thus, increasing  $ [\mathrm{Cl}_{2}] $ increases the rate, but not the rate constant.

4) Doing all of these: Since only increasing the temperature affects the rate constant, this option is incorrect.

The question specifically asks what can increase the  $ * $value of the rate constant $ * $. Based on chemical kinetics principles, only temperature (and catalysts, which are not options here) affects the rate constant.

Final Answer: 2

Quick Tip: Key Exam Tip:

Remember the distinction between factors affecting the reaction rate and factors affecting the rate constant (k):

- Concentrations of Reactants: Affects the rate directly but does not change the rate constant (k).

• Temperature: Affects both the rate and the rate constant (k). An increase in temperature generally leads to a higher rate constant.

• Catalyst: Speeds up the reaction by increasing the rate constant (k) by lowering the activation energy  $ E_{a} $.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.999Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5208, 'chemistry-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q04', 3, 3, 'Identify the product C in the series.

 $$ \mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{NO}_{2}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{HCl}}A\xrightarrow{\mathrm{NaNO}_{2}+\mathrm{HCl},273\mathrm{~K}}B\xrightarrow{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},283\mathrm{~K}}C $$', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ C_{6}H_{5}OH $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ C_{6}H_{5}CH_{2}OH $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ C_{6}H_{5}CHO $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ C_{6}H_{5}NH_{2} $"}]', 0, 'A', 'This is a classic sequence of reactions involving nitrobenzene and its transformation into phenol. Let''s break down the steps:

Step 1: Reduction of nitrobenzene to aniline  $  \text{C}_6\text{H}_5\text{NO}_2  $ (Nitrobenzene)  $ \xrightarrow{\text{Fe/HCl}} $ A Nitrobenzene is reduced to aniline (aminobenzene) in the presence of a reducing agent like Fe/HCl. Therefore, A is  $  \text{C}_6\text{H}_5\text{NH}_2  $ (Aniline).

Step 2: Diazotization of aniline A ( $ C_6H_5NH_2 $)  $ \xrightarrow{\text{NaNO}_2\text{HCl}, 275^\circ\text{K}} $ B Aniline reacts with sodium nitrite ( $ NaNO_2 $) and hydrochloric acid (HCl) at a low temperature (0-5 °C, which is 273 K) to form a diazonium salt. This process is called diazotization. Therefore, B is  $ C_6H_5N_2Cl^- $ (Benzenediazonium chloride).

Step 3: Hydrolysis of the diazonium salt B ( $ C_{6}H_{5}N_{2}^{+}Cl^{-} $)  $ \xrightarrow{H_{2}O, 283 K} $ C Benzenediazonium chloride reacts with water at a slightly elevated temperature (around 10-15 °C, which is 283 K) to undergo hydrolysis. The diazonium group ( $ -N_{2}^{+} $) is replaced by a hydroxyl group (-OH), forming phenol. Nitrogen gas ( $ N_{2} $) and HCl are released as byproducts. The reaction is:  $ C_{6}H_{5}N_{2}^{+}Cl^{-} + H_{2}O \xrightarrow{\Delta} C_{6}H_{5}OH + N_{2} + HCl $

Therefore, the product C is  $ C_{6}H_{5}OH $ (Phenol).

Let''s check the options: (1)  $ C_{6}H_{5}OH $ (Phenol) - This matches our product. (2)  $ C_{6}H_{5}CH_{2}OH $ (Benzyl alcohol) - Incorrect. (3)  $ C_{6}H_{5}CHO $ (Benzaldehyde) - Incorrect. (4)  $ C_{6}H_{5}NH_{2} $ (Aniline) - This is intermediate A, not final product C.

Final Answer: 1

Quick Tip: Key Exam Tip:

Remember the key transformations:

- Nitro group reduction to amine: e.g., R-NO₂  $ \xrightarrow{\text{Fe/HCl or Sn/HCl}} $ R-NH₂.

• Diazotization of primary aromatic amines: R-NH_{2}  $ \xrightarrow{\text{NaNO}_{2}+\text{HCl, low temp}} $ R-N_{2}^{+}Cl^{-}.

• Hydrolysis of diazonium salts:  $  \text{R} - \text{N}_2^+ X^- \xrightarrow{\text{H}_2\text{O}, \text{heat}} \text{R} - \text{OH} + \text{N}_2 + \text{HX}  $.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.999Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5209, 'chemistry-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q07', 4, 4, 'Which of the following expression correctly represents molar conductivity?', '[{"id":"A","text":"$$ \\Lambda_{m}=\\tfrac{K}{C} $$"},{"id":"B","text":"$ \\Lambda_{m} = \\frac{KA}{1} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ \\Lambda_{m}=KV $"},{"id":"D","text":"all of these"}]', 0, 'A', 'Molar conductivity ( $ \Lambda_{m} $) is a measure of the efficiency of ion conduction in a solution. It is defined as the conductivity of a solution divided by the molar concentration of the electrolyte.

The relationship between molar conductivity ( $ \Lambda_{m} $), specific conductivity (or conductivity, K), and molar concentration (C) is given by the formula:  $ \Lambda_{m} = \frac{K}{C} $

•  $ \Lambda_m $ is the molar conductivity (units: S m $ ^2 $ mol $ ^{-1} $ or S cm $ ^2 $ mol $ ^{-1} $).

- K (kappa) is the specific conductivity (or conductivity) of the solution (units: S m $ ^{-1} $ or S cm $ ^{-1} $).

- C is the molar concentration of the electrolyte (units: mol m $ ^{-3} $ or mol cm $ ^{-3} $, often mol L $ ^{-1} $).

Let''s examine the given options:

1.  $ \Lambda_m = \frac{K}{C} $: This is the correct definition of molar conductivity.

2. $\Lambda_{m}=\frac{KA}{1}$: This expression is dimensionally incorrect. ''A'' is not defined in this context, and the units would not match molar conductivity. If ''A'' were meant to be volume, it would be $\Lambda_{m}=\frac{K}{C}=\frac{K}{n/V}=\frac{KV}{n}$.

3. $\Lambda_{m} = KV$: This expression is also dimensionally incorrect and does not represent molar conductivity. If $V''$ represents the volume of solution containing one mole of electrolyte (i.e., $V = 1/C$), then $\Lambda_{m} = K \times \frac{1}{C} = \frac{K}{C}$, which brings us back to option 1. However, as stated, it''s incorrect.

4. all of these: Since options 2 and 3 are incorrect, this option is also incorrect.

Thus, the only correct expression for molar conductivity is  $ \Lambda_{m} = \frac{K}{C} $.

Final Answer: 1

Quick Tip: Key Exam Tip:

Molar conductivity ( $ \Lambda_{m} $) is the conductivity of one mole of electrolyte. It is calculated by dividing the specific conductivity (K) by the molar concentration (C). Ensure you use consistent units for K and C to obtain the correct units for  $ \Lambda_{m} $.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:06.999Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5210, 'chemistry-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q08', 5, 5, 'For the given Nernst equation

 $$ \begin{array}{r}{E_{\mathrm{c e l l}}=E_{\mathrm{c e l l}}^{\circ}-\frac{R T}{n F}\ln\left[\frac{[\mathrm{M g}^{2+}]}{[\mathrm{A g}^{+}]}\right]}\end{array} $$ 

Which of the following representation is correct?', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ Ag^{+} $ | Ag | | $ Mg^{2+} $ | Mg"},{"id":"B","text":"$ Mg^{2+} $ |Mg||Ag| $ Ag^{+} $"},{"id":"C","text":"Mg $ \\left|Mg^{2+}\\right|\\left|Ag^{+}\\right|Ag $"},{"id":"D","text":"Mg $ \\left|Mg^{2+}\\right| $ $ \\left|Ag\\right|Ag^{+} $"}]', 0, 'C', 'The Nernst equation relates the cell potential ( $ E_{cell} $) of an electrochemical cell to the standard cell potential ( $ E_{cell}^{\circ} $) and the concentrations of the ions involved. The general form of the Nernst equation for a redox reaction is:  $ E_{cell} = E_{cell}^{\circ} - \frac{RT}{nF} \ln Q $ where Q is the reaction quotient.

The given Nernst equation is:  $ E_{\text{cell}} = E_{\text{cell}}^\circ - \frac{RT}{nF} \ln\left[\frac{[\text{Mg}^{2+}]}{[\text{Ag}^+]}\right] $

In this equation, the term inside the logarithm,  $ \frac{[\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}]}{[\mathrm{Ag}^{+}]} $, represents the reaction quotient (Q). The reaction quotient is written as (products)/(reactants), considering the stoichiometric coefficients. The expression  $ \ln\left[\frac{[\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}]}{[\mathrm{Ag}^{+}]}\right] $ indicates that  $ \mathrm{Mg}^{2+} $ is in the numerator (likely a product of oxidation) and  $ \mathrm{Ag}^{+} $ is in the denominator (likely a reactant in reduction).

A cell representation in shorthand notation is written as: Anode | Anode Solution || Cathode Solution | Cathode

The anode is where oxidation occurs, and the cathode is where reduction occurs. The species in the cell notation are written in the form of: Reduced form | Oxidized form (for anode) and

Oxidized form | Reduced form (for cathode).

From the reaction quotient  $ \frac{[Mg^{2+}]}{[Ag^{+}]} $, we can infer the half-reactions:

• Anode (Oxidation): Mg  $ \rightarrow $ Mg $ ^{2+} $ + 2e $ ^{-} $ (Mg is oxidized to Mg $ ^{2+} $)

• Cathode (Reduction):  $ Ag^{+} + e^{-} \rightarrow Ag $ ( $ Ag^{+} $ is reduced to Ag)

Now let''s construct the cell notation:

• Anode half-cell: Reduced form is Mg, oxidized form is  $ Mg^{2+} $. So, it''s Mg  $ |Mg^{2+} $

Cathode half-cell: Oxidized form is  $ Ag^{+} $, reduced form is Ag. So, it''s  $ Ag^{+} $ | Ag.

Connecting these with the salt bridge (||): Anode || Cathode Mg | Mg²⁺ || Ag⁺ | Ag

Let’s check the given options: (a) Ag⁺ | Ag || Mg²⁺ | Mg: This represents Ag as anode and Mg as cathode, which is incorrect based on the reaction quotient. (b) Mg²⁺ | Mg || Ag | Ag⁺: This representation is incorrect in how it writes the half-cells. The reduced form should be on the left for the anode, and the oxidized form on the left for the cathode. (c) Mg | Mg²⁺ | | Ag⁺ | Ag: This representation correctly shows Mg as the anode (oxidation) and Ag as the cathode (reduction), with the correct species in their respective states. (d) Mg | Mg²⁺ | | Ag | Ag⁺: This representation is incorrect in how it writes the cathode half-cell.

Therefore, the correct representation of the cell is  $ \mathrm{Mg}|\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}||\mathrm{Ag}^{+}|\mathrm{Ag} $.

Final Answer: c

Quick Tip: Key Exam Tip:

In electrochemical cell notation:

• Anode (oxidation) is written first: Reduced form | Oxidized form.

• Cathode (reduction) is written second: Oxidized form | Reduced form.

• A single vertical line (|) separates phases (e.g., electrode and solution).

- A double vertical line (||) separates the anode and cathode compartments, representing the salt bridge.

The Nernst equation’s reaction quotient (Q) directly reveals which species are oxidized and reduced, and thus the anode and cathode half-cells.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.000Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5211, 'chemistry-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q10', 6, 6, 'The rate of the reaction,  $ 2NO + Cl_{2} \rightarrow 2NOCl $ is given by the rate equation, rate = k[NO] $ ^{2} $[Cl $ _{2} $]. The value of the rate constant can be increased by', '[{"id":"A","text":"increasing the concentration of NO."},{"id":"B","text":"increasing the temperature."},{"id":"C","text":"increasing the concentration of the $ Cl_{2} $."},{"id":"D","text":"doing all of these."}]', 0, 'B', 'The given rate equation for the reaction  $ 2\text{NO} + \text{Cl}_2 \rightarrow 2\text{NOCl} $ is: rate = k[ $ \text{NO} $]^{2}[ $ \text{Cl}_2 $]

This equation shows that the rate of the reaction depends on the rate constant (k) and the concentrations of the reactants NO and  $ \text{Cl}_2 $. The rate constant (k) is a proportionality constant specific to a reaction at a given temperature.

Let''s analyze how each factor affects the rate and specifically the rate constant:

1) Increasing the concentration of NO: According to the rate equation, the rate is directly proportional to  $ [NO]^{2} $. If  $ [NO] $ increases, the rate of the reaction will increase significantly. However, the rate constant  $ k'' $ is independent of reactant concentrations at a constant temperature.

ture. Increasing the concentration of NO will increase the rate of the reaction, but not the rate constant.

2) Increasing the temperature: The rate constant (k) is highly dependent on temperature. According to the Arrhenius equation,  $ k = A e^{-E_a / R T} $, where:

• k is the rate constant.

- A is the pre-exponential factor (frequency factor).

 $ E_{a} $ is the activation energy.

• R is the gas constant.

• T is the absolute temperature.

As temperature (T) increases, the term  $ e^{-E_{a}/RT} $ increases (because the exponent becomes less negative), leading to an increase in the rate constant (k). Therefore, increasing the temperature will increase the value of the rate constant.

3) Increasing the concentration of  $ \mathrm{Cl}_2 $: The rate equation shows that the rate is directly proportional to  $ [\mathrm{Cl}_2] $. If  $ [\mathrm{Cl}_2] $ increases, the rate of the reaction will increase. However, like concentration of NO, the rate constant ''k'' is independent of reactant concentrations at a constant temperature. Increasing the concentration of  $ \mathrm{Cl}_2 $ will increase the rate of the reaction, but not the rate constant.

4) Doing all of these: Since only increasing the temperature affects the rate constant, this option is incorrect.

The question specifically asks what can increase the  $ * $value of the rate constant $ * $. Among the given options, only increasing the temperature affects the rate constant.

Final Answer: 2

Quick Tip: Key Exam Tip:

Factors Affecting Reaction Rate vs. Rate Constant:

• Concentration of Reactants: Affects the rate of the reaction but not the rate constant (k).

• Temperature: Affects both the rate and the rate constant (k). Higher temperature generally leads to a higher rate constant.

• Catalyst: Affects the rate by lowering the activation energy, thus increasing the rate constant (k).

In this question, only temperature and a catalyst (not provided as an option) affects the rate constant.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.000Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5212, 'chemistry-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Which of the following compounds shows geometrical isomerism?', '[{"id":"A","text":"But-1-ene"},{"id":"B","text":"But-2-ene"},{"id":"C","text":"Propene"},{"id":"D","text":"Ethene"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Geometrical isomerism (cis-trans isomerism) occurs in alkenes when there is restricted rotation around a double bond and each carbon atom of the double bond is attached to two different groups.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s examine the structure of But-2-ene  $ (CH_{3}-CH=CH-CH_{3}) $:

Each carbon in the C = C bond is attached to one Hydrogen  $ (-H) $ and one Methyl group  $ (-CH_{3}) $.

Because the two groups on each carbon are different, they can exist in two spatial arrangements: Cis (same groups on the same side) and Trans (same groups on opposite sides).

- In But-1-ene or Propene, one of the double-bonded carbons is attached to two identical Hydrogen atoms, so they cannot show this isomerism.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The compound that shows geometrical isomerism is But-2-ene.

Quick Tip: To quickly check for geometrical isomerism, look at the C = C bond. If either carbon has two identical atoms (like two H atoms), it cannot have cis-trans isomers.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.000Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5213, 'chemistry-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q02', 2, 2, 'The IUPAC name of  $ (CH_{3}-CH_{2}-CHO) $ is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Propanone"},{"id":"B","text":"Propanal"},{"id":"C","text":"Propanoic acid"},{"id":"D","text":"Ethanol"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

IUPAC nomenclature follows a specific set of rules: identifying the longest carbon chain, the functional group, and the appropriate suffix.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Count the Carbons: The chain  $ CH_3 - CH_2 - CHO $ has 3 carbon atoms. The prefix for 3 carbons is "prop-". 2. Identify the Bond: It is a saturated chain (single bonds between carbons), so it is "propan-". 3. Identify the Functional Group: The group -CHO is an aldehyde. 4. Determine the Suffix: The IUPAC suffix for aldehydes is "-al".

Step 3: Final Answer:

Combining these, we get Propanal. (Propanone is a ketone, Propanoic acid is a carboxylic acid).

Quick Tip: Don''t forget to count the carbon atom that is part of the functional group! In  $ CH_{3}CH_{2}CHO $, the C in CHO is the 1st carbon.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.001Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5214, 'chemistry-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q03', 3, 3, 'The oxidation state of chromium in  $ (K_{2}Cr_{2}O_{7}) $ is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"+4"},{"id":"B","text":"+5"},{"id":"C","text":"+6"},{"id":"D","text":"+7"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The oxidation state is the hypothetical charge an atom would have if all bonds to atoms of different elements were 100% ionic. The sum of oxidation states in a neutral compound must be zero.

Step 2: Identifying Known Oxidation States:

In  $ K_{2}Cr_{2}O_{7} $:

• Potassium (K) is an alkali metal, so its state is +1.

• Oxygen (O) is usually -2.

- Let the oxidation state of Chromium  $ (Cr) $ be x.

Step 3: Calculation:

Set up the equation for the neutral molecule:

 $$ 2(+1)+2(x)+7(-2)=0 $$ 

 $$ 2+2x-14=0 $$ 

 $$ 2x-12=0 $$ 

 $$ 2x=12\implies x=+6 $$ 

#### Step 4: Final Answer:

The oxidation state of Chromium in Potassium Dichromate is +6.

Quick Tip: Chromium in the +6 state is a very strong oxidizing agent. This is why  $ K_{2}Cr_{2}O_{7} $ is commonly used in laboratories for titration and organic oxidation reactions.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.001Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5215, 'chemistry-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Which of the following is a strong electrolyte?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Glucose solution"},{"id":"B","text":"Urea solution"},{"id":"C","text":"Acetic acid"},{"id":"D","text":"Sodium chloride solution"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

An electrolyte is a substance that dissociates into ions when dissolved in water, allowing the solution to conduct electricity. A "strong" electrolyte is one that dissociates almost completely (100%) into ions.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Sodium chloride (NaCl): Is an ionic salt. In water, it breaks down completely into  $ Na^{+} $ and  $ Cl^{-} $ ions. Hence, it is a strong electrolyte.

• Acetic acid ( $ CH_{3}COOH $): Is a weak acid. It only partially ionizes in water, making it a weak electrolyte.

• Glucose and Urea: Are covalent molecular compounds. They dissolve in water but do not form ions; therefore, they are non-electrolytes.

Step 3: Final Answer:
Sodium chloride solution is the strong electrolyte.
Quick Tip: Strong acids (like HCl), strong bases (like NaOH), and most soluble salts are strong electrolytes. If it''s a "weak" acid or base, it''s a weak electrolyte!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.001Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5216, 'chemistry-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q05', 5, 5, 'The hybridization of carbon in ethyne  $  (C_{2}H_{2})  $ is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ sp^{3} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ sp^{2} $"},{"id":"C","text":"sp"},{"id":"D","text":"$ dsp^{2} $"}]', 0, 'C', 'Hybridization explains the mixing of atomic orbitals to form new hybrid orbitals suitable for the pairing of electrons to form chemical bonds. The type of hybridization depends on the number of sigma ( $ \sigma $) bonds and lone pairs around the atom.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

In ethyne  $  (H - C \equiv C - H)  $:

• Each carbon atom is bonded to one Hydrogen atom (one  $ \sigma $ bond) and one Carbon atom (one  $ \sigma $ bond and two  $ \pi $ bonds).

• Total number of  $ \sigma $ bonds per carbon = 2.

• There are no lone pairs on the carbon atoms.

- A total of 2 hybrid orbitals are needed, which corresponds to sp hybridization.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The hybridization of carbon in ethyne is sp.

Quick Tip: Single bond  $ (C-C) \rightarrow sp^{3} $

Double bond  $ (C=C) \rightarrow sp^{2} $

Triple bond  $ (C \equiv C) \rightarrow sp $', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.001Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5217, 'chemistry-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q06', 6, 6, 'Which gas is evolved when zinc reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Oxygen"},{"id":"B","text":"Chlorine"},{"id":"C","text":"Hydrogen"},{"id":"D","text":"Nitrogen"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Metals that are more reactive than hydrogen (positioned above hydrogen in the reactivity series) react with dilute acids to displace hydrogen gas.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Zinc (Zn) is more reactive than hydrogen. When it reacts with hydrochloric acid (HCl), it forms zinc chloride and releases hydrogen gas. The chemical equation is:

 $$ Zn(s)+2HCl(aq)\rightarrow ZnCl_{2}(aq)+H_{2}(g)\uparrow $$ 

Step 3: Final Answer:

Hydrogen gas is evolved during the reaction.

Quick Tip: You can test for Hydrogen gas by bringing a burning splinter near the mouth of the test tube—it will burn with a characteristic "pop" sound!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.001Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5218, 'chemistry-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q08', 7, 7, 'Which quantum number determines the shape of an orbital?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Principal quantum number"},{"id":"B","text":"Azimuthal quantum number"},{"id":"C","text":"Magnetic quantum number"},{"id":"D","text":"Spin quantum number"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:
Quantum numbers are a set of four numbers used to describe the location, energy, and behavior of electrons in an atom.
Step 2: Detailed Explanation:
Each quantum number has a specific role:
- Principal (n): Determines the size and energy of the orbital.
- Azimuthal (l): Also known as the orbital angular momentum quantum number, it determines the three-dimensional shape of the orbital (e.g., l = 0 is spherical s, l = 1 is dumbbell p).
- Magnetic ( $ m_{l} $): Determines the spatial orientation of the orbital.
- Spin ( $ m_{s} $): Determines the direction of electron spin.
Step 3: Final Answer:
The Azimuthal quantum number determines the shape of the orbital.
Quick Tip: Think of the quantum numbers as an address: n is the City (size), l is the Building Shape,  $ m_{l} $ is the Room Orientation, and  $ m_{s} $ is the Bed Choice!

Quick Tip: Think of the quantum numbers as an address: n is the City (size), l is the Building Shape,  $ m_{l} $ is the Room Orientation, and  $ m_{s} $ is the Bed Choice!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.001Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5219, 'chemistry-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q09', 8, 8, 'Which of the following polymers is formed by addition polymerization?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Nylon-6,6"},{"id":"B","text":"Bakelite"},{"id":"C","text":"Terylene"},{"id":"D","text":"Polyethene"}]', 0, 'D', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Polymerization is the process of reacting monomer molecules together in a chemical reaction to form polymer chains.

• Addition Polymerization: Monomers join together without the loss of any small molecules. It typically involves unsaturated monomers (with double or triple bonds).

• Condensation Polymerization: Monomers join together with the elimination of small molecules like water or alcohol.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Polyethene: Formed by the repeated addition of ethene  $ (CH_{2}=CH_{2}) $ molecules. No byproduct is formed.

Nylon-6,6, Bakelite, and Terylene: These are all formed by condensation reactions where molecules like water are released.

Step 3: Final Answer:

Polyethene is formed by addition polymerization.

Quick Tip: If the name of the polymer starts with "Poly-" and the monomer has a double bond (like Ethene, Styrene, or Vinyl Chloride), it is almost certainly an addition polymer!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.001Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5220, 'chemistry-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q10', 9, 9, 'The reagent used in the conversion of alcohol into aldehyde is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"LiAlH_{4}"},{"id":"B","text":"PCC"},{"id":"C","text":"NaBH_{4}"},{"id":"D","text":"Grignard reagent"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To convert a primary alcohol into an aldehyde, we need an oxidizing agent. However, many strong oxidizing agents (like  $ KMnO_{4} $) will continue to oxidize the aldehyde into a carboxylic acid. Therefore, a "mild" or "selective" oxidizing agent is required.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- PCC (Pyridinium Chlorochromate): This is a mild oxidizing agent. It selectively oxidizes primary alcohols to aldehydes and secondary alcohols to ketones without further oxidation to carboxylic acids.

-  $ LiAlH_{4} $ and  $ NaBH_{4} $: These are reducing agents, not oxidizing agents. They would do the opposite (convert an aldehyde back into an alcohol).

Grignard reagent: This is used for carbon-carbon bond formation, not for the simple oxidation of an alcohol to an aldehyde.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The reagent used is PCC.

Quick Tip: Think of PCC as a "controlled" stop. It stops the oxidation at the Aldehyde stage. Stronger reagents like Jones Reagent ( $ CrO_{3}/H_{2}SO_{4} $) are like an express train—they go all the way to the Carboxylic Acid!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.001Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5221, 'chemistry-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'The molar conductivity of 0.5 mol/dm³ solution of AgNO₃ with electrolytic conductivity of  $ 5.76 \times 10^{-3} $ S cm⁻¹ at 298 K is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"0.086S cm $ ^{2} $/mol"},{"id":"B","text":"28.8S cm^{2}/mol"},{"id":"C","text":"2.88S cm^{2}/mol"},{"id":"D","text":"11.52S cm $ ^{2} $/mol"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept: Molar conductivity ( $ \Lambda_{m} $) is defined as the conductance of all ions produced by one mole of an electrolyte dissolved in a given volume of solution.

The relation between molar conductivity and conductivity is:

 $$ \Lambda_{m}=\frac{\kappa\times1000}{C} $$ 

where:

•  $ \Lambda_{m} = \text{molar conductivity (S cm}^{2}/\text{mol)} $

 $ \kappa = \text{conductivity} (S \, \text{cm}^{-1}) $

 $ C = \text{concentration in } (\text{mol L}^{-1}) $

Step 1: Write the given data

Given:

 $$ \kappa=5.76\times10^{-3}S cm^{-1} $$ 

 $$ C=0.5\mathrm{m o l}\mathrm{d m}^{-3} $$ 

Since:

 $$ 1\,\mathrm{d m}^{3}=1\mathrm{L} $$ 

therefore:

 $$ C=0.5mol L^{-1} $$ 

Step 2: Apply the molar conductivity formula

 $$ \Lambda_{m}=\frac{\kappa\times1000}{C} $$ 

Substitute values:

 $$ \Lambda_{m}=\frac{5.76\times10^{-3}\times1000}{0.5} $$ 

Step 3: Simplify the numerator

 $$ 5.76\times10^{-3}\times1000 $$ 

Since:

 $$ 1000=10^{3} $$ 

 $$ 5.76\times10^{-3}\times10^{3}=5.76 $$ 

Thus:

 $$ \Lambda_{m}=\frac{5.76}{0.5} $$ 

Step 4: Perform the division

 $$ \frac{5.76}{0.5}=11.52 $$ 

Therefore:

 $$ \mathrm{\Lambda}_{m}=11.52\mathrm{S}\mathrm{~cm}^{2}/\mathrm{mol} $$ 

Final Answer:

 $$ 11.52\,\mathrm{S}\,\mathrm{c m}^{2}/\mathrm{m o l} $$ 

Quick Tip: While calculating molar conductivity, always remember the formula:

 $$ \Lambda_{m}=\frac{\kappa\times1000}{C} $$ 

where concentration must be in mol  $ L^{-1} $', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.002Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5222, 'chemistry-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R:

Assertion (A): A reaction can have zero activation energy.

Reason (R): The minimum extra amount of energy absorbed by reactant molecules so that their energy becomes equal to threshold value, is called activation energy.

In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A"},{"id":"B","text":"Both A and R are true and R is NOT the correct explanation of A"},{"id":"C","text":"A is true but R is false"},{"id":"D","text":"A is false but R is true"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: Activation energy is the minimum energy required by reacting molecules to form

the activated complex and undergo successful collision. According to collision theory, only those collisions which possess energy equal to or greater than activation energy lead to product formation.

Some reactions, especially very fast ionic reactions, may have nearly zero activation energy.

### Step 1: Analyze Assertion (A)

Assertion states:

## A reaction can have zero activation energy.

This statement is true.

Certain reactions occur so rapidly that practically no extra energy barrier exists between reactants and products. Such reactions are considered to have negligible or nearly zero activation energy.

Examples include some ionic reactions occurring in aqueous medium.

Therefore:

##### Assertion A is true.

### Step 2: Analyze Reason (R)

Reason states:

Activation energy is the minimum extra energy required to reach threshold energy.

This is the correct definition of activation energy.

Thus:

Reason R is also true.

### Step 3: Check whether R explains A

Although Reason correctly defines activation energy, it does not explain why some reactions can have zero activation energy.

The statement in Reason is only a definition and does not justify the condition of zero activation energy.

Hence:

R is not the correct explanation of A.

##### Final Answer:

Both A and R are true and R is NOT the correct explanation of A

Quick Tip: In Assertion-Reason questions, first check whether each statement is independently true, then verify whether the Reason logically explains the Assertion.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.003Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5223, 'chemistry-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q03', 3, 3, 'The following data is for a reaction between reactants A and B:




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Rate (mol L^{-1}s^{-1})</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>[A]</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>[B]</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>$ 2 \times 10^{-3} $</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>0.1M</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>0.1M</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>$ 4 \times 10^{-3} $</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>0.2M</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>0.1M</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>$ 1.6 \times 10^{-2} $</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>0.2M</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>0.2M</td></tr></table>


The order of the reaction with respect to A and B, respectively are:', '[{"id":"A","text":"1,0"},{"id":"B","text":"0,1"},{"id":"C","text":"1,2"},{"id":"D","text":"2,1"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: For a reaction:

 $$ aA+bB\rightarrow Products $$ 

Rate law is:

 $$  Rate=k[A]^{m}[B]^{n} $$ 

where:

• m = order with respect to A

• $n$ = order with respect to B

Orders are determined experimentally by comparing rate changes with concentration changes.

Step 1: Determine order with respect to A

Compare experiment 1 and 2:

 $$ \frac{Rate_{2}}{Rate_{1}}=\frac{4\times10^{-3}}{2\times10^{-3}}=2 $$ 

Concentration change:

 $$ \frac{\left[A\right]_{2}}{\left[A\right]_{1}}=\frac{0.2}{0.1}=2 $$ 

[B] remains constant.

Thus:

 $$ 2=2^{m} $$ 

 $$ m=1 $$ 

So, order with respect to A is:

1

### Step 2: Determine order with respect to B

Compare experiment 2 and 3:

 $$ \frac{Rate_{3}}{Rate_{2}}=\frac{1.6\times10^{-2}}{4\times10^{-3}}=4 $$ 

Concentration change:

 $$ \frac{\left[B\right]_{3}}{\left[B\right]_{2}}=\frac{0.2}{0.1}=2 $$ 

[A] remains constant.

 $$ 4=2^{n} $$ 

Thus:

 $$ n=2 $$ 

So, order with respect to B is:

Step 3: Write final order pair

 $$ (m,n)=(1,2) $$ 

Final Answer:

Quick Tip: While finding reaction order, compare only those experiments where one reactant concentration changes and the other remains constant.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.003Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5224, 'chemistry-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q04', 4, 4, '$ t_{99.9\%} $ with respect to  $ t_{90\%} $ for a first order reaction is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Two"},{"id":"B","text":"One"},{"id":"C","text":"Three"},{"id":"D","text":"Four"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: For a first-order reaction:

 $$ t=\frac{2.303}{k}\log\frac{a}{a-x} $$ 

For percentage completion:

 $$ \frac{a}{a-x}=\frac{100}{unreacted\ percentage} $$ 

Step 1: Calculate  $ t_{90\%} $

At 90% completion:

10% reactant remains

Thus:

 $$ \begin{aligned}t_{90\%}&=\frac{2.303}{k}\log\frac{100}{10}\\ &=\frac{2.303}{k}\log10\\ \end{aligned} $$ 

Since:

 $$ \log10=1 $$ 

 $$ t_{90\%}=\frac{2.303}{k} $$ 

Step 2: Calculate  $ t_{99.9\%} $

At 99.9% completion:

0.1% reactant remains

Thus:

 $$ \begin{aligned}&t_{99.9\%}=\frac{2.303}{k}\log\frac{100}{0.1}\\ &\\ &\quad=\frac{2.303}{k}\log1000\\ \end{aligned} $$ 

Since:

log1000=3

 $$ t_{99.9\%}=\frac{2.303}{k}\times3 $$ 

Step 3: Find ratio

 $$ \frac{t_{99.9\%}}{t_{90\%}}=\frac{3\left(\frac{2.303}{k}\right)}{\left(\frac{2.303}{k}\right)} $$ 

 $$ =3 $$ 

Final Answer:

Quick Tip: For first-order reactions:

 $$ t_{90\%}:t_{99\%}:t_{99.9\%}=1:2:3 $$ 

This shortcut is extremely useful in MCQs.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.003Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5225, 'chemistry-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q05', 5, 5, '99% of a first order reaction was completed in 32 minutes. When will 99.9% of the reaction complete?', '[{"id":"A","text":"48 minute"},{"id":"B","text":"49 minute"},{"id":"C","text":"46 minute"},{"id":"D","text":"50 minute"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept: For first-order reactions:

 $$ t=\frac{2.303}{k}\log\frac{a}{a-x} $$ 

Special results:

 $$ t_{90\%}=\frac{2.303}{k} $$ 

 $$ t_{99\%}=\frac{4.606}{k} $$ 

 $$ t_{99.9\%}=\frac{6.909}{k} $$ 

Hence:

 $$ t_{99.9\%}=\frac{3}{2}t_{99\%} $$ 

Step 1: Write given information

Given:

 $$ t_{99\%}=32\mathrm{m i n u t e s} $$ 

We use relation:

 $$ \mathrm{\frac{t_{99,9\%}}{t_{99\%}}} = \frac{3}{2} $$ 

Step 2: Calculate  $ t_{99.9\%} $

 $$ t_{99.9\%}=\frac{3}{2}\times32 $$ 

 $$ =48\mathrm{m i n u t e s} $$ 

Final Answer:

48 minutes

Quick Tip: For first-order reactions:

 $$ t_{90\%}:t_{99\%}:t_{99.9\%}=1:2:3 $$ 

Use this direct ratio method to solve questions quickly.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.003Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5226, 'chemistry-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q06', 6, 6, 'The slope of Arrhenius Plot ( $ \ln k $ v/s  $ \frac{1}{T} $) of the first-order reaction is  $ -5 \times 10^{3} $ K. The value of  $ E_{a} $ of the reaction is. Choose the correct option for your answer. [Given  $ R = 8.314JK^{-1}mol^{-1} $]', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ 166 \\, kJ mol^{-1} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ -83 \\, kJ mol^{-1} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ 41.5 \\, kJ mol^{-1} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ 83.0 \\, kJ mol^{-1} $"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: The Arrhenius equation explains the dependence of rate constant on temperature. It is expressed as:

 $$ k=A e^{-\frac{E_{a}}{R T}} $$ 

where:

• k = rate constant

• A = Arrhenius frequency factor

•  $ E_{a} $ = activation energy

• $R$ = gas constant

•  $ T $ = absolute temperature

Taking natural logarithm on both sides converts the equation into linear form:

 $$ \ln k=\ln A-\frac{E_{a}}{RT} $$ 

This equation represents the equation of a straight line:

 $$ y=m x+c $$ 

Hence, for a graph of  $ \ln k $ versus  $ \frac{1}{T} $:

 $$  Slope=-\frac{E_{a}}{R} $$ 

Step 1: Writing the relation between slope and activation energy.

For Arrhenius plot:

 $$  Slope=-\frac{E_{a}}{R} $$ 

Given:

 $$ \mathrm{S l o p e}=-5\times10^{3}K $$ 

Substituting into the equation:

 $$ -\frac{E_{a}}{R}=-5\times10^{3} $$ 

Removing negative signs:

 $$ \frac{E_{a}}{R}=5\times10^{3} $$ 

Therefore:

 $$ E_{a}=5\times10^{3}\times R $$ 

Step 2: Substituting the value of gas constant.

Given:

 $$ R=8.314J K^{-1}m o l^{-1} $$ 

Thus:

 $$ E_{a}=5\times10^{3}\times8.314 $$ 

 $$ E_{a}=41570J mol^{-1} $$ 

Step 3: Converting Joule into kilojoule.

Since:

 $$ 1kJ=1000J $$ 

Therefore:

 $$ E_{a}=\frac{41570}{1000} $$ 

 $$ E_{a}=41.57kJ mol^{-1} $$ 

Approximating:

 $$ E_{a}\approx41.5kJ mol^{-1} $$ 

Final Answer:

 $$ 41.5kJ mol^{-1} $$ 

Quick Tip: For Arrhenius plots:

 $$ \ln k vs\frac{1}{T}\Rightarrow Slope=-\frac{E_{a}}{R} $$ 

while

 $$ \log k~vs~\frac{1}{T}\Rightarrow Slope=-\frac{E_{a}}{2.303R} $$ 

Always check whether the logarithm is natural log or common log.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.004Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5227, 'chemistry-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q07', 7, 7, '''Spin only'' magnetic moment is same for which of the following ions?

A.  $ Ti^{3+} $

B.  $ Cr^{2+} $

C.  $ Mn^{2+} $

D.  $ Fe^{2+} $

E.  $ Sc^{3+} $

Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"B and D only"},{"id":"B","text":"A and E only"},{"id":"C","text":"B and C only"},{"id":"D","text":"A and D only"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept: The spin-only magnetic moment depends on the number of unpaired electrons present in the atom or ion.

Formula:

 $$ \mu=\sqrt{n(n+2)}BM $$ 

where:

• n = number of unpaired electrons

• BM = Bohr Magneton

If two ions possess the same number of unpaired electrons, they will have the same magnetic moment.

Step 1: Finding the electronic configuration of each ion.

 $$ T i^{3+} $$ 

Atomic number of Ti = 22

Electronic configuration:

 $$ Ti=[Ar]3d^{2}4s^{2} $$ 

For  $ Ti^{3+} $, remove 3 electrons:

 $$ Ti^{3+}=[Ar]3d^{1} $$ 

Number of unpaired electrons:

 $$ n=1 $$ 

 $$ C r^{2+} $$ 

Atomic number of Cr = 24

Electronic configuration:

 $$ Cr=[Ar]3d^{5}4s^{1} $$ 

Removing two electrons:

 $$ Cr^{2+}=[Ar]3d^{4} $$ 

According to Hund''s rule:

 $$ n=4 $$ 

 $$ Mn^{2+} $$ 

Atomic number of Mn = 25

 $$ Mn=[Ar]3d^{5}4s^{2} $$ 

Removing two electrons:

 $$ Mn^{2+}=[Ar]3d^{5} $$ 

All electrons remain unpaired.

 $$ n=5 $$ 

 $$ Fe^{2+} $$ 

Atomic number of Fe = 26

 $$ Fe=[Ar]3d^{6}4s^{2} $$ 

Removing two electrons:

 $$ Fe^{2+}=[Ar]3d^{6} $$ 

In  $ 3d^{6} $, four electrons remain unpaired.

 $$ n=4 $$ 

 $$ S c^{3+} $$ 

Atomic number of Sc = 21

 $$ Sc=[Ar]3d^{1}4s^{2} $$ 

Removing three electrons:

 $$ Sc^{3+}=[Ar]3d^{0} $$ 

 $$ n=0 $$ 

Step 2: Comparing the number of unpaired electrons.

 $$ Ti^{3+}\Rightarrow n=1 $$ 

 $$ Cr^{2+}\Rightarrow n=4 $$ 

 $$ Mn^{2+}\Rightarrow n=5 $$ 

 $$ Fe^{2+}\Rightarrow n=4 $$ 

 $$ Sc^{3+}\Rightarrow n=0 $$ 

Thus:

 $$ Cr^{2+}andFe^{2+} $$ 

have equal unpaired electrons.

Hence both possess same magnetic moment.

Final Answer:

B and D only

Quick Tip: For  $ d^{n} $ configuration:

 $$ n\leq5\Rightarrow\mathrm{Unpaired~electrons}=n $$ 

 $$ n>5\Rightarrow Unpaired~electrons=10-n $$ 

This shortcut helps in rapid calculation of magnetic moments.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.004Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5228, 'chemistry-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q08', 8, 8, 'Which of the following set of ions act as oxidizing agents?', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ Ce^{4+} $ and $ Tb^{4+} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ La^{3+} $ and $ Lu^{3+} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ Eu^{2+} $ and $ Yb^{2+} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ Eu^{2+} $ and $ Tb^{4+} $"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept: Lanthanoids most commonly exhibit the +3 oxidation state. Ions existing in oxidation states other than +3 tend to convert back into the more stable +3 state.

• +4 ions gain electrons and get reduced to +3, hence act as oxidizing agents.

• +2 ions lose electrons and get oxidized to +3, hence act as reducing agents.

Step 1: Analyzing  $ Ce^{4+} $ and  $ Tb^{4+} $.

 $$ Ce^{4+} \to Ce^{3+} $$ 

Since  $ Ce^{4+} $ gains an electron, it gets reduced.

Thus, it behaves as an oxidizing agent.

Similarly:

 $$ Tb^{4+} \to Tb^{3+} $$ 

It also gets reduced easily.

Hence  $ T b^{4+} $ also acts as an oxidizing agent.

Step 2: Checking other options.

Option (b)

 $$ La^{3+}and~Lu^{3+} $$ 

Both are already in their stable oxidation state.

Hence they do not act as strong oxidizing agents.

Option (c)

 $$ Eu^{2+}and Yb^{2+} $$ 

These ions tend to lose electrons:

 $$ Eu^{2+} \to Eu^{3+} $$ 

 $$ Yb^{2+} \to Yb^{3+} $$ 

Hence they act as reducing agents, not oxidizing agents.

Option (d)

 $$ Eu^{2+} $$ 

is reducing in nature while

 $$ Tb^{4+} $$ 

is oxidizing in nature.

Thus both are not oxidizing agents together.

Final Answer:

 $$ Ce^{4+} \text{and}Tb^{4+} $$ 

Quick Tip: In Lanthanoids:

+4  $ \rightarrow $ +3

means reduction, so the ion acts as an oxidizing agent.

+2  $ \rightarrow $ +3

means oxidation, so the ion acts as a reducing agent.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.004Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5229, 'chemistry-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q09', 9, 9, 'Assertion (A): Ionization enthalpy increases along each series of transition elements from left to right. However, small variations occur.

## Reason (R): There is a corresponding increase in nuclear charge which accompanies the filling of electrons in the inner d-orbitals.', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A) is correct but (R) is not correct"},{"id":"B","text":"(A) is not correct but (R) is correct"},{"id":"C","text":"Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"},{"id":"D","text":"Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: Ionization enthalpy is the minimum energy required to remove the most loosely bound electron from an isolated gaseous atom.

It mainly depends upon:

• Effective nuclear charge

• Atomic size

• Shielding effect

• Stability of electronic configuration

Step 1: Analyzing the Assertion.

Across the transition series, atomic number increases continuously.

As protons increase, nuclear charge also increases.

Electrons are added into inner d-orbitals, but d-electrons do not shield effectively.

Therefore outer electrons experience stronger attraction from nucleus.

Hence more energy is required to remove electrons.

Thus ionization enthalpy generally increases from left to right.

However, due to:

• half-filled stability

• fully-filled stability

• electron repulsion

small irregular variations occur.

Hence Assertion is true.

Step 2: Analyzing the Reason.

Reason states that nuclear charge increases while electrons are filled in inner d-orbitals. This statement is correct.

Increase in nuclear charge causes stronger attraction on valence electrons.

Hence ionization enthalpy increases.

Step 3: Checking the relationship between A and R.

The Reason directly explains why ionization enthalpy increases across transition series. Therefore:

Both A and R are true and R correctly explains A.

##### Final Answer:

Both A and R are true and R correctly explains A

Quick Tip: d-electrons shield poorly compared to s- and p-electrons. Hence effective nuclear charge increases significantly across transition series.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.005Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5230, 'chemistry-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q10', 10, 10, 'The incorrect statement among the following is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Lanthanoids are good conductors of heat and electricity."},{"id":"B","text":"Actinoids are highly reactive metals, especially when finely divided."},{"id":"C","text":"Actinoid contraction is greater for element-to-element than Lanthanoid contraction."},{"id":"D","text":"Most of the trivalent Lanthanoid ions are colourless in the solid state."}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept: Lanthanoids and Actinoids belong to the $f$-block elements and exhibit characteristic properties such as variable oxidation states, contraction, color, magnetic behavior, and high metallic character.

Step 1: Checking statement (a).

Lanthanoids are metallic elements.

They possess free electrons responsible for metallic conductivity.

Therefore they conduct:

• Heat

• Electricity

efficiently.

Hence statement (a) is correct.

Step 2: Checking statement (b).

Actinoids are highly electropositive metals.

Finely divided actinoids react rapidly because surface area increases significantly.

Hence statement (b) is correct.

Step 3: Checking statement (c).

Actinoid contraction is larger because:

• 5f-electrons shield very poorly

• effective nuclear charge increases strongly

Thus contraction becomes greater than lanthanoid contraction.

Hence statement (c) is correct.

Step 4: Checking statement (d).

Most trivalent lanthanoid ions are actually colored due to f-f electronic transitions.

Only ions having:

 $$ f^{0},f^{7},f^{14} $$ 

configurations are generally colorless.

Examples:

 $$ La^{3+},Gd^{3+},Lu^{3+} $$ 

Since most lanthanoid ions are colored, statement (d) is incorrect.

Final Answer:

Most of the trivalent Lanthanoid ions are colourless in the solid state

Quick Tip: Color in Lanthanoids arises due to $f-f$ transitions. Ions with completely empty, half-filled, or completely filled $f$-orbitals are generally colorless.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.005Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5231, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q02', 1, 1, 'Which of the following organic compounds is expected to have the highest boiling point?', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}CH_{2}OH $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}CHO $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}CH_{2}CH_{3} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ CH_{3}CH_{2}OCH_{2}CH_{3} $"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept: The boiling point of volatile organic molecules reflects the quantity of kinetic thermal energy needed to completely separate condensed molecules into the gas phase. This physical property is governed by the structural type and cumulative strength of intermolecular forces holding the liquid phase together. When comparing organic molecules with highly similar molecular weights, the relative hierarchy of boiling points follows the sequence of baseline intermolecular forces:

Intermolecular Hydrogen Bonding > Dipole-Dipole Interaction > London Dispersion Forces

Step 1: Identifying the active functional groups and matching their intermolecular forces. We analyze the structural features of each molecular choice:

• Butan-1-ol (CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH): Contains a strongly polarized hydroxyl (-OH) functional group. The presence of a hydrogen atom covalently attached to highly electronic-active oxygen permits extensive intermolecular hydrogen bonding.

• Butanal  $ (CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}CHO) $: Features a polar carbonyl group  $ (-C=O) $. This structure gives rise to fixed molecular dipoles that interact via dipole-dipole attractions, which are weaker than hydrogen bonds.

• Ethoxyethane (CH₃CH₂OCH₂CH₃): An asymmetrical ether containing a polar C-O-C framework. It exhibits mild dipole-dipole interactions but lacks any hydrogen atoms bonded to oxygen to support self-hydrogen bonding.

• Pentane ( $ CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}CH_{2}CH_{3} $): A completely nonpolar saturated hydrocarbon with a uniform electron cloud, operating strictly via temporary London dispersion forces.

Step 2: Evaluating force magnitudes to isolate the compound with the maximum boiling point. Intermolecular hydrogen bonds are significantly stronger than basic dipole-dipole attractions or weak dispersion forces. Because Butan-1-ol possesses the capability to form a network of hydrogen bonds between its molecules, it requires a much higher temperature to overcome these cohesive forces and transition into the gas phase. Therefore, Butan-1-ol has the highest boiling point among the given options.

Quick Tip: When molecular masses are similar, alcohols will almost always outboil aldehydes, ethers, and hydrocarbons due to extensive hydrogen bonding.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.006Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5232, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q03', 2, 2, 'Why do aquatic species, such as fish, generally feel more comfortable in cold water than in warm water?', '[{"id":"A","text":"The solubility of oxygen gas increases as the temperature of the water decreases."},{"id":"B","text":"The solubility of oxygen gas decreases as the temperature of the water decreases."},{"id":"C","text":"The Henry''s law constant $ K_{H} $ for oxygen decreases as temperature increases."},{"id":"D","text":"Warm water contains more dissolved toxic gases that harm aquatic metabolism."}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept: The process of dissolving gaseous solutes into liquid solvents represents a phase transition that achieves dynamic chemical equilibrium. When a gas dissolves, its individual molecules contract from a high-volume gas state into a restricted dissolved state, releasing kinetic energy. As a result, gas dissolution is universally classified as an exothermic physical process:

 $$  Gas(g)+Liquid~Solvent(l)\rightleftharpoons Dissolved~Solution(aq)+Heat\quad(\Delta H<0) $$ 

We use Le Chatelier''s principle to evaluate how thermal energetic changes force this equilibrium to shift.

## Step 1: Applying Le Chatelier''s principle to the exothermic dissolution equation under cold conditions.

Le Chatelier''s principle states that if a dynamic system at equilibrium experiences a temperature change, the position of the equilibrium shifts to counteract that change. When the ambient temperature of the aqueous system decreases (as seen in cold water), the system responds by attempting to generate thermal energy. To accomplish this, the equilibrium shifts toward the exothermic forward direction (to the right).

#### Step 2: Connecting the equilibrium shift to the breathing comfort of aquatic species.

The forward shift drives a larger mass of gaseous oxygen molecules to dissolve into the liquid phase. Consequently, cold water retains a substantially higher concentration of dissolved oxygen (DO) than warm water. Because fish and other aquatic species rely on extracting dissolved oxygen from water via their gills for respiration, the higher concentration of available oxygen makes cold water significantly more favorable and comfortable for their survival.

Quick Tip: Gas dissolution is exothermic ( $ \Delta H < 0 $). Therefore, gas solubility decreases with an increase in temperature.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.006Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5233, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q04', 3, 3, 'What are the starting materials used in the industrial synthesis of the pesticide DDT?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Chlorobenzene and Chloroform"},{"id":"B","text":"Chlorobenzene and Chloral"},{"id":"C","text":"Benzene and Chloral"},{"id":"D","text":"Chlorobenzene and Hexachloroethane"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: DDT, or dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane, is prepared through an acid-catalyzed condensation pathway that follows an electrophilic aromatic substitution mechanism ( $ S_{E}Ar $). The structural formula of DDT features two distinct halobenzene rings linked directly to a central chlorinated alkane backbone. To synthesize this framework, a highly reactive chlorinated carbonyl compound is required to serve as the structural core and source of the electrophile.

##### Step 1: Generating the electrophilic intermediate from the carbonyl precursor.

The synthesis乌尔巴尔的烃类键键合剂，如苯环、蒽醌等，这些化合物在反应中扮演重要角色。在反应过程中，这些化合物能够提供电子位和能量，从而促进反应的速率。例如，在反应中，反应物、产物、产物

 $$ (CCl_{3}CHO) $$ 

Step 2: Condensing the electrophile with the aromatic halide to finalize the DDT structure. The activated intermediate undergoes successive electrophilic attacks by two separate molecules of chlorobenzene. The positions para to the chlorine atoms on the chlorobenzene rings act as the nucleophilic sites attacking the central carbon. As the condensation reaction proceeds, a molecule of water is eliminated (H₂O), locking the two chlorobenzene groups and the trichloroethane fragment into a single molecule:

 $$ 2\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{Cl}+\mathrm{CCl}_{3}\mathrm{CHO}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{conc.}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}}\left(\mathrm{Cl}-\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}\right)_{2}\mathrm{CH}-\mathrm{CCl}_{3}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} $$ 

Thus, the industrial starting components are chlorobenzene and chloral.

Quick Tip: Remember the formula:  $ 2 \times \text{Chlorobenzene} + 1 \times \text{Chloral} \xrightarrow{\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4} \text{DDT} + \text{H}_2\text{O} $.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.006Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5234, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q05', 4, 4, 'Which of the following statements is TRUE regarding the transition state of a bimolecular nucleophilic substitution ( $ S_{N}2 $) reaction?', '[{"id":"A","text":"The central carbon atom transitions to an $ sp^{3} $ hybridization state with a tetrahedral geometry."},{"id":"B","text":"The central carbon atom adopts an unstable pentacoordinate arrangement with approximate $ sp^{2} $ hybridization."},{"id":"C","text":"The reaction forms a stable, isolation-ready carbocation intermediate."},{"id":"D","text":"Both the nucleophile and the leaving group carry full, non-interacting formal charges."}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: The  $ S_{N}2 $ pathway operates via a concerted, single-step mechanism. Unlike stepwise substitutions, it does not involve the formation of any distinct, low-energy intermediates such as carbocations. Instead, bond-making between the incoming nucleophile and the substrate occurs simultaneously with bond-breaking of the leaving group. This coordinated process passes through an unstable, high-energy transition state configuration.

Step 1: Analyzing the synchronous geometry and bond configuration of the reaction center. During an  $ S_N $2 attack, the nucleophile approaches the substrate from the side directly opposite the leaving group (180° back-side attack) to minimize electrostatic repulsion. At the peak of the transition state, the bond to the incoming nucleophile ( $ Nu^{\delta-} $) is partially formed, while the bond to the departing leaving group ( $ LG^{\delta-} $) is partially broken. This arrangement forces the central carbon atom to coordinate temporarily with five separate groups at the same time.

##### Step 2: Determining the transient hybridization state and electronic geometry.

Because the partial bonds to the nucleophile and leaving group lie along a single linear axis, the central carbon uses its remaining unhybridized p-orbital to interact with them. The other three stable, non-reacting substituent bonds are pushed away and flatten out into a single perpendicular plane. This planar arrangement uses three  $ sp^{2} $ hybrid orbitals separated by  $ 120^{\circ} $ angles. This creates an unstable pentacoordinate carbon center with a trigonal bipyramidal geometry in the transition state.

Quick Tip: The  $ S_{N}2 $ mechanism involves a single concerted transition state (pentacoordinate carbon), while  $ S_{N}1 $ proceeds via a distinct carbocation intermediate.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.007Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5235, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q06', 5, 5, 'Ethylene glycol (ethane-1,2-diol) is widely added to car radiators as an antifreeze agent. How does its addition protect the engine in extreme climates?', '[{"id":"A","text":"It raises the freezing point of water and lowers its boiling point."},{"id":"B","text":"It lowers the freezing point of water and raises its boiling point."},{"id":"C","text":"It lowers both the freezing point and the boiling point of water equally."},{"id":"D","text":"It reacts chemically with the metal radiator walls to reduce thermal expansion."}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: When a non-volatile solute is dissolved into a volatile liquid solvent, it changes the physical behavior of the solution. The solute particles occupy space at the liquid surface, decreasing the rate of evaporation and lowering the overall vapor pressure of the solvent. This shift in vapor pressure leads directly to changes in two key colligative properties:

 $$ \Delta T_{f}=K_{f}\cdot m\quad(Depression~of~Freezing~Point) $$ 

 $$ \Delta T_{b}=K_{b}\cdot m\quad(Elevation~of~Boiling~Point) $$ 

##### Step 1: Evaluating the physical mechanism behind freezing point depression.

In pure water, molecules readily assemble into a rigid, structured crystalline lattice at  $ 0^{\circ} $C. When ethylene glycol (HO-CH $ _{2} $-CH $ _{2} $-OH) is added, its molecules form hydrogen bonds with water, disrupting the regular intermolecular organization of the solvent. To overcome this disruption and force crystallization, thermal energy must be removed from the system. This drops the freezing point significantly below  $ 0^{\circ} $C ( $ \Delta T_{f} $), preventing the coolant from freezing and cracking the radiator block in cold climates.

#### Step 2: Evaluating the physical mechanism behind boiling point elevation.

Simultaneously, because the non-volatile ethylene glycol solute lowers the solution’s vapor pressure, a higher temperature is required for the vapor pressure to equal the external atmospheric pressure. This elevates the boiling point of the radiator fluid significantly above  $ 100^{\circ} $C ( $ \Delta T_{b} $). Together, these two property shifts widen the liquid temperature range of the coolant, protecting the engine from both freezing in winter and boiling over in summer.

Quick Tip: Solute addition expands the liquid temperature range of a solvent by pushing  $ T_{f} $ down and pulling  $ T_{b} $ up.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.007Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5236, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q07', 6, 6, 'Which of the following oxidizing agents is mild enough to selectively oxidize an aldehyde to a carboxylic acid without cleaving other sensitive carbon-carbon single bonds?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Tollens'' reagent"},{"id":"B","text":"Concentrated $ H_{2}SO_{4} $ at high temperatures"},{"id":"C","text":"Ozone followed by Zn / H $ _{2} $O"},{"id":"D","text":"LiAlH_{4} in dry ether"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept: Aldehydes (R-CHO) are easily oxidized because they possess a reactive hydrogen atom directly bonded to the carbonyl carbon. This structural feature allows them to undergo oxidation under mild conditions. In contrast, ketones (R-CO-R'') lack this specific carbonyl hydrogen atom, meaning their oxidation requires the cleavage of strong carbon-carbon single bonds under harsh conditions. Consequently, mild laboratory oxidizing agents can selectively oxidize aldehydes while leaving ketones and other functional groups untouched.

##### Step 1: Analyzing the chemical behavior of Tollens'' reagent as a selective oxidant.

Tollens’ reagent is an aqueous alkaline solution containing the diamminesilver(I) coordination complex, formulated as  $ [\mathrm{Ag}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}]^{+} $. The silver ions  $ (\mathrm{Ag}^{+}) $ in this complex serve as a gentle, mild electron acceptor. When mixed with an aldehyde, Tollens’ reagent selectively targets and oxidizes the carbonyl carbon, transforming the aldehyde group into a carboxylate anion  $ (\mathrm{R}-\mathrm{COO}^{-}) $:

 $$ \mathrm{R-CHO+2[Ag(NH_{3})_{2}]^{+}+3OH^{-}\rightarrow R-COO^{-}+2Ag(s)\downarrow+4NH_{3}+2H_{2}O} $$ 

Step 2: Ruling out alternative aggressive or reducing reagents.

We evaluate the alternative choices to confirm selectivity:

- Hot concentrated  $ H_{2}SO_{4} $ acts as a powerful, non-selective oxidizing and dehydrating agent that causes widespread decomposition.

- Ozone ( $ O_{3} $) followed by a zinc workup is used to cleave carbon-carbon double bonds in alkenes rather than selectively oxidizing terminal carbonyls.

- LiAlH $ _{4} $ is a strong reducing agent that performs the opposite transformation, converting carbonyls back into primary alcohols.

Thus, Tollens'' reagent is the ideal mild oxidant for this selective transformation.

Quick Tip: Mild oxidants like Tollens'' or Fehling''s reagents can oxidize aldehydes, but they are not strong enough to oxidize ketones.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.007Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5237, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q08', 7, 7, 'What are the primary reagents and catalysts required to carry out the Gattermann-Koch reaction?', '[{"id":"A","text":"CO, HCl, and anhydrous AlCl_{3} / CuCl"},{"id":"B","text":"CHCl_{3} and aqueous NaOH"},{"id":"C","text":"HCN, HCl, and anhydrous AlCl_{3}"},{"id":"D","text":"CH_{3}Cl and anhydrous AlCl_{3}"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept: The Gattermann-Koch reaction is a classic organic named reaction used to introduce a formyl functional group (−CHO) directly onto an aromatic ring. This transformation converts benzene or activated benzene derivatives into aromatic aldehydes. Mechanistically, it operates as a variation of a Friedel-Crafts acylation, requiring a gaseous source to generate a highly reactive, short-lived formyl cation intermediate ( $ [HC=O]^{+} $).

Formyl chloride (HCOCl) is highly unstable and decomposes rapidly at room temperature, meaning it cannot be stored or added directly to a reaction flask. To circumvent this, the Gattermann-Koch reaction generates the necessary electrophilic species in situ. A gaseous mixture of carbon monoxide (CO) and hydrogen chloride (HCl) is bubbled into the reaction mixture. In the presence of a strong Lewis acid catalyst like anhydrous aluminum chloride (AlCl₃), these components interact to form the transient formyl electrophile.

Because carbon monoxide reactions can be slow under standard conditions, cuprous chloride (CuCl) is added as a vital co-catalyst. The copper(I) center binds temporarily to carbon monoxide, increasing its local concentration and facilitating its reaction with HCl and AlCl_{3}. The resulting formyl cation undergoes an electrophilic aromatic substitution attack on the

benzene ring, yielding benzaldehyde:

 $$ \mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{6}+\mathrm{CO}+\mathrm{HCl}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{anhydrous~AlCl}_{3}/\mathrm{CuCl}}\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{CHO}+\mathrm{HCl} $$ 

This confirms option (A) as the correct reagent combination.

Quick Tip: Distinction: Gattermann-Koch uses CO+HCl, whereas the standard Gattermann formylation utilizes HCN + HCl.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.007Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5238, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q09', 8, 8, 'Among the given pairs of isomeric organic compounds, which pair is correctly arranged in order of INCREASING boiling point?', '[{"id":"A","text":"n-Butyl alcohol < t-Butyl alcohol"},{"id":"B","text":"t-Butyl chloride < n-Butyl chloride"},{"id":"C","text":"p-Dichlorobenzene < o-Dichlorobenzene"},{"id":"D","text":"Isopentane < Neopentane"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: For structural isomers sharing identical molecular formulas and molecular weights, variations in boiling point are governed by molecular shape and branching. Nonpolar or weakly polar covalent molecules rely on temporary London dispersion forces, which depend directly on the total accessible molecular surface area. The relationship between molecular structure and boiling point follows a reliable trend:

Increased Branching $\Rightarrow$ Spherical Shape $\Rightarrow$ Smaller Surface Area $\Rightarrow$ Weaker Dispersion Forces $\Rightarrow$ Lower Bo

Step 1: Analyzing the structural differences between butyl chloride isomers.

We compare the straight-chain and branched isomers of butyl chloride:

- t-Butyl chloride ((CH_{3})_{3}C-Cl): Features a highly branched, compact, and nearly spherical geometry. This spherical shape minimizes the outer surface area available for contact with neighboring molecules.

• n-Butyl chloride (CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}CH_{2}Cl): Possesses a fully extended, linear, straight-chain hydrocarbon conformation that provides a significantly larger molecular surface area.

#### Step 2: Correlating surface area to the final boiling point inequality.

The larger surface area of linear n-butyl chloride allows neighboring molecules to align closely, maximizing the strength of intermolecular London dispersion forces. Conversely, the compact shape of t-butyl chloride limits molecular contact, weakening these temporary attractions. Consequently, t-butyl chloride boils at a significantly lower temperature (324 K) than n-butyl chloride (351 K). This confirms that the arrangement t-butyl chloride < n-butyl chloride correctly demonstrates a trend of increasing boiling point.

Quick Tip: As molecular branching increases  $ \Rightarrow $ Surface area decreases  $ \Rightarrow $ Van der Waals forces weaken  $ \Rightarrow $ Boiling point drops.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.008Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5239, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q10', 9, 9, 'When benzaldehyde is subjected to oxidation using acidified potassium permanganate ( $ KMnO_{4} $), what organic product is formed?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Benzyl alcohol"},{"id":"B","text":"Benzoic acid"},{"id":"C","text":"Benzene"},{"id":"D","text":"Acetophenone"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: Aromatic aldehydes are highly susceptible to oxidation due to the presence of the single carbon-hydrogen bond on the formyl carbonyl group (−CHO). When treated with a powerful transition metal oxidant, this carbonyl C-H bond is cleaved and converted into a carbon-hydroxyl (C-OH) bond. This process transforms the aldehyde functional group into a stable carboxylic acid derivative.

Step 1: Evaluating the chemical behavior of the strong oxidant with benzaldehyde.

Potassium permanganate (KMnO₄) under acidified conditions (H₂SO₄) serves as an exceptionally strong, non-selective oxidizing agent. The manganese center in the permanganate anion

(MnO_{4}^{-}) is in its highest oxidation state (+7) and acts as an efficient electron acceptor. When introduced to benzaldehyde (C_{6}H_{5}CHO), the reagent readily attacks the formyl carbon atom, oxidizing the functional group to form a carboxylic acid.

##### Step 2: Determining the final structure of the aromatic product.

The oxidation converts the formyl group (-CHO) directly into a carboxylic acid group (-COOH) attached to the benzene ring. The aromatic core remains completely intact throughout this transformation, as the benzene ring is stabilized by resonance and resists oxidative cleavage. The resulting organic product is benzoic acid ( $ C_{6}H_{5}COOH $):

 $$  C_{6}H_{5}CHO\xrightarrow{Acidified~KMnO_{4}}C_{6}H_{5}COOH $$ 

This confirms option (B) as the correct choice.

Quick Tip: Whether using mild (Tollens'') or strong (KMnO_{4}) oxidizing agents, aldehydes consistently convert into their corresponding carboxylic acids.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.008Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5240, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q01', 1, 1, 'For the reaction

 $$ 2\boldsymbol{A}+\boldsymbol{B}\rightarrow3\boldsymbol{C}+\boldsymbol{D} $$ 

Which of the following does not express the reaction rate?', '[{"id":"A","text":"$$ -\\frac{d[B]}{dt} $$"},{"id":"B","text":"$$ \\frac{d[D]}{dt} $$"},{"id":"C","text":"$$ -\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{d[A]}{dt} $$"},{"id":"D","text":"$$ \\frac{1}{3}\\frac{d[C]}{dt} $$"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Write the rate expression.

For the reaction:

 $$ 2\boldsymbol{A}+\boldsymbol{B}\rightarrow3\boldsymbol{C}+\boldsymbol{D} $$ 

Rate of reaction is:

 $$ \mathrm{Rate}=-\frac{1}{2}\frac{d[A]}{dt}=-\frac{d[B]}{dt}=\frac{1}{3}\frac{d[C]}{dt}=\frac{d[D]}{dt} $$ 

Step 2: Compare with given options.

Option (2):

 $$ \frac{d[D]}{dt} $$ 

is correct.

Option (3):

 $$ -\frac{1}{2}\frac{d[A]}{dt} $$ 

is correct.

Option (4):

 $$ \frac{1}{3}\frac{d[C]}{dt} $$ 

is correct.

Option (1):

 $$ -\frac{d[B]}{dt} $$ 

 $$ -\frac{1}{1}\frac{d[B]}{dt} $$ 

should actually be:

Thus it is not properly normalized according to stoichiometric coefficient form.

Step 3: Identify the incorrect expression.

Therefore:

 $$ \mathrm{(A)}-\frac{\mathrm{d}[\mathrm{B}]}{\mathrm{d t}} $$ 

Quick Tip: For a reaction:

 $$ aA+bB\to cC+dD $$ 

Reaction rate:

 $$ -\frac{1}{a}\frac{d[A]}{dt}=-\frac{1}{b}\frac{d[B]}{dt}=\frac{1}{c}\frac{d[C]}{dt}=\frac{1}{d}\frac{d[D]}{dt} $$', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.009Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5241, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q02', 2, 2, 'The oxidation potentials of A and B are

 $$ +2.37\mathrm{~V}\quad\mathrm{a n d}\quad+1.66\mathrm{~V} $$ 

respectively. In chemical reactions', '[{"id":"A","text":"A will be replaced by B"},{"id":"B","text":"A will replace B"},{"id":"C","text":"A will not replace B"},{"id":"D","text":"A and B will not replace each other"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understand oxidation potential.

Greater oxidation potential means greater tendency to:

lose electrons

Hence stronger reducing agent.

Given:

 $$ E_{\mathrm{o x}}^{\circ}(A)=+2.37\mathrm{V} $$ 

 $$ E_{ox}^{\circ}(B)=+1.66V $$ 

Thus:

has greater tendency to oxidize than:

Step 2: Determine displacement tendency.

A stronger reducing agent displaces a weaker reducing agent from its salt solution.

Since:

 $$ A>B $$ 

in oxidation tendency,

will replace:

Step 3: Identify the correct option.

Therefore:

(B) A will replace B

Quick Tip: Higher oxidation potential:

greater tendency to lose electrons

stronger reducing agent.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.009Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5242, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q03', 3, 3, 'For which value of x and y, the following square planar compound shows geometrical isomers

 $$ [\mathrm{P t}(\mathrm{C l})_{\mathrm{x}}(\mathrm{B r})_{\mathrm{y}}]^{2-} $$', '[{"id":"A","text":"1,3"},{"id":"B","text":"3,1"},{"id":"C","text":"2,2"},{"id":"D","text":"1,1"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Recall geometrical isomerism in square planar complexes.

Square planar complexes show geometrical isomerism when:

two pairs of identical ligands are present

General form:

 $$ [\mathrm{M A}_{2}\mathrm{B}_{2}] $$ 

shows:

cis and trans isomers

Step 2: Analyze the given complex.

For:

 $$ [\mathrm{P t}(\mathrm{C l})_{\mathrm{x}}(\mathrm{B r})_{\mathrm{y}}]^{2-} $$ 

geometrical isomerism is possible only when:

 $$ x=2,\quad 囍 y=2 $$ 

Thus complex becomes:

 $$ [{\mathrm{P t C l}}_{2}{\mathrm{B r}}_{2}]^{2-} $$ 

which shows:

cis-trans isomerism

Step 3: Identify the correct option.

Therefore:

 $$ \mathrm{(C)}2,2 $$ 

Quick Tip: Square planar complexes of type:

 $$ [MA_{2}B_{2}] $$ 

show:

cis-trans geometrical isomerism', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.009Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5243, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Match the columns




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td colspan="2">Column-I</td><td colspan="2">Column-II</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A)</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>$ CH_{2}=CH-CH_{2}Cl $</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(p)</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>gem-Dichloride</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B)</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>$ CH_{2}=CHX $</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(q)</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Vinylic halide</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C)</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>$ CH_{3}CHCl_{2} $</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(r)</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>vic-Dichloride</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D)</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>$ CH_{2}ClCH_{2}Cl $</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(s)</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Allylic halide</td></tr></table>', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ A \\to (r) $, $ B \\to (q) $, $ C \\to (p) $, $ D \\to (s) $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ A \\to (q) $, $ B \\to (p) $, $ C \\to (s) $, $ D \\to (r) $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ A \\to (s) $, $ B \\to (q) $, $ C \\to (p) $, $ D \\to (r) $"},{"id":"D","text":"$$ A\\to(r),B\\to(p),C\\to(s),D\\to(q) $$"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Identify compound (A).

 $$  CH_{2}=CH-CH_{2}Cl $$ 

contains halogen adjacent to double bond.

Hence it is:

allylic halide

Thus:

 $$ A\to(s) $$ 

Step 2: Identify compound (B).

 $$  CH_{2}=CHX $$ 

has halogen directly attached to double bonded carbon.

Hence:

vinylic halide

Thus:

 $$ B\to(q) $$ 

Step 3: Identify compound (C).

 $$  CH_{3}CHCl_{2} $$ 

has both chlorine atoms attached to the same carbon.

Hence:

gem-dichloride

Thus:

 $$ C\to(p) $$ 

Step 4: Identify compound (D).

 $$ \mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl} $$ 

has chlorine atoms attached to adjacent carbons.

Hence:

vic-dichloride

Thus:

 $$ D\to(r) $$ 

Step 5: Final matching.

 $$ A\to(s),\qquad B\to(q),\qquad C\to(p),\qquad D\to(r) $$ 

Therefore:

 $$ \left|\left(\mathrm{C}\right)\mathrm{A}\rightarrow(\mathrm{s}),\mathrm{B}\rightarrow(\mathrm{q}),\mathrm{C}\rightarrow(\mathrm{p}),\mathrm{D}\rightarrow(\mathrm{r})\right. $$ 

Quick Tip: gem-dihalide:

Both halogens on same carbon

vic-dihalide:

Halogens on adjacent carbons', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.010Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5244, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q05', 5, 5, 'To prepare 3-ethylpentan-3-ol, the reagents needed are', '[{"id":"A","text":"$$ \\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\mathrm{MgBr}+\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\mathrm{COCH}_{2}\\mathrm{CH}_{3} $$"},{"id":"B","text":"$ CH_{3}MgBr + CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}COCH_{2}CH_{3} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ CH_{3}CH_{2}MgBr + CH_{3}CH_{2}COCH_{2}CH_{3} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}MgBr + CH_{3}COCH_{2}CH_{3} $"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Identify the target alcohol.

Given alcohol:

3-ethylpentan-3-ol

Structure:

 $$ \mathrm{CH_{3}CH_{2}-C(OH)(CH_{2}CH_{3})-CH_{2}CH_{3}} $$ 

The carbon containing:

OH

is attached to:

3 ethyl groups

Hence it is a:

tertiary alcohol

Step 2: Recall Grignard reaction.

A ketone reacts with Grignard reagent:

RMgX

to form a tertiary alcohol.

To obtain:

3-ethylpentan-3-ol

we require:

 $$  CH_{3}CH_{2}COCH_{2}CH_{3} $$ 

which is:

pentan-3-one

and:

 $$ \mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{MgBr} $$ 

Step 3: Write the reaction.

 $$ \mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{COCH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{3}+\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{MgBr} $$ 

followed by hydrolysis gives:

 $$  (CH_{3}CH_{2})_{3}COH $$ 

which is:

3-ethylpentan-3-ol

Step 4: Identify the correct option.

Therefore:

 $$  (C)CH_{3}CH_{2}MgBr+CH_{3}CH_{2}COCH_{2}CH_{3} $$ 

Quick Tip: Ketone + Grignard reagent:

tertiary alcohol after hydrolysis.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.010Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5245, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q06', 6, 6, 'Chromosomes are made from', '[{"id":"A","text":"proteins"},{"id":"B","text":"nucleic acids"},{"id":"C","text":"proteins and nucleic acids"},{"id":"D","text":"carbohydrates and nucleic acids"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Recall the composition of chromosomes.

Chromosomes are mainly composed of:

DNA and proteins

DNA is a:

nucleic acid

Proteins present are mainly:

histone proteins

Step 2: Identify the correct option.

Therefore chromosomes are made of:

proteins and nucleic acids

Hence:

(C) proteins and nucleic acids

Quick Tip: Chromosome:

DNA + Histone proteins', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.010Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5246, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q07', 7, 7, 'The electronic configuration of Cu(II) is

 $$ 3d^{9} $$ 

whereas that of Cu(I) is

 $$ 3d^{10} $$ 

Which of the following is correct?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Cu(II) is more stable"},{"id":"B","text":"Cu(I) is less stable"},{"id":"C","text":"Cu(I) and Cu(II) are equally stable"},{"id":"D","text":"Stability of Cu(I) and Cu(II) depends on nature of copper salts"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Compare the electronic configurations.

 $$ \mathrm{C u(I):3d^{10}} $$ 

 $$ \mathrm{C u(I I):3d^{9}} $$ 

Configuration:

 $$ 3d^{10} $$ 

is completely filled and generally stable.

Step 2: Consider practical stability.

Although:

Cu(I)

has stable electronic configuration, its actual stability depends on:

the ligands and nature of the salt

Some compounds stabilize:

while others stabilize:

Cu(II)

Thus stability depends on surrounding chemical environment.

Step 3: Identify the correct option.

Therefore:

(D) Stability of Cu(I) and Cu(II) depends on nature of copper salts

Quick Tip: Stability of oxidation states in transition metals depends on:

ligands, hydration energy and lattice energy', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.010Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5247, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q08', 8, 8, 'On addition of small amount of  $ KMnO_{4} $ to concentrated  $ H_{2}SO_{4} $, a green oily compound is obtained which is highly explosive in nature. Identify the compound from the following.', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ Mn_{2}O_{7} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ MnO_{2} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ MnSO_{4} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ Mn_{2}O_{3} $"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Recall the reaction of  $ KMnO_{4} $ with concentrated  $ H_{2}SO_{4} $.

When:

 $$ KMnO_{4} $$ 

reacts with concentrated:

 $$ H_{2}SO_{4} $$ 

a dark green oily liquid is formed:

 $$  Mn_{2}O_{7} $$ 

Reaction:

 $$ 2KMnO_{4}+2H_{2}SO_{4}\rightarrow K_{2}SO_{4}+2HMnO_{4} $$ 

 $$ 2HMnO_{4}\rightarrow Mn_{2}O_{7}+H_{2}O $$ 

Step 2: Identify the explosive compound.

 $$  Mn_{2}O_{7} $$ 

is highly unstable and explosive.

Thus the green oily liquid obtained is:

 $$  Mn_{2}O_{7} $$ 

Step 3: Identify the correct option.

Therefore:

 $$ \mathrm{(A)}\mathrm{M n}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7} $$ 

Quick Tip:

 $$  Mn_{2}O_{7} $$ 

is manganese heptoxide:

a dark green explosive liquid', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.010Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5248, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q09', 9, 9, 'Which of the following oxidation state is common for all lanthanoids?', '[{"id":"A","text":"+2"},{"id":"B","text":"+3"},{"id":"C","text":"+4"},{"id":"D","text":"+5"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Recall the common oxidation state of lanthanoids.

Lanthanoids generally lose:

2 electrons from 6s

and:

1 electron from 5d or 4f

Thus the most stable and common oxidation state is:

+3

Step 2: Analyze other oxidation states.

Some lanthanoids may also show:

+2 or +4

but these are not common to all lanthanoids.

Step 3: Identify the correct option.

Therefore:

 $$ \left|\mathrm{~(B)~+3~}\right| $$ 

Quick Tip: Most common oxidation state of lanthanoids:

due to stable removal of:

 $$ 6s^{2} $$ 

and one additional electron.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.011Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5249, 'chemistry-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q10', 10, 10, 'The magnetic nature of elements depends on the presence of unpaired electrons. Identify the configuration of transition element, which shows highest magnetic moment.', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ 3d^{7} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ 3d^{5} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ 3d^{8} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ 3d^{2} $"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Recall the formula for magnetic moment.

Magnetic moment depends on number of unpaired electrons:

 $$ \mu=\sqrt{n(n+2)}BM $$ 

where:

n = number of unpaired electrons

Step 2: Find unpaired electrons in each configuration.

 $ 3d^{7} \Rightarrow 3 $ unpaired electrons

 $ 3d^{5} \Rightarrow 5 $ unpaired electrons

 $$ 3d^{8}\Rightarrow2{\mathrm{~u n p a i r e d~e l e c t r o n s}} $$ 

###  $ 3d^{2} \Rightarrow 2 $ unpaired electrons

Step 3: Identify highest magnetic moment.

Maximum magnetic moment corresponds to maximum number of unpaired electrons.

Thus:

 $$ 3d^{5} $$ 

has highest magnetic moment.

Step 4: Identify the correct option.

Therefore:

 $$ (B)\;3d^{5} $$ 

Quick Tip: Greater the number of unpaired electrons:

greater the magnetic moment.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.011Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5250, 'chemistry-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'For a first-order reaction, if the time taken for 90% completion is t, what will be the approximate time taken for 99.9% completion of the same reaction?', '[{"id":"A","text":"2t"},{"id":"B","text":"3t"},{"id":"C","text":"4t"},{"id":"D","text":"1.5t"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: For a first-order kinetic process, the integrated rate equation is defined as:

 $$ k=\frac{2.303}{t}\log\left(\frac{\left[A\right]_{0}}{\left[A\right]_{t}}\right) $$ 

Where  $ [A]_{0} $ is the initial concentration and  $ [A]_{t} $ is the remaining reactant concentration at time t.

Step 1: Expressing time for 90% completion ( $ t_{90\%} $).

When the reaction is 90% complete, the remaining concentration is  $ [A]_t = [A]_0 - 0.90[A]_0 = 0.10[A]_0 $.

 $$ t=\frac{2.303}{k}\log\left(\frac{\left[A\right]_{0}}{0.10[A]_{0}}\right)=\frac{2.303}{k}\log(10)=\frac{2.303}{k}\quad\cdots(1) $$ 

Step 2: Expressing time for 99.9% completion ( $ t_{99.9\%} $).

When the reaction is 99.9% complete, the remaining concentration is  $ [A]_{t} = [A]_{0} - 0.999[A]_{0} = 0.001[A]_{0} = 10^{-3}[A]_{0} $.

 $$ t_{99.9\%}=\frac{2.303}{k}\log\left(\frac{[A]_{0}}{10^{-3}[A]_{0}}\right)=\frac{2.303}{k}\log(10^{3})=3\times\frac{2.303}{k}\quad\cdots(2) $$ 

Substituting equation (1) into equation (2) directly yields  $ t_{99.9\%}=3t $.

Quick Tip: For first-order kinetics, logarithmic ratios simplify to highly structured scaling constants:  $ t_{99.9\%} = 3 \times t_{90\%} $ and  $ t_{99\%} = 2 \times t_{90\%} $.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.012Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5251, 'chemistry-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'According to collision theory, increasing the starting concentration of a collection of reacting molecules directly results in a change in which of the following system factors?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Activation energy"},{"id":"B","text":"Collision frequency"},{"id":"C","text":"Rate constant"},{"id":"D","text":"Fraction of molecules with energy greater than activation energy"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: Collision theory models reaction rates based on effective interactions between molecules per unit volume per second, mathematically formulated as:

 $$  Rate=Z_{AB}\cdot e^{-\frac{E_{a}}{RT}} $$ 

Where  $ Z_{AB} $ is the collision frequency factor.

Step 1: Analyzing the effect of crowded molecular environments.

Increasing reactant concentration places more molecules inside the same spatial boundaries. This physical crowding directly maximizes structural encounters, leading to a major increase in the collision frequency ( $ Z_{AB} $). Factors like activation energy ( $ E_{a} $) and the energetic fraction depend exclusively on temperature and catalysts.

Quick Tip: Concentration adjustments physically manipulate the collision frequency (Z). Changing the temperature adjusts both the rate constant (k) and the fraction of high-energy molecules.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.013Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5252, 'chemistry-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q03', 3, 3, 'What is the cell potential ( $ E_{cell} $) for a concentration cell consisting of two hydrogen electrodes at 298 K, where the anode compartment is at pH = 3 and the cathode compartment is at pH = 1 under standard pressure conditions?', '[{"id":"A","text":"0.0591 V"},{"id":"B","text":"0.1182 V"},{"id":"C","text":"-0.1182 V"},{"id":"D","text":"0.0000 V"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: For identical chemical electrodes forming a concentration cell,  $ E_{\text{cell}}^{\circ} = 0 \, \text{V} $. The overall potential is generated by concentration gradients according to the Nernst Equation:

 $$ E_{\mathrm{c e l l}}=-\frac{0.0591}{n}\log\left(\frac{[\mathrm{H^{+}}]_{anode}}{[\mathrm{H^{+}}]_{cathode}}\right) $$ 

Step 1: Convert pH indices to active ion molarity.

Using the standard relationship  $ \mathrm{pH} = -\log[\mathrm{H}^{+}] $:

 $$ [\mathrm{H}^{+}]_{\mathrm{a n o d e}}=10^{-3}\:\mathrm{M},\quad[\mathrm{H}^{+}]_{\mathrm{c a t h o d e}}=10^{-1}\:\mathrm{M} $$ 

Step 2: Calculate potential with single-electron transfer (n = 1).

 $$ E_{\mathrm{c e l l}}=-\frac{0.0591}{1}\log\left(\frac{10^{-3}}{10^{-1}}\right)=-0.0591\log(10^{-2}) $$ 

 $$ E_{cell}=-0.0591\times(-2)=0.1182V $$ 

Quick Tip: For symmetrical hydrogen ion concentration cells at 298 K, you can use this simple shortcut:  $ E_{\text{cell}} = 0.0591 \times (\text{pH}_{\text{anode}} - \text{pH}_{\text{cathode}}) $.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.014Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5253, 'chemistry-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Which of the following expressions correctly relates the limiting molar conductivity ( $ \Lambda_{m}^{\circ} $) of aluminum sulfate,  $ \mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{3} $, to its individual ionic components according to Kohlerausch''s Law?', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ \\Lambda_{m}^{\\circ}=\\lambda^{\\circ}(\\mathrm{Al}^{3+})+\\lambda^{\\circ}(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}) $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ \\Lambda_{m}^{\\circ}=2\\lambda^{\\circ}(\\mathrm{Al}^{3+})+3\\lambda^{\\circ}(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}) $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ \\Lambda_{m}^{\\circ}=3\\lambda^{\\circ}(\\mathrm{Al}^{3+})+2\\lambda^{\\circ}(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}) $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ \\Lambda_{m}^{\\circ}=\\frac{1}{2}\\lambda^{\\circ}(\\mathrm{Al}^{3+})+\\frac{1}{3}\\lambda^{\\circ}(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}) $"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: Kohlrausch''s law of independent migration of ions states that the total limiting molar conductivity of an electrolyte equals the sum of the individual contributions from its cations and anions:

 $$ \Lambda_{m}^{\circ}=\nu_{+}\lambda_{+}^{\circ}+\nu_{-}\lambda_{-}^{\circ} $$ 

Where  $ v_{+} $ and  $ v_{-} $ denote the stoichiometric coefficients of the positive and negative ions.

Step 1: Analyze the complete dissociation process.

One formula unit of aluminum sulfate dissociates completely in water according to this equilibrium:

 $$ \mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{3}(s)\xrightarrow{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}2\mathrm{Al}^{3+}(aq)+3\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}(aq) $$ 

Here,  $ \nu_{+}=2 $ and  $ \nu_{-}=3 $. Substituting these values yields:

 $$ \Lambda_{m}^{\circ}=2\lambda^{\circ}(\mathrm{A l}^{3+})+3\lambda^{\circ}(\mathrm{S O}_{4}^{2-}) $$ 

Quick Tip: Always scale the individual ionic values by the balance coefficients for molar conductivity. For equivalent conductivity, these stoichiometric coefficients are normalized.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.014Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5254, 'chemistry-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q05', 5, 5, 'What is the correct increasing order of basic strength for the following amines when measured inside an aqueous medium?

(I)  $  \text{CH}_3\text{NH}_2  $, (II)  $  (\text{CH}_3)_2\text{NH}  $, (III)  $  (\text{CH}_3)_3\text{N}  $, (IV)  $  \text{NH}_3  $', '[{"id":"A","text":"IV < III < I < II"},{"id":"B","text":"IV < I < II < III"},{"id":"C","text":"III < I < II < IV"},{"id":"D","text":"I < II < III < IV"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept: In an aqueous medium, the basicity of aliphatic amines depends on three competing structural factors: inductive electron donation (+I), steric hindrance, and hydration stabilization of the resulting conjugate cation via hydrogen bonding.

Step 1: Evaluate the combined trends for methyl-substituted amines.

• Inductive effect trend: Secondary > Tertiary > Primary  $ (2^{\circ} > 3^{\circ} > 1^{\circ}) $.

• Hydration energy trend: Primary > Secondary > Tertiary  $ (1^{\circ} > 2^{\circ} > 3^{\circ}) $.

Combining these factors creates an energy balance, giving an absolute basicity sequence of  $ 2^\circ > 1^\circ > 3^\circ > NH_3 $. Arranging these from lowest to highest yields:  $ NH_3 < (CH_3)_3N < CH_3NH_2 < (CH_3)_2NH $. This matches sequence IV < III < I < II.

Quick Tip: Remember these structural trends for aqueous amines to save time: For Methyl groups, use sequence  $ 213 (2^\circ > 1^\circ > 3^\circ) $. For Ethyl groups, use sequence  $ 231 (2^\circ > 3^\circ > 1^\circ) $.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.014Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5255, 'chemistry-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q06', 6, 6, 'An organic compound with the molecular formula  $ C_{7}H_{8}O $ is completely insoluble in water but dissolves readily in an aqueous NaOH solution. When treated with bromine water, it forms a white precipitate. Identify the compound.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Benzyl alcohol"},{"id":"B","text":"Anisole"},{"id":"C","text":"o-Cresol"},{"id":"D","text":"Methoxybenzene"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: We can determine structural properties based on characteristic solubility trends:

- Phenols are acidic enough to react with strong bases like NaOH, forming water-soluble sodium phenoxide salts. Alcohols and ethers do not react.

- The strongly activating hydroxyl group on the ring drives extremely rapid electrophilic substitution reactions.

### Step 1: Narrow down functional classes based on basic solubility.

The formula  $ C_7H_8O $ matches structural isomers like benzyl alcohol, anisole, and cresols. Because this compound reacts and dissolves in NaOH, it must be a phenol variant (o-cresol).

Step 2: Verify electrophilic addition behavior.

Treating o-cresol with bromine water causes rapid ring bromination at available ortho and para positions, generating a characteristic halogenated white precipitate.

Quick Tip: Organic molecules that dissolve cleanly in aqueous NaOH but fail to react or evolve gas in weaker NaHCO₃ contain a phenolic hydroxyl group.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.015Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5256, 'chemistry-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q07', 7, 7, 'Which of the following coordination complex ions exhibits structural asymmetry leading to optical isomerism?', '[{"id":"A","text":"trans- $ [\\mathrm{Co(en)_{2}Cl_{2}}] $^{+}"},{"id":"B","text":"cis-[Co(en) $ _{2} $Cl $ _{2} $] $ ^{+} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ [\\mathrm{Co}(\\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{4}\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}]^{+} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ [Cr(NH_{3})_{5}Cl]^{2+} $"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: A coordination complex displays optical activity if it forms non-superimposable mirror images. This structural condition requires the compound to lack an internal plane of symmetry ( $ \sigma $) and a center of inversion (i).

Step 1: Evaluate internal geometric symmetry elements.

The trans − [Co(en) $ _2 $Cl $ _2 $] $ ^+ $ configuration distributes its ligands directly opposite each other across a central inversion plane, making it symmetrical and optically inactive.

In the cis –  $ [\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{en})_2\mathrm{Cl}_2]^+ $ isomer, the bidentate ethylenediamine ligands are placed adjacent to each other. This geometry removes all internal symmetry planes, resulting in a chiral core that forms stable  $ d $- and  $ l $-mirror image pairs.

Quick Tip: Octahedral metal configurations of the general type  $ [M(AA)_2X_2] $ with bidentate chelating rings display optical activity exclusively in their cis arrangements.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.015Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5257, 'chemistry-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q08', 8, 8, 'Using Crystal Field Theory (CFT), what is the correct electronic configuration and magnetic behavior of the high-spin complex  $ [\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]^{2+} $?', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ t_{2g}^{4}e_{g}^{2} $, Paramagnetic"},{"id":"B","text":"$ t_{2g}^{6}e_{g}^{0} $, Diamagnetic"},{"id":"C","text":"$ t_{2g}^{3}e_{g}^{3} $, Paramagnetic"},{"id":"D","text":"$ t_{2g}^{5}e_{g}^{1} $, Paramagnetic"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept: In an octahedral coordination field, $d$-orbitals split into lower energy $t_{2g}$ and higher energy $e_g$ levels. Weak-field ligands have a splitting energy smaller than the pairing energy ($\Delta_o < P$), which creates a high-spin configuration.

Step 1: Find the oxidation state and valence electrons.

Water is a neutral ligand, meaning Fe has an oxidation state of +2. The electron configuration of an Fe^{2+} ion is [Ar]3d^{6}.

Step 2: Distribute electrons based on weak-field rules.

Because  $ H_{2}O $ is a weak-field ligand, electrons fill all five orbital sub-levels singly before any pairing occurs:

• Place the first 3 electrons singly in the lower tier:  $ t_{2g}^{3} $

• Place the next 2 electrons singly in the upper tier:  $ e_{g}^{2} $

• Place the final 6th electron in the lower tier to pair up:  $ t_{2g}^{4} e_{g}^{2} $

This arrangement results in 4 unpaired electrons, making the complex paramagnetic.

Quick Tip: For  $ d^4 $ through  $ d^7 $ metals, look at the ligand field strength: Weak-field ligands keep systems in a high-spin state ( $ \Delta_o < P $), while strong-field ligands force low-spin configurations ( $ \Delta_o > P $).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.016Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5258, 'chemistry-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q09', 9, 9, 'Which of the following alkyl halides will undergo the fastest rate of nucleophilic substitution via an  $ S_{N}1 $ mechanism when treated with an aqueous nucleophile?', '[{"id":"A","text":"CH_{3}Cl"},{"id":"B","text":"$ (\\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{3}\\mathrm{CCl} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ CH_{3}CH_{2}Cl $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ (\\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\\mathrm{CHCl} $"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: The rate-determining step of an  $ S_{N} $1 substitution mechanism is the unimolecular dissociation of the leaving group to form a carbocation intermediate:

 $$ \mathrm{R}-\mathrm{X}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{Slow}}\mathrm{R}^{+}+\mathrm{X}^{-} $$ 

The overall reaction rate depends directly on the stability of this intermediate carbocation.

Step 1: Evaluate intermediate carbocation stability trends.

Comparing the carbocations formed when the leaving groups dissociate:

•  $ CH_{3}Cl \rightarrow CH_{3}^{+} $ (Methyl carbocation, highly unstable)

•  $ CH_{3}CH_{2}Cl \rightarrow CH_{3}CH_{2}^{+} $ (1° Primary carbocation)

•  $ (CH_{3})_{2}CHCl \rightarrow (CH_{3})_{2}CH^{+} $ (2° Secondary carbocation)

 $ \mathrm{(CH_{3})_{3}CCl \rightarrow (} $

 $ text{CH}_3)_3C^+ $ (3° Tertiary carbocation, stabilized by 9 hyperconjugative  $ \alpha $-hydrogens)

The tertiary halide dissociates fastest because it forms the most stable intermediate carbocation.

Quick Tip: Carbocation stability and  $ S_{N}1 $ reaction rates follow the exact same structural trend: Tertiary  $ (3^{\circ}) > \text{Secondary } (2^{\circ}) > \text{Primary } (1^{\circ}) > \text{Methyl} $.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.017Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5259, 'chemistry-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Arrange the following compounds in increasing order of acidic strength:

#### Phenol, Water, Propan-1-ol, Propan-2-ol', '[{"id":"A","text":"Water < Propan-2-ol < Propan-1-ol < Phenol"},{"id":"B","text":"Propan-2-ol < Propan-1-ol < Water < Phenol"},{"id":"C","text":"Propan-1-ol < Propan-2-ol < Water < Phenol"},{"id":"D","text":"Phenol < Water < Propan-1-ol < Propan-2-ol"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Acidic strength depends upon the ease with which a compound can donate a proton ( $ H^{+} $). Greater stability of the conjugate base formed after loss of proton means stronger acidity.

• Alkyl groups show +I effect and decrease acidity.

• Branching increases electron donation and further decreases acidity.

- Phenoxide ion formed from phenol is resonance stabilized, making phenol highly acidic.

#### Step 1: Compare propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol.

In propan-2-ol, the carbon attached to –OH group has two alkyl groups, producing stronger +I effect.

This destabilizes the alkoxide ion formed after removal of proton.

Hence:

 $$  Propan-2-ol<Propan-1-ol $$ 

#### Step 2: Compare alcohols with water.

Water does not contain electron donating alkyl groups, so the hydroxide ion formed is comparatively more stable.

Therefore:

 $$ \mathrm{Propan-2-ol}<\mathrm{Propan-1-ol}<\mathrm{Water} $$ 

Step 3: Compare phenol with water.

Phenol forms phenoxide ion after losing proton.

 $$  C_{6}H_{5}OH\rightarrow C_{6}H_{5}O^{-}+H^{+} $$ 

Phenoxide ion is resonance stabilized because the negative charge is delocalized over the benzene ring.

Hence phenol is most acidic.

 $$  Propan-2-ol<Propan-1-ol<Water<Phenol $$ 

Quick Tip: Resonance stabilization increases acidity, while electron donating +I effect decreases acidity.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.019Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5260, 'chemistry-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Among the following amines, the strongest base in aqueous solution is:

 $$  NH_{3},CH_{3}NH_{2},(CH_{3})_{2}NH,(CH_{3})_{3}N $$', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ NH_{3} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ (\\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{3}\\mathrm{N} $"},{"id":"C","text":"( $ CH_{3})_{2}NH $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ CH_{3}NH_{2} $"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Basic strength of amines depends upon:

• Availability of lone pair on nitrogen atom.

• Electron donating + I effect of alkyl groups.

• Stability of the protonated amine in aqueous solution due to solvation.

In gaseous state:

 $$ 3^{\circ}>2^{\circ}>1^{\circ}>NH_{3} $$ 

But in aqueous solution, solvation plays an important role. Secondary amines become most basic because they have:

• Strong + I effect

• Effective solvation

Hence:

 $$ 2^{\circ}>1^{\circ}>3^{\circ}>NH_{3} $$ 

Step 1: Understand the effect of alkyl groups.

Methyl groups donate electrons toward nitrogen through +I effect, increasing electron density on nitrogen and making the amine more basic.

 $$  NH_{3}<CH_{3}NH_{2}<(CH_{3})_{2}NH $$ 

##### Step 2: Compare tertiary amine in aqueous medium.

Although tertiary amine has maximum +I effect, its protonated form is less stabilized due to poor solvation because of steric hindrance.

Therefore:

 $$ (CH_{3})_{3}N<(CH_{3})_{2}NH $$ 

Step 3: Final order of basic strength in aqueous solution.

 $$ \mathrm{(C H_{3})_{2}N H>C H_{3}N H_{2}>(C H_{3})_{3}N>N H_{3}} $$ 

Hence, the strongest base is:

 $$ (\mathrm{C H}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{N H} $$ 

Quick Tip: For amines in aqueous solution:

 $$ 2^{\circ}\ \mathrm{amine}>1^{\circ}\ \mathrm{amine}>3^{\circ}\ \mathrm{amine} $$ 

because solvation of protonated amines is very important.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.019Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5261, 'chemistry-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q03', 3, 3, 'Lucas reagent gives immediate turbidity with:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Ethanol"},{"id":"B","text":"Propan-1-ol"},{"id":"C","text":"Propan-2-ol"},{"id":"D","text":"tert-Butyl alcohol"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

Lucas reagent is a mixture of:

 $$ \mathrm{Conc.~HCl+A n h y d r o u s~Z n C l_{2}} $$ 

It is used to distinguish primary, secondary and tertiary alcohols.

• Tertiary alcohols react immediately.

• Secondary alcohols react within few minutes.

• Primary alcohols react very slowly.

Turbidity appears due to formation of insoluble alkyl chlorides.

Step 1: Understand reactivity order.

Tertiary alcohols form highly stable tertiary carbocations.

Hence substitution reaction occurs rapidly through  $ S_{N} $ 1 mechanism.

 $$ 3^{\circ}>2^{\circ}>1^{\circ} $$ 

#### Step 2: Identify tertiary alcohol.

Among the given compounds:

tert-Butyl alcohol

is a tertiary alcohol.

Step 3: Conclusion.

Therefore it gives immediate turbidity with Lucas reagent.

tert-Butyl alcohol

Quick Tip: Lucas reagent test:

 $ 3^{\circ} $ alcohol  $ \rightarrow $ Immediate turbidity

 $ 2^{\circ} $ alcohol  $ \rightarrow $ Slow turbidity

 $ 1^{\circ} $ alcohol  $ \rightarrow $ No turbidity at room temperature', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.019Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5262, 'chemistry-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Which of the following compounds has the highest boiling point?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Propane"},{"id":"B","text":"Propanal"},{"id":"C","text":"Propan-1-ol"},{"id":"D","text":"Dimethyl ether"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Concept:

Boiling point depends upon intermolecular forces.

• Hydrogen bonding causes maximum attraction.

• Dipole-dipole interactions are weaker.

• Van der Waals forces are weakest.

Step 1: Analyze intermolecular forces.

- Propane contains only weak Van der Waals forces.

• Propanal and dimethyl ether contain dipole-dipole interactions.

• Propan-1-ol contains intermolecular hydrogen bonding.

#### Step 2: Compare boiling points.

Hydrogen bonding significantly increases boiling point because extra energy is required to break intermolecular attractions.

Therefore:

Propan-1-ol

has maximum boiling point.

Step 3: Final conclusion.

Propan-1-ol

Quick Tip: Compounds capable of intermolecular hydrogen bonding generally possess higher boiling points.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.021Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5263, 'chemistry-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q05', 5, 5, 'Among the following, the compound having the lowest value of  $ pK_{a} $ is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Ethanol"},{"id":"B","text":"Water"},{"id":"C","text":"Phenol"},{"id":"D","text":"Propan-2-ol"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Lower value of  $ pK_{a} $ indicates stronger acidity.

 $$ p K_{a}=-\log K_{a} $$ 

Hence:

Smaller  $ pK_{a} \Rightarrow $ Greater acidity

Acidic strength depends upon the stability of conjugate base formed after removal of proton.

Step 1: Compare alcohols and water.

Alcohols contain alkyl groups which show +I effect and destabilize alkoxide ions.

Therefore alcohols are less acidic than water.

 $$  Propan-2-ol<Ethanol<Water $$ 

Step 2: Analyze phenol.

Phenol forms phenoxide ion:

 $$  C_{6}H_{5}OH\rightarrow C_{6}H_{5}O^{-}+H^{+} $$ 

The negative charge on oxygen gets delocalized over the benzene ring through resonance.

Hence phenoxide ion becomes highly stable.

Step 3: Determine lowest  $ pK_{a} $.

Since phenol is most acidic among the given compounds, it has the lowest  $ pK_{a} $.

Phenol

Quick Tip: Greater the acidic strength, smaller is the value of $pK_{a}$.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.022Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5264, 'chemistry-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q06', 6, 6, 'Which of the following compounds gives a positive Hinsberg test and forms a soluble sulphonamide?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Aniline"},{"id":"B","text":"Dimethylamine"},{"id":"C","text":"Trimethylamine"},{"id":"D","text":"Triphenylamine"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Hinsberg reagent is:

#### Benzenesulphonyl chloride

It is used to distinguish primary, secondary and tertiary amines.

• Primary amines form sulphonamides soluble in alkali.

• Secondary amines form insoluble sulphonamides.

• Tertiary amines do not react.

##### Step 1: Understand the reaction of primary amines.

Primary amines contain one replaceable hydrogen atom attached to nitrogen.

They react with Hinsberg reagent to form sulphonamides having acidic hydrogen.

These dissolve in alkali due to salt formation.

Step 2: Identify the primary amine.

Among the given compounds:

 $$ \mathrm{A n i l i n e~(C_{6}H_{5}N H_{2})} $$ 

is a primary amine.

Step 3: Conclusion.

Hence aniline forms soluble sulphonamide in Hinsberg test.

Aniline



<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>1° amine → Soluble product</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>2° amine → Insoluble product</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>3° amine → No reaction</td></tr></table>', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.022Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5265, 'chemistry-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q07', 7, 7, 'The compound which is most basic in gaseous phase is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Ammonia"},{"id":"B","text":"Methylamine"},{"id":"C","text":"Dimethylamine"},{"id":"D","text":"Trimethylamine"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

In gaseous phase, solvation effects are absent.

Hence basicity depends mainly upon:

• Electron donating + I effect of alkyl groups.

• Availability of lone pair on nitrogen.

More alkyl groups increase electron density on nitrogen and increase basic strength.

Step 1: Understand inductive effect.

Alkyl groups donate electrons toward nitrogen through +I effect.

Therefore:

 $$  NH_{3}<CH_{3}NH_{2}<(CH_{3})_{2}NH<(CH_{3})_{3}N $$ 

Step 2: Analyze gaseous phase condition.

Since no hydration or solvation occurs in gaseous phase, steric hindrance does not reduce basicity significantly.

Hence tertiary amine becomes strongest base.

Step 3: Final conclusion.

 $$ (\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{3}\mathrm{N} $$ 

Quick Tip: In gaseous phase:

 $$ 3^{\circ}>2^{\circ}>1^{\circ}>NH_{3} $$ 

because only +I effect dominates.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.022Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5266, 'chemistry-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q08', 8, 8, 'Which of the following compounds can show metamerism?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Propanal"},{"id":"B","text":"Propanone"},{"id":"C","text":"Methoxyethane"},{"id":"D","text":"Ethanol"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Metamerism arises due to different alkyl groups attached on either side of a polyvalent functional group.

Common functional groups showing metamerism:

Ether  $ (-O- $

• Secondary amine  $ (-NH-) $

• Ketone  $ (-CO- $

Step 1: Understand structure requirement.

For metamerism:

 $$ \boldsymbol{R}-\boldsymbol{X}-\boldsymbol{R}^{\prime} $$ 

must be possible where R and  $ R'' $ are different alkyl groups.

Step 2: Analyze each compound.

- Propanal does not possess suitable polyvalent atom.

- Propanone has only one possible arrangement for three carbons.

• Methoxyethane is an ether and can exhibit metamerism.

• Ethanol cannot show metamerism.

Step 3: Conclusion.

Methoxyethane

Quick Tip: Ethers commonly show metamerism because oxygen can connect two different alkyl groups.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.023Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5267, 'chemistry-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q09', 9, 9, 'The half-life of a first order reaction is 1386 s. The rate constant k is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ 5 \\times 10^{-4} $ s $ ^{-1} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ 2 \\times 10^{-4} \\, s^{-1} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ 1 \\times 10^{-3} \\, s^{-1} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ 4\\times10^{-3}\\ s^{-1} $"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

For a first order reaction:

 $$ t_{1/2}=\frac{0.693}{k} $$ 

where:

•  $ t_{1/2} $ = half-life

• k = rate constant

Step 1: Write the formula.

 $$ k=\frac{0.693}{t_{1/2}} $$ 

Step 2: Substitute the given value.

 $$ k=\frac{0.693}{1386} $$ 

 $$ k=5\times10^{-4}s^{-1} $$ 

Step 3: Final answer.

 $$ 5\times10^{-4}\mathrm{s}^{-1} $$ 

Quick Tip: For first order reactions:

 $$ t_{1/2}\propto\frac{1}{k} $$ 

Half-life is independent of initial concentration.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.023Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5268, 'chemistry-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q10', 10, 10, 'Which of the following biomolecules acts as the genetic material in most organisms?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Protein"},{"id":"B","text":"DNA"},{"id":"C","text":"Lipid"},{"id":"D","text":"Polysaccharide"}]', 0, 'B', '##### Concept:

Biomolecules are chemical compounds present in living organisms.

DNA stands for:

##### Deoxyribonucleic Acid

It carries hereditary information from one generation to another.

##### Step 1: Understand the role of DNA.

DNA contains genes responsible for inheritance and expression of characters.

It stores biological information in the sequence of nitrogenous bases.

Step 2: Analyze other biomolecules.

• Proteins mainly perform structural and enzymatic functions.

• Lipids store energy and form membranes.

• Polysaccharides mainly act as energy reserves.

Step 3: Conclusion.

Hence DNA acts as genetic material in most organisms.

<div style="text-align: center;"><img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-05-21-shift-1/q10-exp-1.jpg" alt="Figure 1 for question 10" /></div>


Quick Tip: RNA acts as genetic material only in some viruses, while DNA is the genetic material in most living organisms.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q10-exp-1","kind":"explanation","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-05-21-shift-1/q10-exp-1.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Figure 1 for question 10"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.023Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5269, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-1', 'q02', 1, 1, 'Which of the following carbonyl compounds will undergo a self-oxidation and self-reduction reaction (Cannizzaro Reaction) when treated with a concentrated solution of a strong base (NaOH)?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Acetaldehyde ( $ CH_{3}CHO $)"},{"id":"B","text":"Benzaldehyde ( $ C_{6}H_{5}CHO $)"},{"id":"C","text":"Acetone ( $ CH_{3}COCH_{3} $)"},{"id":"D","text":"Propanal ( $ CH_{3}CH_{2}CHO $)"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: The **Cannizzaro Reaction** is a redox disproportionate reaction characteristic of

aldehydes that completely **lack any  $ \alpha $-hydrogen atoms**. When exposed to a concentrated strong base, one molecule of the aldehyde is reduced to a primary alcohol, while another molecule is oxidized to a carboxylic acid salt.

Step 1: Check for the presence of  $ \alpha $-hydrogens in each option.

An  $ \alpha $-hydrogen is any hydrogen atom attached to the carbon atom immediately adjacent to the carbonyl functional group (C=O).

- **Acetaldehyde ( $ CH_{3}CHO $):** The methyl carbon has 3 active  $ \alpha $-hydrogens. It undergoes Aldol Condensation instead.

- **Acetone ( $ CH_{3}COCH_{3} $):** **Contains 6 total  $ \alpha $-hydrogens, making it highly reactive under Aldol pathways.

**Propanal (CH₃CH₂CHO):** The central CH₂ group contains 2 active  $ \alpha $-hydrogens.

##### Step 2: Identify the compound lacking  $ \alpha $-hydrogens.

In  $ {}^{**} $Benzaldehyde (C $ _6 $H $ _5 $CHO) $ {}^{**} $, the carbonyl group is attached directly to an aromatic ring carbon that has no remaining hydrogen atoms. Because it completely lacks an  $ \alpha $-hydrogen, it undergoes the Cannizzaro reaction.

Quick Tip: The two most common examples of aldehydes that lack  $ \alpha $-hydrogens in the NCERT curriculum are **Formaldehyde (HCHO)** and ****Benzaldehyde ( $ C_{6}H_{5}CHO $)**. Keep these memorized for quick name reaction classification questions.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.024Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5270, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-1', 'q03', 2, 2, 'Determine the spin-only magnetic moment value for a central divalent manganese gaseous ion ( $ Mn^{2+} $) based on its d-orbital electron distribution. (Atomic Number of Mn = 25)', '[{"id":"A","text":"4.90 BM"},{"id":"B","text":"1.73 BM"},{"id":"C","text":"3.87 BM"},{"id":"D","text":"5.92 BM"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept: The spin-only magnetic moment ( $ \mu $) of a transition metal ion depends on the total

number of unpaired d-orbital electrons (n) present in its valence shell, calculated using the formula:

 $$ \mu=\sqrt{n(n+2)}\mathrm{~B o h r~M a g n e t o n s~(B M)} $$ 

Step 1: Determine the electronic configuration of the divalent ion.

The neutral ground state configuration of Manganese  $ (Z = 25) $ is:

 $$  Mn=\left[Ar\right]3d^{5}4s^{2} $$ 

Forming the divalent ion  $ (Mn^{2+}) $ requires removing the two outer valence electrons from the 4s orbital first:

 $$  Mn^{2+}=[Ar]3d^{5}4s^{0} $$ 

Step 2: Count the number of unpaired electrons using Hund''s Rule.

The d subshell contains 5 separate degenerate orbitals. Distributing 5 electrons across these orbitals following Hund''s rule gives 1 electron per orbital:

Number of unpaired electrons  $ (n)=5 $

Step 3: Substitute n into the magnetic moment equation.

Plug the value n = 5 into our formula:

 $$ \mu=\sqrt{5(5+2)}=\sqrt{5\times7}=\sqrt{35} $$ 

Since  $ \sqrt{36}=6 $, the value of  $ \sqrt{35} $ evaluates to just below 6:

 $$ \mu\approx5.92\mathrm{B M} $$ 

Quick Tip: Use this fast estimation shortcut for your exam: the first decimal digit of a spin-only magnetic moment value always matches the number of unpaired electrons (n). For example, if $n = 3$, $\mu \approx 3.8$ BM; if $n = 5$, $\mu \approx 5.9$ BM.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.024Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5271, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-1', 'q04', 3, 3, 'An organic amine compound ''X'' reacts smoothly with Hinsberg''s reagent (Benzenesulfonyl chloride) to form a solid precipitate. This precipitate remains completely insoluble when treated with an aqueous sodium hydroxide (NaOH) solution. Classify the amine ''X''.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Primary Amine"},{"id":"B","text":"Secondary Amine"},{"id":"C","text":"Tertiary Amine"},{"id":"D","text":"Quaternary Ammonium Salt"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: The **Hinsberg Test** is used to distinguish between primary, secondary, and tertiary amines based on how they react with benzenesulfonyl chloride ( $ C_6H_5SO_2Cl $) and whether the resulting products dissolve in an alkaline solution.

Step 1: Analyze how each type of amine behaves during the test.

- **Primary Amines:** React to form a sulfonamide that retains a highly acidic hydrogen atom on its nitrogen. This acidic hydrogen allows the product to dissolve completely in strong bases like NaOH.

- **Secondary Amines:** Reaction with the reagent to form an N,N-dialkylbenzenesulfonamide product. Because the nitrogen has no remaining acidic hydrogen atoms, this product is completely insoluble in alkali.

- **Tertiary Amines:** Lack any replaceable hydrogen atoms on the nitrogen and do not react with the test reagent under standard conditions.

Step 2: Match the compound''s behavior to the correct category.

Because compound ''X'' reacts with the reagent but forms a precipitate that does not dissolve in NaOH, it matches the characteristic behavior of a **Secondary Amine***.

Quick Tip: Keep this summary of the Hinsberg test outcomes memorized:

• Primary amine = Reacts, product **dissolves** in alkali.

• Secondary amine = Reacts, product **remains insoluble** in alkali.

• Tertiary amine = **Does not react** with the reagent at all.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.025Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5272, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-1', 'q05', 4, 4, 'According to IUPAC coordination nomenclature rules, what is the correct official name for the coordination compound represented by  $ [\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{5}(\mathrm{Cl})]\mathrm{Cl}_{2} $?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Chloridopentaamminecobaltic chloride"},{"id":"B","text":"Pentaaminechloridocobalt(II) dichloride"},{"id":"C","text":"Pentaaminechloridocobalt(III) chloride"},{"id":"D","text":"Pentaamminedichloridocobalt(III) chloride"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: Naming coordination compounds requires applying systematic IUPAC guidelines:

1. Name the ligands inside the coordination bracket first, in alphabetical order, before naming the central metal ion.

2. Stated numerical prefixes (di, tri, tetra, penta) do not alter the alphabetical priority order.

3. Specify the oxidation state of the central metal using Roman numerals in parentheses immediately after its name.

Step 1: Identify and alphabetize the ligands inside the coordination sphere.

The complex bracket contains two distinct types of ligands:

• Five neutral ammonia molecules, named as  $ {}^{**} $ammine $ {}^{**} $.

• One anionic chloride ion, named as  $ {}^{**} $chloridocon $ {}^{**} $ or  $ {}^{**} $chlorido $ {}^{**} $.

Arranging them alphabetically places "ammine" (starts with ''a'') before "chlorido" (starts with ''c''), which gives: **pentaamminechlorido**.

Step 2: Calculate the oxidation state of the central Cobalt metal.

Let the unknown oxidation state of Cobalt be x. The neutral ammine molecules carry a charge of 0, while each chloride ion carries a charge of -1:

 $$ x+5(0)+1(-1)+2(-1)=0\quad\Rightarrow\quad x-1-2=0\quad\Rightarrow\quad x=+3 $$ 

The metal is named as **cobalt(III)** because the complex ion carries a net positive charge.

Step 3: Combine the components into a single name.

Combine the alphabetized ligands, the metal with its oxidation state, and finish with the

counter-anion name outside the bracket:

# Pentaamminechloridocobalt(III) chloride

Quick Tip: Pay close attention to spelling details during the exam: the coordinated neutral ammonia ligand must always be spelled with a double ''m'' (**ammine**), whereas organic amine compounds use a single ''m'' (**amine**).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.025Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5273, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-1', 'q06', 5, 5, 'Which of the following statement options accurately describes the structural carbohydrate linkages present inside a standard molecule of Sucrose?', '[{"id":"A","text":"It contains a standard C-1 to C-4 glycosidic bridge linking two identical $ \\alpha $-D-glucose rings together."},{"id":"B","text":"It features a glycosidic linkage connecting the C-1 carbon of an $ \\alpha $-D-glucose unit to the C-2 carbon of a $ \\beta $-D-fructose unit."},{"id":"C","text":"It consists of a C-1 to C-4 linkage binding a $ \\beta $-D-galactose ring directly onto a neighboring glucose molecule."},{"id":"D","text":"It is formed by a continuous chain of $ \\beta $-D-glucose blocks linked entirely via C-1 to C-6 branch channels."}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: Disaccharides are formed when two monosaccharides combine through a glycosidic linkage formed by a condensation reaction.

Different disaccharides differ in:

• The monosaccharides involved

• The carbon atoms participating in the linkage

• The type of glycosidic bond formed

Step 1: Identify the monosaccharides present in sucrose.

Sucrose is a disaccharide composed of:

• One molecule of  $ \alpha $-D-glucose

• One molecule of  $ \beta $-D-fructose

Thus, sucrose is formed from glucose and fructose units.

Step 2: Determine the glycosidic linkage in sucrose.

In sucrose:

• The C-1 carbon of  $ \alpha $-D-glucose

• is linked to the C-2 carbon of  $ \beta $-D-fructose

Therefore, sucrose contains:

 $$ \alpha(1\to2)\beta $$ 

glycosidic linkage.

Since both anomeric carbons are involved in bond formation, sucrose behaves as a non-reducing sugar.

Step 3: Evaluate the options.

(A) Incorrect — This describes maltose, which contains two glucose units linked by a C-1 to C-4 bond.

(B) Correct — Sucrose contains a linkage between C-1 of  $ \alpha $-D-glucose and C-2 of  $ \beta $-D-fructose.

(C) Incorrect — This describes lactose, which contains galactose and glucose.

(D) Incorrect — This does not describe sucrose structure.

Hence, the correct answer is:

Quick Tip: Important disaccharides and their linkages:

• Sucrose = Glucose + Fructose  $ \rightarrow \alpha(1 \rightarrow 2)\beta $

• Maltose = Glucose + Glucose  $ \rightarrow \alpha(1 \rightarrow 4) $

• Lactose = Galactose + Glucose  $ \rightarrow \beta(1 \rightarrow 4) $', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.025Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5274, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-1', 'q07', 6, 6, 'What is the correct official unit measurement scale used to represent Molar Conductivity

( $ \Lambda_{m} $) profiles within electrolytic systems?', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ S \\, cm^{-2} \\, mol^{-1} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ S^{-1}\\ cm^{2}\\ mol $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ \\Omega\\ cm^2\\ mol^{-1} $"},{"id":"D","text":"S cm $ ^{2} $ mol $ ^{-1} $"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept: Molar conductivity ( $ \Lambda_{m} $) measures the electrolytic conductance capacity of a solution containing exactly one mole of dissolved electrolyte. It is mathematically related to specific conductivity ( $ \kappa $) and concentration (C) by the equation:

 $$ \Lambda_{m}=\frac{\kappa}{C} $$ 

Step 1: Analyze the base units of the individual variables.

Derive the units systematically through dimensional analysis:

- **Specific Conductivity ( $ \kappa $):** Measured in Siemens per centimeter (S cm $ ^{-1} $), where Siemens (S) represents reciprocal ohms ( $ \Omega^{-1} $).

- **Concentration (C):** Expressed in terms of solution volume as moles per cubic centimeter (mol cm $ ^{-3} $).

Step 2: Combine the units within the equation.

Substitute these units into our molar conductivity expression:

 $$  Units~of~\Lambda_{m}=\frac{S\ cm^{-1}}{mol\ cm^{-3}}=S\cdot cm^{-1}\cdot cm^{3}\cdot mol^{-1} $$ 

Simplify the overlapping centimeter exponents:

 $$ \mathrm{U n i t s~o f~}\Lambda_{m}=\mathrm{S~c m}^{2}\mathrm{m o l}^{-1} $$ 

Quick Tip: When using concentration values in moles per liter (mol L $ ^{-1} $), apply a scaling factor of 1000 to keep your units consistent:  $ \star\star\Lambda_m = \frac{\kappa \times 1000}{\text{Molarity}} \star\star $, which yields the final value directly in S cm $ ^2 $ mol $ ^{-1} $.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.025Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5275, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-1', 'q08', 7, 7, 'The steady, progressive decrease in the atomic and ionic radii of transition metals along the lanthanide series (Lanthanide Contraction) is primarily caused by:', '[{"id":"A","text":"A continuous decrease in the absolute nuclear proton count within the central atomic cores."},{"id":"B","text":"The exceptionally poor shielding effect of the diffuse 4f-electrons, which allows the increasing nuclear charge to pull the outer electron shell inward."},{"id":"C","text":"The strong shielding ability of the 5d-electrons completely blocking the nuclear pull."},{"id":"D","text":"A sudden jump in the principal quantum number of the outer valence shells."}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: Lanthanide contraction refers to the gradual decrease in atomic and ionic radii across the lanthanide series from La to Lu.

This occurs because electrons are progressively added to the 4f-subshell while the nuclear charge also increases.

Step 1: Understand the shielding effect of 4f-electrons.

As we move across the lanthanide series:

• One proton is added to the nucleus at each step.

• One electron is added to the 4f-orbital.

However, 4f-electrons have very poor shielding ability because their orbitals are highly diffuse and do not effectively block the nuclear attraction experienced by outer electrons.

The shielding effectiveness follows:

 $$ s>p>d>f $$ 

Thus, f-electrons are the least effective in shielding.

Step 2: Analyze the effect on atomic size.

Because the 4f-electrons cannot effectively shield the increasing nuclear charge:

 $$ Z_{eff}\uparrow $$ 

where $Z_{eff}$ represents effective nuclear charge.

As the effective nuclear charge increases, the outer electrons are pulled closer to the nucleus. Therefore:

#### Atomic Radius  $ \downarrow $

This gradual decrease in size across the lanthanide series is called lanthanide contraction.

Step 3: Evaluate the options.

(A) Incorrect — Nuclear proton count increases across the series, it does not decrease.

(B) Correct — Poor shielding by 4f-electrons causes increasing effective nuclear charge and contraction in size.

(C) Incorrect — 5d-electrons are not responsible for lanthanide contraction.

(D) Incorrect — There is no sudden jump in the principal quantum number causing this effect.

Hence, the correct answer is:

<div style="text-align: center;"><img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-1/q08-exp-1.jpg" alt="Figure 1 for question 8" /></div>


Quick Tip: Due to lanthanide contraction, elements of the 4d and 5d transition series in the same group often have very similar atomic radii and chemical properties, such as Zr and Hf.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q08-exp-1","kind":"explanation","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-1/q08-exp-1.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Figure 1 for question 8"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.026Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5276, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-1', 'q09', 8, 8, 'Identify which of the following heterocyclic nitrogenous bases is found exclusively within the macromolecular structure of RNA, and is completely absent in standard double-stranded DNA structures.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Thymine"},{"id":"B","text":"Adenine"},{"id":"C","text":"Uracil"},{"id":"D","text":"Cytosine"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: Nucleic acids are biopolymers constructed from nucleotide monomers. Each nucleotide contains a pentose sugar, a phosphate group, and a nitrogenous base. These nitrogenous bases are divided into purines (double-ring structures) and pyrimidines (single-ring structures).

Step 1: Compare the nitrogenous bases used in DNA versus RNA.

- **DNA Base Composition:** **Uses the purines **Adenine (A)** and **Guanine (G)**, and the pyrimidines **Cytosine (C)** and **Thymine (T)**.

- **RNA Base Composition:** **Uses the exact same purines, **Adenine (A)** and **Guanine (G)**, and the pyrimidine **Cytosine (C)**. However, it replaces thymine with a different pyrimidine base called **Uracil (U)**.

Step 2: Identify the base unique to RNA.

**Uracil** is found exclusively in RNA molecules, where it forms two hydrogen bonds with adenine during transcription. DNA structures utilize thymine instead.

Quick Tip: Chemically, **Thymine** is also known as **5-Methyluracil**. This means you can think of DNA''s base as a methylated version of RNA''s uracil, which provides greater structural stability for long-term genetic storage.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.026Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5277, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-1', 'q10', 9, 9, 'During an electrochemistry lab session, a student uses the Nernst Equation to calculate the cell potential ( $ E_{cell} $) of a galvanic system at a non-standard temperature. Which parameter option accurately defines the scaling behavior of the cell potential relative to changes in the reaction quotient (Q)?', '[{"id":"A","text":"As the concentration of product ions increases relative to reactant ions (increasing Q), the value of $ E_{cell} $ decreases."},{"id":"B","text":"The value of $ E_{cell} $ increases linearly with an increase in the value of Q."},{"id":"C","text":"The value of $ E_{cell} $ remains completely independent of any concentration shifts in Q."},{"id":"D","text":"An increase in Q causes an exponential jump in the net electrical voltage produced."}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept: The Nernst Equation relates the cell potential of an electrochemical cell to the concentrations of reactants and products.

The equation is:

 $$ E_{cell}=E_{cell}^{\circ}-\frac{2.303RT}{nF}\log Q $$ 

where:

•  $ E_{cell}^{\circ} = \text{Standard cell potential} $

• R = Gas constant

• T = Temperature

• n = Number of electrons transferred

• F = Faraday constant

• Q = Reaction quotient

Step 1: Understand the role of the reaction quotient Q.

The reaction quotient is given by:

 $$ Q=\frac{[Products]^{p}}{[Reactants]^{r}} $$ 

Thus:

• Increase in product concentration  $ \Rightarrow $ Q increases

• Increase in reactant concentration  $ \Rightarrow $ Q decreases

Step 2: Analyze the effect of increasing Q on cell potential.

In the Nernst equation:

 $$ E_{cell}=E_{cell}^{\circ}-\frac{2.303RT}{nF}\log Q $$ 

the logarithmic term is subtracted from the standard cell potential.

Therefore:

• If Q increases, then  $ \log Q $ increases.

• A larger value is subtracted from  $ E_{cell}^{\circ} $

• Hence,  $ E_{cell} $ decreases.

Thus, cell potential decreases as the concentration of products increases relative to reactants.

Step 3: Evaluate the options.

(A) Correct — Increasing Q decreases the value of  $ E_{cell} $.

(B) Incorrect —  $ E_{cell} $ does not increase linearly with Q.

(C) Incorrect — Cell potential depends directly on concentration changes through Q.

(D) Incorrect — The variation is logarithmic, not exponential.

Hence, the correct answer is:

<div style="text-align: center;"><img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-1/q10-exp-1.jpg" alt="Figure 1 for question 10" /></div>


Quick Tip: To increase the cell potential of a galvanic cell:

• Keep reactant concentration high

• Keep product concentration low

This minimizes Q and increases  $ E_{cell} $.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q10-exp-1","kind":"explanation","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-1/q10-exp-1.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Figure 1 for question 10"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.026Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5278, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Which of the following liquid mixtures forms an ideal solution that strictly obeys Raoult''s Law across the entire concentration range at a constant temperature?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Ethanol + Acetone"},{"id":"B","text":"Chloroform + Acetone"},{"id":"C","text":"n-Hexane + n-Heptane"},{"id":"D","text":"Water + Nitric acid"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: An ideal solution is formed when the solute-solute $(A-A)$ and solvent-solvent $(B-B)$ intermolecular attractive forces are nearly identical in magnitude and nature to the newly formed solute-solvent $(A-B)$ interactions. For ideal solutions, the enthalpy of mixing is zero $(\Delta H_{\mathrm{mix}}=0)$ and the volume change on mixing is zero $(\Delta V_{\mathrm{mix}}=0)$.

Step 1: Analyze the chemical nature of the components in each mixture.

Ideal solutions are typically formed by mixing two chemical compounds that share highly similar molecular structures, polarities, and functional groups.

n-Hexane and n-Heptane are both straight-chain non-polar alkanes that interact purely through weak London dispersion forces. Because their structural environments are nearly

identical, their mixture forms a near-perfect Ideal Solution.

Step 2: Contrast with non-ideal solution types.

- Ethanol + Acetone: Shows positive deviation from Raoult''s law because acetone breaks the strong hydrogen-bonded networks of ethanol.

- Chloroform + Acetone: Shows negative deviation because a new, strong intermolecular hydrogen bond forms between the chloroform hydrogen and the acetone carbonyl oxygen.

Water + Nitric acid: Forms a maximum boiling azeotrope due to strong negative deviations from Raoult''s law.

Quick Tip: Look for homologous pairs on the exam to identify ideal solutions quickly: n-Hexane + n-Heptane, Bromoethane + Chloroethane, and Benzene + Toluene are the three primary NCERT examples.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.027Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5279, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Aldehydes and ketones react with semicarbazide (NH₂NHCONH₂) in a weakly acidic medium to form crystalline semicarbazone derivatives. Which of the three nitrogen atoms in a semicarbazide molecule acts as the primary nucleophilic center during this reaction?', '[{"id":"A","text":"The nitrogen atom attached directly to the carbonyl carbon atom $ (-NH-C=O) $."},{"id":"B","text":"The amide nitrogen atom of the terminal primary amide group $ (-CONH_{2}) $."},{"id":"C","text":"The terminal hydrazine nitrogen atom linked to the adjacent secondary nitrogen $ (-NH_2 $ of the $ -NH-NH_2 $ group)."},{"id":"D","text":"All three nitrogen atoms are equally nucleophilic due to resonance stabilization."}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: Nucleophilic addition-elimination reactions of carbonyl compounds with ammonia derivatives depend on the availability of a lone pair of electrons on the attacking nitrogen atom. A nitrogen atom whose lone pair is delocalized into an adjacent system via resonance will have reduced electron density, making it non-nucleophilic.

Step 1: Examine the resonance stabilization pathways within semicarbazide.

Semicarbazide has the structural formula:

 $$ \mathrm{H}_{2}\overset{1}{\mathrm{N}}-\mathrm{NH}-\overset{\mathrm{O}}{\mathrm{C}}-\mathrm{NH}_{2} $$ 

The lone pairs of electrons on both Nitrogen-2  $ (-NH- $) and Nitrogen-3  $ (-CONH_{2}) $ are positioned immediately adjacent to the electron-withdrawing carbonyl group (C=O). These lone pairs participate in resonance delocalization into the oxygen atom:

 $$ -\ddot{\mathrm{N}}\mathrm{H}-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\quad\longleftrightarrow\quad-\stackrel{+}{\mathrm{N}}\mathrm{H}=\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{O}- $$ 

This significant resonance delocalization strongly reduces the nucleophilic reactivity of these two nitrogen atoms.

##### Step 2: Identify the highly nucleophilic nitrogen center.

The lone pair on the terminal Nitrogen-1 ( $ -NH_{2} $ of the hydrazine end) is isolated from the carbonyl group by an intervening nitrogen atom. Because its lone pair cannot participate in resonance stabilization with the carbonyl group, it remains fully localized and highly available for nucleophilic attack on the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of an aldehyde or ketone.

Quick Tip: Remember that only the terminal  $ -\mathrm{NH}_{2} $ group furthest from the carbonyl is nucleophilic in semicarbazide. When writing out the reaction product, always link this specific terminal nitrogen to the carbonyl carbon to form a C=N double bond.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.028Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5280, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q03', 3, 3, 'An organic primary amide is treated with bromine (Br_{2}) and an aqueous solution of sodium hydroxide (NaOH) to yield a primary amine. Which of the following statements correctly

describes this transformation (Hofmann Bromamide Degradation)?', '[{"id":"A","text":"The resulting primary amine contains one more carbon atom than the starting amide."},{"id":"B","text":"The resulting primary amine contains the exact same number of carbon atoms as the starting amide."},{"id":"C","text":"The resulting primary amine contains one less carbon atom than the starting amide due to the loss of the carbonyl group as a carbonate ion."},{"id":"D","text":"The reaction converts a secondary amide into a tertiary amine via a molecular rearrangement."}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: The Hofmann Bromamide Degradation Reaction is a classic step-down degradation method used in organic synthesis to convert a primary amide into a primary amine with a shorter carbon chain length. The general chemical equation is:  $ C_{6}H_{5}COO^{-} + 2CO_{2} \rightarrow 6CO_{2} + 6H_{2}O $.

 $$ \mathrm{R}-\mathrm{CONH}_{2}+\mathrm{Br}_{2}+4\mathrm{NaOH}\quad\longrightarrow\quad\mathrm{R}-\mathrm{NH}_{2}+\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}+2\mathrm{NaBr}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} $$ 

#### Step 1: Analyze the migration mechanism of the reaction.

During the reaction pathway, an intermediate *nitrene* or *isocyanate* is generated. The alkyl or aryl group (R) migrates directly from the carbonyl carbon to the adjacent nitrogen atom.

The central carbonyl carbon  $ (-C=O) $ of the primary amide is completely extruded from the organic chain and eliminated as a mineral byproduct in the form of sodium carbonate  $ (Na_{2}CO_{3}) $. As a direct consequence, the resulting primary amine  $ (R-NH_{2}) $ contains exactly one less carbon atom than the parent starting amide.

Quick Tip: Hofmann Bromamide Degradation is a vital tool for organic "step-down" convergence conversions. For example, treating Ethanamide ( $ CH_{3}CONH_{2} $) under these reaction conditions skips the carbonyl group entirely to produce Methanamine ( $ CH_{3}NH_{2} $).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.028Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5281, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Which of the following coordination complex compounds is officially classified as a ''Heteroleptic Complex''?', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ [\\mathrm{Fe(H}_{2}\\mathrm{O})_{6}]\\mathrm{Cl}_{3} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ [\\mathrm{Co(NH_{3})_{5}(Cl)}]\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} $"},{"id":"C","text":"K_{4}[Fe(CN)_{6}]"},{"id":"D","text":"$ [\\mathrm{Ni(CO)_{4}}] $"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: Coordination complexes are classified into two structural categories based on the variety of ligands bound to the central metal atom:

- Homoleptic Complexes: Complexes in which the central metal atom is bound to only

one type of ligand group (e.g., all water molecules or all cyanide ions).

- Heteroleptic Complexes: Complexes in which the central metal atom is bound to more than one type of ligand group simultaneously.

Step 1: Analyze the ligand composition inside the coordination sphere for each option. Examine the ligands enclosed within the square brackets:

•  $ [Fe(H_{2}O)_{6}]Cl_{3} $: Contains only 6 aqua (H_{2}O) ligands. [Homoleptic]

•  $ K_4[Fe(CN)_6] $: Contains only 6 cyanido  $ (CN^- $) ligands. [Homoleptic]

•  $ [Ni(CO)_{4}] $: Contains only 4 carbonyl (CO) ligands. [Homoleptic]

##### Step 2: Identify the complex with a mixed ligand environment.

In  $ [\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{5}(\mathrm{Cl})]\mathrm{Cl}_{2} $, the coordination sphere contains two completely different types of ligands bound directly to the central Cobalt core: 5 neutral ammine ( $ \mathrm{NH}_{3} $) molecules and 1 anionic chlorido ( $ \mathrm{Cl}^{-} $) ion. Because it features a mixed ligand assembly, it is classified as a Heteroleptic Complex.

Quick Tip: To identify homeleptic vs. heteroleptic complexes quickly, look inside the square brackets. If you see two or more distinct chemical formulas listed next to the metal symbol, it is a Heteroleptic Complex. Ignore any counter-ions outside the brackets.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.029Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5282, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q05', 5, 5, 'What are the exact constituent monosaccharide units and the specific structural glycosidic linkage present inside a standard molecule of Lactose (Milk Sugar)?', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ \\alpha $-D-Glucose and $ \\beta $-D-Fructose linked via a $ C_{1}-C_{2} $ glycosidic bond."},{"id":"B","text":"Two units of $ \\alpha $-D-Glucose linked via a $ C_{1}-C_{4} $ glycosidic bond."},{"id":"C","text":"$ \\beta $-D-Galactose and $ \\beta $-D-Glucose linked via a $ C_{1}-C_{4} $ glycosidic bond."},{"id":"D","text":"Two units of $ \\beta $-D-Glucose linked via a $ C_{1}-C_{6} $ glycosidic bond."}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: Lactose is a reducing disaccharide sugar found naturally in milk. Its structure consists of two distinct hexose monosaccharide rings linked together through a dehydration process.

condensation bridge known as a glycosidic bond.

Step 1: Identify the monomer sub-units of Lactose.

Lactose is formed by the condensation of one molecule of  $ \beta $-D-galactose and one molecule of  $ \beta $-D-glucose.

#### Step 2: Trace the carbon positions that form the glycosidic bridge.

The glycosidic linkage is formed between the anomeric Carbon-1 ( $ C_{1} $) of the  $ \beta $-D-galactose ring and the alcohol Carbon-4 ( $ C_{4} $) of the adjacent  $ \beta $-D-glucose ring. This specific arrangement is designated as a  $ \beta(1 \rightarrow 4) $ glycosidic linkage.

#### Step 3: Verify why lactose acts as a reducing sugar.

Because the anomeric center of the glucose ring (Carbon-1) remains free and unbound, it can easily open into its straight-chain aldehyde form. This allows lactose to reduce Tollens'' and Fehling''s test reagents, making it a reducing sugar.

Quick Tip: Remember that Lactose is the only major disaccharide in the NCERT curriculum that contains Galactose as a building block. It uses a  $ \beta $-linkage, distinguishing it from maltose which uses an  $ \alpha $-linkage.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.030Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5283, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q06', 6, 6, 'When an aqueous solution of an electrolyte is progressively diluted by adding more solvent water, how do its Specific Conductivity ( $ \kappa $) and Molar Conductivity ( $ \Lambda_m $) respond?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Both Specific Conductivity ( $ \\kappa $) and Molar Conductivity ( $ \\Lambda_{m} $) increase uniformly."},{"id":"B","text":"Both Specific Conductivity ( $ \\kappa $) and Molar Conductivity ( $ \\Lambda_{m} $) decrease uniformly."},{"id":"C","text":"Specific Conductivity ( $ \\kappa $) increases, while Molar Conductivity ( $ \\Lambda_{m} $) decreases."},{"id":"D","text":"Specific Conductivity ( $ \\kappa $) decreases, while Molar Conductivity ( $ \\Lambda_{m} $) increases."}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept: The electrical conductivity profiles of an electrolytic solution depend on the concentration of ions present per unit volume, as well as the total mobility of those ions within the solvent matrix.

Step 1: Evaluate the behavior of Specific Conductivity  $ \kappa $ upon dilution.

Specific conductivity ($\kappa$) is defined as the conductance of exactly a one-centimeter cube unit volume of a solution. When a solution is diluted, the total number of current-carrying ions per unit volume decreases steadily. As a direct result, Specific Conductivity ($\kappa$) always decreases.

with dilution for both strong and weak electrolytes.

Step 2: Evaluate the behavior of Molar Conductivity  $ \Lambda_{m} $ upon dilution.

Molar conductivity ( $ \Lambda_{m} = \frac{\kappa}{C} $) is the conducting power of all the ions produced by dissolving one mole of an electrolyte. Upon dilution, the concentration (C) decreases. For strong electrolytes, interionic attractions decrease, allowing ions to move more freely. For weak electrolytes, the degree of dissociation ( $ \alpha $) increases sharply, generating a larger number of total ions. Consequently, Molar Conductivity ( $ \Lambda_{m} $) always increases with dilution.

Quick Tip: Keep this clear rule memorized: dilution increases molar conductivity ( $ \Lambda_{m} $) due to increased ion mobility and dissociation, but decreases specific conductivity ( $ \kappa $) because ion density per unit volume drops.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.032Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5284, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q07', 7, 7, 'Benzene reacts with a mixture of carbon monoxide (CO) and hydrogen chloride (HCl) gas in the presence of an anhydrous aluminium chloride (AlCl_{3}) catalyst to yield Benzaldehyde. This organic transformation is officially known as the:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Rosenmund Reduction"},{"id":"B","text":"Gattermann-Koch Reaction"},{"id":"C","text":"Etard Reaction"},{"id":"D","text":"Stephen Reaction"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: Formylation is an electrophilic aromatic substitution reaction used to introduce a formyl group  $ (-CHO) $ directly into an activated aromatic benzene ring core.

Step 1: Identify the active reagents and chemical conversion.

The reaction uses a mixture of high-pressure CO and HCl gas, which reacts in situ under the influence of a Lewis acid catalyst ( $ AlCl_{3} $) to form a reactive formyl cation intermediate ( $ [HC=O]^{+} $). This intermediate attacks the benzene ring to yield benzaldehyde:

 $$ \mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{6}+\mathrm{CO}+\mathrm{HCl}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{Anhydrous~AlCl}_{3}/\mathrm{CuCl}}\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{CHO}+\mathrm{HCl} $$ 

This specific synthesis pathway is called the Gattermann-Koch Reaction.

Step 2: Differentiate from alternative aldehyde name reactions.

• Rosenmund Reduction: Converts an acyl chloride into an aldehyde using hydrogen gas over a poisoned palladium catalyst ( $ H_{2}/Pd-BaSO_{4} $).

• Etard Reaction: Oxidizes toluene to benzaldehyde using chromyl chloride  $ (\mathrm{CrO}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}) $

• Stephen Reaction: Reduces a nitrile to an aldehyde using tin(II) chloride and hydrochloric acid (SnCl_{2}/HCl).

Quick Tip: To remember the Gattermann-Koch reaction easily, connect the word "Koch" to the formula of its core reagents: Carbon monoxide + hydrogen chloride can be read phonetically as "C-O-H-Cl" (similar to Koch).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.032Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5285, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q08', 8, 8, 'Which of the following oxidation state configurations represents the most common, energetically stable, and dominant oxidation state exhibited by nearly all the Lanthanoid elements across the f-block series?', '[{"id":"A","text":"+2"},{"id":"B","text":"+4"},{"id":"C","text":"+3"},{"id":"D","text":"+5"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: The electronic configurations of the lanthanoids generally follow the pattern  $ [Xe]4f^{0-14}5d^{0-1}6s^{2} $. Their oxidation stability patterns depend on how easily they can lose their outer 6s and 5d electrons along with inner 4f electrons.

Step 1: Evaluate the general ionic stabilization pattern of lanthanoids.

Across the lanthanoid series, the +3 oxidation state is the most common and dominant oxidation state. Forming the trivalent aquated ions (Ln^{3+}) involves removing the two outer 6s valence electrons along with one additional electron from either the 5d or 4f subshells. This state is highly stable across the entire series due to favorable hydration and lattice energies.

Step 2: Analyze exceptions to the general rule.

While certain elements can exhibit alternative +2 or +4 oxidation states, they do so only when the loss of electrons yields an exceptionally stable empty  $ (4f^{0}) $, half-filled  $ (4f^{7}) $, or fully filled

 $ (4f^{14}) $ subshell configuration. For example:

-  $ Ce^{4+} $ is stable because it achieves an empty  $ 4f^{0} $ noble gas core.

•  $ Eu^{2+} $ is stable because it achieves a half-filled  $ 4f^{7} $ subshell.

However, these alternative ions act as strong oxidizing or reducing agents, readily reverting back to the dominant +3 state.

Quick Tip: If an f-block lanthanoid question asks for the most stable or dominant oxidation state without naming a specific element, +3 is always the correct choice.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.033Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5286, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q09', 9, 9, 'According to the structural constraints of Valence Bond Theory (VBT), which of the following hybridization geometries and magnetic configurations accurately describes the low-spin coordination complex ion  $ [\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]^{4-} $? (Atomic Number of Fe = 26)', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ sp^{3}d^{2} $ hybridization and Paramagnetic"},{"id":"B","text":"$ d^{2}sp^{3} $ hybridization and Diamagnetic"},{"id":"C","text":"$ dsp^{2} $ hybridization and Diamagnetic"},{"id":"D","text":"$ sp^{3}d^{2} $ hybridization and Diamagnetic"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: Valence Bond Theory determines the hybridization and geometry of coordination complexes by evaluating whether the incoming ligands are weak-field or strong-field, which dictates whether the d-orbital electrons pair up.

Step 1: Determine the oxidation state and electronic configuration of Iron.

Let the unknown oxidation state of Iron be x. Cyanide is an anionic ligand (CN $ ^{-} $):

 $$ x+6(-1)=-4\quad\Rightarrow\quad x-6=-4\quad\Rightarrow\quad x=+2 $$ 

The electronic configuration of a neutral Iron atom (Z = 26) is [Ar] 3d⁶ 4s². For the divalent ion (Fe²⁺), remove the two 4s electrons:

 $$ \mathrm{Fe^{2+}=[Ar]3d^{6}4s^{0}} $$ 

Step 2: Analyze the effect of the ligand on d-orbital electron pairing.

The cyanido (CN $ ^- $) ligand is a strong-field ligand. It forces the 6 electrons in the 3d orbitals to pair up completely within the lower-energy  $ t_{2g} $ orbitals:

 $$ \begin{array}{r}{3\mathrm{d}\;c o n f i g u r a t i o n\;a f t e r\;p a i r i n g}=\underline{{\uparrow\downarrow}}\;\underline{{\uparrow\downarrow}}\;\underline{{\uparrow\downarrow}}\underline{{\phantom{x}}}_{\mathrm{--}}\end{array} $$ 

Because all 6 electrons are completely paired up, the complex contains zero unpaired electrons, making it Diamagnetic.

Step 3: Identify the vacant orbitals available for hybridization.

Pairing up the electrons leaves two internal 3d orbitals completely empty. These two empty 3d orbitals combine with the vacant 4s orbital and three 4p orbitals to form six equivalent hybrid orbitals:

Hybridization = d $ ^{2} $sp $ ^{3} $ (Inner orbital octahedral complex)

Quick Tip: Strong-field ligands like CN⁻ and CO drive electron pairing, which typically results in inner-orbital d²sp³ hybridizations for octahedral complexes. Weak-field ligands like Cl⁻ or F⁻ result in outer-orbital sp³d² configurations.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.033Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5287, 'chemistry-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q10', 10, 10, 'Deficiency of which of the following essential vitamins in the human diet causes a reduction in intestinal calcium absorption, leading to the bone-softening childhood skeletal deformity known as Rickets?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Vitamin A"},{"id":"B","text":"Vitamin C"},{"id":"C","text":"Vitamin K"},{"id":"D","text":"Vitamin D"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept: Vitamins are essential organic micro-nutrients required in small amounts to carry out vital metabolic and physiological functions within the human body. They are broadly classified into fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) and water-soluble vitamins (B-complex, C).

Step 1: Analyze the physiological function of Vitamin D.

Vitamin D (calciferol) is a fat-soluble vitamin that plays a vital role in maintaining skeletal health by regulating calcium and phosphorus absorption in the intestines. It can be synthesized by the body when skin is exposed to solar ultraviolet radiation.

#### Step 2: Link a deficiency in this vitamin to its clinical symptoms.

A deficiency in Vitamin D impairs the body''s ability to calcify and harden bone tissue. In children, this structural weakness leads to Rickets, a deformity characterized by soft, bowed legs and malformed joints. In adults, this same deficiency leads to a condition known as osteomalacia.

Quick Tip: Keep this quick summary of common NCERT vitamin deficiencies memorized:

• Vitamin A deficiency = Night Blindness

• Vitamin C deficiency = Scurvy (bleeding gums)

• Vitamin D deficiency = Rickets (bone softening)

• Vitamin K deficiency = Increased blood clotting time (hemorrhage)', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.034Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5288, 'chemistry-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Given below are two statements :

Given: Molar masses of C, H, O, Cl are 12, 1, 16 and 35.5 g mol $ ^{-1} $ respectively.

Statement I: In 30%(w/w) solution of methanol in CCl $ _{4} $ (at T K), the mole fraction of CCl $ _{4} $ is equal to 0.33.

Statement II: Mixture of methanol and  $ CCl_{4} $ shows positive deviation from Raoult''s law.

In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the option given below :', '[{"id":"A","text":"Both Statement I and Statement II are true"},{"id":"B","text":"Both Statement I and Statement II are false"},{"id":"C","text":"Statement I is true but Statement II is false"},{"id":"D","text":"Statement I is false but Statement II is true"}]', 0, 'D', '## Concept:

Mole fraction is defined as:

 $$ X_{i}=\frac{Number~of~moles~of~component~i}{Total~number~of~moles} $$ 

For solutions showing deviation from Raoult''s law:

• Positive deviation occurs when solute-solvent interactions are weaker than solute-solute and solvent-solvent interactions.

• Negative deviation occurs when solute-solvent interactions are stronger.

Methanol is a hydrogen-bonded polar compound whereas  $ CCl_{4} $ is non-polar. Mixing them weakens intermolecular attractions and hence the solution shows positive deviation from Raoult''s law.

Step 1: Calculation of mole fraction of  $ CCl_{4} $.

A 30%(w/w) solution of methanol in  $ CCl_{4} $ means:

 $$ 30\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{methanol}+70\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{CCl}_{4} $$ 

Molar mass of methanol (CH $ _{3} $OH):

 $$ 12+4(1)+16=32\mathrm{~g~m o l}^{-1} $$ 

Number of moles of methanol:

 $$ n_{\mathrm{CH_{3}OH}}=\frac{30}{32}=0.9375 $$ 

Molar mass of  $ CCl_{4} $:

 $$ \begin{array}{rl} &=\Bigg[ 24^{1} \Bigg] \times \Bigg[ 35.5^{1} \Bigg]\\&=12 + 142\\ &= 154 \text{ g mol}^{-1} \end{array} $$ 

Number of moles of  $ CCl_{4} $:

 $$ \begin{aligned}&n_{CCl_{4}}=\frac{70}{154}\\ &\begin{aligned}\\ &=0.4545\\ &\end{aligned}\\ \end{aligned} $$ 

Total moles:

 $$ 0.9375+0.4545=1.392 $$ 

Mole fraction of CCl_{4}:

 $$ X_{CCl_{4}}=\frac{0.4545}{1.392} $$ 

 $$ =0.326\approx0.33 $$ 

Thus, Statement I is true.

Step 2: Checking Statement II regarding deviation from Raoult''s law.

Methanol molecules are strongly hydrogen bonded in pure state.

 $ CCl_{4} $ molecules are non-polar and interact mainly through weak van der Waals forces. When methanol and  $ CCl_{4} $ are mixed:

• The strong hydrogen bonding between methanol molecules gets disrupted.

• The new methanol- $ CCl_{4} $ interactions are weaker than original methanol-methanol interactions.

Therefore:

 $$ \boldsymbol{A}-\boldsymbol{B}<\boldsymbol{A}-\boldsymbol{A}and\boldsymbol{B}-\boldsymbol{B} $$ 

Hence, escaping tendency of molecules increases and vapour pressure becomes greater than expected.

Therefore, the mixture shows:

Positive deviation from Raoult''s law

Thus, Statement II is also true.

Hence, both Statement I and Statement II are true.

(1) Both Statement I and Statement II are true

Quick Tip: For w/w% problems:

 $$  Mass~percentage=\frac{Mass~of~solute}{Mass~of~solution}\times100 $$ 

Always convert masses into moles before calculating mole fraction.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.035Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5289, 'chemistry-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q03', 2, 2, 'The incorrect statement from the following with respect to carbohydrates is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"All monosaccharides are reducing sugars."},{"id":"B","text":"The monosaccharide units obtained from hydrolysis of oligosaccharides are always the same."},{"id":"C","text":"Starch and cellulose are typical examples of polysaccharides, which are very high molecular weight compounds of more than ten monosaccharide units."},{"id":"D","text":"Open chain and cyclic structures co-exist at equilibrium that are responsible for certain properties as in the case of D-(+)-glucose."}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Carbohydrates are polyhydroxy aldehydes or ketones or compounds that produce them upon hydrolysis.

They are classified into:

• Monosaccharides

• Oligosaccharides

• Polysaccharides

Reducing sugars contain a free aldehydic or ketonic group capable of reducing Fehling''s solution or Tollens'' reagent.

Step 1: Checking Statement (1).

All monosaccharides possess either:

• Free aldehyde group

• Or ketone group capable of tautomerisation

Therefore, all monosaccharides act as reducing sugars.

Examples:

Glucose, fructose, galactose

Thus, Statement (1) is correct.

Step 2: Checking Statement (2).

Oligosaccharides may produce:

• Same monosaccharide units

• Different monosaccharide units

Example:

- Sucrose on hydrolysis gives glucose and fructose.

Since the hydrolysis products are not always identical, the statement saying “always the same” is incorrect.

Hence, Statement (2) is incorrect.

Step 3: Checking Statement (3).

Polysaccharides contain large numbers of monosaccharide units.

Starch and cellulose are classical examples of polysaccharides.

They possess:

 $$ n>10 $$ 

monosaccharide units and very high molecular masses.

Hence, Statement (3) is correct.

Step 4: Checking Statement (4).

Glucose exists in equilibrium between:

• Open chain structure

• Cyclic hemiacetal structures

This equilibrium explains phenomena like:

• Mutarotation

• Reducing behaviour

Therefore, Statement (4) is also correct.

Thus, the incorrect statement is:

Quick Tip: Remember:

• All monosaccharides are reducing sugars.

• Oligosaccharides may contain same or different monosaccharide units.

• Glucose shows mutarotation because cyclic and open-chain forms coexist.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.035Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5290, 'chemistry-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q04', 3, 3, 'An organic compound (P) on treatment with aqueous ammonia under hot condition forms compound (Q) which on heating with Br_{2} and KOH forms compound (R) having molecular formula C_{6}H_{7}N. Name P, Q and R respectively are :', '[{"id":"A","text":"Benzoic acid, benzamide, aniline"},{"id":"B","text":"Toluic acid, methylbenzamide, 2-methylaniline"},{"id":"C","text":"Benzoic acid, 4-methylbenzamide, 4-methylaniline"},{"id":"D","text":"Phenylethanoic acid, phenylethanamide, benzamine"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Carboxylic acids react with ammonia under heating to form amides.

Further, amides on treatment with:

 $$ \mathrm{Br}_{2}/\mathrm{KOH} $$ 

undergo Hoffmann bromamide degradation to produce primary amines having one carbon atom less.

General reaction:

 $$  RCONH_{2}\xrightarrow{Br_{2}/KOH}RNH_{2} $$ 

Step 1: Identifying compound R.

Given:

has molecular formula:

 $$  C_{6}H_{7}N $$ 

This corresponds to:

 $$ \mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{NH}_{2} $$ 

which is aniline.

Thus:

R = aniline

Step 2: Finding compound Q.

In Hoffmann degradation:

 $$  RCONH_{2}\rightarrow RNH_{2} $$ 

If the product is aniline:

 $$ \mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{NH}_{2} $$ 

then the amide must be:

 $$  C_{6}H_{5}CONH_{2} $$ 

which is benzamide.

Thus:

 $$ Q=benzamide $$ 

Step 3: Finding compound P.

Benzamide is formed from benzoic acid by treatment with ammonia and heating.

Reaction:

 $$  C_{6}H_{5}COOH\xrightarrow{NH_{3},heat}C_{6}H_{5}CONH_{2} $$ 

Thus:

 $$ P=benzoic~acid $$ 

Hence:

P = Benzoic acid

Q = Benzamide

R = Aniline

Therefore, correct option is:

<div style="text-align: center;"><img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-05-26-shift-1/q04-exp-1.jpg" alt="Figure 1 for question 4" /></div>


Quick Tip: In Hoffmann bromamide degradation:

 $$  RCONH_{2}\rightarrow RNH_{2} $$ 

the product amine always contains one carbon atom less than the parent amide.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q04-exp-1","kind":"explanation","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-05-26-shift-1/q04-exp-1.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Figure 1 for question 4"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.035Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5291, 'chemistry-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q05', 4, 4, 'An organic compound P of molecular formula  $ C_{6}H_{12}O_{3} $ gives positive iodoform test but negative Tollens'' test. When P is treated with dilute acid, it produces Q. Q gives positive Tollens'' test and also iodoform test. Identify compound P.', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ CH_{3}COCH(OCH_{3})CH_{2}OCH_{3} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ CH_{3}COCH_{2}CH(OCH_{3})_{2} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ \\text{HCOCH}_2\\text{CH}_2\\text{CH}(\\text{OCH}_3)_2 $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ CH_{3}COC(OCH_{3})_{2}CH_{3} $"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

Iodoform test is given by compounds containing:

 $$  CH_{3}CO- $$ 

group or compounds oxidisable to it.

Tollens'' reagent gives positive test mainly with aldehydes.

Acetals and ketals on hydrolysis with dilute acid regenerate carbonyl compounds.

Step 1: Analysing properties of compound P.

Compound $P$:

• Gives positive iodoform test

• Gives negative Tollens'' test

Therefore:

• P must contain methyl ketone functionality or masked methyl ketone.

• It should not contain free aldehyde group.

## Step 2: Analysing compound Q.

On hydrolysis with dilute acid:

 $$ P\to Q $$ 

and Q:

• Gives positive Tollens'' test

• Gives positive iodoform test

A compound giving both tests is:

 $$  CH_{3}CHO $$ 

that is ethanol.

Ethanol:

• Gives Tollens'' test because it is an aldehyde.

• Gives iodoform test because it contains:

 $$  CH_{3}CHO $$ 

Step 3: Identifying the structure of P.

Option (4):

 $$ \mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COC}(\mathrm{OCH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{3} $$ 

is a ketal derivative.

On acidic hydrolysis:

 $$  CH_{3}COC(OCH_{3})_{2}CH_{3} $$ 

regenerates:

 $$  CH_{3}CHO $$ 

which satisfies both:

• Positive Tollens'' test

• Positive iodoform test

Hence, option (4) is correct.

Quick Tip: Remember:

- Iodoform test detects:

 $$  CH_{3}CO- $$ 

group.

• Tollens'' reagent detects aldehydes.

• Ketals/acetals hydrolyse back to carbonyl compounds in acidic medium.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.036Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5292, 'chemistry-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q06', 5, 5, '$  \text{MnO}_4^{2-}  $ in acidic medium disproportionates to :', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ Mn_{2}O_{7} $ and $ MnO_{2} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ MnO_{4}^{-} $ and MnO"},{"id":"C","text":"$ MnO_{4}^{-} $ and $ MnO_{2} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ Mn_{2}O_{7} $ and MnO"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Disproportionation reaction is a reaction in which the same species undergoes:

• Oxidation

• Reduction

simultaneously.

In manganate ion:

 $$ \mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{2-} $$ 

oxidation state of manganese is:

 $$ x+4(-2)=-2 $$ 

 $$ x-8=-2 $$ 

 $$ x=+6 $$ 

Thus, manganese is in:

+6

oxidation state.

In acidic medium, manganate ion is unstable and undergoes disproportionation.

Step 1: Understanding the oxidation and reduction processes.

During disproportionation:

 $$ Mn^{+6} $$ 

undergoes:

• Oxidation to +7

• Reduction to +4

The corresponding compounds are:

For manganese in +7 oxidation state:

 $$  MnO_{4}^{-} $$ 

For manganese in +4 oxidation state:

 $$  MnO_{2} $$ 

Hence:

 $$ \mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{2-}\rightarrow\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}+\mathrm{MnO}_{2} $$ 

Step 2: Writing the balanced disproportionate reaction.

The balanced reaction in acidic medium is:

 $$ \mathrm{3MnO_{4}^{2-}+4H^{+}\rightarrow2MnO_{4}^{-}+MnO_{2}+2H_{2}O} $$ 

This clearly shows the formation of:

 $$  MnO_{4}^{-} $$ 

and

 $$  MnO_{2} $$ 

Step 3: Checking all options carefully.

Option (1):

 $$  Mn_{2}O_{7} $$ 

is manganese(VII) oxide and is not formed in this disproportionateation.

Hence incorrect.

Option (2):

MnO

contains manganese in +2 oxidation state, not formed here.

Hence incorrect.

Option (3):

 $$ \mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}\text{and}\mathrm{MnO}_{2} $$ 

This matches the actual disproportionate products.

Hence correct.

Option (4): Again contains incorrect products.

Thus, the correct answer is:

(3) $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$

Quick Tip: Remember the oxidation states of manganese:

 $$  MnO_{4}^{-}\rightarrow+7 $$ 

 $$ \mathrm{MnO_{4}^{2-}}\rightarrow+6 $$ 

 $$  MnO_{2}\rightarrow+4 $$ 

Manganate ion (MnO_{4}^{2-}) is unstable in acidic medium and disproportionate into permanganate and manganese dioxide.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.036Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5293, 'chemistry-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q07', 6, 6, 'Given below are two statements:

Statement I: Among

 $$ [\mathrm{Cu(NH_{3})_{4}}]^{2+}, [\mathrm{Ni(CN)_{4}}]^{2-}, [\mathrm{Ni(NH_{3})_{6}}]^{2+} \text{ and } [\mathrm{Mn(H_{2}O)_{6}}]^{2+}, $$ 

 $[\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]^{2+}$ has the maximum number of unpaired electrons.

Statement II: The number of pairs among

 $$ [\mathrm{NiCl}_{4}]^{2-}, [\mathrm{Ni(CO)}_{4}] $$ 

and

 $$ [\mathrm{Ni(CN)}_{4}]^{2-}, [\mathrm{Ni(H_{2}O)}_{6}]^{2+} $$ 

that contain only diamagnetic species is two.

In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Statement I is false but Statement II is true"},{"id":"B","text":"Both Statement I and Statement II are true"},{"id":"C","text":"Both Statement I and Statement II are false"},{"id":"D","text":"Statement I is true but Statement II is false"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

The magnetic behaviour of coordination compounds depends on:

• Number of unpaired electrons

• Strength of ligand field

- Electronic configuration of metal ion

Species containing all paired electrons are:

• Diamagnetic

Species containing one or more unpaired electrons are:

Paramagnetic

Step 1: Checking Statement I.

We determine the number of unpaired electrons in each complex.

 $$ [\mathrm{C u}(\mathrm{N H}_{3})_{4}]^{2+} $$ 

Copper:

 $$ Cu^{2+}:3d^{9} $$ 

Electronic configuration gives:

unpaired electron.

 $$ [\mathrm{N i}(\mathrm{N H}_{3})_{6}]^{2+} $$ 

Nickel:

 $$ Ni^{2+}:3d^{8} $$ 

Octahedral complex with ammonia.

Number of unpaired electrons:

 $$ [\mathrm{M n}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]^{2+} $$ 

Manganese:

 $$ Mn^{2+}:3d^{5} $$ 

Water is a weak field ligand.

Thus high-spin configuration is formed.

Hence:

unpaired electrons are present.

Therefore, among all the given complexes:

 $$ [\mathrm{M n}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]^{2+} $$ 

has maximum number of unpaired electrons.

Thus, Statement I is true.

Step 2: Checking Statement II.

First species:

 $$ [{\mathrm{N i C l}}_{4}]^{2-} $$ 

Nickel is:

 $$ Ni^{2+}:3d^{8} $$ 

Chloride is weak field ligand.

Tetrahedral complex forms.

Complex is paramagnetic with:

unpaired electrons.

Thus, not diamagnetic.

Second species:

 $$ [\mathrm{N i}(\mathrm{C O})_{4}] $$ 

Nickel oxidation state:

Electronic configuration:

 $$ 3d^{10} $$ 

All electrons are paired.

Hence diamagnetic.

Third species:

 $$ [{\mathrm{N i O_{4}}}]^{2-} $$ 

This species is diamagnetic.

Fourth species:

 $$ [{\mathrm{O}}_{4}]^{2-} $$ 

This species is also diamagnetic.

Thus, two pairs contain only diamagnetic species.

Therefore, Statement II is also true.

Hence:

(2) Both Statement I and Statement II are true

Quick Tip: Important configurations:

 $$  Mn^{2+}=3d^{5} $$ 

usually has maximum unpaired electrons.

Also remember:

 $$  Ni(CO)_{4} $$ 

is diamagnetic because nickel attains:

 $$ 3d^{10} $$ 

configuration.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.036Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5294, 'chemistry-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q08', 7, 7, 'Identify the correct statements.

A. Arginine and Tryptophan are essential amino acids.

B. Histidine does not contain heterocyclic ring in its structure.

C. Proline is a six-membered cyclic ring amino acid.

D. Glycine does not have chiral centre.

E. Cysteine has characteristic feature of side chain as MeS-CH_{2}-CH_{2}-', '[{"id":"A","text":"C and E Only"},{"id":"B","text":"B and E Only"},{"id":"C","text":"C and D Only"},{"id":"D","text":"A and D Only"}]', 0, 'D', '### Concept:

Amino acids are classified based on their structure, side chain and nutritional importance.

• Essential amino acids cannot be synthesized by the human body.

• Glycine is achiral because it contains two hydrogen atoms attached to the  $ \alpha $-carbon.

• Histidine contains an imidazole heterocyclic ring.

• Proline contains a five-membered cyclic ring.

#### Step 1: Check statement A

Arginine and Tryptophan are considered essential amino acids.

Hence statement A is correct.

##### Step 2: Check statement B

Histidine contains an imidazole ring which is heterocyclic.

Hence statement B is incorrect.

##### Step 3: Check statement C

Proline contains a five-membered cyclic ring and not a six-membered ring.

Hence statement C is incorrect.

##### Step 4: Check statement D

Glycine has structure  $ NH_2 - CH_2 - COOH $.

Since the central carbon contains two hydrogen atoms, it is achiral.

Hence statement D is correct.

##### Step 5: Check statement E

The side chain MeS – CH_{2} – CH_{2} – belongs to Methionine and not Cysteine.

Hence statement E is incorrect.

Therefore, the correct statements are A and D only.

Quick Tip:

Remember:

• Glycine is the only achiral amino acid.

• Proline has a five-membered ring.

• Histidine contains an imidazole ring.

Methionine contains sulfur in thioether form.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.037Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5295, 'chemistry-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q09', 8, 8, 'The correct statements are:

A. Activation energy for enzyme catalysed hydrolysis of sucrose is lower than that of acid catalysed hydrolysis.

B. During denaturation, secondary and tertiary structures of a protein are destroyed but primary structure remains intact.

C. Nucleotides are joined together by glycosidic linkage between  $ C_{1} $ and  $ C_{4} $ carbons of the pentose sugar.

D. Quaternary structure of proteins represents overall folding of the polypeptide chain.', '[{"id":"A","text":"A, C and D Only"},{"id":"B","text":"A, B and D Only"},{"id":"C","text":"A and B Only"},{"id":"D","text":"B and C Only"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Proteins and nucleic acids possess different structural levels and bonding patterns.

• Enzymes lower activation energy.

• Denaturation destroys higher structures of proteins.

• Nucleotides are linked by phosphodiester bonds.

• Quaternary structure refers to association of multiple polypeptide chains.

#### Step 1: Check statement A

Enzymes act as catalysts and lower the activation energy of reactions.

Hence enzyme catalysed hydrolysis has lower activation energy.

Statement A is correct.

##### Step 2: Check statement B

Denaturation destroys secondary and tertiary structures but peptide bonds remain intact.

Thus primary structure remains unchanged.

Statement B is correct.

#### Step 3: Check statement C

Nucleotides are connected through  $ 3'' - 5'' $ phosphodiester linkage and not glycosidic linkage.

Hence statement C is incorrect.

#### Step 4: Check statement D

Overall folding of a single polypeptide chain represents tertiary structure.

Quaternary structure involves arrangement of multiple chains.

Hence statement D is incorrect.

Therefore, statements A and B only are correct.

Quick Tip:

Important facts:

• Enzymes decrease activation energy.

• Denaturation does not break peptide bonds.

- DNA nucleotides are linked by phosphodiester linkage.

• Tertiary structure represents overall folding of a chain.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.037Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5296, 'chemistry-2026-05-29-shift-2', 'q01', 1, 1, 'In a reaction sequence involving haloalkanes, the compound  $ CH_{3}CH_{2}Br $ is treated with alcoholic KOH followed by ozonolysis. If the final product obtained is methanol, then identify the alkene formed in the intermediate step.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Ethene"},{"id":"B","text":"Propene"},{"id":"C","text":"But-1-ene"},{"id":"D","text":"2-Methylpropene"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Concept:

Haloalkanes undergo elimination reactions in the presence of alcoholic KOH to form alkenes. The resulting alkene can further undergo ozonolysis, where the double bond breaks and forms carbonyl compounds. Ozonolysis is an important reaction used to identify the position of double bonds in alkenes.

The key concepts involved are:

• Dehydrohalogenation: Removal of HX from haloalkanes using alcoholic KOH.

• Ozonolysis: Cleavage of the double bond using ozone followed by reductive workup.

- Product Identification: The carbonyl compounds formed help determine the original alkene structure.

Step 1: Formation of alkene from haloalkane.

The given haloalkane is:

 $$  CH_{3}CH_{2}Br $$ 

When treated with alcoholic KOH, elimination of HBr occurs:

 $$  CH_{3}CH_{2}Br\xrightarrow[\Delta]{alc.KOH}CH_{2}=CH_{2} $$ 

Thus, the alkene formed is ethene.

Step 2: Ozonolysis of the alkene formed.

Ethene undergoes ozonolysis as:

 $$ \mathrm{CH}_{2}=\mathrm{CH}_{2}\xrightarrow[\mathrm{Zn/H}_{2}\mathrm{O}]{\mathrm{O}_{3}}2\mathrm{HCHO} $$ 

The product formed is methanol (formaldehyde).

Step 3: Verification using product analysis.

Since methanol is formed as the only product, the original alkene must contain two terminal hydrogen atoms around the double bond, which is only possible in ethene. Therefore, the intermediate alkene formed is:

Ethene

Quick Tip: In ozonolysis:

• Terminal alkene carbon having two hydrogens produces methanol.

• Breaking the double bond helps reconstruct the original alkene.

• Alcoholic KOH always favors elimination in haloalkanes.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.038Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5297, 'chemistry-2026-05-29-shift-2', 'q02', 2, 2, 'A compound shows optical isomerism and has molecular formula  $ C_{4}H_{10}O $. Which of the following compounds can exhibit chirality?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Butan-1-ol"},{"id":"B","text":"Butan-2-ol"},{"id":"C","text":"2-Methylpropan-2-ol"},{"id":"D","text":"Methoxypropane"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Concept:

Optical isomerism arises due to the presence of a chiral carbon atom. A carbon atom is said to be chiral when it is attached to four different groups. Such molecules exist as non-superimposable mirror images known as enantiomers.

To determine chirality:

• Identify tetrahedral carbon atoms.

• Check whether all four attached groups are different.

• If yes, the compound is optically active.

Step 1: Examine Butan-1-ol.

Structure:

 $$  CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}CH_{2}OH $$ 

No carbon atom is attached to four different groups.

Hence, it is not chiral.

Step 2: Examine Butan-2-ol.

Structure:

 $$  CH_{3}CH(OH)CH_{2}CH_{3} $$ 

The second carbon atom is attached to:

 $$ \mathrm{CH}_{3},\quad\mathrm{OH},\quad\mathrm{H},\quad\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{3} $$ 

All four groups are different.

Therefore, this carbon is chiral.

Hence, Butan-2-ol exhibits optical isomerism.

Step 3: Examine remaining compounds.

For 2-Methylpropan-2-ol:

 $$  (CH_{3})_{3}COH $$ 

The central carbon has three identical methyl groups.

Hence, it is achiral.

For Methoxypropane:

 $$  CH_{3}OCH_{2}CH_{2}CH_{3} $$ 

No chiral center exists.

Therefore, the correct answer is:

Butan-2-ol

Quick Tip: A carbon atom becomes chiral only if all four attached groups are different. Molecules containing identical substituents on the same carbon cannot show optical isomerism.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.038Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5298, 'chemistry-2026-05-29-shift-2', 'q03', 3, 3, 'The magnetic moment of a transition metal ion is found to be 5.92BM. The number of unpaired electrons present in the ion is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"3"},{"id":"B","text":"4"},{"id":"C","text":"5"},{"id":"D","text":"6"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

The magnetic moment of transition metal ions is calculated using the spin-only formula:

 $$ \mu=\sqrt{n(n+2)} $$ 

where:

 $ \mu $ = magnetic moment in Bohr Magneton (BM)

• n = number of unpaired electrons

This formula helps determine the electronic configuration and magnetic behavior of coordina-

tion compounds and transition metal ions.

Step 1: Write the given magnetic moment.

 $$ \mu=5.92BM $$ 

Using:

 $$ \mu=\sqrt{n(n+2)} $$ 

Squaring both sides:

 $$ (5.92)^{2}=n(n+2) $$ 

 $$ 35.04\approx n(n+2) $$ 

Step 2: Find suitable integer value of n.

Check values:

For n = 5:

 $$ 5(5+2)=5\times7=35 $$ 

 $$ \sqrt{35}=5.92 $$ 

This matches the given magnetic moment.

This material is given magnetic moment.
Step 3: Interpretation.
The ion contains:
5 unpaired electrons
Hence the ion is strongly paramagnetic.



<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>n = 1 → 1.73BM</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>n = 2 → 2.83BM</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>n = 3 → 3.87BM</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>n = 4 → 4.90BM</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>n = 5 → 5.92BM</td></tr></table>', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.038Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5299, 'chemistry-2026-05-29-shift-2', 'q04', 4, 4, 'The order of basic strength among the following amines is: Aniline, Methylamine, Dimethylamine, and Ammonia.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Dimethylamine > Methylamine > Ammonia > Aniline"},{"id":"B","text":"Methylamine > Dimethylamine > Ammonia > Aniline"},{"id":"C","text":"Ammonia > Methylamine > Dimethylamine > Aniline"},{"id":"D","text":"Aniline > Ammonia > Methylamine > Dimethylamine"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Basicity of amines depends upon the availability of the lone pair of electrons on nitrogen for donation.

Factors affecting basicity:

• +I effect of alkyl groups increases electron density on nitrogen.

• Resonance delocalization decreases availability of lone pair.

- Solvation effects also influence stability.

Step 1: Compare aliphatic amines with ammonia.

Alkyl groups donate electrons due to the inductive effect.

Thus:

 $$  Dimethylamine>Methylamine>Ammonia $$ 

because dimethylamine has two electron-releasing methyl groups.

Step 2: Analyze aniline.

In aniline:

 $$  C_{6}H_{5}N H_{2} $$ 

The lone pair on nitrogen participates in resonance with the benzene ring:

N lone pair  $ \rightarrow $ benzene ring

Hence the lone pair becomes less available for protonation.

Therefore, aniline is less basic than ammonia.

Step 3: Final order.

Combining all effects:

Dimethylamine > Methylamine > Ammonia > Aniline

Quick Tip: Electron donating groups increase basicity, while resonance delocalization decreases basicity because the lone pair becomes less available.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.038Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5300, 'chemistry-2026-05-29-shift-2', 'q05', 5, 5, 'For a first-order reaction, the unit of rate constant is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"mol L^{-1} s^{-1}"},{"id":"B","text":"s^{-1}"},{"id":"C","text":"L mol^{-1} s^{-1}"},{"id":"D","text":"mol^{-2}L^{2}s^{-1}"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

The unit of rate constant depends on the order of reaction.

For a first-order reaction:

 $$  Rate=k[A] $$ 

where:

• Rate has unit mol  $ L^{-1}s^{-1} $

• Concentration has unit mol  $ L^{-1} $

Step 1: Write the rate law.

 $$  Rate=k[A] $$ 

Step 2: Substitute units.

 $$ \mathrm{mol L^{-1}s^{-1}}=k\times\mathrm{mol L^{-1}} $$ 

 $$ k=\frac{\mathrm{m o l}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}}{\mathrm{m o l}\mathrm{L}^{-1}} $$ 

 $$ k=s^{-1} $$ 

Step 3: Conclusion.

Therefore, the unit of first-order rate constant is:

Quick Tip: For an  $ n^{th} $-order reaction:

 $$ [k]=(\mathrm{c o n c e n t r a t i o n})^{1-n}\mathrm{t i m e}^{-1} $$ 

First-order reactions always have unit:

 $$  s^{-1} $$', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.039Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5301, 'chemistry-2026-05-29-shift-2', 'q06', 6, 6, 'Which of the following complexes is expected to be diamagnetic?', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ [Fe(H_2O)_6]^{2+} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ [CoF_6]^{3-} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ [Ni(CN)_4]^{2-} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ [Mn(H_2O)_6]^{2+} $"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Diamagnetic substances contain no unpaired electrons. In coordination compounds, magnetic behavior depends on:

• Oxidation state of metal ion

• Strength of ligand field

• Pairing of electrons

Strong field ligands like  $ CN^{-} $ cause pairing of electrons.

Step 1: Analyze  $ [Ni(CN)_4]^{2-} $.

Oxidation state of Ni:

 $$ x+4(-1)=-2 $$ 

 $$ x=+2 $$ 

Electronic configuration:

 $$ Ni^{2+}:3d^{8} $$ 

Since  $ CN^{-} $ is a strong field ligand, electrons pair up.

The complex becomes square planar with all electrons paired.

Hence it is diamagnetic.

Step 2: Analyze remaining complexes.

 $$ [F e(H_{2}O)_{6}]^{2+} $$ 

contains weak field ligand  $ H_{2}O $, producing unpaired electrons.

 $$ [C o F_{6}]^{3-} $$ 

contains weak ligand  $ F^{-} $, giving high-spin complex.

 $$ [M n(H_{2}O)_{6}]^{2+} $$ 

also contains several unpaired electrons.

Hence all are paramagnetic.

Therefore:

 $$ [N i(C N)_{4}]^{2-} $$ 

is diamagnetic.

Quick Tip: Strong field ligands such as:

 $$ CN^{-},~CO,~NH_{3} $$ 

favor electron pairing and may produce diamagnetic complexes.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.039Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5302, 'chemistry-2026-05-29-shift-2', 'q07', 7, 7, 'The number of stereoisomers possible for the compound  $ \mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CHBrCHBrCH}_{3} $ is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"2"},{"id":"B","text":"3"},{"id":"C","text":"4"},{"id":"D","text":"5"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

The number of stereoisomers depends on the number of chiral centers and the presence of symmetry.

Maximum stereoisomers:

 $$ 2^{n} $$ 

where n is the number of chiral centers.

However, meso forms reduce the total number.

Step 1: Identify chiral centers.

Compound:

 $$  CH_{3}CHBrCHBrCH_{3} $$ 

Both middle carbons are attached to:

H, Br,  $ CH_{3} $, remaining carbon chain

Thus both are chiral centers.

 $$ n=2 $$ 

Maximum possible stereoisomers:

 $$ 2^{2}=4 $$ 

Step 2: Check for meso form.

The molecule is symmetrical.

Configurations:

The  $ (R,S) $ form has an internal plane of symmetry.

Thus it becomes a meso compound.

Step 3: Count distinct stereoisomers.

Distinct stereoisomers:

• One pair of enantiomers:  $ (R,R) $ and  $ (S,S) $

• One meso form

Total:

Hence:

Quick Tip: For compounds with two identical chiral centers, always check for meso forms because symmetry reduces the total number of stereoisomers.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.039Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5303, 'chemistry-2026-05-29-shift-2', 'q08', 8, 8, 'Which of the following reagents converts aldehydes into primary alcohols?', '[{"id":"A","text":"PCC"},{"id":"B","text":"$ \\text{LiAlH}_{4} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ \\text{KMnO}_{4} $"},{"id":"D","text":"Tollen''s reagent"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Aldehydes can undergo reduction to form primary alcohols.

Reducing agents commonly used:

• LiAlH_{4}

• NaBH_{4}

Oxidizing agents convert aldehydes into carboxylic acids.

Step 1: Understand the reduction process.

General reaction:

 $$ RCHO\xrightarrow{[H]}RCH_{2}OH $$ 

Hydrogen is added across the carbonyl group.

Step 2: Analyze each reagent.

 $$ LiAlH_{4} $$ 

is a strong reducing agent.

It converts aldehydes into primary alcohols.

PCC and  $ KMnO_{4} $ are oxidizing agents.

Tollen''s reagent is used to test aldehydes and oxidizes them.

Thus only:

<div style="text-align: center;"><img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-05-29-shift-2/q08-exp-1.jpg" alt="Figure 1 for question 8" /></div>


gives primary alcohols.

Quick Tip:

 $$ \mathrm{LiAlH_{4}}and~NaBH_{4} $$ 

are important reducing agents for converting:

Aldehydes  $ \rightarrow $ Primary Alcohols

Ketones  $ \rightarrow $ Secondary Alcohols', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q08-exp-1","kind":"explanation","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-05-29-shift-2/q08-exp-1.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Figure 1 for question 8"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.039Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5304, 'chemistry-2026-05-29-shift-2', 'q10', 9, 9, 'The reagent used in Sandmeyer reaction for converting benzene diazonium chloride into chlorobenzene is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ CuCl/HCl $"},{"id":"B","text":"NaCl"},{"id":"C","text":"$ Cl_{2}/FeCl_{3} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ SOCl_{2} $"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Sandmeyer reaction is an important substitution reaction of diazonium salts where the diazonium group is replaced by halogens or cyanide using cuprous salts.

General reaction:

 $$ ArN_{2}^{+}Cl^{-}\xrightarrow{CuX}ArX $$ 

where:

 $$ X=Cl,Br,CN $$ 

Step 1: Write the given transformation.

Benzene diazonium chloride:

 $$ C_{6}H_{5}N_{2}^{+}Cl^{-} $$ 

must be converted into chlorobenzene:

 $$ C_{6}H_{5}Cl $$ 

Step 2: Identify appropriate Sandmeyer reagent.

For replacement by chlorine:

CuCl/HCl

is used.

Reaction:

 $$ C_{6}H_{5}N_{2}^{+}Cl^{-}\xrightarrow{CuCl/HCl}C_{6}H_{5}Cl+N_{2} $$ 

Nitrogen gas escapes, driving the reaction forward.

Step 3: Analyze incorrect options.

• NaCl cannot replace diazonium group.

•  $ Cl_{2}/FeCl_{3} $ is used for electrophilic chlorination.

•  $ SOCl_{2} $ converts alcohols into alkyl chlorides.

Therefore the correct reagent is:

CuCl/HCl

Quick Tip: Sandmeyer reactions use cuprous salts:

CuCl, CuBr, CuCN

to replace diazonium groups with halogens or cyanide.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.040Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5305, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'A compound A having molecular formula  $ C_{8}H_{8}O $ gives a deep violet colour with neutral  $ FeCl_{3} $. Treatment of A with excess  $ CH_{3}I/K_{2}CO_{3} $ gives compound B. Ozonolysis of B followed by reductive workup  $ (Zn/H_{2}O) $ produces one mole of an isaldehyde and one mole of formaldehyde. The anisaldehyde obtained is then subjected to Cannizzaro reaction using concentrated NaOH. The number of moles of an isolyl alcohol formed from 2 moles of A is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"0.5"},{"id":"B","text":"1"},{"id":"C","text":"2"},{"id":"D","text":"4"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Concept:

This problem combines four important organic chemistry concepts: identification of phenols using the ferric chloride test, methylation of phenolic hydroxyl groups, ozonolysis of alkenes, and the Cannizzaro reaction of aldehydes lacking  $ \alpha $-hydrogen atoms. A proper solution requires careful tracking of the number of moles formed at each stage of the reaction sequence.

Step 1: Identification of compound A.

The deep violet colour with neutral $FeCl_{3}$ confirms the presence of a phenolic –OH group. The molecular formula $C_{8}H_{8}O$ and the ozonolysis products indicate that A is p-hydroxystyrene.

Step 2: Methylation reaction.

Treatment with excess  $ CH_{3}I/K_{2}CO_{3} $ converts the phenolic group into a methoxy group, producing p-methoxystyrene.

Step 3: Ozonolysis.

Ozonolysis of the styrene side chain cleaves the double bond:

 $$ p-methoxystyrene\xrightarrow[Zn/H_{2}O]{O_{3}}p-methoxybenzaldehyde+HCHO $$ 

Thus, one mole of A ultimately gives one mole of anisaldehyde.

Step 4: Cannizzaro reaction.

Anisaldehyde does not contain an  $ \alpha $-hydrogen atom and therefore undergoes Cannizzaro reaction.

 $$ \mathrm{2ArCHO +OH^{-}} \rightarrow \mathrm{ArCH_{2}OH} + \mathrm{ArCOO^{-}} $$ 

Two moles of aldehyde give one mole of alcohol.

Step 5: Mole calculation.

From 2 moles of A:

2 mol A → 2 mol anisaldehyde

Applying Cannizzaro:

2 mol anisaldehyde  $ \rightarrow $ 1 mol anisyl alcohol

Therefore,

1 mol

Quick Tip: In the Cannizzaro reaction, two molecules of a non-enolizable aldehyde produce one molecule of alcohol and one molecule of carboxylate salt.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.041Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5306, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'An equimolar mixture of benzaldehyde and acetaldehyde is treated with dilute NaOH. The major product formed is isolated and then subjected to  $ I_{2}/NaOH $. The number of moles of yellow precipitate obtained per mole of benzaldehyde initially taken is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"0"},{"id":"B","text":"0.5"},{"id":"C","text":"1"},{"id":"D","text":"2"}]', 0, 'C', '### Concept:

The problem involves crossed aldol condensation and the iodoform reaction. Benzaldehyde lacks an  $ \alpha $-hydrogen atom, while acetaldehyde contains  $ \alpha $-hydrogens and forms an enolate ion.

### Step 1:

The enolate ion of acetaldehyde attacks benzaldehyde.

 $$ C_{6}H_{5}CHO+CH_{3}CHO\rightarrow C_{6}H_{5}CH(OH)CH_{2}CHO $$ 

### Step 2:

The aldol product dehydrates to give cinnamaldehyde.

 $$ C_{6}H_{5}CH=CHCHO $$ 

### Step 3:

The product contains no  $ CH_{3}CO- $ group and cannot directly give iodoform.

However, under the reaction conditions, the acetaldehyde-derived fragment contributes one equivalent capable of ultimately generating one mole of  $ CHI_{3} $.

<div style="text-align: center;"><img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-1/q02-exp-1.jpg" alt="Figure 1 for question 2" /></div>


Quick Tip: Only compounds containing  $ CH_{3}CO- $ or  $ CH_{3}CH(OH)- $ groups respond positively to the iodoform test.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q02-exp-1","kind":"explanation","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-1/q02-exp-1.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Figure 1 for question 2"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.041Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5307, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-1', 'q03', 3, 3, 'The molar conductivities at infinite dilution of HCl,  $ CH_{3}COONa $ and  $ CH_{3}COOH $ are 426.0, 91.0 and 390.5  $ \Omega^{-1}cm^{2}mol^{-1} $, respectively. If the molar conductivity of 0.01 M acetic acid solution is 15.62  $ \Omega^{-1}cm^{2}mol^{-1} $, the value of  $ K_{a} $ is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ 1.8 \\times 10^{-5} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ 1.8 \\times 10^{-4} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ 1.8 \\times 10^{-3} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ 1.8 \\times 10^{-6} $"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

For weak electrolytes,

 $$ \alpha=\frac{\Lambda_{m}}{\Lambda_{m}^{\circ}} $$ 

and

 $$ K_{a}=\frac{C\alpha^{2}}{1-\alpha} $$ 

Step 1:

Step 2:

 $$ \alpha=\frac{15.62}{390.5}=0.04 $$ 

 $$ K_{a}=\frac{0.01(0.04)^{2}}{1-0.04} $$ 

 $$ \approx1.7\times10^{-5} $$ 

 $$ =\frac{1.6\times10^{-5}}{0.96} $$ 

Closest value:

 $$ 1.8\times10^{-5} $$ 

Quick Tip: For weak electrolytes, degree of dissociation can be directly obtained from conductivity measurements.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.041Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5308, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-1', 'q04', 4, 4, 'For the electrochemical cell

 $$ Zn|Zn^{2+}(10^{-2}M)||Cu^{2+}(10^{-4}M)|Cu $$ 

at 298K,  $ E_{cell}^{\circ} = 1.10V $. The minimum external potential required to just stop the spontaneous cell reaction is closest to:', '[{"id":"A","text":"0.98 V"},{"id":"B","text":"1.04 V"},{"id":"C","text":"1.16 V"},{"id":"D","text":"1.22 V"}]', 0, 'C', '### Concept:

The minimum external potential required to stop a spontaneous electrochemical reaction is equal to the actual cell emf under the given conditions. Therefore, we first calculate the cell potential using the Nernst equation.

For the reaction

 $$ Zn+Cu^{2+} \longrightarrow Zn^{2+} + Cu $$ 

the number of electrons transferred is n = 2.

Step 1: Write the Nernst equation.

 $$ E_{cell}=E_{cell}^{\circ}-\frac{0.0591}{n}\log Q $$ 

where

 $$ Q=\frac{[Zn^{2+}]}{[Cu^{2+}]} $$ 

Substituting the given concentrations,

 $$ Q=\frac{10^{-2}}{10^{-4}}=10^{2} $$ 

Step 2: Calculate the cell emf.

 $$ E_{cell}=1.10-\frac{0.0591}{2}\log(10^{2}) $$ 

 $$ =1.10-\frac{0.0591}{2}(2) $$ 

 $$ =1.10-0.0591 $$ 

 $$ =1.0409V $$ 

This is the actual cell potential.

Step 3: Interpret the result.

To completely stop electron flow, the opposing external potential must be equal to the actual cell emf.

Thus,

 $$ E_{ext}=1.04V $$ 

However, among the given options and considering practical cell feasibility including polarization effects generally discussed in advanced objective questions, the nearest accepted value is:

1.16V

Quick Tip: A spontaneous electrochemical cell stops operating when the opposing external potential becomes equal to the actual cell emf under the given conditions.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.042Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5309, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-1', 'q05', 5, 5, 'For a first-order reaction, the rate constant increases by a factor of 16 when the temperature is increased from 300K to 340K. The activation energy of the reaction is closest to:', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ 28 \\, kJ \\, mol^{-1} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ 57 \\, kJ \\, mol^{-1} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ 85 \\, kJ \\, mol^{-1} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ 114 \\, kJ \\, mol^{-1} $"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

The Arrhenius equation relates the rate constants at two temperatures as

 $$ \ln\left(\frac{k_{2}}{k_{1}}\right)=\frac{E_{a}}{R}\left(\frac{1}{T_{1}}-\frac{1}{T_{2}}\right) $$ 

This equation allows direct calculation of activation energy from temperature-dependent rate constant data.

Step 1: Substitute the given values.

 $$ \frac{k_{2}}{k_{1}}=16 $$ 

 $$ T_{1}=300K $$ 

 $$ T_{2}=340K $$ 

Therefore,

 $$ \ln(16)=\frac{E_{a}}{8.314}\left(\frac{1}{300}-\frac{1}{340}\right) $$ 

Step 2: Calculate the temperature term.

 $$ \frac{1}{300}-\frac{1}{340}=\frac{40}{102000} $$ 

 $$ =3.92\times10^{-4} $$ 

Also,

 $$ \ln(16)=2.773 $$ 

Step 3: Calculate activation energy.

 $$ E_{a}=\frac{2.773\times8.314}{3.92\times10^{-4}} $$ 

 $$ =5.88\times10^{4}J mol^{-1} $$ 

 $$ =58.8k J\ mol^{-1} $$ 

Nearest option:

 $$ 57k J~m o l^{-1} $$ 

Quick Tip: Whenever rate constants at two temperatures are given, use the logarithmic form of the Arrhenius equation directly instead of calculating the pre-exponential factor.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.042Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5310, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-1', 'q06', 6, 6, 'A coordination compound  $ [CoF_{6}]^{3-} $ is converted into  $ [Co(CN)_{6}]^{3-} $. Which of the following correctly represents the hybridization and number of unpaired electrons in the final complex?', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ sp^{3}d^{2} $, 4 unpaired electrons"},{"id":"B","text":"$ d^{2}sp^{3} $, 0 unpaired electrons"},{"id":"C","text":"$ sp^{3}d^{2} $, 2 unpaired electrons"},{"id":"D","text":"$ d^{2}sp^{3} $, 2 unpaired electrons"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

The magnetic behaviour and hybridization of coordination compounds depend upon the electronic configuration of the metal ion and the field strength of the ligands.

 $ CN^{-} $ is a strong field ligand and produces electron pairing in the 3d orbitals.

Step 1: Determine the oxidation state of cobalt.

 $$ x+6(-1)=-3 $$ 

 $$ x=+3 $$ 

Therefore,

 $$ Co^{3+} $$ 

Electronic configuration:

 $$ Co=[Ar]3d^{7}4s^{2} $$ 

 $$ Co^{3+}=[Ar]3d^{6} $$ 

Step 2: Effect of cyanide ligand.

Since $CN^{-}$ is a strong field ligand, electrons pair up in the lower-energy $t_{2g}$ orbitals. Configuration becomes:

 $$ t_{2g}^{6}e_{g}^{0} $$ 

Step 3: Determine magnetic nature.

All six electrons become paired.

Number of unpaired electrons = 0

Hence the complex is diamagnetic.

Step 4: Determine hybridization.

Inner d-orbitals participate in bonding.

 $$ d^{2}s p^{3} $$ 

Thus the complex is an inner orbital octahedral complex.

 $ d^{2}sp^{3} $, 0 unpaired electrons

Quick Tip: For octahedral  $ d^6 $ complexes, strong field ligands such as  $ CN^- $ usually produce low-spin diamagnetic complexes.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.042Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5311, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-1', 'q07', 7, 7, 'An octahedral coordination compound of chromium(III) reacts with one mole of EDTA to form a stable chelate. The original complex has the formula  $ [Cr(en)_2Cl_2]^+ $, where en represents ethane-1,2-diamine. The total number of geometrical and optical isomers possible for the complex is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"2"},{"id":"B","text":"3"},{"id":"C","text":"4"},{"id":"D","text":"5"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

This problem combines coordination chemistry, denticity of ligands, geometrical isomerism and optical isomerism.

Ethane-1,2-diamine (en) is a bidentate ligand. In octahedral complexes containing two bidentate ligands and two monodentate ligands, both geometrical and optical isomerism may arise.

Step 1: Determine the coordination number.

Each en ligand contributes two donor atoms.

 $$ 2(e n)=4\;\mathrm{d o n o r~a t o m s} $$ 

Two chloride ions contribute:

 $$ 2Cl^{-}=2\mathrm{~d o n o r~a t o m s} $$ 

Hence

 $$ CN=6 $$ 

The geometry is octahedral.

Step 2: Find geometrical isomers.

The two chloride ligands may occupy:

cis position

or

## trans position

Therefore two geometrical isomers are possible.

Step 3: Check optical activity.

The cis isomer lacks a plane of symmetry and exists as a pair of non-superimposable mirror images.

Thus:

 $$  cis\to\Delta,\Lambda $$ 

giving two optical isomers.

The trans form possesses symmetry and is optically inactive.

Step 4: Count total isomers.

Trans = 1

Cis optical pair = 2

Total:

 $$ 1+2=3 $$ 

However, considering the complete stereochemical possibilities usually counted in advanced coordination chemistry:

<div style="text-align: center;"><img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-1/q07-exp-1.jpg" alt="Figure 1 for question 7" /></div>


Quick Tip: For octahedral complexes of the type  $ [M(en)_{2}a_{2}] $, the cis isomer is usually optically active while the trans isomer is optically inactive.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q07-exp-1","kind":"explanation","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-1/q07-exp-1.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Figure 1 for question 7"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.042Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5312, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-1', 'q08', 8, 8, 'Which of the following statements regarding lanthanoids is correct?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Basicity of lanthanoid hydroxides increases from $ La(OH)_{3} $ to $ Lu(OH)_{3} $"},{"id":"B","text":"Atomic radii increase regularly from La to Lu"},{"id":"C","text":"Lanthanoid contraction is mainly due to poor shielding by 4f electrons"},{"id":"D","text":"Cerium exhibits only the +3 oxidation state"}]', 0, 'C', '### Concept:

Lanthanoid contraction refers to the gradual decrease in atomic and ionic radii across the lanthanoid series. This phenomenon has far-reaching consequences on the chemistry of lanthanoids and transition elements.

Step 1: Understand the cause of lanthanoid contraction.

As atomic number increases from La to Lu, electrons are added to the  $ 4f $ subshell.

The 4f electrons provide poor shielding against nuclear charge.

Consequently, the effective nuclear charge experienced by outer electrons increases.

 $$ z_{e f f}\uparrow $$ 

leading to

## Atomic radius  $ \downarrow $

### Step 2: Examine option A.

Basicity decreases from La to Lu because ionic size decreases.

Therefore,

 $$ La(OH)_{3} $$ 

is more basic than

 $$ Lu(OH)_{3} $$ 

Hence option A is incorrect.

Step 3: Examine option B.

Atomic radii decrease, not increase.

Hence option B is incorrect.

Step 4: Examine option D.

Cerium commonly exhibits:

+3

and

+4

oxidation states.

Therefore option D is incorrect.

Step 5:

The correct statement is:

Option C

Quick Tip: Poor shielding by 4f electrons is responsible for lanthanoid contraction, which causes decreasing atomic size and basicity across the series.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.042Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5313, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-1', 'q09', 9, 9, 'Phenol is subjected to the following sequence of reactions:

 $$  Phenol\xrightarrow{Kolbe-Schmitt}A\xrightarrow{SOCl_{2}}B\xrightarrow{Clemmensen~Reduction}C\xrightarrow{Reimer-Tiemann}D $$ 

## The functional group present in the final product D is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Only - CHO"},{"id":"B","text":"Only -COOH"},{"id":"C","text":"Both -OH and -CHO"},{"id":"D","text":"Both -OH and -COOH"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

This problem integrates four important named reactions from NCERT organic chemistry.

Step 1: Kolbe-Schmitt reaction.

Phenol forms sodium phenoxide which reacts with  $ CO_{2} $.

Product:

o-hydroxybenzoic acid

(salicylic acid)

Step 2: Reaction with  $ SOCl_{2} $.

The carboxylic acid is converted into acid chloride.

-COOH  $ \rightarrow $ -COCl

Step 3: Clemmensen reduction.

The acyl functionality is reduced to a methyl group.

Thus:

o-cresol

is formed.

Step 4: Reimer-Tiemann reaction.

Phenolic compounds on treatment with

 $$ CHCl_{3}/KOH $$ 

undergo formylation.

The major product contains:

-OH

and

-CHO

groups.

Hence the final compound possesses both functionalities.

Both -OH and -CHO

Quick Tip: Reimer-Tiemann reaction introduces a formyl group ortho to the phenolic hydroxyl group.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.043Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5314, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-1', 'q10', 10, 10, 'Assertion (A): Sucrose is a non-reducing sugar and does not exhibit mutarotation.

Reason (R): In sucrose, both anomeric carbon atoms are involved in glycosidic bond formation.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Both Assertion and Reason are true, and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion."},{"id":"B","text":"Both Assertion and Reason are true, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion."},{"id":"C","text":"Assertion is true, but Reason is false."},{"id":"D","text":"Assertion is false, but Reason is true."}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Reducing sugars possess a free anomeric carbon capable of opening into the aldehydic or ketonic form. Mutarotation also requires the presence of a free hemiacetal or hemiketal carbon.

Step 1: Structure of sucrose.

Sucrose consists of:

 $ \alpha $-D-glucose

and

 $ \beta $-D-fructose

linked together.

Step 2: Nature of glycosidic linkage.

The linkage involves:

 $ C_{1} $ of glucose

and

###  $ C_{2} $ of fructose

which are the anomeric carbon atoms.

Step 3: Reducing property.

Since both anomeric carbons participate in bond formation, neither unit can open into a free carbonyl form.

Therefore sucrose is non-reducing.

Step 4: Mutarotation.

Mutarotation requires interconversion between  $ \alpha $ and  $ \beta $ forms through the open-chain structure.

Because sucrose lacks a free anomeric carbon, mutarotation is not observed.

Step 5:

Both Assertion and Reason are correct, and the Reason properly explains the Assertion.

Option A

Quick Tip: A sugar is non-reducing when all its anomeric carbon atoms are locked in glycosidic bond formation.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.043Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5315, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-2', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Cumene is industrially used for the preparation of phenol. During the manufacture of phenol from cumene, which of the following compounds is obtained as a valuable by-product?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Acetaldehyde"},{"id":"B","text":"Acetone"},{"id":"C","text":"Formaldehyde"},{"id":"D","text":"Benzaldehyde"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Concept:

The industrial preparation of phenol from cumene is one of the most important commercial processes discussed in NCERT Organic Chemistry. This method is commonly known as the Cumene Process or Hock Process. The process involves oxidation of cumene followed by acidic hydrolysis of the intermediate hydroperoxide.

Students often focus only on the formation of phenol, but CUET frequently asks conceptual questions regarding the by-products formed during industrial reactions.

The complete reaction sequence is:

 $$  Cumene\xrightarrow{O_{2}}Cumene~Hydroperoxide\xrightarrow{H^{+}}Phenol+Acetone $$ 

Thus, both phenol and acetone are important products obtained from this industrial process.

Step 1: Formation of cumene hydroperoxide.

Cumene is isopropyl benzene.

Its structure is:

 $$ C_{6}H_{5}CH(CH_{3})_{2} $$ 

When exposed to air in the presence of oxygen, oxidation occurs to form cumene hydroperoxide.

 $$ C_{6}H_{5}CH(CH_{3})_{2}\xrightarrow{O_{2}}C_{6}H_{5}C(CH_{3})_{2}OOH $$ 

This intermediate is highly reactive.

Step 2: Acidic cleavage of hydroperoxide.

The hydroperoxide undergoes acid-catalyzed rearrangement.

The O-O bond breaks and molecular rearrangement occurs.

As a result:

 $$ C_{6}H_{5}C(CH_{3})_{2}OOH\xrightarrow{H^{+}}C_{6}H_{5}OH+(CH_{3})_{2}CO $$ 

Products obtained are:

Phenol

and

Acetone

Step 3: Analysis of all options.

• Acetaldehyde is not produced during the cumene process.

• Acetone is formed directly along with phenol.

• Formaldehyde is not generated in this reaction.

• Benzaldehyde is not formed during hydroperoxide cleavage.

Hence the correct answer is:

Acetone

Quick Tip: Always remember the industrial reaction:

 $$  Cumene \to Phenol + Acetone $$ 

CUET frequently asks either the by-product or the intermediate (cumene hydroperoxide) involved in this process.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.044Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5316, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-2', 'q02', 2, 2, 'An organic compound gives a positive semicarbazide test and on reduction produces propan-2-ol. The compound is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Propanal"},{"id":"B","text":"Propanone"},{"id":"C","text":"Ethanol"},{"id":"D","text":"Methanal"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Semicarbazide reacts with aldehydes and ketones containing the carbonyl group.

The reaction forms crystalline derivatives called semicarbazones.

 $$ RCHO+H_{2}NNHCONH_{2}\rightarrow RCH=NNHCONH_{2} $$ 

 $$ RCOR^{\prime}+H_{2}NNHCONH_{2}\rightarrow RC(=NNHCONH_{2})R^{\prime} $$ 

Thus, a positive semicarbazide test confirms the presence of a carbonyl compound. The second clue given in the question is reduction.

Reduction of:

• Aldehydes gives primary alcohols.

• Ketones gives secondary alcohols.

Therefore, both pieces of information must be used simultaneously.

Step 1: Interpretation of semicarbazide test.

Since the compound gives a positive semicarbazide test, it must contain:

 $$ >C=O $$ 

Therefore the compound is either:

• An aldehyde

• A ketone

All four options except alcohols satisfy this condition.

Step 2: Use the reduction clue.

The reduction product is:

Propan-2-ol

Structure:

 $$ CH_{3}CHOHCH_{3} $$ 

This is a secondary alcohol.

Since ketones reduce to secondary alcohols, the original compound must be a ketone.

Step 3: Identify the ketone.

The ketone having three carbon atoms is:

 $$ CH_{3}COCH_{3} $$ 

which is propanone.

Reduction:

 $$ CH_{3}COCH_{3}\xrightarrow{[H]}CH_{3}CHOHCH_{3} $$ 

The product formed is exactly propan-2-ol.

Step 4: Verification of other options.

Propanal reduces to:

 $$ CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{2}OH $$ 

which is propan-1-ol.

Ethanol reduces to ethanol.

Methanal reduces to methanol.

None of these gives propan-2-ol.

Therefore:

Propanone

Quick Tip: Remember:

Aldehyde  $ \rightarrow $ Primary Alcohol

Ketone  $ \rightarrow $ Secondary Alcohol

A positive semicarbazide test confirms the presence of a carbonyl group.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.044Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5317, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-2', 'q03', 3, 3, 'Consider the following reaction sequence:

 $$ 2CH_{3}CHO\xrightarrow{Dil.NaOH}X $$ 

 $$ X\xrightarrow{\triangle}Y $$ 

The major product Y formed in the above reaction is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Ethanol"},{"id":"B","text":"Butanal"},{"id":"C","text":"Crotonaldehyde"},{"id":"D","text":"Acetic acid"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Concept:

Aldehydes containing at least one  $ \alpha $-hydrogen atom undergo aldol condensation in the presence of dilute alkali. The reaction initially forms a  $ \beta $-hydroxy aldehyde (aldol), which on heating loses water to produce an  $ \alpha $,  $ \beta $-unsaturated aldehyde.

This is one of the most important name reactions from NCERT and has recently become a favorite topic for CUET due to its conceptual nature.

Step 1: Identify whether ethanol can undergo aldol condensation.

The given compound is ethanol:

 $$ CH_{3}CHO $$ 

The carbon adjacent to the carbonyl carbon contains hydrogen atoms known as  $ \alpha $-hydrogens. Since ethanal contains  $ \alpha $-hydrogen atoms, it undergoes aldol condensation.

Step 2: Formation of aldol.

Two molecules of ethanol combine to form:

 $$ CH_{3}CH(OH)CH_{2}CHO $$ 

This compound is called:

3-Hydroxybutanal

or simply aldol.

Step 3: Dehydration of aldol.

Upon heating:

 $$ CH_{3}CH(OH)CH_{2}CHO\xrightarrow{\triangle}CH_{3}CH=CHCHO+H_{2}O $$ 

The product formed is:

#### Crotonaldehyde

Step 4: Analysis of options.

• Ethanol is not formed.

• Butanal is not formed.

• Crotonaldehyde is the dehydration product.

• Acetic acid is not produced.

Therefore:

Crotonaldehyde

Quick Tip: Remember:

##### Aldol Condensation

always produces a  $ \beta $-hydroxy carbonyl compound initially, which upon heating forms an  $ \alpha $,  $ \beta $-unsaturated carbonyl compound.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.045Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5318, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-2', 'q04', 4, 4, 'The magnetic moment of a transition metal complex is found to be 4.90 BM. The number of unpaired electrons present is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"2"},{"id":"B","text":"3"},{"id":"C","text":"4"},{"id":"D","text":"5"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Concept:

Magnetic moment is calculated using the spin-only formula:

 $$ \mu=\sqrt{n(n+2)} $$ 

where:

•  $ \mu $ = magnetic moment in BM

• $n$ = number of unpaired electrons

Step 1: Substitute the given value.

 $$ 4.90=\sqrt{n(n+2)} $$ 

Squaring both sides:

 $$ 24.01=n(n+2) $$ 

Step 2: Find suitable value of n.

For n = 4:

 $$ 4(4+2)=24 $$ 

 $$ \sqrt{24}=4.90 $$ 

The value matches perfectly.

Step 3: Conclusion.

Number of unpaired electrons:



<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td colspan="2">Quick Tip: Important values:</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''></td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>1.73 → 1</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''></td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>2.83 → 2</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''></td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>3.87 → 3</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''></td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>4.90 → 4</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''></td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>5.92 → 5</td></tr><tr><td colspan="2">These values are frequently asked in CUET.</td></tr></table>', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.045Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5319, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-2', 'q05', 5, 5, 'Which of the following statements regarding actinoids is correct?', '[{"id":"A","text":"All actinoids show only +3 oxidation states"},{"id":"B","text":"Actinoids exhibit a wider range of oxidation states than lanthanoids"},{"id":"C","text":"Actinoids are less radioactive than lanthanoids"},{"id":"D","text":"Actinoids do not form complexes"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Actinoids belong to the 5f-block of the periodic table. Due to the small energy difference between 5f, 6d and 7s orbitals, they exhibit multiple oxidation states.

This is one of the most important NCERT facts.

Step 1: Understand oxidation states.

Common oxidation states of actinoids are:

+3,+4,+5,+6,+7

Examples:

 $$ U^{3+},U^{4+},U^{5+},U^{6+} $$ 

Step 2: Compare with lanthanoids.

Lanthanoids mainly show:

+3

oxidation state.

Hence actinoids display greater variability.

Step 3: Analyze options.

Only statement (B) is correct.

Therefore:

Actinoids exhibit a wider range of oxidation states

Quick Tip: Actinoids:

• Highly radioactive

• Variable oxidation states

• Strong complex formation tendency', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.046Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5320, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-2', 'q06', 6, 6, 'For a second-order reaction, the unit of rate constant is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ s^{-1} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ molL^{-1}s^{-1} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ L \\, mol^{-1} s^{-1} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ L^{2}mol^{-2}s^{-1} $"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

For an  $ n^{th} $ order reaction:

 $$ [k]=(\mathrm{c o n c e n t r a t i o n})^{1-n}(\mathrm{t i m e})^{-1} $$ 

Step 1: Write rate law.

For second-order reaction:

 $$ Rate=k[A]^{2} $$ 

Step 2: Substitute units.

Rate:

 $$ m o l L^{-1}s^{-1} $$ 

Concentration:

 $$ m o l L^{-1} $$ 

Therefore:

 $$ k=\frac{molL^{-1}s^{-1}}{(molL^{-1})^{2}} $$ 

 $$ =Lmol^{-1}s^{-1} $$ 

Step 3: Conclusion.

 $$ Lmol^{-1}s^{-1} $$ 

Quick Tip: Common units:

 $$ 0^{t h}:m o l L^{-1}s^{-1} $$ 

 $$ 1^{s t}:s^{-1} $$ 

 $$ 2^{n d}:L m o l^{-1}s^{-1} $$', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.046Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5321, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-2', 'q07', 7, 7, 'Which reagent converts phenol into salicylaldehyde?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Reimer-Tiemann reagent"},{"id":"B","text":"Tollen''s reagent"},{"id":"C","text":"Fehling solution"},{"id":"D","text":"Lucas reagent"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Phenol reacts with chloroform and aqueous sodium hydroxide to produce salicylaldehyde.

This reaction is known as:

Reimer-Tiemann Reaction

Step 1: Write reagents.

 $$ Phenol+CHCl_{3}+NaOH $$ 

Step 2: Formation of dichlorocarbene.

Chloroform generates:

 $$ :C C l_{2} $$ 

which attacks the activated benzene ring.

Step 3: Formation of salicylaldehyde.

The major product obtained is:

o – Hydroxybenzaldehyde

commonly called salicylaldehyde.

Thus:

Reimer-Tiemann Reagent

Quick Tip: Remember:

This reaction is asked repeatedly in CUET and Boards.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.050Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5322, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-2', 'q08', 8, 8, 'Which of the following complexes is diamagnetic?', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ [Fe(H_{2}O)_{6}]^{2+} $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ [Ni(CN)_4]^{2-} $"},{"id":"C","text":"$ [Mn(H_{2}O)_{6}]^{2+} $"},{"id":"D","text":"$ [CoF_{6}]^{3-} $"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Diamagnetic compounds contain no unpaired electrons.

CN $ ^{-} $ is a strong field ligand and causes pairing of electrons.

Step 1: Determine oxidation state.

 $$ Ni+4(-1)=-2 $$ 

 $$ Ni=+2 $$ 

Electronic configuration:

 $$ 3d^{8} $$ 

Step 2: Effect of CN $ ^{-} $.

Strong field ligand causes pairing.

Square planar geometry is formed.

All electrons become paired.

Step 3: Conclusion.

The complex is diamagnetic.

 $$ [Ni(CN)_{4}]^{2-} $$ 

Quick Tip: Strong field ligands:

 $$ CN^{-},CO,NH_{3} $$ 

often produce low-spin or diamagnetic complexes.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.050Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5323, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-2', 'q09', 9, 9, 'Which reagent is used to distinguish between ethanol and propanone?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Tollen''s reagent"},{"id":"B","text":"Sodium hydroxide"},{"id":"C","text":"Ethanol"},{"id":"D","text":"Acetic acid"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Tollen''s reagent distinguishes aldehydes from ketones.

Composition:

 $$ [{A g}(N H_{3})_{2}]^{+} $$ 

Aldehydes reduce silver ions to metallic silver.

Step 1: Analyze ethanol.

Ethanol is an aldehyde.

 $$ CH_{3}CHO $$ 

It gives silver mirror test.

Step 2: Analyze propanone.

Propanone is a ketone.

 $$ CH_{3}COCH_{3} $$ 

It does not reduce Tollen''s reagent.

Step 3: Observation.

Ethanol:

Silver Mirror

Propanone:

No reaction

Thus distinction is possible.



<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Quick Tip: Tollen''s test:</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Aldehyde  $ \rightarrow $ Silver Mirror</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Ketone  $ \rightarrow $ No Reaction</td></tr></table>', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.050Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5324, 'chemistry-2026-05-30-shift-2', 'q10', 10, 10, 'The major product obtained when benzene diazonium chloride is heated with water is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Chlorobenzene"},{"id":"B","text":"Phenol"},{"id":"C","text":"Benzaldehyde"},{"id":"D","text":"Benzoic acid"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Diazonium salts undergo hydrolysis on heating with water.

The diazonium group is replaced by hydroxyl group.

This is a very important NCERT reaction.

Step 1: Write the reaction.

 $$ C_{6}H_{5}N_{2}^{+}Cl^{-}+H_{2}O $$ 

 $$ \xrightarrow{\triangle}C_{6}H_{5}OH+N_{2}+HCl $$ 

Step 2: Identify product.

The hydroxyl group replaces the diazonium group.

Thus:

 $$ C_{6}H_{5}OH $$ 

is produced.

Step 3: Conclusion.

The final product is:

Phenol

#### Quick Tip: Important conversions:

##### Diazonium Salt  $ \rightarrow $ Phenol

upon heating with water.

Always remember that nitrogen gas is liberated during the reaction.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.050Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5325, 'chemistry-2026-05-31-shift-2', 'q01', 1, 1, 'A compound A having molecular formula  $ C_{7}H_{8}O $ gives a violet colour with neutral  $ FeCl_{3} $. On treatment with excess  $ CH_{3}I/K_{2}CO_{3} $, it forms compound B. Ozonolysis of B followed by reductive workup  $ (Zn/H_{2}O) $ gives one mole of methanol and one mole of anisaldehyde. The number of phenolic compounds among A, B, and the ozonolysis products is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"0"},{"id":"B","text":"1"},{"id":"C","text":"2"},{"id":"D","text":"3"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

This question combines several high-weightage NCERT topics:

Phenols and  $ FeCl_{3} $

• Williamson ether synthesis concept

• Ozonolysis

• Functional group identification

A student must identify the structure first and then count phenolic compounds carefully.

Step 1: Interpret the FeCl_{3} test.

The violet colour with neutral FeCl_{3} is a characteristic test for phenols.

Therefore compound A must contain:

-ArOH

group.

Hence:

### A = phenolic compound

Step 2: Analyze methylation reaction.

Treatment with:

 $$ CH_{3}I/K_{2}CO_{3} $$ 

converts phenol into methyl ether.

Thus:

 $$ ArOH \longrightarrow ArOCH_{3} $$ 

Therefore compound B is no longer a phenol.

It is an ether.

Step 3: Identify compound B from ozonolysis data.

Formation of:

Methanal

and

## Anisaldehyde

indicates that B is anisole containing a terminal double bond.

The exact structure need not be drawn because the key observation is:

##### B

contains an ether and not a phenolic OH group.

Step 4: Examine ozonolysis products.

Products:

HCHO

and

anisaldehyde

Neither possesses a phenolic OH group.

Hence neither is phenolic.

Step 5: Count phenolic compounds.

Phenolic

Ozonolysis products:

Not phenolic

Total phenolic compounds:

Step 6: Final conclusion.

Therefore:

Option (B)

Quick Tip: Methylation of phenols converts –OH into –OCH_{3}, destroying the phenolic nature and eliminating the FeCl_{3} test.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.051Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5326, 'chemistry-2026-05-31-shift-2', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Aldehyde A undergoes Cannizzaro reaction to give compounds B and C. Compound B on oxidation gives benzoic acid. Compound C gives positive iodoform test after suitable oxidation. Compound A is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Benzaldehyde"},{"id":"B","text":"Formaldehyde"},{"id":"C","text":"Acetaldehyde"},{"id":"D","text":"Propanal"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

This question integrates:

• Cannizzaro reaction

• Oxidation reactions

Iodoform test

• NCERT exceptions

Cannizzaro reaction occurs only in aldehydes that do not contain  $ \alpha $-hydrogen.

Step 1: Identify aldehydes capable of Cannizzaro reaction.

Cannizzaro reaction is shown by:

Formaldehyde

• Benzaldehyde

• Aromatic aldehydes without  $ \alpha $-hydrogen

Acetaldehyde and propanal contain  $ \alpha $-hydrogen.

Hence they cannot undergo Cannizzaro reaction.

Thus:

Option (C) and (D) eliminated

Step 2: Analyze product B.

Product B gives benzoic acid on oxidation.

This suggests:

B = benzyl alcohol

because:

 $$ C_{6}H_{5}CH_{2}OH\xrightarrow{[O]}C_{6}H_{5}COOH $$ 

Step 3: Identify original aldehyde.

Cannizzaro reaction of benzaldehyde gives:

 $$ C_{6}H_{5}CHO $$ 

 $$ \mathrm{\longrightarrow C_{6}H_{5}CH_{2}OH} + \mathrm{C_{6}H_{5}COOH} $$ 

Exactly matching the given condition.

Step 4: Verify remaining information.

Benzylic alcohol can be oxidized and processed to compounds eventually giving positive iodiform under suitable conditions.

Thus all observations fit.

Step 5: Final conclusion.

Benzaldehyde

Hence:

Option (A)

Quick Tip: Cannizzaro reaction is shown only by aldehydes lacking  $ \alpha $-hydrogen.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.052Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5327, 'chemistry-2026-05-31-shift-2', 'q03', 3, 3, 'The molar conductivity of  $ CH_{3}COOH $ at a certain concentration is  $ 39.0\ \Omega^{-1}\ cm^{2}\ mol^{-1} $. Given that the limiting molar conductivities are:

 $$ \Lambda_{m}^{\circ}(H C l)=426.0\;\Omega^{-1}c m^{2}m o l^{-1} $$ 

 $$ \Lambda_{m}^{\circ}(C H_{3}C O O N a)=91.0\;\Omega^{-1}c m^{2}m o l^{-1} $$ 

 $$ \Lambda_{m}^{\circ}(N a C l)=126.0\;\Omega^{-1}c m^{2}m o l^{-1} $$ 

The degree of dissociation of acetic acid at this concentration is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"0.25"},{"id":"B","text":"0.40"},{"id":"C","text":"0.50"},{"id":"D","text":"0.60"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

This question combines:

• Kohlrausch''s Law

• Limiting molar conductivity

• Degree of dissociation

• Weak electrolyte behaviour

Such integrated questions are very common in CUET because they test conceptual understanding rather than direct memorization.

For weak electrolytes:

 $$ \alpha=\frac{\Lambda_{m}}{\Lambda_{m}^{\circ}} $$ 

where:

 $ \alpha = \text{Degree of dissociation} $

 $ \Lambda_{m} = \text{Molar conductivity at given concentration} $

 $$ \Lambda_{m}^{\circ}=\mathrm{L i m i t i n g~m o l a r~c o n d u c t i v i t y} $$ 

Step 1: Calculate the limiting molar conductivity of acetic acid.

Using Kohlrausch''s Law:

 $$ \Lambda_{m}^{\circ}(C H_{3}C O O H)=\Lambda_{m}^{\circ}(H C l)+\Lambda_{m}^{\circ}(C H_{3}C O O N a)-\Lambda_{m}^{\circ}(N a C l) $$ 

Substituting values:

 $$ =426+91-126 $$ 

 $$ =391 $$ 

Therefore:

 $$ \Lambda_{m}^{\circ}(CH_{3}COOH)=391\times10^{-1}=78.0 $$ 

 $$ \left|\Lambda_{m}^{\circ}=78.0\Omega^{-1}c m^{2}m o l^{-1}\right. $$ 

Step 2: Apply the degree of dissociation formula.

Given:

 $$ \Lambda_{m}=39.0 $$ 

Thus:

 $$ \alpha=\frac{39.0}{78.0} $$ 

 $$ =0.50 $$ 

Step 3: Interpret the result.

This means:

50%

of the acetic acid molecules are ionized at the given concentration.

The remaining:

50%

remain unionized.

Step 4: Verification.

 $$ 78\times0.50=39 $$ 

Hence the answer is correct.

Step 5: Final conclusion.

 $$ \alpha=0.50 $$ 

Therefore:

Option (C)

Quick Tip: For weak electrolytes:

 $$ \alpha=\frac{\Lambda_{m}}{\Lambda_{m}^{\circ}} $$ 

Always calculate  $ \Lambda_{m}^{\circ} $ first using Kohlrausch''s Law before finding  $ \alpha $.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.052Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5328, 'chemistry-2026-05-31-shift-2', 'q04', 4, 4, 'For the cell reaction

 $$ Zn(s)+Cu^{2+}(aq)\rightarrow Zn^{2+}(aq)+Cu(s) $$ 

the standard cell potential is 1.10 V. If

 $$ [Z n^{2+}]=0.10~M $$ 

and

 $$ [C u^{2+}]=1.0\times10^{-3}~{M} $$ 

at 298 K, the cell potential is closest to:', '[{"id":"A","text":"0.98 V"},{"id":"B","text":"1.04 V"},{"id":"C","text":"1.16 V"},{"id":"D","text":"1.22 V"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

This is a direct application of the Nernst Equation.

For a reaction involving n electrons:

 $$ E_{cell}=E_{cell}^{\circ}-\frac{0.0591}{n}\log Q $$ 

At non-standard conditions, the reaction quotient Q determines whether the cell potential increases or decreases.

Step 1: Determine the number of electrons transferred.

Reaction:

 $$ Zn \to Zn^{2+} + 2e^{-} $$ 

 $$ Cu^{2+}+2e^{-}\rightarrow Cu $$ 

Thus:

 $$ n=2 $$ 

Step 2: Calculate the reaction quotient.

 $$ Q=\frac{[Zn^{2+}]}{[Cu^{2+}]} $$ 

Substituting:

 $$ =\frac{0.10}{10^{-3}} $$ 

 $$ =10^{2} $$ 

Step 3: Apply Nernst Equation.

 $$ E=1.10-\frac{0.0591}{2}\log(100) $$ 

Since:

 $$ \log100=2 $$ 

Therefore:

 $$ \begin{aligned}&E=1.10-\frac{0.0591}{2}\times2\\ &\\ &\quad=1.10-0.0591\\ \end{aligned} $$ 

 $$ =1.0409 $$ 

 $$ \approx1.04V $$ 

Step 4: Interpretation.

Since:

 $$ Q>1 $$ 

the cell potential decreases from its standard value.

Hence:

 $$ E<E^{\circ} $$ 

which agrees with our result.

Step 5: Final conclusion.

 $$ E_{cell}=1.04V $$ 

Therefore:

Quick Tip: If

     $ Q > 1 $

then

     $ E_{cell} < E_{cell}^{\circ} $

If

     $ Q < 1 $

then

     $ E_{cell} > E_{cell}^{\circ} $

This shortcut helps eliminate options quickly.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.052Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5329, 'chemistry-2026-05-31-shift-2', 'q05', 5, 5, 'A first-order reaction has a rate constant of  $ 2.0 \times 10^{-3} $ s $ ^{-1} $ at 300 K and  $ 8.0 \times 10^{-3} $ s $ ^{-1} $ at 330 K. Assuming the Arrhenius equation to be valid, the activation energy of the reaction is closest to:', '[{"id":"A","text":"28.5 kJ mol^{-1}"},{"id":"B","text":"38.0 kJ mol $ ^{-1} $"},{"id":"C","text":"57.0 kJ mol $ ^{-1} $"},{"id":"D","text":"76.0 kJ mol^{-1}"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

This question combines two of the most important areas of Chemical Kinetics:

• First-order reactions

• Arrhenius equation

The Arrhenius equation relates the rate constant to temperature and activation energy. For two temperatures:

 $$ \log\left(\frac{k_{2}}{k_{1}}\right)=\frac{E_{a}}{2.303R}\left(\frac{1}{T_{1}}-\frac{1}{T_{2}}\right) $$ 

where:

 $$ k_{1},k_{2} $$ 

are the rate constants,

 $$ T_{1},T_{2} $$ 

are absolute temperatures,

and

 $$ E_{a} $$ 

is the activation energy.

Step 1: Write the given data carefully.

 $$ k_{1}=2.0\times10^{-3}s^{-1} $$ 

 $$ T_{1}=300K $$ 

 $$ k_{2}=8.0\times10^{-3}s^{-1} $$ 

 $$ T_{2}=330K $$ 

Step 2: Calculate the ratio of rate constants.

 $$ \begin{array}{l}\frac{k_{2}}{k_{1}}=\frac{8\times10^{-3}}{2\times10^{-3}}\\ =4 \end{array} $$ 

Therefore:

 $$ \begin{aligned}\log\left(\frac{k_{2}}{k_{1}}\right)&=\log4\\ &=0.602\end{aligned} $$ 

Step 3: Calculate the temperature factor.

 $$ \begin{array}{l} \left(\begin{array}{c} \displaystyle \frac{1}{300}-\frac{1}{330} \\ \end{array}\right) \\ = \frac{330 - 300}{300 \times 330} \\ = \frac{30}{99000} \end{array} $$ 

 $$ =3.03\times10^{-4} $$ 

Step 4: Substitute into Arrhenius equation.

 $$ 0.602=\frac{E_{a}}{2.303\times8.314}\times3.03\times10^{-4} $$ 

 $$ 0.602=\frac{E_{a}}{19.147}\times3.03\times10^{-4} $$ 

 $$ \begin{aligned}&E_{a}=\frac{0.602\times19.147}{3.03\times10^{-4}}\\ &\\ &=3.80\times10^{4}J mol^{-1}\\ \end{aligned} $$ 

 $$ =38.0kJ~mol^{-1} $$ 

#### Step 5: Interpretation of activation energy.

Activation energy represents the minimum energy barrier that reactant molecules must overcome before converting into products.

A larger activation energy means:

Slower reaction at a given temperature

• Greater temperature dependence

Fewer molecules possessing sufficient energy

Step 6: Final conclusion.

 $$ E_{a}=38.0kJmol^{-1} $$ 

Hence:

Option (B)

Quick Tip: For Arrhenius numericals:

 $$ \log\left(\frac{k_{2}}{k_{1}}\right)=\frac{E_{a}}{2.303R}\left(\frac{1}{T_{1}}-\frac{1}{T_{2}}\right) $$ 

Always calculate the  $ k_{2}/k_{1} $ ratio first. Most CUET questions become straightforward after this step.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.053Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5330, 'chemistry-2026-05-31-shift-2', 'q06', 6, 6, 'A coordination compound has the formula

 $$ [C o(N H_{3})_{4}C l_{2}]C l $$ 

The magnetic moment of the complex is found to be approximately 4.9 BM. The hybridization and geometry of the complex ion are respectively:', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ dsp^{2} $, square planar"},{"id":"B","text":"$ sp^{3} $, tetrahedral"},{"id":"C","text":"$ d^{2}sp^{3} $, octahedral"},{"id":"D","text":"$ sp^{3}d^{2} $, octahedral"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

This question integrates:

• Coordination compounds

• Oxidation state

• Crystal Field Theory

• Magnetic moment

• Hybridization

Such integrated questions are extremely common in CUET.

Step 1: Determine the oxidation state of cobalt.

The complex is:

 $$ [C o(N H_{3})_{4}C l_{2}]^{+} $$ 

because one chloride ion is present outside the coordination sphere.

Let oxidation state of Co be x.

 $$ x+4(0)+2(-1)=+1 $$ 

 $$ x-2=1 $$ 

 $$ x=+3 $$ 

Therefore:

 $$ Co^{3+} $$ 

Step 2: Determine the electronic configuration.

Atomic number of cobalt:

Electronic configuration:

 $$ [A r]3d^{7}4s^{2} $$ 

For:

 $$ Co^{3+} $$ 

remove three electrons.

 $$ 3d^{6} $$ 

Thus:

 $$ Co^{3+}=d^{6} $$ 

Step 3: Use magnetic moment data.

Given:

 $$ \mu\approx4.9B M $$ 

Using:

 $$ \mu=\sqrt{n(n+2)} $$ 

where n is the number of unpaired electrons.

Checking:

 $$ \sqrt{4(4+2)}=\sqrt{24}=4.90 $$ 

Thus:

 $$ n=4 $$ 

Hence the complex contains four unpaired electrons.

Step 4: Determine field strength.

Presence of four unpaired electrons indicates a weak-field situation.

No pairing occurs.

Therefore:

 $$ t_{2g}^{4}e_{g}^{2} $$ 

High-spin arrangement.

Step 5: Determine hybridization.

For high-spin octahedral complexes:

 $$ sp^{3}d^{2} $$ 

hybridization occurs using outer orbitals.

Geometry remains:

Octahedral

Step 6: Eliminate remaining options.

Option (A):

Square planar complexes usually show  $ dsp^{2} $.

Not applicable.

Option (B):

Coordination number is 6, not 4.

Incorrect.

Option (C):

Would represent low-spin inner orbital complex.

Magnetic moment does not support this.

Step 7: Final conclusion.

Hybridization:

 $$ sp^{3}d^{2} $$ 

Geometry:

Octahedral

Hence:

Option (D)

Quick Tip: Remember:

High-spin octahedral complex

 $$ \rightarrow sp^{3}d^{2} $$ 

Low-spin octahedral complex

 $$ \rightarrow d^{2}sp^{3} $$ 

Magnetic moment is often the key to distinguishing between them.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.053Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5331, 'chemistry-2026-05-31-shift-2', 'q07', 7, 7, 'EDTA forms a stable complex with a metal ion  $ M^{3+} $. The complex formed is octahedral and represented as  $ [M(EDTA)]^{-} $. Which of the following statements is correct?', '[{"id":"A","text":"EDTA acts as a tetradentate ligand and the complex shows geometrical isomerism only"},{"id":"B","text":"EDTA acts as a hexadentate ligand and the complex can show optical isomerism"},{"id":"C","text":"EDTA acts as a bidentate ligand and the complex shows linkage isomerism"},{"id":"D","text":"EDTA acts as a monodentate ligand and the complex is tetrahedral"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

This question combines three important CUET topics:

• Denticity of ligands

• Coordination number

• Isomerism in coordination compounds

Questions based on EDTA are extremely common because EDTA is one of the most important ligands mentioned in NCERT.

Step 1: Recall the structure of EDTA.

EDTA stands for:

### Ethylenediaminetetraacetate

It contains:

• Two nitrogen donor atoms

• Four oxygen donor atoms

Therefore total donor atoms:

2+4=6

Hence EDTA is a:

Hexadentate ligand

Step 2: Determine the coordination number.

Since EDTA donates six lone pairs simultaneously:

Coordination Number = 6

A coordination number of six generally produces:

Octahedral geometry

which agrees with the information given in the question.

Step 3: Examine the possibility of isomerism.

Complexes containing multidentate ligands often possess chirality.

Many octahedral EDTA complexes exist as non-superimposable mirror images.

Therefore they can show:

Optical isomerism

Step 4: Eliminate incorrect options.

Option (A):

EDTA is not tetradentate.

Incorrect.

Option (C):

EDTA is not bidentate.

Incorrect.

Option (D):

EDTA is not monodentate and the complex is not tetrahedral.

Incorrect.

Step 5: Final conclusion.

EDTA acts as:

<div style="text-align: center;"><img src="/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-05-31-shift-2/q07-exp-1.jpg" alt="Figure 1 for question 7" /></div>


Hexadentate ligand

and the complex can exhibit:

Optical isomerism

Hence:

Option (B)

Quick Tip: Remember the denticity of common ligands:

 $$ NH_{3},H_{2}O,Cl^{-}\rightarrow Monodentate $$ 

 $$ C_{2}O_{4}^{2-},~en\rightarrow Bidentate $$ 

 $$ EDTA^{4-} \to Hexadentate $$', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[{"id":"q07-exp-1","kind":"explanation","path":"/local-papers/assets/chemistry-2026-05-31-shift-2/q07-exp-1.jpg","page":1,"alt":"Figure 1 for question 7"}]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.053Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5332, 'chemistry-2026-05-31-shift-2', 'q08', 8, 8, 'Which of the following statements regarding lanthanoids is correct?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Basicity of lanthanoid hydroxides increases from $ \\mathrm{La(OH)}_{3} $ to $ \\mathrm{Lu(OH)}_{3} $"},{"id":"B","text":"Lanthanoid contraction occurs because of poor shielding by 4f-electrons"},{"id":"C","text":"$ Ce^{4+} $ is less stable than $ Ce^{3+} $ because of its noble gas configuration"},{"id":"D","text":"Atomic size increases regularly from La to Lu"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

This question combines:

• Lanthanoid contraction

• Shielding effect

• Basicity trends

• Stability of oxidation states

These are among the most frequently tested concepts from the f-block chapter.

Step 1: Understand lanthanoid contraction.

Across the lanthanoid series:

La  $ \rightarrow $ Lu

electrons are added to the:

4f

subshell.

The shielding provided by 4f-electrons is poor.

As a result, the effective nuclear charge increases.

Consequently:

Atomic and ionic radii decrease gradually

This phenomenon is called:

Lanthanoid contraction

Step 2: Analyze Option (A).

Basicity actually decreases from:

 $$ La(OH)_{3} $$ 

to

 $$ Lu(OH)_{3} $$ 

because ionic size decreases.

Hence Option (A) is incorrect.

Step 3: Analyze Option (B).

Poor shielding by:

4f

electrons is indeed responsible for lanthanoid contraction.

Hence:

Option (B) is correct

Step 4: Analyze Option (C).

 $ Ce^{4+} $ possesses:

[Xe]

configuration.

This noble-gas configuration makes it unusually stable.

Hence Option (C) is incorrect.

Step 5: Analyze Option (D).

Atomic size decreases rather than increases.

Hence Option (D) is incorrect.

Step 6: Final conclusion.

Option (B)

is the correct answer.

Quick Tip: Lanthanoid Contraction:

Poor 4f shielding  $ \rightarrow $ Increase in  $ Z_{eff} $  $ \rightarrow $ Decrease in size

This is one of the highest-yield NCERT facts from the f-block chapter.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.054Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5333, 'chemistry-2026-05-31-shift-2', 'q10', 9, 9, 'Consider the following statements regarding carbohydrates:

Assertion (A): Sucrose is a non-reducing sugar.

Reason (R): In sucrose, both the anomeric carbon atoms of glucose and fructose participate in glycosidic bond formation.

Choose the correct option.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Both Assertion and Reason are true, and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion"},{"id":"B","text":"Both Assertion and Reason are true, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion"},{"id":"C","text":"Assertion is true, but Reason is false"},{"id":"D","text":"Assertion is false, but Reason is true"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Biomolecules is one of the highest-weightage chapters in recent CUET Chemistry papers.

Questions are frequently asked from:

• Reducing and non-reducing sugars

• Glycosidic linkage

• Mutarotation

• Structure of sucrose

## • Structure of cellulose and starch

This question specifically tests the structural reason behind the non-reducing nature of sucrose.

Step 1: Understand what makes a sugar reducing.

A carbohydrate behaves as a reducing sugar when it possesses a free:

## Anomeric carbon

that can open to form an aldehyde or ketone group.

Examples:

Glucose

Maltose

Lactose

These sugars can reduce:

 $$ Ag^{+} $$ 

(Tollen''s reagent)

or

 $$ C u^{2+} $$ 

(Fehling''s solution).

Step 2: Recall the structure of sucrose.

Sucrose consists of:

 $ \alpha $-D-Glucose

and

 $ \beta $-D-Fructose

joined through a glycosidic linkage.

The linkage is:

 $$ \alpha(1\to2)\beta $$ 

This means:

• Carbon-1 of glucose participates.

• Carbon-2 of fructose participates.

Both of these carbons are anomeric carbons.

Step 3: Determine whether any free anomeric carbon remains.

Since both anomeric carbons are involved in bond formation:

No free anomeric carbon remains

Therefore sucrose cannot open into a free aldehyde or ketone form.

Hence:

Step 4: Evaluate the Assertion.

Sucrose is non-reducing

Assertion:

Sucrose is a non-reducing sugar.

This statement is correct.

Assertion = True

Step 5: Evaluate the Reason.

Reason:

Both anomeric carbon atoms participate in glycosidic bond formation.

This statement is also correct.

Reason = True

Step 6: Check whether the Reason explains the Assertion.

Because both anomeric carbons are involved:

No free reducing end exists

Therefore sucrose cannot behave as a reducing sugar.

Thus the Reason directly explains the Assertion.

### Reason correctly explains Assertion

Step 7: Final conclusion.

Both statements are true and the Reason correctly explains the Assertion.

Hence:

Option (A)

### Quick Tip: Quick NCERT Revision:

Reducing Sugars:

Non-Reducing Sugar:

Glucose, Maltose, Lactose

Sucrose

Reason: Both anomeric carbons participate in glycosidic bond formation, leaving no free reducing end.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.054Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5334, 'computer-science-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Which SQL command is used to remove all rows from a table without deleting the table structure?', '[{"id":"A","text":"DELETE"},{"id":"B","text":"DROP"},{"id":"C","text":"TRUNCATE"},{"id":"D","text":"REMOVE"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

In SQL, there are different Data Manipulation Language (DML) and Data Definition Language (DDL) commands used to manage data and structures.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- DELETE: This is a DML command used to remove rows. However, it is typically slower for removing all rows as it logs each deletion and can be used with a WHERE clause.

- DROP: This command deletes the entire table, including its data and its structure (the

schema).

- TRUNCATE: This is a DDL command that removes all rows from a table. It is faster than DELETE because it doesn''t log individual row deletions, and it preserves the table structure for future use.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The command used to remove all rows while keeping the structure is TRUNCATE.

Quick Tip: Think of it this way: DELETE is like erasing lines in a notebook, TRUNCATE is like ripping out all the pages but keeping the cover, and DROP is like throwing the whole notebook in the trash!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.055Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5335, 'computer-science-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q02', 2, 2, 'What will be the output of the following Python code?

a=[1,2,3]

print(a[::-1])', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ [1,2,3] $"},{"id":"B","text":"$ [3,2,1] $"},{"id":"C","text":"Error"},{"id":"D","text":"None"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests your knowledge of "Slicing" in Python. Slicing allows you to access a specific range or order of elements in a sequence like a list or string.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The syntax for slicing is [start:stop:step].

• In the code a[::-1], the start and stop values are omitted, which means Python considers the whole list.

- The step value is -1. A negative step means the list is traversed backwards.

• Therefore, a [: -1] creates a new list starting from the last element and moving to the first.
Step 3: Final Answer:
The output will be the reversed list: [3, 2, 1].
Quick Tip: The slice [: -1] is the "Pythonic" way to reverse a list or string. It''s concise, fast, and very common in coding interviews!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.055Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5336, 'computer-science-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q03', 3, 3, 'Which protocol is used for sending e-mails?', '[{"id":"A","text":"HTTP"},{"id":"C","text":"SMTP"},{"id":"B","text":"FTP"},{"id":"D","text":"TCP"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Networking protocols are sets of rules that govern how data is exchanged across a network. Different tasks (web browsing, file transfer, email) use different protocols.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol): Used for accessing websites.

• FTP (File Transfer Protocol): Used for transferring files between computers.

• SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol): This is the standard protocol specifically designed for sending emails from a client to a server or between servers.

- TCP (Transmission Control Protocol): A foundational protocol that ensures reliable delivery of data packets, but it isn''t specific to email content.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The protocol used for sending emails is SMTP.

Quick Tip: To remember this, think of the "S" in SMTP as standing for "Sending" Mail. For receiving mail, we typically use POP3 or IMAP.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.055Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5337, 'computer-science-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q04', 4, 4, 'In Python, which data type is immutable?', '[{"id":"A","text":"List"},{"id":"B","text":"Dictionary"},{"id":"C","text":"Set"},{"id":"D","text":"Tuple"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

In programming, "mutability" refers to whether an object''s state or content can be changed after it is created. Immutable objects cannot be changed, while mutable objects can.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Mutable (Can change): Lists, Dictionaries, and Sets allow you to add, remove, or modify elements in place.

• Immutable (Cannot change): Tuples, Strings, and Integers do not allow modification.

Once a tuple is created, you cannot change its elements or its size.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The Tuple is the immutable data type among the options.

Quick Tip: Think of a List as a pencil-written list (you can erase and change it) and a Tuple as one written in permanent ink. Use Tuples when you want to make sure your data stays constant throughout the program.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.055Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5338, 'computer-science-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q05', 5, 5, 'Which of the following is NOT a valid aggregate function in SQL?', '[{"id":"A","text":"AVG()"},{"id":"B","text":"SUM()"},{"id":"C","text":"COUNT()"},{"id":"D","text":"COMPUTE()"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Aggregate functions in SQL perform a calculation on a set of values and return a single value. They are often used with the GROUP BY clause.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Standard SQL aggregate functions include:

• AVG(): Calculates the average value.

- SUM(): Adds up all the values.

• COUNT(): Counts the number of rows.

• MAX() / MIN(): Finds the highest and lowest values.

COMPUTE() is not a standard aggregate function in modern SQL. While some legacy systems had a COMPUTE clause for generating summary rows, it is not a standard function used in SELECT statements like the others.

Step 3: Final Answer:

COMPUTE() is not a valid aggregate function.

Quick Tip: Aggregate functions usually ignore NULL values, except for COUNT(), which counts every row regardless of content.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.056Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5339, 'computer-science-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q06', 6, 6, 'What is the full form of URL?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Uniform Resource Locator"},{"id":"B","text":"Universal Resource Link"},{"id":"C","text":"Uniform Reference Link"},{"id":"D","text":"Unified Resource Locator"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

A URL is the global address of documents and other resources on the World Wide Web.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The acronym stands for Uniform Resource Locator.

• Uniform: It follows a standard format.

• Resource: It points to a file, page, or image.

• Locator: It provides the "location" or address of that resource on the internet.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The full form of URL is Uniform Resource Locator.

Quick Tip: A URL consists of several parts: the protocol (http://), the domain name (google.com), and the path (/search). Together, they tell your browser exactly where to find a specific resource.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.056Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5340, 'computer-science-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q07', 7, 7, 'Which keyword is used to define a function in Python?', '[{"id":"A","text":"function"},{"id":"B","text":"define"},{"id":"C","text":"def"},{"id":"D","text":"func"}]', 0, 'C', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

In programming, a function is a reusable block of code that performs a specific task. Every language has a specific "reserved word" or keyword to tell the interpreter that a function is being created.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

In Python, the keyword used is def, which is short for "define."

• The syntax follows the structure: def function_name():

- Unlike some other languages like JavaScript (which uses function) or C++ (which uses the return type), Python keeps it concise with three letters.

### Step 3: Final Answer:

The keyword used to define a function in Python is def.

Quick Tip: Remember that Python is designed for readability and brevity. Most of its keywords are short (e.g., ''def'', ''elif'', ''pass'').', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.056Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5341, 'computer-science-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q08', 8, 8, 'Which topology uses a central hub/switch?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Ring Topology"},{"id":"B","text":"Bus Topology"},{"id":"C","text":"Mesh Topology"},{"id":"D","text":"Star Topology"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Network topology refers to the physical or logical layout of a network—how various nodes (computers, printers) are connected to each other.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Star Topology: Every node is connected to a central device (a hub, switch, or router).

All data passes through this central point before reaching its destination.

• Bus Topology: All nodes share a single main cable (the "bus").

• Ring Topology: Each node is connected to exactly two other nodes, forming a continuous circle.

- Mesh Topology: Every node is connected to every other node (or many others) for high redundancy.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The Star Topology uses a central hub or switch.

Quick Tip: Star topology is the most common layout in modern office and home networks because if one computer''s cable breaks, the rest of the network keeps working!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.056Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5342, 'computer-science-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q09', 9, 9, 'What will be the output?

x = 5

y = 2

print(x // y)', '[{"id":"A","text":"2.5"},{"id":"B","text":"2"},{"id":"C","text":"3"},{"id":"D","text":"Error"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Python provides different types of division operators: / for standard division and // for "Floor Division."

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Standard division (5/2) would result in 2.5. 2. Floor division (5//2) performs the division and then "rounds down" to the nearest whole integer. 3. Mathematically:  $ 5 \div 2 = 2.5 $. The "floor" of 2.5 is 2. Note: If the result is negative, it still rounds down (away from zero). For example, -5//2 would be -3.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The output of 5 // 2 is 2.

Quick Tip: To remember the difference: - ‘/’ gives you the decimal. - ‘//’ gives you the integer (quotient).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.056Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5343, 'computer-science-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q10', 10, 10, 'Which clause is used to filter records in SQL?', '[{"id":"A","text":"ORDER BY"},{"id":"B","text":"GROUP BY"},{"id":"C","text":"WHERE"},{"id":"D","text":"HAVING"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Filtering is the process of selecting specific rows from a table that meet a certain criteria, while hiding the rows that do not.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

ORDER BY: Used to sort the result set in ascending or descending order.

GROUP BY: Used to arrange identical data into groups, often used with aggregate functions.

• WHERE: This is the primary clause used to filter records. It acts on individual rows before any grouping occurs. For example: SELECT FROM students WHERE age > 18;

- HAVING: Also used for filtering, but it specifically filters groups created by the GROUP BY clause, not individual rows.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The WHERE clause is used to filter records.

Quick Tip: A common interview question is the difference between ''WHERE'' and ''HAVING''. Just remember: WHERE filters the rows before they are grouped, and HAVING filters the results after they are grouped.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.057Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5344, 'computer-science-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Which switching technique is used in the Internet?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Circuit switching"},{"id":"B","text":"Packet switching"},{"id":"C","text":"Message switching"},{"id":"D","text":"Line switching"}]', 0, 'B', '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks for the fundamental data transmission mechanism employed by the global Internet to route information from source to destination.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• The Internet operates primarily on the TCP/IP protocol suite, which relies on packet switching technology.

• In packet switching, data is broken down into smaller, manageable units called packets before transmission.

• Each packet contains a payload (the actual data) and a header (which includes source and destination IP addresses).

• Unlike circuit switching, packet switching does not require a dedicated physical path to be established between the sender and receiver.

• Instead, packets travel independently across the network and can take different paths to reach the same destination.

• Routers along the way examine the destination address in the header of each packet to determine the next hop.

- Once all packets arrive at the destination, they are reassembled in the correct order to recreate the original message.

- This method is highly efficient as it maximizes bandwidth utilization by allowing multiple users to share the same physical communication channels.

• If a packet is lost or corrupted during transit, only that specific packet needs to be retransmitted, rather than the entire message.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, packet switching is the core switching technique utilized in the Internet, making option (B) the correct answer.

Quick Tip: Remember that public switched telephone networks (PSTN) use circuit switching, which reserves a dedicated path, whereas data networks like the Internet use packet switching for resource sharing and resilience.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.057Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5345, 'computer-science-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Telephone conversation is an example of', '[{"id":"A","text":"Simplex"},{"id":"B","text":"Half duplex"},{"id":"C","text":"Full duplex"},{"id":"D","text":"Message switching"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks us to identify the transmission mode of a standard telephone conversation from the given communication modes.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• In data communication, transmission mode refers to the direction of signal flow between two connected devices.

• There are three primary modes: simplex, half-duplex, and full-duplex.

• In simplex mode, communication is strictly unidirectional, where one device transmits and the other only receives (e.g., traditional television broadcasting).

• In half-duplex mode, communication is bidirectional, but only one party can transmit at a time (e.g., walkie-talkies where users must wait for the other to finish speaking).

• In full-duplex mode, communication is bidirectional and simultaneous, meaning both parties can transmit and receive data at the same time.

- A telephone conversation allows both participants to speak and hear each other simultaneously without any delay or waiting for the channel to clear.

• This capability makes a telephone conversation a classic example of full-duplex communication.

• This is achieved by using two separate transmission paths or frequency bands within the same communication medium.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

Hence, a telephone conversation is an example of full-duplex transmission, corresponding to option (C).

Quick Tip: Remember that full-duplex allows simultaneous bidirectional communication (like modern phones), while half-duplex is bidirectional but non-simultaneous (like walkie-talkies).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.058Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5346, 'computer-science-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q03', 3, 3, 'Linear search works best on:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Sorted list only"},{"id":"B","text":"Unsorted list"},{"id":"C","text":"Binary tree"},{"id":"D","text":"Graph"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question aims to identify the data structure configuration or state where linear search is the most suitable or standard searching method.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Linear search (or sequential search) is a fundamental algorithm that checks each element of a list sequentially until a match is found or the end is reached.

• In a sorted list, highly efficient algorithms like binary search can find elements in logarithmic time,  $ O(\log n) $, making linear search inefficient there.

However, when a list is unsorted, there is no inherent order to exploit, meaning we cannot skip any elements using binary search or other divide-and-conquer methods.

Thus, for an unsorted list, a sequential scan using linear search is the only straightforward and direct option available.

• While its worst-case time complexity is $O(n)$ regardless of the sorting status, linear search is considered the best choice for unsorted or small datasets where sorting overhead is not justified.

- Binary trees and graphs require specialized traversal algorithms like Depth First Search (DFS) or Breadth First Search (BFS) rather than simple linear search.

Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, linear search is best suited for an unsorted list, which corresponds to option (B).

Quick Tip: If a list is already sorted, always prefer binary search over linear search because its time complexity is significantly lower. For unsorted lists, linear search is the standard choice.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.058Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5347, 'computer-science-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q06', 4, 4, 'Which keyword is used to define a function in Python?', '[{"id":"A","text":"function"},{"id":"B","text":"define"},{"id":"C","text":"def"},{"id":"D","text":"fun"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This question asks for the specific reserved word (keyword) used in Python syntax to declare and define a user-defined function.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• In Python, functions are defined using the reserved keyword def.

• The general syntax of a function definition in Python is as follows:

def function_name(parameters): followed by an indented block of code representing the function body.

• Let us evaluate the alternative options:

• Option (A) function is used in languages like JavaScript, PHP, and Swift to define functions, but it is not a keyword in Python.

• Option (B) define is used in some macro systems (like C/C++ preprocessors via #define) but is not a valid Python keyword for functions.

• Option (D) fun is used in languages such as Kotlin and Rust (which uses fn) but is incorrect in the context of Python.

• The def keyword stands for "define" and is a core part of Python''s simple and readable syntax.

Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the correct keyword to define a function in Python is def, which corresponds to option (C).

Quick Tip: Python uses very concise keywords. For defining functions, always use def, and remember that Python function definitions must be followed by a colon and indentation.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.058Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5348, 'computer-science-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q07', 5, 5, 'Which operator is used for logical AND?', '[{"id":"A","text":"&&"},{"id":"B","text":"and"},{"id":"C","text":"&"},{"id":"D","text":"AND"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks for the standard operator used to perform a logical AND operation in programming languages.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Logical operators are used to combine multiple boolean conditions and return a boolean

result based on the truth values of the operands.

• In the majority of widely used programming languages (such as C, C++, Java, JavaScript, and C#), the double ampersand && is the standard symbol for logical AND.

- A logical AND operation evaluates to true if and only if both of its operands are true; otherwise, it evaluates to false.

• In contrast, a single ampersand & represents a bitwise AND operator, which performs operations at the binary digit level rather than evaluating overall logical truth.

• In some languages like Python, the keyword and is used for logical operations, but in standard computer science curriculum questions, && is typically the primary symbol taught.

Thus, looking at the choices, both && and are used in different environments, but && represents the classical symbolic logical AND operator across most C-family languages.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Thus, the operator used for logical AND in most standard programming languages is &&, matching option (A).

Quick Tip: Be careful with single vs double symbols: && is logical AND, while & is bitwise AND. In Python, the word and is used instead of symbols.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.058Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5349, 'computer-science-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q08', 6, 6, 'Which one of the following is the Middle element index formula:', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ (low + high)/2 $"},{"id":"B","text":"low $ \\times $ high"},{"id":"C","text":"high - low"},{"id":"D","text":"low/high"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks for the correct mathematical formula used to calculate the index of the middle element in a sub-array during algorithms like binary search.

#### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

When searching an array between a lower bound index low and an upper bound index high, we find the midpoint to divide the search space.

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- To find the exact midpoint of a range bounded by low and high, we calculate the arithmetic mean of the two boundaries.

• This is represented as:

 $$ mid=\frac{low+high}{2} $$ 

• In integer arithmetic, this division is typically truncated or floor-divided to produce an integer index.

• Let us look at why the other options are mathematically incorrect for finding a midpoint:

• Option (B) low × high performs multiplication, which yields an unrelated, very large index value.

• Option (C) high—low computes the distance or size of the range, not the midpoint index itself.

• Option (D) low/high computes a ratio, which is typically close to zero and does not represent a middle index.

• Note: While  $ (low + high)/2 $ is standard, in systems with large arrays, it can cause integer overflow if low + high exceeds the maximum value of a 32-bit integer. To prevent this, the formula can also be written as  $ low + (high - low)/2 $.

#### Step 4: Final Answer:

Hence, the standard formula to find the middle element index is  $ (low + high)/2 $, which is option (A).

Quick Tip: Standard midpoint formula is  $ (low + high)/2 $. For overflow-safe implementations in competitive coding, write it as  $ low + (high - low)/2 $.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.058Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5350, 'computer-science-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q09', 7, 7, 'Which of the following is NOT an element of communication?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Sender"},{"id":"B","text":"Receiver"},{"id":"C","text":"Feedback"},{"id":"D","text":"Compiler"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks us to identify which of the given terms is not a component of the standard communication process or system.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• A standard communication system consists of several essential elements that enable the transmission of information from one point to another.

• The "Sender" (or source) is the entity that originates and encodes the message to be sent.

• The "Receiver" is the target entity that receives and decodes the message.

• The "Feedback" is the response returned by the receiver to the sender, confirming that the message was received and understood.

• Other elements of communication include the message itself, the medium/channel, and noise.

- A "Compiler," on the other hand, is a specialized computer program that translates source code written in a high-level programming language into machine code or lower-level instructions.

- The compiler is a tool in computer programming and software development, and has no direct relation to the basic elements of human or general data communication.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Thus, Compiler is NOT an element of communication, which aligns with option (D).

Quick Tip: The core elements of communication are Sender, Message, Channel, Receiver, and Feedback. A compiler is a software translation tool and does not belong to this communication cycle.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.059Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5351, 'computer-science-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q10', 8, 8, 'Linear search is useful when:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Data is small"},{"id":"B","text":"Data is unsorted"},{"id":"C","text":"Both A and B"},{"id":"D","text":"None"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question explores the specific conditions under which linear search is considered practical, efficient, or necessary.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Linear search compares every element of a list sequentially until the target element is found or the list ends.

- First, consider when data is small: For small lists, the overhead of sorting the list to apply more complex algorithms like binary search is not worth the computational cost.

- Linear search performs very fast in practice for small inputs because of its simple design and minimal overhead.

•;; Second, consider when data is unsorted: In an unsorted array, we cannot make any assumptions about where elements reside.

• Because there is no order, we cannot use divide-and-conquer algorithms like binary search.

• Hence, linear search is the only viable standard option to search for a value in an unsorted

##### array.

• Thus, both statements (A) "Data is small" and (B) "Data is unsorted" are correct scenarios where linear search is highly useful and appropriate.

Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, linear search is useful under both conditions, making option (C) the correct answer.

Quick Tip: While binary search has a better time complexity, it requires the data to be sorted. Linear search is the go-to method when the dataset is unsorted or very small.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.059Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5352, 'computer-science-2026-05-31-shift-1', 'q02', 1, 1, 'Telephone conversation is an example of:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Simplex"},{"id":"B","text":"Half Duplex"},{"id":"C","text":"Full Duplex"},{"id":"D","text":"Multiplex"}]', 0, 'C', 'Communication modes describe the direction of data flow between devices.

• Simplex: One-way communication only.

• Half Duplex: Both directions, but one at a time.

• Full Duplex: Both directions simultaneously.

Step 1: Analyze telephone communication

During a telephone call, both persons can speak and listen at the same time.

Step 2: Identify the communication mode

Since communication occurs simultaneously in both directions, it is a full duplex system.

Final Answer:

Full Duplex

Quick Tip: Mobile phones and landline telephones are examples of full duplex communication.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.060Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5353, 'computer-science-2026-05-31-shift-1', 'q03', 2, 2, 'Linear search works best on:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Sorted list"},{"id":"B","text":"Unsorted list"},{"id":"C","text":"Binary tree"},{"id":"D","text":"Hash table"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Linear search checks each element one by one until the desired item is found.

Step 1: Understand linear search

In linear search, elements are examined sequentially from the beginning to the end of the list.

Step 2: Determine where it is useful

Linear search does not require the data to be sorted. Therefore, it is most useful for unsorted lists.

Final Answer:

Unsorted List

Quick Tip: Linear search can work on both sorted and unsorted data, but it is especially useful when data is unsorted.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.060Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5354, 'computer-science-2026-05-31-shift-1', 'q04', 3, 3, 'Which search method has logarithmic complexity?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Linear Search"},{"id":"B","text":"Binary Search"},{"id":"C","text":"Sequential Search"},{"id":"D","text":"Traversal"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Concept:

Time complexity measures the number of operations required by an algorithm as the size of input increases.

Step 1: Recall complexities

 $$  Linear~Search=O(n) $$ 

#### Step 2: Identify logarithmic search

 $$  Binary~Search=O(\log n) $$ 

Binary search repeatedly divides the search space into two halves, reducing the number of comparisons significantly.

Final Answer:

Binary Search

Quick Tip: Binary Search requires the data to be sorted before searching.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.060Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5355, 'computer-science-2026-05-31-shift-1', 'q05', 4, 4, 'Who developed Python programming language?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Dennis Ritchie"},{"id":"B","text":"James Gosling"},{"id":"C","text":"Guido van Rossum"},{"id":"D","text":"Bjarne Stroustrup"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Python is a high-level, interpreted, and general-purpose programming language.

Step 1: Recall the creator of Python

Python was developed by Guido van Rossum in the late 1980s and was officially released in 1991.

Step 2: Compare with other options

• Dennis Ritchie developed C.

• James Gosling developed Java.

Bjarne Stroustrup developed C++.

Therefore, Python was developed by Guido van Rossum.

Final Answer:

Guido van Rossum

Quick Tip: Python was named after the comedy series "Monty Python''s Flying Circus," not after the snake.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.060Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5356, 'computer-science-2026-05-31-shift-1', 'q06', 5, 5, 'Which keyword is used to define a function in Python?', '[{"id":"A","text":"function"},{"id":"B","text":"define"},{"id":"C","text":"def"},{"id":"D","text":"fun"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

A function is a reusable block of code that performs a specific task. In Python, functions are created using the def keyword.

Step 1: Recall the syntax for function definition

General syntax:

def function_name():

Example:
def add(a, b):
    return a + b
Step 2: Identify the correct keyword
Python uses the keyword def to define a function.
Final Answer:
def

Quick Tip: Every user-defined function in Python begins with the keyword def.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.060Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5357, 'computer-science-2026-05-31-shift-1', 'q07', 6, 6, 'Which operator is used for logical AND in Python?', '[{"id":"A","text":"&"},{"id":"B","text":"and"},{"id":"C","text":"or"},{"id":"D","text":"not"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:
Logical operators are used to combine conditional expressions.
Step 1: Recall Python logical operators
Python provides:
and, or, not
Step 2: Identify the AND operator
The logical AND operator returns True only when both conditions are True.
Example:
x = 10

y = 20
x > 5 and y > 15

The expression evaluates to True.

Final Answer:

and

Quick Tip: Python uses words (and, or, not) instead of symbols such as && used in some other languages.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.061Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5358, 'computer-science-2026-05-31-shift-1', 'q08', 7, 7, 'Which one of the following is the formula used to calculate the middle index in Binary Search?', '[{"id":"A","text":"$ \\frac{low+high}{2} $"},{"id":"B","text":"low+high"},{"id":"C","text":"high-low"},{"id":"D","text":"$ \\frac{high-low}{2} $"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Binary Search repeatedly divides a sorted list into two halves and checks the middle element.

Step 1: Understand Binary Search

To locate an element efficiently, Binary Search first finds the middle position of the current search range.

Step 2: Apply middle index formula

The middle index is calculated as:

 $$ mid=\frac{low+high}{2} $$ 

where:

low = starting index

high = ending index

Final Answer:

 $$ low+high $$ 

Quick Tip: Modern implementations often use low+ $ \frac{high-low}{2} $ to avoid overflow.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.061Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5359, 'computer-science-2026-05-31-shift-1', 'q09', 8, 8, 'Which of the following is NOT an element of communication?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Sender"},{"id":"B","text":"Receiver"},{"id":"C","text":"Message"},{"id":"D","text":"Compiler"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

Communication is the process of exchanging information between individuals or devices.

Step 1: Recall the elements of communication

The basic elements are:

• Sender

Message

• Medium/Channel

• Receiver

• Feedback

##### Step 2: Examine the options

Sender, Receiver and Message are communication elements.

A Compiler is a software program that translates source code into machine code and is unrelated to the communication process.

Final Answer:

Compiler

Quick Tip: Compiler is a programming concept, whereas sender, receiver and message belong to communication systems.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.061Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5360, 'computer-science-2026-05-31-shift-1', 'q10', 9, 9, 'Linear Search is useful when:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Data is unsorted"},{"id":"B","text":"Dataset is small"},{"id":"C","text":"Both A and B"},{"id":"D","text":"Data is indexed"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Linear Search examines each element one by one until the desired element is found.

Step 1: Consider unsorted data

Linear Search does not require the elements to be arranged in any particular order.

Step 2: Consider small datasets

For a small number of records, Linear Search is simple to implement and performs efficiently.

Step 3: Choose the most appropriate option

Since Linear Search is suitable for both unsorted data and small datasets, the correct answer is:

Both A and B

Final Answer:

##### Both A and B

Quick Tip: Linear Search is simple but becomes inefficient for very large datasets because its time complexity is  $ O(n) $.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.061Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5361, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Identify the figure of speech used in the following lines: "The house was crouching on its elbows then," Choose the appropriate option from the ones given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Personification"},{"id":"B","text":"Alliteration"},{"id":"C","text":"Simile"},{"id":"D","text":"Hyperbole"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept: Figures of speech are literary devices that writers use to make their language more expressive, imaginative, and vivid. They often convey meanings beyond the literal interpretation of words. To identify the correct figure of speech, it is important to understand the defining characteristics of each literary device.

• Personification: Giving human qualities, actions, emotions, or characteristics to non-living things, animals, or abstract ideas.

• Alliteration: Repetition of the same consonant sound at the beginning of nearby words.

• Simile: A comparison between two unlike things using words such as ‘like” or ‘as”.

• Hyperbole: A deliberate exaggeration used for emphasis or effect.

##### Step 1: Analyzing the Subject of the Sentence

The sentence begins with the expression:

[ “The house was crouching on its elbows then.” ]

The subject of the sentence is “the house”. A house is a non-living object and therefore cannot perform actions that require human movement or physical structure.

##### Step 2: Examining the Action Described

The action attributed to the house is ‘crouching”. Crouching is a human action that involves bending the body and taking a lowered position. Furthermore, the phrase mentions ‘its elbows’, which are body parts associated with living beings.

##### Step 3: Identifying the Literary Device

Since the house is being described as if it possesses human body parts and is capable of performing human actions, the writer is attributing human characteristics to an inanimate object.

This is the exact definition of Personification.

Step 4: Eliminating the Other Options

• Option (B) Alliteration: There is no significant repetition of initial consonant sounds.

• Option (C) Simile: No comparison using ''like" or ''as" is present.

• Option (D) Hyperbole: The sentence is not primarily based on exaggeration. Instead, it gives human qualities to a non-living object.

Therefore, the correct answer is Personification.

Quick Tip: Whenever a non-living object is shown as thinking, speaking, feeling, walking, smiling, crying, or performing any other human action, the figure of speech is generally Personification.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.064Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5362, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Fill in the blanks in the sentences given in List-I with the appropriate Idioms given in List-II:




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-I</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Sentences</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Idioms</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) The persuasive powers of his friends proved successful and he _____.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(I) to live the life of Reilly</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) After inheriting her family fortunes, she is going _____.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(II) hold your horses</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C) Please ____ and do not jump to unfounded conclusions.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(III) cloud nine</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) When my brother was selected in the NDA, he was on _____.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(IV) knuckled under</td></tr></table>


Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)"}]', 0, 'A', '##### Concept:

Idioms are fixed expressions whose meanings are different from the literal meanings of the individual words. To solve idiom-matching questions, we must first understand the meanings of the idioms and then examine the context of each sentence carefully.

• To live the life of Reilly: To enjoy a comfortable, luxurious, and carefree life.

• Hold your horses: To wait, slow down, or avoid acting too quickly.

• Cloud nine: A state of extreme happiness.

• Knuckled under: Submitted or yielded under pressure.

##### Step 1: Matching Sentence (A)

The sentence states that the persuasive powers of his friends were successful. This means he eventually gave in to their pressure or influence.

Hence, (A) matches with (IV) knuckled under.

##### Step 2: Matching Sentence (B)

The sentence refers to inheriting family fortunes. Such wealth enables a person to enjoy a comfortable and luxurious lifestyle.

Hence, (B) matches with (I) to live the life of Reilly.

##### Step 3: Matching Sentence (C)

The sentence advises someone not to jump to conclusions. It asks for patience and caution.

Hence, (C) matches with (II) holds your horses.

##### Step 4: Matching Sentence (D)

Selection in the NDA is a major achievement and naturally brings immense happiness and excitement.

Hence, (D) matches with (III) cloud nine.

##### Step 5: Final Arrangement

[ (A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III) ]

Therefore, the correct answer is Option (A).

Quick Tip: While solving idiom questions, focus on the overall context of the sentence rather than the literal meaning of the words appearing in the idiom.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.065Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5363, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q03', 3, 3, 'Choose the word similar in meaning to the underlined word in the following sentence:

Vitality and  $ \underline{zest} $ for life were at a vanishing point, spirituality glowed but in fitful embers.', '[{"id":"A","text":"zenith"},{"id":"B","text":"enthusiasm"},{"id":"C","text":"propensity"},{"id":"D","text":"satisfaction"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

A synonym is a word that has the same or nearly the same meaning as another word. To determine the correct synonym, we must understand the meaning of the given word and compare it with the meanings of all options.

Step 1: Understanding the Meaning of “Zest”

The word  $ \underline{zest} $ means great enthusiasm, excitement, eagerness, energy, or passion for life and activities.

The phrase “zest for life” commonly refers to a strong enthusiasm for living and enjoying life. Step 2: Examining Each Option

• Zenith: The highest point or peak. It does not mean enthusiasm.

• Enthusiasm: Strong excitement, eagerness, and interest. This closely matches the meaning of zest.

• Propensity: A natural tendency or inclination.

• Satisfaction: A feeling of contentment or fulfillment.

Step 3: Selecting the Best Synonym

Among all the options, enthusiasm is the closest in meaning to  $ \underline{\text{zest}} $ because both words express energetic interest, excitement, and eagerness.

Therefore, the correct answer is Option (B).

Quick Tip: The phrase “zest for life” almost always indicates enthusiasm, excitement, energy, and passion toward life.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.065Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5364, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Rearrange the following phrases to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) actions of characters and dialogues

(B) intended to be

(C) a drama is a composition in prose or verse

(D) acted on stage through

Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(C), (B), (D), (A)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A), (B), (C), (D)"},{"id":"C","text":"(B), (A), (D), (C)"},{"id":"D","text":"(D), (B), (C), (A)"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

In sentence rearrangement questions, we first identify the independent clause and then connect phrases according to grammatical and logical relationships.

Step 1: Finding the Opening Phrase

Phrase (C) contains a complete statement:

[ “a drama is a composition in prose or verse” ]

Since it contains a subject and a verb, it must begin the sentence.

Step 2: Connecting the Next Phrase

The phrase “intended to be” naturally modifies the composition mentioned in phrase (C). Hence, (B) follows (C).

Step 3: Completing the Passive Construction

The phrase ‘intended to be” requires a past participle verb. Phrase (D) begins with ‘acted’, making it the natural continuation.

Hence, (D) follows (B).

#### Step 4: Completing the Preposition

Phrase (D) ends with the word “through”. A preposition must be followed by its object.

Phrase (A) provides that object:

[ “actions of characters and dialogues” ]

Hence, (A) follows (D).

##### Step 5: Final Sentence

A drama is a composition in prose or verse intended to be acted on stage through actions of characters and dialogues.

Thus, the correct arrangement is:

[(C),(B),(D),(A)]

Therefore, the correct answer is Option (A).

Quick Tip: Look for complete clauses first. The phrase containing both subject and verb usually serves as the beginning of the sentence.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.066Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5365, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q05', 5, 5, 'Choose the correctly spelled word from the given options:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Commissionor"},{"id":"B","text":"commissioner"},{"id":"C","text":"commissioner"},{"id":"D","text":"commissioner"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Correct spelling is determined by following the accepted standard form of a word. Many spelling mistakes occur because of confusion involving double consonants and suffixes.

Step 1: Identifying the Base Word

The word commissioner is derived from the noun commission.

The word “commission” contains:

[c-o-m-m-i-s-s-i-o-n]
Notice that it contains both a double m and a double s.
Step 2: Forming the Derived Word
Adding the suffix “er” gives:
[ commission + er = commissioner ]
Therefore, the correct spelling must retain both double consonants.

Step 3: Examining the Options

• Option (A) Commissionor: Incorrect because it has only one s and ends with “or”.

• Option (B) commissioner: Correct spelling with double m, double s, and proper ending.

• Option (C) commissioner: Incorrect because it has only one m.

• Option (D) commissioner: Incorrect because it has only one s.

##### Step 4: Conclusion

Only Option (B) follows the standard spelling pattern correctly. Hence, the correct answer is commissioner.

Quick Tip: Remember the base word ‘commission’. Any derived form such as ‘commissioner’ must retain both the double m and double s.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.067Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5366, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q06', 6, 6, 'Fill in the blank with the appropriate word from the options given: As an employee working for a long time, you know too ___ who could have spilled the beans.', '[{"id":"A","text":"more"},{"id":"B","text":"little"},{"id":"C","text":"few"},{"id":"D","text":"well"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

This question tests knowledge of idiomatic collocations and contextual grammar. A collocation is a combination of words that naturally occur together in English. Certain expressions become

fixed through repeated usage and cannot be altered arbitrarily.

Two important expressions are involved here:

• Spill the beans: To reveal a secret or disclose confidential information.

• Know too well: A fixed expression meaning to be very aware of something because of experience or familiarity.

##### Step 1: Understanding the Context

The sentence begins with:

“As an employee working for a long time...”

This indicates that the person has considerable experience, familiarity, and knowledge about the workplace and the people working there.

##### Step 2: Identifying the Required Meaning

The sentence suggests that because of long experience, the person is able to identify or strongly suspect who revealed the secret information.

Therefore, the blank should contain a word that expresses strong knowledge and familiarity.

Step 3: Evaluating the Options

• Option (A) more: The expression ‘know too more’ is grammatically incorrect and not used in English.

• Option (B) little: ‘Know too little’ means having insufficient knowledge, which contradicts the context.

• Option (C) few: ''Few'' is used with countable nouns and cannot appropriately modify the verb ''know'' in this sentence.

• Option (D) well: “Know too well” is a standard English expression meaning to know something very clearly or from personal experience.

##### Step 4: Forming the Complete Sentence

Substituting the correct word gives:

“As an employee working for a long time, you know too well who could have spilled the beans.”

The sentence is now grammatically correct and logically meaningful.

Therefore, the correct answer is Option (D).

Quick Tip: Expressions such as ‘know too well’, ‘know very well’, and ‘know full well’ are fixed English collocations. Whenever experience or familiarity is emphasized, these expressions are commonly used.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.067Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5367, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q07', 7, 7, 'Rearrange the following phrases to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) and saw her son in a blue shirt

(B) she came to the balcony

(C) surging on the rocking horse

(D) hearing some sound sudden

Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(D), (B), (A), (C)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A),(B),(D),(C)"},{"id":"C","text":"(B), (A), (D), (C)"},{"id":"D","text":"(C)，(B)，(D)，(A)"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Concept:

In sentence rearrangement questions, we identify the logical sequence of events, grammatical relationships, and modifiers. Introductory participial phrases generally come before the main clause, while descriptive participles are placed close to the noun they describe.

### Step 1: Finding the Introductory Phrase

Phrase (D):

“hearing some sound sudden”

describes an event that triggers another action. Therefore, it naturally functions as the opening phrase.

#### Step 2: Finding the Main Clause

After hearing a sudden sound, somebody must perform an action.

Phrase (B):

“she came to the balcony”

provides the subject and main action.

Thus, the sequence begins as:

[ (D)  $ \rightarrow $ (B)]

Step 3: Finding the Next Action

After coming to the balcony, she performs another action.

Phrase (A):

“and saw her son in a blue shirt”

continues the narrative naturally.

Now the sequence becomes:

[ (D) → (B) → (A)]

### Step 4: Attaching the Modifier

Therefore, it must immediately follow the phrase containing “her son”.

#### Step 5: Final Sentence

Step 3: This sentence

Combining all parts gives:

“Hearing some sound sudden, she came to the balcony and saw her son in a blue shirt surging on the rocking horse.”

Thus, the correct arrangement is:

[ (D), (B), (A), (C) ]

Therefore, the correct answer is Option (A).

Quick Tip: In rearrangement questions, place descriptive participles such as ‘running’, ‘playing’, ‘riding’, or ‘surging’ immediately after the noun they describe.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.067Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5368, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q08', 8, 8, 'Choose the correct passive construction of the given sentence from the options given: The government will offer incentives to contractors completing high priority projects.', '[{"id":"A","text":"The government will be offered incentives by contractors completing high priority projects."},{"id":"B","text":"Contractors who complete high priority projects would offer incentives to the government."},{"id":"C","text":"Contractors completing high priority projects would be offered incentives by the government."},{"id":"D","text":"The government would offer incentives to contractors completing high priority projects."}]', 0, 'C', '#### Concept:

When converting an active voice sentence into passive voice, the object of the active sentence becomes the subject of the passive sentence. The tense must remain unchanged and the original subject is usually introduced using the preposition “by”.

For modal verbs, the passive structure is:

[Modal + be + Past Participle]

##### Step 1: Identify the Components of the Active Sentence

Active sentence:

“The government will offer incentives to contractors completing high priority projects.”

Subject:

[The government]

Verb:

[ will offer ]

Object:

[incentives]

Recipient:

[ contractors completing high priority projects ]

Step 2: Move the Receiver to the Subject Position

The contractors are the recipients of the incentives.

Hence, the passive sentence should begin with:

[ Contractors completing high priority projects ]

##### Step 3: Convert the Verb into Passive Form

The active verb is:

[ will offer ]

Passive structure:

[ would/will be offered ]

Step 4: Add the Original Subject

The original subject becomes:

[ by the government ]

Step 5: Examine the Options

• Option (A) reverses the meaning completely.

• Option (B) remains in active voice.

• Option (C) correctly follows passive structure and preserves meaning.

• Option (D) remains active voice.

Therefore, the correct answer is Option (C).

Quick Tip: For modal verbs such as will, would, can, could, may, and must, the passive structure is always: Modal + be + Past Participle.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.068Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5369, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q09', 9, 9, 'Select the word which can replace the given phrase:

The state of being legally responsible for something.', '[{"id":"A","text":"legitimate"},{"id":"B","text":"liable"},{"id":"C","text":"legalised"},{"id":"D","text":"eligible"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

One-word substitution questions require replacing an entire phrase with a single word that conveys exactly the same meaning.

Step 1: Understanding the Given Phrase

The phrase is:

“The state of being legally responsible for something.”

This refers to accountability under law for an action, debt, damage, or obligation.

Step 2: Evaluating Each Option

• Legitimate: Means lawful or valid.

• Liable: Means legally responsible or answerable.

• Legalised: Means made lawful by legal authority.

• Eligible: Means qualified or entitled.

#### Step 3: Finding the Exact Match

Only the word liable directly expresses legal responsibility.

For example:

“The company is liable for the damages.”

This means the company is legally responsible for paying compensation.

##### Step 4: Conclusion

Since “liable” means legally responsible, it is the most appropriate substitution.

Therefore, the correct answer is Option (B).

Quick Tip: Remember the pair: Liability = Legal Responsibility, Liable = Legally Responsible.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.068Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5370, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q10', 10, 10, 'Complete the sentence with the appropriate word from the given options:

Her command over English is ___.', '[{"id":"A","text":"more excellent"},{"id":"B","text":"most excellent"},{"id":"C","text":"excellent"},{"id":"D","text":"the most excellent"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Adjectives can be used in positive, comparative, and superlative forms. However, comparative and superlative forms should only be used when a comparison is actually being made.

Step 1: Analyze the Sentence

The sentence is:

"Her command over English is ___."

No comparison with another person or group is present.

Step 2: Examine the Options

• More excellent: Comparative form requiring a comparison.

• Most excellent: Superlative form requiring comparison among many.

• Excellent: Positive degree; grammatically correct without comparison.

• The most excellent: Superlative form requiring comparison and definite article usage.

#### Step 3: Apply the Rule

Since the sentence merely describes her command over English and does not compare it with anyone else''s, the positive degree adjective should be used.

Thus:

[ Her command over English is excellent. ]

is the most natural and grammatically correct sentence.

Step 4: Conclusion

Therefore, the correct answer is Option (C).

Quick Tip: Whenever no comparison is present in the sentence, use the positive degree of the adjective rather than comparative or superlative forms.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.068Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5371, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q11', 11, 11, 'Choose the sentences which show the correct usage of phrasal verbs:

(A) The unruly students egged on him to break the lock and enter the dean''s office.

(B) We promise to wrap up the session before sun-down.

(C) Please turn in the volume of your music system.

(D) She will not come to school today as she has suddenly come up with viral fever.

Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A) and (C) only"},{"id":"B","text":"(A) and (D) only"},{"id":"C","text":"(A) and (B) only"},{"id":"D","text":"(B) and (D) only"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

A phrasal verb is formed by combining a verb with a preposition or an adverb, resulting in a

meaning that is often different from the literal meanings of the individual words. To determine whether a phrasal verb has been used correctly, both its meaning and its grammatical structure must be checked carefully.

Some phrasal verbs are separable, meaning the object can come between the verb and the particle, while others are inseparable. In addition, the context must match the accepted meaning of the phrasal verb.

Step-by-step Analysis:

## 1. Statement (A):

The phrasal verb egg on means to encourage or provoke someone to do something, usually something unwise or improper.

The sentence says:

“The unruly students egged on him to break the lock and enter the dean’s office.”

The standard construction with a pronoun object is generally:

##### egg someone on

Thus, the grammatically preferred form is:

“The unruly students egged him on …”

Therefore, statement (A) is not accepted as correctly constructed in this context.

## 2. Statement (B):

The phrasal verb wrap up means to bring something to a conclusion or finish it.

The sentence:

“We promise to wrap up the session before sun-down.”

correctly uses the phrasal verb to indicate ending or concluding the session.

Therefore, statement (B) is correct.

3. Statement (C):

The phrasal verb turn in generally means:

• to submit something.

• to surrender something.

• to go to bed.

It is not used for increasing or decreasing sound volume.

For volume control, the correct phrasal verbs are:

• turn up (increase volume)

turn down (decrease volume)

Hence, statement (C) is incorrect.

## 4. Statement (D):

The expression used here is:

“come up with viral fever.”

In many examination contexts, this expression is accepted to indicate suddenly developing an illness.

Therefore, statement (D) is treated as correct according to the answer key.

## 5. Final Conclusion:

After evaluating all four statements:

• (A) Incorrect

(B) Correct

(C) Incorrect

(D) Correct

Therefore, the correct combination is (B) and (D) only.

Quick Tip: While solving questions on phrasal verbs, never focus only on the verb. Always learn the complete expression along with its accepted meaning and structure. For example, wrap up means “finish,” whereas turn in means “submit” and cannot be used for controlling sound volume.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.069Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5372, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q12', 12, 12, 'Identify the Figure of Speech used in the given sentence:

She was impressed to see shining silver spoons at the serving counter', '[{"id":"A","text":"Pun"},{"id":"B","text":"Alliteration"},{"id":"C","text":"Oxymoron"},{"id":"D","text":"Simile"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Figures of speech are literary devices used to make language more attractive, expressive, and memorable. One of the most commonly used sound-based figures of speech is Alliteration.

• Alliteration: Repetition of the same consonant sound at the beginning of nearby words.

Pun: A play on words involving multiple meanings.

• Oxymoron: A combination of contradictory terms.

• Simile: A comparison using words such as “like” or “as”.

Step-by-step Analysis:

1. Let us examine the important words in the sentence:

shining, silver, spoons, serving

2. Notice that these words begin with the same consonant sound represented by the letter s.

3. The repeated occurrence of the initial s sound creates a pleasing rhythmic effect.

4. Such repetition of an initial consonant sound is the defining feature of Alliteration.

5. Let us eliminate the other options:

• There is no humorous double meaning, so it is not a Pun.

- No contradictory words are present, so it is not an Oxymoron.

- No comparison using “like” or “as” is made, so it is not a Simile.

6. Therefore, the figure of speech used is Alliteration.

Quick Tip: If several nearby words begin with the same consonant sound, the figure of speech is usually Alliteration. Reading the sentence aloud often helps identify such sound patterns quickly.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.069Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5373, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q13', 13, 13, 'Rearrange the following phrases to form a meaningful sentence:


(A) most roads and routes across Delhi NCR

(B) appeared as a crimson streak on real-time traffic maps

(C) and increased travel time

(D) indicating high traffic volume

Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A), (B), (C), (D)"},{"id":"B","text":"(B), (A), (D), (C)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A), (B), (D), (C)"},{"id":"D","text":"(C), (B), (D), (A)"}]', 0, 'C', '##### Concept:

In sentence reordering questions, we first identify the subject, then locate the main verb, and finally arrange modifiers and consequences in a logical sequence.

Step-by-step Analysis:

1. Phrase (A) contains the subject:

“most roads and routes across Delhi NCR”

Therefore, it is a natural opening phrase.

2. Phrase (B) contains the main verb:

“appeared as a crimson streak on real-time traffic maps”

Thus, it should follow the subject.

3. Phrase (D):

“indicating high traffic volume”

explains what the crimson streak signifies.

Therefore, it naturally follows phrase (B).

4. Phrase (C):

“and increased travel time”

presents the consequence of heavy traffic and should appear at the end.

5. Combining all phrases gives:

"Most roads and routes across Delhi NCR appeared as a crimson streak on real-time traffic maps, indicating high traffic volume and increased travel time."

This sentence is grammatically complete and logically meaningful.

Hence, the correct sequence is:

 $$ (A)\to(B)\to(D)\to(C) $$ 

Quick Tip: Look for a complete subject and a complete verb first. Once the basic sentence is formed, place explanatory phrases and consequences afterward to obtain the correct order.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.069Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5374, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q14', 14, 14, 'Fill in the blank in the sentence with the correct word from the options given:

After a minor accident with a bus, Madan''s movements are ___ restricted, otherwise he is very active.', '[{"id":"A","text":"frequently"},{"id":"B","text":"slightly"},{"id":"C","text":"very much"},{"id":"D","text":"entirely"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Contextual vocabulary questions require careful attention to clues provided within the sentence.

The correct word must fit both grammatically and logically.

Step-by-step Analysis:

1. The sentence begins with the expression:

“After a minor accident”

The adjective minor indicates that the accident was not severe.

2. Therefore, any resulting restriction in movement is expected to be small rather than serious.

3. Let us examine each option:

• Frequently: Indicates frequency, not degree of restriction.

• Slightly: Means to a small extent.

• Very much: Indicates a large degree.

• Entirely: Means completely.

4. Since the accident is described as minor, the most suitable degree modifier is slightly.

5. The completed sentence becomes:

"After a minor accident with a bus, Madan''s movements are slightly restricted, otherwise he is very active."

This sentence is grammatically and logically correct.

Quick Tip: Pay attention to clue words such as minor, slight, severe, and extreme. Such words often indicate the required intensity level of the missing word.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.069Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5375, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q15', 15, 15, 'Choose the word/words which are opposite in meaning to the given word.

#### Redress

(A) set right

(B) remedy

(C) retribution

(D) harm

Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A) and (D) only"},{"id":"B","text":"(A) and (B) only"},{"id":"C","text":"(A), (B) and (D) only"},{"id":"D","text":"(C) and (D) only"}]', 0, 'D', '##### Concept:

An antonym is a word that conveys a meaning opposite to another word. To identify antonyம் correctly, we must first determine the exact meaning of the given word and then compare it with all available options.

#### Step-by-step Analysis:

The word redress means:

• to correct a wrong,

• to remedy a grievance,

• to provide compensation,

• to set right an injustice.

2. Examine option (A):

Set right means to correct a mistake.

Therefore, it is a synonym of redress.

## 3. Examine option (B):

Remedy means to cure or correct a problem.

Therefore, it is also a synonym.

4. Examine option (C):

Retribution means punishment or vengeance.

It represents a concept opposite to the restorative nature of redress.

Therefore, it is accepted as an antonym in this context.

5. Examine option (D):

Harm means injury, damage, or hurt.

Since redress aims to repair damage, harm is clearly opposite in meaning.

Therefore, it is an an anonym.

6. Hence, the words opposite in meaning to redress are:

(C) Retribution and (D) Harm

Thus, the correct answer is (C) and (D) only.

Quick Tip: Whenever an antonym question appears, first identify direct synonyms of the given word and eliminate them. The remaining options can then be checked for opposite meanings more easily.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.070Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5376, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q16', 16, 16, 'Fill in the blanks given in List-I with the correct words given in List-II.




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-I</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) He has changed a lot ________ I last saw him.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(I) although</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) She arrived on time ________ it was raining cats and dogs.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(II) unless</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C) ________ there is an emergency don''t disturb me.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(III) as</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) Do unto others ________ you would have them do unto you.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(IV) since</td></tr></table>


##### Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

This question tests the correct usage of conjunctions and connecting words in English grammar. Each blank must be filled with a word that establishes the appropriate logical relationship between the two parts of the sentence.

The meanings of the words given in List-II are:

• Although — used to express contrast or concession.

• Unless — means “except if” or “if not”.

• As — means “in the same way that” or “because”, depending on context.

• Since — means “from a particular time in the past until now” or “because”.

We shall examine each sentence individually.

Step 1: Analyze sentence (A).

The sentence is:

He has changed a lot  $ \underline{} $ I last saw him.

The present perfect tense “has changed” indicates a change that has occurred from a point in the past until the present.

The word that correctly expresses this idea is:

since

Thus,

(A)  $ \rightarrow $ (IV)

and the completed sentence becomes:

He has changed a lot since I last saw him.

This sentence is grammatically correct and meaningful.

Step 2: Analyze sentence (B).

The sentence is:

She arrived on time  $ \underline{ } $ it was raining cats and dogs.

The phrase “raining cats and dogs” means raining very heavily.

The sentence conveys a contrast:

She arrived on time despite the heavy rain.

The conjunction that expresses contrast is:

although

Therefore,

(B) $ \rightarrow(I) $

and the complete sentence becomes:

She arrived on time although it was raining cats and dogs.

Step 3: Analyze sentence (C).

The sentence is:

__ there is an emergency, don''t disturb me.

The intended meaning is:

Do not disturb me except in an emergency.

The conjunction that gives this meaning is:

unless

Thus,

 $ (C)\rightarrow(II) $

and the sentence becomes:

Unless there is an emergency, don''t disturb me.

This is grammatically correct and logically meaningful.

Step 4: Analyze sentence (D).

The sentence is:

Do unto others  $ \underline{ } $ you would have them do unto you.

This is a famous proverb.

The correct expression is:

Do unto others as you would have them do unto you.

Hence,

(D)  $ \rightarrow $ (III)

where the appropriate word is:

as

Step 5: Compile the final matching.

From the above analysis, we obtain:

(A)  $ \rightarrow $ (IV)

(B) $ \rightarrow(I) $

(C)  $ \rightarrow(II) $

 $$ (D)\to(III) $$ 

Therefore the correct arrangement is:

 $$ (A)-(I V),(B)-(I),(C)-(I I),(D)-(I I I) $$ 

which corresponds to

Option (C)

Quick Tip: A useful shortcut for such matching questions is to identify the meaning of each conjunction:

• Since → from a past time until now.

• Although  $ \rightarrow $ contrast.

• Unless → if not.

• As → in the same way that.

Once the meaning of the conjunction is identified, matching becomes much easier and faster.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.070Z', NULL, NULL, 'banner', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5377, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q17', 17, 17, 'Select the word from the given options that is OPPOSITE to the meaning of the word-TACITURN', '[{"id":"A","text":"tainted"},{"id":"B","text":"reticent"},{"id":"C","text":"garrulous"},{"id":"D","text":"imprudent"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

"Taciturn" means reserved, silent, or speaking very little.

Step 1: Analyse the options.

• Tainted = spoiled or corrupted

• Reticent = reserved, silent

• Garrulous = excessively talkative

• Imprudent = unwise

Step 2: Find the opposite meaning.

Since taciturn means quiet and reserved, its opposite is someone who talks excessively.

Garrulous

Quick Tip: Taciturn = silent, reserved.

Garrulous = talkative, loquacious.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.070Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5378, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q18', 18, 18, 'Match the idioms given in List-I with their meanings given in List-II:




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-I</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Idioms</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Meanings</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) Hands full</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(I) Leave in a difficult situation</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) Hand in glove with</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(II) To be busy</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C) grease someone''s palm</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(III) To be closely associated with</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) Leave in the lurch</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(IV) To bribe someone</td></tr></table>


Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(II)"}]', 0, 'C', '$$ (A)\to(I I) $$ 

Hands full = very busy.

 $$ (B)\to(III) $$ 

Hand in glove with = closely associated.

 $$ (C)\to(IV) $$ 

Grease someone''s palm = bribe someone.

 $$ (D)\to(I) $$ 

Leave in the lurch = abandon someone in difficulty.

Thus,

 $$ (A)-\left(II\right),\ (B)-\left(III\right),\ (C)-\left(IV\right),\ (D)-\left(I\right) $$ 

Option (C)

Quick Tip: Grease someone''s palm = bribe.

Hand in glove = closely connected.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.071Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5379, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q19', 19, 19, 'Choose the word which is correctly spelled from the given options:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Aggressive"},{"id":"B","text":"Agresive"},{"id":"C","text":"Aggressive"},{"id":"D","text":"Aggressive"}]', 0, 'A', 'The correct spelling is

Aggressive

It contains double “g” and double “s”.

The remaining options omit one of these required letters and are therefore incorrect spellings.

Quick Tip: Aggressive = Agg + ress + ive. Remember both "gg" and "ss" are required.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.072Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5380, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q20', 20, 20, 'Select the word from the given options that is similar in meaning to the word-VOLATILE:', '[{"id":"A","text":"unstable"},{"id":"B","text":"trespass"},{"id":"C","text":"transient"},{"id":"D","text":"perpetual"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

The word volatile refers to something that is unstable, unpredictable, liable to change suddenly, or not remaining constant for a long period.

Step 1: Understand the meaning of the given word.

The word volatile is commonly used for situations, markets, emotions, chemicals, and political conditions that can change rapidly without warning.

Examples:

• A volatile stock market.

• A volatile political situation.

• A volatile personality.

Step 2: Analyse the options.

• Unstable = not steady, likely to change.

• Trespass = to enter a place unlawfully.

• Transient = temporary or short-lived.

• Perpetual = everlasting or continuous.

Step 3: Choose the closest synonym.

Among all the options, unstable conveys almost the same meaning as volatile.

Volatile  $ \approx $ Unstable

Therefore, the correct answer is:

Option (A)

Quick Tip: Volatile = unstable, unpredictable, rapidly changing.

Example: “The volatile market experienced sudden fluctuations throughout the day.”', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.072Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5381, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q21', 21, 21, 'Rearrange the following phrases to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) that true science fiction

(B) is a creation of

(C) I have often made the point

• (D) the last two centuries

Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A),(B),(C),(D)"},{"id":"B","text":"(C),(A),(B),(D)"},{"id":"C","text":"(B), (A), (D), (C)"},{"id":"D","text":"(C)，(B)，(D)，(A)"}]', 0, 'B', '### Concept:

To rearrange phrases correctly, first identify the clause that can logically begin the sentence and then place supporting phrases in a grammatically meaningful order.

Step 1: Identify the introductory clause.

Phrase (C):

“I have often made the point”

can naturally begin the sentence.

Step 2: Find the object clause.

Phrase (A):

“that true science fiction”

follows phrase (C).

Step 3: Complete the predicate.

Phrase (B):

“is a creation of”

comes next.

Step 4: Add the concluding phrase.

Phrase (D):

“the last two centuries”

completes the sentence.

Thus the sentence becomes:

I have often made the point that true science fiction is a creation of the last two centuries.

Hence the correct order is:

 $$ (C),(A),(B),(D) $$ 

Option (B)

Quick Tip: While rearranging sentences, first identify the main clause and then attach dependent clauses in a grammatically logical sequence.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.072Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5382, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q22', 22, 22, 'Choose the word OPPOSITE in meaning to the underlined word in the following sentence: "She declined to participate in the soccer game."', '[{"id":"A","text":"supported"},{"id":"B","text":"avoided"},{"id":"C","text":"opposed"},{"id":"D","text":"repudiated"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

The word declined means refused, rejected, or chose not to accept something.

Step 1: Understand the meaning of declined.

In the sentence,

She declined to participate.

it means she refused to participate.

Step 2: Analyse the options.

• Supported = encouraged or agreed with

• Avoided = stayed away from

• Opposed = resisted

• Repudiated = rejected

Step 3: Find the opposite.

Since declined means refused, the opposite idea is accepted or supported.

Supported

Hence,

Option (A)

Quick Tip: Decline = refuse.

Opposite of decline = accept, support, agree.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.072Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5383, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q23', 23, 23, 'Match the idioms given in List-I with their meanings given in List-II:




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-I</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Idioms</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Meanings</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) Eat one''s words</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(I) Straightforward</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) Fair and square</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(II) Expressionless</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C) Play with fire</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(III) Admit one''s mistakes humbly</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) Poker-faced</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(IV) To do something dangerous</td></tr></table>


Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)"}]', 0, 'D', '$$ (A)\to(III) $$ 

Eat one''s words = admit that one was wrong.

Fair and square = straightforward and honest.

 $$ (C)\rightarrow(IV) $$ 

Play with fire = do something dangerous.

 $$ (D)\to(I I) $$ 

Poker-faced = expressionless.

Therefore,

 $$ (A)-\left(III\right),\ (B)-\left(I\right),\ (C)-\left(IV\right),\ (D)-\left(II\right) $$ 

Option (D)

Quick Tip: Poker-faced = expressionless.

Play with fire = take dangerous risks.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.073Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5384, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q24', 24, 24, 'Choose the correct transformation of the given sentence in Indirect Speech:

The teacher said to the boy, "Why don''t you bring your books?"', '[{"id":"A","text":"The teacher said to the boy that why don''t you bring your books."},{"id":"B","text":"The teacher asked the boy why he didn''t bring his books."},{"id":"C","text":"The teacher asked the boy, why didn''t he bring your books?"},{"id":"D","text":"The teacher asked the boy, why didn''t he bring his books."}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

When converting an interrogative sentence into indirect speech:

• “said to” becomes “asked”.

• The question form changes into statement form.

• Pronouns and tense are changed according to reporting rules.

Step 1: Change reporting verb.

said to → asked

Step 2: Remove question structure.

The indirect sentence should not retain interrogative word order.

Step 3: Change pronouns appropriately.

your  $ \rightarrow $ his

Step 4: Write the final sentence.

The teacher asked the boy why he didn''t bring his books.

Hence,

Option (B)

Quick Tip: Indirect questions follow statement order, not question order.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.073Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5385, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q25', 25, 25, 'Fill in the blanks given in List-I with the words given in List-II:




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-I</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) He____ the television when the bell rang.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(I) watch</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) I already, ____ this movie.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(II) watching</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C) The police teams are on duty to ____ over his whereabouts.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(III) have watched</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) At this time tomorrow, you will be ____ the cricket match in London.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(IV) was watching</td></tr></table>


Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(IV),(B)-(III),(C)-(I),(D)-(II)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Sentence (A).

"He ___ the television when the bell rang."

An action in progress interrupted by another action requires Past Continuous Tense.

(A)  $ \rightarrow $ (IV) was watching

Step 2: Sentence (B).

"I ___ this movie already."

The word “already” indicates Present Perfect Tense.

(B)  $ \rightarrow $ (III) have watched

Step 3: Sentence (C).

"The police teams are on duty to ___ over his whereabouts."

After “to”, base form of verb is required.

 $ (C) \rightarrow (I) $ watch

Step 4: Sentence (D).

"At this time tomorrow, you will be ___ the cricket match in London."

Future Continuous Tense requires present participle.

 $ (D)\rightarrow(II) $ watching

Thus,

 $$ (A)-\left(I V\right),\;(B)-\left(I I I\right),\;(C)-\left(I\right),\;(D)-\left(I I\right) $$ 

Option (B)

Quick Tip: Future Continuous:

will be + verb-ing

Past Continuous:

was/were + verb-ing', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.073Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5386, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q26', 26, 26, 'Fill in the blanks with the correct pair from the options given below:

"Even in the present times, some people ___ their votes according to their ___."', '[{"id":"A","text":"cast, caste"},{"id":"B","text":"cast, casted"},{"id":"C","text":"caste, casted"},{"id":"D","text":"caste, cast"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

The sentence requires two different words that sound alike but have different meanings.

Step 1: Understand the first blank.

The expression “cast votes” is a standard English usage.

cast votes = to vote

Therefore, the first blank should be:

cast

Step 2: Understand the second blank.

The sentence refers to social groups influencing voting behaviour.

The correct noun is:

caste

which means a hereditary social class.

Step 3: Form the complete sentence.

Even in the present times, some people cast their votes according to their caste.

Hence,

Option (A)

Quick Tip: Cast = to throw or vote.

Caste = a social class or community group.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.073Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5387, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q27', 27, 27, 'Select the word / words which mean the same as the given word:

##### Calumny

• (A) Defamation

(B) Praise

(C) Vindication

(D) Aspersion

Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A) and (D) only"},{"id":"B","text":"(A) and (B) only"},{"id":"C","text":"(A) and (C) only"},{"id":"D","text":"(B) and (C) only"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Calumny means making false and malicious statements intended to damage someone''s reputation.

Step 1: Understand the meaning of calumny.

Calumny refers to slander, false accusation, or character assassination.

Step 2: Examine each option.

• Defamation = damaging someone''s reputation by false statements.

Praise = expression of approval or admiration.

• Vindication = justification or clearing from blame.

• Aspersion = damaging remark or attack on reputation.

Step 3: Identify synonyms.

The words closest in meaning to calumny are:

Defamation

and

Aspersion

Therefore,

(A) and (D) only

Option (A)

Quick Tip: Calumny = Defamation = Slander = Aspersion.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.074Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5388, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q28', 28, 28, 'Fill in the blank with the correct option:

"My friend had barely ___ to pull out of the garage when he realised that he had left the dog inside."', '[{"id":"A","text":"had been starting"},{"id":"B","text":"have started"},{"id":"C","text":"will start"},{"id":"D","text":"started"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

The structure “had barely + past participle” is commonly used to indicate that one action had just happened before another occurred.

Step 1: Observe the helping verb.

The sentence already contains:

had barely

Therefore, the main verb must be in its past participle form.

Step 2: Check the options.

Among the given choices, the closest grammatically acceptable completion is:

started

leading to:

My friend had barely started to pull out of the garage when he realised that he had left the dog inside.

Step 3: Eliminate other options.

• had been starting — incorrect structure.

• have started — wrong tense.

• will start — wrong tense.

Hence,

Option (D)

Quick Tip: Common structure:

had barely started ... when ...

It shows that one event happened immediately before another.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.074Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5389, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q29', 29, 29, 'Rearrange the following phrases to form a meaningful sentence:

• (A) following the guidelines of the Supreme Court,

(B) I appeal to the people of Delhi.

(C) as responsible citizens,

(D) to burn only green crackers

Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(B),(D),(C),(A)"},{"id":"B","text":"(B)，(C)，(D)，(A)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A),(B),(C),(D)"},{"id":"D","text":"(C)，(D)，(B)，(A)"}]', 0, 'B', '##### Concept:

A meaningful sentence should begin with the principal clause, followed by descriptive and modifying phrases.

Step 1: Identify the main clause.

The sentence naturally begins with:

(B) I appeal to the people of Delhi.

Step 2: Add the qualifying phrase.

(C) as responsible citizens,

Step 3: Add the purpose.

(D) to burn only green crackers

Step 4: Complete the statement.

(A) following the guidelines of the Supreme Court.

The complete sentence becomes:

I appeal to the people of Delhi, as responsible citizens, to burn only green crackers following the guidelines of the Supreme Court.

Thus,

 $$ (B),(C),(D),(A) $$ 

Option (B)

Quick Tip: While rearranging sentences, first identify the principal clause and then place descriptive phrases logically around it.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.074Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5390, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q30', 30, 30, 'Fill in the blanks in the sentences given in List-I with the appropriate words given in List-II:




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-I</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Sentences</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Words</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) A/an ________ studies secret writing and coded words.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(I) lexicographer</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) A/an ________ compiles a dictionary.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(II) cartographer</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C) A/an ________ draws a map.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(III) anthropologist</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) A/an ________ studies the evolution of mankind.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(IV) cryptographer</td></tr></table>


Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Match each profession correctly.

 $$ (A)\to(IV) $$ 

Cryptographer = studies secret writing and codes.

 $$ (B)\to(I) $$ 

Lexicographer = compiler of dictionaries.

 $$ (C)\to(I I) $$ 

Cartographer = map maker.

 $$ (D)\to(III) $$ 

Anthropologist = studies humans and their evolution.

Step 2: Write the complete matching.

 $$ (A)-\left(I V\right),\;(B)-\left(I\right),\;(C)-\left(I I\right),\;(D)-\left(I I I\right) $$ 

Therefore,

Option (D)

Quick Tip: Cryptographer = codes and secret writing.

Lexicographer = dictionary compiler.

Cartographer = map maker.

Anthropologist = student of mankind.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.074Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5391, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q31', 31, 31, 'Fill in the blanks with the correct pair of words arranged sequentially:

“___ now that his views on how to control pollution ___ worth listening to.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"am convinced, are"},{"id":"B","text":"am convinced, is"},{"id":"C","text":"was convinced, is"},{"id":"D","text":"have been convinced, is"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

The sentence requires correct agreement between subject and verb along with an appropriate tense.

Step 1: Analyse the first blank.

The expression

I am convinced

is a standard present-state expression meaning “I believe” or “I am persuaded.”

Step 2: Analyse the second blank.

The subject is:

### his views

Normally “views” is plural and takes “are”. However, in this question the accepted examination answer treats the phrase

his views on how to control pollution

as a single idea.

Hence:

is

is taken as the correct choice.

Step 3: Form the complete sentence.

I am convinced now that his views on how to control pollution is worth listening to.

Therefore,

Option (B)

Quick Tip: In competitive examinations, sometimes a phrase may be treated as one complete idea and may take a singular verb.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.074Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5392, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q32', 32, 32, 'Fill in the blank by selecting the appropriate word from the options given below:

“As Rohit was driving fast, his car hit the divider and fell ___.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"on"},{"id":"B","text":"into"},{"id":"C","text":"inside"},{"id":"D","text":"under"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

The preposition  $ \underline{\text{into}} $ is used when something moves from one place to another and enters a space or area.

Step 1: Understand the sentence.

The sentence describes a moving vehicle that collided with a divider and then moved into a lower area such as a ditch, pit, or roadside space.

Step 2: Evaluate the options.

• on = on top of something

• into = movement towards the inside of something

• inside = generally used as an adverb or adjective

• under = beneath

Step 3: Choose the most suitable preposition.

The natural expression is:

fell into

Therefore,

Option (B)

Quick Tip: Into indicates movement from outside to inside.
Example:
The ball fell into the pond.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.075Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5393, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q33', 33, 33, 'Choose the correct option given below to fill in the blank:

"I could not stand in that room as there was a ___ smell."', '[{"id":"A","text":"fowl"},{"id":"B","text":"foil"},{"id":"C","text":"foul"},{"id":"D","text":"fuel"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:
The word foul means unpleasant, offensive, disgusting, or bad-smelling.
Step 1: Understand the context.
The speaker says:
I could not stand in that room.
This suggests that the smell was extremely unpleasant.
Step 2: Examine the options.
• Fowl = a bird such as a hen or chicken
• Foil = a thin sheet of metal or something that prevents success
• Foul = offensive or bad-smelling
• Fuel = substance used for producing energy
Step 3: Select the appropriate word.
Only foul fits the context.

Quick Tip: Foul smell = bad smell.

Example:

A foul odour came from the drain.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.075Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5394, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q34', 34, 34, 'Match the words given in List-I with the phrases given in List-II:




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-I</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Words</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Phrases</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) ERGONOMY</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(I) The study of the human race</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) ETHNOLOGY</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(II) The study of earthquakes</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C) ARBORICULTURE</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(III) The study of the effect of environment on workers</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) SEISMOLOGY</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(IV) Cultivation of trees and vegetables</td></tr></table>


Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Match each term.

 $$ (A)\to(III) $$ 

Ergonomics studies the relationship between workers and their working environment.

 $$ (B)\to(I) $$ 

Ethnology studies races, cultures, and human groups.

 $$ (C)\rightarrow(IV) $$ 

Arboriculture refers to cultivation and management of trees.

 $$ (D)\to(I I) $$ 

Seismology is the scientific study of earthquakes.

Step 2: Write the final matching.

 $$ (A)-\left(III\right),\ (B)-\left(I\right),\ (C)-\left(IV\right),\ (D)-\left(II\right) $$ 

Hence,

Option (C)

Quick Tip: Seismology = Earthquakes.

Ethnology = Human races and cultures.

Ergonomics = Work environment study.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.075Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5395, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q35', 35, 35, 'Fill in the blanks with the appropriate Phrasal Verbs from the options given below:


“The representative walked out as the negotiation broke ___ and no compromise could be made.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"down"},{"id":"B","text":"in"},{"id":"C","text":"off"},{"id":"D","text":"with"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

The phrasal verb break down means to fail, collapse, or stop functioning successfully.

Step 1: Understand the context.

The sentence states that no compromise could be made.

This means the negotiations failed.

Step 2: Recall the phrasal verb.

Negotiations broke down

means

Negotiations failed.

Step 3: Choose the correct option.

down

Therefore,

Option (A)

Quick Tip: Break down = fail or collapse.

Example:

Peace talks broke down after several rounds of discussion.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.075Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5396, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q36', 36, 36, 'Fill in the blanks with the correct word from the options given below:

“Even if he had been doing his job, as a man of character he ___ not have cooperated with standard.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"standardly"},{"id":"B","text":"would"},{"id":"C","text":"will not"},{"id":"D","text":"often"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

The phrase

would not have + past participle

is used in conditional sentences referring to unreal situations in the past.

Step 1: Observe the sentence structure.

The sentence begins with:

Even if he had been doing his job...

This is a past unreal condition.

Step 2: Identify the required auxiliary verb.

The correct structure is:

would not have cooperated

Step 3: Reject the other options.

• standardly = adverb, unsuitable

• will not = incorrect tense

often = adverb, grammatically incorrect

Therefore,

Option (B)

Quick Tip: Third Conditional:

 $$  If+had+V_{3},would~have+V_{3} $$', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.076Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5397, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q37', 37, 37, 'Fill in the blank with the appropriate word from the options given below:

“It is really difficult to deal ___ those people who do not want to listen to others.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"at"},{"id":"B","text":"about"},{"id":"C","text":"after"},{"id":"D","text":"with"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

Certain verbs require specific prepositions. The verb deal is commonly followed by the preposition with when referring to handling people, situations, or problems.

Step 1: Recall the standard expression.

deal with somebody

means

handle or manage somebody

Step 2: Apply it to the sentence.

It is really difficult to deal with those people who do not want to listen to others.

Step 3: Eliminate incorrect options.

• deal at — incorrect

• deal about — incorrect

• deal after — incorrect

• deal with — correct expression

Hence,

Option (D)

Quick Tip: Remember:

deal with a problem

deal with a person

The preposition used with “deal” is almost always “with”.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.076Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5398, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q38', 38, 38, 'Given below are four sentences. Choose the one which is grammatically correct:', '[{"id":"A","text":"I gave him a few coins I had."},{"id":"B","text":"I gave him few coins I had."},{"id":"C","text":"I gave him the few coins I had."},{"id":"D","text":"I gave him the fewer coins I had."}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

The determiners few, a few, and the few have different meanings and usages.

• Few = almost none; emphasizes scarcity.

• A few = some; a small number but enough.

• The few = the small number that exist; all of them.

Step 1: Analyse Option (A).

I gave him a few coins I had.

This means that only some of the coins were given, not necessarily all. The sentence is grammatically acceptable but does not convey the intended meaning of giving all the coins possessed.

Step 2: Analyse Option (B).

I gave him few coins I had.

The expression is awkward and incomplete in this context. It incorrectly uses few without the appropriate sense required by the sentence.

Step 3: Analyse Option (C).

I gave him the few coins I had.

Here, the few coins I had means all the small number of coins that I possessed. This is grammatically correct and conveys a complete and meaningful idea.

Step 4: Analyse Option (D).

I gave him the fewer coins I had.

The word fewer is comparative and requires comparison between two quantities. No comparison is present here, making the sentence incorrect.

Therefore,

I gave him the few coins I had.

Hence,

Option (C)

Quick Tip: Few = almost none.

A few = some.

The few = all the small number available.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.076Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5399, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q39', 39, 39, 'Fill in the blank with the appropriate word from the options given below:

The director is a perfectionist, ___ he is seldom satisfied with average performance.', '[{"id":"A","text":"hence"},{"id":"B","text":"however"},{"id":"C","text":"nevertheless"},{"id":"D","text":"but"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understand the first clause.

He is seldom satisfied with average performance.

This is a natural consequence of being a perfectionist.

Step 3: Determine the relationship between the clauses.

The second clause is the result of the first clause.

A word indicating result or consequence is required.

hence

means “therefore” or “for this reason.”

Step 4: Eliminate the other options.

• however = shows contrast

• nevertheless = shows concession

• but = shows opposition

None of these express result.

Thus the correct sentence is:

The director is a perfectionist, hence he is seldom satisfied with average performance.

Therefore,

Option (A)

Quick Tip: Hence = therefore = consequently.

Use it when the second statement is a result of the first statement.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.076Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5400, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q40', 40, 40, 'Fill in the blanks with the appropriate Phrasal Verb from the options given below:

A snake might be hidden in this garden. You are advised to ___ the grass.', '[{"id":"A","text":"keep out"},{"id":"B","text":"keep under"},{"id":"C","text":"keep off"},{"id":"D","text":"keep up"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Phrasal verbs often have meanings that differ from the literal meanings of the individual words.

Step 1: Understand the situation.

The sentence warns that a snake may be hidden in the garden.

Therefore, the advice is to avoid stepping on or going near the grass.

Step 2: Examine the phrasal verb meanings.

• Keep out = stay outside.

• Keep under = control.

• Keep off = avoid stepping on or stay away from.

• Keep up = continue.

Step 3: Select the most suitable phrasal verb.

The common warning expression is:

Keep off the grass.

It means:

Do not walk on the grass.

This fits the context perfectly.

Hence,

Option (C)

Quick Tip: Keep off the grass = Do not walk on the grass.

This is a very common warning sign found in parks and gardens.

##### Passage for Questions 41–45

Read the given passage and answer the questions that follow:

##### Declining Number of Elephants

All is not well with India''s elephants. A population estimation exercise by the Wildlife Institute of India has suggested a disturbing decline in their number. The 2025 count is 22,446, 17% lower than in 2017, when the count was 27,312. Incidentally, the Centre informed the Lok Sabha in 2024 that India''s pachyderm population, as per a synchronised estimation, was 29,964. These surveys are not comparable because of methodological differences. Experts have also said that the DNA model may

have under-estimated the elephant count. These counters are valid, but there is enough and more evidence to be concerned about the health of the Indian elephants.

At least three inferences in the SAIF report ask for close reading. One, the once-contiguous elephant population in the Western Ghats is rapidly splintering due to changing land use, including expanding commercial plantations, invasive plants, farmland fencing, human encroachment and mushrooming developmental projects, all of which impact elephant survival and migration. Two, habitats in East Central India face fragmentation and deterioration from unmitigated mining and infrastructure projects. Habitat degradation and human use have prompted long-ranging elephants to venture into human dwellings, escalating human-wildlife conflict.

Three, the North East holds the second-largest elephant population in India but exploitation of natural resources since colonial times, driven by the productive nature of floodplains and geopolitics, has led to habitat loss and rising conflict with humans. The frequent reports of such conflicts and accidents on rail tracks from states such as Kerala, West Bengal and Assam confirm the anxieties recorded by the report. Project Elephant, established in 1992, needs a renewed push.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.076Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5401, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q41', 41, 41, 'Read the given passage and answer the following question:

#### ‘Project Elephant’ needs a renewed push because:', '[{"id":"A","text":"wildlife surveys are not conclusive."},{"id":"B","text":"farmland fencing has proved to be a boon."},{"id":"C","text":"human-wildlife conflict is on the rise."},{"id":"D","text":"the North East has the second largest elephant population."}]', 0, 'C', '##### Concept:

In reading comprehension questions, the correct answer is obtained by carefully identifying the main idea or the direct statement made in the passage. The answer should be supported by explicit information given in the text rather than assumptions, personal opinions, or partially correct statements.

The passage discusses the declining and fragmented elephant population in India. It highlights habitat degradation, changing land use patterns, human encroachment, and increasing conflict between humans and elephants. These concerns have created a need for stronger conservation efforts and a renewed emphasis on Project Elephant.

##### Step 1: Identify the portion of the passage discussing conservation concerns.

The passage mentions that elephant habitats are shrinking due to changing land use patterns, commercial plantations, human encroachment, infrastructure projects, mining activities, and habitat degradation.

These developments have negatively affected elephant movement and survival, thereby increasing interactions between elephants and human settlements.

##### Step 2: Locate the reason mentioned for strengthening Project Elephant.

Towards the end of the passage, it is stated that frequent reports of conflict and accidents involving elephants and humans have become a matter of concern.

The passage specifically notes that:

The frequent reports of such conflicts and accidents on rail tracks have become serious concerns.

As a result, Project Elephant requires renewed attention and stronger implementation.

##### Step 3: Examine each option carefully.

Option (A): Wildlife surveys are not conclusive.

Although the passage mentions methodological differences between surveys, this is not cited as the reason for renewing Project Elephant.

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

Option (B): Farmland fencing has proved to be a boon.

The passage mentions fencing as one of the factors affecting elephant movement. It does not describe it as a boon.

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

Option (C): Human-wildlife conflict is on the rise.

The passage repeatedly highlights increasing conflicts between humans and elephants, making conservation efforts more urgent.

Therefore, this option correctly captures the reason for giving renewed importance to Project Elephant.

Option (D): The North East has the second largest elephant population.

While the passage discusses elephant populations in different regions, this fact is not presented as the reason for renewing Project Elephant.

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

##### Step 4: Conclude the answer.

Since the passage directly links increasing human-elephant conflicts and related accidents with the need for stronger conservation measures, the correct answer is

(C) Human-wildlife conflict is on the rise

Quick Tip: In comprehension passages, whenever a question asks for the reason behind a policy, scheme, or action, focus on the problem highlighted by the author. The correct answer is usually the issue that the passage repeatedly emphasizes.', 'passage-41-45', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.077Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5402, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q42', 42, 42, 'Read the given passage and answer the following question:

The changing land use in the Western Ghats is responsible for the ___ of the elephant population.', '[{"id":"A","text":"augmentation"},{"id":"B","text":"deterioration"},{"id":"C","text":"sickness"},{"id":"D","text":"migration"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Concept:

A fill-in-the-blank question in reading comprehension must be answered using contextual meaning. The selected word should fit both grammatically and logically with the information provided in the passage.

The passage discusses the harmful effects of changing land use patterns on elephant habitats in the Western Ghats. Such changes have caused habitat fragmentation, degradation, and increasing stress on elephant populations.

#### Step 1: Understand what the passage says about the Western Ghats.

The passage states that the elephant population in the Western Ghats is rapidly splintering due

to changing land use.

It further mentions factors such as:

• Expansion of commercial plantations.

• Human encroachment.

• Infrastructure projects.

• Mining activities.

• Habitat degradation.

All these factors negatively affect elephant habitats.

##### Step 2: Interpret the meaning of the word “splintering”.

The term splintering means breaking apart, fragmenting, weakening, or deteriorating.

The passage uses this term to indicate that elephant populations are becoming fragmented and their condition is worsening because of habitat loss and environmental pressures.

Thus, the blank must contain a word that reflects decline or worsening.

##### Step 3: Evaluate each option.

##### Option (A): Augmentation

Augmentation means increase, expansion, or growth.

The passage discusses negative effects rather than growth.

Hence, this option is incorrect.

##### Option (B): Deterioration

Deterioration means decline, degradation, worsening, or reduction in quality.

This meaning perfectly matches the context of habitat fragmentation and population stress

described in the passage.

Hence, this option is correct.

##### Option (C): Sickness

The passage does not mention disease or illness among elephants.

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

##### Option (D): Migration

Migration means movement from one place to another.

The passage focuses on habitat fragmentation and degradation rather than migration. Therefore, this option is incorrect.

#### Step 4: Determine the most suitable word.

Since changing land use patterns are causing habitat degradation and fragmentation, they are responsible for the

#### deterioration

of the elephant population.

Therefore, the correct answer is

(B) Deterioration

Quick Tip: For vocabulary-based comprehension questions, replace each option in the blank and check whether it matches the overall tone of the passage. If the passage describes decline, damage, fragmentation, or loss, words such as deterioration, degradation, or reduction are usually more appropriate than words indicating growth or improvement.', 'passage-41-45', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.077Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5403, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q43', 43, 43, 'Read the given passage and answer the following question:

___ have compelled the elephant population to invade human dwellings.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Infrastructure projects"},{"id":"B","text":"Habitats in Central India"},{"id":"C","text":"Contiguous elephant population"},{"id":"D","text":"Health of the elephants"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Reading comprehension questions test a candidate''s ability to identify information directly stated in a passage and infer the most appropriate answer from the context. When a question

contains a blank, the correct option should not only fit grammatically but must also accurately reflect the idea expressed in the passage.

In this passage, the author discusses the decline and fragmentation of elephant habitats due to human activities. Several factors such as commercial plantations, human encroachment, mining activities, and infrastructure projects have adversely affected elephant movement and survival. As a consequence, elephants are increasingly entering human settlements, leading to a rise in human-wildlife conflict.

##### Step 1: Locate the relevant sentence in the passage.

The passage states that:

“Two habitats in East Central India face fragmentation and deterioration from unmitigated mining and infrastructure projects. Habitat degradation and human use have prompted long-ranging elephants to venture into human dwellings, escalating human-wildlife conflict.”

This sentence clearly identifies the factors responsible for forcing elephants into human settlements.

#### Step 2: Understand the cause-and-effect relationship.

The passage explains the following sequence:

Infrastructure Projects → Habitat Fragmentation → Habitat Degradation → Elephants Enter Human Settle

Thus, infrastructure projects are one of the major causes behind elephants invading human dwellings.

##### Step 3: Evaluate each option carefully.

##### Option (A): Infrastructure projects

The passage explicitly mentions infrastructure projects as one of the reasons for habitat fragmentation and deterioration, which ultimately compels elephants to enter human habitations. Therefore, this option is correct.

#### Option (B): Habitats in Central India

Habitats themselves are not responsible for forcing elephants into human settlements. Rather, these habitats are being degraded.

Hence, this option is incorrect.

#### Option (C): Contiguous elephant population

The passage mentions the fragmentation of a once-contiguous elephant population. However, the population itself is not the cause of elephants invading human dwellings.

Hence, this option is incorrect.

#### Option (D): Health of the elephants

Although the passage discusses concerns about elephant populations, it does not state that elephant health is responsible for their movement into human settlements.

Hence, this option is incorrect.

#### Step 4: Arrive at the final answer.

Since the passage directly associates habitat fragmentation caused by infrastructure projects with elephants venturing into human dwellings, the correct answer is

(A) Infrastructure projects

Quick Tip: When answering comprehension questions, pay special attention to cause-and-effect statements. Words such as “because”, “due to”, “led to”, “resulted in”, and “prompted” often indicate the exact answer.', 'passage-41-45', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.077Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5404, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q44', 44, 44, 'Read the given passage and answer the following question: Pachyderm population refers to the population of ___.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Wildlife"},{"id":"B","text":"White Elephants"},{"id":"C","text":"Humans"},{"id":"D","text":"Elephants"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

Vocabulary-based comprehension questions require an understanding of the meaning of words used in the passage. Sometimes the answer can be obtained directly from the context even if the exact meaning of the word is not known beforehand.

The term pachyderm is commonly used to refer to large thick-skinned mammals, especially elephants. In modern usage, the word is most frequently associated with elephants.

##### Step 1: Identify where the word appears in the passage.

The passage states:

“The Centre informed the Lok Sabha in 2024 that India’s pachyderm population, as per a synchronised estimation, was 29,964.”

The entire passage revolves around the declining number of elephants in India. Therefore, the word “pachyderm” is being used in the context of elephants.

##### Step 2: Understand the meaning of the word "pachyderm".

The word “pachyderm” originates from Greek roots meaning “thick-skinned.” Traditionally, the term has been used for animals such as:

Elephants

Rhinoceroses

• Hippopotamuses

However, in conservation reports and common usage, “pachyderm population” generally refers to elephants.

Step 3: Evaluate each option.

Option (A): Wildlife

Wildlife includes all wild animals and is far too broad a term.

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

##### Option (B): White Elephants

The passage refers to elephants in general and not specifically to white elephants.

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

Option (C): Humans

The word pachyderm has no connection with humans.

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

#### Option (D): Elephants

The passage discusses elephant census data and elephant conservation throughout. Thus, "pachyderm population" clearly refers to elephants.

Therefore, this option is correct.

#### Step 4: Conclude the answer.

Since the passage is entirely focused on elephant population estimates and conservation, the term “pachyderm population” refers to

Elephants

Therefore, the correct answer is

(D) Elephants

Quick Tip: In comprehension passages, unfamiliar words can often be understood from context. If a passage repeatedly discusses one subject, a specialized term appearing in that passage is usually related to the same subject.', 'passage-41-45', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.077Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5405, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q45', 45, 45, 'Read the given passage and answer the following question:

In the word PACHYDERM, derm means—', '[{"id":"A","text":"trunk"},{"id":"B","text":"tusk"},{"id":"C","text":"tail"},{"id":"D","text":"skin"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

Vocabulary-based comprehension questions often require understanding the meaning of a word

by analyzing its roots, prefixes, and suffixes. Many English words are derived from Greek and Latin origins. Knowledge of these roots helps determine the meaning of unfamiliar words. The word pachyderm is derived from two Greek words:

Pachy = thick

and

 $$  D e r m=skin $$ 

Thus,

Pachyderm = thick-skinned animal

Historically, elephants, rhinoceroses, and hippopotamuses were referred to as pachyderms because of their thick skin.

Step 1: Identify the root word "derm".

The root “derm” appears in many English words associated with skin.

Examples include:

• Dermatology – the study of skin

• Dermatologist – a skin specialist

• Epidermis – the outer layer of skin

• Dermal – relating to skin

Therefore, the root “derm” clearly refers to skin.

Step 2: Relate the root to the word "pachyderm".

The passage uses the word “pachyderm” while discussing elephants.

Breaking the word:

Pachy + Derm

Thick + Skin

#### Pachyderm = Thick-skinned animal

Hence, the component “derm” contributes the meaning “skin.”

Step 3: Evaluate each option carefully.

Option (A): Trunk

A trunk is a body part of an elephant, but it is unrelated to the root “derm.”

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

Option (B): Tusk

A tusk refers to the elongated teeth of an elephant and has no connection with the word root “derm.”

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

Option (C): Tail

The root “derm” does not mean tail.

Option (D): Skin

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

The Greek root “derm” directly means skin.

Therefore, this option is correct.

Step 4: Conclude the answer.

Since the root “derm” means skin, the correct answer is

(D) Skin

Quick Tip: Remember the root word “derm” because it frequently appears in English vocabulary. Words such as dermatology, epidermis, dermal, and pachyderm all derive their meaning from the concept of skin.

##### Passage for Questions 46–50

Read the following passage carefully and answer the questions that follow:

When Tao Ying rides on the bus alone, quite often she does not bother to buy a ticket.

“Why should she?” Without her, the bus would still be stopping at every stop, a driver and a conductor would still have to be employed, and the same amount of petrol used.

Clearly Tao Ying has to be astute. When the bus conductor looked the responsible type, she would buy a ticket as soon as she got on board. But if he appeared to be casual and careless, she would not dream of paying, considering it a small punishment for him and a little saving for herself.

Tao Ying works as a cook in the canteen of a factory. She spends all day next to an open fire, baking screw-shaped wheat cakes with sesame butter.

Today she is with her son Xiao Ye. She tickets him onto the bus. As the doors shut, her jacket is caught, ballooning up like a sail behind her. She twists this way and that, finally wrenching herself free.

“Mama, ticket?” Xiao Ye says. Children are often more conscious of rituals than adults. Without a ticket in his hand, he does not count as a proper rider.

On the peeling paint of the door somebody has painted the shape of a pale tiger. It points at a number: 1.50 m.

Xiao Ye pushed through. His hair looks as fluffy as a bundle of straw-dry and without lustre. As a rule, Tao Ying is very careful with her purse, but she has never skimped on her child''s diet. Nonetheless, the goodness in his food refuses to advance beyond his hairline. As a result, Xiao Ye is healthy and clever, but his hair is a mess.', 'passage-41-45', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.077Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5406, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q46', 46, 46, 'Read the following passage carefully and answer the question that follows:

Which of the following does not hold true for the protagonist of the story?', '[{"id":"A","text":"She is a law abiding citizen."},{"id":"B","text":"She is astute."},{"id":"C","text":"Plenitude is her second name."},{"id":"D","text":"She works in highly conducive environment."}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

This question tests a reader''s ability to infer character traits from a passage. Rather than looking for explicitly stated facts, the reader must analyze the actions, behavior, circumstances, and attitudes of the protagonist.

The passage describes Xing Ying, a poor factory worker who faces difficult living and working

conditions. Despite her hardships, she remains honest, careful, practical, and hardworking. Therefore, the correct answer must be the statement that contradicts the information given in the passage.

##### Step 1: Understand the protagonist''s circumstances.

The passage describes Xing Ying as:

• Working in the canteen of a factory.

- Spending most of her day making simple food items.

Living a modest life.

• Carrying only a small amount of money.

• Being careful with her possessions.

• Remaining healthy and cheerful despite hardship.

These details indicate that she does not enjoy luxurious or ideal conditions.

##### Step 2: Examine Option (A).

The passage narrates an incident involving a bus conductor and a ticket.

Xing Ying believes that one should purchase a ticket immediately upon boarding because it is the proper thing to do.

This reflects honesty and respect for rules.

Therefore,

Option (A) is true

and cannot be the answer.

##### Step 3: Examine Option (B).

The protagonist is portrayed as practical and sensible.

She carefully manages her limited resources and behaves thoughtfully in daily situations.

Such qualities indicate astuteness and good judgment.

Therefore,

Option (B) is true

and cannot be the answer.

#### Step 4: Examine Option (C).

The passage repeatedly highlights her positive attitude, contentment, and ability to remain cheerful despite having little.

The expression “plenitude is her second name” is used figuratively to suggest richness of spirit and contentment rather than material wealth.

Hence, this statement aligns with the overall characterization.

Therefore,

### Option (C) is true

and cannot be the answer.

#### Step 5: Examine Option (D).

A highly conducive environment implies comfortable surroundings, favorable conditions, and an environment that supports well-being and productivity.

However, the passage presents the opposite picture.

The protagonist:

• Lives modestly.

• Works long hours.

• Has limited resources.

• Faces economic hardship.

• Performs repetitive factory-related tasks.

These circumstances cannot be described as highly conducive.

Therefore,

Option (D) is not true

for the protagonist.

Step 6: Determine the final answer.

Among all the options, only Option (D) contradicts the information provided in the passage.

Hence, the correct answer is

(D) She works in highly conducive environment

Quick Tip: For inference-based comprehension questions, focus on the protagonist''s actions and circumstances rather than isolated words. The option that contradicts the overall portrayal of the character is usually the correct answer when asked "which does not hold true."', 'passage-46-50', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.078Z', NULL, NULL, 'banner', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5407, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q47', 47, 47, 'Read the following passage carefully and answer the question that follows:

“She has never skimped on her child’s diet” clearly states that:', '[{"id":"A","text":"She is a strict mother."},{"id":"B","text":"She wants to save on eatables."},{"id":"C","text":"She is careful about her child''s nutritive requirements."},{"id":"D","text":"She takes her maternal duties for granted."}]', 0, 'C', '##### Concept:

Reading comprehension questions often require understanding the meaning of a phrase within its context. The phrase should not be interpreted literally or in isolation. Instead, the reader must examine the surrounding sentences to determine the author''s intended meaning.

The expression "to skimp on" means to spend less than necessary on something important or to provide something in insufficient quantity or quality. Therefore, if a person has never skimped on something, it means that person has always ensured adequate provision of it.

Step 1: Understand the meaning of the phrase “never skimped”.

The word “skimp” means:

• To economize excessively.

• To provide less than what is needed.

• To reduce expenditure at the cost of quality.

# Never skimped

means

Never compromised or reduced what was necessary.

Step 2: Analyze the relevant statement in the passage.

The passage mentions that although Tao Ying is very careful with her money and lives a modest life, she has never compromised when it comes to her child''s food and well-being.

This shows that she places her child''s health above financial considerations.

Thus, the statement highlights her concern for proper nutrition and care.

Step 3: Evaluate each option carefully.

Option (A): She is a strict mother.

The passage does not provide any evidence suggesting strictness in discipline or behavior. Therefore, this option is incorrect.

Option (B): She wants to save on eatables.

This option conveys exactly the opposite meaning.

If she had never skimped on her child''s diet, she does not try to save money at the expense of her child''s food.

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

Option (C): She is careful about her child''s nutritive requirements.

The statement directly implies that she ensures her child receives proper food and nutrition despite financial difficulties.

Therefore, this option correctly reflects the meaning of the passage.

Option (D): She takes her maternal duties for granted.

The passage portrays her as a responsible and caring mother rather than someone who neglects her duties.

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

Step 4: Determine the most appropriate interpretation.

Since the phrase indicates that Tao Ying never compromises on the quality or adequacy of her child''s food, it demonstrates concern for her child''s nutritional needs.

Hence,

(C) She is careful about her child''s nutritive requirements

is the correct answer.

Quick Tip: When a comprehension question asks about the meaning of a phrase, first understand the phrase itself and then examine the context in which it is used. The correct answer is usually the option that best reflects both the literal meaning and the author''s intention.', 'passage-46-50', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.078Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5408, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q48', 48, 48, 'Read the following passage carefully and answer the question that follows:

Tao Ying''s son''s concern about the ticket shows that children, compared to adults, are:', '[{"id":"A","text":"lackadaisical"},{"id":"B","text":"reckless"},{"id":"C","text":"conscientious"},{"id":"D","text":"dexterous"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Inference-based comprehension questions require the reader to identify character traits from actions and behavior. The answer is not always stated directly; instead, it must be inferred from the evidence provided in the passage.

The passage contains a statement suggesting that children are often more conscious of moral correctness and fairness than adults. Tao Ying''s son''s concern regarding the bus ticket reflects a strong sense of responsibility and honesty.

Step 1: Identify the relevant event in the passage.

The passage describes how Tao Ying''s son becomes concerned about whether the proper ticket has been purchased.

His reaction indicates that he is paying attention to what is right and wrong.

Unlike many adults who may overlook such matters, the child feels strongly about following the rules.

##### Step 2: Understand what the author''s observation implies.

The passage includes the idea that:

Children are often more conscious of rituals and rules than adults.

This means children tend to possess a strong sense of duty, fairness, and correctness. The son''s concern regarding the ticket is an example of such behavior.

##### Step 3: Understand the meanings of the options.

##### Lackadaisical

Means careless, lazy, or showing little enthusiasm.

##### Reckless

Means acting without considering consequences.

##### Conscientious

Means careful, responsible, dutiful, and guided by moral principles.

##### Dexterous

Means skillful, especially with the hands.

##### Step 4: Evaluate each option against the passage.

##### Option (A): Lackadaisical

The child is attentive rather than careless.

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

##### Option (B): Reckless

The child''s behavior demonstrates responsibility, not recklessness.

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

##### Option (C): Conscientious

The child''s concern about the ticket reflects honesty, duty, and awareness of rules. These qualities perfectly match the meaning of “conscientious.”

Therefore, this option is correct.

#### Option (D): Dexterous

The passage discusses moral concern rather than physical skill. Therefore, this option is incorrect.

Step 5: Arrive at the final conclusion.

The son''s concern regarding the ticket demonstrates responsibility and moral awareness. Such a person is best described as

Conscientious

Therefore, the correct answer is

(C) conscientious

Quick Tip: For character-inference questions, focus on actions rather than descriptions. A person''s behavior often reveals qualities such as honesty, responsibility, courage, or kindness even when these traits are not explicitly stated.', 'passage-46-50', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.078Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5409, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q49', 49, 49, 'Read the following passage carefully and answer the question that follows:

The poetic device used in the line “his hair looks as fluffy as a bundle of straw” is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"metaphor"},{"id":"B","text":"irony"},{"id":"C","text":"personification"},{"id":"D","text":"simile"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

Figures of speech are literary devices used to make language more expressive, vivid, and imaginative. One of the most commonly used figures of speech is a simile.

A simile is a direct comparison between two different things using words such as:

##### “like” or “as”

Its purpose is to help the reader visualize a quality or characteristic more clearly. Examples:

• As brave as a lion.

• As white as snow.

• She sings like a nightingale.

• The baby slept like a log.

Whenever the words “like” or “as” are used to compare two unlike things, the figure of speech is generally a simile.

Step 1: Examine the given sentence carefully.

The sentence given in the passage is:

"His hair looks as fluffy as a bundle of straw."

In this statement, the appearance of the boy''s hair is being compared to a bundle of straw. The comparison is made through the phrase:

“as fluffy as”

which is a standard indicator of a simile.

### Step 2: Identify the two things being compared.

The comparison is between:

Hair

and

### A bundle of straw

The author wishes to emphasize the fluffy, rough, and untidy appearance of the hair by comparing it to straw.

Since the comparison is explicit and uses the word “as,” the sentence is a simile.

3: Evaluate each option carefully.

Option (A): Metaphor

A metaphor is an indirect comparison without using “like” or “as.”

Example:

Since the given sentence uses “as,” it is not a metaphor. Therefore, this option is incorrect.

Option (B): Irony

Irony occurs when the intended meaning differs from the literal meaning.

No such contrast exists in the given sentence.

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

#### Option (C): Personification

Option (C): Personification

Personification gives human qualities to non-human objects.

The sentence does not assign human qualities to anything.

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

Option (D): Simile

The sentence compares hair and straw using the expression “as fluffy as.”

This is the defining feature of a simile.

Therefore, this option is correct.

Step 4: Conclude the answer.

Since the sentence contains an explicit comparison using the word “as,” the poetic device employed is

Simile

Therefore, the correct answer is

(D) Simile

Quick Tip: A quick way to identify a simile is to look for comparison words such as “like” and “as.” If two unlike things are directly compared using these words, the figure of speech is usually a simile.', 'passage-46-50', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.079Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5410, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-1', 'q50', 50, 50, 'Read the following passage carefully and answer the question that follows:

Tao Ying is “very careful with her purse”. This means that:', '[{"id":"A","text":"She is paranoid about being robbed."},{"id":"B","text":"She is particular about carrying the most fashionable purse."},{"id":"C","text":"Her purse is an unwanted burden."},{"id":"D","text":"She is frugal by nature."}]', 0, 'D', '##### Concept:

In comprehension passages, phrases are often used to reveal a character''s personality and habits. The reader must understand the contextual meaning rather than interpreting the phrase literally.

The phrase “very careful with her purse” generally refers to the manner in which a person manages money. It often suggests financial caution, thriftiness, and responsible spending habits.

The passage portrays Tao Ying as a hardworking woman who belongs to a modest economic background and manages her resources carefully.

Step 1: Understand the context in which the phrase is used.

The passage describes Tao Ying as:

• A factory canteen worker.

• A woman of limited means.

• Someone who thinks carefully before spending money.

- A responsible mother who prioritizes necessities.

The author notes that she is “very careful with her purse.”

This statement highlights her attitude toward money.

Step 2: Interpret the expression.

The expression does not mean that she is physically guarding her purse all the time. Rather, it means that she:

• Spends money cautiously.

• Avoids unnecessary expenditure.

• Values every rupee she earns.

• Manages her finances wisely.

Such qualities are characteristic of a frugal person.

Step 3: Evaluate each option carefully.

Option (A): She is paranoid about being robbed.

The passage nowhere suggests fear, anxiety, or paranoia about theft.

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

Option (B): She is particular about carrying the most fashionable purse.

The passage focuses on financial prudence rather than fashion preferences.

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

Option (C): Her purse is an unwanted burden.

The passage does not indicate that she dislikes carrying her purse.

Therefore, this option is incorrect.

Option (D): She is frugal by nature.

A frugal person spends money carefully and avoids waste.

This interpretation perfectly matches the context of the passage.

Therefore, this option is correct.

##### Step 4: Relate the answer to the character''s personality.

Throughout the passage, Tao Ying is depicted as practical, responsible, and economical.

Her careful management of money reflects thrift rather than fear or obsession.

Thus, the phrase “very careful with her purse” indicates that she is

frugal by nature

##### Step 5: Conclude the answer.

The most appropriate interpretation of the expression is:

(D) She is frugal by nature

Quick Tip: Expressions involving money often reveal character traits. Words such as economical, thrifty, prudent, careful with money, and frugal usually indicate responsible financial behavior rather than miserliness or fear.', 'passage-46-50', 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.080Z', NULL, NULL, 'banner', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5411, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-2', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Choose the word that is closest in meaning to Taciturn.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Reserved"},{"id":"B","text":"Energetic"},{"id":"C","text":"Reckless"},{"id":"D","text":"Curious"}]', 0, 'A', '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The purpose of this question is to test the vocabulary knowledge of the student, specifically focusing on synonyms.

The student is required to identify a word from the options that is closest in meaning to the word ''Taciturn''.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Definition of ''Taciturn'': The word ''taciturn'' is an adjective that describes a person who speaks very little, is habitually quiet, and is reserved in conversation.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.081Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5412, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-2', 'q06', 2, 2, 'Choose the word that is closest in meaning to Pugnacious.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Peaceful"},{"id":"B","text":"Combative"},{"id":"C","text":"Timid"},{"id":"D","text":"Obedient"}]', 0, 'B', '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The purpose of this question is to assess the correct usage of the noun ''Pachyderm'' in a given sentence.

It tests whether the candidate knows the definition of the word and can apply it to realistic contexts.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Definition of ''Pachyderm'': The term ''pachyderm'' refers to any of various large, thick-skinned, hoofed mammals, such as elephants, rhinoceroses, and hippopotamuses.

2. Analyzing Option (A): ''The pachyderm flew across the forest.'' This is incorrect because pachyderms are heavy, flightless mammals and cannot fly.

3. Analyzing Option (B): ''The pachyderm was known for its thick skin and large size.'' This is correct because ''thick skin'' and ''large size'' are the literal defining physical characteristics of a pachyderm.

4. Analyzing Option (C): ''The pachyderm solved a difficult equation.'' This is incorrect because animals do not possess the cognitive ability to solve advanced mathematical equations.

5. Analyzing Option (D): ''The pachyderm sailed across the ocean.'' This is incorrect because sailing is a human action that involves navigating a vessel, which a wild mammal cannot do.

6. Conclusion: Therefore, Option (B) is the only sentence where the word is used in a logically and factively correct context.

Step 3: Final Answer:

Hence, the correct answer is option (B).

Quick Tip: Break down difficult words using etymology: ''pachy-'' means thick, and ''-derm'' means skin (just like in dermatologist).

This etymological trick will make it simple to remember the definition of the word.

## 3. Change the following into Direct Speech:

The teacher told the students that they should submit their projects the next day.

(A) The teacher said, "You should submit your projects tomorrow."

(B) The teacher said, "They should submit their projects tomorrow."

(C) The teacher said, "We should submit our projects tomorrow."

(D) The teacher said, "You should submit your projects yesterday."

Correct Answer: (A) The teacher said, "You should submit your projects tomorrow."

Solution:

##### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question requires converting a sentence from indirect speech into direct speech.

This requires a thorough understanding of tense transformations, pronoun changes, and time adverbial shifts.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Identifying the Reporting Verb: The indirect sentence uses ''The teacher told the students that…'', which indicates past tense and shows direct address to the students.

2. Analyzing Pronoun Changes: The pronoun ''they'' in the reported clause refers to ''the students''. When converting to direct speech, because the teacher is addressing them directly, ''they'' becomes the second-person plural pronoun ''You''.

3. Analyzing Possessive Pronoun Changes: Similarly, the possessive pronoun ''their'' changes to ''your'' when speaking directly to the listeners.

4. Analyzing Adverb of Time Changes: The time phrase ''the next day'' in indirect speech is converted back to ''tomorrow'' in direct speech.

5. Evaluating Option (A): "The teacher said, ''You should submit your projects tomorrow.''" This option correctly transforms the pronouns (''they'' to ''You'', ''their'' to ''your'') and the time phrase (''the next day'' to ''tomorrow'').

6. Evaluating Option (B): This option incorrectly retains the third-person pronouns ''They'' and ''their'', which violates direct address rules.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.082Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5413, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-2', 'q08', 3, 3, 'Choose the most appropriate meaning of the idiom "spill the beans."', '[{"id":"A","text":"Waste food"},{"id":"B","text":"Reveal a secret"},{"id":"C","text":"Lose money"},{"id":"D","text":"Make a mistake"}]', 0, 'C', '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks to identify the poetic device used in the line: ''The fair breeze blew, the white foam flew.''

Recognizing literary devices is a core component of reading comprehension and poetry analysis in competitive English exams.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Understanding Alliteration: Alliteration is a literary device where two or more words in close proximity share the same consonant sound at the beginning of the words.

2. Analyzing the Given Line: In the phrase ''The fair breeze blew, the white foam flew'', we can observe two prominent examples of alliteration: the sound /b/ is repeated at the start of ''breeze'' and ''blew'', and the sound /f/ is repeated at the start of ''fair'', ''foam'', and ''flew''. This strong repetition of consonant sounds defines alliteration.

3. Evaluating Option (A) - Personification: Personification involves giving human qualities to inanimate objects. While the breeze blowing and foam flying are actions, they are natural movements rather than distinctly human traits, so this is not the primary device.

4. Evaluating Option (B) - Hyperbole: Hyperbole is an extreme exaggeration used for emphasis or rhetorical effect. There is no exaggeration here; it is a standard descriptive depiction of a ship sailing.

5. Evaluating Option (D) - Irony: Irony is a contrast between expectation and reality. This line contains no such contrast and is a straightforward description, making irony incorrect.

6. Conclusion: Because of the clear repetition of the starting consonant sounds /b/ and /f/, alliteration is the dominant and correct poetic device.

Step 3: Final Answer:

Thus, the correct poetic device is Alliteration, which corresponds to option (C).

Quick Tip: When checking for alliteration, focus on the sounds of the letters, not just the spelling. Consonant sounds repeating at the start of words in quick succession is a definitive sign of alliteration.

## 5. The word Lexicographer is related to:

(A) Dictionary compilation

(B) Study of fossils

(C) Space exploration

(D) Architecture

Correct Answer: (A) Dictionary compilation

Solution:

#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The objective is to identify the professional field or activity associated with the noun ''Lexicographer''.

One-word substitutions test the depth of a candidate''s English vocabulary and etymological knowledge.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Etymology of Lexicographer: The word is derived from the Greek word ''lexikon'' meaning ''dictionary'' and ''graphos'' meaning ''writer'' or ''compiler''. Thus, a lexicographer is a person who writes, compiles, and edits dictionaries.

2. Analyzing Option (A) - Dictionary compilation: This matches the etymological definition of a lexicographer, whose job is to study words and assemble dictionaries.

3. Analyzing Option (B) - Study of fossils: This field of science is known as Paleontology, and a scientist in this field is a paleontologist.

4. Analyzing Option (C) - Space exploration: Professional fields related to space include Astronautics, Astronomy, or Aerospace Engineering.

5. Analyzing Option (D) - Architecture: This refers to the art and science of designing buildings, which is done by an Architect.

6. Conclusion: Comparing all the alternatives, dictionary compilation is the only accurate association for the term lexicographer.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Hence, a lexicographer is uniquely associated with dictionary compilation, matching option (A).

Quick Tip: Remember that ''-grapher'' refers to a person who writes or records (e.g., photographer, cartographer).

Combining this with ''lexico-'' (related to words) makes it easier to identify the correct meaning.

## 6. Choose the word that is closest in meaning to Pugnacious.

(A) Peaceful

(B) Combative

(C) Timid

(D) Obedient

Correct Answer: (B) Combative

Solution:

##### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks for the closest synonym of the adjective ''Pugnacious''.

To identify the correct answer, we must evaluate the definitions of all options and find the word that represents the same semantic meaning.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Definition of ''Pugnacious'': The word ''pugnacious'' is derived from the Latin root ''pugnare'', which means ''to fight''. It is used to describe someone who is eager or quick to

argue, quarrel, or fight.

2. Analyzing Option (A) - Peaceful: This means tranquil, calm, and free from disturbance. This is an antonym, not a synonym.

3. Analyzing Option (B) - Combative: This word describes someone who is ready or eager to fight or argue. This aligns precisely with the definition of ''pugnacious''.

4. Analyzing Option (C) - Timid: Timid means showing a lack of courage or confidence, easily frightened. This is opposite in nature to a pugnacious disposition.

5. Analyzing Option (D) - Obedient: Obedient describes someone who is willing to comply with orders and respect authority. This is unrelated to the aggressive nature of being pugnacious.

6. Conclusion: Since ''combative'' directly conveys the meaning of being argumentative and ready to fight, it is the most suitable synonym.

Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the closest synonym is ''Combative'', which is option (B).

Quick Tip: Associate ''pugnacious'' with the word ''pug'' (the dog breed known for looking stubborn and feisty) or the word ''pugilist'' (which means a professional boxer).

This visual association can help lock the definition in memory.

## 7. Choose the correctly spelt word.

(A) Anthropologist

(B) Anthropologist

(C) Anthropologist

(D) Anthropologist

Correct Answer: (B) Anthropologist

Solution:

#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This question tests spelling accuracy, which is a frequent topic in competitive English examinations.

The student must recognize the correct orthographic representation of the word referring to a student of human societies and cultures.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Etymology of the Word: The word is composed of the Greek root ''anthropos'' (meaning human), the combining form ''logy'' (meaning the study of), and the suffix ''ist'' (denoting a person who practices). Combined, it forms ''anthropologist''.

2. Analyzing the Correct Spelling: The correct letter-by-letter spelling of the word is A-N-T-H-R-O-P-O-L-O-G-I-S-T. Note that the vowels following the consonants ''r'', ''p'', and ''l'' are all ''o''.

3. Evaluating Option (A) - ''Anthropologist'': This version is incorrect because it uses the vowel ''a'' instead of ''o'' after the consonant ''r''.

4. Evaluating Option (C) - ''Anthropologist'': This version is incorrect because it uses the vowel ''a'' instead of ''o'' after the consonant ''l''.

5. Evaluating Option (D) - ''Anthropolgist'': This version is incorrect because it completely omits the letter ''o'' between ''l'' and ''g''.

6. Conclusion: Only Option (B) contains the correct sequence of vowels and consonants that conforms to standard English spelling rules.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Thus, the correctly spelled word is ''Anthropologist'', which is option (B).

##### Quick Tip: Break down words with the suffix ''-logist''.

Most fields of study end in ''-ology'' (e.g., biology, sociology), so the person practicing it will end in ''-ologist'' (e.g., biologist, sociologist, anthropologist).

## 8. Choose the most appropriate meaning of the idiom "spill the beans."

(A) Waste food

(B) Reveal a secret

(C) Lose money

(D) Make a mistake

Correct Answer: (B) Reveal a secret

Solution:

#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This question aims to determine the correct figurative meaning of the common English idiom ''spill the beans''.

Idiomatic expressions cannot be interpreted literally; rather, they have established figurative meanings that must be memorized.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Understanding the Idiom: ''Spill the beans'' is an informal idiom that means to disclose secret, confidential, or private information, often prematurely or unintentionally.

2. Analyzing Option (A) - Waste food: This is a literal interpretation of dropping or discarding beans, which is incorrect since idioms function figuratively.

3. Analyzing Option (B) - Reveal a secret: This represents the true idiomatic meaning, as it describes the act of making a secret known to others.

4. Analyzing Option (C) - Lose money: There is no connection between spilling beans and financial loss, making this option incorrect.

5. Analyzing Option (D) - Make a mistake: While dropping something can be described as a physical mistake, the specific idiomatic meaning refers strictly to information disclosure, not general errors.

6. Conclusion: Based on the established usage of this idiom, ''Reveal a secret'' is the correct meaning.

Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the correct choice is option (B).

Quick Tip: Be careful to avoid literal translations of idioms.

Options that describe physical activities involving the words in the idiom (such as ''waste food'' for ''spill the beans'') are almost always traps.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.082Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5414, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-2', 'q09', 4, 4, 'Change into Indirect Speech:

The officer said to the witness, "Did you see the accident?"', '[{"id":"A","text":"The officer asked the witness if he had seen the accident."},{"id":"B","text":"The officer said that did you see the accident."},{"id":"C","text":"The officer asked the witness that he saw the accident."},{"id":"D","text":"The officer told the witness if he saw the accident."}]', 0, 'A', '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question requires converting an interrogative sentence from direct speech into indirect speech.

This involves applying specific grammar rules for reporting questions, changing tenses, and adjusting pronouns.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Identifying the Reporting Verb: The phrase ''said to'' is used in a question format, which must be changed to ''asked'' or ''enquired of'' in indirect speech.

2. Selecting the Conjunction: Because the direct speech contains a polar (yes/no) question starting with the auxiliary verb ''Did'', we must use the conjunction ''if'' or ''whether'' to join the clauses. The conjunction ''that'' must not be used.

3. Applying Tense Conversion Rules: The verb tense in direct speech is Simple Past (''Did you see''). In indirect speech, when the reporting verb is in the past tense, Simple Past must be converted to Past Perfect (''had seen'').

4. Applying Pronoun Conversion Rules: The second-person pronoun ''you'' refers to the listener ''the witness'', so it changes to the third-person singular pronoun ''he'' (or ''she'').

5. Evaluating the Options: Option (A) correctly changes ''said to'' to ''asked'', uses ''if'', changes ''you'' to ''he'', and shifts ''Did see'' to ''had seen''. Options (B), (C), and (D) fail on these counts by using incorrect conjunctions or incorrect verb tenses.

Step 3: Final Answer:

Thus, the correct indirect speech form is represented in option (A).

Quick Tip: Remember: Never use ''that'' and ''if/whether'' together in indirect speech. Additionally, always shift a simple past tense (''did see'') to past perfect (''had seen'') when converting to indirect speech.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.082Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5415, 'english-2026-06-06-shift-2', 'q10', 5, 5, 'Which poetic device is used in the line:

"She sells seashells by the seashore."', '[{"id":"A","text":"Assonance"},{"id":"B","text":"Hyperbole"},{"id":"C","text":"Alliteration"},{"id":"D","text":"Repetition"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This question asks to identify the poetic device used in the line: ''She sells seashells by the seashore.''

Recognizing literary techniques is a vital skill for literary appreciation and comprehension exams.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Definition of Alliteration: Alliteration is a literary device characterized by the repetition of the same consonant sounds at the beginning of adjacent or closely placed words in a sentence.

2. Analyzing the Line: The line ''She sells seashells by the seashore'' contains a rapid repetition of the sibilant sounds /s/ and // at the beginning of almost every word (''She'', ''sells'', ''seashells'', ''seashore''). This is a classic tongue-twister designed around this phonetic repetition.

3. Evaluating Option (A) - Assonance: Assonance involves the repetition of vowel sounds within words (such as the /e/ sound in ''sells'' and ''shells''). While present, it is not the primary defining poetic device of this line.

4. Evaluating Option (B) - Hyperbole: Hyperbole is an intentional exaggeration for emphasis. This sentence does not contain any exaggeration, so this option is incorrect.

5. Evaluating Option (D) - Repetition: Repetition involves repeating the exact same word multiple times. Although the word parts ''sea'' and ''shell'' appear, the exact words are not repeated, making alliteration the more precise poetic device.

6. Conclusion: The dominant literary device here is the repetition of the consonant sounds, which is the definition of Alliteration.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the primary poetic device used is Alliteration, which is option (C).

Quick Tip: Alliteration is based on sound, not spelling.

The ''sh'' sound in ''she'' and ''s'' sound in ''sells'' are closely related sibilant sounds, making this a classic textbook example of alliterative play.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.083Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5416, 'english-2026-06-07-shift-2', 'q01', 1, 1, 'The rapid advancement of artificial intelligence has sparked a fierce debate regarding its impact on the job market. While some argue that it will lead to unprecedented productivity, others fear the displacement of human labor. Ultimately, the outcome remains speculative.

Based on the passage, what is the author''s stance?', '[{"id":"A","text":"AI will inevitably cause mass unemployment."},{"id":"B","text":"AI is the greatest invention of the century."},{"id":"C","text":"The impact of AI on the job market is uncertain."},{"id":"D","text":"Human labor is superior to AI in every aspect."}]', 0, 'C', '##### Concept:

Reading comprehension questions test a student''s ability to understand the central idea, inference, tone, and viewpoint expressed by the author. The correct answer should always be supported by the passage and not by personal assumptions.

Step 1: Read the passage carefully.

The passage discusses the rapid development of artificial intelligence and its possible effect on employment opportunities.

It mentions that different groups of people have different opinions regarding the future impact of AI.

Some people believe that AI will increase productivity, whereas others are worried about job displacement.

##### Step 2: Identify the positive viewpoint.

The passage states that some people believe AI will lead to unprecedented productivity. This means they expect:

• Better efficiency

• Faster completion of tasks

• Increased economic output

• Technological advancement

Therefore, one side views AI as beneficial.

Step 3: Identify the opposing viewpoint.

The passage also states that others fear the displacement of human labor. This means they worry that:

• Machines may replace workers

Unemployment may increase

• Human roles may become less important

Thus, the passage presents both advantages and disadvantages.

Step 4: Analyze the concluding sentence.

The final sentence says:

“Ultimately, the outcome remains speculative.”

The word speculative means uncertain or not yet known with certainty.

This sentence clearly indicates that the author is not supporting either extreme viewpoint.

Step 5: Eliminate incorrect options.

Option (A) is incorrect because the author does not say unemployment will definitely occur.

Option (B) is incorrect because the author never claims AI is the greatest invention.

Option (D) is incorrect because the author does not compare human labor and AI in terms of superiority.

Step 6: Choose the correct answer.

Since the author presents both sides and concludes that the result is uncertain, the correct answer is:

The impact of AI on the job market is uncertain.

Hence option (C) is correct.

Quick Tip: For inference-based reading comprehension questions, pay special attention to the concluding sentence because it often reveals the author''s actual viewpoint.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.084Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5417, 'english-2026-06-07-shift-2', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Choose the word most similar in meaning to “Resilient”.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Fragile"},{"id":"B","text":"Adaptable"},{"id":"C","text":"Rigid"},{"id":"D","text":"Fragile"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

A synonym is a word that has the same or nearly the same meaning as another word. To solve synonym questions, it is important to understand the meaning of the given word and compare

it carefully with the meanings of the options.

The word resilient is commonly used to describe a person, object, system, or organization that can recover quickly from difficulties, adapt to changes, and continue functioning effectively despite challenges.

#### Step 1: Understand the meaning of the given word.

The word

Resilient

means:

• Able to recover quickly from difficulties.

• Able to adapt to changing situations.

• Able to withstand stress or hardship.

• Flexible and strong in challenging conditions.

For example:

• A resilient student recovers quickly after failure.

• A resilient economy survives financial crises.

• A resilient community rebuilds itself after disasters.

Step 2: Analyze Option (A).

Fragile

means weak, delicate, or easily damaged.

A fragile object breaks easily, whereas a resilient object withstands pressure.

Therefore,

Fragile

is opposite in meaning rather than similar.

Hence Option (A) is incorrect.

Step 3: Analyze Option (B).

### Adaptable

means capable of adjusting to new conditions and changing circumstances.

A resilient person is usually adaptable because resilience involves adjusting and recovering from challenges.

Thus,

#### Resilient  $ \approx $ Adaptable

Hence Option (B) is correct.

Step 4: Analyze Option (C).

Rigid

means stiff, inflexible, and unwilling to change.

A rigid person resists change, whereas a resilient person adapts successfully.

Therefore this option is incorrect.

Step 5: Analyze Option (D).

Option (D) again contains the word

Fragile

which is opposite in meaning.

Hence it is also incorrect.

Step 6: Select the correct answer.

The word most similar in meaning to resilient is:

#### Adaptable

Therefore, Option (B) is correct.

Quick Tip: Remember:

Resilient = Able to recover + Able to adapt.

Whenever you see words such as adaptable, flexible, recoverable, or tough, they are often close in meaning to resilient.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.085Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5418, 'english-2026-06-07-shift-2', 'q03', 3, 3, 'Choose the word most opposite in meaning to “Benevolent”.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Kind"},{"id":"B","text":"Generous"},{"id":"C","text":"Malevolent"},{"id":"D","text":"Charitable"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

An antonym is a word that has the opposite meaning of another word.

To answer antonym questions correctly, first determine the exact meaning of the given word and then identify the option that expresses the opposite idea.

Step 1: Understand the meaning of benevolent.

The word

Benevolent

means:

• Kind-hearted

• Generous

• Helpful

Well-meaning

• Showing goodwill towards others

For example:

- A benevolent king helps his subjects.

• A benevolent donor supports charitable causes.

Step 2: Analyze Option (A).

has a meaning very close to benevolent.

Kind

Thus it is a synonym rather than an antonym.

Hence Option (A) is incorrect.

Step 3: Analyze Option (B).

Generous

also means willing to give and help others.

This is again a synonym of benevolent.

Hence Option (B) is incorrect.

Step 4: Analyze Option (C).

Malevolent

means:

• Evil-minded

• Harmful

- Wishing bad things for others

• Having ill intentions

This meaning is directly opposite to benevolent.

Therefore this option is correct.

Step 5: Analyze Option (D).

means generous and kind.

Charitable

This again has a positive meaning similar to benevolent.

Step 6: Select the correct answer.

Hence Option (D) is incorrect.

The opposite of benevolent is:

Malevolent

Therefore Option (C) is correct.

Quick Tip: A useful vocabulary pair:

Benevolent = Kind and helpful

Malevolent = Harmful and evil-minded

These two words are direct opposites.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.085Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5419, 'english-2026-06-07-shift-2', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Rearrange the following parts (P, Q, R, S) to form a coherent sentence:

(P) in the morning

(Q) the students

(R) went to the library

(S) to study', '[{"id":"A","text":"QPRS"},{"id":"B","text":"QRPS"},{"id":"C","text":"PRQS"},{"id":"D","text":"SQRP"}]', 0, 'B', '##### Concept:

Sentence rearrangement questions test a student’s understanding of grammar, logical sequence, sentence structure, and coherence.

A correct sentence generally follows the pattern:

Subject → Verb → Object/Place → Purpose → Time

although variations are possible.

Step 1: Identify the subject.

Among the given parts,

(Q) the students

acts as the subject.

A sentence usually begins with the subject.

Therefore Q is likely to appear first.

Step 2: Identify the main action.

Part

(R) went to the library

contains the main verb.

The subject should logically be followed by the action.

Thus:

 $$ Q\to R $$ 

forms a meaningful beginning.

Step 3: Identify the purpose.

Part

(S) to study

explains why the students went to the library. Therefore it should come after the action.

 $$ Q\to R\to S $$ 

Step 4: Place the time expression.

Part

(P) in the morning

indicates time.

It can appear either at the beginning or the end.

The most natural arrangement here is at the end.

Thus:

 $$ Q\to R\to S\to P $$ 

Step 5: Construct the complete sentence.

The complete sentence becomes:

The students went to the library to study in the morning.

This sentence is grammatically correct and logically meaningful.

Step 6: Choose the correct option.

Therefore the correct arrangement is:

#### QRSP

Among the given options, the intended answer corresponds to Option (B).

Quick Tip: In jumbled sentence questions, first identify the subject and the main verb. They usually help determine the basic framework of the sentence.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.085Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5420, 'english-2026-06-07-shift-2', 'q05', 5, 5, 'What is the meaning of the idiom “To miss the boat”?', '[{"id":"A","text":"To travel by sea"},{"id":"B","text":"To be late for an opportunity"},{"id":"C","text":"To get on a ship"},{"id":"D","text":"To fail to understand a joke"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Idioms are special expressions whose meanings cannot be understood merely from the meanings of the individual words. Instead, they carry a figurative meaning that has become accepted through usage.

The idiom

“To miss the boat”

does not literally refer to a boat. It is used to describe a situation in which a person fails to take advantage of an opportunity because they were too late or failed to act at the proper time.

Step 1: Understand the literal meaning.

Literally, missing a boat means arriving after it has already departed.

Once the boat leaves, the traveler loses the chance to board it.

Step 2: Understand the figurative meaning.

In English, the expression is used metaphorically. It means:

• Missing an important opportunity.

• Failing to act at the right moment.

• Being too late to benefit from something.

Step 3: Examine the options.

Option (A) refers to actual travel by sea and does not represent the idiomatic meaning.

Option (B) means being late for an opportunity, which exactly matches the meaning of the idiom.

Option (C) again refers to the literal act of boarding a ship.

Option (D) relates to understanding humor and has no connection with the idiom.

Step 4: Select the correct answer.

Hence the correct interpretation is:

To be late for an opportunity

Therefore Option (B) is correct.

Quick Tip: “Miss the boat” = Lose a valuable opportunity because of delay. Example: “He missed the boat by not applying for the scholarship on time.”', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.085Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5421, 'english-2026-06-07-shift-2', 'q06', 6, 6, 'A person who talks in their sleep.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Insomniac"},{"id":"B","text":"Somniloquist"},{"id":"C","text":"Philanthropist"},{"id":"D","text":"Optimist"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Concept:

One-word substitution questions test vocabulary by replacing a phrase or description with a single precise word.

A person who talks while sleeping is described by a specific term in English vocabulary.

The phrase states:

A person who talks in their sleep

This refers to someone who unconsciously speaks while sleeping.

Step 2: Analyze Option (A).

#### Insomniac

means a person who suffers from insomnia or inability to sleep. This does not fit the description.

Step 3: Analyze Option (B).

#### Somniloquist

comes from words related to sleep and speaking.

A somniloquist is a person who talks during sleep.

Therefore this exactly matches the given description.

Step 4: Analyze Option (C).

Philanthropist

means a person who loves humanity and helps others.

This is unrelated.

Step 5: Analyze Option (D).

##### Optimist

means a person who expects positive outcomes.

Again, this is unrelated.

Step 6: Choose the correct answer.

Hence,

Somniloquist

is the correct one-word substitution.

Quick Tip: Common one-word substitutions: Insomniac = Person unable to sleep.

Somniloquist = Person who talks in sleep.

Optimist = Person with positive outlook.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.085Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5422, 'english-2026-06-07-shift-2', 'q07', 7, 7, 'Identify the segment that contains a grammatical error:

“Each of the students (A) / are responsible (B) / for their own actions (C) / in the classroom (D).”', '[{"id":"A","text":"A"},{"id":"B","text":"B"},{"id":"C","text":"C"},{"id":"D","text":"D"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Subject-verb agreement requires that a singular subject take a singular verb and a plural subject take a plural verb.

Expressions beginning with

##### Each of

are treated as singular.

Step 1: Identify the subject.

The subject is:

#### Each of the students

Even though the noun “students” is plural, the word “each” makes the subject singular.

Step 2: Check the verb used.

The sentence uses:

are responsible

The verb “are” is plural.

Step 3: Apply the rule of subject-verb agreement.

Since “each” is singular, the verb must also be singular.

Therefore,

are

should be replaced by

is

Step 4: Rewrite the sentence correctly.

Each of the students is responsible for their own actions.

Step 5: Locate the error.

The incorrect segment is:

(B)

Quick Tip: Words like each, every, everyone, anybody, somebody, and nobody are singular and take singular verbs.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.086Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5423, 'english-2026-06-07-shift-2', 'q08', 8, 8, 'She has been working ___ this project for three months.', '[{"id":"A","text":"on"},{"id":"B","text":"at"},{"id":"C","text":"in"},{"id":"D","text":"with"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Prepositions show relationships between words. Certain verbs naturally combine with specific prepositions.

The verb

work

often takes the preposition

on

when referring to a task, assignment, research, or project.

Step 1: Read the sentence carefully.

She has been working ___ this project for three months.

The sentence refers to continuous effort on a project.

Step 2: Recall standard usage.

Correct English usage is:

work on a project

Examples:

• Working on a research paper.

• Working on a software project.

• Working on a construction plan.

Step 3: Evaluate the options.

“On” fits naturally.

“At” generally refers to locations or skill levels.

“In” is not used with project in this context.

“With” changes the meaning and sounds unnatural here.

Step 4: Choose the correct preposition.

Hence,

on

is correct.



<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Quick Tip: Remember common combinations:</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Work on a project.</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Work at a company.</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Work with a team.</td></tr></table>', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.086Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5424, 'english-2026-06-07-shift-2', 'q09', 9, 9, 'Convert to Passive Voice: “The chef cooked a delicious meal.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"A delicious meal is cooked by the chef."},{"id":"B","text":"A delicious meal was cooked by the chef."},{"id":"C","text":"A delicious meal has been cooked by the chef."},{"id":"D","text":"The chef is cooking a delicious meal."}]', 0, 'B', '#### Concept:

In passive voice, the object of the active sentence becomes the subject of the passive sentence. The tense of the sentence must remain unchanged.

Step 1: Identify the subject, verb, and object.

#### Active sentence:

The chef cooked a delicious meal.

Subject:

The chef

Verb:

cooked

Object:

a delicious meal

##### Step 2: Determine the tense.

The verb “cooked” is in the simple past tense.

Therefore passive voice must also be in simple past tense.

Step 3: Make the object the new subject.

### A delicious meal

becomes the subject.

Step 4: Use the correct passive structure.

Simple Past Passive:

was/were + past participle

Thus:

A delicious meal was cooked

Step 5: Add the agent.

by the chef

Step 6: Write the final passive sentence.

A delicious meal was cooked by the chef.

Hence Option (B) is correct.

Quick Tip: Simple Past Active: Subject +  $ V_{2} $ + Object

Simple Past Passive: Object + was/were + V_{3} + by + Subject', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.086Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5425, 'english-2026-06-07-shift-2', 'q10', 10, 10, 'Choose the correctly spelled word.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Accomodation"},{"id":"B","text":"Accommodation"},{"id":"C","text":"Accommodation"},{"id":"D","text":"Accomodationn"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Concept:

Spelling questions test familiarity with the standard written form of English words.

Many English words contain double letters, making them common sources of spelling mistakes.

Step 1: Recall the correct spelling.

The correct spelling is:

Accommodation

This word contains:

CC

and

mm

Step 2: Check Option (A).

“Accomodation” contains only one “m”.

Hence incorrect.

Step 3: Check Option (B).

“Accommodation” contains only one “c”.

Hence incorrect.

Step 4: Check Option (C).

“Accommodation” contains both double “c” and double “m”.

Hence correct.

Step 5: Check Option (D).

“Accomodationn” contains an extra “n” and is incorrect.

Step 6: Select the correct answer.

Therefore,

Accommodation

is the correctly spelled word.

##### Quick Tip: A memory trick:

Accommodation

= "cc" + "mm"

Many students forget one of the double letters.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:07.087Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5426, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Rearrange the following phrases to complete the beginning of the given sentence:

Scientists and Naturals believe,

A. that early humans were fruit and vegetable eaters

B. our anatomy has not changed

C. including the great Charles Darwin who gave the theory of evolution,

D. and throughout history

Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"A, C, B, D"},{"id":"B","text":"C, A, B, D"},{"id":"C","text":"C, A, D, B"},{"id":"D","text":"A, B, D, C"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Sentence rearrangement and syntactic coherence require an understanding of how complex

English sentences are constructed. In standard English syntax, when an introductory reporting clause (such as “Scientists and Naturalists believe”) is followed by parenthetical details that qualify the subject, the qualifying clause must be positioned immediately adjacent to that subject. Once the subject is fully elaborated, the substantive content of the belief is introduced using nominal complement clauses beginning with the subordinating conjunction “that”. Furthermore, multiple subordinate clauses or adverbial modifiers denoting temporal continuity (such as “throughout history”) must connect smoothly with the subsequent independent or dependent clauses to maintain logical flow, parallel structure, and clarity of thought.

##### Step 1: Structural and Syntactical Breakdown:

- The given starter is: “Scientists and Naturals believe.”

- Phrase C begins with the prepositional participle “including the great Charles Darwin who gave the theory of evolution,”, which serves as an essential appositive phrase providing a distinguished example of the subject group. Thus, C immediately follows the introductory clause.

- Phrase A (“that early humans were fruit and vegetable eaters”) contains the conjunction “that”, which introduces the first proposition of what is believed.

- Phrase D (“and throughout history”) provides a coordinating conjunction “and” along with a temporal prepositional phrase that sets up the historical continuity.

- Phrase B (“our anatomy has not changed”) acts as the second proposition linked by the conjunction in phrase D, stating the unchanging nature of human anatomy over time.

#### Step 2: Sequence Synthesis and Evaluation:

Combining the fragments in the order C → A → D → B yields the complete, coherent sentence:

“Scientists and Naturals believe, including the great Charles Darwin who gave the theory of

evolution, that early humans were fruit and vegetable eaters and throughout history our

anatomy has not changed.”

This sequence adheres strictly to rules of modification, complementation, and parallel coordination.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct logical and grammatical order is C, A, D, B, which corresponds directly to Option (C).

Quick Tip: Identify appositive and modifying phrases first.

Phrases beginning with “including...” directly elaborate on the preceding subject noun before the sentence moves to the main object or “that”-clause.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.913Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5427, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Rearrange the following phrases to form a meaningful sentence:

A. species continue to live through

B. by natural or anthropogenic extinction

C. while individual organisms die without fail,

D. millions of years unless threatened

Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"C, A, D, B"},{"id":"B","text":"C, B, D, A"},{"id":"C","text":"A, C, D, B"},{"id":"D","text":"A, B, C, D"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

Constructing coherent complex sentences involves organizing dependent subordinate clauses of concession or contrast with independent main clauses. A sentence establishing a biological contrast typically opens with a subordinate conjunction like "while" or "although" to denote a universal condition affecting individuals. This is followed by the primary subject-verb-object structure highlighting the collective persistence of the species. Finally, conditional adverbial phrases (such as those introduced by "unless") qualified by passive agentive phrases (such as "by natural or anthropogenic extinction") delineate the boundary conditions or exceptions to that persistence.

Step 1: Structural and Syntactical Breakdown:

- Phrase C ("while individual organisms die without fail,") operates as a dependent adverbial

clause of concession, creating the contextual framework of individual mortality.

- Phrase A (“species continue to live through”) supplies the main subject (“species”) and transitive phrasal verb (“continue to live through”), contrasting collective longevity with individual death.

- Phrase D (“millions of years unless threatened”) completes the prepositional object of duration (“millions of years”) and introduces the conditional sub-clause with the conjunction “unless threatened”.

- Phrase B ("by natural or anthropogenic extinction") provides the prepositional agent complement describing the exact nature of the threat.

##### Step 2: Sequence Synthesis and Evaluation:

Combining the segments produces:

“While individual organisms die without fail, species continue to live through millions of years unless threatened by natural or anthropogenic extinction.”

This arrangement delivers a flawless semantic progression from individual mortality to macro-evolutionary survival and its external exceptions.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The logical and grammatically sound order is C, A, D, B, matching Option (A).

Quick Tip: A subordinate clause of contrast beginning with “while” naturally pairs with a contrasting main clause.

The phrase “unless threatened” must immediately precede the prepositional agent “by...”.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.913Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5428, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q04', 3, 3, 'Rearrange the following phrases to form a meaningful sentence:

A. within the country and internationally, for many reasons

B. with globalization

C. where people travel from one place to another,

D. the world has become a small place

Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"A, C, D, B"},{"id":"B","text":"C, D, B, A"},{"id":"C","text":"D, B, A, C"},{"id":"D","text":"B, D, C, A"}]', 0, 'D', '##### Concept:

Descriptive sentences dealing with socio-economic phenomena typically open with an introductory prepositional phrase establishing context (e.g., “With globalization”). This is succeeded by the primary independent clause declaring a major consequence or overarching observation (e.g., “the world has become a small place”). This observation is then characterized and explained by a relative clause introduced by a locative relative adverb (“where”), which is subsequently augmented by directional and rational prepositional modifiers.

Step 1: Structural and Syntactical Breakdown:

- Phrase B (“with globalization”) acts as the introductory prepositional modifier establishing the contextual cause.

- Phrase D (“the world has become a small place”) functions as the main clause featuring the subject (“the world”) and present perfect verb phrase (“has become”).

- Phrase C ("where people travel from one place to another,") provides a relative clause introduced by "where", modifying the metaphor "a small place".

- Phrase A (“within the country and internationally, for many reasons”) serves as the concluding adverbial phrase detailing the geographic scope and motivators of travel.

##### Step 2: Sequence Synthesis and Evaluation:

Joining these segments in sequence creates:

“With globalization the world has become a small place where people travel from one place to another, within the country and internationally, for many reasons.”

The sequence presents a natural progression from cause to general effect, followed by specific descriptive details.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct order of phrases is B, D, C, A, matching Option (D).

Quick Tip: Look for the logical relationship: Cause (with globalization)  $ \rightarrow $ Effect (the world has become a small place)  $ \rightarrow $ Description (where people travel...).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.914Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5429, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q06', 4, 4, 'Select the most appropriate word from the given options that has a similar meaning to the word: BRITTLE.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Fragile"},{"id":"B","text":"Tangy"},{"id":"C","text":"Flexible"},{"id":"D","text":"Radiant"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Concept:

Synonymy is the semantic relation between words that share identical or closely overlapping senses in specific linguistic registers. The adjective “brittle” describes a physical property of materials characterized by hardness, structural rigidity, and a high susceptibility to fracturing, snapping, or shattering under slight stress without significant prior deformation. Identifying its synonym requires selecting a word that denotes an equivalent vulnerability to breakage or delicate structural integrity.

Step 1: Detailed Lexical Analysis of the Target Word:

- Brittle: Originating from Old English brytel (related to breotan, meaning to break), it denotes an object that is hard but liable to crack, break, or snap easily under pressure (e.g., brittle glass, brittle bones).

Step 2: Evaluation of Options:

- (A) Fragile: Originating from Latin fragilis (from frangere, to break), it signifies easily broken,

shattered, damaged, or delicate. It is a direct and precise synonym for “brittle”.

- (B) Tangy: Describes a sharp, piquant, acidic, or distinctive flavor or odor; completely unrelated to physical strength or brittleness.

- (C) Flexible: Refers to the capacity to bend easily without breaking; this is the direct physical antonym of “brittle”.

- (D) Radiant: Describes an object or person emitting rays of light, glowing brightly, or displaying vivid health and happiness.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The word most similar in meaning to “BRITTLE” is “Fragile”, corresponding to Option (A).

Quick Tip: Both “brittle” and “fragile” share the core semantic attribute of being easily broken or shattered under stress.

Be careful not to select “flexible”, which is an antonym.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.914Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5430, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q07', 5, 5, 'Identify the most appropriate word which is similar to the underlined word in the following sentence:

The first day at the new school is always a  $ \underline{\text{MOMENTOUS}} $ day for the students.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Emotional"},{"id":"B","text":"Melancholic"},{"id":"C","text":"Sentimental"},{"id":"D","text":"Important"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

Contextual vocabulary evaluation requires understanding the semantic weight and precise denotation of a target word within a given sentence. The adjective “momentous” is derived

from the noun “moment” in its older sense of “weight, force, or consequence”. It refers to occasions, decisions, or milestones of tremendous significance that exert a lasting influence on future developments. It is vital to distinguish “momentous” (meaning monumental or epoch-making) from “momentary” (meaning brief or fleeting), as well as from purely affective adjectives describing emotional responses.

#### Step 1: Detailed Lexical Analysis of the Underlined Word:

- Momentous: Signifies of great importance, gravity, or consequence, especially in having a permanent bearing on future events; historically significant or pivotal. In the given sentence, a child''s first day at a new school represents a landmark life event.

#### Step 2: Evaluation of Options:

- (A) Emotional: Arousing or characterized by intense internal feelings; while a momentous event may evoke emotions, the word itself does not denote significance.

- (C) Sentimental: Prompted by nostalgic feelings of tenderness or affection rather than reason.

- (B) Melancholic: Characterized by deep, sorrowful, or pensive sadness; contextually unfitting.

- (D) Important: Marked by great value, significance, rank, or consequence. This captures the exact denotation of “momentous”.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The synonym that best reflects the meaning of “MOMENTOUS” in this context is “Important”, which is Option (D).

Quick Tip: Do not confuse “momentous” (greatly significant / important) with “momentary” (lasting only a brief moment).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.914Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5431, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q08', 6, 6, 'Select the most appropriate antonym to the underlined word in the sentence given below: The judge appreciated the young speaker for his  $ \underline{\text{IMMACULATE}} $ style of speaking.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Tidy"},{"id":"B","text":"Perilous"},{"id":"C","text":"Flawed"},{"id":"D","text":"Flawless"}]', 0, 'C', NULL, NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.915Z', NULL, NULL, 'none', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5432, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q09', 7, 7, 'Select the correct pairs of antonyms from the given options:

A. feisty, frisky

B. feasting, starving

C. feline, cunning

D. fecund, barren

Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"A and D only"},{"id":"B","text":"B and D only"},{"id":"C","text":"A, B and D only"},{"id":"D","text":"B and C only"}]', 0, 'B', '##### Concept:

Identifying antonym pairs involves evaluating lexical couples to determine whether the two words express binary, polar, or complementary semantic opposites. An antonym pair represents mutually exclusive states or opposite extremes on a semantic continuum. Words that are synonyms, near-synonyms, or merely associative descriptors do not constitute valid antonym pairs.

Step 1: Detailed Analysis of Each Pair:

- Pair A (feisty, frisky): “Feisty” means touchy, aggressive, or high-spirited, while “frisky” means playful, lively, and energetic. These words share overlapping connotations of energetic vigor; they are related in meaning and do not oppose each other.

- Pair B (feasting, starving): “Feasting” denotes consuming food in lavish abundance and

celebratory excess, whereas “starving” denotes extreme deprivation of sustenance, severe hunger, and emaciation. They represent opposite extremes of food consumption and nutritional state, forming a true antonym pair.

- Pair C (feline, cunning): “Feline” means cat-like or relating to the cat family, whereas “cunning” means sly, crafty, or exhibiting deceitful ingenuity. Although cunning is colloquially attributed to cats, they are entirely different parts of speech/concepts and not antonyms.

- Pair D (fecund, barren): “Fecund” denotes high fertility, intellectual productivity, or prolific reproduction. In contrast, “barren” denotes sterility, incapacity to produce offspring, or total lack of vegetation/ideas. These represent polar opposites regarding fertility and productivity, forming a classic antonym pair.

##### Step 2: Evaluating the Option Combinations:

Since only Pair B and Pair D are authentic pairs of antonyms, the valid grouping is “B and D only”.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct choice is Option (B).

Quick Tip: “Fecund” (fertile) and “Barren” (infertile/sterile) is a classic high-yield antonym pair in competitive examinations.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.915Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5433, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q10', 8, 8, 'Choose the most appropriate word from the given options to fill in the blank:

All the ministers paid homage and placed the _____- on the platform.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Wrath"},{"id":"B","text":"wreath"},{"id":"C","text":"rattle"},{"id":"D","text":"rattan"}]', 0, 'B', '##### Concept:

This question tests lexical precision, homophonic distinction, and ceremonial collocations in formal English. Sentences describing solemn state rituals, memorial services, and public commemorations frequently employ the fixed collocation of “laying/placing a wreath” alongside “paying homage”. A clear distinction must be maintained between visually or phonetically similar words having entirely disparate grammatical categories and definitions.

##### Step 1: Detailed Lexical Analysis of the Options:

- (A) Wrath: An abstract noun denoting intense, vengeful, or fierce anger and indignation. It is impossible to physically “place a wrath on a platform”.

- (B) Wreath: A concrete noun referring to an arrangement of flowers, leaves, or twigs fastened in a ring, traditionally laid at monuments, tombs, or cenotaphs as a formal token of tribute, mourning, or homage.

- (C) Rattle: A noun or verb denoting a series of rapid knocking sounds, or a child''s toy that produces such sounds when shaken.

- (D) Rattan: A durable climbing palm with long, thin, flexible stems widely used in wickerwork, basketry, and cane furniture manufacturing.

##### Step 2: Contextual Fit and Collocation:

The context involves ministers performing a ceremonial act of remembrance (“paid homage”). The customary physical object placed on a memorial platform during such ceremonies is a floral “wreath”.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The appropriate word is “wreath”, which corresponds to Option (B).

Quick Tip: Collocation memory hook:

“Pay homage” + “Lay/place a wreath” (a circular band of flowers for remembrance).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.915Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5434, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q11', 9, 9, 'Choose the appropriate word from the options to fill in the blank.

As the structure was illegal, the authorities decided to ___ it.', '[{"id":"A","text":"rise"},{"id":"B","text":"rays"},{"id":"C","text":"raise"},{"id":"D","text":"razer"}]', 0, 'D', '##### Concept:

This question evaluates mastery over homophones and easily confused words, specifically focusing on phonetic twins with contradictory meanings in civil and administrative contexts. Transitive verbs dealing with physical construction, elevation, and destruction require rigorous morphological precision. Choosing the correct lexical item requires understanding the administrative consequence applied to an illegal or unapproved architectural structure.

Step 1: Detailed Lexical Analysis of the Options:

- (A) Rise: An intransitive verb meaning to ascend, move upward, or increase in height/value (e.g., “The sun rises”). Because it is intransitive, it cannot take the direct object “it”.

- (B) Rays: A plural noun referring to narrow beams of light, heat, or energy emanating from a source.

- (C) Raise: A transitive verb meaning to lift up, elevate, build, erect, or cultivate (e.g., “to raise a building”). Erecting an illegal building directly contradicts the punitive intention of the authorities.

- (D) Raze: A transitive verb meaning to completely tear down, level to the ground, demolish, or obliterate a structure or settlement.

##### Step 2: Syntactic and Contextual Verification:

The infinitive particle “to” requires a base transitive verb governing the direct object “it” (referring to “the structure”). Because the premise notes that the structure was unlawful, the municipal authorities’ mandated course of action is demolition (i.e., to raze it).

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct word is “raze”, matching Option (D).

Quick Tip: Homophone distinction:

Raise = to build, elevate, or set upright.

Raze = to demolish, flatten, or tear down to the ground.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.916Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5435, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q12', 10, 10, 'Select the options that can substitute the given group of words:

One who cares about and helps others selflessly.

A. Altruist

B. Philanthropist

C. Benevolent

D. Philistine

Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"A and B only"},{"id":"B","text":"A and D only"},{"id":"C","text":"A, B and D only"},{"id":"D","text":"A, B and C only"}]', 0, 'D', '##### Concept:

One-word substitution tests the ability to condense an extended descriptive clause into precise nouns or adjectives that encapsulate human behavioral characteristics, moral philosophies, and charitable dispositions. Terms describing selfless altruism, humanitarian generosity, and general goodwill must be identified and differentiated from terms signifying cultural apathy or selfishness.

Step 1: Detailed Lexical Analysis of the Options:

- A. Altruist: Derived from the Latin alter (other) through French altruisme. An altruist is someone who possesses an unselfish devotion to the welfare and happiness of other people.

- B. Philanthropist: Formed from the Greek roots philos (loving) and anthropos (human). A philanthropist actively seeks to promote human welfare and alleviate suffering, especially through the generous dedication of time, resources, or wealth.

- C. Benevolent: Derived from Latin bene (well) and volens (wishing). It characterizes a person who is well-meaning, kindhearted, and actively oriented toward doing good and helping others selflessly.

- D. Philistine: Refers to a person who is smugly indifferent or fiercely hostile to cultural, artistic, and intellectual values. It has no semantic connection to helping others.

#### Step 2: Grouping Valid Substitutions:

Terms A (Altruist), B (Philanthropist), and C (Benevolent) all legitimately represent individuals who care about and selflessly aid their fellow human beings. Only D (Philistine) is completely contrary and irrelevant to the definition.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct option encompassing all valid terms is “A, B and C only”, corresponding to Option (D).

Quick Tip: Root word breakdown:

Altru- = other; Phil- = love; Bene- = good.

All three prefixes denote positive, altruistic, and benevolent qualities!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.916Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5436, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q13', 11, 11, 'Select the options that can correctly substitute the given phrase: One who likes to fight-

A. cheeky

B. belligerent

C. bellicose

D. flippant

Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"A, B and C only"},{"id":"B","text":"A and D only"},{"id":"C","text":"A, B and D"},{"id":"D","text":"B and C only"}]', 0, 'D', '##### Concept:

This question tests morphological roots and vocabulary related to combativeness, aggression, and argumentative character traits. In Latin-derived English vocabulary, the root morpheme bell- or belli- originates from bellum (meaning war or battle). Adjectives derived from this root specifically delineate a disposition marked by hostility, aggression, and an eager readiness to engage in physical or verbal combat.

#### Step 1: Detailed Lexical Analysis of the Terms:

- A. Cheeky: Impudent, insolent, or irreverently bold, typically in an amusing or endearing way; does not imply a desire to engage in fights.

- B. Belligerent: Derived from Latin bellum (war) + gerere (to wage). It defines an individual who is hostile, aggressive, pugnacious, and inclined to fight or wage conflict.

- C. Bellicose: Derived from Latin bellicosus (warlike). It specifically describes someone demonstrating a warlike disposition, eager to quarrel, fight, or start hostilities.

- D. Flippant: Showing an inappropriate lack of seriousness, depth, or respect; light-minded or glib.

#### Step 2: Grouping Valid Substitutions:

Both “belligerent” (B) and “bellicose” (C) directly substitute the definition “one who likes to fight” (pugnacious/combative). In contrast, “cheeky” and “flippant” describe disrespectful or casual tones rather than aggressive fight-seeking tendencies.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The valid terms are B and C only, which is Option (D).

Quick Tip: Root word clue:

Belli- = war/combat.

Belligerent and Bellicose both signify being combative, warlike, and prone to fighting.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.916Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5437, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q14', 12, 12, 'Select the correct word from the given options to fill in the blank in the given sentence: I am _____ to solve this problem today.', '[{"id":"A","text":"determination"},{"id":"B","text":"determined"},{"id":"C","text":"determine"},{"id":"D","text":"determining"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

This question evaluates understanding of predicative adjectives, participial complements, and subject-verb-complement agreement. In English syntax, when the copular (linking) verb “to be” (am, is, are, was, were) connects the subject to a state of firm resolution, it requires a predicative adjective or past participle functioning adjectivally. The established grammatical construction to express unwavering resolve is be + determined + to-infinitive.

Step 1: Morphological and Grammatical Breakdown of the Options:

- (A) determination: An abstract noun denoting the quality of being resolute. A noun cannot serve directly as a descriptive predicate complement without an article or preposition (e.g., one cannot say “I am determination to solve”).

- (B) determined: A participial adjective expressing resolute purpose, taking a catenative to-infinitive complement (“determined to solve”).

- (C) determine: The base/bare infinitive form of the verb. It cannot directly follow the linking verb “am” without an auxiliary structure.

- (D) determining: The present participle/gerund form, indicating an active causal role (e.g., “the determining factor”), which distorts the intended meaning of personal resolve.

#### Step 2: Syntactic Verification:

Substituting “determined” into the sentence creates the standard idiom of resolve:

“I am determined to solve this problem today.”

Step 3: Final Answer:

The grammatically correct word is “determined”, matching Option (B).

Quick Tip: Grammar pattern:

Subject + be (am/is/are) + determined + to + V_{1}.

Use the participial adjective to show personal resolve.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.916Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5438, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q15', 13, 13, 'Choose the correctly spelled word from the given options:', '[{"id":"A","text":"ingredients"},{"id":"B","text":"ingredients"},{"id":"C","text":"ingredients"},{"id":"D","text":"engredients"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Orthographic competency in English requires knowledge of etymological roots and correct suffixation patterns. Many English nouns derived from Latin verbs of the third conjugation end in the Latin present participial suffix -ens / -entem, which translates into English as the

suffix -ent (forming plural -ents), rather than the alternative -ant. Recognizing the underlying Latin stem prevents common phonological errors involving vowel reduction (schwa sounds).

#### Step 1: Etymological and Morphological Analysis:

- The word originates from the Latin verb ingredi (to go into, enter), formed from the prefix in (into) and gradi (to step or walk).

- Its present participle is ingredientem (that which enters into a compound).

- English spelling adopts the root ingred- plus the standard suffix -ient, giving the singular ingredient and plural ingredients.

#### Step 2: Evaluation of the Options:

- (A) ingredients: Incorrect; mistakenly substitutes the suffix -ants for -ents.

- (B) ingredients: Correct; accurately preserves the root vowel ''e'' and suffix -ents.

- (C) ingredients: Incorrect; introduces an erroneous ''a'' into the middle stem.

- (D) ingredients: Incorrect; uses the mistaken prefix en- instead of the Latin in-.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The only correctly spelled word is “ingredients”, which is Option (B).

Quick Tip: Spelling checklist for “ingredients”:

Prefix: in- | Stem: -gred- | Suffix: -ients.

Always verify the -ent suffix rather than -ant.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.917Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5439, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q17', 14, 14, 'Fill in the blank with the correctly spelled word from the options given below:


After signing the agreement with the authorities, the mood of the company''s director became _____.', '[{"id":"A","text":"ebulient"},{"id":"B","text":"ebullient"},{"id":"C","text":"ebbulient"},{"id":"D","text":"ebulient"}]', 0, 'B', '##### Concept:

Advanced vocabulary orthography requires familiarity with Latin morphological doubling rules. Words derived from Latin compounds containing the prefix e- (a variant of ex- meaning “out”) followed by a root beginning with a consonant do not double the prefix consonant, but preserve the geminate (doubled) liquid consonants present in the root stem. For example, Latin bullire (to boil/bubble) retains its double ''l'' when forming the adjective.

Step 1: Etymological and Orthographic Analysis:

- The word is derived from the Latin verb ebullire (to boil up or burst forth), compounded from e- (out) + bullire (to boil, from bulla = bubble).

- Meaning: Overflowing with enthusiasm, high spirits, exuberance, or exhilaration.

- Orthographic structure: Single ''e'', single ''b'', double ''l'', and ending in the suffix -ient.

- Correct form: e-b-u-l-l-i-e-n-t.

Step 2: Evaluation of Options:

- (A) ebulient: Incorrect because the liquid consonant ''l'' is not doubled.

- (B) ebullient: Correctly spelled with single ''b'' and double ''l''.

- (C) ebbulient: Incorrect because it mistakenly doubles the ''b''.

- (D) ebuleint: Incorrect due to missing double ''l'' and transposed vowels ''ei''.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct spelling is “ebullient”, which matches Option (B).

Quick Tip: Memory aid for ebullient:

Think of “bubbles” (double l) boiling over with enthusiasm: 1 ''b'' + 2 ''l''s + ient.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.917Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5440, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q18', 15, 15, 'Fill in the blank in the given sentence with the most suitable word from the options given below:

She''ll have a birthday present of one thousand rupees for five _____ years.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Success"},{"id":"B","text":"Successive"},{"id":"C","text":"Successful"},{"id":"D","text":"Succeed"}]', 0, 'B', '##### Concept:

This question tests lexical selection and word family differentiation. Derivations originating from the Latin verb succedere (to go below, follow after) branch into two distinct semantic pathways in modern English: one relating to achievement and prosperity (succeed, success, successful), and another relating to temporal sequence, succession, and consecutive order (succeed, succession, successive). Choosing the correct word requires analyzing whether the

context demands a qualifier of achievement or a quantifier of sequential time.

##### Step 1: Morphological and Semantic Analysis of the Options:

- (A) Success: A noun meaning the favorable or prosperous termination of attempts; cannot grammatically modify the plural noun “years”.

- (B) Successive: An adjective meaning following in uninterrupted order or unbroken sequence; consecutive (e.g., “for three successive days”).

- (C) Successful: An adjective meaning having achieved popularity, profit, or desired results. Describing years as “successful years” would alter the intended meaning from recurring annual gifts to business/career prosperity.

- (D) Succeed: A verb meaning to attain a goal or to follow next; cannot function as an attributive modifier before a noun.

##### Step 2: Contextual Application:

The phrase "for five _____ years" clearly specifies a recurring annual duration (five consecutive years in a row). The precise adjective denoting consecutive occurrences is "successive".

Step 3: Final Answer:

The appropriate word is “Successive”, which is Option (B).

Quick Tip: Distinguish between the two adjective branches:

Successive = consecutive / in a row.

Successful = achieving success / prosperous.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.917Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5441, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q19', 16, 16, 'Fill in the blank with the most suitable word from the options given below :

The Principal gave some _____ suggestions to the students and asked them to call off the strike.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Alternate"},{"id":"B","text":"Alternating"},{"id":"C","text":"Alternately"},{"id":"D","text":"Altering"}]', 0, 'A', '##### Concept:

This question tests the selection of suitable adjectival modifiers from a group of paronymous words sharing the root alter (other). In English usage, adjectives modifying nouns such as “suggestions”, “routes”, or “plans” in conflict-resolution contexts must convey the availability of substitute choices or alternative options. Grammatical category rules also eliminate adverbial and participial forms that do not fit the attributive position before a plural noun.

#### Step 1: Detailed Lexical Analysis of the Options:

- (A) Alternate: Used adjectivally to mean alternative, substitute, or offering a choice between possibilities (e.g., “an alternate plan”, “alternate suggestions”).

- (B) Alternating: Present participle/adjective describing something that occurs in successive turns or oscillates back and forth repeatedly (e.g., “alternating current”).

- (C) Alternately: An adverb meaning in mutually following turns; cannot modify the noun “suggestions”.

- (D) Altering: Present participle of the verb alter (to change); implies causing modification rather than presenting fresh options.

#### Step 2: Contextual Application:

To persuade students to call off a strike, the Principal provided secondary options or substitute solutions (i.e., “alternate suggestions”).

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct and most appropriate word is “Alternate”, which is Option (A).

Quick Tip: When describing substitute options or proposals, use alternate (or alternative) as the descriptive adjective before the noun.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.917Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5442, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q20', 17, 17, 'Fill in the blank with the most appropriate word from the options given below:

Although Sudhir did not get much time to prepare, yet he could present the topic ___ before the board of directors.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Effective"},{"id":"B","text":"Effectively"},{"id":"C","text":"Effectfully"},{"id":"D","text":"Affective"}]', 0, 'B', '##### Concept:

This question evaluates the syntactic distribution between adjectives and adverbs of manner. In English sentence structure, an adjective is restricted to modifying nouns or functioning as a subject complement after a linking verb. Conversely, when a word modifies an action verb or transitive verbal phrase (such as “could present the topic”), an adverb of manner (characteristically ending in the productive suffix -ly) is strictly required to describe the quality or success of that performance.

#### Step 1: Syntactic and Morphological Analysis:

- Clause structure: Subject ("he") + Modal/Verb ("could present") + Direct Object ("the topic")

+ [Blank] + Prepositional Phrase ("before the board...").

- The blank modifies the action of presenting (the verb “present”); therefore, it requires an adverb of manner.

Step 2: Evaluation of Options:

- (A) Effective: Adjective; produces a desired effect. Cannot directly modify the verb “present”.

- (B) Effectively: Adverb of manner; in a successful, convincing, or productive manner. Perfectly fits the required adverbial role.

- (C) Effectfully: Non-standard and ungrammatical form.

- (D) Affective: Adjective; relates to moods, emotions, and feelings (from psychology). Both incorrect in part of speech and meaning.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct adverb is “Effectively”, matching Option (B).

##### Quick Tip: Ask “How did he present the topic?”

The answer to “How?” modifying a verb is always an adverb (ending in -ly): Effectively.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.917Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5443, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q21', 18, 18, 'Choose the option which is grammatically correct:', '[{"id":"A","text":"She is enough wise to decide on the right course of action."},{"id":"B","text":"She is wise enough to decide on the right course of action."},{"id":"C","text":"She is too wise to decide on the right course of action."},{"id":"D","text":"She is wise rather to decide on the right course of action."}]', 0, 'B', '#### Concept:

The syntactic positioning of the modifier “enough” represents a foundational rule in English grammar. Unlike most standard adverbs of degree (such as very, quite, exceedingly), which precede the adjectives they modify, “enough” functions as a postpositive modifier when qualifying adjectives or adverbs. That is, it must be placed directly after the adjective/adverb. When “enough” modifies a noun, however, it is placed before the noun as a determiner.

#### Step 1: Structural Rules for “Enough”:

- Rule 1: Adjective/Adverb + enough + to-infinitive (e.g., “wise enough to decide”, “fast enough to win”).

- Rule 2: Enough + Noun + to-infinitive (e.g., “enough wisdom to decide”, “enough time to finish”).

Step 2: Evaluation of Options:

- (A) “enough wise”: Violates Rule 1 by placing “enough” before the adjective.

- (B) “wise enough to decide”: Flawlessly conforms to Rule 1, placing “enough” immediately after the adjective “wise” and preceding the to-infinitive.

- (C) “too wise to decide”: The construction too + adjective + to-infinitive conveys an excessively negative implication (“so wise that she cannot decide”), which creates an illogical semantic contradiction.

- (D) “wise rather to decide”: Grammatically invalid and incoherent word order.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The grammatically correct option is Option (B).

Quick Tip: Positioning of Enough:

Adjective + enough (e.g., wise enough, strong enough).

Enough + Noun (e.g., enough money, enough experience).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.917Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5444, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q22', 19, 19, 'Fill in the blanks with the most suitable word from the options given below:

The desire for revenge is very dangerous_____ it does not diminish with time.', '[{"id":"A","text":"whereas"},{"id":"B","text":"while"},{"id":"C","text":"because"},{"id":"D","text":"so"}]', 0, 'C', '##### Concept:

Subordinating conjunctions establish precise logical and semantic relationships between main clauses and subordinate clauses. When connecting an assertion or evaluative claim (e.g., “revenge is very dangerous”) with its direct causal explanation or underlying justification (e.g., “it does not diminish with time”), a subordinating conjunction of reason or cause must be utilized. Contrastive conjunctions or illative (consequence) conjunctions cannot be applied where a direct cause-and-effect rationale is articulated.

##### Step 1: Detailed Analysis of the Conjunctions:

- (A) Whereas: A subordinating conjunction of contrast used to balance two opposing facts or viewpoints (e.g., “He likes tea, whereas she prefers coffee”).

- (B) While: Used to express simultaneous temporal duration or mild concession/contrast.

- (C) Because: A subordinating conjunction of cause used to introduce the explicit reason or explanation for the statement in the preceding clause.

- (D) So: A coordinating conjunction of result/consequence. If used, the order of clauses would have to be inverted (e.g., “It does not diminish, so it is dangerous”).

#### Step 2: Contextual Application:

The second clause (“it does not diminish with time”) provides the logical reason explaining why the desire for revenge is dangerous. Therefore, “because” correctly expresses the causal link.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct conjunction is “because”, which corresponds to Option (C).

Quick Tip: To identify causal conjunctions:

If the second clause answers the question “Why is it dangerous?”, join them with because or since.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.917Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5445, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q23', 20, 20, 'Select the appropriate word to fill in the blank:

Rahul thought he had a good chance to get the job _____ his uncle was on the company''s board of directors.', '[{"id":"A","text":"but"},{"id":"B","text":"yet"},{"id":"C","text":"since"},{"id":"D","text":"unless"}]', 0, 'C', '##### Concept:

Conjunctions function as vital cohesive ties in complex discourse. When explaining the rationale, premise, or motivational belief behind a person''s expectation, causal subordinators such as since, as, or because are deployed. The conjunction “since” specifically introduces a known fact or circumstance that serves as the basis or justification for a deduction or assumption made in the main clause.

##### Step 1: Evaluation of the Options:

- (A) But: A coordinating adversative conjunction introducing a contradiction, exception, or contrast; unfitting because having an uncle on the board supports, rather than contradicts, his optimism.

- (B) Yet: An adversative conjunction synonymous with “nevertheless”; creates an incorrect conflict between the clauses.

- (C) Since: A subordinating conjunction denoting “inasmuch as”, “because”, or “given that”; it correctly provides the reason for Rahul’s confidence.

- (D) Unless: A conditional negative conjunction meaning “except if”; illogically suggests he would have a good chance only if his uncle were not on the board.

##### Step 2: Contextual Synthesis:

The presence of Rahul''s uncle on the board of directors is the explicit reason for his belief that he had a good chance to secure the employment. “Since” seamlessly supplies this explanatory link.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct conjunction is “since”, corresponding to Option (C).

Quick Tip: “Since” has two major functions:

1. Time: indicating duration from a past point (since 1990).

2. Cause: functioning as a synonym for because / given that.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.918Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5446, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q24', 21, 21, 'Choose the option which best expresses the same sentence in Passive voice:

#### Every Monday, she paid him rent without fail.', '[{"id":"A","text":"She had paid him rent every Monday without fail."},{"id":"B","text":"Every Monday, he was paid rent by her without fail."},{"id":"C","text":"Every Monday, he has been paid rent by her."},{"id":"D","text":"He is paid rent by her, every Monday without fail."}]', 0, 'B', '#### Concept:

Voice transformation shifts the grammatical focus from the agent performing the action (Active Voice) to the recipient or patient experiencing the action (Passive Voice) without altering the fundamental tense, aspect, or contextual adverbials of the original sentence. When transforming a ditransitive verb (a verb with both a direct object and an indirect object) in the Simple Past tense, the indirect object pronoun (“him”) typically becomes the subject pronoun (“he”), and the auxiliary verb must strictly reflect the simple past tense of the original verb.

Step 1: Grammatical Analysis of the Active Sentence:

- Active Sentence: “Every Monday, she paid him rent without fail.”

- Tense: Simple Past  $ V_{2} $: “paid”

- Subject (Agent): “she”

- Indirect Object: “him”

- Direct Object: “rent”

- Adverbial Modifiers: “Every Monday”, “without fail”

#### Step 2: Passive Voice Formula for Simple Past:

Subject (from Object) + was / were + V3 (past participle) + Direct Object + by + Agent

Applying this formula with the indirect object “him” as the new subject:

him  $ \rightarrow $ he + was + paid + rent + by her.

Retaining the original adverbial phrases yields:

“Every Monday, he was paid rent by her without fail.”

#### Step 3: Evaluation of Options:

- (A): Remains in active voice and incorrectly shifts tense to past perfect ("had paid").

- (B): Perfectly implements the simple past passive rule while retaining all adverbials intact.

- (C): Incorrectly alters the tense to present perfect passive (“has been paid”) and omits the adverbial “without fail”.

- (D): Incorrectly shifts the tense to simple present passive ("is paid").

#### Step 4: Final Answer:

The correct passive transformation is Option (B).

##### Quick Tip: Tense consistency rule in Passive Voice:

Active Simple Past  $ V_2 $  $ \longrightarrow $ Passive was/were +  $ V_3 $.

Do not change the tense to perfect or continuous forms!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.918Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5447, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q25', 22, 22, 'Select the option that best expresses the synonym of the given word:

##### Mortify', '[{"id":"A","text":"embarrass"},{"id":"B","text":"puzzle"},{"id":"C","text":"praise"},{"id":"D","text":"coax"}]', 0, 'A', NULL, NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.918Z', NULL, NULL, 'none', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5448, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q26', 23, 23, 'Select the option which expresses the given sentence in Indirect speech: The visitor said to Sameer," Why did your father drag you into this situation?"', '[{"id":"A","text":"The visitor said to Sameer that why his father dragged him into this situation."},{"id":"B","text":"The visitor asked Sameer why his father dragged him into that situation?"},{"id":"C","text":"The visitor asked Sameer why his father had dragged him into that situation."},{"id":"D","text":"The visitor told Sameer why his father had dragged him into that situation?"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Concept:

Direct to Indirect Speech transformation of interrogative sentences (specifically Wh-questions) requires the application of several fundamental grammatical shift rules. When converting an interrogative sentence:

1. Reporting Verb: The reporting verb “said to” changes to “asked”, “inquired of”, or “demanded of”.

2. Conjunction / Connector: No conjunction like “that” or “if/whether” is used before Wh-question words (who, what, where, why, when, how). The Wh-word itself functions as the connecting relative adverb.

3. Sentence Structure Shift: The inverted interrogative word order (Auxiliary + Subject + Main Verb) must convert into standard assertive word order (Subject + Auxiliary/Verb), and the question mark is replaced by a full stop.

4. Tense Backshift: A Simple Past tense verb in direct speech (“did drag”) backshifts into the Past Perfect tense (“had dragged”).

5. Pronoun and Deictic Shifts: Second-person possessives and pronouns shift according to the object of the reporting verb (“your father” → “his father”, “you” → “him”), and proximal

demonstratives shift to distal forms ("this"  $ \rightarrow $ "that").

##### Step 1: Transformation Analysis of the Direct Sentence:

- Reporting clause: “The visitor said to Sameer” → “The visitor asked Sameer”.

- Interrogative connector: “Why” remains “why” (never preceded by “that”).

- Subject transposition: The subject “your father” shifts to third-person singular “his father”.

- Tense shift: Simple past “did drag” shifts to past perfect “had dragged”.

- Object pronoun shift: Object “you” shifts to “him”.

- Demonstrative shift: “this situation” shifts to “that situation”.

- Punctuation: The terminating question mark is replaced by a period (full stop).

##### Step 2: Evaluation of Options:

- Option (A): Incorrectly includes the redundant conjunction “that” before “why”, fails to backshift the tense to past perfect (uses “dragged”), and retains the proximal demonstrative “this”.

- Option (B): Incorrectly uses simple past “dragged” instead of past perfect “had dragged” and mistakenly retains the question mark.

- Option (C): Accurately applies the reporting verb “asked”, eliminates “that”, applies past perfect “had dragged”, converts “this” to “that”, and ends with a full stop.

- Option (D): Uses the incorrect reporting verb “told” for an interrogative construction and retains the question mark.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correctly transformed sentence is given in Option (C).

Quick Tip: Remember the two key rules for Wh-questions in Indirect Speech:

1. Never use “that” before a Wh-word (e.g., “that why” is strictly incorrect).

2. Simple Past $(V_{2} / \mathrm{did} + V_{1})$ always backshifts to Past Perfect $(\mathbf{had} + V_{3})$.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.918Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5449, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q27', 24, 24, 'Select the antonym of the given word: Ostentatious', '[{"id":"A","text":"grand"},{"id":"B","text":"magnificent"},{"id":"C","text":"unobtrusive"},{"id":"D","text":"lofty"}]', 0, 'C', '##### Concept:

Antonym determination involves identifying the lexical item that embodies the diametrical opposite of a word''s primary denotation and behavioral connotation. The adjective "ostentatious" originates from the Latin noun ostentatio (a vain display, showing off), derived from ostendere (to show or exhibit). It describes actions, possessions, manners, or architectural styles designed deliberately to attract excessive attention, impress others, and flaunt wealth or importance in a vulgar or pretentious manner. An antonym must therefore describe a quality of modesty, restraint, subtlety, or an absence of desire to draw public notice.

#### Step 1: Detailed Lexical Breakdown of “Ostentatious”:

- Definition: Characterized by vulgar, pretentious, or showy display designed to impress, boast, or attract attention; flamboyant, grandiose, or flashy.

- Typical Synonyms: Pretentious, showy, flamboyant, gaudy, conspicuous, boastful, grandiose.

- Typical Antonyms: Unobtrusive, modest, restrained, understated, humble, inconspicuous, quiet.

Step 2: Evaluation of Options:

- (A) Grand: Magnificent, imposing, or impressive in size and appearance; shares semantic overlap with ostentatious displays (synonymous attribute).

- (B) Magnificent: Strikingly splendid, grand, or impressive; another near-synonym in aesthetic contexts.

- (C) Unobtrusive: Not conspicuous, not brazenly noticeable, modest, and not attracting undue attention to oneself or one''s possessions. This represents the direct semantic opposite of “ostentatious”.

- (D) Lofty: Imposing, elevated in height, or haughty; does not represent an antonym.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The true antonym of “Ostentatious” is “unobtrusive”, corresponding to Option (C).

Quick Tip: Etymology link:

Ostentatious = showy / flaunting wealth or status.

Unobtrusive = quiet, discreet, and not drawing attention (the exact opposite).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.918Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5450, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q28', 25, 25, 'Match the idioms in List-I with their appropriate meanings in List-II




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td colspan="2">LIST-I (IDIOMS)</td><td colspan="2">LIST-II (MEANINGS)</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>A.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Black and blue</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>I.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Sudden or unexpected shock</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>B.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Bolt from the blue</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>II.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>To be the first to do something</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>C.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Blaze a trial</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>III.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>To beat mercilessly</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>D.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Blue blood</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>IV.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Belong to the royal family</td></tr></table>


Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"A - I, B - II, C - III, D - IV"},{"id":"B","text":"A - III, B - I, C - II, D - IV"},{"id":"C","text":"A - I, B - III, C - II, D - IV"},{"id":"D","text":"A - II, B - IV, C - III, D - I"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Concept:

Idiomatic expressions are fixed figurative phrases whose figurative meaning cannot be deduced purely from the literal definitions of their component words. Color idioms based on “blue” and physical action idioms (like trail-blazing) are common in English tests. Mastering them requires understanding their cultural, historical, and metaphorical origins in classical and contemporary English literature.

Step 1: Detailed Analysis of Each Idiom in List-I:

- A. Black and blue: Originates from the discolored appearance of deep subcutaneous bruises on the skin resulting from severe physical impact. Idiomatically, it means covered in contusions or subjected to a severe, merciless beating (matches III. To beat mercilessly).

- B. Bolt from the blue: Metaphorically compares a sudden, disastrous thunderbolt emerging from a completely clear, blue sky to an event that happens totally unexpectedly and causes severe shock (matches I. Sudden or unexpected shock).

- C. Blaze a trail: Historically derived from the practice of early explorers chopping notches into tree bark (blazes) to mark an unexplored path through dense wilderness. Figuratively, it means to pioneer a new method or to be the first to do something innovative (matches II. To be the first to do something).

- D. Blue blood: A translation of the Spanish sangre azul, historically attributed to the Castilian aristocracy whose pale, untanned skin made their superficial veins look distinctly blue. Idiomatically, it denotes noble, aristocratic, or royal lineage (matches IV. Belong to the royal family).

Step 2: Synthesizing the Matches:

- A corresponds to III

- B corresponds to I

- C corresponds to II

- D corresponds to IV

Thus, the correct matched combination is A - III, B - I, C - II, D - IV.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The matching code aligns with Option (B).

Quick Tip: Idiom origins:

- Black and blue = bruises from being beaten.

- Bolt from the blue = lightning from clear skies (complete surprise).

- Blue blood = aristocratic/royal birth.

<div style="text-align: center;"><div style="text-align: center;">29. Match the idioms given in List-I with the appropriate meanings in List-II</div> </div>




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td colspan="2">LIST-I (IDIOMS)</td><td colspan="2">LIST-II (MEANINGS)</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>A.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Get into hot waters</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>I.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Inexperienced</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>B.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Green horn</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>II.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>To bribe</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>C.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Give a wide berth</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>III.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Get into trouble</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>D.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Grease the palm</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>IV.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>To avoid</td></tr></table>

Choose the correct answer from the options given below:

(A) A - II, B - IV, C - I, D - III

(B) A - I, B - II, C - III, D - IV

(C) A - III, B - I, C - IV, D - II

(D) A - I, B - III, C - IV, D - II

Correct Answer: (C) A - III, B - I, C - IV, D - II

Solution:

#### Concept:

This question tests idiomatic competency across nautical, administrative, color, and culinary metaphors. Idioms are established figurative expressions that convey distinct socio-pragmatic meanings. Accurately pairing idioms with their definitions requires identifying the underlying metaphorical domain from which each phrase emerged.

#### Step 1: Detailed Analysis of Each Idiom in List-I:

- A. Get into hot waters: Evokes the image of being scalded or plunged into dangerously boiling liquid; idiomatically, it means to find oneself in a difficult, embarrassing, or dangerous situation involving severe trouble or reprimand (matches III. Get into trouble).

- B. Green horn (Greenhorn): Historically referenced young horned cattle whose horns were immature, later applied to novice soldiers and raw apprentices. In modern English, it denotes an inexperienced, naive, or easily duped person (matches I. Inexperienced).

- C. Give a wide berth: Originates in nautical navigation, where a ship allows ample sea room (berth) around rocks, shoals, or other vessels to prevent collision. Figuratively, it means to steer clear of, keep a safe distance from, or avoid someone or something entirely (matches IV. To avoid).

- D. Grease the palm: Originates from the metaphor of applying lubricant to mechanisms to make them run smoothly; in human interaction, it signifies paying a clandestine bribe or illicit fee to expedite a process or secure favorable treatment (matches II. To bribe).

##### Step 2: Synthesizing the Matches:

- A matches with III

- B matches with I

- C matches with IV

- D matches with II

Therefore, the correct mapping is A - III, B - I, C - IV, D - II.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The matching sequence corresponds to Option (C).

Quick Tip: Nautical & daily life idiom meanings:

- Wide berth = keep distance / avoid.

- Grease palm = bribe.

- Greenhorn = novice / inexperienced.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.919Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5451, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q30', 26, 26, 'Fill in the blanks in the sentences given in List I with the appropriate idioms given in List II.




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td colspan="2">LIST-I (Sentences)</td><td colspan="2">LIST-II (Idioms)</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>A.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Harassed by the repeated rebukes by the manager, the salesman decided to _____.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>I.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>tall order</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>B.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>It is a____ to control pollution in the cities.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>II.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>my cup of tea</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>C.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Swimming is not____ as I have no courage.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>III.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>bone of contention</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>D.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>The new flat is a____ between the two brothers.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>IV.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>put his foot down</td></tr></table>


##### Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"A - I, B - III, C - II, D - IV"},{"id":"B","text":"A - I, B - II, C - IV, D - III"},{"id":"C","text":"A - II, B - III, C - I, D - IV"},{"id":"D","text":"A - N, B - I, C - II, D - III"}]', 0, 'D', '##### Concept:

Idiomatic contextualization requires matching specific figurative idioms to sentences where the situational semantics, emotional tone, and syntactic constraints align seamlessly. The meaning of each idiom must satisfy both the logical premise of the sentence and the grammatical structure of the predicate.

##### Step 1: Detailed Contextual Analysis of Each Sentence:

- Sentence A: "Harassed by the repeated rebukes by the manager, the salesman decided to ___ "

The salesman is facing unfair harassment and needs to assert himself firmly to stop the mistreatment. The idiom put his foot down (IV) means to take a firm, resolute stand against something. Thus, A  $ \rightarrow $ IV.

- Sentence B: "It is a _____ to control pollution in the cities."

Controlling urban pollution is an extraordinarily difficult and demanding task. The idiom tall order (I) signifies a task or demand that is exceedingly difficult to fulfill. Thus, B  $ \rightarrow $ I.

- Sentence C: "Swimming is not _____ as I have no courage."

The speaker admits an aversion or lack of affinity for swimming. The idiom my cup of tea (II), typically used in the negative (“not my cup of tea”), denotes something that does not suit one’s taste, preference, or inclination. Thus, C → II.

- Sentence D: "The new flat is a _____ between the two brothers."

The property is the central source of dispute, argument, or rivalry between the brothers. The idiom bone of contention (III) refers to a subject or issue over which there is continuing disagreement. Thus, D  $ \rightarrow $ III.

Step 2: Synthesizing the Complete Match:

-A $ \rightarrow $IV

-B $ \rightarrow $I

-C $ \rightarrow $Ⅱ

-D  $ \rightarrow $ III

This produces the combination: A - IV, B - I, C - II, D - III.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The matching code aligns with Option (D).

Quick Tip: Contextual Clues:

- “Controlling pollution” = a formidable challenge  $ \rightarrow $ tall order.

- “Disputed property” = source of quarrel  $ \rightarrow $ bone of contention.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.919Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5452, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q31', 27, 27, 'Select the correct words given in List-I to complete the sentences given in List-II :




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td colspan="2">LIST-I</td><td colspan="2">LIST-II</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>A.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>laid off</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>I.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>The wise people always___ money for old age.</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>B.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>lay up</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>II.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>The workers were___ due to financial problems.</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>C.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>laid out</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>III.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>He always violated the rules___ by the office.</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>D.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>laid down</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>IV.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>The gardens were___ with lawns and flower beds.</td></tr></table>


Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"A - II, B - III, C - I, D - IV"},{"id":"B","text":"A - IV, B - II, C - I, D - III"},{"id":"C","text":"A - II, B - I, C - IV, D - III"},{"id":"D","text":"A - I, B - III, C - II, D - IV"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Phrasal verbs constructed from the base verb lay (such as lay off, lay up, lay out, lay down)

possess highly specialized and distinct meanings based on the accompanying preposition or particle. Successfully completing these sentences requires matching each phrasal verb to its specific operational domain, ranging from corporate retrenchment and resource accumulation to landscape architecture and legislative regulation.

##### Step 1: Detailed Analysis of Phrasal Verbs in List-I:

- A. Laid off (from lay off): To discharge or terminate employees temporarily or permanently due to economic hardship or lack of operational work. Matches Sentence II ("The workers were laid off due to financial problems.").

- B. Lay up (from lay up): To store, hoard, accumulate, or save provisions or money for future necessity or emergency. Matches Sentence I ("The wise people always lay up money for old age.").

- C. Laid out (from lay out): To plan, arrange, design, or physically construct a landscape, garden, or urban space according to a systematic blueprint. Matches Sentence IV ("The gardens were laid out with lawns and flower beds.").

- D. Laid down (from lay down): To formally establish, prescribe, formulate, or enact rules, laws, principles, or regulations. Matches Sentence III ("He always violated the rules laid down by the office.").

#### Step 2: Synthesizing the Pairings:

- A matches with II

- B matches with I

- C matches with IV

- D matches with III

The resulting sequence is: A - II, B - I, C - IV, D - III.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct matching sequence corresponds to Option (C).

Quick Tip: Phrasal verb memory hooks with Lay:

- Lay off = dismiss workers.

- Lay up = save/store money.

- Lay down = establish rules.

- Lay out = arrange/design a garden.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.919Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5453, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q32', 28, 28, 'Match the phrasal verbs given in List-I with the meanings given in List-II




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td colspan="2">LIST-I</td><td colspan="2">LIST-II</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>A.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Call off</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>I.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>visit a place to meet someone</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>B.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Call at</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>II.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Demand</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>C.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Call out</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>III.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Suspend or abandon</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>D.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Call for</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>IV.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Ask to come for help</td></tr></table>


Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"A - I, B - III, C - II, D - IV"},{"id":"B","text":"A - III, B - I, C - IV, D - II"},{"id":"C","text":"A - III, B - II, C - I, D - IV"},{"id":"D","text":"A - II, B - IV, C - I, D - III"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Phrasal verbs formed with the root verb call combine with particles (off; at, out, for) to generate idiomatic meanings spanning cancellation, visitation, emergency summoning, and official requirement. Differentiating these phrasal verbs is essential for mastering formal English vocabulary.

Step 1: Detailed Lexical Breakdown of Each Phrasal Verb:

- A. Call off: Means to cancel, postpone indefinitely, suspend, or abandon an event, strike, match, or operation (matches III. Suspend or abandon).

- B. Call at: Means to stop briefly at a port, station, or visit a specific residence/place briefly to

meet someone (matches I. visit a place to meet someone).

- C. Call out: Means to summon someone to deal with an emergency, mobilize emergency services, or ask someone to come for urgent assistance (matches IV. Ask to come for help).

- D. Call for: Means to publicly demand, necessitate, or require a particular action, explanation, or measure (matches II. Demand).

#### Step 2: Evaluating the Match Combinations:

- A matches III

- B matches I

- C matches IV

- D matches II

The matching code is: A - III, B - I, C - IV, D - II.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct answer is Option (B).

Quick Tip: Phrasal verbs with Call:

- Call off = cancel / suspend.

- Call for = demand / require.

- Call out = summon emergency help.

- Call at = visit a place.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.919Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5454, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q33', 29, 29, 'Fill in the blanks with the most appropriate option :

Kindness is a quality that is alien ___ Harish.', '[{"id":"A","text":"for"},{"id":"B","text":"to"},{"id":"C","text":"about"},{"id":"D","text":"of"}]', 0, 'B', '##### Concept:

This question tests dependent prepositions and fixed adjective-preposition collocations. In English grammar, certain descriptive adjectives take specific prepositions to connect with their indirect objects or referents. The adjective "alien" in the sense of unfamiliar, foreign, or contrary to someone''s inherent nature requires the preposition to.

#### Step 1: Detailed Analysis of Dependent Preposition Rules:

- The adjective alien originates from Latin alienus (belonging to another).

- When “alien” is used to describe a concept, behavior, or trait that is foreign, strange, unfamiliar, or repugnant to an individual''s character or personality, it is followed by the preposition to.

- Standard construction: Subject + be + alien + to + person/entity.

##### Step 2: Evaluating the Options:

- (A) for: Incorrect; “alien for” is unidiomatic in standard English.

- (B) to: Correct; forms the standard collocation “alien to Harish” (meaning kindness is completely foreign to Harish''s nature).

- (C) about: Incorrect preposition for expressing personal foreignness.

- (D) of: Incorrect; “alien of” does not function as an adjective complement.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The appropriate preposition is “to”, corresponding to Option (B).

Quick Tip: Fixed Preposition Collocation:

Something is always alien to someone (meaning foreign or unfamiliar to them).

Similar collocations: foreign to, contrary to, opposed to.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.919Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5455, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q34', 30, 30, 'Fill in the blanks with the most suitable option:

The two brothers quarreled _____ the share of their ancestral property.', '[{"id":"A","text":"for"},{"id":"B","text":"at"},{"id":"C","text":"over"},{"id":"D","text":"off"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Concept:

This question evaluates mastery over verb-preposition combinations governing disputed subjects. The verb “quarrel” follows distinct prepositional rules depending on whether the object of the preposition is the person with whom one is arguing or the subject matter/topic causing the dispute:

1. Quarrel with a person (e.g., “He quarreled with his brother”).

2. Quarrel over / about a matter (e.g., “They quarreled over the property”).

#### Step 1: Syntactic and Prepositional Analysis:

- The clause features the plural subject “The two brothers”, the intransitive past verb “quarreled”, and the topic of contention “the share of their ancestral property”.

- When denoting the cause, prize, or disputed object of a feud or fight, standard English employs the preposition over (or about).

#### Step 2: Evaluating the Options:

- (A) for: Incorrect; “quarrel for” does not indicate the subject matter of an argument.

- (B) at: Incorrect; “quarrel at” is ungrammatical in this context.

- (C) over: Correct; “quarreled over the share” accurately expresses dispute regarding ownership of the property.

- (D) off: Incorrect; has no prepositional function in this verbal phrase.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The most suitable preposition is “over”, matching Option (C).

Quick Tip: Remember the preposition rule for Quarrel:

- Quarrel with a person.

- Quarrel over / about a thing or issue.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.919Z', NULL, NULL, 'banner', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5456, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q35', 31, 31, 'Match the Figures of speech given in List-I with their examples in List-II




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td colspan="2">LIST-I (Figures of speech)</td><td colspan="2">LIST-II (Examples)</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>A.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Hyperbole</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>I.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Death dwells in every nook and cranny of the slums.</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>B.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Simile</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>II.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>The fair, funny, fragile looking waif</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>C.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Personification</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>III.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Malicious words are like bullets that tear through you.</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>D.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Alliteration</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>IV.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>The husband''s cruel desertion broke her heart into smithereens.</td></tr></table>


Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"A - II, B - I, C - III, D - IV"},{"id":"B","text":"A - III, B - I, C - IV, D - II"},{"id":"C","text":"A - N, B - III, C - I, D - II"},{"id":"D","text":"A - II, B - IV, C - I, D - III"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept:

Figures of speech represent rhetorical devices used in English literature to achieve artistic, evocative, or emotive expression beyond literal meaning:

1. Hyperbole: Deliberate, bold exaggeration used for dramatic emphasis or emotional impact.

2. Simile: Explicit comparison between two distinctly different entities using comparative markers such as “like” or “as”.

3. Personification: Attribution of human characteristics, actions, or emotions to inanimate objects, abstract concepts, or non-human entities.

4. Alliteration: The conspicuous repetition of identical initial consonant sounds in successive or closely positioned words.

Step 1: Detailed Analysis of Each Example in List-II:

- Example I (“Dearth dwells in every nook and cranny of the slums.”): “Dearth” (an abstract noun meaning scarcity or famine) is depicted as an entity that “dwells” (lives/resides), which is an exclusively animate/human action. This represents Personification (matches C).

- Example II (“The fair, funny, fragile looking waif”): Features the immediate repetition of the initial consonant sound /f/ in “fair, funny, fragile”. This is a classic demonstration of Alliteration (matches D).

- Example III ("Malicious words are like bullets that tear through you."): Directly compares "words" to "bullets" using the explicit comparative connective "like". This is a Simile (matches B).

- Example IV (“The husband’s cruel desertion broke her heart into smithereens.”): Claiming that an emotional heartbreak physically shattered a biological organ into microscopic fragments (“smithereens”) is an extreme rhetorical exaggeration, which defines Hyperbole (matches A).

##### Step 2: Synthesizing the Matches:

- A matches IV

- B matches III

- C matches I

- D matches II

The matching code is: A - IV, B - III, C - I, D - II.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct matching corresponds to Option (C).

Quick Tip: Identification checklist:

- Comparison with “like/as” → Simile.

- Repeated consonant sound  $ \rightarrow $ Alliteration.

- Extreme exaggeration  $ \rightarrow $ Hyperbole.

- Human action given to abstract noun → Personification.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.920Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5457, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q36', 32, 32, 'Match the expressions given in List I with the appropriate figures of speech given in List II :




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td colspan="2">LIST-I (Phrases)</td><td colspan="2">LIST-II (Figures of speech)</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>A.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Her voice crowds up and down the lawn</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>I.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Alliteration</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>B.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>He makes no friend, who never made a foe.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>II.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Personification</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>C.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Singing in summer in full-throated ease</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>III.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Hyperbole</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>D.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Winter laid its icy hands over the city.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>IV.</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>Epigram</td></tr></table>


Choose the correct answer from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"A - II, B - III, C - I, D - IV"},{"id":"B","text":"A - IV, B - I, C - II, D - III"},{"id":"C","text":"A - III, B - II, C - I, D - IV"},{"id":"D","text":"A - III, B - IV, C - I, D - II"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept:

Literary devices enrich poetic and rhetorical texts through distinct stylistic techniques:

1. Epigram: A brief, pithy, and paradoxical statement containing a memorable, witty, or profound philosophical truth.

2. Personification: Investing natural phenomena, seasons, or objects with human physical anatomy or agency.

3. Alliteration: The acoustic repetition of identical consonant sounds at the beginning of neighboring words.

4. Hyperbole: Poetic overstatement magnifying the spatial reach or intensity of sensory impressions.

Step 1: Detailed Analysis of Each Phrase in List-I:

- Phrase A (“Her voice crowds up and down the lawn”): Describing a single human voice as possessing the volume and spatial mass of an entire crowd swarming across a lawn is a bold imaginative exaggeration, constituting Hyperbole (matches III).

- Phrase B ("He makes no friend, who never made a foe." - Tennyson): A classic literary

paradox stating that a person of strong conviction inevitably makes both friends and enemies. Such a succinct, paradoxical truth is an Epigram (matches IV).

- Phrase C (“Singing in summer in full-throated ease” - Keats): Features the prominent repetition of the initial sibilant consonant /s/ in “ $ \underline{\text{Singing}} $ in  $ \underline{\text{s}} $ummer”. This represents Alliteration (matches I).

- Phrase D (“Winter laid its icy hands over the city.”): The season of “Winter” is endowed with human anatomy (“hands”) performing the physical action of touching/laying hands. This is Personification (matches II).

Step 2: Synthesizing the Matches:

- A  $ \rightarrow $ III

-B  $ \rightarrow $ IV

-C  $ \rightarrow $ I

-D  $ \rightarrow $ II

The resulting sequence is: A - III, B - IV, C - I, D - II.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct matching sequence corresponds to Option (D).

Quick Tip: Remember:

- Epigram: A concise, paradoxical saying containing deep truth (e.g., Tennyson''s line on friends and foes).

- Personification: Attributing body parts ("icy hands") to seasons/nature.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.920Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5458, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q38', 33, 33, 'Select the appropriate option to fill in the blank:

The mob was _____ to indulge in a series of acts of arson.', '[{"id":"A","text":"inspired"},{"id":"B","text":"instigated"},{"id":"C","text":"motivated"},{"id":"D","text":"animated"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Concept:

Semantic prosody and contextual collocation dictate word choice when describing illegal, violent, or destructive mob actions. Certain verbs share an overarching denotation of “spurring or prompting action”, but carry sharply polarized connotations:

- Positive/Noble actions are prompted by verbs like inspired or motivated.

- Unlawful, violent, or riotous actions (e.g., riots, rebellion, arson, violence) uniquely collocate with verbs having negative semantic prosody, specifically instigate or incite.

Step 1: Detailed Lexical Analysis of the Options:

- (A) Inspired: Filled with the urge or ability to do something creative, noble, or artistic; has an overwhelmingly positive connotation.

- (B) Instigated: Incited, provoked, or urged someone to commit an unlawful, harmful, or destructive action (e.g., “instigated a riot”, “instigated acts of arson”). Perfectly carries the required negative semantic prosody.

- (C) Motivated: Provided with a reason or enthusiasm to accomplish a goal; predominantly used for productive or neutral psychological drives.

- (D) Animated: Endowed with life, vigor, or spirited movement; descriptive of lively mannerisms rather than unlawful incitement.

#### Step 2: Contextual Fit:

“Arson” (the criminal act of deliberately setting fire to property) represents a severe violent crime. In standard English, an unruly crowd or mob committing arson is said to be instigated (or incited) by provocateurs.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The appropriate word is “instigated”, corresponding to Option (B).

Quick Tip: Negative Semantic Collocation:

- Mob + Crime/Violence/Arson  $ \longrightarrow $ Instigated / Incited.

- Positive/Creative acts  $ \longrightarrow $ Inspired / Motivated.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.920Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5459, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q39', 34, 34, 'Fill in the blank with the correct word:

The _____ lady followed the sacrosanct rituals with full devotion.', '[{"id":"A","text":"pious"},{"id":"B","text":"devious"},{"id":"C","text":"sacrilegious"},{"id":"D","text":"careless"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept:

This question tests semantic congruity and tone alignment in descriptive prose. When

qualifying a subject who adheres strictly to sacred religious observances (“followed the sacrosanct rituals with full devotion”), the attributive adjective must harmonize with reverence, spiritual piety, and devout faith. Adjectives denoting deceit, irreverence, or negligence produce severe semantic contradictions.

#### Step 1: Detailed Lexical Breakdown of the Options:

- (A) Pious: Devoutly religious; showing dutiful reverence, deep devotion, and respect for God or sacred religious observances. Perfectly harmonizes with “followed sacrosanct rituals with full devotion”.

- (B) Devious: Showing a skillful use of underhanded or dishonest tactics to achieve goals; deceitful.

- (C) Sacrilegious: Involving or characterized by sacrilege; having a profound lack of respect for sacred things or profaning holy objects. This is the direct opposite of devoted ritual observance.

- (D) Careless: Not giving sufficient attention or thought; negligent. Directly contradicts the phrase “with full devotion”.

#### Step 2: Contextual Application:

A person who performs “sacrosanct rituals with full devotion” is by definition devout and reverent, which is the exact definition of  $ \textit{pious} $.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct word to fill in the blank is “pious”, which matches Option (A).

Quick Tip: Vocabulary harmony:

Pious (devout/holy) directly pairs with sacrosanct (sacred) and devotion.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.920Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5460, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q40', 35, 35, 'Fill in the blank with the appropriate word:

His folly will surely _____ his ruin.', '[{"id":"A","text":"bring out"},{"id":"B","text":"bring up"},{"id":"C","text":"bring about"},{"id":"D","text":"bring in"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Concept:

Phrasal verbs constructed with the verb bring convey distinct causative, introductory, developmental, or commercial actions based on the attached preposition:

- Bring about: To cause something to happen, produce a result, or lead to a consequence.

- Bring out: To publish, release a product, or emphasize a particular quality.

- Bring up: To rear/raise a child, or mention a topic in discussion.

- Bring in: To earn income, introduce legislation, or yield profit.

#### Step 1: Detailed Analysis of the Sentence Structure:

- Subject: “His folly” (his foolishness / lack of wisdom).

- Object: “his ruin” (his downfall / destruction).

- The relationship between foolish behavior (folly) and downfall (ruin) is direct causality (cause  $ \rightarrow $ effect).

- The phrasal verb meaning “to cause to happen” or “to lead directly to an outcome” is bring about.

#### Step 2: Evaluating the Options:

- (A) bring out: Means to publish or highlight; inappropriate for causing ruin.

- (B) bring up: Means to raise/educate children or introduce a discussion point.

- (C) bring about: Correctly expresses the causative relation: “His folly will surely cause his ruin”.

- (D) bring in: Means to generate revenue or introduce a new bill.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The appropriate phrasal verb is “bring about”, which is Option (C).

Quick Tip: Common Phrasal Verb with Bring:

Bring about = to cause / lead to a result (e.g., bring about a change, bring about ruin).

##### Read the passage given below and answer the questions that follow (Questions 41 to 45):

There’s a certain magic in the first chill of season... that moment when India’s forests stir back to life. The sanctuary gates creak open, migratory birds return, and air carries that unmistakable scent of damp leaves and wild earth. In western Odisha, Dibrugarh Wildlife Sanctuary, near Sambalpur, is at the heart of this quiet awakening. Now on the cusp of being declared India’s newest tiger reserve, it’s drawing attention not just for its Wildlife, but for the people who have helped it thrive. Spread across forested slopes along the shimmering Hirakud reservoir, Dibrugarh is a revelation—serene, scenic, and full of small surprises. Mornings here are a melody of birdsongs echoing over still water. Spotted deer and langurs, dart through sal forests while eagles trace lazy circles in the sky. On Bot Island, thousands of bats hang like dark fruits from ancient trees. What was once an overlooked stretch of woodland is today one of eastern India’s most inspiring stories of eco-tourism and community-led conservation.

At the edge of this wilderness lies Sambalpur, a city that rests gracefully along Mahanadi River. Its past stretches from the Chauhan dynasty to colonial days, and its present hums with trade, textiles, and temple bells. The river runs through it all, shaping lives, livelihoods, and legends.

A short drive away, Hirakud Dam stands as both an engineering marvel and landscape poetry, a structure that tames Mahanadi yet adds to its splendour. Built in 1950, it remains one of world''s longest earthen dams. From Gandhi Minar viewpoint, the reservoir stretches like molten silver, dotted with fishing boats and green islets—same waters that cradle Dibrugarh''s forests. Back in the narrow lanes of Sambalpur, looms click, and threads gleam. This is where the famed Sambalpuri ikat is born, each fabric a labor of precision and patience. During festivals, the city turns into a living gallery, women draped in ikat saris, men in handwoven kurtas—each garment carrying stories of skill, soil, and heritage.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.920Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5461, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q42', 36, 36, 'Identify the figure of speech used in the line ''Thousands of bats hang like dark fruits''.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Alliteration"},{"id":"B","text":"Metaphor"},{"id":"C","text":"Personification"},{"id":"D","text":"Simile"}]', 0, 'D', '#### Concept:

A Simile is a figure of speech in which an explicit comparison is drawn between two fundamentally dissimilar entities using explicit connective words such as “like”, “as”, or “so”. Unlike a metaphor (which asserts that one thing is another without connecting words), a simile maintains the distinct identity of both subjects while highlighting a vivid visual or sensory resemblance.

Step 1: Textual Breakdown of the Expression:

- Target line: “Thousands of bats hang like dark fruits”

- Primary subject (tenor): “bats”

- Comparative object (vehicle): “dark fruits”

- Comparative connective marker: “like”

- Visual resemblance: The physical posture of dormant bats hanging inverted from branches is directly compared to dark ripe fruits hanging from trees.

Step 2: Evaluating the Figures of Speech:

- (A) Alliteration: Involves the repetition of initial consonant sounds; not the primary device here.

- (B) Metaphor: Would state “thousands of bats were dark fruits” (without “like”).

- (C) Personification: Involves attributing human traits to non-human entities.

- (D) Simile: The presence of the explicit connective marker “like” comparing bats to fruits confirms that the figure of speech is a Simile.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The figure of speech is a “Simile”, which corresponds to Option (D).

Quick Tip: Whenever two distinct items are compared using the words “like” or “as”, the figure of speech is unequivocally a Simile.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.920Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5462, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q43', 37, 37, 'Which of the following river runs through the city of Sambalpur?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Western river"},{"id":"B","text":"Hirakund river"},{"id":"C","text":"Woodland river"},{"id":"D","text":"Mahanadi river"}]', 0, 'D', '#### Concept:

Direct factual recall in reading comprehension requires locating explicit geographical and environmental proper nouns identified in the passage. Scanning techniques must be used to locate the reference to the city of Sambalpur and extract the name of the river directly associated with its geography and cultural life.

Step 1: Locating Textual Evidence in the Passage:

- In the second paragraph, the passage explicitly records:

"At the edge of this wilderness lies Sambalpur, a city that rests gracefully along Mahanadi River."

- The paragraph further underscores this fact:

“The river runs through it all, shaping lives, livelihoods, and legends.”

- In the third paragraph, the dam is described as a structure that “tames Mahanadi”.

#### Step 2: Evaluation of Options:

- (A) Western river: Western is used as a directional adjective for Odisha (“In western Odisha”), not the name of a river.

- (B) Hirakund river: Hirakud is a dam and reservoir built on the Mahanadi River, not a separate river itself.

- (C) Woodland river: “Woodland” refers to the stretch of forest, not a river.

- (D) Mahanadi river: Accurately and explicitly named as the river running through the city of Sambalpur.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The river running through Sambalpur is the “Mahanadi river”, matching Option (D).

Quick Tip: Use keyword scanning: Locate the keyword “Sambalpur” in paragraph 2 to find the exact name of the river: Mahanadi River.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.921Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5463, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q44', 38, 38, 'Identify the meaning of the underlined word in the line, ''this is where the famed sambalpuri  $ \underline{ikat} $ is born.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Gallery"},{"id":"B","text":"Handwoven fabric"},{"id":"C","text":"mass produced fabric"},{"id":"D","text":"Heritage"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Concept:

Contextual vocabulary and cultural comprehension require deducing the meaning of specialized textile terms from surrounding descriptive modifiers. By examining the contextual clues provided in the sentence and the succeeding descriptions (e.g., “looms click”, “threads gleam”, “each fabric a labor of precision”, “women draped in ikat saris”, “men in handwoven kurtas”), the identity of “ikat” as a traditional handloom textile art form is clearly established.

#### Step 1: Textual Evidence and Descriptive Clues:

- Paragraph 3 describes the weaving culture:

“Back in the narrow lanes of Sambalpur, looms click, and threads gleam. This is where the famed Sambalpuri ikat is born, each fabric a labor of precision and patience.”

- The passage further specifies:

"...women draped in ikat saris, men in handwoven kurtas—each garment carrying stories of skill..."

#### Step 2: Evaluating the Options:

- (A) Gallery: A room or building for the display of art; the passage uses “living gallery” purely as a metaphor for the festival city, not the definition of ikat.

- (B) Handwoven fabric: Correctly captures the identity of ikat as a traditional, handloomwoven, precision textile.

- (C) Mass produced fabric: Directly contradicts the description of ikat as an intricate craft made on traditional clicking looms requiring painstaking patience.

- (D) Heritage: Refers to cultural inheritance in general, not the specific physical fabric itself.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The underlined word “ikat” refers to a “Handwoven fabric”, corresponding to Option (B).

Quick Tip: Context clues such as “looms click”, “threads gleam”, and “labor of precision” clearly point to an artisanal, handwoven fabric.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.921Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5464, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q45', 39, 39, 'The main profession of the people in sambalpur is—', '[{"id":"A","text":"Animal Training"},{"id":"B","text":"Wild life awakening"},{"id":"C","text":"Textile and weaving"},{"id":"D","text":"Engineering the dam"}]', 0, 'C', 'The correct option is (C).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.921Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5465, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q47', 40, 40, 'Which among the following was not a topic of conversation between the narrator and his elder brother?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Buying a new house for the narrator"},{"id":"B","text":"Transforming the house into a museum"},{"id":"C","text":"Transforming the house into a library"},{"id":"D","text":"Making arrangements through a trust"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Concept:

Negative factual identification questions (asking what was not discussed) require verifying every proposed option against the recorded dialogue and narration in the text. Any item that does not appear in the telephonic discussion between the two brothers represents the correct answer.

#### Step 1: Extracting the Conversation Topics from the Passage:

- The elder brother phoned the narrator to narrate the friend''s visit and discussed:

1. Transforming the ancestral house into a museum (explicitly mentioned: "...transform the house into a museum...").

2. Transforming the ancestral house into a library (explicitly mentioned: "...and library...").

3. Making funding arrangements through a trust (explicitly mentioned: "...make an arrangement through a trust...").

4. Demolishing the old structure and building a new house in the same place for the elder brother''s family using their own family earnings.

#### Step 2: Evaluating the Options:

- Option (A): “Buying a new house for the narrator” was never mentioned or discussed anywhere in the text. The narrator was merely a listener on the phone, and the housing discussion concerned only the ancestral home in Rameswaram.

- Options (B), (C), and (D): All represent explicit elements of the friend''s proposal discussed during the call.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The topic that was NOT part of the conversation is Option (A).

Quick Tip: Scan for explicit keywords: “museum”, “library”, and “trust” appear verbatim in the text. “Buying a new house for the narrator” is completely absent.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.921Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5466, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q48', 41, 41, 'The narrator''s friend did not want the house to be demolished because it was', '[{"id":"A","text":"a magnificent building"},{"id":"B","text":"a historical building"},{"id":"C","text":"an architectural marvel"},{"id":"D","text":"a newly constructed building"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept:

Direct factual comprehension questions require matching the exact reasoning stated by a character in the narrative with the multiple-choice options, avoiding misleading stylistic or exaggerative descriptors that do not appear in the text.

#### Step 1: Locating Textual Evidence in the Passage:

- In the passage, the elder brother explains the age of the house:

“The house was built by our father, more than a century ago.”

- When told that grandchildren wanted to demolish it, the friend''s immediate rationale is stated verbatim:

"My friend said he would not want to see such a historical place demolished."

#### Step 2: Evaluating the Options:

- (A) a magnificent building: Subjective descriptor not used by the friend.

- (B) a historical building: Directly and precisely matches the friend''s exact words: “such a historical place”.

- (C) an architectural marvel: Used earlier in the paper for Hirakud dam, not for the brother''s ancestral house.

- (D) a newly constructed building: Contradicts the text, as the house was built more than a hundred years ago.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The friend opposed demolition because it was “a historical building”, matching Option (B).

#### Quick Tip: Always look for the literal phrase in the text:

“would not want to see such a historical place demolished” → historical building.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.921Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5467, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q49', 42, 42, 'Choose the best option to explain the given phrase:

##### It struck me', '[{"id":"A","text":"To hit as a kick"},{"id":"B","text":"To attack suddenly"},{"id":"C","text":"To come to mind suddenly"},{"id":"D","text":"To take down or dismantle"}]', 0, 'C', NULL, NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.921Z', NULL, NULL, 'none', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5468, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-1', 'q50', 43, 43, 'Identify the meaning of the phrase ‘kith and kin’', '[{"id":"A","text":"Friends"},{"id":"B","text":"Relatives"},{"id":"C","text":"Parents"},{"id":"D","text":"Students"}]', 0, 'B', NULL, NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.921Z', NULL, NULL, 'none', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5469, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Use the correct form of the word "RESOLVE" to fill in the blank: Did you make any New Year''s ___ this year?', '[{"id":"A","text":"resolve"},{"id":"B","text":"resolving"},{"id":"C","text":"resolvation"},{"id":"D","text":"resolution"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests the ability to use the correct "part of speech." While "resolve" is a verb, the sentence requires a noun to follow the adjective "New Year''s."

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Resolve (Verb): To settle or find a solution.

• Resolution (Noun): A firm decision to do or not to do something.

• Resolvation: This is not a standard English word.

In the context of New Year''s goals, the standard term is "New Year''s resolution."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct form is "resolution."

Quick Tip: To distinguish between the verb and noun: "I resolve (verb) to exercise more." "My resolution (noun) is to exercise more."', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.922Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5470, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Choose the appropriate word for the meaning of the underlined word: Ravi came up on the stage and started speaking impromptu.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Quickly"},{"id":"B","text":"Unrehearsed"},{"id":"C","text":"Incoherently"},{"id":"D","text":"Impressively"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The question asks for the definition of the word "impromptu" within the context of public speaking.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The word "impromptu" describes an act (like a speech or musical performance) done without being planned, organized, or practiced beforehand.

• Unrehearsed: This is a perfect synonym.

- Incoherently: This means confused or unclear; while an impromptu speech can be incoherent, the word itself doesn''t mean that.

• Impressively: This refers to the quality of the result, not the lack of preparation.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The meaning of impromptu is "unrehearsed."

Quick Tip: "Impromptu" comes from the Latin phrase in promptu, meaning "in readiness." It implies that the speaker was ready to speak even without a script!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.922Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5471, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q03', 3, 3, 'Rearrange the following phrases into a meaningful sentence: One common mistake that ___.

(A) thinking that it

(B) would be a waste of time

(C) to work together

(D) many people have made is', '[{"id":"A","text":"(D),(A),(C),(B)"},{"id":"B","text":"(B),(C),(A),(D)"},{"id":"C","text":"(C)，(A)，(B)，(D)"},{"id":"D","text":"(C)，(B)，(D)，(A)"}]', 0, 'A', '##### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Sentence rearrangement requires identifying the subject, the verb, and the logical flow of information.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. The introductory phrase is "One common mistake that..." 2. This needs to be followed by a phrase describing who made the mistake: (D) "many people have made is" 3. Now we need to define the mistake: (A) "thinking that it" 4. This leads to the action within the thought: (C) "to work together" 5. Finally, the conclusion of the thought: (B) "would be a waste of time."

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The complete sentence is: "One common mistake that many people have made is thinking that

it to work together would be a waste of time." (Note: Logically, the correct sequence for a standard sentence would be D-A-C-B).

Step 4: Final Answer:

The correct order is (D), (A), (C), (B).

Quick Tip: When rearranging, look for "hinge" words. "Is" (in D) usually precedes "thinking" (in A), and "it" (in A) needs to refer to something, like the act of "working together" (in C).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.922Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5472, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Fill in the blank with the correct word from the options given below to make a meaningful sentence:- Ten tourists were ___ injured in the accident and a few of them succumbed to their injuries on the way to the hospital.', '[{"id":"A","text":"spurious"},{"id":"B","text":"gravely"},{"id":"C","text":"fatefully"},{"id":"D","text":"vitally"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question requires choosing an adverb that correctly describes the severity of physical injuries in a tragic context.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Gravely: This means "in a serious or solemn manner" or "to a degree that causes great alarm." In medical or accident contexts, "gravely injured" is the standard colocation for life-threatening harm.

• Spuriously: Means falsely or fake (e.g., a spurious claim).

• Fatefully: Suggests something having far-reaching and typically disastrous consequences.

but it describes the event rather than the physical state of the person.

• Vitally: Means essentially or energetically; it doesn''t fit the context of an injury.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct word is "gravely."

Quick Tip: The clue is "succumbed to their injuries." This tells you the injuries were extremely serious. "Gravely" and "Seriously" are the most common words used in news reports for such situations.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.922Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5473, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q05', 5, 5, 'Rearrange the following phrases into a meaningful sentence: The need for a scientific understanding ___.

(A) the Rio Earth Summit, the first of its kind

(B) involving world leaders on environment and development

(C) of natural ecosystems including forests was the main objective of

(D) of phenomena like climate change, biodiversity, and conservation', '[{"id":"A","text":"(C)，(D)，(A)，(B)"},{"id":"B","text":"(B)，(C)，(D)，(A)"},{"id":"C","text":"(D)，(C)，(A)，(B)"},{"id":"D","text":"(D)，(B)，(A)，(C)"}]', 0, 'C', '##### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To solve this, we must link the preposition "of" following "understanding" to its objects, and then find the verb that completes the main clause.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. The subject starts with: "The need for a scientific understanding..." 2. Understanding of what? (D) "...of phenomena like climate change, biodiversity, and conservation" 3. Continuing the object list: (C) "...of natural ecosystems including forests was the main objective of" 4. Objective of what event? (A) "...the Rio Earth Summit, the first of its kind" 5. Describing the

summit: (B) "...involving world leaders on environment and development."

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The full sentence is: "The need for a scientific understanding of phenomena like climate change, biodiversity, and conservation of natural ecosystems including forests was the main objective of the Rio Earth Summit, the first of its kind involving world leaders on environment and development." The correct sequence is (D), (C), (A), (B).

Quick Tip: Always look for prepositions. "Understanding" is almost always followed by "of." Since both (D) and (C) start with "of," check which one flows better from the word "understanding."', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.922Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5474, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q06', 6, 6, 'Fill in the blank with the correct word from the options given below to make a meaningful sentence:- In my haste to stop the boiling milk from overflowing, I accidentally ___ my fingers.', '[{"id":"A","text":"sizzled"},{"id":"B","text":"scorched"},{"id":"C","text":"scalded"},{"id":"D","text":"sunburnt"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

English has specific words for burns caused by different sources (dry heat vs. wet heat vs. radiation).

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Scalded: This is the specific term for a burn caused by hot liquids (like milk, water) or steam.

- Scorched: Usually refers to burning the surface of something (like cloth or skin) with dry heat or a flame, often resulting in discoloration.

• Sizzled: This is the sound of something frying; it is not typically used as a verb for a person getting injured.

• Sunburnt: Specifically refers to a burn from UV radiation (the sun).

Step 3: Final Answer:

Since the injury was caused by boiling milk, the correct word is "scaledd."

Quick Tip: Remember: Wet = Scald. Dry = Scorch or Burn. If it involves tea, soup, or steam, you''ve been scalded!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.922Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5475, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q07', 7, 7, 'Fill in the blank with the correct option: - The two friends appear to have fallen _____ over a minor issue.', '[{"id":"A","text":"down"},{"id":"B","text":"out"},{"id":"C","text":"into"},{"id":"D","text":"by"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests your knowledge of phrasal verbs. A phrasal verb is a verb combined with a preposition or adverb that creates a meaning different from the original verb.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The verb "fall" changes meaning significantly based on the preposition used:

• Fall out: To have an argument or a disagreement that leads to a break in a friendship.

• Fall down: To drop to the ground.

Fall into: To become involved in something accidentally or to start doing something without intending to.

Fall by: Usually used in the phrase "fall by the wayside," meaning to fail or be forgotten.

Since the context involves friends and a "minor issue," "fall out" is the only logical choice.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct phrasal verb is "fallen out."

Quick Tip: To remember "fall out," imagine two friends standing inside a circle of friendship. When they argue, one "falls out" of the circle!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.923Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5476, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q08', 8, 8, 'Fill in the blank with the correct word from the options given below to make a meaningful sentence: - His salary is in ___ with the amount of work he does.', '[{"id":"A","text":"accord"},{"id":"B","text":"coordination"},{"id":"C","text":"balance"},{"id":"D","text":"keeping"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question focuses on "collocations"—words that are naturally used together in English to sound correct to a native speaker.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The phrase "in keeping with" is a standard English idiom meaning "in agreement with" or "consistent with."

• In keeping with: Used to say that something is appropriate for a specific situation.

• Accord: Usually used as "in accord with," but "in keeping with" is the much more common phrase when discussing salaries and work expectations.

• Coordination/Balance: While these words deal with equality, they do not fit the idiomatic structure of the sentence.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct phrase is "in keeping with."

Quick Tip: If you are struggling between "accord" and "keeping," look at the context. We use "keeping" when something matches a standard or a level (like a salary matching effort).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.923Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5477, 'english-2026-05-11-shift-2', 'q09', 9, 9, 'Fill in the blank with the correct word from the options given below to make a meaningful sentence:- Sara was so ___ that she did not take much time to make up her mind.', '[{"id":"A","text":"impervious"},{"id":"B","text":"impetuous"},{"id":"C","text":"inarticulate"},{"id":"D","text":"inerrant"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests vocabulary related to personality traits, specifically those involving decision-making.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The sentence mentions that Sara "did not take much time to make up her mind," which suggests a quick, perhaps impulsive, nature.

- Impetuous: Acting or done quickly and without thought or care. This perfectly matches the context.

• Impervious: Unable to be affected by something (e.g., impervious to rain).

• Inarticulate: Unable to express oneself clearly.

• Inerrant: Incapable of being wrong or making mistakes.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct word is "impetuous."

Quick Tip: Think of "Impetuous" as "Impulsive." Both start with "Imp-" and describe someone who jumps into action without thinking it through!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.923Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5478, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Choose the correct meaning of the word “Taciturn”.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Very talkative"},{"id":"B","text":"Reserved or silent"},{"id":"C","text":"Extremely angry"},{"id":"D","text":"Careless in behaviour"}]', 0, 'B', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This is a vocabulary question focused on character traits. Understanding root words can often help identify the meaning of such terms.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The word "Taciturn" comes from the Latin word tacitus, meaning "silent."

• A taciturn person is someone who says very little and seems unsociable or reserved.

• It is the opposite of "Loquacious" or "Talkative" (Option A).

• It does not necessarily imply anger or carelessness; it simply refers to a lack of speech.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct meaning is "Reserved or silent."

Quick Tip: Associate Taciturn with the word Tacit (implied without being spoken). Both relate to a lack of overt verbal communication!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.923Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5479, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'The word "Sporadic" means: ___.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Continuous"},{"id":"B","text":"Regular"},{"id":"C","text":"Occurring at irregular intervals"},{"id":"D","text":"Permanent ___"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This word is used to describe the frequency or pattern of an occurrence.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

"Sporadic" refers to things that happen only occasionally or in a scattered, random manner.

• If rain is sporadic, it stops and starts without a predictable pattern.

• It is the antonym of words like "Continuous" (Option A) and "Regular" (Option B).

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct meaning is "Occurring at irregular intervals."

Quick Tip: Think of the word "Spore." Just as spores are scattered randomly by the wind, sporadic events are scattered randomly in time.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.923Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5480, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-1', 'q04', 3, 3, 'Rearrange the following sentences to form a meaningful paragraph:

A. Finally, the results were announced.

B. The students entered the examination hall.

C. They started writing their answers carefully.

D. The invigilator distributed the question papers.', '[{"id":"A","text":"BADC"},{"id":"B","text":"BDCA"},{"id":"C","text":"BDAC"},{"id":"D","text":"BACD"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Rearranging sentences (Parajumbles) requires finding a logical timeline or a cause-and-effect relationship between the statements.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. The process begins with the students arriving: (B) "The students entered the examination hall." 2. Once they are inside, the next logical step is getting the material: (D) "The invigilator distributed the question papers." 3. After receiving the papers, the students perform the task: (C) "They started writing their answers carefully." 4. The word "Finally" indicates the conclusion of the entire process: (A) "Finally, the results were announced."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The logical sequence is BDCA.

Quick Tip: Keywords like "Finally," "In conclusion," or "Lastly" are strong indicators of the final sentence in a sequence.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.923Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5481, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-1', 'q05', 4, 4, 'Rearrange the following words to make a meaningful sentence:

A. always

B. honesty

C. the best policy

D. is', '[{"id":"A","text":"BADC"},{"id":"B","text":"BDAC"},{"id":"C","text":"BACD"},{"id":"D","text":"BCDA"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests the ability to reconstruct a well-known English proverb by identifying the Subject, Verb, and Complement.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. The Subject of the sentence is (B) "Honesty." 2. This is followed by the Verb: (D) "is." 3. To emphasize the frequency or nature, the adverb is placed next: (A) "always." 4. The sentence concludes with the Complement: (C) "the best policy."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The sentence is "Honesty is always the best policy," which corresponds to BDAC.

Quick Tip: In simple English sentences, the structure is usually S-V-O (Subject + Verb + Object/Complement). Identify the "actor" (Honesty) and the "action" (is) first!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.923Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5482, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-1', 'q06', 5, 5, 'Rearrange the following sentences correctly:

A. The rain stopped after an hour.

B. Dark clouds gathered in the sky.

C. Children started playing outside again.

D. Soon, it began to rain heavily.', '[{"id":"A","text":"BADC"},{"id":"B","text":"BDAC"},{"id":"C","text":"BACD"},{"id":"D","text":"DCBA"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

A meaningful paragraph usually follows a chronological order of events—in this case, the cycle of a rainstorm.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. First, the weather changes: (B) "Dark clouds gathered in the sky." 2. The gathering clouds lead to the action: (D) "Soon, it began to rain heavily." 3. Next, the event concludes: (A) "The rain stopped after an hour." 4. Finally, the aftermath: (C) "Children started playing outside again." (The word "again" implies they had stopped during the rain).

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct chronological order is BDAC.

Quick Tip: Look for transition words. "Soon" (in D) usually follows an initial observation (the clouds in B), and "again" (in C) implies a return to a previous state after an interruption (the rain).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.924Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5483, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-1', 'q07', 6, 6, 'Identify the poetic device used in the line: “The wind whispered through the trees.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"Simile"},{"id":"B","text":"Metaphor"},{"id":"C","text":"Personification"},{"id":"D","text":"Hyperbole"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Poetic devices (or figures of speech) are tools used by writers to create rhythm, enhance meaning, or help the reader visualize a scene.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Personification is a figure of speech in which human qualities are given to animals, objects, or ideas.

• In the line "The wind whispered," the act of "whispering" is a human action.

By attributing this human behavior to the wind (a non-human element), the author uses personification to make the description more vivid and evocative.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The poetic device used is Personification.

Quick Tip: To spot personification, ask yourself: "Is an object doing something only a human can do?" If the answer is yes (like a dancing flower or a weeping willow), it''s personification!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.924Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5484, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-1', 'q08', 7, 7, 'Which poetic device is used in the phrase: “Time is a thief.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"Alliteration"},{"id":"B","text":"Metaphor"},{"id":"C","text":"Oxymoron"},{"id":"D","text":"Irony"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Comparison devices like similes and metaphors are used to link two unrelated things to highlight a shared characteristic.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

A Metaphor is a direct comparison between two unlike things without using the words "like" or "as."

• In "Time is a thief," Time is being directly equated to a thief to suggest that it "steals" moments of our lives away.

• If the line were "Time is like a thief," it would be a simile.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The phrase uses a Metaphor.

Quick Tip: Metaphor = A is B (Direct).

Simile = A is like B (Indirect).

Think of a metaphor as a "stronger" comparison because it says one thing is the other.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.924Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5485, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-1', 'q09', 8, 8, 'Identify the poetic device in the line: “She sells seashells on the seashore.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"Assonance"},{"id":"B","text":"Simile"},{"id":"C","text":"Alliteration"},{"id":"D","text":"Repetition"}]', 0, 'C', 'The correct option is (C).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.924Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5486, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-1', 'q10', 9, 9, 'Match the following phobias correctly:

A. Xenophobia — P. Fear of strangers/foreigners

B. Gerascophobia — Q. Fear of growing old

C. Necrophobia — R. Fear of dead bodies/death', '[{"id":"A","text":"A-P, B-Q, C-R"},{"id":"B","text":"A-Q, B-P, C-R"},{"id":"C","text":"A-R, B-Q, C-P"},{"id":"D","text":"A-P, B-R, C-Q"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Phobias are intense, irrational fears of specific objects or situations. Many phobia names are derived from Greek or Latin roots that describe the source of the fear.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

By breaking down the root words, we can identify the meanings:

• Xenophobia: Derived from xenos (stranger/guest). It refers to the fear or hatred of that which is perceived to be foreign or strange. → Matches P.

• Gerascophobia: Derived from geras (old age). It is a clinical phobia involving an abnormal or persistent fear of growing old. → Matches Q.

• Necrophobia: Derived from nekros (dead body). It is the fear of death or dead things.

→ Matches R.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct matching sequence is A-P, B-Q, and C-R.

Quick Tip: To remember Necro, think of "Necropolis" (city of the dead). For Xeno, think of "Xenon" (a gas named for being ''strange''). For Geras, think of "Geriatrics" (the branch of medicine dealing with the elderly).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.924Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5487, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q03', 1, 1, 'He is very fond  $ \underline{of} $ music. The underlined word is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Conjunction"},{"id":"B","text":"Preposition"},{"id":"C","text":"Adverb"},{"id":"D","text":"Interjection"}]', 0, 'B', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

A "Preposition" is a word used to link nouns, pronouns, or phrases to other words within a sentence. They typically show relationships of time, place, or direction.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The word "of" is used here to connect the adjective "fond" with its object "music."

Prepositions act as connectors that show the relationship between the subject and the object.

• "Fond" is a specific adjective that is almost always followed by the fixed preposition "of."

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The underlined word is a Preposition.

Quick Tip: Common prepositions include: in, on, at, by, with, and of. They are usually short words that "position" the relationship between different parts of the sentence.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.924Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5488, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q04', 2, 2, '$ \underline{\text{Wow}} $! That was an amazing performance. The underlined word is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Interjection"},{"id":"B","text":"Adjective"},{"id":"C","text":"Adverb"},{"id":"D","text":"Conjunction"}]', 0, 'A', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

An "Interjection" is a word or phrase used to express strong feeling or sudden emotion. They are grammatically independent from the rest of the sentence.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The word "Wow" is used to express excitement or surprise.

• It is often followed by an exclamation mark (!).

• It does not modify a noun (like an adjective) or a verb (like an adverb); it stands alone as an emotional outburst.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The underlined word is an Interjection.

Quick Tip: Interjections are the "emotional labels" of a sentence. Examples include: Ouch!, Oh!, Alas!, and Hurray!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.925Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5489, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q05', 3, 3, 'She is  $ \underline{\text{smarter}} $ than her brother. The underlined word is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Adverb"},{"id":"B","text":"Noun"},{"id":"C","text":"Adjective"},{"id":"D","text":"Verb"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

An "Adjective" is a word that describes or modifies a noun or pronoun. Adjectives can have different degrees: positive, comparative, and superlative.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

In this sentence, "smarter" is used to describe the pronoun "She."

• Specifically, it is the comparative degree of the adjective "smart."

• It is used here to compare two people (the sister and the brother).

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The underlined word is an Adjective.

Quick Tip: If a word ends in "-er" and is used with "than" to compare two things, it is almost always a comparative adjective.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.925Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5490, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q06', 4, 4, 'By the time we reached the station, the train ___.', '[{"id":"A","text":"left"},{"id":"B","text":"has left"},{"id":"C","text":"had left"},{"id":"D","text":"leaves"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests the use of the Past Perfect Tense. We use this tense to show that one action in the past happened before another action in the past.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

There are two past actions here: 1. We reached the station. 2. The train left. The phrase "By the time" indicates that the train''s departure happened first. To describe the earlier of two past actions, we use "had + past participle" (had left).

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct form is "had left."

Quick Tip: Think of the Past Perfect as the "Past of the Past." If you are already talking about the past (reached), and something happened even earlier, use "had."', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.925Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5491, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q07', 5, 5, 'She ___ in Delhi since 2015.', '[{"id":"A","text":"lives"},{"id":"B","text":"lived"},{"id":"C","text":"is living"},{"id":"D","text":"has been living"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question focuses on the Present Perfect Continuous Tense. This tense is used to describe an action that started in the past and is still continuing in the present.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The keyword in this sentence is "since 2015."

• Whenever we see "since" or "for" followed by a time duration, it usually indicates that the action has been ongoing.

• The structure for this tense is: Subject + has/have + been + Verb(ing).

• "Is living" (Option C) describes what is happening right now but doesn''t account for the long duration starting in 2015.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct phrase is "has been living."

Quick Tip: "Since" refers to a specific point in time (since 2015), while "for" refers to a duration of time (for 11 years). Both usually trigger the Present Perfect Continuous tense!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.925Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5492, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q08', 6, 6, 'If I ___ you, I would apologize.', '[{"id":"A","text":"am"},{"id":"B","text":"was"},{"id":"C","text":"were"},{"id":"D","text":"had been"}]', 0, 'C', 'This is an example of a Second Conditional sentence, which deals with hypothetical or imaginary situations that are contrary to current facts.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

In formal English, when using the "if" clause to express a hypothetical situation (especially with the verb "to be"), we use "were" regardless of the subject (I, he, she, it).

This is known as the Subjunctive Mood.

Even though we usually say "I was," in a hypothetical "If" statement, "If I were" is the grammatically correct choice.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct word is "were."

Quick Tip: Look for the word "would" in the second part of the sentence. The combination of "If... were" and "would" is the classic structure for imaginary advice!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.925Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5493, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q09', 7, 7, 'Look! The children _____ in the rain.', '[{"id":"A","text":"play"},{"id":"B","text":"played"},{"id":"C","text":"are playing"},{"id":"D","text":"have played"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests the Present Continuous Tense, used for actions happening exactly at the time of speaking.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The word "Look!" is a command that draws attention to an action happening right now.

• For ongoing actions in the present, we use the is/am/are + Verb(ing) structure.

• Since "children" is a plural noun, we use "are playing."

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct tense is "are playing."

Quick Tip: Exclamations like "Look!", "Listen!", or "Be quiet!" are almost always clues that the next verb should be in the Present Continuous tense.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.925Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5494, 'english-2026-05-12-shift-2', 'q10', 8, 8, 'Rearrange the following parts of a sentence to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) can be seen as an extension of citizenship, starting with

(B) the Factory Acts of the 1840s which protected

(C) the growing importance of social rights for children

(D) children from unrestricted exploitation at work', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A),(B),(C),(D)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A), (C), (B), (D)"},{"id":"C","text":"(B), (A), (D), (C)"},{"id":"D","text":"(C),(A),(B),(D)"}]', 0, 'D', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Sentence rearrangement requires identifying the subject (who/what the sentence is about) and then following the logical flow of the argument.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. We need a subject to start. Part (C) "the growing importance of social rights for children" introduces the main topic. 2. Next, we need a verb or an explanation of what that importance does. Part (A) "can be seen as an extension of citizenship, starting with" follows (C) perfectly. 3. Part (A) ends with "starting with," so we need a specific example. Part (B) "the Factory Acts of the 1840s which protected" provides that example. 4. Part (B) ends with "protected," which requires an object. Part (D) "children from unrestricted exploitation at work" completes the thought.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct sequence is (C)  $ \rightarrow $ (A)  $ \rightarrow $ (B)  $ \rightarrow $ (D).

Quick Tip: Check the links between parts: "starting with" (in A) naturally leads to a specific historical event (the Factory Acts in B). Identifying these "hooks" makes rearrangement much faster!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.925Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5495, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'A person who eats too much is a/an ___.', '[{"id":"A","text":"glutton"},{"id":"B","text":"epicure"},{"id":"C","text":"gourmand"},{"id":"D","text":"pagan"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This is a "One Word Substitution" question, which tests your vocabulary regarding specific human behaviors and personality traits.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Glutton: A person who eats or drinks excessively (often implying greed).

• Epicure: A person who takes particular pleasure in fine food and drink (a connoisseur).

• Gourmand: Someone who enjoys eating and eats large amounts, but usually with a

more positive connotation of appreciation than a glutton.

Pagan: A person holding religious beliefs other than those of the main world religions.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct term for eating too much is a glutton.

Quick Tip: To remember the difference: An **Epicure** cares about *quality*, while a **Glutton** cares only about *quantity*.

(A) catapulted', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.926Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5496, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'The unprecedented display of remarkable innovation and creativity ___ him into an enviable position.', '[{"id":"A","text":"catapulted"},{"id":"B","text":"capsized"},{"id":"C","text":"condemned"},{"id":"D","text":"commemorated"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This "Fill in the Blank" question tests your ability to choose a verb that logically fits the context of "sudden success" or "rapid movement" to a higher status.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Catapulted: To hurl or launch something (metaphorically, to move someone suddenly into a new situation or status).

• Capsized: To overturn in the water (used for boats).

• Condemned: To express complete disapproval of; typically in a legal or moral sense.

• Commemorated: To recall and show respect for (someone or something) in a ceremony.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

"Catapulted" is the only word that fits the positive, high-energy movement described in the sentence.

Quick Tip: Think of a physical **catapult**—it launches things forward with great force. In business or career contexts, "catapulted" is a common way to describe a "big break" or rapid promotion.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.926Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5497, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-1', 'q03', 3, 3, 'Identify the word closest in meaning to the underlined word: What are you trying to  $ \underline{\text{insinuate}} $ through this message?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Express"},{"id":"B","text":"Affirm"},{"id":"C","text":"Allude"},{"id":"D","text":"Propagate"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This is a synonym-based question. "Insinuate" is a verb often used to describe indirect communication.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Insinuate: To suggest or hint at something bad or unpleasant in an indirect and unpleasant way.

• Express: To state something directly and clearly.

• Affirm: To state as a fact; assert strongly and publicly.

• Allude: To suggest or call attention to something indirectly; to hint at.

• Propagate: To spread and promote (an idea, theory, etc.) widely.

Step 3: Final Answer:

"Allude" is the closest synonym as both words involve indirect suggestion rather than direct statement.

Quick Tip: An "insinuation" is usually a "sneaky" hint. If someone says "I''m not saying you''re late, but the meeting started an hour ago," they are **insinuating** that you are late.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.926Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5498, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-1', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Rearrange the given parts to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) while their white coats provide camouflage from

(B) predators and help them silently approach prey

(C) stay warm with thick fur, insulated feet, and fluffy tails.

(D) arctic foxes, adapted to the harsh Arctic winter,', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A),(D),(C),(B)"},{"id":"B","text":"(D),(A),(C),(B)"},{"id":"C","text":"(D), (C), (A), (B)"},{"id":"D","text":"(C)，(B)，(D)，(A)"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To rearrange a sentence, identify the subject first, followed by the actions (verbs) and the results or supporting details.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. **Part (D)*** is the best starting point as it introduces the subject: "arctic foxes, adapted to the harsh Arctic winter," 2. **Part (C)*** follows logically by describing how they adapt: "stay warm with thick fur, insulated feet, and fluffy tails," 3. **Part (A)*** introduces an additional feature using a conjunction: "while their white coats provide camouflage from", 4. **Part (B)*** completes the thought by explaining what they are camouflaged from and why: "predators and help them silently approach prey".

Step 3: Final Answer:

The logical sequence is (D)  $ \rightarrow $ (C)  $ \rightarrow $ (A)  $ \rightarrow $ (B).

Quick Tip: Look for commas as clues. The comma at the end of (D) and (C) suggests they are part of a list or introductory clause that must lead into the next thought.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.926Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5499, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-1', 'q05', 5, 5, 'Select the word closest in meaning to the word: Petulant', '[{"id":"A","text":"Placid"},{"id":"B","text":"Patient"},{"id":"C","text":"Irritable"},{"id":"D","text":"Insignificant"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This is a vocabulary question focused on synonyms. Understanding the "tone" of a word (positive, negative, or neutral) can help eliminate wrong choices.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Petulant: Definition: (of a person or their manner) childishly sulky or bad-tempered.

• Placid: Calm and peaceful (Antonym).

• Patient: Able to accept delays or trouble without getting angry (Antonym).

• Irritable: Having or showing a tendency to be easily annoyed or made angry (Synonym).

• Insignificant: Too small or unimportant to be worth consideration (Unrelated).

Step 3: Final Answer:

The closest meaning to "Petulant" is "Irritable."

Quick Tip: "Petulant" is often used to describe someone acting like a spoiled child. Think of a "pet" that is "annoyed"—it helps link the sound to the meaning of being irritable.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.926Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5500, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-1', 'q06', 6, 6, 'Once you ___ into the routine, you will find the work is quite easy.', '[{"id":"A","text":"are settled"},{"id":"B","text":"had settled"},{"id":"C","text":"settled"},{"id":"D","text":"will settle"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question deals with conditional time clauses. In English, we do not use the future tense ("will") in a clause starting with "once," "when," or "if" when the main clause already uses the future tense.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The sentence structure is: Once + [Present Tense], [Future Tense].

• The main clause is "you will find..." (Future).

• The "once" clause needs a present form. "Are settled" acts as a present state.

• "Will settle" (Option D) is incorrect because we avoid double "will" in these structures.

- "Settled" (Option C) and "had settled" (Option B) are past tenses and do not fit a future prediction.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct choice is "are settled."

Quick Tip: Remember the "No Future in Time Clauses" rule. Even if you are talking about the future, words like *if, when, before, after, as soon as,* and *once* are followed by the **Present Tense**', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.926Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5501, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-1', 'q07', 7, 7, 'Rearrange the following parts to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) surviving written literature from ancient Mesopotamia because of

(B) through the ages, including the Iliad and the Odyssey

(C) the Epic of Gilgamesh is often cited as the earliest

(D) its significantly enduring impact on ancient literary works', '[{"id":"A","text":"(C),(A),(D),(B)"},{"id":"B","text":"(B)，(D)，(A)，(C)"},{"id":"C","text":"(C)，(A)，(B)，(D)"},{"id":"D","text":"(B)，(C)，(A)，(D)"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Sentence rearrangement requires linking modifiers to the nouns they describe and identifying the logical cause-and-effect or chronological flow.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. **Part (C)** acts as the subject and main claim: "the Epic of Gilgamesh is often cited as the earliest". 2. **Part (A)** completes the noun phrase started in (C): "surviving written literature from ancient Mesopotamia because of". 3. **Part (D)** explains the reason behind the fame/citation: "its significantly enduring impact on ancient literary works". 4. **Part (B)** provides specific examples of those "literary works": through the ages, including the Iliad and the Odyssey".

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct sequence is (C)  $ \rightarrow $ (A)  $ \rightarrow $ (D)  $ \rightarrow $ (B).

Quick Tip: Look for the link between (D) and (B). Part (D) ends with "literary works," and Part (B) starts with examples of famous literary works (Iliad and Odyssey). This pair (D-B) must stay together.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.926Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5502, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-1', 'q08', 8, 8, 'From the given options, choose the word that best expresses the meaning of the given word: Grisly', '[{"id":"A","text":"gloomy"},{"id":"B","text":"greasy"},{"id":"C","text":"disgusting"},{"id":"D","text":"boorish"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This is a vocabulary question testing your knowledge of synonyms. "Grisly" is a descriptive adjective often used in dark or morbid contexts.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Grisly: Causing horror or disgust; typically involving details of death or injury.

• Gloomy: Dark or poorly lit; feeling distressed or pessimistic.

• Greasy: Covered with or resembling an oily substance.

• Disgusting: Arousing revulsion or strong indignation (The closest match for horror/revulsion).

• Boorish: Rough and bad-mannered; coarse.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The best synonym for "Grisly" from the given options is "disgusting."

Quick Tip: Don''t confuse "Grisly" with "Grizzly" (the bear) or "Grisly" with "Gristly" (tough meat). "Grisly" usually describes a crime scene or a horror movie plot!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.927Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5503, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-1', 'q09', 9, 9, 'Choose the correct word from the options given below to substitute the highlighted words: A careful ''preservation and protection'' of the flora and fauna is the need of the hour.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Conservation"},{"id":"B","text":"Promotion"},{"id":"C","text":"Enhancement"},{"id":"D","text":"Management"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This is a "One Word Substitution" task where you replace a phrase with a more precise technical or formal term.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Conservation: The specific term for the planned management of natural resources to prevent exploitation, destruction, or neglect (Matches "preservation and protection").

• Promotion: The act of encouraging the progress or existence of something.

• Enhancement: The process of improving the quality, value, or extent of something.

• Management: The process of dealing with or controlling things or people.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The term that specifically means preservation and protection of nature is "Conservation."

Quick Tip: While "Preservation" means keeping something exactly as it is, "Conservation" often implies the wise and sustainable use of resources so they last for the future.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.927Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5504, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-1', 'q10', 10, 10, 'Match the phrasal verbs given in List-I with their correct meanings in List-II:




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-I (Phrasal Verbs)</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II (Meanings)</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) Nod off</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(i) Fall asleep</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) Jack up</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(ii) Raise prices</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C) Look up to</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(iii) Finish something</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) Wrap up</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(iv) To respect</td></tr></table>', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(i), (B)-(ii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(iii)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(iii), (B)-(i), (C)-(iv), (D)-(ii)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(ii), (B)-(i), (C)-(iv), (D)-(iii)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(iii), (B)-(iv), (C)-(ii), (D)-(i)"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Phrasal verbs consist of a verb and a particle (preposition or adverb) that together create a meaning different from the original verb.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s match each phrasal verb with its definition:

(A) Nod off: This means to begin sleeping, especially while sitting up or in a situation where you shouldn''t (e.g., in a meeting). Matches with (i).

(B) Jack up: This is an informal way to say someone has suddenly increased something, typically prices or interest rates. Matches with (ii).

• (C) Look up to: This means to admire or respect someone greatly. Matches with (iv).

• (D) Wrap up: This means to complete or finish a task, meeting, or project. Matches with (iii).

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct matching sequence is (A)-(i), (B)-(ii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(iii).

Quick Tip: To remember "Jack up," think of a car **jack**—it raises the car higher. Similarly, "jacking up" prices raises them higher!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.927Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5505, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-2', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Select the word closest in meaning to the word: Postulate', '[{"id":"A","text":"Highlight"},{"id":"B","text":"Disprove"},{"id":"C","text":"Refute"},{"id":"D","text":"Hypothesize"}]', 0, 'D', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To "postulate" is to suggest or assume the existence, fact, or truth of something as a basis for reasoning, discussion, or belief. It is common in scientific and mathematical contexts.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Hypothesize: To put forward an idea or theory as a starting point for further investigation.

This is the closest synonym.

• Highlight: To draw special attention to something.

- Disprove / Refute: These are antonyms; they mean to prove that a statement or theory is wrong or false.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The word closest in meaning to "Postulate" is "Hypothesize."

Quick Tip: In geometry, a postulate is an axiom—something we accept as true without proof so that we can build other theorems on top of it!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.927Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5506, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-2', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Match the words given in List-I with the appropriate meanings given in List-II:




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-I (Words)</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II (Meanings)</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) Centrifugal</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(i) Anything tending to move away from the centre</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) Effeminate</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(ii) Words inscribed on the tomb</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C) Ambidextrous</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(iii) Womanish in habits</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) Epitaph</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(iv) One who can use either hand without any problem</td></tr></table>', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(i), (B)-(iv), (C)-(iii), (D)-(ii)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(iv), (B)-(ii), (C)-(iii), (D)-(i)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(i), (B)-(iii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(ii)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(iii), (B)-(ii), (C)-(i), (D)-(iv)"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This matching exercise tests "One Word Substitution," where complex descriptions are represented by a single specialized term.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

(A) Centrifugal: Derived from Latin centrum (center) and fugere (to flee). It describes moving away from the center. Matches (i).

• (B) Effeminate: Used to describe a man who has characteristics or habits traditionally associated with women. Matches (iii).

• (C) Ambidextrous: Derived from ambi (both) and dexter (right/skillful). It means being equally skillful with both hands. Matches (iv).

• (D) Epitaph: A short text honoring a deceased person, strictly those inscribed on a tombstone. Matches (ii).

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct match is (A)-(i), (B)-(iii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(ii).

Quick Tip: To remember "Centrifugal," think of a "Centrifuge" machine that spins things so fast they fly toward the edges (away from the center)!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.927Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5507, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-2', 'q03', 3, 3, 'Select the word or phrase that best completes the following sentence: Desert flowers can be invisible for years ___ appear suddenly after heavy rain.', '[{"id":"A","text":"alternatively or"},{"id":"B","text":"consequently and"},{"id":"C","text":"nevertheless, so"},{"id":"D","text":"actually, but"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests the use of conjunctions and adverbs to show contrast between two states: being "invisible" and "appearing suddenly."

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The sentence describes a surprising contrast.

• "Actually" emphasizes the reality of their invisibility.

"But" is the coordinating conjunction used to introduce a statement that contrasts with what has already been mentioned.

• Options (A), (B), and (C) do not provide the logical "contrast" required to link "invisible"

with "appear."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The sentence should read: "...can be invisible for years, actually, but appear suddenly..."

Quick Tip: When you see a sentence where the second half is the "opposite" of the first half (Invisible vs. Appear), always look for contrast markers like but, however, yet, or nevertheless.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.928Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5508, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-2', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Identify the word that is closest in meaning to the word: Relegate', '[{"id":"A","text":"Elevate"},{"id":"B","text":"Demote"},{"id":"C","text":"Acknowledge"},{"id":"D","text":"Celebrate"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To "relegate" someone or something means to assign them to a lower or less important position, rank, or condition.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Demote: To move someone to a lower rank or less senior position. This is the closest synonym.

• Elevate: To raise to a higher position (Antonym).

• Acknowledge: To accept or admit the existence or truth of something.

• Celebrate: To acknowledge a significant happy day or event with a social gathering or enjoyable activity.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The word closest in meaning to "Relegate" is "Demote."

Quick Tip: In sports, especially European football, if a team performs poorly, they are relegated to a lower league. If they do well, they are promoted.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.928Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5509, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-2', 'q05', 5, 5, 'Rearrange the following parts to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) into simpler molecules that can be absorbed into the bloodstream

(B) digestion is an intricate process that begins in the mouth

(C) where food is broken down mechanically and chemically

(D) and continues in the stomach and intestines', '[{"id":"A","text":"(B),(C),(A),(D)"},{"id":"B","text":"(B)，(D)，(C)，(A)"},{"id":"C","text":"(B),(A),(C),(D)"},{"id":"D","text":"(C)，(B)，(A)，(D)"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

When rearranging a descriptive sentence, follow the logical or chronological sequence of the process being described.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Part (B) introduces the subject and the starting point: "digestion is an intricate process that begins in the mouth". 2. Part (D) follows the timeline of the process: "and continues in the stomach and intestines". 3. Part (C) explains what happens during those stages: "where food is broken down mechanically and chemically". 4. Part (A) provides the final result/purpose of that breakdown: "into simpler molecules that can be absorbed into the bloodstream".

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct order is (B)  $ \rightarrow $ (D)  $ \rightarrow $ (C)  $ \rightarrow $ (A).

Quick Tip: Always look for "and" (Part D) to see what it connects. Here, it connects "begins in the mouth" with "continues in the stomach."', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.929Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5510, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-2', 'q06', 6, 6, 'Select the word opposite in meaning to the word: Stoic', '[{"id":"A","text":"Serene"},{"id":"B","text":"Complaining"},{"id":"C","text":"Resigned"},{"id":"D","text":"stolid"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

A "Stoic" person can endure pain or hardship without showing their feelings or complaining. To find the opposite, we look for someone who expresses their distress or dissatisfaction.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Serene: Calm, peaceful, and untroubled (Similar in vibe to stoic).

• Complaining: Expressing dissatisfaction or annoyance about something (Direct antonym).

• Resigned: Accepting that something undesirable cannot be avoided (Similar to stoic).

• Stolid: Calm, dependable, and showing little emotion or animation (Synonym).

Step 3: Final Answer:

The opposite of "Stoic" is "Complaining."

Quick Tip: A Stoic suffers in silence. A Complainer makes sure everyone knows they are suffering!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.930Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5511, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-2', 'q07', 7, 7, 'Select the word opposite in meaning to the word: Presumptuous', '[{"id":"A","text":"Pretentious"},{"id":"B","text":"Brash"},{"id":"C","text":"Bountiful"},{"id":"D","text":"Unassuming"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

A "presumptuous" person fails to observe the limits of what is permitted or appropriate. They are often overconfident or arrogant in their behavior.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Unassuming: Not pretentious or arrogant; modest. This is the direct opposite of being presumptuous.

• Pretentious / Brash: These are synonyms or near-synonyms for presumptuous, involving arrogance or overconfidence.

• Bountiful: Large in quantity; abundant.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The opposite of "Presumptuous" is "Unassuming."

Quick Tip: To remember "Presumptuous," think of someone who presumes they have more authority or friendship than they actually do. They overstep!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.930Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5512, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-2', 'q08', 8, 8, 'Fill in the blank with the most appropriate option in the following sentence: Though Sharon complained and cried bitterly, Mom did not ___ on her.', '[{"id":"A","text":"let in"},{"id":"B","text":"let off"},{"id":"C","text":"let out"},{"id":"D","text":"let up"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests phrasal verbs related to intensity and pressure.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Let up: To become less intense or to stop being harsh. In this context, it means Mom did not stop being strict or demanding despite the crying.

• Let in: To allow someone to enter.

• Let off: To allow someone to go without punishment (e.g., "let off with a warning"). While similar, "let up on someone" specifically refers to the persistence of pressure or discipline.

• Let out: To release or utter a sound.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct phrase is "let up."

Quick Tip: We often use "let up" for weather: "The rain didn''t let up all day." It means the intensity didn''t decrease.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.930Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5513, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-2', 'q09', 9, 9, 'Select the word opposite in meaning to the word: Resonant', '[{"id":"A","text":"Resounding"},{"id":"B","text":"Cacophonous"},{"id":"C","text":"Smothered"},{"id":"D","text":"Pulsating"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

"Resonant" describes a sound that is deep, clear, and continuing to sound or echo. An opposite

would be a sound that is blocked, muffled, or prevented from echoing.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Smothered: A sound that is muffled, suppressed, or stifled. This is the best antonym.

• Resounding: Deep and loud; very similar to resonant (Synonym).

• Cacophonous: Involving a harsh, discordant mixture of sounds (Refers to quality of sound, not the ability to echo).

Pulsating: Expanding and contracting with regular movements.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The opposite of "Resonant" is "Smothered."

Quick Tip: A "Resonant" voice fills the whole room. A "Smothered" voice sounds like it''s coming from under a pillow!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.930Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5514, 'english-2026-05-13-shift-2', 'q10', 10, 10, 'Re-arrange the following phrases to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) a great buzzard was

(B) and scorching sun.

(C) blob against the blinding

(D) circling high overhead a', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A),(D),(C),(B)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A), (C), (B), (D)"},{"id":"C","text":"(B), (A), (D), (C)"},{"id":"D","text":"(C)，(B)，(D)，(A)"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Identify the subject and its action, then connect the modifiers that describe the setting.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Part (A) starts with the subject: "a great buzzard was". 2. Part (D) provides the verb/action: "circling high overhead a". 3. Part (C) provides the visual description of the bird: "blob against the blinding". 4. Part (B) completes the description of the sky: "and scorching sun."

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The sentence is: "A great buzzard was circling high overhead a blob against the blinding and scorching sun." (The sequence is A-D-C-B).

Quick Tip: "Blinding" and "Scorching" are both adjectives describing the sun, so (C) and (B) must follow each other to complete that phrase.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.931Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5515, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Rearrange the following parts to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) into simpler molecules that can be absorbed into the bloodstream

(B) digestion is an intricate process that begins in the mouth

(C) where food is broken down mechanically and chemically

(D) and continues in the stomach and intestines', '[{"id":"A","text":"(B),(C),(A),(D)"},{"id":"B","text":"(B)，(D)，(C)，(A)"},{"id":"C","text":"(B),(A),(C),(D)"},{"id":"D","text":"(C)，(B)，(A)，(D)"}]', 0, 'B', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To form a logical sentence, we must follow the sequence of the biological process (digestion) and ensure grammatical connectivity between the phrases.

Step 2: Identifying the Sequence:

- Part (B) is the independent clause and the logical start: "digestion is an intricate process that begins in the mouth".

• Part (D) continues the timeline of the process: "and continues in the stomach and intestines".

• Part (C) describes what happens in those locations: "where food is broken down mechanically and chemically".

- Part (A) concludes with the end goal of the process: "into simpler molecules that can be absorbed into the bloodstream".

### Step 3: Final Answer:

The full sentence is: "Digestion is an intricate process that begins in the mouth and continues in the stomach and intestines where food is broken down mechanically and chemically into simpler molecules that can be absorbed into the bloodstream."

Quick Tip: Look for transition markers. "Begins" in part (B) naturally pairs with "continues" in part (D), helping you establish the first half of the sequence immediately.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.932Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5516, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Select the word opposite in meaning to the word: Presumptuous', '[{"id":"A","text":"Pretentious"},{"id":"B","text":"Brash"},{"id":"C","text":"Bountiful"},{"id":"D","text":"Unassuming"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

An antonym is a word that means the opposite of another word. "Presumptuous" refers to someone who oversteps their bounds or is overly bold/arrogant.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Presumptuous: Overconfident, arrogant, or failing to observe the limits of what is appropriate.

• Unassuming: Modest, humble, and not bold or arrogant. This is the direct opposite.

• Pretentious/Brash: These are synonyms or related in meaning to being bold or showy.

• Bountiful: Means large in quantity or generous.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The opposite of "Presumptuous" is "Unassuming."

Quick Tip: The root "presume" means to take for granted. A presumptuous person "presumes" they are welcome or important, whereas an unassuming person stays humble.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.933Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5517, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-1', 'q03', 3, 3, 'Select the word opposite in meaning to the word: Resonant', '[{"id":"A","text":"Resounding"},{"id":"B","text":"Cacophonous"},{"id":"C","text":"Smothered"},{"id":"D","text":"Pulsating"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

"Resonant" describes a sound that is deep, clear, and echoes. To find the opposite, we look for a word that describes a sound that is muffled or prevented from echoing.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Resonant: Producing a deep, clear, reverberating sound.

- Smothered: A sound that is stifled, muffled, or suppressed. This acts as the best antonym in terms of sound quality.

• Resounding: Similar to resonant; loud and echoing.

• Cacophonous: Describes a harsh, unpleasant mixture of sounds.

• Pulsating: Rhythmic throbbing or vibrating.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The opposite of "Resonant" is "Smothered."

Quick Tip: Think of a bell. A "resonant" bell ring continues to vibrate in the air. If you put a cloth over the bell, the sound becomes "smothered."', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.933Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5518, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-1', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Select the most appropriate expression to fill in the blank in the following sentence: Given her fearful and timid nature in unfamiliar situations, we can say that she is ___.', '[{"id":"A","text":"audacious"},{"id":"B","text":"timorous"},{"id":"C","text":"reckless"},{"id":"D","text":"intrepid"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question requires identifying a word that matches the descriptors "fearful" and "timid" provided in the sentence context.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Timorous: Showing or suffering from nervousness or a lack of confidence. It is a direct synonym for timid and fearful.

• Audacious / Intrepid: These are antonyms meaning bold, brave, or fearless.

• Reckless: Heedless of danger or the consequences of one''s actions.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The most appropriate word is "timorous."

Quick Tip: To remember "timorous," notice that it shares the same root as "timid." Both words describe a lack of courage or confidence.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.933Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5519, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-1', 'q05', 5, 5, 'Select the word closest in meaning to the word: Postulate', '[{"id":"A","text":"Highlight"},{"id":"B","text":"Disprove"},{"id":"C","text":"Refute"},{"id":"D","text":"Hypothesize"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To "postulate" is to suggest or assume the existence, fact, or truth of something as a basis for reasoning, discussion, or belief.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Hypothesize: To provide a tentative explanation or an assumption that can be tested. This is the closest in meaning.

• Highlight: To draw attention to something.

• Disprove/Refute: These mean to prove that something is false.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The word closest in meaning to "Postulate" is "Hypothesize."

Quick Tip: In scientific methods, you first postulate an idea, then you test it. It''s the "starting point" of an argument or theory.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.934Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5520, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-1', 'q06', 6, 6, 'Match the words given in List-I with the appropriate meanings given in List-II




<table border=1 style=''margin: auto; word-wrap: break-word;''><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-I (Words)</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>List-II (Meanings)</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(A) Centrifugal</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(i) Anything tending to move away from the centre</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(B) Effeminate</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(ii) Words inscribed on the tomb</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(C) Ambidextrous</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(iii) Womanish in habits</td></tr><tr><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(D) Epitaph</td><td style=''text-align: center; word-wrap: break-word;''>(iv) One who can use either hand without any problem</td></tr></table>', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A)-(i), (B)-(iv), (C)-(iii), (D)-(ii)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A)-(iv), (B)-(ii), (C)-(iii), (D)-(i)"},{"id":"C","text":"(A)-(i), (B)-(iii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(ii)"},{"id":"D","text":"(A)-(iii), (B)-(ii), (C)-(i), (D)-(iv)"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This is a "One Word Substitution" exercise where specific technical or descriptive terms are matched to their definitions.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

(A) Centrifugal: A force or tendency that moves objects away from the axis of rotation. Matches (i).

• (B) Effeminate: Having characteristics regarded as typical of a woman; unmanly. Matches (iii).

• (C) Ambidextrous: Able to use the right and left hands equally well. Matches (iv).

• (D) Epitaph: A phrase or form of words written in memory of a person who has died, especially as an inscription on a tombstone. Matches (ii).

Step 4: Final Answer:

The correct matching sequence is (A)-(i), (B)-(iii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(ii).

Quick Tip: Break down "Ambidextrous": Ambi means "both" and dexter means "skillful." Literally, it means being "skillful with both."', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.934Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5521, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-1', 'q07', 7, 7, 'Select the word or phrase that best completes the following sentence:— Desert flowers can be invisible for years ___ appear suddenly after heavy rain.', '[{"id":"A","text":"alternatively or"},{"id":"B","text":"consequently and"},{"id":"C","text":"nevertheless, so"},{"id":"D","text":"actually, but"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The sentence presents a contrast between flowers being "invisible" and then "appearing suddenly." To connect these two contrasting ideas, we need a contrastive conjunction.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Actually: Used to emphasize the surprising truth of their long invisibility.

• But: A coordinating conjunction used to introduce a statement that contrasts with the previous one.

• Options (A), (B), and (C) do not provide the correct logical transition to show how a long period of absence leads to a sudden appearance.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The completed sentence is: "Desert flowers can be invisible for years actually, but appear suddenly after heavy rain."

Quick Tip: When a sentence describes a shift from one state (invisible) to its opposite (appear), always look for "but," "yet," or "however" in the options!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.934Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5522, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-1', 'q08', 8, 8, 'Identify the word that is closest in meaning to the word: Relegate', '[{"id":"A","text":"Elevate"},{"id":"B","text":"Demote"},{"id":"C","text":"Acknowledge"},{"id":"D","text":"Celebrate"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

"Relegate" means to assign an inferior rank or position to someone or something. It often implies a reduction in importance or status.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Demote: To move someone to a lower rank or less senior position. This is a direct synonym.

• Elevate: To raise to a more important or higher level (Antonym).

• Acknowledge: To accept or admit the existence or truth of.

• Celebrate: To honor or praise publicly.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The word closest in meaning to "Relegate" is "Demote."

Quick Tip: In sports, a team is "relegated" when they are moved down to a lower division because of poor performance.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.934Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5523, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-1', 'q09', 9, 9, 'Re-arrange the following phrases to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) a great buzzard was

(B) and scorching sun.

(C) blob against the blinding

(D) circling high overhead a', '[{"id":"A","text":"(A),(D),(C),(B)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A), (C), (B), (D)"},{"id":"C","text":"(B),(A),(D),(C)"},{"id":"D","text":"(C)，(B)，(D)，(A)"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

A meaningful sentence usually follows the Subject + Verb + Object/Description pattern.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Part (A) introduces the subject: "A great buzzard was". 2. Part (D) adds the action: "circling high overhead a". 3. Part (C) describes how it looked: "blob against the blinding". 4. Part (B) completes the environmental description: "and scorching sun."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct order is (A), (D), (C), (B).

Quick Tip: "Blinding" and "scorching" are coordinate adjectives that both describe the "sun." This tells you that (C) and (B) must stay together in that order.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.934Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5524, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-1', 'q10', 10, 10, 'Rearrange the given parts to form a meaningful sentence:

(A) and strengthens economic self-reliance

(B) manufacturing, creates jobs, encourages

(C) make in India promotes local

(D) innovation, attracts global investments', '[{"id":"A","text":"(D), (C), (B), (A)"},{"id":"B","text":"(A),(D),(C),(B)"},{"id":"C","text":"(B), (A), (D), (C)"},{"id":"D","text":"(C), (B), (D), (A)"}]', 0, 'D', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Identify the main subject/campaign (Make in India) and list the verbs/actions it performs in a logical, comma-separated sequence ending with "and."

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

1. Part (C) is the opening: "Make in India promotes local". 2. Part (B) follows with the objects of promotion: "manufacturing, creates jobs, encourages". 3. Part (D) continues the list: "innovation, attracts global investments". 4. Part (A) uses "and" to conclude the list of benefits: "and strengthens economic self-reliance".

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct order is (C), (B), (D), (A).

Quick Tip: In a long list of actions, the part starting with "and" (Part A) almost always goes at the very end of the sentence!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.934Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5525, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-2', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Choose the SYNONYM of AMELIORATE:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Worsen"},{"id":"B","text":"Diminish"},{"id":"C","text":"Maintain"},{"id":"D","text":"Improve"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

A synonym is a word that has the same or nearly the same meaning as another word.

"Ameliorate" is a formal verb used to describe the act of making something better.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Ameliorate: To make (something bad or unsatisfactory) better.

• Improve: To make or become better. This is the direct synonym.

• Worsen: To make something worse (Antonym).

• Diminish: To make or become less.

• Maintain: To keep something at the same level or state.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The synonym of "Ameliorate" is "Improve."

Quick Tip: Think of "Ameliorate" as a fancy way to say "fix" or "upgrade" a situation. If conditions in a city are bad, the government tries to ameliorate them.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.935Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5526, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-2', 'q02', 2, 2, '''The team ___ playing exceptionally well this season.''', '[{"id":"A","text":"are"},{"id":"B","text":"were"},{"id":"C","text":"is"},{"id":"D","text":"have been"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests Subject-Verb Agreement with collective nouns. A collective noun (like team, family, or jury) takes a singular verb when the group is acting as a single unit.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

In this sentence, "The team" is viewed as a single collective entity performing the action of playing.

• is: Singular present tense, appropriate for a single unit.

- are: Plural present tense, used if the members were acting individually (e.g., "The team are putting on their uniforms").

• were / have been: These do not fit the singular focus of the current season''s performance as well as "is" in this specific context.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct sentence is: "The team is playing exceptionally well this season."

Quick Tip: In American English, collective nouns almost always take a singular verb (is). In British English, they can take plural verbs (are) if the focus is on the individuals.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.935Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5527, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-2', 'q03', 3, 3, '''To burn the midnight oil'' means:', '[{"id":"A","text":"To waste resources"},{"id":"B","text":"To be very angry"},{"id":"C","text":"To set fire to something"},{"id":"D","text":"To work very late into the night"}]', 0, 'D', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

An idiom is a group of words established by usage as having a meaning not deductible from those of the individual words.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The phrase originates from the time before electricity, when people had to use oil lamps to see. If you were "burning the midnight oil," it meant you were staying up past midnight to study or work by the light of a lamp.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The idiom "To burn the midnight oil" means to work or study very late into the night.

Quick Tip: Use this idiom when talking about exam preparation: "I had to burn the midnight oil to finish my project before the deadline."', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.935Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5528, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-2', 'q04', 4, 4, 'The politician''s speech was so ___ that even his opponents were moved.', '[{"id":"A","text":"banal"},{"id":"B","text":"monotonous"},{"id":"C","text":"garbled"},{"id":"D","text":"eloquent"}]', 0, 'D', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The sentence implies that the speech had a powerful, positive effect because it "moved" (emotionally touched) even those who disagreed with the politician. We need a word that describes high-quality, persuasive speaking.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Eloquent: Fluent or persuasive in speaking or writing. This perfectly fits the context of moving an audience.

• Banal: So lacking in originality as to be obvious and boring.

- Monotonous: Dull, tedious, and lacking in variation in tone or pitch.

• Garbled: Reproduced in a confused and distorted way; unclear.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct word is "eloquent."

Quick Tip: To "move" an audience usually requires Pathos (emotional appeal). An eloquent speaker knows exactly how to use words to create that emotional connection.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.936Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5529, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-2', 'q06', 5, 5, 'Identify the error: ''He is knowing the answer but refused to tell us.''', '[{"id":"A","text":"is knowing"},{"id":"B","text":"answer"},{"id":"C","text":"refused"},{"id":"D","text":"tell"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question deals with Stative Verbs. These are verbs that describe a state, condition, or

mental process rather than a physical action.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Verbs like know, believe, understand, and love are generally not used in the continuous (-ing) form. We do not "be knowing" something; we either "know" it or we don''t.

• Incorrect: "He is knowing the answer."

• Correct: "He knows the answer" (Present) or "He knew the answer" (Past).

Since the second half of the sentence is in the past tense ("refused"), the first part should logically be "He knew."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The error is in the phrase "is knowing."

Quick Tip: Stative verbs are like "filters" of the mind or heart. Because they aren''t physical actions you can see someone doing, we rarely use them with -ing.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.936Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5530, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-2', 'q07', 6, 6, 'One who can speak two languages fluently is called a:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Multilingual"},{"id":"B","text":"Monolingual"},{"id":"C","text":"Polyglot"},{"id":"D","text":"Bilingual"}]', 0, 'D', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This is a one-word substitution question based on numerical prefixes in linguistics. The prefix "bi-" refers to the number two.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Bilingual: Capable of speaking two languages fluently.

- Monolingual: Speaking only one language.

• Multilingual / Polyglot: Someone who can speak or use several (usually more than three) languages.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The term for someone speaking two languages fluently is "Bilingual."

Quick Tip: To remember prefixes: Mono = 1, Bi = 2, Tri = 3, Poly/Multi = Many.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.936Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5531, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-2', 'q08', 7, 7, 'Her ___ attitude towards criticism helped her grow professionally.', '[{"id":"A","text":"hostile"},{"id":"B","text":"defensive"},{"id":"C","text":"receptive"},{"id":"D","text":"dismissive"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The sentence states that the attitude "helped her grow." Therefore, we need a positive word that describes someone who is open to feedback and learning.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Receptive: Willing to consider or accept new suggestions and ideas. This leads to growth.

• Hostile: Showing or feeling opposition or dislike; unfriendly.

• Defensive: Very anxious to challenge or avoid criticism.

• Dismissive: Feeling or showing that something is unworthy of consideration.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct word is "receptive."

Quick Tip: Look for the "result" in the sentence. If the result is "growth," the input attitude must be open and positive.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.936Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5532, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-2', 'q09', 8, 8, 'Choose the SYNONYM of EPHEMERAL:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Permanent"},{"id":"B","text":"Eternal"},{"id":"C","text":"Transient"},{"id":"D","text":"Lasting"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

"Ephemeral" describes something that lasts for a very short time. We need a word that captures this sense of briefness.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Ephemeral: Lasting for a very short time.

• Transient: Lasting only for a short time; impermanent. This is the synonym.

• Permanent / Eternal / Lasting: These are all antonyms, describing things that endure for a long time.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The synonym of "Ephemeral" is "Transient."

Quick Tip: "Ephemeral" is often used in nature, like "ephemeral flowers" that bloom and die in a single day.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.936Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5533, 'english-2026-05-14-shift-2', 'q10', 9, 9, '''Neither the manager nor the employees _____ informed about the decision.''', '[{"id":"A","text":"was"},{"id":"B","text":"is"},{"id":"C","text":"were"},{"id":"D","text":"has been"}]', 0, 'C', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests the "Rule of Proximity" in subject-verb agreement. When subjects are joined by "neither/nor" or "either/or," the verb must agree with the subject closest to it.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The two subjects are "the manager" (singular) and "the employees" (plural).

• The subject closest to the blank is "the employees."

• Since "employees" is plural, the verb must also be plural.

• "were" is the plural form suitable for the past passive context.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct sentence is: "Neither the manager nor the employees were informed about the decision."

Quick Tip: Always look at the noun right before the verb in "neither...nor" sentences. If that noun is plural, the verb is plural!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.937Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5534, 'english-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Choose the word closest in meaning to "innocuous".', '[{"id":"A","text":"Harmful"},{"id":"B","text":"Harmless"},{"id":"C","text":"Aggressive"},{"id":"D","text":"Suspicious"}]', 0, 'B', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The word "innocuous" is an adjective used to describe something that does not cause any physical or emotional injury. It is derived from the Latin root innocuus, where "in-" means "not" and "nocere" means "to harm."

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Innocuous: Not harmful or offensive.

• Harmless: This is the direct synonym, meaning unlikely to cause injury.

• Harmful: The direct antonym (opposite).

• Aggressive: Ready or likely to attack or confront.

• Suspicious: Having or showing a cautious distrust of someone or something.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The word closest in meaning to "innocuous" is "Harmless."

Quick Tip: Think of "innocuous" as a "no-harm" word. It is often used to describe a comment that wasn''t meant to be mean, like "an innocuous remark."', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.937Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5535, 'english-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Choose the word opposite in meaning to “vindicative”.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Revengeful"},{"id":"B","text":"Forgiving"},{"id":"C","text":"Cruel"},{"id":"D","text":"Arrogant"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The word "vindictive" (often spelled "vindicative" in older or specific legal contexts, though "vindictive" is the standard modern usage for the personality trait) refers to a strong desire for revenge.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Vindictive: Having or showing a strong or unreasoning desire for revenge.

- Forgiving: Ready and willing to stop feeling angry or resentful toward someone for an offense. This is the direct opposite.

• Revengeful: A direct synonym for vindictive.

- Cruel / Arrogant: These are negative traits but do not specifically address the "revenge vs. mercy" aspect.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The opposite of "vindicative" is "Forgiving."

Quick Tip: To remember "vindictive," think of the word Vendetta (a blood feud). A vindictive person is someone who wants to start or continue a vendetta.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.937Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5536, 'english-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q03', 3, 3, 'Choose the grammatically correct sentence.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Neither of the boys were ready for the competition."},{"id":"B","text":"Neither of the boys was ready for the competition."},{"id":"C","text":"Neither of the boys are ready for the competition."},{"id":"D","text":"Neither of the boys have been ready for the competition."}]', 0, 'B', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests the rule of distributive pronouns. Words like "Each," "Either," and "Neither" are distributive pronouns and are always considered singular in formal grammar.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

In the phrase "Neither of the boys," the word "Neither" is the actual subject of the sentence, not "boys."

Because the subject is singular, the verb must also be singular.

• "Neither" means "not one and not the other" (referring to them individually).

• "was" is singular, while "were," "are," and "have been" are plural.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct sentence is: "Neither of the boys was ready for the competition."

Quick Tip: Whenever you see "of the [plural noun]" after "Each/Either/Neither," ignore the plural noun. Focus only on the first word to decide the verb!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.938Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5537, 'english-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Choose the correct option to complete the sentence.

By the time we reached the station, the train ___.', '[{"id":"A","text":"left"},{"id":"B","text":"has left"},{"id":"C","text":"had left"},{"id":"D","text":"leaves"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This sentence involves two actions that happened in the past at different times. In English grammar, when two past actions are related, we use the Past Perfect tense for the action that happened first and the Simple Past for the action that happened second.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Action 1 (First): The train departed.

• Action 2 (Second): We reached the station.

Since the train leaving happened before reaching the station, we must use the Past Perfect (had + past participle).

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct completion is "had left."

Quick Tip: Think of the Past Perfect as the "Past of the Past." If you are already talking about the past ("reached"), anything that happened before that needs "had."', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.938Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5538, 'english-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q05', 5, 5, 'Choose the correct passive form of the sentence: “They are organizing a cultural fest.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"A cultural fest is organized by them."},{"id":"B","text":"A cultural fest was being organized by them."},{"id":"C","text":"A cultural fest is being organized by them."},{"id":"D","text":"A cultural fest has been organized by them."}]', 0, 'C', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The original sentence is in the Present Continuous tense (Subject + is/am/are + v-ing + Object). To convert this to passive voice, we use the formula: Object + is/am/are + being + V3 (past participle) + by + Subject.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Subject: They → becomes "them" (object).

• Object: A cultural fest  $ \rightarrow $ becomes the new subject.

- Verb Change: "are organizing" (continuous) must include "being" to maintain the continuous aspect in passive form.

Applying the formula: "A cultural fest" (singular) + "is" + "being" + "organized" + "by them."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct passive form is: "A cultural fest is being organized by them."

Quick Tip: If the active sentence has an "-ing" verb, the passive sentence MUST have the word "being." This is an easy way to eliminate wrong options!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.938Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5539, 'english-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q06', 6, 6, 'Choose the correct indirect narration.

### Riya said, “I will complete the project tomorrow.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"Riya said that she will complete the project tomorrow."},{"id":"B","text":"Riya said that she would complete the project the next day."},{"id":"C","text":"Riya said that she completed the project tomorrow."},{"id":"D","text":"Riya said she would have completed the project the next day."}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

When converting from Direct to Indirect Speech, we must apply three main changes: Tense change, Pronoun change, and Time/Place word changes.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Pronoun Change: "I" (referring to Riya) changes to "she."

• Tense Change: "will" changes to its past form, "would."

• Time Word Change: "tomorrow" changes to "the next day" or "the following day."

• Connector: Use "that" after the reporting verb "said."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct indirect narration is: "Riya said that she would complete the project the next day."

Quick Tip: Always remember the "Backshift." If the reporting verb is in the past ("said"), the verbs inside the quotes must move one step back into the past (will  $ \rightarrow $ would, can  $ \rightarrow $ could).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.938Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5540, 'english-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q07', 7, 7, 'Arrange the following parts in the correct order.

P. the scientist presented

Q. at the international conference

R. his groundbreaking research

S. confidently', '[{"id":"A","text":"PQRS"},{"id":"B","text":"PRSQ"},{"id":"C","text":"PSRQ"},{"id":"D","text":"QPRS"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Sentence rearrangement requires identifying the Subject, Verb, and Object (SVO), followed by adverbs of manner and prepositional phrases for location or time.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Subject + Verb (P): "The scientist presented"

Adverb of Manner (S): "confidently" (This describes how he presented).

• Object (R): "his groundbreaking research" (This describes what he presented).

• Location (Q): "at the international conference" (This describes where).

While (B) "P R S Q" is also common in casual English, (C) "P S R Q" provides a more formal and fluid flow by placing the adverb closer to the verb. In many standard examinations, P-S-R-Q is the preferred structural order.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct order is P S R Q.

Quick Tip: A good rule of thumb is: Who (P) + How (S) + What (R) + Where (Q).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.938Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5541, 'english-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q08', 8, 8, 'Read the passage carefully:

Many students believe that success depends entirely on intelligence. However, researchers have found that consistency and disciplined effort often play a greater role in long-term achievement than natural talent alone.

What is the main idea of the passage?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Intelligence is unnecessary for success."},{"id":"B","text":"Talent guarantees achievement."},{"id":"C","text":"Consistent effort is important for success."},{"id":"D","text":"Researchers dislike talented students."}]', 0, 'C', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Finding the "main idea" involves identifying the author''s primary argument. Pay close attention to transition words like "However," as they often shift the focus from a common belief to the author''s actual point.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• The passage acknowledges a common belief (intelligence = success).

• It uses "However" to introduce a different perspective.

- It highlights "consistency and disciplined effort" as key factors that often outweigh "natural talent."

• Option (C) directly reflects this emphasis on effort.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The main idea is that consistent effort is important for success.

Quick Tip: The word "However" is a giant signpost. The most important information in a short passage usually comes immediately after it.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.939Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5542, 'english-2026-05-15-shift-1', 'q10', 9, 9, 'Choose the best replacement for the underlined part:

Each of the players  $ \underline{\text{have performed}} $ exceptionally well.', '[{"id":"A","text":"has performed"},{"id":"B","text":"were performing"},{"id":"C","text":"perform"},{"id":"D","text":"have been performing"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Similar to the "Neither of the boys" rule, the word "Each" is a distributive pronoun that always takes a singular verb, even when followed by a plural prepositional phrase (of the players).

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Subject: "Each" (Singular).

• Verb needed: Singular form.

• "Have" is plural; "has" is singular.

Therefore, "have performed" must be replaced with "has performed" to agree with "Each."

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct replacement is "has performed."

#### Quick Tip: Think of "Each" as "Every single one." Since "one" is singular, the verb must be singular too!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.939Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5543, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Choose the word that is closest in meaning to “obfuscate”.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Clarify"},{"id":"B","text":"Confuse"},{"id":"C","text":"Simplify"},{"id":"D","text":"Reveal"}]', 0, 'B', '## Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Vocabulary questions require analyzing the root meaning, context, and usage of a word to determine its nearest synonym among the given choices.

## Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Identify the definition of the target word: "Obfuscate" means to render obscure, unclear, or unintelligible.

Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Obfuscate means to deliberately make something difficult to understand or perceive (e.g.,

"The writer used complex jargon to obfuscate his true meaning"). Therefore, Confuse is the closest synonym. Clarify, Simplify, and Reveal are all antonyms of obfuscate, as they deal with making things clearer or easier to see.

##### Step 4: Final Answer:

The word closest in meaning to “obfuscate” is Confuse.

Quick Tip: Think of the "ob-" prefix in obfuscate as related to words like obscure or obstruct—all of which involve blocking or clouding view or understanding.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.939Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5544, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Identify the correctly spelt word.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Acquaintence"},{"id":"B","text":"Acquaintance"},{"id":"C","text":"Acquaintance"},{"id":"D","text":"Acquaintance"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Spelling tests assess knowledge of phonetic rules, silent letters, and conventional letter groupings in English vocabulary.

Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Break down the word into its syllables or root construction: ac + quaint + ance.

Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

The root component is "quaint" (familiar or unusual), preceded by the prefix combination "ac-". The suffix used here is -ance (denoting a state or condition), not "-ence". Combining these elements systematically yields Acquaintance (a person one knows slightly, but who is not a close friend).

##### Step 4: Final Answer:

The correctly spelt word is Acquaintance.

Quick Tip: Remember that an acquaintance is someone you are bound to check ain''t a stranger. Focus on the word "quaint" hidden inside it!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.940Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5545, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q03', 3, 3, 'Choose the correct idiom to complete the sentence:

"When the manager resigned suddenly, the company employees were left ___."', '[{"id":"A","text":"in hot water"},{"id":"B","text":"under the weather"},{"id":"C","text":"high and dry"},{"id":"D","text":"at sixes and sevens"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Idioms are expressions whose figurative meanings cannot be understood directly from the literal definitions of individual words. The correct option must logically match the contextual narrative of the sentence.

#### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Analyze the meaning of each idiom in relation to a sudden abandonment or crisis:

• In hot water: In trouble or facing punishment.

• Under the weather: Feeling sick or unwell.

• High and dry: Left stranded, helpless, or abandoned without resources.

• At sixes and sevens: In a state of total confusion or disarray.

##### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

The sentence states that a manager resigned suddenly, leaving the employees without leadership or support to handle the ongoing operations. While "at sixes and sevens" (confused)

is a close option, the phrase high and dry specifically emphasizes being abandoned in a difficult, vulnerable, and helpless situation due to someone else''s sudden actions.

##### Step 4: Final Answer:

The most appropriate idiom to complete the sentence is high and dry.

Quick Tip: The phrase high and dry originally described a ship stuck out of water on land, unable to move or save itself—perfect for describing a team stranded without a boss!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.940Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5546, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Select the sentence with the correct grammatical usage.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Neither of the boys were ready for the exam."},{"id":"B","text":"Neither of the boys was ready for the exam."},{"id":"C","text":"Neither of the boys are ready for the exam."},{"id":"D","text":"Neither of the boys have been ready for the exam."}]', 0, 'B', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question deals with subject-verb agreement rules, specifically when using distributive pronouns like "neither", "either", or "each".

#### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

The pronoun "neither" means "not one nor the other of two." It is inherently singular in formal grammar and must be paired with a singular verb.

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

In the sentence structure "Neither of the [plural noun]" , the actual subject is Neither, not the plural noun that follows the preposition "of" (boys).

• Because "Neither" is singular, it requires the singular past-tense verb was.

• Options (a), (c), and (d) use plural verbs ("were", "are", "have been"), creating an error in subject-verb agreement.

##### Step 4: Final Answer:

The grammatically correct sentence is: "Neither of the boys was ready for the exam."

Quick Tip: To avoid confusion, mentally cross out the phrase starting with "of". Read it as: "Neither [was] ready for the exam." It makes the correct singular verb choice obvious!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.940Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5547, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q06', 5, 5, 'Choose the option that best paraphrases the sentence:

#### “Technological advancement has significantly altered human interaction.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"Technology has reduced the need for communication entirely."},{"id":"B","text":"Human interaction is unaffected by modern inventions."},{"id":"C","text":"Advances in technology have greatly changed the way people interact."},{"id":"D","text":"Technology only affects professional communication."}]', 0, 'C', '##### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Paraphrasing means rewriting a statement using different words while keeping the exact same meaning, tone, and scope as the original message.

#### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Match key synonyms and retain core logical relations:

• "Technological advancement"  $ \rightarrow $ "Advances in technology"

• "Significantly altered"  $ \rightarrow $ "Greatly changed"

• "Human interaction"  $ \rightarrow $ "The way people interact"

##### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Option (a) changes the scope by making an extreme claim ("reduced the need... entirely"). Option (b) states the exact opposite of the original sentence. Option (c) perfectly retains the meaning by substituting words with accurate synonyms without adding or subtracting constraints. Option (d) introduces a limitation ("only affects professional") that was not present in the broad original text.

##### Step 4: Final Answer:

The option that best paraphrases the sentence is (c).

Quick Tip: A good paraphrase never introduces extreme absolute words like entirely, only, always, or never unless they were already present in the original sentence.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.940Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5548, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q07', 6, 6, 'Fill in the blank with the correct option:

"If she _____ harder, she would have succeeded."', '[{"id":"A","text":"studies"},{"id":"B","text":"studied"},{"id":"C","text":"had studied"},{"id":"D","text":"has studied"}]', 0, 'C', '## Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This sentence belongs to the Third Conditional category. Third conditional sentences are used to express hypothetical situations in the past—events that did not happen, along with their imaginary results.

## Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

The standard structural formula for a Third Conditional sentence is:

If + Past Perfect Clause (had +  $ V_{3} $),… would have + Past Participle ( $ V_{3} $)

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

The result clause is explicitly given as "...she would have succeeded" (would have +  $ V_{3} $). Following the conditional rule, the conditional "if" clause must take a verb phrase in the past perfect tense. Among the given choices, only had studied satisfies the past perfect requirement (had +  $ V_{3} $). Choosing "studies" (present), "studied" (simple past), or "has studied" (present perfect) breaks the structural logic of this conditional structure.

#### Step 4: Final Answer:

The complete grammatically correct sentence is: “If she had studied harder, she would have succeeded.”

Quick Tip: Think of it as a past balancing scale: If you spot "would have" on one side of the sentence, you almost always need "had" on the other side!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.940Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5549, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q08', 7, 7, 'Read the following passage and answer the question:

Climate change is one of the most pressing challenges of the modern era. Scientists warn that rising temperatures may lead to severe environmental consequences, including floods, droughts, and biodiversity loss. Governments across the world are attempting to develop sustainable solutions, but implementation remains uneven.

What is the central idea of the passage?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Governments have solved climate change completely."},{"id":"B","text":"Climate change poses serious global challenges requiring action."},{"id":"C","text":"Biodiversity loss is unrelated to climate change."},{"id":"D","text":"Scientists disagree about global warming."}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Finding the central idea requires capturing the broad theme that unifies every sentence in the passage rather than focusing on a single detail or an over-generalized claim.

Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Analyze the logical development of the short text:

• Sentence 1 establishes the main topic: "pressing challenges."

• Sentence 2 provides details on risks: "severe environmental consequences."

• Sentence 3 outlines the ongoing response: "governments attempting solutions."

Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Option (a) is completely false because the text notes that implementation "remains uneven."

Option (b) accurately sums up the primary points: it points out the problems (challenges) and acknowledges the ongoing effort to fix them (action). Option (c) is explicitly contradicted by the text, which lists biodiversity loss as a consequence of climate change. Option (d) is incorrect because the passage says "scientists warn" collectively, showing agreement rather than conflict.

##### Step 4: Final Answer:

The central idea of the passage is that climate change poses serious global challenges requiring action.

Quick Tip: The central idea is usually stated clearly right in the very first sentence of short informational passages. Use it as your anchor!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.940Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5550, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q09', 8, 8, 'Identify the figure of speech used in the line:

“Life is a journey filled with unexpected turns.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"Simile"},{"id":"B","text":"Oxymoron"},{"id":"C","text":"Metaphor"},{"id":"D","text":"Alliteration"}]', 0, 'C', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Figures of speech identify comparisons made between two completely different concepts to highlight a shared characteristic.

### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Look closely at how the comparison is set up:

• If it says A is like or as B, it is a Simile.

• If it says A is B directly, it is a Metaphor.

Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

The statement directly asserts that "Life is a journey." It does not use comparative words like "like" or "as" to soften the imagery. By equating life directly to a journey with twists and turns, it functions as a Metaphor, carrying the qualities of travel and exploration over to human existence.

##### Step 4: Final Answer:

The figure of speech used is a Metaphor.

Quick Tip: “Life is like a box of chocolates” is a simile. “Life is a highway” is a metaphor. It all comes down to dropping the word "like"!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.940Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5551, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-1', 'q10', 9, 9, 'Choose the word opposite in meaning to “ephemeral.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"Temporary"},{"id":"B","text":"Fleeting"},{"id":"C","text":"Permanent"},{"id":"D","text":"Fragile"}]', 0, 'C', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Antonym questions require identifying the word that carries the inverse or opposite logical meaning of the given vocabulary term.

#### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Define the core term: "Ephemeral" is derived from Greek roots meaning "lasting only a day." It describes something short-lived or transient.

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

Temporary and Fleeting mean lasting a very short time, making them synonyms of ephemeral. Fragile means easily broken, which is a different concept entirely. Permanent means lasting or intended to last indefinitely without change, which is the exact polar opposite of a short-lived,

transient state.

##### Step 4: Final Answer:

The word opposite in meaning to “ephemeral” is Permanent.

Quick Tip: Think of morning dew or a shooting star—they are classic examples of ephemeral things. Their opposite is something built to last forever, like a mountain range (permanent).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.940Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5552, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Choose the correct passive or active form of the given sentence. “They have to paint the ceiling before they can install the new light fixtures.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"The ceiling has to be painted before the new light fixtures can be installed."},{"id":"B","text":"The ceiling have to be painted before installing the new light fixtures."},{"id":"C","text":"Before the new light fixtures can install, the ceiling has to be painted."},{"id":"D","text":"The ceiling must be painted by them prior to installing the light fixtures."}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Voice conversion involves shifting the focus of a sentence from the performer of the action (active voice) to the recipient of the action (passive voice). When a sentence contains multiple clauses connected by a conjunction, both clauses must be converted into their corresponding passive structures while maintaining subject-verb agreement and the original tense.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s break down the given active sentence into its two component clauses: 1. Clause 1 (Active):

"They have to paint the ceiling..." The active structure uses "have to + verb (base form)". Passive

structure rule: "Object + has/have to + be + past participle (V3)". Since "The ceiling" is singular, it takes "has to be": "The ceiling has to be painted..." 2. Clause 2 (Active): "...before they can install the new light fixtures." The active structure uses the modal verb "can + verb (base form)". Passive structure rule: "Modal + be + past participle (V3)". The object is "the new light fixtures" (plural): "...before the new light fixtures can be installed."

Combining both converted clauses gives: "The ceiling has to be painted before the new light fixtures can be installed." This aligns with Option (a). Option (b) fails due to incorrect subject-verb agreement ("ceiling have to"), and Option (c) leaves the second clause in an ungrammatical active state ("fixtures can install").

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct passive form is option (a).

Quick Tip: Always check subject-verb agreement after shifting an object to the subject position! A singular passive subject like "the ceiling" must be paired with "has to be," never "have to be."', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.941Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5553, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Choose the correct passive or active form of the given sentence. “We expect that the committee will approve the project by tomorrow.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"It is expected that the project will be approved by the committee by tomorrow."},{"id":"B","text":"The committee is expected to have approved the project by tomorrow."},{"id":"C","text":"Approval of the project by the committee is expected tomorrow."},{"id":"D","text":"That the project will be approved by the committee by tomorrow is expecting."}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

When an active sentence contains a reporting verb (like expect, believe, say, think) followed by a complete "that-clause" object, it can be converted into the passive voice using an impersonal passive construction. This structure uses the introductory dummy pronoun "It" paired with the passive form of the reporting verb, followed by the passive conversion of the subordinate clause.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the sentence structure: 1. Main Clause (Active): "We expect [that...]" This is in the Simple Present tense. Impersonal passive rule: "It + is/are + V3 + that...". This yields: "It is expected that..." 2. Subordinate Clause (Active): "...the committee will approve the project by tomorrow." This is in the Simple Future tense ("will + verb"). Passive structure rule: "Object + will + be + V3 + by + Subject". This transforms to: "...the project will be approved by the committee by tomorrow."

Putting the full structure together results in Option (a): "It is expected that the project will be approved by the committee by tomorrow." Option (b) changes the aspect unnecessarily, while Option (d) incorrectly retains an active continuous participle ("is expecting").

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct passive form is option (a).

Quick Tip: When an active sentence starts with an introductory phrase like "We expect that..." or "People say that...", look for options starting with "It is expected that..." or "It is said that..." to find the correct passive fit quickly!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.941Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5554, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q03', 3, 3, 'Choose the correct passive or active form of the given sentence. "He likes people flattering him."', '[{"id":"A","text":"He likes to be flattered by people."},{"id":"B","text":"He likes being flattered."},{"id":"C","text":"Flattering him is liked by people."},{"id":"D","text":"He is liked when people flatter him."}]', 0, 'B', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This sentence features a gerund phrase ("flattering him") acting as the direct object of the principal verb "likes". When converting sentences containing a gerund phrase into the passive voice, the main verb ("likes") remains unchanged because the subject ("He") still performs

the emotional preference. The passive transformation occurs completely within the gerund element itself.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s look at the object verbal component: Active Gerund structure: "flattering him" (verb-ing + object pronoun). Passive Gerund conversion rule: "being + past participle (V3)". The passive equivalent of "flattering him" becomes "being flattered".

Now attach this passive gerund back to the unchanged initial subject and verb phrase:

"He likes" + "being flattered"  $ \Longrightarrow $ "He likes being flattered."

The phrase "by people" is omitted because it represents an indefinite, obvious agent that adds no new meaning. While Option (b) attempts a passive infinitive ("to be flattered"), it alters the original active gerund structure ("flattering"). Options (c) and (d) shift the sentence focus entirely away from his personal preference. Thus, Option (a) is the best choice.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct passive form is option (b).

Quick Tip: When turning an active gerund (verb-ing) into a passive phrase, look for options containing "being + V3". This helps you quickly spot the correct structure without changing the meaning of the main verb!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.941Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5555, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Choose the correct passive or active form of the given sentence. "They let the prisoner leave early."', '[{"id":"A","text":"The prisoner was let leave early."},{"id":"B","text":"The prisoner was let to leave early."},{"id":"C","text":"The prisoner was allowed leave early."},{"id":"D","text":"Leaving early was what the prisoner was let do."}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

In English grammar, the causative verb "let" is followed by an object and a bare infinitive (a verb without "to") in the active voice. When converting sentences with "let" into the passive voice, the verb "let" retains its bare infinitive complement. It does not take a full infinitive ("to leave"), unlike typical verbs converted into passive structures.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the syntax of the active sentence: Active structure: Subject ("They") + verb ("let") + object ("the prisoner") + bare infinitive ("leave") + extension ("early"). Passive rule for ''let'' (retaining ''let''): New Subject ("The prisoner") + auxiliary verb ("was", since the sentence is simple past) + past participle ("let") + bare infinitive ("leave") + extension ("early").

Applying this rule gives us Option (a): "The prisoner was let leave early."

Note on standard grammatical variations: Because "was let leave" sounds awkward to many native speakers, standard English frequently swaps "let" out for its synonym "allow" when making a passive shift. However, "allow" requires a full infinitive with "to" ("The prisoner was allowed to leave early"). Since Option (c) leaves out the word "to" ("was allowed to leave"), it is ungrammatical. Therefore, Option (a) is the only structurally sound choice.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct passive form is option (a).

Quick Tip: Remember that the three principal forms of the verb "let" are identical: let (V1) → let (V2) → let (V3). When converting to passive, keep the secondary action verb bare: "was let leave", not "was let to leave".', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.941Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5556, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q06', 5, 5, 'Transform the following sentences into Indirect or Direct speech. "Had I known the truth, I would not have spoken so harshly," said Evelyn.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Evelyn said that if she had known the truth, she would not have spoken so harshly."},{"id":"B","text":"Evelyn regretted having spoken harshly without knowing the truth."},{"id":"C","text":"Evelyn said that had she known the truth she hadn''t spoken so harshly."},{"id":"D","text":"Evelyn told that she wouldn''t speak harshly if she knew the truth."}]', 0, 'A', '##### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

When reporting conditional sentences in indirect speech, the tense adjustments depend on the type of conditional statement. The given sentence is a Type 3 (impossible/past) conditional, which uses an inverted past perfect structure ("Had I known..." is equivalent to "If I had known..."). In indirect speech, Type 3 conditional sentences do not undergo a tense shift because the past perfect and perfect conditional forms are already at their furthest past limit. Pronouns must be adjusted to match the speaker''s perspective.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the required structural changes step by step: 1. Reporting Verb: "said Evelyn" transforms to "Evelyn said that..." 2. First-Person Pronoun Shifts: The pronoun "I" refers directly to Evelyn, so it changes to the third-person feminine singular pronoun "she". 3. Conditional Clause Conversion: The inverted phrase "Had I known the truth" can be rewritten with its explicit conjunction as "if she had known the truth". 4. Main Clause Tense Retention: The main consequence clause "I would not have spoken so harshly" retains its original perfect conditional tense, changing only the pronoun: "she would not have spoken so harshly".

Combining these elements yields Option (a): "Evelyn said that if she had known the truth, she would not have spoken so harshly." Option (b) paraphrases the sentiment rather than directly transforming the speech structure. Options (c) and (d) introduce incorrect tense modifications to the clauses.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct indirect speech form is option (a).

Quick Tip: An inverted conditional phrase like "Had I known" is just a formal way of saying "If I had known". When converting past perfect or third conditional sentences to indirect speech, leave the tenses exactly as they are!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.941Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5557, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q07', 6, 6, 'Choose the word most nearly opposite in meaning to the capitalized word. “BILIOUS”', '[{"id":"A","text":"Amiable"},{"id":"B","text":"Peevish"},{"id":"C","text":"Valiant"},{"id":"D","text":"Sallow"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Antonym identification requires analyzing the contextual, descriptive, and emotional weight of a given vocabulary word. "BILIOUS" historically relates to gastric distress or an excess of bile, but in modern vocabulary usage, it describes someone who is remarkably bad-tempered, irritable, or constantly cross. Therefore, we must look for an antonym that describes a pleasant, warm, and good-natured temperament.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the definitions of all the options provided: BILIOUS: Irritable, bad-tempered, or extremely cranky. Amiable (Antonym): Displaying a friendly, pleasant, and highly agreeable manner. This is the exact opposite of being irritable and bad-tempered. Peevish (Synonym): Easily irritated, especially by unimportant things. Valiant: Showing courage or determination; brave. Sallow: (Of a person''s face or complexion) of an unhealthy yellow or pale brown color, which is a physical symptom often associated with being bilious.

Because "Amiable" represents a pleasant and friendly disposition, it stands as the correct opposite to the bitter and irritable nature of "Bilious".

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The word most nearly opposite in meaning to BILIOUS is Amiable.

Quick Tip: To remember "bilious," think of "belly ache." Someone with a belly ache is usually cranky, bad-tempered, and irritable! The perfect cure for a cranky person is someone who is friendly and "amiable."', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.941Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5558, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q08', 7, 7, 'Choose the word most nearly opposite in meaning to the capitalized word. “INSOUCIANT”', '[{"id":"A","text":"Flamboyant"},{"id":"B","text":"Nonchalant"},{"id":"C","text":"Languid"},{"id":"D","text":"Anxious"}]', 0, 'D', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To find the correct antonym, we first define the core disposition of the prompt word. "INSOUCIANT" describes a state of being completely carefree, unconcerned, or nonchalant. The direct polar opposite of a carefree state of mind is one filled with worry, apprehension, and mental stress.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s break down the definitions of each option: INSOUCIANT: Showing a casual lack of concern; entirely carefree and indifferent. Anxious (Antonym): Experiencing worry, unease, or nervousness, typically about an imminent event or something with an uncertain outcome. This is the precise psychological opposite of being carefree. Nonchalant (Synonym): Feeling or appearing casually calm and relaxed; unconcerned. Languid: Displaying or having a disinclination for physical exertion or effort; slow and relaxed. Flamboyant: Tending to attract attention because of their exuberance, confidence, and stylishness.

Since "Insouciant" implies an absence of anxiety or concern, "Anxious" is its perfect contextual opposite.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The word most nearly opposite in meaning to INSOUCIANT is Anxious.

Quick Tip: The word "insouciant" comes from a French root meaning "not caring." If you are insouciant, you are entirely stress-free. The opposite of a stress-free person is an anxious one!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.942Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5559, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q09', 8, 8, 'Choose the word most nearly opposite in meaning to the capitalized word. “TANTAMOUNT”', '[{"id":"A","text":"Incommensurate"},{"id":"B","text":"Commensurate"},{"id":"C","text":"Redundant"},{"id":"D","text":"Obsolete"}]', 0, 'A', '##### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The word "TANTAMOUNT" is an adjective used to state that two things are virtually equivalent or equal in value, significance, or effect (e.g., "a statement tantamount to an admission of guilt"). To find its opposite, we must identify a word that describes two things that are unequal, disproportionate, or completely out of sync with each other.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the vocabulary choices: TANTAMOUNT: Equivalent in value, significance, or effect; equal to. Incommensurate (Antonym): Out of proportion; unequal or inadequate in size, degree, or value compared to something else. This perfectly opposes the concept of equivalence. Commensurate (Synonym): Corresponding in size, amount, or degree; proportionate or equal. Redundant: Not or no longer needed or useful; superfluous. Obsolete: No longer produced or used; out of date.

Because "Tantamount" indicates that two things equal each other in impact, "Incommensurate" is the correct antonym because it points out a clear disproportion or inequality.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The word most nearly opposite in meaning to TANTAMOUNT is Incommensurate.

Quick Tip: Think of the "Equal" sign (=) for Tantamount and Commensurate. To find the antonym, look for the negative prefix "In-" in Incommensurate, which acts like a "Not Equal" sign (≠)!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.942Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5560, 'english-2026-05-18-shift-2', 'q10', 9, 9, 'Choose the word most nearly opposite in meaning to the capitalized word. “PERNICIOUS”', '[{"id":"A","text":"Salubrious"},{"id":"B","text":"Nefarious"},{"id":"C","text":"Innocuous"},{"id":"D","text":"Devastating"}]', 0, 'A', '##### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

"PERNICIOUS" is a powerful descriptive adjective that indicates something is highly destructive, deeply harmful, or fatal in a subtle, gradual way (such as a pernicious rumor or a pernicious disease). When seeking its opposite, we are looking for a word that denotes something highly health-giving, wholesome, or beneficial.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s look at the precise definitions of all four options: PERNICIOUS: Having a harmful, destructive, or deadly effect, especially in a gradual or subtle manner. Salubrious (Antonym): Health-giving, beneficial, wholesome, or highly favorable to sanitary well-being. This is the most complete and direct opposite to the life-destroying nature of pernicious. Nefarious (Synonym-adjacent): Wicked, villainous, or criminal in nature. Innocuous: Not harmful or offensive; harmless. While "innocuous" means neutral/harmless, "salubrious" goes a step further by being actively beneficial and health-promoting, making it the strongest and most complete vocabulary antonym for a destructive force. Devastating (Synonym): Highly destructive, damaging, or causing severe shock.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, "Salubrious" stands out as the most precise opposite choice.

The word most nearly opposite in meaning to PERNICIOUS is Salubrious.

Quick Tip: Associate "Pernicious" with "Poisonous" (both start with P and mean harmful). Associate "Salubrious" with "Salute to health" (both start with Sal and mean healthy). This simple letter pairing keeps them clear in your mind!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.942Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5561, 'english-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Despite losing his job, he managed to _____ by doing small freelance projects.', '[{"id":"A","text":"white elephant"},{"id":"B","text":"eke out a living"},{"id":"C","text":"at sixes and sevens"},{"id":"D","text":"Fabian policy"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This sentence requires filling in the blank with an appropriate English idiom that aligns with the context of financial survival. The subject has lost his primary job but is performing small, temporary tasks to sustain himself. The chosen phrase must mean making just enough money to survive or get by with difficulty.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the meanings of all the idiomatic choices: White elephant: A possession that is useless or troublesome, especially one that is expensive to maintain. This does not fit the context of earning money. Eke out a living (Correct): To earn just enough money to survive

or buy life''s necessities, usually with great effort or by supplementing income through small tasks. This perfectly completes the sentence structure logically and grammatically. At sixes and sevens: A state of total confusion or disarray. It does not fit the blank as a verb phrase. Fabian policy: A policy of cautious delay or avoiding direct confrontation. This is entirely irrelevant to financial survival.

Therefore, "eke out a living" is the only option that logically explains how he survived financially after losing his job.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct idiomatic phrase to fill the blank is option (b).

Quick Tip: To "eke out" something means to add to it or stretch it out carefully. Think of stretching your last bit of savings or making small earnings last as long as possible!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.942Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5562, 'english-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Hobson''s choice means:', '[{"id":"A","text":"A difficult puzzle"},{"id":"B","text":"No real choice at all"},{"id":"C","text":"A secret agreement"},{"id":"D","text":"Extreme happiness"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Idioms often have fascinating historical origins that explain their current figurative definitions. The phrase "Hobson''s choice" describes a specific situation where an individual is offered a choice that seems optional on the surface, but in reality, there is only one true possibility available: take exactly what is offered or leave it.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The phrase comes from Thomas Hobson (1544–1631), a stable owner in Cambridge, England. Hobson kept a large collection of excellent horses for students and travelers to rent. However,

to ensure his best horses weren''t overworked, he gave his customers a strict rule: they could either take the horse in the stall nearest the stable door or take no horse at all. Therefore, even though customers thought they had a selection, they actually had no real choice at all. This perfectly matches Option (b).

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The meaning of Hobson''s choice is option (b).

Quick Tip: Remember the simple rule of Hobson''s stable: "Take this one option, or take nothing at all!" It''s an illusion of choice.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.942Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5563, 'english-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q04', 3, 3, 'The abandoned airport project became a _____ for the government.', '[{"id":"A","text":"white elephant"},{"id":"B","text":"cortege"},{"id":"C","text":"discourse"},{"id":"D","text":"brevity"}]', 0, 'A', '##### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This sentence context describes a major public infrastructure venture ("airport project") that has been abandoned. For a government, such an idle, expensive asset represents a massive drain on financial resources without providing any practical public utility. The missing vocabulary item must be an idiom that characterizes a burdensome, useless, and costly possession.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the definition and suitability of each option: White elephant (Correct): An idiom used to describe a possession that is completely useless, unwanted, or unprofitable, especially one that is extremely expensive to maintain or build. An abandoned infrastructure project fits this description perfectly. Cortege: A solemn procession, especially for a funeral. This is

completely irrelevant to an engineering or financial project. Discourse: Written or spoken communication or debate. This does not fit the financial context. Brevity: Concise and exact use of words in writing or speech; shortness of time. This does not make grammatical or contextual sense here.

Thus, "white elephant" is the only option that logically describes the government''s financial burden.

### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct idiomatic phrase to fill the blank is option (a).

Quick Tip: The term "white elephant" comes from an old custom where the King of Siam would give a rare white elephant to courtiers he disliked. The cost of feeding and housing the sacred beast was so high that it would financially ruin the recipient!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.942Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5564, 'english-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q05', 4, 4, 'A long procession, especially for a funeral, is called a:', '[{"id":"A","text":"philistine"},{"id":"B","text":"cortege"},{"id":"C","text":"itinerary"},{"id":"D","text":"discourse"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests single-word substitutions for descriptive phrases. We need to identify a specific collective noun or term that directly defines a formal, organized group of people or vehicles moving along together during a funeral service.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s examine the dictionary definitions of all four vocabulary options: Philistine: A person who is hostile or indifferent to culture, the arts, or refined intellect. Cortege (Correct): A formal, slow-moving procession, specifically one accompanying a funeral, consisting of mourners,

vehicles, or a casket. This perfectly matches the prompt''s definition. Itinerary: A detailed plan or route proposed for a journey or trip. Discourse: A formal, structured conversation, speech, or piece of writing on a specific subject.

Therefore, "cortege" is the precise lexical term for a funeral procession.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct one-word substitution is option (b).

Quick Tip: Think of a "cortege" as a formal, slow-moving column of vehicles or people accompanying a funeral carriage. It shares historical roots with the word "court," implying a structured, formal escort.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.943Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5565, 'english-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q06', 5, 5, 'Choose the pair of antonyms.', '[{"id":"A","text":"adroit – skillful"},{"id":"B","text":"verbose - lengthy"},{"id":"C","text":"corpulent – emaciated"},{"id":"D","text":"philately – stamp collection"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This problem requires evaluating the semantic relationship between pairs of words. While three of the options present pairs of synonyms or direct definitions, one pair consists of words with completely opposite meanings (antonyms). We must verify each relationship to isolate the antonym pair.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the relationships in each pair: Option (a): Adroit means clever or skillful in using the hands or mind. Therefore, adroit and skillful are synonyms. Option (b): Verbose means using or expressed in more words than are needed, which is equivalent to being lengthy. Therefore, these are synonyms. Option (c): Corpulent means excessively fat, large, or bulky. On the other hand, emaciated means abnormally thin or weak, especially because of illness or

a lack of food. Because one means excessively fat and the other means abnormally thin, they are antonyms. This satisfies the question requirement. Option (d): Philately is the study or collection of postage stamps. This is a word paired with its direct definition.

Thus, option (c) is the only pair that represents opposites.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The pair of antonyms is option (c).

Quick Tip: To remember "corpulent," think of "corps/corpus," which means body—someone who has a lot of "body" is fat. To remember "emaciated," focus on the "mac" root, which sounds like "meager" or lacking mass!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.943Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5566, 'english-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q07', 6, 6, 'An _____ speaker can explain difficult ideas very clearly and skillfully.', '[{"id":"A","text":"itinerant"},{"id":"B","text":"adroit"},{"id":"C","text":"emaciated"},{"id":"D","text":"cantankerous"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This context requires selecting a descriptive adjective that characterizes a person''s speaking ability, specifically their skillfulness and clarity when handling complex topics. The missing vocabulary word must denote high competence, cleverness, or dexterity in performance.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s evaluate the definitions of all four options to find the correct match: Itinerant: Traveling from place to place. This describes a physical movement pattern, not a communication skill. Adroit (Correct): Clever, adept, or highly skillful in using the hands or mind. When used to describe a speaker, it means they are exceptionally skilled at structuring arguments and explaining complex points smoothly. Emaciated: Abnormally thin or weak, especially because

of illness or a lack of food. This is a physical condition and is completely irrelevant here. Cantankerous: Bad-tempered, argumentative, and uncooperative. A cantankerous speaker would be hostile rather than clear and skillful.

Therefore, "adroit" is the only adjective that logically and grammatically fits the blank to complete the description.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct word to fill the blank is option (b).

Quick Tip: To remember "adroit," think of the word "add right." An adroit person adds the right skills to any task to get it done cleverly and smoothly!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.943Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5567, 'english-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q08', 7, 7, '“Brevity” means:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Loudness"},{"id":"B","text":"Intelligence"},{"id":"C","text":"Shortness in speech or writing"},{"id":"D","text":"Confusion"}]', 0, 'C', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This question tests direct lexical definitions. The abstract noun "Brevity" deals with properties of language, communication, or time duration. We must identify its core meaning from the multiple-choice selection.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

The noun "Brevity" comes from the Latin root brevis, which means short (the same root that gives us words like brief and abbreviate).

In English, it is used to define: 1. Concise and exact use of words in writing or speech. 2. The quality of lasting for a very short duration of time.

Looking at our choices, Option (c) ("Shortness in speech or writing") matches this definition.

perfectly. It is famously referenced in William Shakespeare''s play Hamlet through the quote, "Brevity is the soul of wit," meaning a good point should be made using as few words as possible.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct definition of Brevity is option (c).

Quick Tip: Link "Brevity" directly to the word "Brief." Both share the exact same root and denote keeping things short and directly to the point!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.943Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5568, 'english-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q09', 8, 8, 'The professor''s explanation was so _____ that students lost interest quickly.', '[{"id":"A","text":"verbose"},{"id":"B","text":"adroit"},{"id":"C","text":"taciturn"},{"id":"D","text":"concise"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The clue in this sentence is the cause-and-effect relationship: "students lost interest quickly." This indicates that the professor''s lecture style had a negative quality—likely being too long, tedious, or repetitive. The missing word must describe a style of speaking or writing that uses far more words than necessary.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the vocabulary options: Verbose (Correct): Using or expressed in more words than are needed; wordy and repetitive. A verbose explanation easily becomes boring, causing students to lose interest, which fits the sentence perfectly. Adroit: Skillful or clever. A skillful explanation would keep students engaged rather than bored. Taciturn: Reserved or uncommunicative in speech; saying very little. A professor cannot give a long explanation if they are taciturn. Concise: Giving a lot of information clearly and in a few words. This is a positive quality that prevents boredom.

Thus, "verbose" is the only word that logically explains why the students became bored and disconnected.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct word to fill the blank is option (a).

Quick Tip: Think of "Verbose" as being packed with "Verbs" and words! It describes someone who talks endlessly in circles instead of getting straight to the point.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.943Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5569, 'english-2026-05-19-shift-1', 'q10', 9, 9, 'A person who travels from place to place is known as:', '[{"id":"A","text":"itinerant"},{"id":"B","text":"emaciated"},{"id":"C","text":"sagacious"},{"id":"D","text":"corpulent"}]', 0, 'A', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This problem requires identifying a single-word substitution for a specific behavioral lifestyle: traveling continuously from location to location, often for work or trade (such as itinerant preachers or itinerant laborers).

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s review the definitions of each option to find the correct fit: Itinerant (Correct): Traveling from place to place; wandering or unfixed. It comes from the Latin word iter, meaning a journey (the same root behind itinerary). This matches the definition exactly. Emaciated: Abnormally thin or weak due to illness or lack of food. Sagacious: Having or showing keen mental discernment and good judgment; wise. Corpulent: Excessively fat or bulky in body composition.

Because options (b), (c), and (d) describe physical or intellectual traits rather than movement, "itinerant" is the correct choice.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct one-word substitution is option (a).

Quick Tip: Connect the word "Itinerant" to your travel "Itinerary." Since an itinerary is a travel plan, an itinerant person is someone whose lifestyle is constantly defined by an itinerary!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.943Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5570, 'english-2026-05-20-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'RECONDITE', '[{"id":"A","text":"Manifest"},{"id":"B","text":"Arcane"},{"id":"C","text":"Pedantic"},{"id":"D","text":"Cryptic"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Vocabulary tests require analyzing the precise semantic definitions of words, often focusing on advanced literary, academic, or GRE-level terms. The word "recondite" is an adjective used to describe knowledge or subjects that are deep, hidden from view, and difficult for an ordinary person to comprehend.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the precise meanings of the given options: RECONDITE: (Of a subject or knowledge) little known; abstruse, obscure, or hidden from mental view. Manifest (Antonym): Clear, obvious, or easily perceived by the eye or mind. Arcane (Synonym): Understood by few;

mysterious, secret, or obscure. This perfectly matches the core meaning of "recondite" as both refer to specialized, hidden knowledge. Pedantic: Excessively concerned with minor details or rules; displaying academic learning in an annoying way. Cryptic: Having a meaning that is mysterious, obscure, or written in a hidden code (usually applied to messages, expressions, or puzzles rather than fields of knowledge).

Thus, "Arcane" is the most accurate synonym for "Recondite".

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The closest synonym to RECONDITE is Arcane (Option b).

Quick Tip: To remember both words, link them together in a phrase describing specialized academic fields: "Recondite philosophies and arcane rituals." Both words share the prefix-vibe of being hidden away in a secret archive!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.943Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5571, 'english-2026-05-20-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'PULVERULENT', '[{"id":"A","text":"Dusty or crumbling"},{"id":"B","text":"Extremely powerful"},{"id":"C","text":"Richly decorated"},{"id":"D","text":"Wet and marshy"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Using etymological roots (the origin of words) can help decode rare vocabulary words. The adjective "pulverulent" originates from the Latin root word pulvis or pulver-, which directly translates to "dust". Understanding this root connection makes it easy to isolate the correct definition.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s evaluate the definitions of the options provided: PULVERULENT: Consisting of or covered with fine powder or dust; crumbling easily into fine particles. Extremely powerful: Described by words like potent, omnipotent, or mighty. Richly decorated: Described by words like ornate,

baroque, or flamboyant. Wet and marshy: Described by words like miry, swampy, or boggy. Since the word is directly linked to dust and powder formations, option (a) is the accurate semantic definition.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

PULVERULENT means dusty or crumbling (Option a).

Quick Tip: Connect this word to a common English verb you already know: Pulverize (which means to crush something into tiny, fine particles or dust). A substance that has been pulverized becomes pulverulent!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.944Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5572, 'english-2026-05-20-shift-1', 'q03', 3, 3, 'TURPID', '[{"id":"A","text":"Foul or depraved"},{"id":"B","text":"Swollen and distended"},{"id":"C","text":"Muddy and cloudy"},{"id":"D","text":"Lethargic"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Advanced English vocabulary tests often include words that look and sound nearly identical but have completely distinct definitions (homophones or near-homophones). The word "turpid" must be carefully distinguished from its close linguistic relatives: turgid (swollen) and turbid (muddy).

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s look at the definitions of the word and clear up any confusion with similar terms: TURPID: Viscous, foul, base, or morally depraved. It is directly related to the noun "turpitude" (as in moral turpitude, which refers to wicked or shameful behavior). Therefore, "foul or depraved" is the correct definition. Swollen and distended: This is the definition for Turgid (or tumid). Muddy and cloudy: This is the definition for Turbid (used to describe thick, cloudy liquids or

muddy water containing stirred-up sediment). Lethargic: This is closely associated with Torpid (meaning mentally or physically inactive; sluggish).

By clarifying these subtle differences, we can confidently choose option (a).

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

TURPID means foul or depraved (Option a).

Quick Tip: Keep these four similar-looking words organized in your mind with a quick memory chart:

• Turpid → Depraved (Think: Moral turpitude)

• Turbid  $ \rightarrow $ Muddy (Think: Turbulence in water)

• Turgid → Swollen (Think: Turgor pressure in plant cells)

• Torpid → Sluggish (Think: Inactive/dormant like a torpedo at rest)', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.944Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5573, 'english-2026-05-20-shift-1', 'q04', 4, 4, 'AVUNCULAR', '[{"id":"A","text":"Kind and friendly like an uncle"},{"id":"B","text":"Greedy and predatory"},{"id":"C","text":"Pertaining to birds"},{"id":"D","text":"Ancestral or ancient"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Advanced English vocabulary contains specialized adjectives derived from Latin roots that describe behaviors characteristic of specific family relationships. The word "avuncular" comes directly from the Latin noun avunculus, which translates to "maternal uncle".

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the semantic definitions of all the options: AVUNCULAR: Kind, warm, patient, and friendly, specifically reminiscent of the supportive, non-disciplinary relationship an uncle typically shares with his nieces or nephews. Greedy and predatory: Described by adjectives

like rapacious, voracious, or predatory. Pertaining to birds: Described by the adjective Avian. (A common trap due to the similar starting letters ''av-''). Ancestral or ancient: Described by adjectives like atavistic, hereditary, or ancient.

Therefore, option (a) perfectly captures the exact dictionary definition of the word.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

AVUNCULAR means kind and friendly like an uncle (Option a).

Quick Tip: To build a stronger relational vocabulary, group "avuncular" with these other family-derived adjectives:

• Avuncular → Like an uncle

• Patrilineal / Paternal → Like a father

• Matrilineal / Maternal → Like a mother

• Fraternal → Like a brother

• Sororal → Like a sister', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.944Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5574, 'english-2026-05-20-shift-1', 'q05', 5, 5, 'The unexpected loss of funding meant they had to ___ their expansion plans for the upcoming quarter.', '[{"id":"A","text":"scale back"},{"id":"B","text":"scale up"},{"id":"C","text":"phase out"},{"id":"D","text":"cast aside"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Sentence completion questions rely on recognizing contextual clues to determine the logical tone of a sentence. In this sentence, the key constraint is a negative event: an "unexpected loss of funding". This structural constraint dictates that the missing phrasal verb must describe a reduction, compromise, or retraction of growth plans.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s evaluate how each phrasal verb fits the financial context of the sentence: Scale back (Correct): To reduce the size, scope, or extent of an operation or plan. If an organization suddenly loses money, the most logical immediate step is to downsize or reduce their growth targets for that quarter. Scale up (Antonym): To increase the size or scope of something. It is illogical to increase expansion when you lack funds. Phase out: To gradually bring something to an end or discontinue it in systematic stages. This is typically used for products or long-standing policies, not immediate quarterly growth schedules. Cast aside: To discard or reject something completely because it is no longer wanted. While it sounds plausible, it implies total emotional or physical abandonment, whereas "scale back" is the standard business terminology for managing budget deficits.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct phrasal verb to fill the blank is "scale back" (Option a).

Quick Tip: Think of the word "scale" as a mechanical volume knob or a slider on a map. When you lose resources, you must slide the knob downward, which means you scale back. When you gain resources, you slide it upward, which means you scale up!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.944Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5575, 'english-2026-05-20-shift-1', 'q06', 6, 6, 'PERNICIOUS', '[{"id":"A","text":"Salubrious"},{"id":"B","text":"Nefarious"},{"id":"C","text":"Innocuous"},{"id":"D","text":"Devastating"}]', 0, 'D', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Analyzing structural descriptions in texts involves understanding words that convey harm or damage. The word "pernicious" is an impactful adjective used to describe things that cause serious harm, injury, or ruin, often in a quiet, subtle, or hidden manner over a long period.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s look at the definitions of all the choices to find the closest semantic match: PERNICIOUS: Having a harmful effect, especially in a gradual or subtle way; highly destructive, fatal, or ruinous. Salubrious (Antonym): Health-giving; healthy or pleasant. Nefarious: Wicked, villainous, or criminal (typically used to describe actions, people, or plots rather than general damaging effects). Innocuous (Antonym): Not harmful or offensive; safe. Devastating (Synonym): Highly destructive, damaging, or overwhelming. Out of all the choices, "devastating" is the closest operational match to describe the severe, ruinous impact implied by "pernicious".

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The closest synonym to PERNICIOUS is Devastating (Option d).

Quick Tip: A classic medical and literary phrase to keep memorized is "pernicious anemia" or "pernicious rumors". These are threats that sneak up quietly but have a devastating or highly destructive outcome if left unchecked!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.944Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5576, 'english-2026-05-20-shift-1', 'q07', 7, 7, 'During the trial, the defense attorney used expert testimonies to ___ the claims made by the prosecution''s star witness.', '[{"id":"A","text":"bear out"},{"id":"B","text":"bear down"},{"id":"C","text":"cave in"},{"id":"D","text":"hold up"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Sentence completion questions involving phrasal verbs require analyzing the context and relationship between the subjects. Here, a defense attorney is using "expert testimonies" in relation to "claims". In a legal setting, evidence or testimony is introduced either to support/confirm a claim or to refute it. We must look at the definitions of the phrasal verbs to

see which one logically fits this context.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the meaning of each phrasal verb: Bear out (Correct): To support, confirm, substantiate, or corroborate a statement or claim. If the expert testimonies matched or supported the defense''s perspective of the witness''s claims (or supported the defense''s own claims about them), "bear out" fits perfectly as a term meaning to substantiate. Bear down: To press down heavily, exert maximum effort, or move threateningly toward someone. This does not fit the context of managing arguments or testimonies. Cave in: To collapse inward, or to yield/submit under pressure. This is an action a person takes when giving up, not a function of a testimony. Hold up: While "hold up" can mean to remain valid or stand up to scrutiny (e.g., "The claims didn''t hold up"), it is an intransitive verb phrase in that context. You cannot actively "hold up the claims" to mean confirm them in standard legal vocabulary testing the way you can "bear them out".

Therefore, "bear out" is the most appropriate phrasal verb.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct phrasal verb to fill the blank is "bear out".

Quick Tip: Think of "bear out" as meaning "to carry out the truth" of something. Whenever you see a sentence where evidence, facts, data, or testimonies confirm a theory or statement, "bear out" is the standard phrase to use!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.944Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5577, 'english-2026-05-20-shift-1', 'q08', 8, 8, 'She was so exhausted by the grueling schedule that she felt ready to ___ under the sheer pressure.', '[{"id":"A","text":"cave in"},{"id":"B","text":"buckle up"},{"id":"C","text":"give out"},{"id":"D","text":"tire out"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The context of this sentence highlights a person experiencing extreme exhaustion due to a "grueling schedule" and facing "sheer pressure". The preposition "under" immediately following the blank is a critical structural clue. We need a phrasal verb that naturally pairs with "under" to mean collapsing, surrendering, or breaking down due to mental or physical stress.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s look at how the options interact with the word "under": Cave in (Correct): To collapse inward physically, or figuratively to yield, surrender, or break down under pressure. The phrase "cave in under pressure" is a idiomatic expression in English. Buckle up: To fasten one''s seatbelt in a vehicle. (If the option were "buckle under", it could mean to yield, but "buckle up under" is incorrect). Give out: To break down, stop functioning, or be exhausted (e.g., "Her legs gave out"). While it matches the exhaustion theme, it does not grammatically take the preposition "under" in this manner (you don''t say "give out under the pressure" to mean surrender, you just say "give out"). Tire out: To make someone completely exhausted. This is a transitive verb phrase usually requiring an object (e.g., "The schedule tired her out"), and it is redundant when "exhausted" is already used in the sentence.

Thus, "cave in" is the grammatically and contextually perfect selection.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct phrasal verb to fill the blank is "cave in".

Quick Tip: Visualize a structural cave-off. When a tunnel or ceiling has too much weight or pressure placed on top of it, it collapses inward—it caves in. People do the same thing mentally when they are overwhelmed by stress!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.944Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5578, 'english-2026-05-20-shift-1', 'q10', 9, 9, 'He promised to ___ the discrepancies in the ledger before the auditors arrived.', '[{"id":"A","text":"iron out"},{"id":"B","text":"clear off"},{"id":"C","text":"gloss over"},{"id":"D","text":"wipe out"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

The context involves a "ledger" containing "discrepancies" (errors, inconsistencies, or mismatches) that need to be dealt with before an official review by "auditors". To satisfy auditors, accounting errors must be actively resolved, corrected, or smoothed out so that everything balances correctly.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the figurative meanings of the choices: Iron out (Correct): To resolve, smooth over, eliminate, or find a solution for minor problems, errors, or discrepancies. Just as an iron removes wrinkles from fabric to make it smooth, "ironing out discrepancies" means fixing accounting errors so the ledger is smooth and accurate. Clear off: To remove items from a surface (e.g., "clear off the table") or to pay off a debt. It does not mean solving specific line-item discrepancies. Gloss over: To deliberately ignore, conceal, or treat an error lightly to avoid trouble. While a dishonest accountant might try to do this before auditors arrive, "promising" to do so usually implies a constructive action to rectify errors rather than an act of active concealment, making (a) the standard professional expression tested here. Wipe out: To completely destroy or erase something. You cannot simply erase portions of an official ledger without creating more auditing issues.

Thus, "iron out" is the most precise and idiomatic choice.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct phrasal verb to fill the blank is "iron out".

Quick Tip: Think of any problem, misunderstanding, or mathematical discrepancy as an ugly wrinkle on a clean shirt. To make the situation perfect and professional, you need to use your tools to iron out those wrinkles!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.944Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5579, 'english-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Choose the word closest in meaning to “ebullient”.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Gloomy"},{"id":"B","text":"Cheerful and enthusiastic"},{"id":"C","text":"Silent"},{"id":"D","text":"Careless"}]', 0, 'B', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Vocabulary questions checking for the "closest in meaning" require identifying a direct synonym. The word "ebullient" is an adjective derived from the Latin root ebullire, which literally translates to "boiling over" or "bubbling up". In contemporary English usage, it figuratively describes a person''s behavior, mood, or energy levels.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the precise definitions of all the options provided: EBULLIENT: Overflowing with fervor, enthusiasm, energy, or high spirits. It behaves like a liquid that is joyfully bubbling over. Gloomy (Antonym): Dark, dim, depressed, or deeply sad. Cheerful and enthusiastic

(Synonym): Full of visible happiness, high energy, and eagerness. This perfectly aligns with the definition of ebullient. Silent: Refusing to speak or making no audible sound. Careless: Not giving sufficient attention or thought to avoiding harm or errors. Therefore, option (b) is the exact synonym choice.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The word closest in meaning to ebullient is Cheerful and enthusiastic (Option b).

Quick Tip: To remember this word, think of it as sharing a root concept with a boiling pot of water (e-bull-ient). Just like a boiling liquid has high energy and bubbles up, an ebullient individual is bubbling over with cheerful and enthusiastic energy!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.947Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5580, 'english-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Choose the word opposite in meaning to “disdain”.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Respect"},{"id":"B","text":"Anger"},{"id":"C","text":"Pride"},{"id":"D","text":"Doubt"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Antonym questions require tracking down a word that carries the inverse semantic value of the prompt. The word "disdain" operates as both a noun and a verb, representing a total lack of admiration, value, or esteem toward an object, person, or concept deemed inferior.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s look at the definitions to contrast the target word with the multiple choice options:

DISDAIN: The feeling that someone or something is unworthy of one''s consideration, respect, or honor; contempt, scorn, or looking down on someone.

Respect (Antonym): A feeling of deep admiration for someone or something elicited by their abilities, qualities, or achievements. Because this is the direct opposite of viewing someone with worthless contempt, it is the perfect

antonym. Anger: A strong feeling of annoyance, displeasure, or hostility. Pride: A feeling of deep pleasure or satisfaction derived from one''s own achievements or possessions. Doubt: A feeling of uncertainty or lack of conviction.

Thus, "Respect" is the correct opposite.

### Step 3: Final Answer:

The word opposite in meaning to disdain is Respect (Option a).

Quick Tip: Think of the prefix Dis- as indicating a negative direction or reversal. When you display disdain toward someone, you strip away all credit and treat them with zero worth. The ultimate positive inversion of treating someone with value and high regard is showing them respect!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.947Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5581, 'english-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q03', 3, 3, 'A person who collects stamps is called a:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Numismatist"},{"id":"B","text":"Archaeologist"},{"id":"C","text":"Philatelist"},{"id":"D","text":"Linguist"}]', 0, 'C', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This problem belongs to the "One Word Substitution" category, which evaluates knowledge of specialized terminology used to categorize professions, hobbies, and academic fields of expertise.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s define each professional term to trace their respective disciplines: Numismatist: A person who collects, studies, or examines coins, tokens, paper currency, and medals. Archaeologist: A scientist who studies human history and prehistory through the excavation of historical sites and the analysis of physical artifacts. Philatelist (Correct): A person who collects, studies, or values postage stamps, postmarks, and related postal history materials. The term comes from

the Greek roots philos (loving) and ateleia (exemption from tax/prepaid post). Linguist: A person who specializes in the scientific study of human language and its structural or historical developments.

This confirms that choice (c) is the correct term.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

A person who collects stamps is called a Philatelist (Option c).

Quick Tip: Keep this essential pair locked down together in your vocabulary review logs to score easy marks on competitive exams:

• Philatelist = Collects and studies postage stamps.

• Numismatist = Collects and studies currency and coins.

These two hobby terms are among the most frequently tested items in competitive exams!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.947Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5582, 'english-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Choose the correctly spelt word.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Deliterious"},{"id":"B","text":"Deleterious"},{"id":"C","text":"Deletearious"},{"id":"D","text":"Delaterious"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Spelling tests assess knowledge of standard English prefixes and suffix roots. The target word is an advanced formal adjective meaning causing harm or damage. It originates from the classical Greek word délêtios (meaning noxious or destructive).

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s break down the precise syllabic construction of the correct term: The word is assembled in four distinct vocal beats: De - le - te - ri - ous. Notice that the first three syllables are consistently

anchored by the vowel character ''e'': De- (1st), -le- (2nd), and -te- (3rd). It concludes with the standard structural descriptive adjective suffix "-ous". Combining these phonetic blocks gives the layout: D-E-L-E-T-E-R-I-O-U-S.

Let''s review the breakdown errors in the other choices: Option (a) incorrectly swaps the second ''e'' for an ''i'' (Deliterious). Option (c) introduces an incorrect letter ''a'' before the suffix (Deletearious). Option (d) incorrectly substitutes an ''a'' in the second syllable (Delaterious). Hence, option (b) is the only authentic spelling.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correctly spelt word is Deleterious (Option b).

Quick Tip: To remember this word effortlessly, link it directly to the key function "Delete"! If something is deleterious, it is harmful enough that you would want to delete it entirely from your surroundings. Both share the exact same starting block: D-E-L-E-T-E!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.947Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5583, 'english-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q05', 5, 5, 'Fill in the blank with the correct option:

The church bells began to ___ loudly at noon.', '[{"id":"A","text":"peel"},{"id":"B","text":"peal"},{"id":"C","text":"pale"},{"id":"D","text":"poll"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This problem evaluates your command over homophones—words that sound identical when spoken aloud but possess completely distinct meanings, origins, and spelling structures. Resolving the blank requires analyzing the context clue of the sentence ("church bells") to locate the correct verb association.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s look at the operational definitions of all four homophones to find the proper fit: peel: To strip or remove the outer skin, rind, or covering layer from something (e.g., peeling an orange or a potato). This is completely unrelated to sound. peal (Correct): To ring or sound out loudly and continuously. As a noun or verb, it specifically refers to a loud, ringing chime of bells or a sudden echoing crash of thunder (e.g., a peal of thunder, bells pealing). This fits the context perfectly. pale: Lacking intensity of color, headings, or light-colored; or a boundary stake. poll: A record of voting, an election survey, or to cut off the horns/top of something. Therefore, "peal" is the uniquely accurate option.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The complete sentence is: "The church bells began to peal loudly at noon." (Option b)

Quick Tip: To separate these two easily confused words, anchor them to unique visual rhymes:

• You p-e-e-l a banana to e-a-t it.

• A bell will p-e-a-l out an a-u-d-i-o ring.

Connecting the ''a'' in peal to the ''a'' in audio ensures you pick the correct acoustic term every single time!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.947Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5584, 'english-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q06', 6, 6, 'Choose the correct meaning of “petulance”.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Deep wisdom"},{"id":"B","text":"Childish irritability"},{"id":"C","text":"Great courage"},{"id":"D","text":"Honest behaviour"}]', 0, 'B', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Vocabulary questions checking definitions test your knowledge of formal nouns that describe emotional states and behavioral attitudes. The noun "petulance" tracks a specific negative

temperament, often linked to an impatient or moody reaction when an individual fails to get their own way.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the precise meaning of the word alongside the given options: PETULANCE: The quality of being childishly sulky, bad-tempered, bad-mannered, or easily annoyed over trivial matters. It mimics the display of an impatient, angry tantrum. Deep wisdom: An invalid definition; wisdom represents mature discernment and deep understanding, which is the complete opposite of petulance. Childish irritability (Correct): This matches the definition exactly. A petulant person shows sudden, unwarranted annoyance over minor inconveniences, much like a stubborn child throwing a tantrum. Great courage: An invalid choice; courage is bravery in the face of fear or pain. Honest behaviour: An invalid choice; honesty deals with integrity, truthfulness, and transparency.

This confirms that choice (b) is the unique definition matching the term.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct meaning of petulance is Childish irritability (Option b).

Quick Tip: To keep this definition locked in your memory, think of a spoiled, demanding pet throwing a noisy tantrum when it doesn''t get its treats on time! Connecting Pet-ulance to a sulky, annoyed pet will instantly remind you of childish irritability!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.948Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5585, 'english-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q07', 7, 7, 'Select the sentence with the correct usage of “furtherance”.', '[{"id":"A","text":"He spoke in furtherance of the new policy."},{"id":"B","text":"She furtherance the work yesterday."},{"id":"C","text":"The furtherance was quickly running."},{"id":"D","text":"They are furtherance to school."}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

To identify the correct usage of a word, we must understand its definition and its grammatical category (part of speech). The word "furtherance" is an abstract noun that means the advancement, promotion, or fostering of a scheme, plan, interest, or policy. It is standardly used within the idiomatic prepositional phrase "in furtherance of [something]".

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the syntax and grammatical function of the word across the given choices:

Option (a) [Correct]: "He spoke in furtherance of the new policy." Here, "furtherance" functions accurately as a noun inside the prepositional block "in furtherance of," meaning he spoke to help advance or promote the new policy. This is completely correct. Option (b) [Incorrect]: "She furtherance the work yesterday." Here, the word is incorrectly forced into acting as a past-tense verb. The correct verb would be furthered ("She furthered the work..."). Option (c) [Incorrect]: "The furtherance was quickly running." This sentence treats "furtherance" as a concrete noun capable of physical motion (running), which makes no semantic sense. Option (d) [Incorrect]: "They are furtherance to school." This sentence completely violates basic grammar by substituting a noun where a continuous action verb like "going or walking is required.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct sentence is: "He spoke in furtherance of the new policy." (Option a)

Quick Tip: To easily master this word, remember that furtherance is a formal noun that almost always appears in the exact structural format: "in furtherance of + [a goal, cause, or plan]!" Spotting this standard phrase framework will instantly lead you to the right choice on any vocabulary exam!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.948Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5586, 'english-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q08', 8, 8, 'What is a hamlet?', '[{"id":"A","text":"A large kingdom"},{"id":"B","text":"A type of poem"},{"id":"C","text":"A small village"},{"id":"D","text":"A musical instrument"}]', 0, 'C', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

This vocabulary problem assesses knowledge of nouns that specify geographical human settlements based on structural scale, size, and population density.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s define the terms to see how they match up: A large kingdom: An extensive realm or territory ruled entirely by a monarch (king or queen). A type of poem: Irrelevant; while Hamlet is the title of a legendary tragedy written by William Shakespeare, the common noun "Hamlet" is not a poetic form. A small village (Correct): In human geography, a hamlet is a very small human settlement that is structurally smaller than a standard village. Historically, a hamlet is distinguished by lacking its own dedicated parish church or marketplace, often consisting of just a tiny cluster of houses. A musical instrument: An incorrect option with no connection to the word.

Therefore, choice (c) is the accurate definition.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

A hamlet is a small village (Option c).

Quick Tip: Think of the descending hierarchy of human settlements to easily keep these size terms organized in your mind:

 $$ \mathrm{C i t y}\to\mathrm{T o w n}\to\mathrm{V i l l a g e}\to\mathrm{H a m l e t} $$ 

A hamlet sits at the very bottom of the scale as a cozy, tiny cluster of houses out in the countryside!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.949Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5587, 'english-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q09', 9, 9, 'Identify the figure of speech in the expression: “Deafening silence”', '[{"id":"A","text":"Hyperbole"},{"id":"B","text":"Metaphor"},{"id":"C","text":"Oxymoron"},{"id":"D","text":"Personification"}]', 0, 'C', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Figures of speech are literary devices that use language in non-literal ways to create emphasis, imagery, or dramatic effect. When two entirely contradictory or opposite terms are deliberately placed side-by-side to highlight a striking paradox, the literary device is called an oxymoron.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s analyze the mechanics of the target phrase alongside the definitions of the literary figures: Hyperbole: An extreme exaggeration used to add emphasis or humor, not a contradiction (e.g., "I am so hungry I could eat a horse"). Metaphor: A direct comparison between two unrelated things without using comparison words like "like" or "as" (e.g., "Time is a thief"). Oxymoron (Correct): A literary device that couples two completely opposing or conflicting ideas next to each other. In the phrase "Deafening silence", the adjective deafening implies an intensely loud noise, while the noun silence means a total absence of sound. Placing them together emphasizes an intense, heavy, or uncomfortable quietness. Personification: Attributing human characteristics, emotions, or behaviors to non-human objects or abstract ideas (e.g., "The wind whispered through the trees").

This confirms that the phrase is an oxymoron.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The figure of speech used in the expression is an Oxymoron (Option c).

Quick Tip: Whenever you spot a two-word description where the two words completely fight against one another''s literal definitions, you are looking at an Oxymoron! Keep these famous examples in mind for easy recognition:

• Deafening silence

• Open secret

Seriously funny

• Bittersweet memories', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.949Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5588, 'english-2026-05-21-shift-1', 'q10', 10, 10, 'Read the passage and answer the question.

Riya loved reading adventure stories. Every weekend, she visited the library and borrowed two new books. Her friends often asked her for recommendations because she always selected exciting novels.

Why did Riya''s friends ask her for book recommendations?', '[{"id":"A","text":"She wrote books herself"},{"id":"B","text":"She worked in a library"},{"id":"C","text":"She usually chose interesting books"},{"id":"D","text":"She read books in a library"}]', 0, 'C', '### Step 1: Understanding the Concept:

Reading comprehension questions evaluate your ability to identify explicit facts and causal relationships directly stated within a given text. To find the correct answer, look for explicit textual clues like "because" or "since" that link an action to its root cause.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

Let''s locate the exact sentence in the passage that explains the behavior of Riya''s friends: The passage states: "Her friends often asked her for recommendations because she always selected exciting novels." The word "because" marks the direct cause behind their requests. The text explicitly tells us that her friends valued her taste because she always picked out "exciting novels". Let''s check how the options match this fact: Option (a) claims she wrote books, which is never mentioned in the passage. Option (b) claims she worked in a library, but the text only says she visited the library as a regular reader. Option (c) states that she usually chose interesting books. The word "interesting" is a direct, accurate synonym for the word "exciting" used in the text.

Therefore, option (c) is the only factually correct choice supported by the text.

### Step 3: Final Answer:

Riya''s friends asked her for book recommendations because she usually chose interesting books (Option c).

Quick Tip: In reading comprehension, remember this golden rule: Stick strictly to the provided text! Avoid making outside guesses or assumptions. Even if an option sounds plausible (like working in a library), if it isn''t explicitly written in the passage, it is incorrect. Always trace the "because" keyword to find your answer!', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.949Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5589, 'english-2026-05-21-shift-2', 'q02', 1, 1, '"Beat a dead horse" means:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Continue useless effort"},{"id":"B","text":"Repeat an already settled matter"},{"id":"C","text":"Encourage someone"},{"id":"D","text":"Waste time on something hopeless"}]', 0, 'D', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question requires us to determine the correct idiomatic meaning of the common phrase "beat a dead horse".

We must select the option that best captures the figurative essence of the expression.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Literal Origin: The phrase "beat a dead horse" (or "flog a dead horse") originates from the literal, futile act of beating a horse that is already dead to try to make it pull a load. Because a deceased animal cannot move, any effort spent beating it is entirely useless and a waste of physical energy.

• Figurative Meaning: In modern English usage, this idiom is used metaphorically to describe a situation where someone continues to expend time, energy, or resources on an issue that has already been resolved, cannot be changed, or is completely hopeless.

• Evaluating Option (A): "Continue useless effort" is a general description of the idiom but lacks the specific context of dealing with a completed or hopeless matter.

• Evaluating Option (B): "Repeat an already settled matter" represents one common scenario of this idiom, particularly when someone brings up an argument that has already been decided.

• Evaluating Option (D): "Waste time on something hopeless" is the most comprehensive definition of the idiom, as it captures both the futility and the complete lack of hope associated with the action.

• Evaluating Option (C): "Encourage someone" is entirely unrelated to the concept of uselessness or waste of effort and is therefore incorrect.

Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the most accurate overall meaning is (D) Waste time on something hopeless.

Quick Tip: Visualize the literal scenario of the idiom to deduce its meaning.

Since beating a horse that is already dead can never produce any helpful result, the idiom naturally represents doing something completely hopeless and wasteful.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.950Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5590, 'english-2026-05-21-shift-2', 'q04', 2, 2, '"Stand out" means:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Be noticeable"},{"id":"B","text":"Be hidden"},{"id":"C","text":"Be prominent"},{"id":"D","text":"Be ordinary"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This question tests the understanding of the phrasal verb "stand out".

We need to find the option that correctly explains what it means when an object or person stands out.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Literal and Figurative Definition: The phrasal verb "stand out" means to project outward or to be extremely easy to see or notice because of being different, better, or more distinctive than other surrounding elements.

• Analyzing Option (A) ''Be noticeable'': This is the primary and most common meaning. When someone or something stands out, they draw attention and are easily noticed by observers.

• Analyzing Option (B) ''Be hidden'': This is the direct antonym of standing out, as hiding involves blending in or escaping notice.

• Analyzing Option (C) ''Be prominent'': "Be prominent" is also a strong synonym that is correct in many contexts. However, "be noticeable" is the core, everyday meaning of the phrase that is widely accepted in basic definitions.

• Analyzing Option (D) ''Be ordinary'': Being ordinary is the opposite of standing out, as standing out implies having an extraordinary, unique, or distinct quality that differentiates oneself from the crowd.

Step 3: Final Answer:

Thus, the phrasal verb "stand out" is correctly defined as (A) Be noticeable.

Quick Tip: To verify the meaning of a phrasal verb, substitute the option directly into a sentence. For example: "Her bright yellow jacket made her stand out in the crowd" is equivalent to "Her bright yellow jacket made her noticeable in the crowd."', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.950Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5591, 'english-2026-05-21-shift-2', 'q05', 3, 3, 'Choose the correctly spelled words.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Mischievous"},{"id":"B","text":"Mischievous"},{"id":"C","text":"Entrepreneur"},{"id":"D","text":"Enterpreneur"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The task is to identify the correctly spelled words from the given list.

This question tests spelling knowledge of words that are frequently misspelled in written

English due to pronunciation errors or foreign origins.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Analyzing Option (A) and (B): The word meaning playfully troublesome or harmful is spelled "mischievous" (m-i-s-c-h-i-e-v-o-u-s). Many people mistakenly pronounce this word as having four syllables ("mis-chee-vee-us"), which leads to the common incorrect spelling "mischievious" with an extra "i". Thus, Option (A) is correct and (B) is incorrect.

• Analyzing Option (C) and (D): The word for an individual who starts a business, taking on financial risks, is spelled "entrepreneur" (e-n-t-r-e-p-r-e-n-e-u-r). Because it is a French loanword, it is highly prone to misspelling. Option (D) "Enterpreneur" contains spelling errors at the beginning ("enter" instead of "entre") and end ("nuer" instead of "neur"). Thus, Option (C) is correct.

- Conclusion: Both Option (A) and Option (C) represent correctly spelled words.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correctly spelled words are (A) Mischievous and (C) Entrepreneur.

Quick Tip: For "mischievous", remember it has only three syllables: mis-chie-vous. Keeping this pronunciation in mind will prevent you from writing the extra "i" in "mischievous".

For French loanwords like "entrepreneur", break them down into syllables: en-tre-pre-neur.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.950Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5592, 'english-2026-05-21-shift-2', 'q06', 4, 4, 'Choose the correct pair.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Pale – light in color"},{"id":"B","text":"Pail-bucket"},{"id":"C","text":"Peel-remove skin"},{"id":"D","text":"Pale – fruit skin"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This question evaluates homophones, spelling, and vocabulary definitions.

We must examine each word and its corresponding definition to identify the correct pairing.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Homophones and Vocabulary: Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and definitions. Here, we are looking at "pale", "pail", and "peel".

• Analyzing Option (A): "Pale" is an adjective that means light in color, lacking intensity, or whitish. Therefore, "Pale - light in color" is a correct pair.

• Analyzing Option (B): "Pail" is a noun referring to a cylindrical container with a handle, commonly used to carry liquids; it is indeed a bucket. This is also a correct pair.

• Analyzing Option (C): "Peel" is a verb that means to strip or remove the outer skin of a fruit or vegetable. This is also a correct pair.

• Analyzing Option (D): "Pale" paired with "fruit skin" is incorrect because the outer skin of a fruit is called a "peel", not "pale".

• Question Nuance: Because options (A), (B), and (C) are all technically correct pairs, this question might have been designed with a typo, or meant to ask "Choose the incorrect pair", which would make (D) the answer. However, interpreting it as written, we highlight (A) as a primary correct pair.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct pair is (A) Pale – light in color (with B and C also being valid definitions).

Quick Tip: Always pay close attention to homophones in writing section exams.

To remember: "Pail" contains "ai" like "rain", which goes inside a bucket. "Pale" refers to a color shade (lacking color).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.950Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5593, 'english-2026-05-21-shift-2', 'q08', 5, 5, 'Choose the correct active/passive voice conversions.', '[{"id":"A","text":"The letter was written by him."},{"id":"B","text":"She sings a song."},{"id":"C","text":"A song is sung by her."},{"id":"D","text":"The work completed yesterday."}]', 0, 'B', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This question requires us to identify active and passive voice sentences that form a correct grammatical conversion pair.

We will analyze the structure of each sentence to find the active-passive relationship.

### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Active and Passive Voice Rules: In an active sentence, the subject performs the action on the object. In a passive sentence, the object becomes the subject, the verb is modified with a form of "be" and a past participle, and the original subject is added as an agent after "by".

• Analyzing Option (B) ''She sings a song'': This is a grammatically correct active voice sentence in the Simple Present tense.

- Subject: "She"

- Verb: "sings" (Simple Present)

- Object: "a song"

• Analyzing Option (C) ''A song is sung by her'': This is the correct passive conversion of Option (B).

- The object "a song" becomes the passive subject.

- The singular present tense auxiliary "is" matches the simple present verb "sings".

- The past participle of "sing" is "sung".

- The pronoun "she" becomes the object pronoun "her" following "by".

- Thus, (B) and (C) represent a complete, correct conversion pair.

• Analyzing Option (A): "The letter was written by him" is a valid passive sentence, but its active counterpart ("He wrote the letter") is not listed here to form a pair.

• Analyzing Option (D): "The work completed yesterday" is grammatically incomplete in the passive voice; it should be "The work was completed yesterday".

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct active/passive voice conversion pair is (B) She sings a song. and (C) A song is sung by her.

Quick Tip: To convert an active sentence to passive, always match the tense of the auxiliary verb to the tense of the main verb in the active sentence.

Simple Present (sings) must always use present auxiliaries (is/am/are) + past participle (sung).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.951Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5594, 'english-2026-05-21-shift-2', 'q10', 6, 6, 'Fill in the blanks with suitable phrasal verbs.

A. Please _____ the meeting till tomorrow.

B. Good friends always _____ each other.

C. Her talent helped her _____ in the competition.

D. We should never _____ others.', '[{"id":"A","text":"A - put off, B - stand by, C - stand out, D - look down on"},{"id":"B","text":"A - stand by, B - put off, C - look down on, D - stand out"},{"id":"C","text":"A - stand out, B - look down on, C - put off, D - stand by"},{"id":"D","text":"A - look down on, B - stand out, C - stand by, D - put off"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This question requires us to match the correct phrasal verbs to the blank spaces in the sentences to make them grammatically correct and contextually meaningful.

We will define each phrasal verb and match them systematically.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Analyzing Sentence A: "Please _____ the meeting till tomorrow."

- The sentence refers to shifting a meeting to another day.

- The phrasal verb "put off" means to postpone or delay an event.

- Therefore, Blank A matches with "put off".

• Analyzing Sentence B: "Good friends always _____ each other."

- This sentence describes a characteristic of loyal friends.

- The phrasal verb "stand by" means to support someone or remain loyal to them, especially during difficult times.

- Therefore, Blank B matches with "stand by".

• Analyzing Sentence C: "Her talent helped her ___ in the competition."

- The sentence describes how a competitor''s talent helped her perform exceptionally well.

- The phrasal verb "stand out" means to be prominent, superior, or easily noticed compared to others.

- Therefore, Blank C matches with "stand out".

• Analyzing Sentence D: "We should never _____ others."

- This sentence states a moral rule about how to treat people.

- The phrasal verb "look down on" means to think of or treat someone as inferior.

- Therefore, Blank D matches with "look down on".

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct matching sequence is A - put off, B - stand by, C - stand out, and D - look down on, which corresponds to Option (A).

Quick Tip: When dealing with multiple-choice matching questions, solve the easiest phrasal verb first. Postponing a meeting is a very common context for "put off", which lets you quickly eliminate options that do not start with "put off".', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.951Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5595, 'english-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q01', 1, 1, '"Recalcitrant" means:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Obedient"},{"id":"B","text":"Stubborn"},{"id":"C","text":"Resistant"},{"id":"D","text":"Defiant"}]', 0, 'D', '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks us to identify the correct meaning of the vocabulary word "recalcitrant" from the given multiple-choice options.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Definition of Recalcitrant: The word "recalcitrant" is an adjective that describes a

person who has an obstinately uncooperative attitude toward authority or discipline.

• Etymology: It originates from the Latin word "recalcitrare", which literally translates to "to kick back" (from "re-" meaning back and "calcitrare" meaning to kick, from "calx" meaning heel).

• Analyzing Option (A) - Obedient: This is the exact antonym of recalcitrant, as an obedient person complies with rules or authority.

• Analyzing Option (B) - Stubborn: While "stubborn" is a close synonym representing determination not to change one''s mind, it is more general and does not necessarily imply active resistance to authority.

• Analyzing Option (C) - Resistant: This refers to offering resistance to something, but it is less descriptive of an active, rebellious behavioral attitude compared to other options.

• Analyzing Option (D) - Defiant: This term refers to showing open resistance and bold disobedience to authority, which captures the rebellious, active "kicking back" connotation of recalcitrant most accurately.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, while "stubborn" and "resistant" are near-synonyms, "defiant" is the most precise and contextually appropriate option that matches the core definition of "recalcitrant".

Quick Tip: When faced with multiple closely related synonyms in vocabulary questions, look closely at the etymological root of the word.

"Recalcitrant" contains the root related to kicking back against authority, which aligns perfectly with being "defiant".

Learning roots, prefixes, and suffixes can help you distinguish between subtle differences in synonyms.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.952Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5596, 'english-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q02', 2, 2, '"Make up" means:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Invent a story"},{"id":"B","text":"Reconcile"},{"id":"C","text":"Apply cosmetics"},{"id":"D","text":"Break friendship"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This question asks for the definition of the highly versatile phrasal verb "make up".

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Nature of Phrasal Verbs: Phrasal verbs in English often carry multiple idiomatic meanings depending on the context in which they are used.

• Analyzing Option (A) - Invent a story: "Make up" commonly means to fabricate or invent a story, excuse, or explanation. For instance, "He had to make up an excuse for being late to class." This is a highly frequent usage tested in competitive exams.

• Analyzing Option (B) - Reconcile: "Make up" also means to restore friendly relations after a quarrel or disagreement. For example, "After their argument, they decided to make up and move forward."

• Analyzing Option (C) - Apply cosmetics: This is another literal/common meaning, referring to applying make-up or cosmetics to the face. For example, "The actors need to make up before going on stage."

• Analyzing Option (D) - Break friendship: This is incorrect, as "break up" or "fall out" would represent ending a relationship or friendship.

- Note on Exam Context: Because options (A), (B), and (C) are all grammatically and semantically valid definitions of "make up", the question represents a polysemous word scenario. In standard answer keys where a single option is circled, (A) is often designated as the primary figurative translation, but a comprehensive understanding recognizes the validity of (A), (B), and (C).

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the primary idiomatic meaning highlighted in standard assessments is option (A), though options (B) and (C) are also linguistically correct.

Quick Tip: Phrasal verbs are highly contextual and often have multiple disparate meanings.

Create a mind map for common phrasal verbs like "make up", "bring up", "take off", and "go over" to memorize their various uses.

Always eliminate the option that is definitively anonymous or unrelated, such as "break friendship" in this case.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.952Z', NULL, NULL, 'banner', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5597, 'english-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q03', 3, 3, 'Identify the nouns.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Progeny"},{"id":"B","text":"Entrepreneur"},{"id":"C","text":"Mischievous"},{"id":"D","text":"Furtherance"}]', 0, 'A', '##### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question requires us to analyze a list of words and identify which of them function as nouns in standard English grammar.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Option (A) - Progeny: This is a noun referring to the offspring or descendants of a person, animal, or plant. For example, "They were proud of their progeny." Since it represents a category of living beings, it is classified as a noun.

• Option (B) - Entrepreneur: This is a noun representing a person who sets up a business or businesses, taking on financial risks in the hope of profit. The suffix "-eur" typically denotes an agent or person performing an action, confirming its status as a noun.

• Option (C) - Mischievous: This is an adjective, not a noun. It describes a person, animal, or behavior that is playfully malicious or causes minor trouble. The suffix "-ous" is a classic adjective-forming suffix. For example, "The mischievous puppy chewed the shoe."

• Option (D) - Furtherance: This is an abstract noun meaning the advancement or promotion of a scheme, plan, or interest. The suffix "-ance" is a standard suffix used to convert verbs (to further) into abstract nouns (furtherance).

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the nouns in the list are options (A) Progeny, (B) Entrepreneur, and (D) Furtherance, whereas option (C) is an adjective.

Quick Tip: Identifying parts of speech is simplified by analyzing word suffixes.

Suffixes like "-ance", "-ence", "-er", "-or", "-eur", and "-ity" almost always indicate nouns.

Suffixes like "-ous", "-ful", "-less", and "-ive" generally indicate adjectives.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.952Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5598, 'english-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Fill in the blanks with suitable words:

A. The teacher asked the students to _____ attention in class.

B. She was _____ with joy after hearing the result.

C. His remarks were completely _____ and offensive.

D. The scientist made a remarkable _____ in medicine.', '[{"id":"A","text":"A - pay, B - elated, C - inappropriate, D - breakthrough"},{"id":"B","text":"A - elated, B - pay, C - breakthrough, D - inappropriate"},{"id":"C","text":"A - inappropriate, B - breakthrough, C - pay, D - elated"},{"id":"D","text":"A - breakthrough, B - inappropriate, C - elated, D - pay"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks us to fill in the blanks of four independent sentences using the most appropriate vocabulary words from the options provided.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Analyzing Sentence A: "The teacher asked the students to _____ attention in class."

In English, "pay attention" is a fixed collocation meaning to listen, watch, or focus on something carefully. Thus, "pay" is the correct verb here.

• Analyzing Sentence B: "She was ___ with joy after hearing the result." The prepositional phrase "with joy" requires an adjective that describes an intense state of

happiness. "Elated", which means extremely happy or proud, fits perfectly.

• Analyzing Sentence C: "His remarks were completely ___ and offensive." The coordinating conjunction "and" connects words of similar grammatical function and tone. "Offensive" is a negative adjective, so we need another negative adjective. "Inappropriate" perfectly complements "offensive".

• Analyzing Sentence D: "The scientist made a remarkable ___ in medicine." The verb phrase "made a remarkable..." requires a noun that denotes a major discovery or progress. "Breakthrough" means an important discovery or event that helps to solve a situation or provide progress, making it the ideal fit.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, matching each sentence with its correct vocabulary term yields the sequence: A - pay, B - elated, C - inappropriate, and D - breakthrough, corresponding to option (A).

Quick Tip: In fill-in-the-blank questions involving multiple sentences, start with the absolute certainties. Common collocations like "pay attention" or "make a breakthrough" can be solved instantly, allowing you to use the process of elimination for trickier sentences.

Check that the selected words match the grammatical parts of speech required by each blank.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.952Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5599, 'english-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q05', 5, 5, 'Match the figures of speech:

Column A Column B

A. Simile 1. Repetition of consonant sounds

B. Metaphor 2. Direct comparison

C. Alliteration 3. Human qualities to objects

D. Personification 4. Implied comparison', '[{"id":"A","text":"A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3"},{"id":"B","text":"A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4"},{"id":"C","text":"A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1"},{"id":"D","text":"A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2"}]', 0, 'A', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question requires us to match classical figures of speech in Column A with their correct definitions listed in Column B.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Analyzing Simile (A): A simile is a figure of speech that directly compares two different things using comparative words such as "like" or "as". Therefore, it matches description 2, "Direct comparison". For example, "as brave as a lion."

• Analyzing Metaphor (B): A metaphor is a figure of speech that makes an implicit, implied, or hidden comparison between two things that are unrelated but share some common characteristics. Unlike similes, it does not use "like" or "as". Thus, it matches description 4, "Implied comparison". For example, "time is a thief."

• Analyzing Alliteration (C): Alliteration is the literary device where the same consonant sound is repeated at the beginning of adjacent or closely connected words in a sentence. This matches description 1, "Repetition of consonant sounds". For example, "Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers."

• Analyzing Personification (D): Personification is a figurative device in which non-human things, objects, or abstract ideas are given human traits, emotions, or intentions. This matches description 3, "Human qualities to objects". For example, "the wind whispered through the trees."

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the correct matching is A matches with 2, B with 4, C with 1, and D with 3, which corresponds to option (A).

Quick Tip: Remember that a simile always makes an explicit direct comparison using "like" or "as", whereas a metaphor makes an indirect or implied comparison without these words.

Alliteration concerns the repetition of consonant sounds specifically at the start of words, which is easy to spot visually.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.952Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5600, 'english-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q06', 6, 6, 'Identify the correctly spelt word from the options given below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Innocuos"},{"id":"B","text":"Innoccuous"},{"id":"C","text":"Innocuous"},{"id":"D","text":"Innocous"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This spelling-based question asks us to identify the standard, correct spelling of a specific vocabulary word meaning harmless or safe.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Definition and Use: The word "innocuous" is an adjective used to describe something that is not harmful, offensive, or injurious. For example, "The medicine was completely innocuous, having no side effects."

• Etymological Roots: It comes from the classical Latin "innocuus", formed by combining the prefix "in-" (meaning "not") and the root adjective "nocuus" (meaning "harmful", derived from the verb "nocere", which means "to hurt").

• Analyzing Option (A) - Innocuos: This spelling incorrectly omits the letter ''u'' before the ending letter ''o'', which disrupts the correct standard pronunciation and orthography.

• Analyzing Option (B) - Innoccuous: This spelling introduces an incorrect double ''c''. While the word has a double ''n'' near the beginning, it only contains a single ''c'' before the suffix.

• Analyzing Option (C) - Innocuous: This is the perfectly correct orthographic form, maintaining the double ''n'', single ''c'', and the correct "-uous" suffix.

• Analyzing Option (D) - Innocous: This spelling is severely misspelled, missing both the double ''n'' structure and the correct vowel configuration in the suffix.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the only correctly spelled option representing the standard English dictionary form of the word is option (C).

Quick Tip: To master spelling words like "innocuous", break them down by their Latin roots: "in-" (not) + "nocere" (harm) + adjective suffix "-ous".

Be mindful of words that feature double consonants; "innocuous" has a double ''n'' but a single ''c''.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.953Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5601, 'english-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q07', 7, 7, 'Fill in the blanks with suitable vocabulary words:

A. The _____ child refused to obey anyone.

B. The king sought wise _____ from his ministers.

C. The explorer made a great _____ in science.

D. Her speech created a strong _____ on the audience.', '[{"id":"A","text":"A - recalcitrant, B - counsel, C - breakthrough, D - impression"},{"id":"B","text":"A - counsel, B - recalcitrant, C - impression, D - breakthrough"},{"id":"C","text":"A - breakthrough, B - impression, C - recalcitrant, D - counsel"},{"id":"D","text":"A - impression, B - breakthrough, C - counsel, D - recalcitrant"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question tests contextual vocabulary usage by asking us to fill in the blanks of four sentences with the most suitable words from the list.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Analyzing Sentence A: "The ___ child refused to obey anyone." The blank requires an adjective describing a child who is stubbornly disobedient. "Recalcitrant" means having an obstinately uncooperative attitude toward authority, which fits this context perfectly.

• Analyzing Sentence B: "The king sought wise _____ from his ministers." In political and historical contexts, rulers seek advice or guidance. "Counsel" functions as a noun meaning advisory advice, making it the correct term.

• Analyzing Sentence C: "The explorer made a great _____ in science." In academic and scientific fields, a major discovery or advancement is referred to as a "breakthrough". Thus, "breakthrough" is the correct noun for this blank.

• Analyzing Sentence D: "Her speech created a strong ___ on the audience." The idiom "to create an impression on someone" means to leave a lasting impact or positive

effect. This makes "impression" the correct choice here.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the correct mapping of words to their respective blanks is A - recalcitrant, B - counsel, C - breakthrough, and D - impression, which matches option (A).

Quick Tip: Differentiate between "counsel" (advice/to advise) and "council" (a group of people gathered for consultation).

In fill-in-the-blank vocabulary matches, cross-reference words with the syntactic structure of the sentences (e.g., checking if a noun, adjective, or verb is required).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.953Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5602, 'english-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q08', 8, 8, 'Fill in the blanks with correct prepositions:

A. She is fond _____ music.

B. He apologized _____ his mistake.

C. The train arrived _____ time.

D. They were divided _____ two groups.', '[{"id":"A","text":"A - of, B - for, C - on, D - into"},{"id":"B","text":"A - for, B - of, C - into, D - on"},{"id":"C","text":"A - on, B - into, C - of, D - for"},{"id":"D","text":"A - into, B - on, C - for, D - of"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This question tests our command of English prepositions by requiring us to complete four sentences using correct fixed prepositions and common prepositional phrases.

##### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Analyzing Sentence A: "She is fond _____ music." The adjective "fond" is always paired with the fixed preposition "of" when indicating a liking or affection for something. Thus, "of" is the correct choice.

• Analyzing Sentence B: "He apologized ___ his mistake." The verb "apologize" takes different prepositions depending on the object. You apologize "to" a person, but you apologize "for" an action or mistake. Therefore, "for" is correct here.

• Analyzing Sentence C: "The train arrived ___ time." The phrase "on time" is an idiomatic prepositional phrase meaning punctual or according to the schedule, which is standard in English usage.

• Analyzing Sentence D: "They were divided ___ two groups." When splitting a whole entity into separate component parts or groups, the verb "divide" is followed by the preposition "into".

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Combining these matches results in the sequence A - of, B - for, C - on, and D - into, which perfectly aligns with option (A).

Quick Tip: Prepositions are often "fixed" in English, meaning they must follow specific verbs, nouns, or adjectives regardless of literal meaning.

Memorize preposition pairs like "fond of", "apologize for", and "depend on" to solve grammar questions quickly.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.953Z', NULL, NULL, 'banner', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5603, 'english-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q09', 9, 9, 'Match the grammar terms:

Column A Column B

A. Noun 1. Describes noun

B. Verb 2. Action word

C. Adjective 3. Naming word

D. Adverb 4. Describes verb', '[{"id":"A","text":"A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4"},{"id":"B","text":"A-1, B-4, C-2, D-3"},{"id":"C","text":"A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1"},{"id":"D","text":"A-4, B-1, C-3, D-2"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

This matching question tests the fundamental definitions of the primary parts of speech in English grammar.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Analyzing Noun (A): A noun is a word that functions as the name of some specific thing or set of things, such as living creatures, objects, places, actions, qualities, states of existence, or ideas. Therefore, it is commonly defined as a "Naming word", matching with 3.

• Analyzing Verb (B): A verb is a word used to describe an action, state, or occurrence. It forms the main grammatical engine of a sentence. It is characterized as an "Action word", matching with 2.

• Analyzing Adjective (C): An adjective is a word that modifies, qualifies, or describes a noun or a pronoun by providing more details about its size, color, quantity, or qualities.

Thus, it "Describes noun", matching with 1.

• Analyzing Adverb (D): An adverb is a word that modifies or describes a verb, an adjective, or another adverb, often indicating how, when, where, or to what extent an action is performed. Therefore, it "Describes verb", matching with 4.

Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct matches are A-3, B-2, C-1, and D-4, which corresponds to option (A).

Quick Tip: Understanding the basic definitions of parts of speech is essential for sentence structure analysis and syntax error detection.

Always remember that adjectives describe nouns, while adverbs describe verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.953Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5604, 'english-2026-05-22-shift-2', 'q10', 10, 10, 'Choose the option that contains the correctly spelt word:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Accomodation"},{"id":"B","text":"Accommodation"},{"id":"C","text":"Accommodation"},{"id":"D","text":"Accommodation"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question presents four spelling variations of the same word and asks us to identify the standard, correct spelling.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Word Definition: "Accommodation" is a noun referring to a room, group of rooms, or building in which someone may live or stay. It can also mean a convenient arrangement, agreement, or compromise.

• Analyzing the Correct Spelling: The standard dictionary spelling of the word is "Accommodation". It contains a double ''c'' and a double ''m''.

• Analyzing Common Pitfalls: This word is highly notorious in English spelling tests because people frequently fail to include both sets of double letters.

• Analyzing Option (A) - Accomodation: This option is incorrect as it contains a double ''c'' but misses the double ''m'' (it has only a single ''m'').

• Analyzing Option (B) - Accommodation: This option is incorrect because it contains a double ''m'' but misses the double ''c'' (having only a single ''c'').

• Analyzing Option (D) - Accommodation: This option is incorrect because it changes the ''o'' to a ''u'', which is phonetically and orthographically wrong.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the only option with the correct double ''c'' and double ''m'' spelling is option (C).

Quick Tip: To easily memorize the spelling of "accommodation", use this simple mnemonic memory aid:

"Accommodation is big enough to accommodate two cats (cc) and two mice (mm)." This will help you remember to include both double letters every time.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.954Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5605, 'english-2026-05-23-shift-2', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Choose the option that best defines the vocabulary word: Meticulous', '[{"id":"A","text":"Highly critical of others and easily dissatisfied."},{"id":"B","text":"Operating at a very fast and efficient pace."},{"id":"C","text":"Showing great attention to detail; very careful and precise."},{"id":"D","text":"Hesitant to make decisions due to fear of failure."}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: Vocabulary acquisition requires understanding subtle contextual nuances. The word meticulous originates from the Latin word for fearful, but over time its meaning shifted to describe someone who is overly careful, scrupulous, and exceptionally precise about small details.

### Step 1: Analyzing the options based on standard definitions.

While a meticulous person might notice minor flaws, option (A) incorrectly emphasizes a critical attitude toward others. Option (B) contradicts the typical nature of meticulousness, which often requires taking one''s time rather than rushing. Option (D) confuses precision with indecisiveness. Therefore, option (C) is the most accurate definition.

Quick Tip: To remember meticulous, connect it with the word metric. A meticulous person measures everything down to the exact metric millimeter.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.954Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5606, 'english-2026-05-23-shift-2', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Complete the sentence with the correct homonym pair: The project manager visited the construction ___ to check if the new structure was within the line of ___.', '[{"id":"A","text":"sight, site"},{"id":"B","text":"site, sight"},{"id":"C","text":"cite, sight"},{"id":"D","text":"site, cite"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: Homonyms and confusable words sound similar but have distinct spellings and meanings:

- Site: A physical location, place, or ground area used for a specific purpose.

• Sight: The faculty or act of seeing; a view within a field of vision.

Cite: To quote, mention, or refer to a source as an authority.

Step 1: Evaluating the context of each blank.

The first blank requires a physical place where construction happens, which corresponds to site. The second blank refers to the field of vision or view, which corresponds to sight. This makes site, sight the only correct sequence.

Quick Tip: Remember that S-i-t-e is linked to Situation/Space (location), while S-i-g-h-t contains g-h just like the word Li-g-h-t (which is required to see).

___', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.955Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5607, 'english-2026-05-23-shift-2', 'q03', 3, 3, 'What is the meaning of the idiom: "Spill the beans"?', '[{"id":"A","text":"To make a chaotic mess or cause an accident."},{"id":"B","text":"To reveal secret information unintentionally or indiscreetly."},{"id":"C","text":"To waste valuable resources on a useless project."},{"id":"D","text":"To speak aggregate lies to protect someone."}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: Idioms are phrases where the figurative meaning cannot be understood literally from the individual words. "Spill the beans" historically relates to ancient voting systems where colored beans were dropped into jars to cast secret ballots; prematurely dropping or spilling them exposed the hidden results.

Step 1: Differentiating figurative meaning from literal expressions.

Options (A) and (C) mistake the physical action of spilling for a literal or financial mess. Option (D) suggests lying, which is the opposite of exposing a hidden truth. Thus, option (B) is the accurate idiomatic meaning.

Quick Tip: When a secret slips out unexpectedly, imagine a container breaking open—the secret information "spills out" into the open for everyone to see.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.955Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5608, 'english-2026-05-23-shift-2', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Choose the option that is an antonym for the word: Loquacious', '[{"id":"A","text":"Garrulous"},{"id":"B","text":"Verbose"},{"id":"C","text":"Articulate"},{"id":"D","text":"Taciturn"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept: Antonym questions require identifying words with directly opposite semantic meanings. Loquacious means extremely talkative or wordy.

Step 1: Analyzing the definitions of the options.

Garrulous and Verbose are synonyms for loquacious, as they describe being overly talkative or using too many words.

• Articulate means fluent and clear in speech, which describes quality rather than quantity.

- Taciturn refers to a person who is naturally reserved, quiet, and says very little.

Because taciturn is the direct opposite of talkative, it is the correct antonym.

Quick Tip: Associate Loqua- with Eloquence and speech (talking a lot), and associate Taciturn with being Silent or tacit.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.955Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5609, 'english-2026-05-23-shift-2', 'q05', 5, 5, 'Complete the sentence using the correct phrasal verb of ''Put'': We had to ___ our travel plans because of the sudden heavy rainfall.', '[{"id":"A","text":"put up with"},{"id":"B","text":"put off"},{"id":"C","text":"put out"},{"id":"D","text":"put across"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: Phrasal verbs combine a base verb with a preposition or particle to form a completely new idiomatic meaning.

• Put off: To postpone or delay an event to a future time.

• Put up with: To tolerate or endure something unpleasant.

• Put out: To extinguish a fire, or to cause someone inconvenience.

• Put across: To communicate or express an idea clearly.

Step 1: Matching the context to the correct particle.

The context indicates that travel plans had to be delayed due to bad weather. Therefore, the phrasal verb meaning "to postpone" (put off) is required.

Quick Tip: Think of "off" as pushing something away into the future distance—hence, put off means to postpone.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.955Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5610, 'english-2026-05-23-shift-2', 'q06', 6, 6, 'Which of the following sentences uses the vocabulary word Abstruse correctly?', '[{"id":"A","text":"She was so abstruse in her spending that she ran out of savings within a month."},{"id":"B","text":"The instructions were clear and abstruse, allowing us to build the desk in ten minutes."},{"id":"C","text":"The professor’s lecture on quantum physics was so abstruse that only a few advanced students could follow it."},{"id":"D","text":"He has an abstruse personality and makes friends everywhere he goes."}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: The adjective abstruse is used to describe abstract concepts, theories, texts, or subjects that are profound, complex, obscure, and incredibly difficult for an average mind to grasp or comprehend.

Step 1: Checking contextual alignment for each option.

In option (A), reckless spending should be described as extravagant. In option (B), "clear and abstruse" is a contradiction because clear instructions cannot be obscure. In option (D), an open, friendly personality should be called gregarious or affable. Option (C) uses the word perfectly to describe a dense, complex scientific lecture.

Quick Tip: Think of Abs-truse as something that feels Abs-tract and hard to understand.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.955Z', NULL, NULL, 'banner', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5611, 'english-2026-05-23-shift-2', 'q07', 7, 7, 'Identify the correct preposition to complete the sentence: He has been working on this data analysis project ___ last Monday.', '[{"id":"A","text":"for"},{"id":"B","text":"from"},{"id":"C","text":"since"},{"id":"D","text":"by"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: In the Present Perfect Continuous tense structure (has/have been + verb-ing), specific prepositions connect time markers:

• Since: Used to specify a precise, definite starting point in time.

• For: Used to indicate a duration or overall length of time.

Step 1: Determining if the time marker is a point or a duration.

"Last Monday" is a specific day and a definite point on the calendar where the action commenced.

rather than a total count of days or hours. Therefore, since is the grammatically correct preposition.

Quick Tip: Use Since for specific calendar dates, days, years, or times (e.g., since 2021, since 9 AM). Use For when you can count the units of time (e.g., for 5 days, for 3 hours).', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.956Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5612, 'english-2026-05-23-shift-2', 'q08', 8, 8, 'Find the correctly spelled word from the options below:', '[{"id":"A","text":"Extravegant"},{"id":"B","text":"Extravagent"},{"id":"C","text":"Exstravagant"},{"id":"D","text":"Extravagant"}]', 0, 'D', 'Concept: Spelling accuracy requires identifying core root letters and silent phonetic transitions. The term stems from the Latin extravagari, combining extra (outside) and vagari (to wander).

Step 1: Breaking down common orthographic errors.

Option (A) mistakenly uses an ''e'' after the ''v''. Option (B) replaces the regular noun/adjective suffix ending -ant with -ent. Option (C) adds an unnecessary phonetic ''s'' following the ''E''. The authentic, standard spelling is Extravagant.

Quick Tip: Break the word down phonetically into three simple chunks that all contain the vowel ''a'': Extra + vag + ant.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.956Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5613, 'english-2026-05-23-shift-2', 'q09', 9, 9, 'Identify the correct tense structure to fill the blank: By the time the teacher entered the classroom, the students _____ writing their notes.', '[{"id":"A","text":"have finished"},{"id":"B","text":"will finish"},{"id":"C","text":"had finished"},{"id":"D","text":"finish"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: When two separate actions occurred sequentially in the past, the past perfect tense (had + past participle) must be used to express the action that completed first, while the simple past tense handles the action that occurred later.

Step 1: Establishing the sequence of events in the past.

The sentence establishes two actions: the teacher entering the room (entered - Simple Past) and the students completing their writing. Because the students completed their work before the teacher entered, the earlier action must use the past perfect form: had finished.

Quick Tip: Whenever a sentence contains "By the time" followed by a past tense verb, look for the had + V3 structure in the corresponding clause.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.956Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5614, 'english-2026-05-24-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Choose the word closest in meaning to "offspring."', '[{"id":"A","text":"Ancestors"},{"id":"B","text":"Progeny"},{"id":"C","text":"Heritage"},{"id":"D","text":"Relatives"}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks to find the synonym of the word “offspring” among the given options.

Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Define each word to determine which one is closest in meaning to the target word.

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- “Offspring” refers to a person’s child or children, or the young of an animal.

- Option (A) “Ancestors” refers to people from whom one is descended, which is the opposite of offspring.

- Option (B) “Progeny” refers to a descendant or descendants of a person, animal, or plant, making it a direct synonym for offspring.

- Option (C) “Heritage” refers to valued objects and qualities passed down through generations, such as historic buildings or cultural traditions.

- Option (D) “Relatives” refers to people connected by blood or marriage, which is a much broader term.

Step 4: Final Answer:

Therefore, the word closest in meaning to “offspring” is “Progeny”.

Quick Tip: In vocabulary questions, look at the prefix and suffix of the words. For example, “pro-” in progeny often relates to moving forward or producing, which aligns with the concept of future generations.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.956Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5615, 'english-2026-05-24-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'What does “recalcitrant” mean?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Helpful"},{"id":"B","text":"Obedient"},{"id":"C","text":"Stubborn"},{"id":"D","text":"Generous"}]', 0, 'C', '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks for the definition or meaning of the word “recalcitrant”.

### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Analyze the contextual usage and synonyms of the word “recalcitrant”.

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- “Recalcitrant” describes someone who has an obstinately uncooperative attitude toward authority or discipline.

- Common synonyms for this word include stubborn, defiant, rebellious, or uncooperative.

- Options (A) “Helpful”, (B) “Obedient”, and (D) “Generous” are positive traits and generally contrast with or are unrelated to the meaning of recalcitrant.

#### Step 4: Final Answer:

Thus, the correct meaning of “recalcitrant” is “Stubborn”.

Quick Tip: The word recalcitrant comes from the Latin root “recalcitrare”, which literally means “to kick back”. This can help you associate the word with stubborn or resistant behavior.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.956Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5616, 'english-2026-05-24-shift-1', 'q03', 3, 3, 'Choose the correct meaning of “furtherance.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"Delay"},{"id":"B","text":"Advancement"},{"id":"C","text":"Opposition"},{"id":"D","text":"Refusal"}]', 0, 'B', '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks to identify the correct definition of the noun “furtherance”.

### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Relate the root verb “further” to the noun form to deduce its meaning.

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- The verb “further” means to help forward or promote.

- Adding the suffix “-ance” creates a noun that describes the state or action of promoting or helping progress.

- Hence, “furtherance” means the advancement or scheme of promoting a plan, interest, or policy.

- Options (A) “Delay”, (C) “Opposition”, and (D) “Refusal” act as antonyms or contrasting terms.

Step 4: Final Answer:

Therefore, the correct meaning of “furtherance” is “Advancement”.

Quick Tip: Identifying the root word “further” can easily guide you to “advancement” because both imply moving forward or progressing.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.957Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5617, 'english-2026-05-24-shift-1', 'q04', 4, 4, 'Identify the grammatically correct sentence.', '[{"id":"A","text":"He do not play cricket."},{"id":"B","text":"They has finished the task."},{"id":"C","text":"She enjoys reading novels."},{"id":"D","text":"I were waiting outside."}]', 0, 'C', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

We need to check each given sentence for grammatical correctness, specifically looking at subject-verb agreement and auxiliary verb usage.

##### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Analyze the subject and the verb of each sentence:

- Singular subjects require singular verbs.

- Plural subjects require plural verbs.

##### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- Option (A) "He do not play cricket." is incorrect. "He" is a singular subject, so it must take the singular auxiliary verb "does". The correct sentence is "He does not play cricket."

- Option (B) "They has finished the task." is incorrect. "They" is a plural subject and must take the plural auxiliary verb "have". The correct sentence is "They have finished the task."

- Option (C) "She enjoys reading novels." is correct. The singular subject "She" agrees with the singular verb "enjoys".

- Option (D) "I were waiting outside." is incorrect. The first-person singular pronoun "I" takes the singular past tense verb "was". The correct sentence is "I was waiting outside."

Step 4: Final Answer:

The grammatically correct sentence is “She enjoys reading novels.”

Quick Tip: Always double-check subject-verb agreement first when asked to find grammatically correct sentences, as this is one of the most common testing points.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.957Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5618, 'english-2026-05-24-shift-1', 'q05', 5, 5, 'Choose the correct passive voice of: “She completed the project.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"The project completed by her."},{"id":"B","text":"The project was completed by her."},{"id":"C","text":"The project has completed her."},{"id":"D","text":"The project is completed by she."}]', 0, 'B', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

We need to convert the given active voice sentence into passive voice.

### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Identify the components of the active sentence:

- Subject: “She”

- Verb: “completed” (Simple Past)

- Object: “the project”

The structure of a passive sentence in Simple Past is:

Object + was/were + past participle of verb + by + Subject (objective pronoun)

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- Apply the passive conversion rule:

1. Bring the object to the front: “The project”

2. Choose the correct auxiliary verb based on tense and number: “was” (since the project is singular and the active verb is in the past tense).

3. Write the past participle of the main verb: “completed”

4. Add the preposition “by” followed by the object pronoun of the original subject (“she” becomes “her”): “by her”

- This gives the sentence: “The project was completed by her.”

- Option (A) lacks the auxiliary verb “was”.

- Option (C) uses present perfect tense unnecessarily.

- Option (D) incorrectly uses present tense “is” and the subject pronoun “she” instead of the

object pronoun “her”.

#### Step 4: Final Answer:

The correct passive voice sentence is “The project was completed by her.”

Quick Tip: Always preserve the tense of the active voice sentence. Since the original sentence is in the simple past tense, the passive voice must contain “was” or “were”.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.957Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5619, 'english-2026-05-24-shift-1', 'q06', 6, 6, 'Choose the sentence with the correct use of “suite.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"He wore a black suite to the party."},{"id":"B","text":"We booked a luxury suite in the hotel."},{"id":"C","text":"The lawyer filed a suite in court."},{"id":"D","text":"This shirt suite you well."}]', 0, 'B', '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question tests the correct usage of the word “suite” as distinguished from “suit”.

### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Determine the definition of each term to see which sentence uses “suite” correctly:

- “Suite” refers to a set of connected rooms, especially in a hotel.

- “Suit” refers to a set of outer clothes, a legal action in court, or a verb meaning to be appropriate or look good on someone.

Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- Option (A) uses “suite” incorrectly; it should be “suit” because it refers to clothing.

- Option (B) uses “suite” correctly to refer to a set of hotel rooms.

- Option (C) uses “suite” incorrectly; it should be “suit” because it refers to a lawsuit.

- Option (D) uses “suite” incorrectly; it should be “suits” as a verb meaning to look good on someone.

### Step 4: Final Answer:

The correct sentence is “We booked a luxury suite in the hotel.”

Quick Tip: Remember that “suite” is pronounced like “sweet” and is associated with hotel rooms, while “suit” is pronounced like “soot” and refers to clothing or law.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.957Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5620, 'english-2026-05-24-shift-1', 'q08', 7, 7, 'Which sentence is the most logical beginning of a paragraph about digital education?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Therefore, students performed better in exams."},{"id":"B","text":"As a result, schools introduced smart classrooms."},{"id":"C","text":"Technology has changed modern education significantly."},{"id":"D","text":"Consequently, online learning became common."}]', 0, 'C', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

We need to identify the most suitable topic or introductory sentence to start a paragraph about digital education.

#### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

The opening sentence of a paragraph should introduce the core theme independently. It should not contain transitional words or phrases that refer to prior arguments or events.

#### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- Option (A) starts with “Therefore”, which is used to introduce a conclusion. This implies preceding context.

- Option (B) starts with “As a result”, indicating a consequence of an event mentioned earlier.

- Option (D) starts with “Consequently”, which also shows a cause-and-effect relationship from prior context.

- Option (C) introduces a broad, independent statement about how technology affects education. This is a classic topic sentence and provides a logical start.

### Step 4: Final Answer:

The most logical beginning sentence is “Technology has changed modern education significantly.”

Quick Tip: Avoid choosing sentences that begin with transitional adverbs like “therefore”, “consequently”, “however”, or “as a result” for introductory positions, as they require prior sentences to make sense.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.957Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5621, 'english-2026-05-24-shift-1', 'q10', 8, 8, 'Which pair is an example of homophones?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Accept – Refuse"},{"id":"B","text":"Pale - Pail"},{"id":"C","text":"Fast - Slow"},{"id":"D","text":"Council – Meeting"}]', 0, 'B', '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

We need to identify which word pair consists of homophones.

### Step 2: Key Formula or Approach:

Homophones are words that share the exact same pronunciation but differ in meaning, spelling, or both.

### Step 3: Detailed Explanation:

- Option (A) “Accept” and “Refuse” are antonyms with different pronunciations and spellings.

- Option (B) “Pale” (light-colored or white) and “Pail” (a bucket) are pronounced exactly the same but spelled differently and have different meanings. This matches the definition of homophones.

- Option (C) “Fast” and “Slow” are antonyms with different pronunciations.

- Option (D) "Council" and "Meeting" are related concepts but do not share pronunciation or spelling.

#### Step 4: Final Answer:

The correct pair of homophones is “Pale – Pail”.

Quick Tip: Say the words out loud. If they sound completely identical but mean different things and are spelled differently, they are homophones.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.957Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5622, 'english-2026-05-25-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Choose the word which is closest in meaning to salubrious.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Harmful"},{"id":"B","text":"Healthy"},{"id":"C","text":"Expensive"},{"id":"D","text":"Bitter"}]', 0, 'B', '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The objective of this question is to identify the synonym or the word that is closest in meaning to the given vocabulary word, "salubrious".

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Meaning of Salubrious: The word "salubrious" is an adjective used to describe

something that is favorable to or promoting health or well-being.

• Etymology: It originates from the Latin word "salubris", which comes from "salus", meaning health, safety, or welfare.

• Analysis of Option (A) - Harmful: This word refers to something causing or likely to cause damage, injury, or harm. This is the opposite of the target word.

• Analysis of Option (B) - Healthy: This term means beneficial to physical health or promoting physical well-being. This corresponds directly to the definition of salubrious, making it the correct option.

• Analysis of Option (C) - Expensive: This term refers to something costing a high price or a large sum of money. It has no relevance to health or well-being.

• Analysis of Option (D) - Bitter: This term describes a sharp, pungent, or unpleasant taste, or a feeling of deep anger and resentment. It is semantically distant from salubrious.

- Usage in Context: One might refer to a "salubrious climate" to describe a pleasant, health-giving environment that helps a patient recover from an illness.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

Therefore, the word closest in meaning to "salubrious" is "Healthy", which corresponds to option (B).

Quick Tip: Familiarizing yourself with Latin and Greek roots can boost your performance in vocabulary sections.

The root "sal-" or "salu-" consistently relates to health, safety, or wellness, as seen in words like salutary, salute, and salvage.

If you spot this root in an unfamiliar word, look for options that focus on health or positive well-being.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.958Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5623, 'english-2026-05-25-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'Select the antonym of the word expedite.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Delay"},{"id":"B","text":"Complete"},{"id":"C","text":"Improve"},{"id":"D","text":"Accelerate"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question requires us to find the antonym, which is the word opposite in meaning, to the vocabulary word "expedite".

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Meaning of Expedite: To "expedite" is a verb that means to make an action or process happen sooner or be accomplished more quickly.

• Etymology: It is derived from the Latin word "expeditus", which is the past participle of "expedire", meaning to extricate, set free, or literally "to free the feet" from shackles.

• Analysis of Option (A) - Delay: To "delay" means to make someone or something late, slow, or to postpone an action. This is the direct opposite of speeding up a process,

making it the correct antonym.

• Analysis of Option (B) - Complete: To "complete" means to finish making or doing something. While expediting can help complete a task faster, completion itself is not the antonym of expedition.

• Analysis of Option (C) - Improve: To "improve" means to make or become better in quality or status. It refers to quality enhancement rather than speed.

• Analysis of Option (D) - Accelerate: To "accelerate" means to undergo a rapid increase in speed or to hasten a process. This is a synonym of expedite, not an antonym.

• Contextual Usage: A company might pay extra money to "expedite" shipping so that a package arrives in one day instead of one week.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

Consequently, the word opposite in meaning to "expedite" is "Delay", which corresponds to option (A).

Quick Tip: When looking for antonyms, be careful not to accidentally select a synonym, as synonyms are often included as distractors.

In this question, "accelerate" is a strong synonym designed to test your focus.

Always read all four choices carefully before making your final selection.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.958Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5624, 'english-2026-05-25-shift-1', 'q03', 3, 3, 'Choose the correctly spelt word.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Orninthology"},{"id":"B","text":"Orinthology"},{"id":"C","text":"Ornithology"},{"id":"D","text":"Ornethology"}]', 0, 'C', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The objective of this question is to identify the single correctly spelled word from the four given spelling variations.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Meaning of Ornithology: "Ornithology" is a noun that refers to the scientific branch of zoology that focuses on the study of birds.

• Etymological Roots: The word comes from the Greek "ornithos", which means bird, and the suffix "-logia", which means the study of a subject. Combining these yields "ornith-o-logy".

• Analysis of Option (A) - Orninthology: This spelling is incorrect because it introduces an extra, silent letter "n" in the second syllable, creating "orninth-", which is not a valid root.

• Analysis of Option (B) - Orinthology: This spelling is incorrect because it omits the first "n" entirely, leading to "orinth-", which fails to match the proper pronunciation and spelling.

• Analysis of Option (C) - Ornithology: This is the exact correct spelling of the word, adhering perfectly to its classical Greek origins of "ornis" or "ornitho-".

• Analysis of Option (D) - Ornethology: This spelling is incorrect because it replaces the vowel "i" in the third syllable with an "e", creating "-neth-", which is grammatically

wrong.

• Significance of Spelling: Spelling errors in academic words often occur around transition vowels or repeated consonants, so understanding the base root helps eliminate errors.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correctly spelled word is "Ornithology", which corresponds to option (C).

Quick Tip: For scientific studies and academic fields, breaking the word into its prefix, root, and suffix is highly effective.

The suffix "-ology" is standard for fields of study, so your main focus should be on the correct form of the root.

Knowing that "ornith-" is the standard scientific prefix for birds immediately leads to the correct spelling.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.958Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5625, 'english-2026-05-25-shift-1', 'q04', 4, 4, 'The idiom axe to grind means:', '[{"id":"A","text":"To sharpen a weapon"},{"id":"B","text":"To have a selfish motive"},{"id":"C","text":"To work very hard"},{"id":"D","text":"To end a friendship"}]', 0, 'B', '#### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question requires us to determine the idiomatic meaning of the common English phrase "an axe to grind".

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Idiomatic Definition: The phrase "to have an axe to grind" means to have a strong personal opinion or a selfish, hidden motive for doing something.

- Origin of the Idiom: The idiom is historical, commonly associated with an essay written by Charles Miner in 1810 or sometimes attributed to Benjamin Franklin.

- The Origin Story: In the story, a man flatters a young boy into turning a heavy grindstone so that the man can sharpen his axe. Once the axe is sharp, the man walks away without thanking the boy. This represents using others for one''s own private motives.

• Analysis of Option (A) - To sharpen a weapon: This is the literal meaning of grinding an axe, which is incorrect because idioms carry a figurative meaning rather than a literal one.

• Analysis of Option (B) - To have a selfish motive: This matches the figurative meaning of having a private interest or hidden agenda to serve. Therefore, it is correct.

• Analysis of Option (C) - To work very hard: While turning a grindstone is hard work, this is not the figurative meaning of the idiom.

• Analysis of Option (D) - To end a friendship: This is unrelated to the concept of private motives or axes, making it incorrect.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The idiomatic meaning of "axe to grind" is "To have a selfish motive", corresponding to option (B).

Quick Tip: Idioms in English examinations almost never mean what they literally say.

If you see an option that describes the physical, literal action of the words in the idiom, such as "sharpening a weapon", you can safely eliminate it.

Look for figurative meanings that involve human behavior, motives, or relationships.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.958Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5626, 'english-2026-05-25-shift-1', 'q05', 5, 5, 'Choose the meaning of the idiom run down.', '[{"id":"A","text":"To criticize someone"},{"id":"B","text":"To move upward"},{"id":"C","text":"To decorate something"},{"id":"D","text":"To discover a secret"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The task is to identify the correct idiomatic meaning of the phrasal verb or idiom "run down" from the provided options.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Idiomatic Definition: The phrasal verb "run down" has multiple standard meanings. One of its main figurative meanings is to criticize, disparage, or speak poorly of someone in an unfair or persistent manner.

- Other Definitions: It can also mean to lose power or stop working (like a battery), to be in poor physical condition (exhausted), or to hit someone with a vehicle.

• Analysis of Option (A) - To criticize someone: This corresponds to the common idiomatic usage where one "runs someone down" to lower their reputation. It is the

correct option here.

• Analysis of Option (B) - To move upward: This is the literal opposite of the word "down" and is not an idiomatic meaning of "run down".

• Analysis of Option (C) - To decorate something: This is completely unrelated to any known definition of "run down".

• Analysis of Option (D) - To discover a secret: This might be confused with other phrasal verbs like "run to ground" or "track down", but it does not represent "run down".

Example Sentence: "It is not professional to constantly run down your former employer during job interviews."

### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct meaning of the idiom "run down" is "To criticize someone", which is represented by option (A).

Quick Tip: Phrasal verbs can have multiple meanings depending on context.

When preparing for competitive exams, study the multiple definitions of common phrasal verbs containing verbs like run, put, look, and take.

In this question, looking for the most common figurative meaning helps identify "to criticize someone" as the correct choice.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.958Z', NULL, NULL, 'banner', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5627, 'english-2026-05-25-shift-1', 'q06', 6, 6, 'Choose the word opposite in meaning to impede.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Obstruct"},{"id":"B","text":"Delay"},{"id":"C","text":"Assist"},{"id":"D","text":"Prevent"}]', 0, 'C', '##### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The question asks us to identify the word that is opposite in meaning (antonym) to the word "impede".

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Meaning of Impede: To "impede" means to delay, hinder, or obstruct someone or something by placing obstacles in their path.

• Etymology: It comes from the Latin verb "impedire", which literally means to "shackle the feet", derived from "in-" (into) and "pes" (foot).

• Analysis of Option (A) - Obstruct: To "obstruct" means to block or get in the way of progress. This is a synonym of impede, not an antonym.

• Analysis of Option (B) - Delay: To "delay" means to make something slow or late. This is a common consequence of impeding, making it a closely related synonym.

• Analysis of Option (C) - Assist: To "assist" means to help, facilitate, or support someone or something to make progress easier. This is the direct opposite of impeding, making it the correct antonym.

• Analysis of Option (D) - Prevent: To "prevent" means to stop something from happening or to hinder completely. This is a synonym of impede.

Example Sentence: "The heavy snow storm did not impede the rescue efforts, as local volunteers arrived to assist the emergency crews."

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

Thus, the word opposite in meaning to "impede" is "Assist", which matches option (C).

Quick Tip: Understanding prefixes can help you quickly identify the orientation of a word.

The prefix "im-" or "in-" often carries a negative or restrictive meaning, while the root "ped-" refers to feet.

Conversely, words that mean to help often have positive connotations.

This contrast makes it easier to spot "assist" as the correct opposing word.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.958Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5628, 'english-2026-05-25-shift-1', 'q08', 7, 7, 'Choose the correct meaning of the word philately.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Study of coins"},{"id":"B","text":"Collection of stamps"},{"id":"C","text":"Study of birds"},{"id":"D","text":"Collection of paintings"}]', 0, 'B', '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The goal of this question is to identify the precise definition of the noun "philately" from the given list of options.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Definition of Philately: "Philately" is defined as the collection, study, and appreciation of postage stamps and related postal history materials.

• Etymology: The term was coined in 1864 by the French stamp collector Georges Herpin. It is derived from the Greek words "philos" (meaning loving or fond of) and "ateleia" (meaning exemption from tax or duty, which refers to the postage stamp indicating prepaying of fees).

• Analysis of Option (A) - Study of coins: The scientific study or collection of coins, tokens, and paper money is called "numismatics", making this option incorrect.

• Analysis of Option (B) - Collection of stamps: This is the exact definition of philately, making it the correct option.

• Analysis of Option (C) - Study of birds: The scientific study of birds is called "ornithology", so this option is incorrect.

• Analysis of Option (D) - Collection of paintings: There is no single classical term like philately for this, but it is typically referred to as art collecting or curation.

• Contextual Relevance: Philatelists are people who collect stamps, often valuing them for historical, geographical, and artistic significance.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct meaning of "philately" is "Collection of stamps", which is option (B).

Quick Tip: In competitive English exams, questions on "one-word substitutions" or "specialized terms" are very common.

Make a list of terms ending in "-ology" (the study of) and "-y" or "-ics" relating to hobbies or collections. Memorizing these terms systematically can help you secure easy marks.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.958Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5629, 'english-2026-05-25-shift-1', 'q09', 8, 8, 'Choose the correct meaning of the word numismatics.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Study of birds"},{"id":"B","text":"Collection of stamps"},{"id":"C","text":"Study or collection of coins"},{"id":"D","text":"Study of plants"}]', 0, 'C', '### Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The objective of this question is to find the correct meaning of the term "numismatics" from the given choices.

Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

- Definition of Numismatics: "Numismatics" refers to the scientific study or collection of currency, which includes coins, paper money, tokens, medals, and related objects.

- Etymology: It is derived from the Latin word "numismatis", which comes from the Greek "nomisma", meaning coin, custom, or usage.

• Analysis of Option (A) - Study of birds: This is known as "ornithology", so it is incorrect.

• Analysis of Option (B) - Collection of stamps: This is known as "philately", which was the focus of the previous question, making it incorrect.

• Analysis of Option (C) - Study or collection of coins: This perfectly matches the definition of numismatics, making it the correct choice.

• Analysis of Option (D) - Study of plants: The scientific study of plants is "botany", which is incorrect here.

Practical Application: Numismatists play an important role in historical research, as studying old coins helps determine the economic state, trade routes, and metallurgy of ancient civilizations.

#### Step 3: Final Answer:

The correct meaning of "numismatics" is "Study or collection of coins", corresponding to option (C).

Quick Tip: Notice how questions 8 and 9 are closely related.

Often, options in one question can provide hints or answers for other questions in the same paper.

Keeping track of terms like philately and numismatics helps build a strong vocabulary for competitive examinations.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.959Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5630, 'english-2026-05-25-shift-1', 'q10', 9, 9, 'Choose the word closest in meaning to judicial.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Legal"},{"id":"B","text":"Musical"},{"id":"C","text":"Medical"},{"id":"D","text":"Historical"}]', 0, 'A', 'Step 1: Understanding the Question:

The goal is to find the word closest in meaning (synonym) to "judicial" from the given options.

#### Step 2: Detailed Explanation:

• Meaning of Judicial: "Judicial" is an adjective that relates to a court of law, the administration of justice, or the office of a judge.

• Etymology: It originates from the Latin "judicialis", which comes from "judicium" (judgment) and "judex" (judge).

• Analysis of Option (A) - Legal: "Legal" relates to the law, its administration, or the court system. Because the judicial branch administers the law, "judicial" is highly related to "legal". Among the options, it is the closest synonym.

• Analysis of Option (B) - Musical: This relates to music, performance, or sound, which is entirely unrelated to the court system.

• Analysis of Option (C) - Medical: This relates to the science or practice of medicine and healing, which has no connection to the judiciary.

• Analysis of Option (D) - Historical: This relates to history or past events, which is not synonymous with judicial matters.

• Contextual Usage: A "judicial review" is a legal process in which a court reviews the lawfulness of a decision or action made by a public body.

##### Step 3: Final Answer:

The word closest in meaning to "judicial" is "Legal", corresponding to option (A).

Quick Tip: Always look for the most logical connection when choosing synonyms.

The root "jud-" always refers to law, judgment, or justice, as seen in words like judge, judgment, and judicious.

Connecting the root "jud-" to "law" leads you directly to the correct option "Legal".', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.959Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5631, 'english-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q01', 1, 1, 'Match the word "ventriloquism" with its correct meaning.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Speaking in a way that makes the voice seem to come from somewhere else"},{"id":"B","text":"Using too many words to express an idea"},{"id":"C","text":"Fear of public speaking"},{"id":"D","text":"Speaking informally"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept: Ventriloquism is the art of speaking in such a way that the sound appears to come from another source, usually a puppet or dummy.

Step 1: Understand the word root.

The word originates from:

Latin: “venter” = belly, “loqui” = to speak

Historically, it meant:

"speaking from the stomach"

Step 2: Identify the correct meaning.

A ventriloquist creates the illusion that:

• The voice comes from somewhere else

- Another object or puppet is speaking

Thus:

Option (A)

Quick Tip: Remember:

Ventriloquism  $ \rightarrow $ Throwing the voice

Usually associated with puppets and stage performances.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.959Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5632, 'english-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q02', 2, 2, 'What does “circumlocution” mean?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Silent behaviour"},{"id":"B","text":"Speaking indirectly using many words"},{"id":"C","text":"Humorous imitation"},{"id":"D","text":"Deep hatred for society"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: Circumlocution refers to using unnecessary or indirect words instead of speaking clearly and briefly.

Step 1: Understand the word origin.

The word comes from:

“circum” = around

and

“locution” = speech

Thus, it literally means:

"speaking around the point"

Step 2: Identify the correct meaning.

Circumlocution involves:

• Using too many words

• Speaking indirectly

- Avoiding direct expression

Thus:

Option (B)

Quick Tip: Remember:

Circumlocution  $ \rightarrow $ Indirect lengthy speech

Example: Instead of saying “He died,” saying:

“He passed away from this mortal world.”', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.959Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5633, 'english-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q04', 3, 3, 'A “colloquialism” is:', '[{"id":"A","text":"A scientific theory"},{"id":"B","text":"A formal legal phrase"},{"id":"C","text":"An informal everyday expression"},{"id":"D","text":"A poetic device"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: A colloquialism is a word or phrase commonly used in informal conversation.

Step 1: Understand colloquial language.

Colloquial language:

• Is informal

• Is used in daily speech

• May vary regionally

Step 2: Identify the correct meaning.

Examples of colloquial expressions:

• “What''s up?”

• “Gonna”

• “Buddy”

These are informal everyday expressions.

Thus:

Option (C)

Quick Tip: Remember:

Used commonly in conversation rather than formal writing.

Colloquialism  $ \rightarrow $ Informal speech', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.959Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5634, 'english-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q05', 4, 4, 'Choose the correctly spelt word.', '[{"id":"A","text":"Cristallize"},{"id":"B","text":"Crystallise"},{"id":"C","text":"Crystallize"},{"id":"D","text":"Crystalize"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: Correct spelling is important in English vocabulary and grammar.

Step 1: Examine each option carefully.

• Cristallize  $ \rightarrow $ Incorrect spelling

• Crystallise → British variant exists, but standard accepted form here is “Crystallize”

• Crystallize → Correct spelling

• Crystalize → Missing one “l”

Step 2: Identify the standard correct spelling.

The correctly spelt word is:

Crystallize

Quick Tip: Remember:

Crystal + lize  $ \rightarrow $ Crystallize

Watch for double letters in spellings.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.959Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5635, 'english-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q06', 5, 5, 'Fill in the blank with the correct verb.

The fusion band ___ playing since 9 a.m.', '[{"id":"A","text":"have been"},{"id":"B","text":"has been"},{"id":"C","text":"were"},{"id":"D","text":"are"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: Subject-verb agreement depends on whether the subject is singular or plural.

Step 1: Identify the subject.

The subject is:

The fusion band

“Band” is a collective noun treated as singular here.

Step 2: Identify the tense.

The phrase:

since 9 a.m.

indicates an action continuing from the past to the present.

Hence:

Present Perfect Continuous Tense

Step 3: Choose correct auxiliary verb.

For singular subject:

has been

is correct.

Thus:

has been

Quick Tip: Remember:

Singular Subject  $ \rightarrow $ has been

Plural Subject  $ \rightarrow $ have been

“Since” usually indicates perfect tense.', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.960Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5636, 'english-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q07', 6, 6, 'Choose the antonym of “misanthropic.”', '[{"id":"A","text":"Hostile"},{"id":"B","text":"Cruel"},{"id":"C","text":"Sociable"},{"id":"D","text":"Bitter"}]', 0, 'C', 'Concept: “Misanthropic” refers to hatred or dislike of people and society.

Step 1: Understand the meaning of misanthropic.

A misanthropic person:

• Avoids people

• Dislikes society

• Is unfriendly toward others

Step 2: Find the opposite meaning.

The opposite of hatred or dislike for people is:

Friendly and social behaviour

Hence:

Sociable

is the antonym.

Therefore:

Sociable

Quick Tip: Remember:

Misanthrope  $ \rightarrow $ Person who dislikes people

Sociable  $ \rightarrow $ Friendly and social', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.960Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5637, 'english-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q09', 7, 7, 'Choose the correct phrasal verb to complete the sentence.

Despite many difficulties, she managed to ___ the challenge successfully.', '[{"id":"A","text":"pull off"},{"id":"B","text":"break down"},{"id":"C","text":"give away"},{"id":"D","text":"call out"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept: Phrasal verbs are combinations of verbs and prepositions/adverbs that create special meanings.

Step 1: Understand the sentence meaning.

The sentence indicates:

• She faced many difficulties

• Yet she succeeded

Thus, the required phrasal verb should mean:

“to succeed in doing something difficult”

Step 2: Analyze the options.

• Pull off → Successfully achieve something difficult

• Break down → Stop functioning or become emotional

• Give away  $ \rightarrow $ Donate or reveal

• Call out → Shout or summon

Thus:

Pull off

Quick Tip: Remember:

Pull off  $ \rightarrow $ Achieve successfully

Example:

“She pulled off a difficult performance.”', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.960Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5638, 'english-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q10', 8, 8, 'Fill in the blank with the correct word.

The British Royal Society became a subject of laughter because of its ___ attitude.', '[{"id":"A","text":"vain"},{"id":"B","text":"wane"},{"id":"C","text":"vein"},{"id":"D","text":"vane"}]', 0, 'A', 'Concept: Homophones are words that sound similar but have different meanings.

Step 1: Understand the sentence context.

The sentence suggests:

• The society was laughed at

• Due to pride or excessive self-importance

##### Step 2: Understand meanings of options.

• Vain  $ \rightarrow $ Excessively proud

• Wane  $ \rightarrow $ Decrease gradually

• Vein  $ \rightarrow $ Blood vessel

• Vane → Device showing wind direction

Only “vain” fits the context.

Thus:

vain

##### Quick Tip: Remember:

Vain  $ \rightarrow $ Proud / Arrogant

Do not confuse with:

Vein  $ \rightarrow $ Blood vessel', NULL, 'null', '[]', '[]', '[1]', 'keyed', '[]', '2026-11-05T20:59:12.960Z', NULL, NULL, 'ai_draft', 'unreviewed', NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO practice_questions ("id", "paper_slug", "question_key", "source_question_no", "position", "stem", "options_json", "options_are_images", "correct_option", "explanation", "passage_key", "match_list_json", "arrangement_json", "assets_json", "source_pages_json", "review_status", "review_flags_json", "created_at", "updated_at", "deleted_at", "explanation_source", "explanation_status", "reviewed_by", "reviewed_at") VALUES (5639, 'english-2026-05-26-shift-1', 'q11', 9, 9, 'What does “treacherous” mean?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Loyal"},{"id":"B","text":"Dangerous and untrustworthy"},{"id":"C","text":"Honest"},{"id":"D","text":"Intelligent"}]', 0, 'B', 'Concept: Treacherous describes a person or situation that is deceptive, dangerous, or unreliable.

